window.VHPEOPLE={"shop:keep":{"name":"Dunla Marrow","age":47,"role":"gate-trader in kit, arrows and bread; retired escort rider","look":"Rangy and sun-dark, a grey braid tied off with a length of bowstring, two fingers gone from her left hand at the second knuckle.","voice":"Prices first, opinions second, and both fast; taps the counter with her short fingers when she's thinking.","greet":"Warm hearth. Arrows are a copper for three, bread's free if you look hungry enough. You look about halfway.","lines":["Lord's lamp was up past midnight again. Boots. Means nails by morning. Buy your arrows now, the price goes up when Rhosk starts reading.","Smeltgate wants our riders for nothing. I rode for nothing once. You see these fingers? That's what nothing costs.","See the proud nail, low on the left, by the hinge? Mine. Twelve years. No, I don't want to talk about it. Yes, I'll sell you a pack."],"about":"I rode escort on the east road for nineteen years and I was good at it, and then for one afternoon I wasn't. Now I sell other people the things I used to need. It's quieter. I don't sleep better, but it's quieter.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, with road swearing"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"enough to haggle with Low Reach horse-sellers and be rude back"}],"childhood":"Dunla grew up in the lowest ring of the Hearthmount terraces, where the Cinderway's glow comes up through the street drains and nobody owns a lamp. Her mother was a fletcher who could split a goose feather with her thumbnail; Dunla's first job, at five, was gluing vanes and getting told off for crooked ones. Supper was a training ground. Her mother made each child refuse one thing at the table every night, a crust, the salt, a chore, an opinion, so that by the First No they would know how it felt in the mouth. Dunla refused the same thing every night for a year: going to bed. She lost every time. She remembers that as the happiest year of her life, which embarrasses her.","schooling":"A bakehouse apprenticeship on Ovenrow under Mistress Gorse, who made the children knead with their eyes shut 'so your hands learn before your head starts lying to them.' Dunla's loaves were tough and even. At her Lamp Test the Ember lamp gave one flicker and went out: a Half-Spark, not enough for Korrhaven. She left the bakehouse at fourteen to ride for an uncle's carting firm and never finished her letters.","history":"She rode carts first, then escorts, then led them. For nineteen years she took merchants east along the road to Smeltgate and beyond, posting her contracts on the Keep's gate like everyone else and flattening her nails one by one; she has forty-one flat ones and can point to each. Twelve years ago she took the contract of an ingot-merchant, Abel Slake of Smeltgate, and lost him at a bend called the Cutter's Elbow, where a bandit with a hatchet took two of her fingers and Abel's life in the same minute. She brought his body home across her own saddle. His widow paid the contract anyway, in full, by courier. Dunla sent the money back. The nail still stands. She stopped riding the next season, rented the stall by the gate, and has sold kit to riders since, hammering her prices up whenever a new contract is posted, which the garrison calls the Marrow Toll. Her son Fynn copies road-maps for the quartermaster and marks the bandit camps in red. She checks his dates. She checks them twice.","family":"Her son Fynn, nineteen, map-copyist. His father was a Stormreach chain-wright passing through who still sends a shouted apology every Great Turning by courier, which she reads aloud to the stall in a bad accent.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"He wouldn't cut his citadel's chain. She won't cut a dead man's contract loose either, and she knows exactly how much that's cost her."},"band":{"act":"The Bellows","realm":"Flameborne","why":"She went to a Bellows show in Hearthmount the year after the Elbow, pumped the crowd-forge with both hands, and for the length of one song forgot she was short two fingers.","rv_vote":"The Bellows, because they'll lose to something polite from Lightforge and somebody should be loyal."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the road levy: it's raised in Flameborne's name and never once paid for a rider on the east road; the Keep's nails do that.","edict":"Against. She'll sell bread to any riot that comes past, and arrows to nobody."},"wants":"To be able to walk past the gate at dawn without looking low and to the left.","site":"keep","town":"Vashen’s Keep","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:keep":{"name":"Tobin Ashgrove","age":61,"role":"garrison smith and maker of every nail on the Contract Gate","look":"Short, very wide, bald and freckled from sparks, with a leather apron so old it has gone stiff and shiny like a beetle's wing.","voice":"Slow, plain, one thought at a time; counts things under his breath while you talk.","greet":"Mind the bucket. Shoes, blades or a nail? Nails I don't sell. You earn those.","lines":["Three thousand four hundred and twelve. That's nails I've forged for that gate. You'll want to know how many stand proud. Forty-four. I know which ones.","Rhosk reads the gate like it's his gate. It's my gate. I made every bit of it but the wood.","Lord came down at midnight for a new boot-nail. Said nothing. Polished it on his sleeve. Smeltgate's going to post. You watch."],"about":"I make nails. I make other things, blades, shoes, a hinge if you're nice, but nails are what I'm for. Every contract on that gate, somebody wanted something done and somebody went and did it, and I made the thing that held the paper up. That's enough of a life for me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, slow"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting; prices and numbers fine, small talk hopeless"}],"childhood":"Tobin was born in Scaldwater, a forge-town strung along a lava-vein north of the Coal Road, the fourth son of a wheelwright with a temper that made the neighbours shut their doors, which in Flameborne is saying something. The house was all shouting, and Tobin, being small and slow, learned that the quickest way out of an argument was to start counting something until it was over: spokes, rivets, steps, ravens. He still does it. His mother kept bees on the roof in a hive made from an old cart wheel, and the one quiet place in the house was up beside it, where Tobin sat counting bees in and out and decided early that he did not need to win anything to be content.","schooling":"No proper school: the Scaldwater bakehouse took him for a season and sent him back for eating the proving dough. His aunt Bryony Ashgrove, a nail-smith, took him at eight and had him forge one nail a day for a full year before he was allowed to make anything else. 'A nail first, then a knife, then a lord's gate.' The Lamp Test lit nothing for him. He says the lamps were shy.","history":"He worked Bryony's forge until she died and left it to his eldest brother, who sold it, so at twenty-two Tobin walked down the Coal Road to the Keep, where the old lord, the present Lord Vashen's father, needed a smith who would not drink on duty. He has been there thirty-nine years. He forges the garrison's shoes and blades, mends the yard grates and hinges, and makes every nail the Contract Gate takes, each with a small mark on the head nobody else notices: a dot for a fair contract, a ring for a hard one. Tally marks for every nail cover the beam above his anvil in chalk he renews at Slag Day. He has watched three generations of riders hammer their own names flat. He was the one who hammered Lord Vashen's first nail for him, when the boy was fourteen and too proud to ask, because the boy missed twice and was about to cry. Neither of them has ever mentioned it. Tobin has never married and has never particularly wanted to, and the garrison long ago stopped trying to set him up with widows.","family":"Two surviving brothers in Scaldwater who write when they want money. He sends it.","hero":{"who":"Iska Vashen","why":"She refused the King over the road and he said 'Then you keep it,' and she didn't argue, she just went and did it. Tobin thinks that is the whole of a good life in five words."},"band":{"act":"Ash-Tongue","realm":"Flameborne","why":"He likes watching Varra Cinder come apart every show because he never has, not once, and it seems a good thing that somebody does it for him.","rv_vote":"Ash-Tongue, and he will tell you so, slowly, as many times as you ask."},"vote":{"local":"He keeps his Refusals for big things and has used only his First No. This year he means to stand at First Coal and refuse the King one thing: letting Lightforge's Edict through the Kiln Gates without a fight.","edict":"Against. He'll shoe every rioter's horse in the Realm for free, and he won't ride with them, because somebody has to keep the forge lit while everyone's shouting."},"wants":"To make the nail for a contract that clears the Cutter's Elbow for good, and to hammer it flat himself.","site":"keep","town":"Vashen’s Keep","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:keep":{"name":"Wenna Teller","age":9,"role":"sergeant's granddaughter, unofficial second reader of the Contract Gate","look":"All elbows and scabbed knees, ash-blonde hair hacked short with a knife after a burr incident, a wooden practice hammer stuck through her belt.","voice":"Loud, quick, swears like Kaelis Emberwarden and immediately looks round to see who heard.","greet":"Grandad reads the gate at dawn. I read it after, properly, with the good bits. Want me to read you one? Copper.","lines":["There's a new nail. Big job. Grandad said the pay was an insult to bread. That means it's a good one.","Smeltgate won't pay us to ride their road. Grandad says they can argue with a nail. I tried arguing with a nail once. The nail won.","When I'm big I'm going to hammer my own nail and the whole wall will shout my name. Once. Twice is showing off. I'll do it once. Probably."],"about":"I'm Wenna and I live at the Keep and my grandad is Sergeant Rhosk Teller who reads the gate, you've heard him, everyone's heard him. I'm doing my Lamp Test this winter in Hearthmount. I'm not scared. I just don't want the lamps to look at me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, and loud"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, mostly ones Gethin Durrow told her not to repeat"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"six words from Gethin: set, karn, dur, rugg, and two she won't say"}],"childhood":"Wenna was born in the Keep's yard, nearly literally: her mother went into labour during a Flattening and refused to go inside until the garrison had finished shouting the rider's name. She has grown up under the wall, among horses and men who smell of oil, and learned to read before she could tie her boots, from the contracts on the gate, standing on an upturned bucket while her grandad read them aloud with opinions. She thinks every adult has a nail somewhere and asks strangers where theirs is. Her best friend is the farrier's dog, Cinders, who bit a lord once. Her worst enemy is the quartermaster's cat. Her mother sells tallow candles to the garrison; her father rides patrol and comes home smelling of road, and she sniffs his coat to guess where he's been.","schooling":"The garrison's little school in the old toll-room, run by Dilys Penry, the quartermaster's wife, who teaches letters on the backs of paid contracts and once made the whole class write a contract for their own good behaviour and nail it to the classroom door. Wenna's has three proud nails. Her Lamp Test is this winter at the lamp-ring in Hearthmount, and she has been practising standing still.","history":"Nine years is not much life, but Wenna has filled hers. At six she climbed the Contract Gate to the top hinge to read a nail nobody could read and got stuck there until Tobin Ashgrove came out with a ladder and said nothing all the way down, which was worse than shouting. At seven she started copying her grandad's dawn reading, a beat behind him, and the garrison began laughing at her lines more than his. At eight she was caught teaching the stable boys Kaelis Emberwarden's swears off a ballad sheet and had her mouth rinsed with ash-water by her mother, which she says was worth it. She spends dawns on the wall-walk doing Hot Corner's dance moves where the sentries can see. She has asked Lord Vashen directly, twice, whether the boot at Clayford is his. The first time he walked off. The second time he said, 'Which boot?' and she has decided that means yes. She has a list for her First No, which is four years away; it is currently eleven items long and includes bedtimes and turnips.","family":"Grandad Rhosk, her mother Catrin, her father Owain who rides patrol, and a baby brother she considers a mistake.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"He's loud and funny and he swears at the Council and they still let him sit there. Wenna would like to do all of that, in order."},"band":{"act":"Hot Corner","realm":"Flameborne","why":"The sentries hum it on the wall and she learned the dance from a rider's daughter who saw them in Hearthmount; she does it badly and with enormous commitment.","rv_vote":"Hot Corner, and if her mother says she's too young to have a lamp she'll borrow Gethin's."},"vote":{"local":"She is too young for First Coal, but if she could refuse the King anything she would refuse him bedtimes on festival nights, for everybody.","edict":"Torn, in her own way: she heard it means Lightforge will write down all the spark children, and she likes being written down, but not by people who aren't from here."},"wants":"To hammer a nail flat on that gate and hear the wall shout her name.","site":"keep","town":"Vashen’s Keep","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:keep:b":{"name":"Gethin Durrow","age":55,"role":"night-wall sentry and gate-lamb cook; born at Hearthward","look":"Tall, heavy-shouldered, black hair gone iron at the sides, one grey thumbnail like a chip of granite; a tin on a cord round his neck and a flat river-stone in his coat pocket.","voice":"Low and even, Stonehold-slow until something annoys him, then sudden and Flameborne-quick; mixes 'Set' and 'Warm hearth' without noticing.","greet":"Set. Warm hearth. Pick whichever one you like, I'm both. You eaten? Lamb's an hour off.","lines":["Lord's lamp's been lit three nights. I'm on the wall, I see it. Man polishes boots like other men walk off a temper.","Smeltgate thinks a road's a road. A road is men on horses who'd like to be paid and come home. Post the contract.","I lift my stone in the morning and I check my coal at night. The garrison thinks I'm hedging. I am."],"about":"I was born at Hearthward, in the customs house, half on the basalt and half on the granite. My mother argued and my father didn't. I've been in Lord Vashen's garrison thirty-five years and I cook the lamb because nobody else will be patient with it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native; his father's, and heavier when he's tired"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, customs-house Bridge with the legal words"}],"childhood":"Gethin grew up in the customs house at Hearthward, where the floor is black basalt in the south rooms and grey granite in the north and his mother, a Flameborne tally-clerk, and his father, a Stonehold mason, slept in a bed that straddled the join. Every morning he lifted a burden-stone with his father on the granite side; every evening he checked the Kept Coal with his mother on the basalt. The two bridgewardens were feuding the whole of his childhood over whose turn it was to oil the arch hinges, and Gethin, aged eight, was sent between them with messages and learned to translate insults into something both men could stand to hear. He thought every child had two greetings. He was eleven before he realised other children did not.","schooling":"A Stonehold road-school taught on the march: old Sett Harrun walked the children from Mile Nought to Mile Twelve and back, one marker a day, teaching letters from the initials carved in the stone and history from whose they were. Harrun made them carry their slates on their heads. The Lamp Test at Hearthward's lamp-ring: the Gravem lamp gave a low hum and a faint grey light, a Half-Spark. He did his First No at thirteen and his Shouldering at fifteen, and nobody in his family agreed on which mattered more.","history":"At twenty he quarrelled with his father over which side of Hearthward he'd work, the granite masons' or the basalt garrison's, and settled it by walking up the Coal Road and joining neither, signing on at Vashen's Keep instead. He has ridden in four of Lord Vashen's wars and patrolled the east road for most of thirty years. In his third war his friend Rook Halloran, Stonehold-born like his father, died at a river crossing; Gethin carried a stone on his back from the Keep to the Stonehold road, a road-mile past Mile Nought, so Rook's initials could go on Mile Nine. Then he cooked the whole garrison a gate-lamb the way Rook had liked it, slow, and has cooked it ever since. He moved to the night wall at fifty when his knees began to talk. He teaches Wenna Teller Holdspeech words that her mother objects to. His wife Maelis, a Kiln Gates mason's daughter, died of a fever six winters ago; the coal in the tin round his neck is from their hearth.","family":"His father, ninety and still lifting at Hearthward. They haven't spoken in six years. A married daughter in Hearthmount who visits at First Coal and makes him eat sitting down.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired and Hollis Brask","why":"They built Hearthward together and never married, and he thinks keeping a thing standing with someone you won't share a bed with is harder than either."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Forty veterans who never sing above a murmur; he hums along on night watch where the sentries can't hear, and the songs are about the dead being carried, which is his kind of song.","rv_vote":"The Lonely Line Choir, though he knows the Flameborne half of the garrison will never forgive him."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King one word: the heralds keep calling the Ember March 'ours' in proclamations, and the March is two Realms' or it's nobody's. In Stonehold he'd stand behind whichever road-elder speaks for the Mile Nought markers.","edict":"Against, but against the riot too. Stonehold refuses quietly, Flameborne breaks pots; he'd like a way that leaves his daughter's pots alone."},"wants":"To get his knees and his father to the same table before one of them gives out.","site":"keep","town":"Vashen’s Keep","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:cindermoot":{"name":"Dorran Fyle","age":44,"role":"trader in moot-seed, kit and salvage; hired chalk-writer on Lost-Voice Day","look":"Thin, neat, chalk dust permanently in the creases of his knuckles; a scarf wound high round a throat that never healed right.","voice":"A dry rasp barely above a whisper, so people lean in; precise, sly, never wastes a word because each one costs him.","greet":"Warm hearth. You'll have to come closer. Closer. There. Seed's hot, packs are cheap, I don't haggle out loud.","lines":["Two brothers in the Ring at dusk over a goat. Stand on the north side. The wind's from the west and one of them sweats oil.","Cinderhill will give us the same answer again this spring. I could write them a better question. I have, actually. For a fee.","Ione Brack has stood in three hundred duels and never fought one. Some say coward. I say she's the only one in town who's never had to wash a wall."],"about":"I sell seed and packs and whatever you've pulled off a dead bandit. And one day a year, when this whole town goes silent, I'm the loudest man in it, because I write the best chalk on the walls. People pay me to argue for them. I'm cheaper than losing.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, whispered"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, and he writes it better than he speaks it"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it slowly; learned to read Lightforge contract-law for fun"}],"childhood":"Dorran was a sickly boy in a town that settles everything at the top of its voice. Croup at four scarred his throat, and by five he could not raise his voice above a rasp. His elder brother Hal won every argument in the house by volume. Dorran began losing them, and then began writing them instead, in charcoal, on the house wall: the case for more honey, the case against Hal, numbered. His mother, a seller of trench-oil for the Ring, would come home, read the wall, laugh, and beat the rug against it to clean it off, and sometimes, about one time in five, give him the honey. That one time in five is, he says, the whole of his education in persuasion.","schooling":"The Little Ring, Cindermoot's moot-school, where children argue inside a circle of chalk. Master Corwen Spill scored each round with pebbles in a bowl and once made two children swap sides halfway and finish each other's cases. Dorran lost every spoken round for six years and won every written one, and Spill gave him a pebble anyway at the end of each term 'for spite.' The Lamp Test lit nothing. He was quietly relieved.","history":"At sixteen he went to work for his mother's oil trade and was useless at shouting prices, so he made painted boards and undercut everyone. At twenty-two he opened a stall by the Moot Ring selling seed-cones and travel kit, and has stood in the same spot for twenty-two years. His real fame came by accident. On his twenty-sixth Lost-Voice Day a neighbour paid him two coppers to chalk her side of a fence quarrel, and she won so thoroughly her neighbour washed the wall in tears. By his thirtieth, half the town was queuing at his stall the week before. He charges by the line, more for rebuttals. He keeps a ledger of every argument he has written, who for, and who lost. He married late, at thirty-eight, to a Ring-oil carter named Aine who talks enough for both of them, and he says it is the first bargain he ever lost on purpose.","family":"His wife Aine, loud and cheerful; his brother Hal, who still shouts, now from a farm two fields away.","hero":{"who":"Vey Who Said It","why":"The one who said her full name aloud in Shadowdeep where nobody does. Dorran can't say much of anything aloud, and he thinks saying the one forbidden thing is braver than saying a thousand allowed ones."},"band":{"act":"Calder Glass","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A battle rapper who can't lie; Dorran has the lyric sheets pinned behind his stall and reads them silently, mouthing the rhymes, admiring the construction like a carpenter admires a joint.","rv_vote":"Calder Glass, which in Cindermoot is nearly a crime, and which he writes on the wall every year to start a fight."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King in writing: a slate, read aloud by Aine, asking why the Hearth Court still won't hear the spring case after a hundred and seventy-five years.","edict":"Against, and he has already been paid to chalk the case against it for six households, and once, quietly, the case for it for one."},"wants":"To write one argument so good that Cinderhill concedes the third day of water and nobody has to wash a wall.","site":"cindermoot","town":"Cindermoot","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:cindermoot":{"name":"Ysolde Cragg","age":33,"role":"town smith: seed-pans, trench grates and Ring-oil ladles; former baker","look":"Broad hands with flour still in the nail-beds out of habit, a burn shaped like a crescent on one cheek, black curls tied up under a red rag.","voice":"Cheerful and blunt, talks while she hammers so you only hear every other word, laughs at her own jokes before she finishes them.","greet":"Warm hearth! Mind the pan, it's ruined anyway, I'm reshaping it. What've you broken?","lines":["Every cone of seed you eat at a duel got popped in one of my pans. Every single one. I'm in all of you.","Goat duel at dusk. Hal Fyle's boys again, or cousins of, I can't keep track. I'm making a bet on the goat.","Ione walks the Ring at night when the trench is cold. Don't tell her I said. I don't know anything. I sleep with my window open."],"about":"I was a baker first. Then the bakehouse oven door fell off its hinges and I fixed it, and it turned out I liked the fixing better than the bread. Now I make the pans the bread goes in. It's the same job from the other side.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting, enough for a seed buyer from Meridian"}],"childhood":"Ysolde and her twin sister Ysbail grew up on an emberseed farm south of the Moot Ring, where the fields go black and gold to the horizon and the whole family sleeps on the roof in harvest season to guard the drying racks. The twins were inseparable and argued constantly, as is proper. Ysbail wanted to fight; Ysolde wanted to cook. Their father settled it by setting them both to pop seed for the duel crowds when they were seven, and Ysolde got good at it, shaking the iron pan so evenly that nothing burned, while Ysbail ate the profits. Ysolde's earliest memory is of the sound of the whole crowd going 'oh' at a first burn, and the smell of hot seed coming up through it.","schooling":"A bakehouse apprenticeship at the Moot Ring bakehouse under Baker Ruadhan Kell, who made his apprentices sing to the proving dough because he swore it rose higher; Ysolde sang badly and her bread rose fine. Her Lamp Test lit nothing at all. She wasn't disappointed; she wanted the bakehouse. She learned to bake slapbread, coal cakes and the town's festival loaves, and how to read an oven's heat by the colour of the brick.","history":"At nineteen the old bakehouse oven's iron door cracked off its hinges on First Coal eve, when every oven in town is needed. The town smith was drunk at a duel. Ysolde dragged the door to his forge, worked out how a hinge was meant to go by staring at the broken one, and had it back on by dawn. The smith, a lanky old man named Garvan Rusk, came back sober, looked at her hinge, and offered her an apprenticeship on the spot, mostly to stop her taking his trade. She took it. When he died seven years later he left her the forge and his dog. She makes the town's seed-pans now, heavy and shallow, and the iron grates that sit over the Ring's oil-trench, and has reforged the trench grates twice after duels melted them. She still bakes on rest days. She has a long-standing arrangement with a Wetherby shears-smith named Elis Shearer that neither of them will call courting.","family":"Her twin Ysbail, now a junior Ring-warden under Ione Brack; their parents still on the emberseed farm; Garvan Rusk's dog, Lump, who is very old.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"The First Hearth was her kitchen fire. A whole Realm lit from somebody's stove; Ysolde finds that the best thing anyone ever did."},"band":{"act":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Seventy years old and kneading dough on stage while she sings; Ysolde cried at a show in Hearthmount and blamed the flour.","rv_vote":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens, every year until Tamsin stops, and then probably still."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King his new bread-tax on festival ovens, and she's baking him a loaf to make the point.","edict":"Against, loudly. If Flameborne riots she'll be at the back making sure everyone gets fed, which she says is the part of a riot that actually works."},"wants":"To bake for her sister's wedding, if Ysbail would only stop picking fights with every man who courts her.","site":"cindermoot","town":"Cindermoot","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cindermoot":{"name":"Teague Brack","age":26,"role":"emberseed farmer and would-be duellist; the Ring-warden's nephew","look":"Big, red-cheeked, singed eyebrows from showing off with seed-pans, a split ear from a tavern argument he says he won.","voice":"Big laugh, bigger stories, talks with his hands, can't keep a secret for longer than it takes to find someone to tell.","greet":"Warm hearth, friend! You come for the duel? Course you did. Everyone did. It's my goat.","lines":["Two brothers in the Ring over a goat. My goat. Well, it was. I might've sold it to them both. Don't look at me like that, they both paid.","My aunt won't ward a duel I'm in. Says I'd burn the stones. I'd burn them well, though, wouldn't I?","Cinderhill keeps the water five days in seven. Five! We get two. I'd go in the Ring for it. Nobody will let me."],"about":"I grow emberseed, like my dad, and his dad, and nobody else in my family ever got their name on a scorched stone except my aunt, and she never even fought. I'd like a stone. I'm working on it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, at volume"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words and big gestures"}],"childhood":"Teague was the youngest of five boys on a seed farm that shared a boundary ditch with his aunt Ione's land, and he spent his childhood wading through the ditch to her house because she was quiet and kept honey cakes in a jar. His brothers wrestled him daily. He lost daily and never once stopped trying, which his mother says was the whole of his personality by age six. He was the boy who climbed onto the Ring's standing stones to watch duels and fell off at least once a season. He learned every scorch mark by heart from the old men in the crowd and could recite who burned which stone, and when, before he could write his name.","schooling":"The Little Ring moot-school under Master Corwen Spill, who said Teague was the only child he ever taught who could lose an argument and finish it louder than he started. He was bad at letters and good at sums, as long as the sums were about seed. His Lamp Test lit nothing; he cried, then laughed about crying, then told everyone he'd cried, because that's Teague.","history":"He took over a share of the family seed fields at nineteen and has run them well, if loudly. He fights in Emberduels at other towns, in Highmarch and once at Smeltgate, where he lost both eyebrows and a duel to a woman a head shorter than him, and he has a set of scorched duel-cloths pinned to his wall. In Cindermoot, though, the Ring-warden must stand inside every duel, and his aunt Ione flatly refuses to ward his. It is the town joke and his life's grievance. Last spring he raised a goat that the whole street agreed was the worst goat they had ever seen, and when two brothers from the Fyle side of town each offered to buy it he said yes to both and spent both payments on cinder-wine. The brothers are now going into the Ring at dusk to decide who owns it. Teague has a cone of seed and the best seat. He is fairly sure his aunt is going to kill him.","family":"Four older brothers, all married; his widowed mother on the home farm; his aunt Ione, the Ring-warden, whom he loves more than anyone and annoys most.","hero":{"who":"Ione Brack, his aunt","why":"He'd never say it in front of her: she stood inside three hundred fights and stopped every one at first burn and never once needed to win anything. He has no idea how she does it."},"band":{"act":"No Knee","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Bakers'-street rap at war with Lightforge; he knows every word and recites them at the drying racks to keep awake, with feeling, wrongly.","rv_vote":"No Knee, because if they win in Korrhaven a Lightforge judge has to say their name out loud."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King his peace: he wants the King to come down to Cindermoot and hear the spring case in the Ring himself.","edict":"Against, and he's going to the riot, if there is one, and probably even if there isn't."},"wants":"His name on a scorched stone of the Moot Ring.","site":"cindermoot","town":"Cindermoot","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cindermoot:b":{"name":"Lorcan Moothe","age":88,"role":"keeper of the Long Moot copy; Cindermoot's water-arguer for fifty-two springs","look":"Bent, bird-thin, a bald freckled head and a Conclave grey sash gone almost white, with three knots he still re-ties every morning.","voice":"Old-fashioned lawyer's Kindle, gentle, endless sub-clauses, then a sudden short sharp line like a slap.","greet":"Warm hearth, young one. Sit, if you have a little while. If you have a long while, sit closer.","lines":["The slab says two days in seven. Two days is not enough. It was not enough in the Concord's one thousand and twenty-first year. It is still not enough.","Lost-Voice Day comes round and the whole town falls silent and argues in chalk, and I think, at last, a civilised place.","I go to Cinderhill every spring. Fenna gives me a bath and the same answer. I lose my voice. It is the best day of my year."],"about":"I am the last of a family of scribes. My great-grandfather's grandfather wrote down the Long Moot as it was spoken. I studied law at Korrhaven on the King's bread and came home with it. I have argued for the third day of water for fifty-two years and lost fifty-two times. I hope to manage fifty-three.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, the old courtly kind"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, Conclave law-Bridge"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it well, speaks it carefully; he likes the evidential endings"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a little, from the Cinderhill bath-house"}],"childhood":"Lorcan grew up in a narrow house full of paper beside the Moot Ring, among the copied records of every Emberduel and moot since the town began. His family had kept the record of the Long Moot for five generations: forty days of argument written down by a great-grandfather's grandfather who lost his own voice copying, and the slab terms in the margins. His mother read him the Long Moot at bedtime instead of stories; he liked the bit on day thirty-one where both speakers ran out of insults and started inventing new ones. He went to Cinderhill for the first time aged nine, to bathe, and a girl about his age at the bath-house told him his family's book was wrong. He never forgot it.","schooling":"His Lamp Test lit nothing, but the old Moot-scribe's family had a claim on the King's bread, and at thirteen he went to the Aether Conclave on Korrhaven as a Hearth Scholar, with a loaf on arrival. He read law in the Glass Library under a Lightforge reader, Master Corin Whitlow, who made him state each case plainly and then state it again for the other side. He hated the porridge and loved the library. He left at seventeen with three knots on his sash and another loaf.","history":"He came home to the family papers and a seat on the town's water-moot, and at thirty-six was chosen to carry Cindermoot's case to Cinderhill for the spring. He has carried it every spring since. Each year he walks up the cinder cone with a satchel of copied clauses, sits on the bench by the Share Stone, and argues from mid-morning until his voice gives out, which is usually mid-afternoon, and Cinderhill's bath-keeper answers him in about four words. He married a seed-merchant's daughter, Grainne, and was content with her for forty years until she died in her sleep; they had a son who went to Hearthmount and became a clerk to the Hearth Court and who, Lorcan suspects, is quietly ashamed of him. He has copied the Long Moot record twice by hand, once for the town and once for the Glass Library at Korrhaven, and his own commentary on it runs to nine volumes. Nobody has read all of them. He doesn't blame them.","family":"His son Fergus, a Hearth Court clerk in Hearthmount; two grandchildren he sees at First Coal; his late wife Grainne's honey jar, which he refills and does not eat from.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"'Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.' Master Whitlow made him write it on his slate every morning for four years, and he still argues that way, and has never won."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Twelve-minute post-rock that takes its time; he says it is the only music made by people who understand how long a proper argument should be.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters, and he will stay seated through the whole set while the presenters sit down too."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King what he refuses him every year: the Hearth Court's refusal to reopen the Long Moot terms. The King always answers with bread. Lorcan always eats it.","edict":"Against, and he wrote a long letter to the Emberwarden saying Arcthen Solbrand would have hated it; he doubts it was read."},"wants":"One more spring.","site":"cindermoot","town":"Cindermoot","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:smeltgate":{"name":"Ilsa Varrow","age":52,"role":"salvage-buyer and kit trader on the east-road market; half Steelgate-born","look":"Compact, quick, iron-grey hair cropped close the Metalcrown way, a jeweller's loupe on a chain, and a scarred left forefinger where a tally plate was once fitted and later taken off.","voice":"Switches between Kindle and Shiftcant mid-sentence, sharp and amused, weighs everything you hand her in her palm before she speaks.","greet":"Warm hearth. Or 'Shift', if you're from over the road. Put it on the counter. I'll tell you what it's worth and you'll tell me I'm wrong.","lines":["Steelgate men say engines do it cleaner. They're right. Then the frost comes. I've bought back a lot of snapped engine-iron. Cheap.","Torva Hesk will argue with a Metalcrown buyer for an hour and then sell to him. I'm the one who tells the buyer which hour.","Smeltgate wants the Keep's riders for free. The Keep wants a contract. They're both right. That's why nobody's riding."],"about":"My father smelted on the vein floors here and my mother came over from Steelgate to buy his iron and stayed to argue with him for thirty years. I grew up on the road between. I buy what's broken and sell what's useful, which is about what both my parents did with each other.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native, her mother's, with the Warm-talk slang"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"understands more than she lets on, through Shiftcant"}],"childhood":"Ilsa's mother was a Steelgate iron-buyer who crossed the road for vein-iron, quarrelled with a Smeltgate smelter over a cart's weight, and married him the next summer, which both towns found scandalous and neither could stop talking about. Ilsa grew up across the border twice a week, sleeping through the shift-whistles in Steelgate and the shouted weights in Smeltgate, and could not sleep well in either town without the other's noise. In Steelgate the other children called her spark-beggar for her father; in Smeltgate they called her a rivet. She learned to answer both in the right tongue, faster than they could. Her mother taught her to read a tally plate. Her father taught her to read a slag lump by the colour of its break. She liked the slag better.","schooling":"Two schools, badly. The Steelgate shift-school, where at ten she was handed her First Tool, a small spanner stamped with her name, by an engine-keeper named Brannick Holt who told her it would be laid in her hands when she died. And the Smeltgate bakehouse, where Baker Mairin Coyle taught her slapbread and swore at her Shiftcant. The Lamp Test at Smeltgate lit nothing.","history":"At eighteen she let a Steelgate fitter fit a tally plate to her wrist and a synthetic joint to her left forefinger, because her mother's crew said she'd never be trusted on the line without one. She worked a year on the Steelgate floors, hated the silence in her own head, and at twenty had the finger unfitted at a cost she still pays off in her knuckles every winter. She came back to Smeltgate and opened a salvage stall at the market's border end, where Metalcrown buyers come in, and has kept it for thirty years. She buys snapped engine-iron and broken tools and sells them back to whoever needs the metal, and the Smeltgate guild suspects her loyalties and buys from her anyway. She married once, to a Lightforge assayer from Clearwater, and it lasted until he discovered she would never move house or keep a Day-Book; they parted friends and he still signs her silver. Her mother died eight years ago in Steelgate.","family":"Her father Brann Varrow, eighty, who still shouts weights at the Weigh Gate when nobody stops him; a half-brother in Steelgate who works the night shift and visits at the Long Whistle.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"She led the Unmarked to the slag-hills with an ugly first hammer and made a Realm out of what was left; Ilsa thinks anyone who can build out of leftovers deserves a statue, and Orra hasn't got one on this side."},"band":{"act":"Oil & Ivory","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Her mother hummed the machinist crooner's songs while she oiled the kitchen hinges; Ilsa plays the songs on a Steelgate music-box at her stall and the Flameborne buyers pretend not to listen.","rv_vote":"Oil & Ivory, who will get no lamps from Lightforge, and she wants one Flameborne lamp at least on the board for them."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the road-toll Flameborne charges Metalcrown carts and not Stormreach ones; it's the sort of unfairness that makes her mother's people smile at Lightforge.","edict":"Torn. Her mother's people are smiling at it because Lightforge will finally audit someone other than them; her father's people want to burn it; she thinks both are being used."},"wants":"To get the Smeltgate guild and the Steelgate buyers to sit down to one meal without anyone leaving early.","site":"smeltgate","town":"Smeltgate","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:smeltgate":{"name":"Cormac Dell","age":29,"role":"vein-floor smelter and smith; left the Aether Conclave in his third year","look":"Lean, long-armed, sleeves always down; orange ember-cracks show at the cuffs when he lifts the tongs, and he has a habit of pressing his wrists against cold stone.","voice":"Careful and soft, a stammer when he's tired, sudden quick jokes at his own expense.","greet":"Warm hearth. Bit hot by the vein, sorry. I'll come out to you.","lines":["Vein-iron doesn't snap in frost because we smelt it slow. Slow is the whole secret. Nobody wants it to be the whole secret.","The Run's slow this season. Emberside says something's turned over in its sleep down there. I believe them. The floor's been cooler by a hand.","Steelgate engines are beautiful. I'd never say it in the guild hall. I'm saying it to you. Don't repeat it."],"about":"I was at Korrhaven, the Ember Tower, three years. Full Spark. I lost my temper in drill when I was sixteen, and somebody got burned, and I came home. Now I smelt iron on a lava floor the old way. It's hot and slow and nothing in it asks me to be angry.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, Conclave Bridge"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"halting; he's been learning from Ilsa Varrow on the quiet"}],"childhood":"Cormac was the eldest of three, son of a vein-floor smelter and a weigh-clerk, in a house so close to the vein that the kitchen floor was always warm and his mother cooked on it without a fire. He was a calm child and an odd one, who lay on the hot stone listening to the vein tick and thought it was talking. He would put his hand on a cooling ingot and say whether it would ring true before anyone struck it, and was right so often his father started asking him. At eight the Lamp Test put a sudden bright orange in the Ember lamp, the brightest the Smeltgate tester had seen in years, and his parents cried on the walk home, from pride, and he did not understand why it made them so sad.","schooling":"The Lamp Years at Smeltgate's lamp-school under Teacher Odhran Sallis, who made spark children carry a full cup of water across the yard at every lesson to teach them steadiness. At thirteen, the Aether Conclave on Korrhaven, the Ember Tower. He liked letters, history, drill, and the Glass Library, where Metalcrown auditors sat in the annex and took the best notes in the building. At sixteen, in a flame-drill, his temper went and he burned a classmate's hand and sash. He left with two knots.","history":"He came home to Smeltgate with ember-cracks to both elbows and an apology he still hasn't finished making, and asked to be taken on at the vein floors as a common smelter, which the guild, embarrassed for him, allowed. He has worked the floors for thirteen years. He is now one of the best smelters on the east road, because he can feel the heat of the vein through the soles of his boots and knows without measuring when an ore-load is ready. He keeps his spark cold. He doesn't light the forge by hand, doesn't do Emberduels, and won't have cinder-wine in the house. He forges tool blanks and good horseshoe stock in a back forge in the evenings. The guild has twice offered him a seat at the guild table and he has twice said he'd rather not, because the guild table is where people shout. He writes once a year to the classmate he burned, a Stormreach woman now a skyray handler, and she has answered twice.","family":"His parents, both still working; a younger sister apprenticed to Torva Hesk's guild who thinks he's wasted; a younger brother who left for Hearthmount and sings in a bakery choir.","hero":{"who":"Solvane, Chief Engineer of Metalcrown","why":"A polite young engineer who read the ninth petition to a Council that laughed, and kept reading. Cormac has stood in rooms where everyone laughed at him and never once finished his sentence."},"band":{"act":"Third Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Industrial metal, and the song 'I Made It' is the only song he'll let himself shout along to, in the back forge, alone, with the door shut.","rv_vote":"Third Whistle, which he'll never admit in the guild hall, and he'll vote by proxy through Ilsa Varrow."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's offer to send him back to Korrhaven at the King's cost; it's kind, and he's said no twice.","edict":"Against, with a quietness people don't expect: a register of sparks means a page with his name on it and the word 'burned' next to it, forever, in Lightforge's hand."},"wants":"To smelt one bar that is perfect from the first ring and to feel nothing at all while he does it.","site":"smeltgate","town":"Smeltgate","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:smeltgate":{"name":"Tam Hesk","age":15,"role":"weight-shouter at the Weigh Gate; grandchild of the guild-mistress","look":"Gangly, dark freckles, a too-big guild apron cinched with rope, hair dyed orange with Copperfield rust-paste in a fit of rebellion and fading badly.","voice":"Voice still breaking: the shouts come out half roar and half squeak, and Tam glares at anyone who laughs.","greet":"Warm hearth. Don't stand under the scale. I'm not saying it'll fall. I'm saying I'd move.","lines":["Cart from the north, two hundred and twelve stone! That was a good one. You hear the squeak? No you didn't.","Gran's been at a Steelgate buyer for an hour. She'll sell to him. She always sells to him. It's the hour that matters, she says.","The Keep wants a contract for the smelt-road. Gran says we don't pay for a road we were here before. I think we should just pay. Don't tell her I think things."],"about":"I'm Tam, Torva Hesk's grandchild, not grandson, not granddaughter, grandchild, I refused it at my First No and the whole guild had to start again. I shout the weights at the Gate. One day I'll run the guild, or I'll run away to Steelgate and look at their engines. Depends on the week.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, at full volume"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good for numbers, bad for anything else"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"swear words and engine parts, learned from the Steelgate drivers"}],"childhood":"Tam was born into the guild hall more or less, the child of Torva Hesk's younger son, who drowned in a flash flood on the Coal Road when Tam was four, and a mother who went back to her family in Hearthmount and sends money and letters. Torva raised Tam among smelters and ore-carts and guild arguments, which were the same thing as dinner. Tam grew up under the Weigh Gate, swinging on the scale chains, learning numbers by the shouted weights. At seven Tam could guess a cart's weight within a stone by the creak of its axle. Torva said it was a gift. Tam's cousins said it was showing off, and they were right.","schooling":"The guild's own school above the hall, taught by an old smelter, Sister Breed Harlow, who lost a foot on the vein floors and taught letters and sums sitting down with her stump on a cushion and a slate in each hand. She made the class shout their sums down the stairwell. The Lamp Test lit nothing for Tam; Torva bought cinder-wine for the guild that night, out of relief. At thirteen Tam did the First No in front of the whole guild.","history":"Tam's First No was famous by Smeltgate standards. Asked by the guild, as custom has it, what Tam refused, Tam said: being called grandson or granddaughter, or son or daughter, by anyone, ever again; grandchild was the word, and child, and Tam. Torva looked at Tam for a long time and then said 'Grandchild, then,' and the hall did the same. At fourteen Tam took over shouting the weights when the old weigh-crier Brann Varrow's voice went, and promptly lost the voice too, to puberty. Tam shouts anyway, through squeaks, and the carters cheer. Tam has been twice to Steelgate on the ore-carts without telling Torva, to watch the engines, and has a drawing of a steam-hammer hidden in the scale's tool box. Tam is very good at maths, very bad at sitting still, and has recently discovered the Sparkbeat Cup and the existence of dancing.","family":"Grandmother Torva Hesk, guild-mistress; mother in Hearthmount; a pack of older cousins in the guild who treat Tam as a mascot.","hero":{"who":"Torva Hesk, guild-mistress","why":"She stood in front of the whole guild after Tam's First No and said one word, 'Grandchild', and made everybody else say it too. Tam has never seen her lose an argument and that was the one she didn't even need to have."},"band":{"act":"Flashpoint","realm":"Flameborne","why":"A DJ with a track called 'Temper'; Tam heard it at a Hearthmount street party and learned that the thing you do with too much energy is dance it out.","rv_vote":"Flashpoint, in the Sparkbeat Cup and the main vote, with Tam's whole lamp and everyone else's Tam can borrow."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal Tam will refuse the King the guild's rule that nobody under sixteen may speak at the guild table.","edict":"Against, mainly because everyone else is, and Tam will be at the riot, at the front, squeaking."},"wants":"To see a Metalcrown engine from the inside and still come home to the guild hall for supper.","site":"smeltgate","town":"Smeltgate","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:smeltgate:b":{"name":"Rhona Slake","age":66,"role":"retired weigh-clerk; famous for the worst confession on Slag Day","look":"Tall, stooped, a black widow's ribbon worn twelve years, round spectacles mended with copper wire; carries a little slag lump in her pocket and turns it like a worry-stone.","voice":"Precise, dry, a clerk's voice; never raises it, which in Smeltgate makes everyone go quiet.","greet":"Warm hearth. You'll want the inn, not me. Or me, if it's about weights. It is always about weights here.","lines":["The Steelgate buyers haggle like it's a war. My husband haggled like it was a dance, and look where it got him. Off the road at the Cutter's Elbow.","The Keep wants a contract for the smelt-road. I'd pay it myself. I've paid a Keep contract before, for a man who didn't come home.","On Slag Day I threw in the ugliest lump and said I'd married the wrong smelter. Bought drinks for a month. He'd have laughed."],"about":"I was a weigh-clerk at the Gate for forty years. I wrote every number they shouted. My husband traded ingots east and died on the road, and I've been writing other people's numbers since. It's what I'm good at. It's not a sad life. It's a tidy one.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, clerk's hand"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads Lightforge assay marks, nothing more"}],"childhood":"Rhona was a clerk's daughter in a family of clerks, who lived above the old guild ledger-room. Her parents argued like proper Flameborne, but in writing, leaving notes on the kitchen table in neat hands, and Rhona thought for years that all marriages were conducted on paper. She was the girl who checked her sums twice and corrected the teacher's. She had one great passion, horses, and none of her family could ride; she learned on a carter's nag in exchange for doing his tolls. She was not a happy child, she says, but she was a competent one, and she thinks that counts.","schooling":"The Smeltgate bakehouse school, as all the guild children did then, under Baker Fionnuala Brask, who weighed every loaf her pupils made and made them write the weight on the crust in ash. Rhona's loaves were never more than half an ounce out. The Lamp Test lit nothing. At fifteen she went to the guild ledger-room as an apprentice clerk.","history":"She clerked at the Weigh Gate from sixteen to fifty-six, the longest in the gate's memory. At twenty-six she married Abel Slake, a loud, handsome ingot-trader who carried vein-iron east to Stormreach markets and argued with every Metalcrown buyer for sport. They had a son, Niall, now a horse-trainer on the Low Reach in Stormreach. Twelve years ago Abel took a Keep escort on the east road against the rider's advice, because he wanted to make a market a day early, and at the Cutter's Elbow he was killed. The rider brought him home over her own saddle. Rhona paid the contract anyway, in full, by courier, because a contract is a contract. The money came back unopened. She has never understood why and has never asked. On her first Slag Day after the funeral she threw the ugliest lump in Smeltgate into the vein and shouted, 'I married the wrong smelter, and I'd do it again,' and the guild bought her drinks for a month. She retired at fifty-six and now keeps the guild's old records in order for nothing.","family":"Her son Niall on the Low Reach, who shouts an apology every Great Turning for not visiting more; three grandchildren she has met once.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"'One shift, one vote. Even you.' Smeltgate's guild votes by tonnage smelted, so the big floors outvote the small ones; she has spent forty years writing down how unfair that is in very small handwriting."},"band":{"act":"Plain Speaking","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Honest country songs with no metaphors; she can't stand songs that say one thing and mean another, and she plays them at the ledger-room on a little Lightforge music-box.","rv_vote":"Plain Speaking, because they never win and they're the only act that says what it means."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the guild's tonnage vote, which he backed at the iron Refusal long ago and has never been asked to look at again.","edict":"Torn: she wants every spark written down plainly for once, but not by Lightforge, and not with the word 'audit', which she says in a clerk's mouth always means somebody's about to be punished."},"wants":"To know why Dunla Marrow sent the money back.","site":"smeltgate","town":"Smeltgate","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:cinderhill":{"name":"Padrig Spurr","age":40,"role":"trader in road kit and spring-salt; married into a Stonehold family","look":"Soft-bellied, curly brown beard, a pair of good Stonehold boots he never takes off, sleeves rolled to show off forearms he's proud of for no reason.","voice":"Friendly patter, smooth as warm butter, until you ask him about the salt, when he goes very slightly too fast.","greet":"Warm hearth! And Set, if you're from the north. Salts? Kit? Salts? Both?","lines":["The Stonehold men say the salts soften the grey in their hands. Who am I to argue with a man's own hands?","Cindermoot will be up in spring for its third day. Old Lorcan will lose his voice by noon. Fenna will have him in the bath by one.","My wife's hand's grey to the wrist. She uses the salt every night. She says it does nothing. She uses it every night."],"about":"I grew up selling to people passing through, so I married one. Ragna came south for the bath and went home with me in the cart, and then we came back here because her knees liked the steam. I sell kit, and I sell salt, and I'm honest about the kit.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good, his wife's, though she says his stresses fall on the wrong end"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, very smooth"}],"childhood":"Padrig grew up at the Halfway, a waystation inn on the Highmarch road two days north of Cinderhill, where his parents fed carters, swapped horses and sold whatever was in the back shed. He was selling bootlaces to Stonehold drovers by the age of six and could read a traveller's purse by their shoes. The inn was busy, loud and kind, and he slept in the hayloft over the horses, where the carters' stories came up through the floor at night. He was the boy who remembered every guest's name and their horse's, and got tips for it. He never had a best friend his own age; he had a hundred grown-up acquaintances who liked him, and he thought that was what friendship was until he was nearly thirty.","schooling":"No school. The Halfway was too far from anything for the bakehouse, and his parents needed him. He learned his letters from the inn's guest-book, copying names and destinations, and his sums from the till. His Lamp Test was done late, at nine, by a travelling tester who stopped for the night; nothing lit, and the tester stayed three extra days on the house because Padrig sold him a horse.","history":"At twenty-four, running the Halfway's back-shed trade, he sold a pair of boots to a Stonehold quarry-mason travelling south for the Cinderhill bath, and she told him they were the worst boots she'd ever been sold. She was right. Her name was Ragna Ottersett; her right hand was grey to the wrist from Gravem, and she came back north two weeks later and said, 'I'll be wanting a refund.' He gave her a refund and a better pair of boots and, by the following spring, himself. They lived a year at her family's hold-house in the mountains, where he was miserable and polite, and then her knees began to stiffen and the Cinderhill steam was the only thing that helped. They moved here eleven years ago. He runs the trader's stall at the bathhouse road, sells kit and spring-salt to the Stonehold carters, and has three children who speak Holdspeech at home and Kindle in the street and argue about which is better in both.","family":"His wife Ragna; their children Brenna, Osk and little Dagda; his parents still at the Halfway, where he takes the children every summer.","hero":{"who":"The Silent Sentinel","why":"Ragna's family keep one of his carved slates at home, from a Hold-Senate meeting her grandmother went to: four words, cut deep. Padrig, who never stops talking, thinks a man who can say everything in four carved words is the most frightening person alive."},"band":{"act":"Low Cloud","realm":"Stormreach","why":"R&B about grounded lovers; he heard it first in a carter's cart the week he met Ragna and has never been able to separate the two.","rv_vote":"Low Cloud, every year, and Ragna votes Mason Grey, and the children vote for whoever makes their parents argue most."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King nothing. He's a married man; he refuses enough at home. If pushed he'd refuse the new toll on Highmarch carts, which is hurting the bathhouse trade.","edict":"Against, softly, and he won't riot; he'll sell the rioters salt for their bruises."},"wants":"For his children to grow up thinking of both Realms as home and neither as the other one.","site":"cinderhill","town":"Cinderhill","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:cinderhill":{"name":"Agna Corrie","age":58,"role":"village smith: egg-net hoops, bathhouse boiler fittings and carters' wheel-rims","look":"Square, sturdy, white hair braided in a crown, enormous forearms, a Stonehold boot-buckle pinned to her apron as a brooch.","voice":"Plain and warm, says things once and expects you to have heard; a bark of a laugh that makes horses look up.","greet":"Warm hearth. If it's a wheel, roll it here. If it's a horse, it'll want Clayford. If it's a boiler, sit down, it'll be a while.","lines":["Every Stonehold traveller gets the first bath. My grandmother would come out of her grave and box my ears if they didn't. She was the child in the pool.","Cindermoot wants a third day. They've wanted it since before I was born. They'll want it after. It keeps the spring busy.","Somebody in Cindermoot's going in the Ring over a goat, I hear. My forge has a goat. Nobody's fought over it. Shows you what kind of goat it is."],"about":"I'm the smith. I make the little iron hoops the egg-nets hang on, and I keep the bath-boilers from blowing the roof off, and I fix the wheels of every Stonehold cart that comes south too fast. My grandmother was pulled out of the deep pool by a Stonehold carter, so you could say I owe the whole north a wheel.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"carters' Holdspeech: wheels, axles, weights, insults, and 'Set'"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"enough"}],"childhood":"Agna was raised by her grandmother, Maeve Corrie, while her parents worked the Highmarch road as carters. Maeve was the child pulled from the deep pool by a Stonehold carter, Durn of Kell Deep, long ago, and she told the story every night as if it had happened last week: the cold shock of the hot water, the sky going green, the huge grey hand closing on her hair. Agna grew up thinking of Stonehold travellers as half-legend and was disappointed, at seven, to meet one who was rude. Her grandmother kept Durn's boot-buckle, which he had lost in the pool, on a nail above the hearth, and made Agna polish it every First Coal. Agna loved the smell of hot iron from the village forge and hung about it until the smith gave her the bellows to shut her up.","schooling":"Cinderhill has no bakehouse school; children walk to Cindermoot's Little Ring or learn at home. Agna walked to the Little Ring for two years, got into fights with Cindermoot children about the water, was sent home twice, and her grandmother taught her the rest at the kitchen table, mostly from the backs of carters' bills. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She was apprenticed to the village smith, Ronan Pyle, at eleven.","history":"Ronan Pyle was a slow and gentle smith who taught her to work small: egg-net hoops, boiler fittings, the copper taps on the bathhouse pipes. When he died she took the forge, at twenty-six, and nobody in Cinderhill thought to question it because she'd been doing his work for years already. She married a Stonehold-born carter, Hadd Brennish, who stopped for a wheel and stayed for her, and they had two children before he went back on the road one autumn and didn't come back from a rockfall on the Kesterrow pass; his initials are on a marker there and she has walked the road to see them. She kept the forge, raised the children, and fixed every Stonehold wheel that came through for free for a year after. She still fixes them cheap. Her son is a carter now; her daughter runs the egg-boiling and is going to inherit Fenna's bathhouse whether she likes it or not.","family":"Her son Callum Brennish, a carter; her daughter Iona Brennish, egg-boiler; her late husband Hadd, carried on a marker on the Kesterrow pass.","hero":{"who":"Durn of Kell Deep, the carter who pulled her grandmother from the pool","why":"Her whole life, and her husband, and her village's manners, came from one stranger's grey hand in the water."},"band":{"act":"The Kept Coals","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stomp-folk bakers; she stamps along to 'Keep Your Coal Lit' at the forge and the anvil rings in time.","rv_vote":"The Kept Coals, as a matter of village pride and because Ronan Pyle used to sing their old songs badly."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the new carting toll on the Highmarch road; it's the Stonehold carters who pay it and it's Cinderhill that loses the bath-trade.","edict":"Against. Cinderhill doesn't riot, she says. It boils. She'll be at home with the forge lit, and anyone who comes back from the riot with a burnt cart will get it mended."},"wants":"To carve Durn of Kell Deep's initial on a Stonehold mile-marker, where it belongs, before she dies.","site":"cinderhill","town":"Cinderhill","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cinderhill":{"name":"Sile Morrow","age":19,"role":"apprentice bath-keeper to Old Fenna; egg-net tender","look":"Pink-cheeked from the steam, black hair in two plaits pinned up so they don't drip, front teeth slightly crooked, always barefoot on the warm stone.","voice":"Quick and teasing, giggles mid-sentence, has been practising Fenna's deadpan and can't hold it more than two words.","greet":"Set. Hand on the doorframe, see? Fenna does it better. I'm practising. Warm hearth too. Bath's that way.","lines":["Fenna says Flameborne jump into hot water and Stonehold lower themselves like a bridge. Lightforge people test it with one finger and tell you what temperature it is.","The Stonehold men say our salts soften the grey in their hands. Fenna says don't tell them they don't. I haven't. I just don't sell them, that's all.","When Cindermoot comes up in spring, they bring a Ring-warden to keep the peace. Last year it was Ysbail Cragg. She's coming again. Not that I asked."],"about":"I'm Fenna's apprentice. I'll keep the bathhouse when she stops, unless she never stops, which is what she says. I like steam and people with their boots off. Everybody's honest in a hot bath. Nobody can stand on their dignity in their skin.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"bathhouse Holdspeech, polite and practised: 'Set', 'is it warm enough', 'mind the step'"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Sile was the youngest of four in an egg-boiling family whose house sits so close to the spring that the steam comes through the floorboards in the cool months, and she grew up thinking every child's bedroom had a warm draught. At six a large Stonehold carter, startled by being offered the first bath, stood up too fast and knocked out her two front milk teeth with his elbow; he was so ashamed he left her a silver crossing and a carved stone bird, and she still has the bird. The new teeth came in crooked. She was a dreamy child who sat in the shallow end with the egg-nets for hours and invented whole lives for the travellers, and she was often right.","schooling":"Old Fenna taught her letters, which she does for any village child who sits still: written with a finger in the steam on the bathhouse's polished copper mirrors, so every word fades before you can be proud of it. Sile learned to read fast to keep up. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She never wanted it to; the bathhouse was the plan from age eight.","history":"She began helping at the bathhouse at ten, folding towels and carrying hot stones, and at fourteen Fenna took her on as apprentice, partly because Sile was the only child in the village who could get the egg-nets out without scalding herself. She has learned to tell a traveller's Realm by how they get into hot water, as Fenna does, and is better than Fenna at guessing their mood. She keeps the bath-ledger and secretly decorates the margins. She has danced in the steam to Warmstone songs at the end of each night since she was fifteen. For two springs now the Cindermoot delegation has brought a junior Ring-warden, Ysbail Cragg, to keep the peace during the water argument, and both springs Sile has offered her the first bath of the day, which is meant only for Stonehold travellers, and both times Ysbail has said yes. Fenna has noticed and says nothing, which is how Sile knows she's noticed.","family":"Parents and three older brothers in the egg-boiling trade; an aunt in Wetherby who makes her an ash-cloak every few years.","hero":{"who":"Old Fenna, the bath-keeper","why":"Fenna has kept the bathhouse for as long as anyone in Cinderhill has been alive, and everybody who comes through the door, any Realm, any trade, leaves warmer than they came. Sile can't think of anyone who's done more good with less fuss."},"band":{"act":"Warmstone","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A sultry hot-spring dance duo; she found a song-sheet left in the steam room by a Stonehold traveller and taught herself the steps alone at night on the wet stone.","rv_vote":"Warmstone, and she has asked every Stonehold guest for a season to give their lamp to them too."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King's heralds the right to call the Stonehold carters 'foreigners' in the Highmarch toll-notice; they're guests, and she says so.","edict":"Against, but she doesn't really understand it, and she'd rather stay here and keep the baths hot for anyone who comes back from the riot."},"wants":"To give Ysbail Cragg the first bath every spring for the rest of her life.","site":"cinderhill","town":"Cinderhill","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cinderhill:b":{"name":"Hollin Karnsett","age":31,"role":"salt-boiler and egg-net hauler; Hearthward-born, of both Realms","look":"Long, rawboned, cropped sandy hair; the right hand grey and smooth as river-stone to the second knuckle, the left forearm with a Flameborne coal-tattoo; neither man nor woman and comfortable with it.","voice":"Low and spare like a Stonehold road-elder, then a sudden quick Flameborne grin; drops Holdspeech words into Kindle without noticing.","greet":"Set. Warm hearth. I'm Hollin. You'll have heard me lifting the salt-pans. Everyone has.","lines":["I lower myself into the bath like Stonehold and then complain like Flameborne. Fenna says I'm the worst of both. She's pleased about it.","The carters say the salts help their hands. I've got a grey hand. They don't. But the warm water does, a little, and it feels kind, and that counts for something.","Up at Hearthward the two bridgewardens are still quarrelling about the hinges. Twelve hundred years. You'd think one of them would just oil it."],"about":"I was born at Hearthward, in the March orchards outside the keep, to a Flameborne baker and a Stonehold quarry-woman. I came here to boil salt because a Gravem accident turned my hand grey, and I heard the salts helped. They don't. I stayed for the steam and the people.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent; the March speaks it at every market"}],"childhood":"Hollin grew up in a stone cottage in the March orchards south of Hearthward's walls, where the soil is half black ash and half grey grit and the apples come out red one side and russet the other. Their father baked slapbread for the keep's basalt garrison; their mother cut stone for the granite masons. Each morning the household lifted a burden-stone together, the father grumbling all the way, and each night they checked the Kept Coal, the mother grumbling all the way, and Hollin decided early that grumbling was the real March tradition. The orchard children played Brannoc-and-Hollis, building forts out of fallen branches and arguing about whose turn it was to be the one who didn't want to marry. Hollin always wanted to be both.","schooling":"The March market-school inside Hearthward, held in the customs house on market days only, taught by a retired customs clerk, Marra Tolland, who made the children translate every lesson from Kindle into Holdspeech and back and gave a dried apple for every joke that survived both. At the Lamp Test the Gravem lamp hummed weakly: a Half-Spark. Hollin did the First No at thirteen and the Shouldering at fifteen, and carried the marker-stone a mile in the rain while their father shouted encouragement in Kindle from under a tarp.","history":"At seventeen Hollin went to work in their mother's quarry, and in the second summer a block slipped its cradle over a crew of four. Hollin reached for the spark without thinking and held the block for the length of three breaths while the others got out, and then the fingers of the right hand went cold and grey and did not come back. The quarry gave Hollin a medal and a lighter job, which Hollin hated. At twenty-two, hearing from carters that Cinderhill's spring-salt softened the grey, Hollin walked south with a sack and a hope, sat in the bath for a week, and found that it didn't. Hollin stayed anyway, took work boiling salt and hauling the egg-nets, and has lived nine years in a room over the salt-shed, where the warm air comes up through the boards. Hollin does not have a partner and has decided not to mind. The grey has crept a little each year; Hollin marks its edge in ink at Marker Day.","family":"Both parents still at the March orchards; a younger sister, a Hearthward bridge-guard on the granite side, who writes every month in two languages.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"'Lift. Then talk.' Hollin did exactly that under a falling block and has never regretted it, only the talking afterwards."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A doom trio whose drummer's hands are turning to stone; Hollin saw them play at Hearthward and the drummer looked straight at Hollin's grey hand in the crowd and nodded.","rv_vote":"Burden, every season they enter, until the drummer can't hold the sticks."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal Hollin will refuse the King's March proclamation calling Hearthward 'Flameborne's southern gate', because it's two Realms' or it's nobody's; and in the Hold-Senate Hollin would stand behind the Mile Nought road-elder, who speaks for the March.","edict":"Against, in the Stonehold way: refuse it, sit down, don't shout. Hollin has had enough of what a spark costs without Lightforge writing it down."},"wants":"To go home to the March orchards with the grey stopped at the second knuckle and the salt-shed money in a sack.","site":"cinderhill","town":"Cinderhill","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:emberside":{"name":"Mairead Scald","age":45,"role":"trader in rakes, tongs and kit; grinds shed serpent-scale into glaze for the Redbank potters","look":"Sturdy, short-haired, cheeks freckled with tiny glaze-burns, and fingernails stained a shimmering red-black from the scale-dust that never fully washes out.","voice":"Fast, cheerful, a potter's Kindle full of kiln words; laughs at the bad bits of her own stories.","greet":"Warm hearth! Don't touch the red jar, it's scale, it'll stain you for a month. Everything else, touch away.","lines":["Smeltgate says the keepers are lazy. Smeltgate should come and lean on that post for one shift. One. I'll give them a free rake.","Scale's been coming up darker this season. Redbank wants it bright. The serpents aren't taking orders.","My mother still won't speak to me. Twenty-five years. She buys my glaze through a cousin. Best customer I've got."],"about":"I'm from Redbank, the potters' town. I came here to buy serpent-scale for my mother's kilns and ended up grinding it myself, which is a long story involving a keeper with lovely forearms. I sell the village's rakes and tongs and the glaze, and the glaze pays for everything.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a few words: Lightforge buyers love the red glaze and haggle in Clear"}],"childhood":"Mairead grew up in Redbank, among stacked kilns and red clay up to the knee, the third daughter of a glaze-mistress named Una Scald who was famous for her temper and her colours. The house smelled of wet clay and smoke and was always full of half-finished jars. At five Mairead was given a lump of clay and a corner of the wheel-room and made a hundred ugly bowls, each of which her mother broke in front of her, kindly, saying 'again.' She learned glaze before she learned letters: the green of ash, the black of iron, and the red that only comes from Magma Serpent scale ground fine. Her mother kept the scale in a locked tin. Mairead picked the lock at nine to see it, and has never forgotten the way it moved in the light like something breathing.","schooling":"A bakehouse apprenticeship at Redbank's Kiln Street bakehouse under Baker Bronach Tierney, who baked bread in the potters' kilns after the firings and made his apprentices guess the kiln heat by sniffing the crust; Mairead was hopeless at bread and brilliant at heat. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She left at fourteen to work the glaze-room full time.","history":"At twenty her mother sent her to Emberside to buy a season's shed scale from the keepers, and she met Bran Ashkell, a keeper who leant on the Raking Post between shifts and told her how the serpents shed, at length, while she pretended to understand. She went home with the scale and came back a month later for more, and then again, and her mother, seeing what was happening, forbade it. Mairead married Bran anyway, in Emberside, at First Coal, and her mother didn't come. They have three children and a loud happy house. She began grinding the scale herself to cut out the Redbank middlemen, which is how she became the first person in Emberside to make real money from it, and her mother has never forgiven her for that either. She runs the trader's stall, sells the long rakes and tongs that Dafydd Rake forges, and keeps the village's accounts with Smeltgate's guild, which she says makes her the only person in Emberside the guild is afraid of.","family":"Her husband Bran Ashkell, a keeper; three children, Oona, Ruairi and baby Cass; her mother Una in Redbank, who buys the glaze through a cousin and pretends not to know it's hers.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright and Idris Hollowfire","why":"They built the first Spark Reactor out of nothing but stubbornness and other people's parts; Mairead, who built a trade out of a dead serpent's shed skin, feels she's in the same line of work."},"band":{"act":"The Trough","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Amapiano played on kneading troughs; she saw them in Redbank the year she left home and it's the last time she and her mother danced together, so it's complicated, and she loves them.","rv_vote":"The Trough, because if they get far enough her mother will hear them on the lamp-boards too."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King Smeltgate's guild-rate for the keepers, which he backed at the iron Refusal and which hasn't risen since.","edict":"Against. She'll riot with a rake if she has to, and she'd rather not; the Run doesn't stop for politics."},"wants":"For her mother to come to Emberside, once, and lean on the Raking Post and see.","site":"emberside","town":"Emberside","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:emberside":{"name":"Dafydd Rake","age":50,"role":"village smith: forges the house-long channel rakes and tongs; never once raked the Run","look":"Short, thick, bow-legged, a great black beard that he tucks inside his shirt at the forge; deep-set worried eyes.","voice":"Gruff, fond of the word 'right', sings power-metal choruses under his breath while hammering and pretends it's coughing.","greet":"Right. Warm hearth. You'll want a rake. Nobody wants a rake. Everybody needs one.","lines":["Smeltgate's guild came down with bread and an argument again. We ate the bread. We had the argument. Nobody's paid.","Every rake on the Run I made, and every one's worn to a different length. I can tell you who's holding it by the wear.","I've never raked the Run. Not once. Fifty years by it. Don't tell the children."],"about":"I'm the smith. My family's always been keepers and I'm the one who couldn't go near the channel without my knees going. So I make the rakes. Somebody has to, and somebody has to wait for them to come back off shift. That's me as well.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting; he points a lot"}],"childhood":"Dafydd was the fourth son of a keeper family and the only one frightened of the Run. At four his father carried him to the Raking Post to see the channel for the first time, and he screamed so long the whole village came out, and after that his father never took him again. He grew up in the village's smallest house, the one furthest from the channel, playing with iron offcuts from the smithy and making little rakes from wire for his brothers' toy channels in the dust. They teased him for it endlessly. His mother didn't. She told him that every keeper on the Run needs a rake, and the best keeper on the Run can't make one, and that was the end of the matter as far as she was concerned.","schooling":"Emberside has no school, so the village children walk the channel-path to Smeltgate's bakehouse two days a week, and Dafydd went by the long road round so he never had to pass the Run. Baker Ciara Malley at Smeltgate let him heat-test the iron baking sheets and said he had a smith's hands, wasted on bread. The Lamp Test lit nothing. At twelve he was taken on by Emberside's old smith, Owen Tongs, who'd also never raked.","history":"Owen Tongs taught him the long work: how to draw iron into a rake-shaft as long as a house without it warping, how to temper the tines so they don't soften in the Run's heat, how to fit an ash-wood grip that won't scorch. Owen died when Dafydd was thirty and left him the forge, and Dafydd has made every rake on the Run since. He knows each keeper by their wear: Garet Ashlow wears the left tines first; the Scald children wear the grips. He sits outside the forge at shift's end and counts the keepers coming off the channel, every evening, and goes in only when the count is right. He married Bronwen Ashkell, the head keeper's cousin, a tall cheerful keeper who isn't afraid of anything, and they have two daughters, both keepers, which keeps him counting. He plays drums on spare rake-shafts in the evenings with three other men, very loudly, and calls it practice.","family":"His wife Bronwen, a keeper; daughters Tegan and Mali, both keepers; three brothers on the Run who still call him 'Wire'.","hero":{"who":"Orrin Who Stayed","why":"The first Anchorite, who stayed still on the cliffs for those who come back. Dafydd stays by the forge and counts the keepers home every night; he says it's the same job and the cliffs had a better view."},"band":{"act":"Thunderhead","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Power metal; he and his rake-shaft band learned three Thunderhead songs off a Stormreach carter's whistling and play them wrong every Serpent Supper.","rv_vote":"Thunderhead, because they'll lose to something polite, and somebody should be loud about it."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the guild's keeper-rate, and he'll bring a rake to the Refusal to make the point, and he'll leave it outside because he's not daft.","edict":"Against, but he can't make himself care about Lightforge while the keepers are short-paid; he'll go to the riot if the guild goes, and it won't."},"wants":"To see the keepers paid properly before his daughters are old enough to lean on the post.","site":"emberside","town":"Emberside","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:emberside":{"name":"Cait Ashlow","age":23,"role":"channel-keeper on the Run; reads the lava's colour","look":"Wiry, freckled, sun-darkened; no eyebrows (shaved, in solidarity with her father, and now habit), a burn-scar shaped like a leaf on one forearm, hair tied up in a wet rag.","voice":"Few words, fast, a keeper's flat voice; then sudden fierce detail about the Run, because it's the only thing she'll talk about for long.","greet":"Warm hearth. Stay back from the edge. Not that far. That far's for Smeltgate people.","lines":["The Run's slow this season. Smeltgate says we're lazy. Look at the colour. Dark orange. Something down there's turned over in its sleep.","My da lost his eyebrows his first season. I shaved mine my first. He cried. He'll say he didn't.","There's a vent past the bend where the crust stays warm and cracked. One night I saw lights coming up out of it. Not lava lights. Lamps."],"about":"I'm a keeper. I rake the Run. My da is Garet Ashlow, the head keeper, and I'll be head keeper after him or I'll die trying, and I don't intend to die. I can read the Run's colour better than him. He knows. He says so when he's had cinder-wine.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"poor"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"six signs, learned from a Shadowdeep tunneller; she isn't sure she's meant to know them"}],"childhood":"Cait was the only child of Garet Ashlow, head keeper, and Moira Ashlow, who ran the coal-cake ovens over the Run on long handles and burned her fingers daily, which she said was part of the recipe. Cait grew up at the Raking Post. By four she could tell the Run's temperature by the colour of the crust; by six she was correcting her father. She had no interest in dolls and some interest in rakes. At eight she went into the shallow edge of the channel after a dropped rake-head and came out with the leaf-shaped burn on her arm and the rake-head, and her father, after shouting for an hour, gave her a rake of her own. It was too long for her by two yards. She dragged it everywhere.","schooling":"The walk to Smeltgate's bakehouse two days a week, taught by Baker Ciara Malley, who gave her a slate and a lump of chalk and said she could write whatever she liked as long as she wrote it every day. Cait wrote the Run's colour. She has kept the habit; the slates fill a shed. The Lamp Test lit nothing, and her father said, 'Good, you'll stay.'","history":"She took her first keeper shift at fifteen and shaved her eyebrows the same morning so they'd be gone before the Run took them, and her father, who had lost his in his first season and never grown them back, cried at the Raking Post in front of everyone. She has raked for eight years, longer shifts than anyone, and keeps a colour-log on slates: light yellow, deep yellow, gold, orange, dark orange, red. The Run has been dark orange for most of this season, which hasn't happened in her lifetime. Smeltgate's guild says the keepers are slacking. Cait says something in the deep vein has moved. One night last winter, alone on the late shift, she saw a Shadowdeep tunneller climb up out of a warm vent past the bend, look at her, put a finger to her lips, and teach her six hand-signs by lamplight before going back down. Cait has told only her father. He believes her, which frightened her more than the tunneller.","family":"Her father Garet, head keeper; her mother Moira, coal-cake baker; a lot of keeper cousins; a girl in Smeltgate she walks out with sometimes, when there's time, which there isn't.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"A Frostgrave Seer who warned the Council of something coming and was struck from the record for it; Cait has been warning Smeltgate about the Run all season, and Smeltgate keeps striking her from its accounts."},"band":{"act":"The Turning","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Goth metal; the tunneller from the vent hummed one of their songs, and Cait found a song-sheet of it at the Smeltgate market and has been learning it alone on the late shift.","rv_vote":"The Turning, for the tunneller, wherever she is."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the guild's claim that the Run belongs to Smeltgate; it runs through Emberside and Emberside keeps it.","edict":"Against, but the Run can't be left for a riot, so she'll take the late shift while everyone else goes."},"wants":"To go down the warm vent past the bend and see what has turned over in its sleep.","site":"emberside","town":"Emberside","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:emberside:b":{"name":"Gwil Emberly","age":81,"role":"retired channel-keeper; the last living person who fed the Magma Serpent","look":"Small, very bent, bald and spotted, a lamb-chain link worn on a leather cord round his neck, and one milky eye from a spatter of lava sixty years ago.","voice":"Slow storyteller's Kindle, pauses longer than you think he should, finishes every story with a short laugh as if he's surprised by it.","greet":"Warm hearth. Sit. You've come about the serpent. Everybody comes about the serpent. Sit anyway.","lines":["I was ten. My grandmother lowered the lamb and I held the chain. Forty nights. It breathed like a bellows full of sand.","Smeltgate's guild came with bread again. I said, 'We lowered lamb to a serpent for forty days rather than fight it. You think we'll go to war over wages?' They didn't laugh. I did.","The Run's dark this season. Cait Ashlow says it's turned over. She's right. I've seen that colour once before. I was ten."],"about":"I raked the Run for fifty-five years and fed a Magma Serpent for forty nights when I was a boy, and that's all anyone wants to know about me, so I tell it well. I'm the oldest in Emberside. I'll eat lamb with you on Serpent Supper whether you're from here or not.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, old village Kindle with words the young ones don't use"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, refuses to learn more"}],"childhood":"Gwil's grandmother Hywela Emberly was head keeper in the summer the Magma Serpent came up into the Run and coiled there like a burning hawser, blocking the channel. The village argued for a day about fighting it. Hywela listened, then went to the butcher, bought a lamb, chained it, and lowered it into the Run beside the serpent's head at dusk. Gwil, aged ten, held the slack of the chain and felt the serpent take the lamb, a single pull, like a fish on a line the size of a house. They did it every night for forty nights. Gwil held the chain every time. On the forty-first night the serpent was gone. He has remembered every one of those nights his whole life, and remembers very little else of being ten except that he was hungry, because all the lamb went to the serpent.","schooling":"None to speak of. In his boyhood the Smeltgate bakehouse took Emberside children only in winter, and his grandmother needed him at the Post in summer. He learned his letters late, at thirty, from his wife, and still writes like a child. The Lamp Test lit nothing; Hywela said she'd have been disappointed if it had, because sparks leave and keepers don't.","history":"He raked the Run from twelve to sixty-seven, through four head keepers, and was never head keeper himself because he didn't want to argue with Smeltgate. In his twenties a lava spatter took the sight of his left eye; he went back on the Run a week later with a patch and his grandmother's rake. He married Eira Pryce, a coal-cake baker with a laugh like a dropped tray, and they had five children, three of whom became keepers. Eira died at seventy. He began, then, telling the serpent story properly, to every child who asked why the village doesn't just kill things, and the story became the custom of Serpent Supper, with the lamb shank thrown into the Run and the whole village eating lamb so it isn't eating alone. He invented that last bit himself, he admits. Hywela didn't say it. He thinks she would have.","family":"Five children, eleven grandchildren, two great-grandchildren; Garet Ashlow is his sister's grandson; his late wife Eira, whose coal-cake mould hangs over his hearth.","hero":{"who":"Hywela Emberly, his grandmother","why":"Faced with a Magma Serpent the whole village wanted to fight, she went and bought a lamb. He thinks that is the bravest thing a person can do, and the most Flameborne, and that nobody understands why."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Throat-singing with a famous forty-second silence in the middle; he heard it on a traveller's music-box and said the silence sounded exactly like the gap between the serpent's breaths.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime, and he'll sit through the silence with his eyes shut, holding the chain-link at his neck."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King nothing; he's old and the King looks fifty and has enough to deal with. If they push him, he'll refuse the King his eyebrows, which is a keeper joke and the King will get it.","edict":"Against, and too old to riot; he'll lower a lamb shank into the Run for anybody who comes back from one."},"wants":"To live to see the Run go bright again, and to hold a chain one more time.","site":"emberside","town":"Emberside","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:emberfall_vf2":{"name":"Tavish Rook","age":36,"role":"village trader in kit, wine and barrels; once a junior clerk to the royal steward","look":"Slim, tidy, ink on one cuff and wine on the other, a clerk's stoop he's trying to grow out of, and a Hearthmount haircut gone shaggy.","voice":"Quick, ironic, apologetic out of old habit, then very firm once he's warmed up, like a man who learned to argue late.","greet":"Warm hearth. Wine's local, kit's from Hearthmount, the barrels are better than both. Mind the step, it's stickier than it looks.","lines":["The steward's clerk came down offering last year's price. I know that clerk. I trained him. I trained him badly, apparently.","Sorcha Vell took him on the press-house steps. I've been on those steps. I came down a different man.","The Three Jars won't be opened while Aoife Lisk is breathing. She disagrees with everything. Out of principle."],"about":"I was a clerk in the royal steward's office in Hearthmount and I came down here five years ago to pay Fieldhaven last year's price for the cellar wine. Sorcha Vell argued with me on the press-house steps for two hours and I went back, resigned, packed a bag, and came here to sell barrels. Best argument I ever lost.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, Hearthmount court Kindle and village Kindle, depending on how much wine"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, clerk's Bridge"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it; the cellar ledgers for Lightforge buyers are kept in Clear"}],"childhood":"Tavish grew up on the third terrace of Hearthmount, in rooms over a tannery, the son of a laundress and a lamp-oil seller, both of whom shouted at each other with deep affection every evening. He was the quiet one in a loud family and was sent, at seven, to sit in the steward's waiting room on errands for his mother's customers, where he watched clerks write and fell in love with ledgers: columns, totals, the sound of a pen. He used to borrow spoiled ledger pages and invent kingdoms with their own accounts. His older sisters called him 'the King's Abacus' and he took it as a compliment, which annoyed them.","schooling":"The bakehouse school on Ovenrow under Mistress Gorse, who made pupils knead with their eyes shut; he couldn't, and peeked, and she caught him every time. The Lamp Test at the Hearthmount ring lit nothing. At twelve he went as an errand-boy to the royal steward's office, and the senior clerk, a dry old woman named Sabeth Iolo, taught him double-entry and the rule that every number you write is somebody's supper.","history":"He worked in the steward's office twenty years, rising to junior cellar-clerk, which meant his job was to make Fieldhaven's wine cheaper. The steward's office ran on a rule nobody wrote down: pay the villages a season late and at last year's rate, because they need the cellar more than the cellar needs them. Five years ago Tavish was sent down with the price. Sorcha Vell met him on the press-house steps and, over two hours, in front of the whole village, explained exactly what last year's price meant in Fieldhaven: how many children, how many barrels, how much ash. He agreed to this year's price, which he wasn't authorised to do, and went back to Hearthmount and was dismissed. He came back to Fieldhaven the following week. He now runs the village stall and keeps the press-house's accounts for nothing but meals, and he sits with Sorcha whenever a buyer comes, to tell her what the buyer's ledger will say before the buyer does.","family":"His parents and three sisters in Hearthmount, who visit at First Coal and drink the village dry; no partner, a long hopeless fondness for the cooper-smith, Morwen Hoop, which everyone except Morwen knows about.","hero":{"who":"The Hammer-Bearer, the Councillor without a Realm","why":"The only one who stood when the Council laughed at Metalcrown's ninth petition. Tavish stood up once, on the press-house steps, against his own office, and knows how long one person standing in a laughing room feels."},"band":{"act":"Sunwarm","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Summer Latin pop; he danced to it once at a Lightforge buyer's wedding and, being a clerk, had never danced before, and it went so well that he's danced to it every Treading Night since.","rv_vote":"Sunwarm, which nobody in Fieldhaven will forgive him for, because Lightforge wins too much already."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll go to the Refusal with Sorcha and refuse the King the steward's season-late payments, and this time he'll bring the ledgers.","edict":"Torn. He's against it, but a riot means the Hearthmount carts stop, and if the carts stop the vintage rots in the barrel."},"wants":"For the King's cellar to pay Fieldhaven on time for one full year, so he can say he did one honest thing for the ledger.","site":"emberfall_vf2","town":"Fieldhaven","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:emberfall_vf2":{"name":"Morwen Hoop","age":42,"role":"cooper-smith: barrel hoops, press screws and vine-stakes; keeper of the Three Jars' grille","look":"Tall, long-necked, hair cropped like a lamb's fleece, one ear pierced with a twist of old hoop-iron, forearms crossed with thin pale scars from springing hoops.","voice":"Low, unhurried, says exactly what she thinks with no softening; doesn't notice when people are fond of her.","greet":"Warm hearth. If it's a barrel, I'll need to see the staves. If it's a horse, there's a farrier in Clayford.","lines":["The Three Jars open when the whole village agrees on something. I've lived here forty-two years. We agree on nothing. Not even the weather.","Treading Night's coming. Couples argue all week for it. I make the hoops for the barrels they argue into. I hear everything.","The steward's men pay a season late. My barrels don't. Funny how that works."],"about":"I make hoops, press screws, vine stakes, anything iron in a vineyard. My grandfather forged the grille on the Three Jars and I keep it oiled. I've got a daughter who wants to be an idol in Hearthmount and a husband who left, and I've got a lot of barrels.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, for the Lightforge and Spiritveil buyers"}],"childhood":"Morwen grew up in the forge behind the press-house, where her grandfather Hew Hoop worked iron and her father worked wood, and the two of them argued every day about whether a barrel is a wooden thing with iron round it or an iron thing with wood inside. She sided with her grandfather. She was a tall solemn child who preferred to hammer things rather than play. At seven her grandfather let her hold the tongs while he re-oiled the grille on the Three Jars and told her that the jars would open when the village agreed on something, and she asked, seriously, 'What would it have to be?' and he couldn't tell her. She has been thinking about it ever since.","schooling":"The Fieldhaven bakehouse, which bakes must-bread in the press season and teaches letters the rest of the year; Baker Lowenna Pask taught the children their sums with grapes, and at the end of each lesson they ate the answer. Morwen's Lamp Test lit nothing. She went into her grandfather's forge at twelve and has never wanted another trade.","history":"She took over the forge at twenty-five when her grandfather's eyes failed. She makes every hoop on every Fieldhaven barrel, and the press screws, and repairs the old Buried Vintage press that nobody uses but nobody will let her melt. At twenty-eight she married Ewin Treddle, a Hearthmount tavern-keeper who came to buy wine, and they had a daughter, Nia. Ewin did not like the village, or ash, or the smell of the press-house in autumn, or, eventually, Morwen, who did not argue with him, which in Flameborne is the worst sign of all. He went back to Hearthmount when Nia was six. Morwen did not argue with that either. Nia, now fourteen, has decided she will join House of the Seven Ovens, and has been practising fire choreography with lit hoops in the forge yard, and Morwen has made her a set of hoops of exactly the right weight and has said nothing else about it, which Nia knows is Morwen's way of being on her side.","family":"Her daughter Nia; her ex-husband Ewin in Hearthmount; her grandfather Hew, dead six years, whose hammer she still uses.","hero":{"who":"The Flameborne King","why":"At First Coal four years ago she refused him the steward's payment terms, in a voice that didn't carry past the third row, and he leaned down to hear her and answered with bread, and then with an order to the steward. It was the first time in years anyone had leaned in to listen to her."},"band":{"act":"House of the Seven Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Seven idols with fire choreography; she didn't care for them until her daughter started practising their routines with the hoops she made, and now she knows every move.","rv_vote":"House of the Seven Ovens, for Nia, who'll have her lamp borrowed off her otherwise."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the steward's season-late payments again, in the same quiet voice, and she expects him to lean in again.","edict":"Against. She won't riot. She'll make iron hoops for the barricades if anyone asks, and not if they don't."},"wants":"For the village to agree on one thing, so she can unlock the grille and watch the jars opened.","site":"emberfall_vf2","town":"Fieldhaven","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:emberfall_vf2":{"name":"Donal Vell","age":30,"role":"vine-dresser and treader; the press-mistress's son","look":"Broad, cheerful, black curls going prematurely grey at the front, purple-stained feet and ankles in all seasons.","voice":"Loud, fast, laughing; argues for the joy of it and switches sides halfway through if it's more fun.","greet":"Warm hearth! Are you here to buy wine or to argue about it? Both? Good, Mam's in.","lines":["Steward's clerk came down offering last year's price. Mam took him on the press-house steps. Poor man's still not sure what happened. I'm not either, and I was there.","Treading Night's in a week. Rhun and I have been arguing since Tuesday. We're behind. The Prosser couple started a month ago.","The Three Jars open when the whole village agrees. I agree with everyone on everything, so it isn't me holding it up."],"about":"I dress the vines and tread the first grapes with my husband Rhun, who's a cook in a Hearthmount tavern and comes down for the season. I'm Sorcha Vell's son. Everyone says I'm nothing like her. They mean it kindly. I'm not sure it is.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words and very good charm"}],"childhood":"Donal grew up in the press-house itself, in a set of rooms above the barrel store, the only child of Sorcha Vell, press-mistress, and a quiet vine-dresser named Emrys Vell who died when Donal was eleven. His mother argued with every buyer, every clerk, every neighbour and every vine and won, and Donal, who adored her, decided he would never fight anyone about anything if he could help it. He spent his boyhood in the vines with his father, who talked to the plants and pruned them with a little silver knife. After his father died, he and his mother argued for the first time, about his father's grave-coal, and the argument went on for a week and ended with them both in tears and laughing, and he says that was the day he understood Flameborne.","schooling":"The Fieldhaven bakehouse, where Baker Lowenna Pask taught sums with grapes; Donal ate most of his answers before he'd checked them. The Lamp Test lit nothing. He was the happiest child in Fieldhaven about it, because sparks go away to Korrhaven and he never wanted to leave the vines.","history":"He took over his father's rows at sixteen and is now the best vine-dresser in the village by a distance, though he won't say so in front of his mother. At twenty-three he met Rhun Carrow, a cook from a Hearthmount tavern who came down to buy must for a recipe, and they argued about whether you can roast a lamb with must or only baste it, for three hours on the press-house steps, and then went inside. They were married at First Coal the year after. Rhun keeps his tavern work in the city and comes down for the press season, and they tread the first grapes together on Treading Night every year, after a week of arguments. Their treading has been the loudest in the village four years running. The wine from their first treading, three years ago, was so good that the steward's clerk asked for it by name. Sorcha sold it to him at three times the price and said it was because of the argument.","family":"His mother Sorcha Vell, press-mistress; his husband Rhun Carrow, a cook; his late father Emrys, whose pruning knife he carries.","hero":{"who":"Sabra Hearth","why":"The pop star who refused to kneel to the King at a festival; Donal, who has never refused anyone anything, thinks it must have been the most frightening moment of her life and wants to know how she did it."},"band":{"act":"Two Windows","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A Lightforge–Shadowdeep couple who sing duets; he and Rhun sing their songs on the press-house steps when Rhun comes down, one taking each window, badly.","rv_vote":"Two Windows, both lamps, his and Rhun's."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll stand next to his mother and agree with everything she refuses, and if she won't go he'll refuse the King a season-late payment on his own and probably get it wrong.","edict":"Against, because his mother is, and because Rhun says the Hearthmount streets are going to go up and he'd rather be with him if they do."},"wants":"To win one argument against his mother before she dies, and to lose it on purpose the same day.","site":"emberfall_vf2","town":"Fieldhaven","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:emberfall_vf2:b":{"name":"Aoife Lisk","age":94,"role":"retired vine-dresser; the last living person who dug out the vines in the Dry Ash Year","look":"Tiny and straight-backed, wisps of white hair, a Frostgrave word-cord with sixty knots worn round her waist over a Flameborne apron.","voice":"Clipped, precise, a dry wit; pauses before promises like a Frostgrave speaker, which she learned from her husband.","greet":"Warm hearth. Halla, too. Say probably and keep it, my husband used to say. Sit, if you're staying. Probably.","lines":["The Three Jars open when the whole village agrees. I disagree. I'll go on disagreeing. Ask me why. I'm not telling you.","The steward pays late. The steward's always paid late. The steward paid late when I was twelve and I dug vines out with my fingers.","Treading Night. When my Tormod and I trod, we argued in two languages. Best wine for forty years, the vintners said. Of course it was."],"about":"I dug out the vines in the Dry Ash Year with my own hands when I was twelve. I married a Frostgrave cooper who came here for the barrels and stayed sixty-one years. I'm ninety-four. I disagree with the village about the jars and I'm not changing my mind at this age.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"good, her husband's tongue, with the pauses"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, from sixty years of buyers"}],"childhood":"Aoife was twelve in the Dry Ash Year, when the ash fell nine days and buried the vines to the shoulder. She remembers the silence most: no birds, no wind, just ash coming down soft as flour. When it stopped the whole village went out with spades and buckets and their bare hands, and she was put on the low vines with the other children because their fingers were small enough to get between the roots without bruising them. She dug for eleven days. Her nails came off. Her mother bandaged them with must-soaked linen at night. That autumn the press ran the best wine anyone had tasted, and she was given a cup of it at the pressing, and remembers it as sweet and smoky and slightly too strong, and as the first time she was ever allowed to drink wine at a grown-up's table.","schooling":"The Fieldhaven bakehouse under old Baker Cledwyn Pask, the present baker's great-grandfather, who taught letters by scratching them into the must-bread crust before baking so the children ate their lessons. Aoife learned to read fast to get the bread. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She went to the vines full time at thirteen.","history":"She dressed vines for seventy years. At twenty-six she married Tormod Rimesson, a Frostgrave cooper who came down the Spiritveil roads to buy oak and Fieldhaven barrels for the Coldwater Step trade and found Flameborne too loud and Aoife too sharp, and stayed for both. Their marriage was argued in two languages. He tied a knot on his word-cord for every promise he kept to her, and when he died at eighty-seven there were sixty knots, and she wears it now. They had four children, all argumentative, all still alive. She taught her daughters to dress vines and her sons to make barrels, and refused to let any of them leave Fieldhaven, and they all left anyway, and they all came back. Every time the village has come near agreement on anything, the weather, a buyer, a feast-day, she has voted against it, loudly, from her chair on the press-house steps. People think she does it for sport.","family":"Four children, nine grandchildren, too many great-grandchildren to name in one breath; her husband Tormod, dead seven years, whose word-cord she wears.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"The Seer who knotted a vision into a cord so it couldn't be lost; Tormod told the story every winter, and Aoife thinks tying down a thing so it can't change is the most stubborn act in any Realm, and she approves of stubborn."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Spiritveil's forty-year love songs; she and Tormod first heard them on a Spiritveil buyer's music-box and laughed at how slow they were, and then, fifty years later, listened to them every night.","rv_vote":"Decades, because nobody else understands that a love song should take forty years to finish."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the same thing she has refused three Kings: the steward's late payments. She's never once had an answer that changed it, and she always takes the bread.","edict":"Against, and too old to riot; she'll disagree with Lightforge from her chair, and she's had practice."},"wants":"For the Three Jars to stay closed until she's gone, and for nobody to know why.","site":"emberfall_vf2","town":"Fieldhaven","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:keep_vf1":{"name":"Neasa Cartwright","age":49,"role":"harness-maker and trader; runs the ford's cart-dragging team for a fee","look":"Rangy, weather-reddened, pale Lightforge-fair hair under a Flameborne leather cap, clay to the knee on every working day, a coil of tow-rope worn over one shoulder like a sash.","voice":"Precise, oddly formal, finishes facts with 'I saw it' or 'I'm told' the Lightforge way and then swears in Kindle.","greet":"Warm hearth. Aure, if you're from the Plains. Harness is on the left, rope on the right, and if your cart's in the ford it's three coppers and my best horses.","lines":["The Stormreach traders laugh at our horses. Then they try to pull a wagon out of that ford. I've dragged out eleven Low Reach carts this year. I saw every one.","The Low Reach man's claiming the mare again. It'll go to the Keep. It always goes to the Keep. Lord Vashen will say 'next season'. I'm told he enjoys it.","I keep a Day-Book. Still. Thirty-nine years in Flameborne and I write down what I did every night. Nobody reads it. That's the point now."],"about":"I was born in Lampwick on the Rim of Lightforge to a tanner and a Flameborne harness-maker, and my mother brought me here when I was ten. I make harness and I pull carts out of the ford. I'm the only person in Clayford who knows how to say 'I saw it' in two languages and mean it in both.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native from ten, with a Rim accent nobody lets her forget"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, childhood Clear, with the evidential endings she still can't drop"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"}],"childhood":"Neasa spent her first ten years in Lampwick, on the Rim of the Noon Plains, where there is real dusk and the tanneries line the stag-antler-lit streets. Her father was a Lightforge tanner who kept a careful Day-Book; her mother was a Flameborne harness-maker who'd come to buy hides. Neasa was taught to write a Day-Book entry at five: what you did, where, who saw. At ten she read her father's open Day-Book on the kitchen table, as anyone may, and found an entry, plainly written, about a woman in the next street. She read it aloud at supper, because in Lightforge that is what you do. Her mother put down her spoon, packed one bag, and took Neasa to Flameborne within the week. Neasa did not understand for years that she'd done anything at all.","schooling":"In Lampwick, a Rim lamp-school under Teacher Merin Glass, who kept one corner of the room unlit on purpose and let children sit there when they cried, which was later reported and he was fined. In Clayford, nothing: her mother apprenticed her to a harness-maker the week they arrived. Her Lamp Test in Lampwick, at eight, lit nothing, though she says the Radiance lamp 'looked at me for a while.'","history":"She learned harness from Old Brannagh Feeney, a Clayford saddler who said she was the only apprentice he'd had who measured twice because she wanted to and not because he told her to. She took over his shop at twenty-six. At twenty-nine, tired of watching Stormreach traders curse at the ford, she bought four of Clayford's heaviest geldings off Hoss Tarrant's father and set up a dragging team; now three crews of six work the ford in season and she gets a third of every fee. She has pulled out three lords' carts, and keeps their boots, which they lost, in a row above her counter. She never married. She lives with her mother, now eighty, who has never once asked about Lampwick, and her mother's three cats. She has kept a Day-Book every night for thirty-nine years in Clear, and keeps them in a locked chest, which in Lightforge would be a scandal and in Flameborne is simply a chest.","family":"Her mother Sinead, eighty, harness-maker retired; her father in Lampwick, who has written to her every Unshadowing for thirty-nine years.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"The anchor-master who held Korr's Span, a Stillman Stormreach riders still throw stones at. Neasa spends her working life holding a cart against the pull of a ford, and she knows holding a thing still is the hardest work and the least thanked."},"band":{"act":"Landslide","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Hard rock; she heard them on a Stonehold carter's music-box while dragging his cart out and played it so loud the horses pulled better.","rv_vote":"Landslide, and she'll write it in her Day-Book, which is the only vote she trusts."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's ford-toll ruling, which gives half her dragging fees to the Keep's horse-farm when it's her team doing the pulling.","edict":"Against, and with a Lightforge reason nobody here expects: she grew up seen, every day, and she knows what it does to a family when every thought has to be written in the light."},"wants":"To read one of her father's letters without wanting to burn it.","site":"keep_vf1","town":"Clayford","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:keep_vf1":{"name":"Bram Farrow","age":64,"role":"village farrier and smith; shod Lord Vashen's first pony","look":"Huge, rounded shoulders, a bald brown head with a ring of white curls, hands like spades, a leather horse-apron so old it has the shape of his knees in it.","voice":"Deep, slow, gentle with horses and with children, short with everyone else; hums in rhythm while he works.","greet":"Warm hearth. Hold the head. Don't pull, just hold. There. That's all most of life is.","lines":["Hoss Tarrant talks to stallions like old friends and to people like stallions. I talk to stallions like stallions. We get on.","The Boot Post? I put it up. Don't ask me whose boot. I'll tell you whose boot it is if you buy a shoe. Maybe.","Stable broth's on the forge. If you're holding a horse's head you get a ladle. If you're not, hold a horse's head."],"about":"I've shod horses at Clayford for fifty years. Heavy horses, patient ones, the kind the Keep likes because they don't bolt. I shod the young lord's pony the morning he rode into the ford on a dare. He came out barefoot. I was there. I'm the only one who was there who's still alive.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, slow"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"horse-words: hoof, shoe, lame, gelding, sound"}],"childhood":"Bram was the son of a Clayford farrier and a mother who kept the village's geese, and he grew up between hooves and wings, with a goose scar on one ear to show for it. He was a big gentle boy who was afraid of nothing that had four legs and a great many things that had two. He used to sleep in the foaling barn in spring to be there when the mares dropped, and at nine he delivered a foal alone in the night, turning it the right way round with both arms in up to the shoulder, and was found at dawn asleep in the straw next to the mare and her colt, both fine. The old village head named the colt across the ford for him: 'Bram's Luck.' It is the only thing anyone ever named for him and he is still proud of it.","schooling":"No school, or the school of the forge: Clayford children who wanted letters walked to the Keep's garrison school, and Bram didn't want letters. His father taught him to read a hoof the way other children read a page. The Lamp Test lit nothing; his father said that was good, because sparks spook horses.","history":"He took over his father's forge at twenty. At twenty-two, on a hot spring morning, he shod a fat grey pony for the Keep's boy heir, who was about ten and very proud and had been dared by the stable-lads to ride into the ford. Bram told him not to. The boy rode in, the clay took the pony to the knees, and the boy came out the far side on foot, barefoot, and walked back to the Keep without a word to anyone. Bram dug the pony out with a rope and two geldings. The boot was never found, but the next spring there was a child-sized boot nailed to a post at the ford. Bram married Gwen Lowry, a stable-hand with a laugh like a whinny, and they raised six children in the forge-house, four of them now farriers or stable-hands at the Keep. He's still shoeing, though his grandson does the hind feet now.","family":"His wife Gwen; six children, four in horse work; eleven grandchildren; a donkey named Lord, which he insists is a coincidence.","hero":{"who":"Amon Greycloak, the first Shardwatch","why":"The first to keep watch at the edge of a shard so nobody else had to. Bram sits up by the ford on bad nights with a lantern for carts that come late, and thinks somebody has to be the one who watches."},"band":{"act":"The Rainmakers","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A drum collective; he shoes in rhythm, and a Stormreach carter once told him he hammered like a Rainmaker, and he took it as the best compliment he's ever had.","rv_vote":"The Rainmakers, because they're drums, and the rest is just people shouting over drums."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the remount levy, which makes Clayford sell its best geldings to the Keep below market; he'll bring a gelding to the Refusal to make the point.","edict":"Against. If there's a riot he'll shoe the horses for it and stay home to watch the ford, because somebody has to."},"wants":"To shoe one more of the Keep's colts for Lord Vashen and see if the man remembers who dug his pony out.","site":"keep_vf1","town":"Clayford","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:keep_vf1":{"name":"Ffion Tarrant","age":12,"role":"stud-master's daughter; foal-minder; wants a fast horse in a slow-horse village","look":"Small and wiry, clay-coloured to the knees, two muddy brown plaits tied with Kestrel Vane ribbons she bought with her own coppers, a racing-crop she made herself from a willow switch.","voice":"Quick, breathless, talks to horses in Hoss's voice and to people in her own, which is faster.","greet":"Warm hearth! Don't stand behind the grey, she kicks, only the people she likes. She likes everyone.","lines":["The Stormreach traders laugh at our horses. Too slow, they say. I'd like to see them try the ford. I'd also like a fast horse. Don't tell Da.","Hoss is my da. He talks to stallions like old friends. He talks to me like a stallion. Mostly like a young one.","The Low Reach man says the bay mare was his. I don't know. She's very fast, for a Clayford horse. Suspiciously fast. I didn't say that."],"about":"I'm Ffion, Hoss Tarrant's daughter. I mind the foals. I want to race like Kestrel Vane, only on a horse, because I can't fly and nobody here will give me a skyray. Da says Clayford horses aren't for racing. Da hasn't seen what I've seen.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, very fast"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few words from Jory Lowe: 'Wahai!', 'on the drop', 'go well, go fast'"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"some"}],"childhood":"Ffion was born in the foaling barn during a thunderstorm while her mother, a stud-groom, was helping a mare; mother and mare delivered within an hour of each other. She has been in the stables since she could stand, and spent her first years asleep in the hay-racks. She has an older brother, Gwion, who is going to be stud-master and is calm about it, and she is not calm about anything. At six she learned to ride bareback on an old remount called Pudding, and she has fallen off every horse in Clayford since, which she counts as an achievement. She discovered Kestrel Vane at nine when a Stormreach trader left a torn poster in the tack room, and stuck it above her bed, and her father took it down and her mother put it back up.","schooling":"The Keep's garrison school in the old toll-room, two days a week, under Dilys Penry, who makes the children write contracts for their own good behaviour; Ffion's has the most proud nails in the class after Wenna Teller's. The rest is the stable. Her Lamp Test at eight lit nothing at all, and she was relieved, because she'd heard sparks have to go to Korrhaven, where there are no horses.","history":"At ten she was given charge of the foals and has kept every one alive two seasons running, which her father says is luck and her mother says isn't. She knows every horse in Clayford by its walk. At eleven she began taking the disputed bay mare, the one the Low Reach trader says was stolen, out at night to the long meadow, because the mare seemed to want to run, and discovered that the mare was the fastest thing in the village by three lengths and could go all night. She has been riding her in secret ever since, with Jory Lowe, who caught her the second night and has been teaching her Stormreach riding in return for her silence. She has entered herself in the Cindermoot duel-crowd horse race next spring under a false name. She's twelve. She thinks of everything.","family":"Her father Hoss Tarrant, stud-master; her mother Elin, stud-groom; her brother Gwion; a lot of horses she considers family.","hero":{"who":"Kestrel Vane","why":"The Stormreach sky-racer who ages every season she flies and flies anyway. Ffion doesn't understand ageing yet, but she understands wanting to go faster more than anyone thinks you should."},"band":{"act":"Kestrel Vane","realm":"Stormreach","why":"She is her hero and her favourite singer, and she can't tell the difference and doesn't want to; she sings the songs to the foals at night.","rv_vote":"Kestrel Vane, every lamp she can find, and she'll beg one off Jory."},"vote":{"local":"She's too young for First Coal, but her First No is next year, and she plans to refuse her father the rule that Clayford horses aren't for racing.","edict":"She doesn't know what it is. When Jory explains it she says it sounds like the Keep counting horses, and that horses hate being counted."},"wants":"To race the bay mare in front of the whole village and win by three lengths.","site":"keep_vf1","town":"Clayford","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:keep_vf1:b":{"name":"Jory Lowe","age":39,"role":"horse-breaker and remount trainer; Low Reach-born, married into Clayford","look":"Lean and long-boned, a hawk nose, one thin storm-grey streak at the left brow from a single charge in his youth, eats every meal standing up.","voice":"Gale-clipped: short, fast phrases with hard stops; laughs loud and never in the past tense when he can help it.","greet":"Wahai! Warm hearth. Go well. Sit down if you want, I won't, it's not a funeral.","lines":["They say Clayford horses are slow. They are. Slow's fine. A slow horse comes home. I come from a place where the fast ones don't.","The Low Reach trader claiming the mare? That's my cousin Harl. Shouting. He's good at shouting. Clayford's good at mud. Mud wins.","I eat standing up. My wife sits. Thirteen years married and every meal she says it's like dining with a ghost."],"about":"I was born on the Low Reach horse-steppe. Fastest horses in the Nine. I came south with a trading string at twenty-two, fell in the ford, was dragged out by Neasa Cartwright's team, and married the harness-maker's apprentice who laughed at me. I break remounts for the Keep now. I teach slow horses to be brave.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"fluent, Gale-accented, no past tense when he's excited"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, horse-trader's"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"understands a little; it's close to Gale"}],"childhood":"Jory grew up on the Low Reach, the horse-steppe of Stormreach, in a family of breeders who never slept in the same place two nights running and kept their whole lives in saddle-bags. He was put on a horse before he could walk and did his First Fall at ten off a cliff over the family net with his cousin Harl, both shrieking the whole way down. He was fast and reckless and happy. At fourteen he discovered a Half-Spark of Tempest, used it once in a race to put a crackle of wind behind his horse, won, and came home with the first grey streak at his left brow. His mother slapped him and then shouted an apology from the tent-roof for slapping him, and he has never used it again.","schooling":"A riding-school on the Low Reach, which moves with the herds, taught by a white-haired horse-mistress called Saba Hightether who taught letters by scratching them into the saddle-leather so children learned them while riding. Jory can still only read at a canter, he says. His Lamp Test, done at a Stormreach chain-yard, lit the Tempest lamp faintly: a Half-Spark, not enough for Korrhaven.","history":"At twenty-two he brought a string of horses south along the east road to sell at the Keep, and at Clayford ford his lead horse balked and he went in to his waist in clay and lost both boots. Neasa Cartwright's team dragged him out, and her apprentice, a short plain-spoken saddler named Bethan Ap Rhys, laughed so hard she fell off the bank. He married her the next spring. He has broken remounts for Lord Vashen's garrison for seventeen years, teaching the heavy Clayford horses to stand under fire and bolt for nobody. He is the only man in the village who can get a stallion to walk into the ford without a fight. He and Bethan have two sons who sit to eat like Flameborne boys and stand up to argue like Stormreach ones. He shouts his apologies from the roof at the Great Turning and the village has started joining in.","family":"His wife Bethan, a saddler; two sons, Rhodri and Kel; his mother still on the Low Reach; his cousin Harl, the trader claiming the mare.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"'No Realm should ever be alone.' Jory lives in a Realm that isn't his own and calls it home, and says that's the whole of what Vael Korr meant."},"band":{"act":"Wahai!","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Party reggaeton with pipes, named for the greeting; he plays it on a Stormreach pipe-box at the Colt Naming and makes the old village head dance.","rv_vote":"Wahai!, always, and he says if Stormreach ever wins they'll shout the result from every roof in Clayford."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the rule that married-in foreigners can't speak at the village colt-naming; and if he were home he'd shout for Seraphine Skyrend at the Free Assembly till his throat bled.","edict":"Against, the Stormreach way: loudly and on the move. He'll ride to Hearthmount for the riot if there is one, and come back the same night."},"wants":"To settle the mare quarrel before his cousin or his wife finds out the truth of it.","site":"keep_vf1","town":"Clayford","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:cindermoot_vf1":{"name":"Ula Feltwright","age":55,"role":"cloak-felter and village trader in ash-cloaks, hoods and kit","look":"Soft-spoken, round-faced, iron-grey hair in a net, hands red and shiny from years of hot felting-water; wears her own ash-cloak indoors and out.","voice":"Calm, warm, very level, which in Wetherby makes people uneasy; never raises her voice and never seems to need to.","greet":"Warm hearth. Come in out of the ash. Try a cloak on. Go on. Shake your head. See? Not a cinder sticks.","lines":["Lion's been by the north fold again. Larkhollow says it's a fine lion. Everyone's shouting. I'm felting. Shouting doesn't keep anyone dry.","Larkhollow pays for the sheep in honey. Wetherby complains about the honey. I like the honey. I keep that to myself.","When the ash comes down, folk buy cloaks. When it stops, they buy hoods for the next time. It's a good trade. It's a patient one."],"about":"I felt ash-cloaks, as my mother did and hers did. I sell them, and kit for the road, and I'm known as the calmest woman in Wetherby, which is not, I'm told, a compliment here.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, quiet"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good; Lightforge and Spiritveil buyers come for the cloaks"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a little, enough to hear what a Lightforge buyer is thinking"}],"childhood":"Ula grew up in the felting-house at the bottom of the village, where every room smells of wet wool and sheep-oil and the floors are always warm and slippery. Her mother, Gráinne Feltwright, was a famous felter with an equally famous temper: she shouted at the wool, the weather, the buyers, the lions and her children, every day, at full Flameborne volume. Ula, the middle of five, decided at about eight that she would not shout, ever, and she has more or less kept to it. She was the child who sat on the felting-table rolling the wool under her hands for hours while the house roared round her. Her brothers called her 'the Ewe' because she never bleated. It still stings, she admits, which is the only thing she'll admit stings.","schooling":"Wetherby has no bakehouse school; the children walked to Cindermoot's Little Ring, where Master Corwen Spill, then young, scored arguments with pebbles. Ula never once raised her voice in a round and lost all of them on volume, and Spill wrote on her slate, 'She is right, and it doesn't matter.' The Lamp Test lit nothing. She went back to the felting-house at twelve.","history":"She took the felting-house at thirty when her mother's hands stiffened, and she has run it in a quiet that has made it the best-run house in the village. Her cloaks are thicker and her prices are steady. At thirty-four she married Huw Penhallow, a Copperfield still-mender's son who sold her copper vats; he is a loud cheerful man and their marriage is the talk of Wetherby because nobody has ever heard them argue. They have one daughter, Mairwen, now a felter herself. Some years ago a Lightforge cloak-buyer, a sharp-eyed woman from Clearwater, sat in Ula's shop for an hour, watched her not lose her temper with a Wetherby shepherd who was screaming at her, and then said, very softly, in Bridge, 'You've banked it, haven't you.' Ula sold her a cloak at a discount and has never seen her since. She runs the shop, sends her daughter to the shearing, and keeps a little hearth in her back room with the coal banked under ash, which she tends herself and lets no one see.","family":"Her husband Huw, loud and contented; her daughter Mairwen; four siblings who all shout, including at her.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder, the Banked Fire","why":"Tried in Lightforge for 'concealed anger'. Ula has never told anyone why she admires her, and never will, and when asked she says it was the trial she liked, and the buyer goes away content."},"band":{"act":"Slow Heat","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Half-speed reggaeton; she plays it in the felting room because the beat is exactly as slow as the wool needs to be rolled.","rv_vote":"Slow Heat, quietly, and she won't tell her husband, who'll vote Hot Corner and want to talk about it."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King nothing, aloud. She'll stand in the crowd and let others shout, and afterwards go home and bank her coal.","edict":"Against, and it frightens her more than anyone in Wetherby knows: a register of sparks will lead to a register of tempers, and she knows which list she'd be on."},"wants":"To be left to keep her coal as she likes and never be asked why.","site":"cindermoot_vf1","town":"Wetherby","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:cindermoot_vf1":{"name":"Elis Shearer","age":34,"role":"village smith: shears, sheep-bells, fold-hinges; grinds blades for the whole valley","look":"Slight, stooped, long hair falling over his face, which he keeps turned to the work; quick clever hands and a tremor in them when he's watched.","voice":"Mumbled, shy, half-finished sentences, then clear and fluent as running water when he's talking about steel.","greet":"Warm hearth. Mm. Shears? Bells? Put it down there, I'll look. Don't watch, it's worse if you watch.","lines":["Lion's been by the north fold again. They want louder bells. A louder bell just tells the lion where dinner is. I'll make them louder anyway.","Larkhollow says the lions are Flameborne's and we don't kill what's ours. They don't keep sheep. I'm not saying I agree. I'm saying they don't keep sheep.","Ysolde Cragg in Cindermoot makes the seed-pans. I make the shears. We trade iron. We trade... other things. Mostly iron."],"about":"My father was a knife-grinder who walked the Coal Road with a wheel on his back. I walked it with him. When I was twelve we stopped here because the shears were so bad. I grind and I forge and I make the sheep-bells, and I'd rather you didn't look at me while I do it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, quiet"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good; road Bridge from his childhood"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"a handful of signs from a Shadowdeep grinder his father once walked with"}],"childhood":"Elis never had a home until he was twelve. His father, Gruff Shearer, was a knife-grinder who carried a treadle-wheel on his back from forge-town to forge-town along the Coal Road, sharpening blades in market squares; his mother had died when he was too small to remember. They slept in barns and lava-tubes and under carts, and Elis learned the whole Realm by its blades: Smeltgate cleavers, Cindermoot seed-knives, Hearthmount bread-knives that were never allowed to cut bread. Crowds watched his father work and Elis hated being looked at so much he learned to hide behind the wheel. One winter they walked for a month with a Shadowdeep grinder who never showed her face and taught Elis to say 'thank you' and 'go' with his hands. He thought she was the most sensible person he'd ever met.","schooling":"No school on the road; his father taught him letters by spelling out the smith's marks stamped on every blade they ground. At Wetherby, the old smith, Dai Bellows, took him on at twelve and found he could already sharpen better than Dai. His Lamp Test had been done at a Coal Road lamp-ring at eight, between jobs; nothing lit, and his father took him to grind the tester's knives.","history":"They stopped at Wetherby when Elis was twelve because the village's shears were so badly kept that Gruff refused to leave until he'd seen to every pair. He stayed a season. Then, coughing, he stayed a year, and died in the felting-house in Ula Feltwright's mother's spare room, and the village buried him under a cairn by the Tube and gave Elis to Dai Bellows. Elis took the forge at twenty-three. He makes the village's shears, short and long, fold-hinges and gate-latches, and the copper-and-iron sheep-bells that give each flock its own note, which Mab Coalby tunes by ear. He has never married. He trades iron with Ysolde Cragg at Cindermoot, who comes every month with seed-pans to be ground and does not let him hide behind his hair; they have been not-courting for three years and the whole of both villages is waiting.","family":"No family left; his father's treadle-wheel in the corner of the forge, still working; Ysolde Cragg, in a way neither of them will name.","hero":{"who":"Veilrend, Shadowdeep's First Blade","why":"A blade-maker nobody ever saw the face of, known only by the work. Elis would like that more than anything."},"band":{"act":"Lights Out","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Dark techno played in total darkness; he went once at a Smeltgate market-night and danced for two hours because no one could see him, and it's the only time in his life he's danced.","rv_vote":"Lights Out, and he's voting for them in the Sparkbeat Cup too, which he hasn't told anyone because it means saying he dances."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King nothing aloud; he'll write his Refusal on a slip and have Ysolde read it, and it's about the road-toll on grinders' wheels, which nearly killed his father's trade.","edict":"Against. He doesn't like anything being written down about anyone, least of all by people who want to look at you while they do it."},"wants":"To finish one sentence to Ysolde Cragg without looking at the floor.","site":"cindermoot_vf1","town":"Wetherby","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cindermoot_vf1":{"name":"Rory Coalby","age":17,"role":"apprentice shepherd; the head shepherd's great-nephew; refused the Conclave","look":"Gangly, freckled, a mop of black hair under a too-small ash-hood, ember-cracks like orange threads at both wrists, and a pocket that clinks with counting-pebbles.","voice":"Cheerful, fast, slightly off-key in everything including speech; laughs at himself first.","greet":"Warm hearth! Mind the ewe. She's called Biscuit. They're all called something. I can't remember most of them. Don't tell Aunt Mab.","lines":["Lion's been by the north fold again. Larkhollow says it's a fine lion. It is a fine lion. It's also eaten Biscuit's sister.","Aunt Mab counts the flock by singing, a note per sheep. I'm tone-deaf. I count by pebbles and hum. She says it's an insult to the sheep.","I had the Ember lamp light up for me when I was eight. Proper bright. I said no to Korrhaven at my First No. Everyone in the Realm thinks I'm mad. The sheep don't."],"about":"I'm Rory, Mab Coalby's great-nephew. I'm learning to be head shepherd, very slowly. I had a spark and I refused it, which you're apparently allowed to do here if you're stubborn enough. I'm stubborn enough. I'm not very good at anything else yet.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"Lamp Years Bridge, getting rusty"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few words, from the Knot-Cord Brothers' songs, including 'forty seconds' and something he thinks is rude"}],"childhood":"Rory was a shepherd's child from birth: his mother, Mab Coalby's niece, carried him out to the ash-meadows in a sling, and he learned to walk holding a ewe's fleece. He was a happy, clumsy, noisy boy who fell into the felting-vats twice and the Tube's drainage channel once, and who sang constantly, very badly, which in a village that counts its flock by song is a disaster. Aunt Mab tried to teach him the counting song at six. He sang a note for each sheep and got all the notes wrong and the flock came out at a hundred and nine when it was eighty-two. Mab laughed until she cried and then told him he'd have to find another way. He's been looking ever since.","schooling":"At eight the Lamp Test at Cindermoot lit the Ember lamp a fierce bright orange: a Full Spark. He went to the Lamp Years at Cindermoot's lamp-school under Teacher Branwen Coldiron, who made her pupils hold a lit candle steady while she told them jokes, to train them in control; Rory always laughed and always blew it out. He learned letters, history and drill, and was good at the history. At thirteen, at his First No, he stood in the Ring at Cindermoot and refused to go to Korrhaven.","history":"The refusal was a scandal for a month. The Lamp-school wrote to the Hearth Court; the Hearth Court wrote to his mother; his mother wrote back that the First No is the First No, and the Hearth Court, being Flameborne, could only agree. He has worked the flock with Aunt Mab since. His spark is still there; at fifteen, angry at a dog that killed a lamb, he flared without meaning to, and the ember-cracks came up orange at both wrists and never went down. He hasn't used it since. He is trying to learn the counting song by rhythm instead of pitch, tapping a beat for each sheep against his thigh, and it works about half the time. The other half he checks with pebbles. He has discovered the Knot-Cord Brothers, Frostgrave's only fast act, whose song 'Forty Seconds' has exactly the right rhythm for counting a fold of forty.","family":"His mother Siân and father Tomos, both shepherds; Great-Aunt Mab Coalby, head shepherd; five younger sisters, all of whom can sing.","hero":{"who":"Mab Coalby, his great-aunt","why":"She can count three hundred sheep in one breath and know which one is missing, and when he was six and got it all wrong she laughed instead of shouting. He thinks that's the most Flameborne thing he's ever seen, and the least."},"band":{"act":"The Knot-Cord Brothers","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Fast folk-house, 'Forty Seconds'; it's the first music whose beat matched the way he counts, and he played it to Aunt Mab and she grudgingly admitted the flock came out right.","rv_vote":"The Knot-Cord Brothers, in the Sparkbeat Cup and the main vote, because they're the only fast thing Frostgrave's ever made and somebody should encourage them."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's letter, still on file, that calls his First No 'a waste of the Realm's fire'.","edict":"Against, more than most: he'd be on Lightforge's list as a Full Spark who refused the Conclave, and he can't think of a list he'd like less."},"wants":"To count the whole flock in one go, by beat, and get it right in front of Aunt Mab.","site":"cindermoot_vf1","town":"Wetherby","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cindermoot_vf1:b":{"name":"Nainsi Drover","age":70,"role":"retired shepherd; holder of the north fold; complains about honey professionally","look":"Sinewy, sun-browned, a man's felt hat with a lark feather in it, a crook taller than she is, and a jar of fireweed honey always in her apron pocket.","voice":"Loud, scornful, tremendously cheerful underneath; every story becomes a complaint and every complaint becomes a story.","greet":"Warm hearth! You're not from Larkhollow? Good. Sit down. Honey? It's theirs. It's dreadful. Have some.","lines":["Lion's been by the north fold again. Larkhollow says it's a fine lion. Of course it's a fine lion. It's eating my sheep. It's eaten better than I have.","Larkhollow sends a jar of honey for every sheep. Forty jars last year. I've a shed full. I'm not complaining. I am complaining. Have a jar.","First fleece goes to the household that lost most. Three times I've had it. I'd rather have had the sheep."],"about":"I've kept the north fold at Wetherby since I was sixteen. I've lost more sheep to Ash Lions than anyone alive and I've got more honey than anyone alive, and I've complained about both every day. I live with Peigi, who's been my friend for fifty-five years, and who says I'd complain about the sun coming up.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, very loud"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words; uses Kindle louder instead"}],"childhood":"Nainsi was a shepherd's daughter from the north fold, the high one under the ash-ridges, nearest the lions, and the hardest. She learned her trade from a grandfather who sang the count like Mab Coalby but swore between notes. At eleven she was out alone with the flock when an ash-fall came in fast and black, and she found her best friend Peigi, a weaver's girl, sheltering under a rock with a sprained ankle, and carried her on her back the three miles down to the Tube, through the falling cinders, with the flock following behind them because it didn't know what else to do. They both got home with burns on their arms and the flock entire. She has told this story perhaps four thousand times and Peigi has corrected her on details perhaps four thousand times.","schooling":"A few winters at Cindermoot's Little Ring under Master Corwen Spill, who said she had the loudest voice he'd ever scored and gave her pebbles for it until he realised she wasn't actually making an argument. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She went back to the fold at twelve and has never wanted letters, though she can read a lion-print and a weather-sky better than most can read a page.","history":"She took the north fold at sixteen when her grandfather's knees gave out and held it for forty-nine years, through more lion-summers than anyone else alive. In the bad year, when an Ash Lion took forty sheep in a summer and the village sat up with drums and torches rather than see a hunt go up, Nainsi was the one who sat up longest, thirty-one nights straight, on the fold wall with a drum and a pot of cinder-wine. She has never married. She and Peigi set up house at twenty-five, when Peigi's parents died, and have lived together since in a stone cottage below the fold, which Peigi keeps neat and Nainsi keeps muddy. She retired from the flock at sixty-five and hands the fold to younger shepherds, but goes up every evening to check it, and to shout at the lions, who ignore her.","family":"Peigi, her friend and housemate of forty-five years; nephews and nieces down in the village who visit to collect honey.","hero":{"who":"Olin Stair","why":"The Thawborn heretic who carried his sister down the Glass Stair. She carried Peigi down from the north fold at eleven, through an ash-fall, and she reckons Olin had it easy, because ice doesn't burn."},"band":{"act":"Sabra Hearth","realm":"Flameborne","why":"The pop star who refused to kneel to the King at a festival. Nainsi, seventy, sings her songs at full volume up at the fold to frighten off the lions, and claims it works.","rv_vote":"Sabra Hearth, and she'll tell the King himself at First Coal if he asks, which he won't."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King his silence about the Ash Lions: Larkhollow's pact never to hunt them isn't the King's law, and he should say so, or say it is.","edict":"Against, and she'd go to the riot if she could still walk that far; she'll shout at the Edict from the fold wall instead, which, she says, the Edict deserves."},"wants":"For Larkhollow to admit, just once, that the lions are a nuisance.","site":"cindermoot_vf1","town":"Wetherby","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:cindermoot_vf2":{"name":"Brisa Hale","age":28,"role":"village trader in honey, wax and lark-whistles; guides visitors up to look at the Ash Lions","look":"Tall, sun-browned, a mane of bleached hair she says is for the lions, a guide's staff with a lion-claw tied to it, bare arms crossed with fine bee-sting welts.","voice":"Bright, teasing, showman's patter that drops into dead seriousness the moment anyone steps off the path.","greet":"Warm hearth! Honey, wax, whistle or lions? Lions are extra. Lions are always extra.","lines":["Go up to the Mane Rocks at noon, quiet as you can. If the big female opens one eye at you, that's good luck. If she opens both, run.","Wetherby wants the lions hunted. We pay in honey for every sheep. Wetherby takes the honey and complains about it. Wetherby would complain about a lion that brought its own lunch.","Cindermoot couples come up to be frightened together. It works. I've had nine weddings out of it. Nobody invites the guide."],"about":"My parents guided lovers through the Bellows caves under Hearthmount for thirty years, and I grew up taking couples somewhere frightening so they'd hold hands. Now I do it with lions. I sell the honey too. The honey's better than the lions, but nobody pays to be scared of honey.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent; tour-guide Bridge with all the jokes in it"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few words; Stormreach visitors love the lions"}],"childhood":"Brisa grew up in the guide-house at the mouth of the Bellows, the wind-caves under Hearthmount where lovers go to hear each other over the roar of the caves breathing. Her parents took couples in by lamplight, told them the cave-stories, and left them in a hollow for an hour, and came back. Brisa was in the caves from the age of three, holding the spare lamp. She learned early that frightened people are honest people, and that couples leaving the caves always tipped more if they'd been really scared. At nine she got lost in the deep Bellows for a whole night, alone, with the wind screaming round her, and came out at dawn on the far side of the caldera, grinning, and asked her parents whether she could charge for it.","schooling":"The bakehouse school on Ovenrow in Hearthmount, under Mistress Gorse, who taught letters by making the children write on the flour-dusted tables with one finger; Brisa wrote jokes, and was made to knead them back in. The Lamp Test lit nothing. At fourteen she left school to guide in the Bellows full time with her parents.","history":"At twenty she came to Larkhollow for a Lark Morning with a boy she'd met in the caves, fell out with the boy and in love with the place, and stayed. She took work with the beekeepers, got stung so often that Nessa Rhoswell says she's the only one who swears more than her, and within a year noticed the Cindermoot couples coming up to stare at the Mane Rocks from the road. She started guiding them up, for a copper a head, and now runs six walks a day in the warm season. She has made the trader's stall her own, and sells honey, wax and lark-whistles from it, and the walk-tickets too. She has had three boyfriends and a girlfriend in Larkhollow and has stayed friends with all of them, which the village thinks is suspicious. She sleeps on the roof in summer so she can hear the lions moving on the rocks.","family":"Her parents still guiding in the Bellows at Hearthmount; a younger brother who became a Kiln Gates mason and thinks lions are a terrible idea.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend","why":"Warden of Skyrend, thirty-one, shouting at the whole Council against the Edict. Brisa is twenty-eight and has never been afraid of a lion, but she'd like to be as unafraid of a room full of people."},"band":{"act":"Updraft","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Trance, 'Freefall'; she plays it on a little music-box at the top of the lion walk and says it's the sound of the moment the big female opens her eye.","rv_vote":"Updraft, in the Sparkbeat Cup and the main vote, because Stormreach never wins anything except shouting matches."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King Wetherby's petition to make the Ash Lions huntable, which she's heard is going up to the Hearth Court this season.","edict":"Against; she'll go to the riot with a lion-claw staff, and she's told her customers she'll guide anyone to it for free."},"wants":"For the big female to live another ten years, so she can keep walking people up to see her.","site":"cindermoot_vf2","town":"Larkhollow","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:cindermoot_vf2":{"name":"Teodor Pennick","age":46,"role":"village smith: hive-frames, smoker-cans, honey-presses; once a Kiln Gates mason","look":"Very tall, long-limbed, stiff in the lower back, close-cropped grey-black beard, a bee always somewhere on him, which he lets be.","voice":"Slow and kind, measured words, the faint mason's habit of calling every job 'a lift'.","greet":"Warm hearth. Watch your step, there's a swarm in the woodpile. They're not angry. They're just busy. Like me.","lines":["Wetherby wants the lions hunted. I understand them. I don't agree with them. Both of those can be true at once, which nobody in Flameborne wants to hear.","My wife's from Spiritveil. She says the honey here is the best in the Nine. She's been alive two hundred years, so she'd know.","I hauled stone at the Kiln Gates. Seven years. I hurt my back and came here for the warm ground. I'm still here. The ground's still warm."],"about":"I was a mason. I hauled basalt for the Kiln Gates in Hearthmount until my back went, and I came to Larkhollow because the warm ground helps it. Now I make hive-frames and smoker-cans and the presses for the honey. My wife is from Spiritveil and will outlive me by a few hundred years. We try not to talk about it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"getting better; his wife's tongue, and their daughter's"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent; mason's Bridge from the Kiln Gates crews"}],"childhood":"Teodor was a mason's son from the terraces of Hearthmount, where the masons' families live together in a stone court and share an oven. He was tall early and strong early and spent his boyhood being asked to carry things, which he liked; he was a gentle boy who never fought because he was afraid of what he'd do if he did. At eight his Lamp Test lit the Ember lamp with a faint warmth, a Half-Spark, and his father, who was afraid of sparks, made him promise never to use it. Teodor kept the promise. He spent his evenings at the mason's court oven, listening to the older men talk about the stone they'd hauled and whose name was cut where, and wanted nothing in the world but to carry stone.","schooling":"The masons' court school in Hearthmount, held round the shared oven by an old mason, Iorwen Sett, who was half Stonehold and taught the boys to lift with their legs and to write their names in stone-cutter's letters before pen letters. The Half-Spark wasn't enough for Korrhaven. Teodor went to the Kiln Gates as a hauler at fourteen.","history":"He hauled basalt for the Kiln Gates crews for seven years and liked every day of it, in a crew that once worked a whole season alongside the King, who hauled stone with his masons as Kings do. At twenty-one Teodor went on the Pillar Road crews into Lightforge to cut lime for the Unshadowing, fell ill with fever in a Rim town, and was nursed through it for a month by Penumbrists who kept a shaded room for the sick when Lightforge law said every room must be lit. At twenty-two he went back to the Kiln Gates and, at twenty-four, a basalt block slipped and broke his lower back. He came to Larkhollow to lie on the warm ground. Ilythe Saelwen, a Spiritveil honey-buyer from the Copying Rooms who comes every year for the fireweed honey, found him lying in a field and asked him if he was dead. He said no. She came back the next year to check. They married at Lark Morning, twenty years ago, and have a daughter, Liss, who is nineteen and will look nineteen for a long time.","family":"His wife Ilythe, about two hundred years old, looks thirty; their daughter Liss, nineteen; his father, a Hearthmount mason, who still won't talk about sparks.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim, the first Penumbrist","why":"'Mercy needs shade.' A Penumbrist woman in a Rim town said it to him when he was feverish in a dark room that the law said had to be lit, and he lived, and he's never forgotten it."},"band":{"act":"Willowroot","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Amapiano played on willow harps; Ilythe plays it in the evenings, and he's learned to dance to it slowly, because of his back, and she says he's the only man in two hundred years who's danced to it correctly.","rv_vote":"Willowroot, and Ilythe's lamp goes the same way, which is the only thing they've ever voted the same on."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's ruling that his daughter, being half Spiritveil, must take the Lamp Test again at Hearthmount every ten years to prove she isn't hiding a spark.","edict":"Against, because it outlaws the Penumbrists who saved his life, and he'll say so to anyone; he can't riot, with his back, so he'll make smoke-pots for the rioters' eyes."},"wants":"For Ilythe to keep a lamp for him in the Archives when he's gone, and to light it now and then.","site":"cindermoot_vf2","town":"Larkhollow","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cindermoot_vf2":{"name":"Ciaran Mote","age":24,"role":"lion-watcher and sketcher; sells his drawings to Spiritveil copyists","look":"Pale for a Flameborne man, painfully thin, charcoal under every fingernail, a battered sketch-board on a strap and a wide-brimmed felt hat for the rocks.","voice":"Soft, dreamy, distracted; forgets what he was saying halfway through and finishes a different sentence.","greet":"Warm hearth. Sorry. I was drawing. The big female's just shifted her paw. Don't go up yet. Give her a minute.","lines":["Brisa says if the big female opens one eye at you it's luck. I've been up there two hundred days. She's opened her eye at me twice. I don't feel especially lucky.","Wetherby wants the lions hunted. I've drawn every lion on that ridge. I know their names. I gave them names. Don't tell Wetherby.","I went to see Mirel of the Last Lamp in Lanthys Vale. They burn one memory from the crowd at every show. I lost one. I don't know what it was. That's the point."],"about":"I draw the Ash Lions. I've been drawing them for six years. I sell the drawings to the Copying Rooms in Spiritveil, where they make books of them. Nobody in Larkhollow thinks it's a real job. My mother thinks it's a phase. I'm twenty-four.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, soft"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"halting, learned from Teodor Pennick's wife Ilythe so he could talk to the copyists"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Ciaran was the only son of a beekeeping family, and a disappointment as a beekeeper: he forgot the smoker, he forgot the hive lids, he forgot to come home, and he spent his childhood lying in the warm grass at the bottom of the Mane Rocks drawing larks on any surface he could find. At seven he climbed the rocks alone to draw a lion cub he'd seen and was found asleep against the warm stone two yards from the big female, who was watching him. His mother had hysterics. His father beat the drum all the way up the ridge to frighten the lions off. The lion did not move. Ciaran still has the drawing, which is very bad, and which he says is the best thing he's ever done.","schooling":"The Little Ring moot-school at Cindermoot, where he lost every argument because he was drawing the other children instead of listening, and Master Corwen Spill confiscated his chalk and gave him pebbles instead, and he drew with pebbles. The Lamp Test lit nothing. He left at twelve and nobody tried to stop him because everyone thought he would come to beekeeping in time.","history":"He did not come to beekeeping. At eighteen he spent a whole summer on the Mane Rocks, drawing the pride from dawn to dusk, and came down with a hundred and twenty sheets. Teodor Pennick's wife, Ilythe, saw them and took six to the Copying Rooms in Lanthys Vale, and the copyists, who never see an Ash Lion in Spiritveil, paid more for those six than his parents made from the hives that month. He has been drawing for them since. He knows the pride: eleven lions, three males, the big female, called Ash by everyone and Grandmother Redmouth by him. He has named all of them and drawn each a hundred times. Two years ago he went to Lanthys Vale for the first time to deliver his drawings, and went to see Mirel of the Last Lamp, and the show took a memory from him, and he came home and his mother says he has been sadder since and doesn't know why. He doesn't know either.","family":"His parents, beekeepers, disappointed and proud; three younger sisters, all beekeepers, who think he's lazy; no partner, though he keeps drawing Brisa Hale and not showing her.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"The Spiritveil copyist who made 'The Lamp That Leaned', the most copied picture in the Archives; Ciaran's drawings are copied in the same rooms and he'd like one, just one, to last that long."},"band":{"act":"Mirel of the Last Lamp","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Symphonic rock, 'Ghostlight of the Forgotten Realm'; he went to a show and lost a memory he can't name, and he goes on listening to them as if the song might tell him what it was.","rv_vote":"Mirel of the Last Lamp, because they owe him something, and maybe the lamp will make them give it back."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King nothing he can think of, and then think of something on the walk home, and be annoyed.","edict":"Against, in a vague way, because he can't imagine Lightforge wanting a list of Ash Lions, and everything else seems to him a bit like a list of Ash Lions."},"wants":"To know which memory he lost.","site":"cindermoot_vf2","town":"Larkhollow","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:cindermoot_vf2:b":{"name":"Wynne Furze","age":77,"role":"maker of the lark-whistles every Larkhollow child is given at birth","look":"Little and wrinkled as a dried apple, white hair in a knot stuck through with a clay pin, clay to the elbows every day, a whistle of every size hung from her apron on a string.","voice":"Chirpy, quick, a habit of humming instead of finishing sentences; laughs like a lark rising.","greet":"Warm hearth! Mind the drying shelf. Every whistle on it is somebody's. Born this year. That one's a girl. That one's twins. Don't touch the twins.","lines":["Lark Morning's in spring. The whole village goes out before dawn and whistles back at the larks, badly. The larks don't mind. I make every whistle, and I've never heard a good one.","Wetherby wants the lions hunted. Wetherby has never once in seventy years sent me a lamb for my whistles. Larkhollow children whistle. Wetherby children shout.","I keep a book of every whistle I've made. Every child born here for fifty-one years. Every note. If you're from Larkhollow I can tell you your note. You probably won't like it."],"about":"I make the lark-whistles. Every child born in Larkhollow gets one, little clay larks that whistle a note, and I've made every one for fifty-one years. I've got a book of every child and every note. I'm seventy-seven. I've never been able to whistle.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, chirpy"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, mostly prices"}],"childhood":"Wynne grew up in Larkhollow in a potter's house at the edge of the bowl, the fourth of seven children of a potter who made honey-jars and coal-cake dishes. She was a tiny, noisy child who followed the larks around in spring and tried to whistle back at them, and couldn't; she tried for years, and her lips simply wouldn't do it. The whole village teased her. At eight, frustrated, she took a lump of her father's clay, shaped it into a fat lark with a hole in its tail, and blew into it, and it whistled. She ran through the whole village blowing it. The old whistle-maker, Gwenllian Ash, whose whistles were all cracked, came out of her house, looked at Wynne's lark, and said, 'Right. You'll do.'","schooling":"She walked to Cindermoot for a year of the Little Ring and was sent home for drawing birds on the chalk circle. After that, Gwenllian Ash's kiln was her school: Gwenllian taught her letters by the names scratched under every whistle, and to tune clay by ear, a note per child, no two the same in the same year. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She took over Gwenllian's kiln at twenty-six.","history":"She has made every child's whistle in Larkhollow for fifty-one years, and she keeps a book: the name, the date, the note, and the colour of the glaze. She can play the whole village's births back in order on a row of whistles, which she does at Lark Morning, and people weep and pretend it's the dawn cold. She married Bedwyr Furze, a quiet beekeeper, at twenty-nine, and they had two children, and he died at sixty of a fall from the hive-ladders, and she went on making whistles the next morning because there was a baby due. Her children are a beekeeper and a potter. Her grandson, aged fourteen, is learning the kiln and can whistle beautifully, which she says is unbearable. She has sold whistles as far as Meridian and Lanthys Vale, but the birth-whistles are never sold; they are given.","family":"Her son Ianto, a beekeeper; her daughter Mererid, a potter in Redbank; her grandson Iolo, apprentice whistle-maker; her late husband Bedwyr.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"She lit the First Hearth on her own kitchen fire and never let anyone kneel to her; Wynne, who has made every child in her village their first gift in a little kiln behind her house, thinks Seryn was a woman who knew what a small fire is for."},"band":{"act":"The Tether Sisters","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Chain-yard harmony folk, 'Cut the Chain'; three voices that never sing the same note, which is how she tunes a year's whistles, and she plays their songs while she works.","rv_vote":"The Tether Sisters, because nobody ever gives a lamp to harmony, and harmony is the hardest thing in music."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's new birth-tally, which wants every village to send its births to Hearthmount on paper; her book is good enough and it's been good enough for fifty-one years.","edict":"Torn, which surprises people: she writes down every child born in Larkhollow already, and it's never hurt any of them, but she writes their note, not their spark, and she doesn't know if Lightforge knows the difference."},"wants":"To hear one of her whistles played well, once, by someone who isn't her grandson.","site":"cindermoot_vf2","town":"Larkhollow","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:smeltgate_vf1":{"name":"Gareth Penhallow","age":58,"role":"village trader in copperware, kit and ore-contracts; a digger for thirty years","look":"Barrel-chested, bald, a neat white beard, both hands green to the wrist like old bronze, and a Lightforge-style Day-Book sticking out of his apron pocket.","voice":"Careful, reasonable, a little pompous, explains things in numbered points, and is hurt when people shout at him, which they do.","greet":"Warm hearth. Pans on the left, kettles on the right, kit at the back. Prices are written down. Everything here is written down. It saves arguments.","lines":["Elmstead's gone soft on its trees again. We'll have no charcoal by midwinter. I've written to them. Three times. In ink.","Smeltgate's guild still wants the diggings. If we had our boundaries written in Hearth Court law, plainly, they'd stop asking. Nobody here agrees with me.","I'm for the Edict. Yes, really. I've been shouted at in four villages for it. I'd like to explain why, if you'll let me get to point two."],"about":"I dug copper for thirty years and I've sold it for fifteen, and I've learned that the people who get cheated are the ones who don't write things down. I keep a Day-Book, like a Lightforge man. I'm the only one in Copperfield for the Edict. I'm used to it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, contract Bridge"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"fair; reads and writes it, speaks it stiffly"}],"childhood":"Gareth was the eldest son of a Copperfield digger family that had once owned a stretch of the diggings and lost it, two generations back, on a handshake deal with a Smeltgate smelter that nobody wrote down. His grandfather told the story every winter as a warning. Gareth grew up digging green earth in the open pits beside his father and brothers, and he was the boy who counted the carts and kept tally on a slate when nobody had asked him to. He was teased for it, and kept doing it. At ten his tally caught a Smeltgate carter short-weighing three loads in a row, and the village got its copper back, and the old diggers bought him a cup of cinder-wine, which made him sick. He remembers that as his first victory.","schooling":"The Copperfield bakehouse, which bakes in copper pans, under Baker Hafren Coombe, who taught sums by weighing dough and made pupils write every loaf's weight in a book; Gareth kept the book better than she did. The Lamp Test lit nothing. He went down the diggings at thirteen.","history":"He dug for thirty years and was never more than an ordinary digger, but he kept the village's ore-tallies the whole time, unpaid, and the guild learned that Copperfield's carts were never short and never let them be. At forty-three his lungs began to fail from the green dust, and he came up out of the pits and took over the trader's stall from a cousin. He sells copper pans, kettles and still-coils, road kit, and handles the village's ore-contracts with the Smeltgate guild, every line of which he writes out twice. Twelve years ago a Lightforge assay-reader visiting Smeltgate gave him a Day-Book as a joke, and he kept it, and has filled one every year since. He has followed the Edict debate in every broadsheet he can buy and believes, unpopularly, that a written register is a protection for small people against large ones. He married Nest, a coppersmith's widow, at thirty; they have no children of their own and raised her son, who is now a still-mender in Hearthmount and says Gareth is the most honest bore he has ever met.","family":"His wife Nest; his stepson Dyfan; his younger brother Huw, married to the Wetherby felter Ula Feltwright.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand, the Lawbringer","why":"He wrote a law down plainly and put his name to it. Gareth's family lost its diggings because nobody wrote anything down, and he admires anyone who'd rather be hated for a written thing than liked for a spoken one."},"band":{"act":"The Noon Chorale","realm":"Lightforge","why":"The most decorated act in the contest, and everyone's sick of them; he isn't. He likes that every voice is exactly where it's supposed to be.","rv_vote":"The Noon Chorale, and he'll write it in his Day-Book, and the whole village will know by supper."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the old handshake grant that lets Smeltgate's guild 'first claim' on Copperfield ore, and he'll bring a written copy of the refusal for the King to keep.","edict":"For. He thinks Flameborne's anger at being written down is a luxury of people who have never been robbed for lack of a page, and he has said this aloud in Smeltgate and been thrown out of an inn."},"wants":"To see Copperfield's diggings written into Hearth Court law, plainly, in his lifetime.","site":"smeltgate_vf1","town":"Copperfield","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:smeltgate_vf1":{"name":"Morag Kettleby","age":37,"role":"coppersmith: still-coils, pans and kettles; once Ewan Greenhand's apprentice, now his rival","look":"Short, quick, bright copper-red hair she claims is natural, green to the knuckles, a still-coil worn as a bracelet, and a burn-scar on her lip from tasting wine too hot.","voice":"Rapid, mischievous, mimics anyone she's talking about; taps everything she touches to hear what it rings.","greet":"Warm hearth! Give it here. Don't tell me what's wrong with it. Let me tap it. Ah. Oh dear. Oh, you've been running plum through this, haven't you.","lines":["Ewan Greenhand can tap a coil and tell you if the wine through it was any good. I can tell you who drank it and what they argued about.","Elmstead's gone soft on its trees again. No charcoal by midwinter. I'll be beating copper by hand over a cooking fire like it's the old days. The old days were rubbish.","Jam from an iron pot tastes of argument. My mother's jam tasted of argument. She made it in copper. It was just her."],"about":"I make stills. Pans and kettles too, but stills are the art. I learned from Ewan Greenhand, the best ear in Flameborne, and now he sends me the jobs he can't hear right, and won't admit it. I'm better than him. Don't tell him I said so. He knows.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, fast"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent; still-buyers come from every Realm"}],"childhood":"Morag's parents were cinder-wine distillers in a Coal Road village, famous for their wine and for drinking most of it, and her childhood was cheerful chaos: the still-house on fire twice, her father asleep in the barrel-store, her mother singing bawdy songs at midnight. Morag, the eldest of four, ran the still from age nine because somebody had to. She learned to tell when a coil was clogging by the sound of the drip and to tell when her father had been at the barrel by the sound of the door. When the still-coil cracked for the third time in a season, she walked it to Copperfield to be mended, aged thirteen, alone, and Ewan Greenhand tapped it and said, 'Who's been looking after this?' and she said, 'Me,' and he said, 'Then you'd better learn to fix it.'","schooling":"Almost none: the Coal Road bakehouse took her for a winter and she spent it asleep, having been up all night with the still. Her Lamp Test showed a faint warmth in the Ember lamp, a Half-Spark, and her parents celebrated with so much cinder-wine they forgot to ask what it meant. Her real school was Ewan Greenhand's forge, from fourteen to twenty-five.","history":"Ewan taught her coppersmithing and the art of tapping a coil to read what had run through it, and found within three years that her ear was better than his, which he took well for a month and then badly for ten years. At twenty-five she set up her own forge at the other end of Copperfield and the village divided, gently, into Greenhand stills and Kettleby stills, and argues about it at every market. She married a Copperfield digger, Alun Kettleby, at twenty-eight, and took his name because she said hers was too dull for a still-mark; he's quiet and patient and the only person who can make her sit down. They have one son, who is eight and tone-deaf, to her lasting sorrow. Her parents still distil on the Coal Road, slightly more soberly; she mends their still for free and drinks their wine at a discount.","family":"Her husband Alun, a digger; their son Cai, eight; her parents and three younger siblings on the Coal Road; Ewan Greenhand, her old master, whom she visits every rest-day to argue with.","hero":{"who":"Ewan Greenhand","why":"He tapped her family's cracked still when she was thirteen and saw who'd been looking after it, and nobody else ever had. She'd never say so to his face; she argues with him instead, which is the same thing in Flameborne."},"band":{"act":"Rivet Gun","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Amapiano built on rivet-hammering; she says it's the only music made by people who understand that metal has a note, and she taps along on her coils.","rv_vote":"Rivet Gun, because Metalcrown's never had a lamp from Lightforge and she'd like them to have at least one from Copperfield."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's new excise on still-coils, which taxes the copper twice: once as ore, once as a still.","edict":"Against, and she'll go to the riot if it's a big one, with a copper kettle to bang, because she says it'll be louder than a drum."},"wants":"For Ewan Greenhand to say out loud, once, before he dies, that she's better than him.","site":"smeltgate_vf1","town":"Copperfield","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:smeltgate_vf1":{"name":"Senna Doorward","age":26,"role":"copper-digger; newly married to an Elmstead charcoal-burner","look":"Broad-shouldered, cropped brown hair, green palms and black fingertips (copper from home, charcoal from her husband), a new marriage-chain of copper and elm-wood beads.","voice":"Steady and practical, says less than she means, listens with her head tilted; a dry humour that sneaks up on people.","greet":"Warm hearth. You're from Elmstead? No? Good. I mean, nothing against Elmstead. I married it.","lines":["Elmstead's gone soft on its trees again, says everyone in Copperfield. Elmstead says Copperfield can grow its own trees. My husband says nothing. I say nothing. We eat supper.","When a couple sets up house here they press green palms on their door. I pressed mine. Jem pressed his. His came out black. It's the only door in Copperfield with a black hand on it.","Don't touch the Green Pool. Everyone's dared to. I did, at nine. My fingers were green for a year. Nothing else happened. I was disappointed."],"about":"I dig copper, like everyone in my family, and last spring I married Jem Clampwright from Elmstead, who burns charcoal for the clamps, and that's made me the most interesting woman in two villages for the wrong reasons. We live at the edge of Copperfield, on the road to Elmstead. That's on purpose.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Senna was a digger's daughter, the middle of seven, in a house whose door has four generations of green handprints on it, her parents' pressed over her grandparents'. She grew up in the pits from the age of five, carrying baskets of green earth up the ladders, and was strong and quiet and never complained, which her mother found unnerving. At nine she was dared to touch the Green Pool, and did, and held her hand under for a count of twenty, and her fingers stayed green for a year and she was the most respected child in Copperfield. At twelve she met Jem Clampwright at the Smeltgate market, where the two villages shout at each other about charcoal; he was throwing elm-nuts at Copperfield children and she threw one back and hit him in the eye. They didn't speak again for nine years.","schooling":"The Copperfield bakehouse under Baker Hafren Coombe, who weighed every loaf; Senna's were always exactly right and always slightly burnt. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She went down the pits full time at thirteen, at her First No, where she refused, famously, to be called 'the quiet one' ever again. Nobody listened. She's still the quiet one.","history":"She dug for her family for eight years and was good at it, steady and safe, and had no particular plans. At twenty-one, at the Smeltgate market, during the annual charcoal shouting, she was standing silent at the back while both villages screamed, and noticed one other person not shouting: Jem Clampwright, an elm-nut scar under his left eye. They looked at each other across the market. After that they met at the halfway stone on the Elmstead road every rest-day for four years and didn't tell anyone, which in Flameborne is an achievement. They married last spring, at First Coal, and both villages were outraged and came to the wedding. They built a house on the road between, and pressed their palms on the door, his black, hers green. Her family won't visit; his mother, Neve, comes every week and brings smoked mutton and complains about Copperfield, then stays for supper. Senna still digs; Jem still burns. They don't talk about charcoal at home.","family":"Her parents and six siblings in Copperfield, who have not yet come to supper; her husband Jem Clampwright; her mother-in-law Neve Clampwright, who has.","hero":{"who":"Vaelora Lanthys","why":"The Spiritveil councillor who 'chose silence'. In two villages that never stop shouting about charcoal, Senna has chosen silence every market-day for five years, and she thinks it's the only reason she has a husband."},"band":{"act":"Echo Bleed","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A DJ sampling dead singers, 'Somebody Else's Summer'; she and Jem danced to it at the halfway stone the night he asked her, and they don't know any of the dead singers, and they don't care.","rv_vote":"Echo Bleed, and Jem's lamp too, which he'll forget to give until she reminds him."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King nothing aloud, but if she has to, she'll refuse his heralds calling the charcoal quarrel 'a village matter'; it isn't, it's a marriage matter now.","edict":"Against, quietly. She'll stay home if there's a riot and keep the house on the road open, so that anyone from either village running from it has somewhere to stop."},"wants":"For her mother to come to supper at the house with the black-and-green door.","site":"smeltgate_vf1","town":"Copperfield","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:smeltgate_vf1:b":{"name":"Iorwerth Digg","age":90,"role":"retired copper-digger; the oldest man in Copperfield, green to the elbow","look":"Thin as a rake-handle, stooped, a few strands of white hair, both arms green from fingertip to elbow like a statue left out in the rain.","voice":"Slow, wheezing, a lot of silence between short sentences, then a dirty laugh that turns into a cough.","greet":"Warm hearth. Sit down. I can't stand up. Well, I can. I'd rather you sat.","lines":["Smeltgate's guild wanted to buy the diggings. I was there at the Refusal. I was twenty-two. I said no so loud the King's steward dropped his pen.","Elmstead's gone soft on its trees again. They were soft on them when I was a boy. We had charcoal then. We'll have charcoal now. Somebody always gives in by midwinter.","Don't touch the Green Pool. I did. Every child does. I'm green to the elbow. That wasn't the Pool. That was seventy years. Same colour."],"about":"I dug copper here from the age of nine to the age of seventy-one. I've been green to the elbow longer than most people in this village have been alive. I dug out three men from a collapse once. I've outlived two wives. I'm ninety. I'm ready, but I'm not in a hurry.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, old pit-Kindle"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a little, learned from a Stonehold digger on his first crew; 'heavy is honest' and swearing"}],"childhood":"Iorwerth was a pit-child in the days when Copperfield's diggings were deeper and more dangerous, before the Green Pool flooded the old workings. He went down the ladder at nine with his father and uncles and loved it from the first day: the dark, the green, the smell of wet copper earth, the songs the diggers sang to keep time with their picks. He was small and quick and was sent into the narrow seams the men couldn't reach, with a lamp tied to his head and a rope round his waist. At eleven he crawled into a side-seam and found a vein of copper so pure it glowed in his lamp, and dragged out a lump of it the size of his head, and his father had it beaten into a kettle that still hangs in Iorwerth's house.","schooling":"None at all; in his boyhood the bakehouse took Copperfield children only if they could be spared from the pits, and Iorwerth couldn't. He learned his letters at fifty from his second wife, Gwenda, and reads slowly, aloud, with his finger, and enjoys it enormously. The Lamp Test lit nothing.","history":"He dug for sixty-two years. At twenty-two, when Smeltgate's guild offered to buy the diggings outright, he went with the village to the Refusal at First Coal and shouted 'No' so loudly, by his own account, that the royal steward dropped his pen. At thirty, in the old deep workings, a seam collapsed on three of his crew, and Iorwerth and two others dug them out over two days, by hand; all three were dead, and Iorwerth carried each of them up the ladder himself, one at a time. A year later the deep workings flooded green and became the Pool, and nobody has dug there since. He married twice, once at twenty-four to Nerys, who died young, and once at forty to Gwenda, who taught him to read and died at eighty. He has four children, twelve grandchildren and great-grandchildren he can't count. He sits outside his house all day now, on a copper-plated bench, and tells children not to touch the Pool, knowing they will.","family":"Four children, many grandchildren and great-grandchildren; two wives buried under green-stained stones; his father's copper kettle on the hearth.","hero":{"who":"Saelen of the Drift, the Lantern of the Dead","why":"A Spiritveil copper-buyer told him the story once: Saelen carried the lanterns of the dead across the black lake. Iorwerth carried three men up a ladder from the dark when he was thirty, and he's been thinking about Saelen ever since."},"band":{"act":"Deep Hold","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Slow heavy-flow rap, 'Ask who's asking'; his great-grandson played it to him as a joke and he loved it, because it's slow and heavy like pick-work, and he nods to it on the bench.","rv_vote":"Deep Hold, because his great-grandson will be appalled, and because it's the only music that sounds like the pits."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the same thing he refused at twenty-two: any sale of the diggings, to anyone. He's never had to refuse it again. He'll go anyway, to be sure.","edict":"Against, because it's Lightforge and he's Flameborne and he's ninety, and he doesn't need a better reason; he's too old to riot but he'll shout from the bench."},"wants":"To be buried with his father's copper kettle, and not in the Green Pool.","site":"smeltgate_vf1","town":"Copperfield","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:smeltgate_vf2":{"name":"Aled Ringwood","age":41,"role":"village trader in kit, smoked mutton and elm-wood; makes coffin-boards on the side","look":"Medium everything: medium height, medium brown hair, a medium beard he trims every rest-day; smells faintly and permanently of smoked mutton.","voice":"Mild, pleasant, a little apologetic; knows he's dull and makes small jokes about it before anyone else can.","greet":"Warm hearth. I'm Aled. I sell things. That's about it, really. Mutton's good, though. The mutton's very good.","lines":["Copperfield wants more charcoal. Copperfield always wants more charcoal. I'd say that's the most exciting thing in Elmstead, and it is.","I make coffin-boards from elm. Lovely wood, elm. Doesn't split. Sorry, that's a bit grim. People do ask.","Somebody, the pop star, nobody knows who she is. I've got a theory. I've got eleven theories. My wife says that's the most interesting thing about me."],"about":"I run the stall. I sell kit and smoked mutton and elm-boards for wheel-hubs and coffins. I've never been further than Smeltgate. I've never been in a duel, never been in love before my wife, never had an adventure. I'm all right with it. Mostly.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, mild"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"enough to sell mutton; he practises from a phrase-book and never gets past the bit about the weather"}],"childhood":"Aled was the only child of the Elmstead trader before him, a cheerful, ordinary man named Dai Ringwood, and a cheerful, ordinary mother who kept hens. Nothing much happened to him. He was neither clever nor slow, neither strong nor weak, neither popular nor teased, and he had a happy childhood and remembers very little of it, which he says is the sign of a happy childhood. He was fifteen the summer of the burning and was sent down to Copperfield in a cart with the hens and the little ones, and on the road a Copperfield stranger gave him a honey cake. He thinks of that honey cake surprisingly often. At ten he discovered that he liked the smell of elm-sawdust better than anything else in the world, and he still does.","schooling":"He walked to Smeltgate's bakehouse two days a week with the other Elmstead children, where Baker Ciara Malley taught letters, sums and slapbread, and wrote on his slate at the end of his last year: 'A pleasant boy. Does what he's told.' He has the slate still and says it's the best summary of him anyone's managed. The Lamp Test lit nothing.","history":"He went to work at his father's stall at fourteen and took it over at twenty-nine when his father's knees failed. He has sold kit, smoked mutton and elm-wood from the same stall for twelve years, and nothing has gone wrong in all that time except once, when a cart of mutton went off on the road to Smeltgate, which the village still mentions. At twenty-five, an elm-wright taught him to cut coffin-boards, and he discovered that he was good at it and that grieving people find him comforting, because he is so calm and so ordinary. He married Elin Dovey, a Copperfield digger's widow seven years older than him, at thirty-two; she has three grown children who like him, and she says marrying him was like getting into a warm bath after a lot of cold weather. He is fascinated by mysteries, because his own life has none; he reads every broadsheet that mentions the Lost Warden and has eleven theories about the pop star Somebody, none of which he will say aloud in case one's right.","family":"His wife Elin and her three grown children; his parents, retired, who live behind the stall and give opinions on every sale.","hero":{"who":"The Lost Warden","why":"Nobody knows who the Lost Warden was. Aled, who has been exactly who everyone expected his whole life, finds that the most exciting thing he's ever heard."},"band":{"act":"Somebody","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"An anonymous pop star nobody has ever identified; he loves the songs, but mostly he loves not knowing, and he keeps a notebook of clues.","rv_vote":"Somebody, because they'll come second on purpose, and he'd like to be part of the reason."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King nothing; he's never refused anyone anything since his First No, when he refused to eat turnips, and he's kept to it.","edict":"Torn. He can see Gareth Penhallow's point about writing things down, and he can see everyone else's point about not, and he'd like to stay at the stall while they argue."},"wants":"To have one adventure before he's fifty, a small one, with a good story at the end.","site":"smeltgate_vf2","town":"Elmstead","realm":"Flameborne"},"smith:smeltgate_vf2":{"name":"Lowri Felling","age":52,"role":"village smith and axe-maker; the one Elmstead voice that wants the grove cut","look":"Tall, lean, black hair cut short with grey at the brow, a long old burn-scar from the 1,170 fire up one forearm, an axe on her back she never uses on trees.","voice":"Cold and precise, the shortest sentences in Elmstead; never shouts; says the unpopular thing once and lets it sit.","greet":"Warm hearth. Axes, hatchets, wedges. I make good axes. Nobody here uses them. Buy one.","lines":["Copperfield wants more charcoal. Copperfield's right. Elmstead won't say so. I will.","Plant two for each tree felled. Forty years to grow. A child doesn't have forty years in a cold winter.","The Grandmother's a fine tree. I'd still cut her. Don't look at me like that. Everyone looks at me like that."],"about":"I make axes for a village that won't cut its trees. I was in the burning in 1,170; I've got the scar to show for it. I think Elmstead loves its trees more than it loves its neighbours, and I've said so, and people stopped coming to my forge for a year. They came back. I make the only good axes.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, clipped"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good; she reads Concord broadsheets"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a little; she buys Metalcrown steel for her axe-edges"}],"childhood":"Lowri was a charcoal-burner's daughter, the eldest of three, in a family that lived by the clamps and loved the elms the way all Elmstead does. She was clever, solemn and unsentimental, and the only child in the village who asked why the first families had carried saplings up in wet sacking instead of just buying charcoal from the March. Nobody gave her a good answer. At her First No they planted an elm for her, as is the custom, and she was expected to refuse something on its behalf every year after; at fourteen, at her first chance, she refused, on its behalf, the right to be sentimental about it. The village laughed. She was serious. She has refused the same thing every year since and her elm is the healthiest in the grove.","schooling":"Smeltgate's bakehouse, two days a week, under Baker Ciara Malley, who once found her calculating the village's charcoal yield per tree per decade on the back of a loaf-tally and pinned it on the wall. The Lamp Test lit nothing. At twelve she went to Elmstead's old smith, Peris Hatch, who made axes and hinges, and found she loved the axes.","history":"She was twenty-six in the burning of 1,170, when a clamp caught in a dry summer and the grove went up for two days; she was on the line with the soaked blankets and the burn on her arm is from a falling branch. She took over Peris Hatch's forge at thirty. Twelve years ago, in a hard midwinter, Elmstead held back charcoal from Copperfield over a quarrel about prices, Copperfield's forges and stills went cold, and the healer there could not keep water boiling for a feverish family; a girl of six died. Lowri knew the girl; she had sold the family an axe. She has never forgiven Elmstead for it and has said so at every market since. She makes the best axes on the east road and sells most of them to Copperfield and Smeltgate. She has never married; she lives with her younger brother Huw Felling, a charcoal-burner who disagrees with her about everything and cooks for her every night.","family":"Her brother Huw, who cooks; a younger sister in Hearthmount; the family of the Copperfield girl, whose grave she visits at Slag Day.","hero":{"who":"The Ninth Dominus of Metalcrown","why":"'Inefficiency kills children. This can be corrected.' She has never been to Metalcrown and would not want his life, but she heard that line in a broadsheet and has thought of the Copperfield girl every time since."},"band":{"act":"Verdict","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Symphonic metal structured as trials, with evidence and a verdict in every song; she likes music where somebody is finally held to account.","rv_vote":"Verdict, though she knows Lightforge wins too much, because they're the only act whose songs end with an answer."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's old ruling that leaves charcoal sales between villages to 'local custom', which is how a child died; she wants a law.","edict":"Torn. She hates that Lightforge is doing it, and she can't help agreeing that someone should be made to write down what they do and answer for it."},"wants":"For Elmstead to admit, once, aloud, what happened in that midwinter.","site":"smeltgate_vf2","town":"Elmstead","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:smeltgate_vf2":{"name":"Jem Clampwright","age":26,"role":"charcoal-burner; the clamp-master's son; married to a Copperfield digger","look":"Wiry, soot-dark, an elm-nut scar under his left eye, black hands to the wrist, and a marriage-chain of elm and copper beads that matches his wife's.","voice":"Easy-going, funny, deflects everything with a joke; goes quiet and serious only when he talks about Senna.","greet":"Warm hearth! Don't stand downwind of the clamps, you'll smell like mutton for a week. Upwind you'll just smell like me.","lines":["Copperfield wants more charcoal. I'm married to Copperfield. You can imagine supper.","My mam sleeps by the clamps when they burn. She can smell a bad burn from the far end of the village. I can smell it from about here. I've got a better nose for supper.","There's a door at the edge of Copperfield with one green hand and one black hand on it. That's my door. The only door in Copperfield I'm allowed to knock on."],"about":"I'm Jem, Neve Clampwright's son. I burn charcoal. I married Senna Doorward from Copperfield last spring, which both villages took as a declaration of war, and then came to the wedding and ate everything. I make jokes. It's what I'm for.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"one song, learned from a Seismic song-sheet; he doesn't know what it means"}],"childhood":"Jem was born in the summer of the burning, in the Concord's 1,170th year, in the middle of it, more or less: his mother Neve went into labour on the second day of the fire, while the village was hanging wet blankets in the Grandmother, and gave birth in a cart on the road to Copperfield. He grew up hearing that story at every Elmstead feast. He was a laughing, mischievous child who climbed every tree in the grove except the Grandmother, who he was forbidden to climb, and threw elm-nuts at Copperfield children at the Smeltgate market every season until a Copperfield girl threw one back and hit him under the eye. He has the scar. He fell in love with the girl about nine years later and says he was just slow.","schooling":"Smeltgate's bakehouse two days a week under Baker Ciara Malley, where he was the class clown and was made to stand on the flour-bin; he made the other children laugh from the flour-bin. The Lamp Test lit nothing. He went to the clamps with his mother at twelve.","history":"He has burned charcoal with Neve for fourteen years and is good at it, though not as good as her; nobody is as good as Neve, who can tell a clamp's temperature through the turf by laying her cheek on it. At twenty-one, at the annual charcoal shouting at the Smeltgate market, he noticed a Copperfield woman standing silent at the back, the girl who'd hit him with the elm-nut; she noticed him not shouting too. They met at the halfway stone on the Elmstead road every rest-day for four years. They married at First Coal last spring. He and Senna have built a house on the road between the two villages, and he goes to the clamps every morning and back to her every night. Lately they have been lining two of his clamps with copper-slag Senna carries up from Copperfield, to see if it burns slower and hotter, and it does. He hasn't told his mother. He hasn't told anyone. He's been making a lot of jokes.","family":"His mother Neve Clampwright, clamp-master; his wife Senna Doorward; his father, a burner, who died of a cough when Jem was fifteen.","hero":{"who":"Neve Clampwright, his mother","why":"She gave birth to him in a cart in the middle of a fire and went back to the clamps the next month, and she's never once made a fuss about either. He makes a fuss about everything, so he thinks she's a wonder."},"band":{"act":"Seismic","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Bass music, 'Aftershock'; a Stonehold carter played it at the Smeltgate market the night he first spoke to Senna, and they stood by the cart feeling it come up through the ground.","rv_vote":"Seismic, in the Sparkbeat Cup and the main vote, and he's asked Senna to vote the same and she's said probably, which from her is yes."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal he'll refuse the King the Hearth Court's refusal to settle the charcoal prices between Copperfield and Elmstead; he's married to the quarrel and he'd like it to stop.","edict":"Against, mildly, with jokes; he'll go to the riot if Senna wants to, and she won't, so he won't."},"wants":"For his mother and his wife's mother to sit at his table at the same time and say one kind thing each.","site":"smeltgate_vf2","town":"Elmstead","realm":"Flameborne"},"vill:smeltgate_vf2:b":{"name":"Morfudd Ash","age":64,"role":"retired charcoal-burner; climbed the Grandmother with wet blankets in the burning; keeper of the Grandmother's coal-pot","look":"Short, stocky, white hair cropped close, both palms shiny and stiff with old burn-scars, a felted Wetherby cloak she wears even in summer.","voice":"Gruff, slow, says very little and then something very funny with a perfectly straight face.","greet":"Warm hearth. You're looking at the Grandmother. Everybody does. Don't climb her. I did. Look at my hands.","lines":["Copperfield wants more charcoal. Copperfield wanted more charcoal the week the grove burned. I said, come and get it then. They didn't laugh.","I hung the coal-pot on the Grandmother's lowest branch the morning after the fire. It's been there twenty-six years. Nobody's dared take it down. Nobody's dared look inside it either.","Lowri Felling wants the grove cut. Lowri's a good smith and a terrible guest. She was on the blanket line next to me. She's allowed."],"about":"I burned charcoal for forty years. In the burning I was one of the six who climbed the Grandmother with soaked blankets and hung them in her branches. My hands haven't bent right since. I keep the coal-pot on her branch. That's my job now. It's not much of a job. I like it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native, gruff"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Morfudd was the daughter of an elm-wright who made wheel-hubs and coffin-boards, and she grew up in his workshop among planks and shavings and the sweet bitter smell of elm. She was a quiet, sturdy child who climbed trees all day, every tree in the grove, including, once, the Grandmother, for which she was beaten by her father and secretly congratulated by her grandmother. At ten she fell out of an elm and broke an arm and climbed back up the same tree a month later with the arm still in splints, because she'd left her lunch on a branch. She was not afraid of heights or fire or her father. She was afraid of the dark, and is still, and keeps a lamp lit all night.","schooling":"The Smeltgate bakehouse two days a week, where Baker Ciara Malley's mother, then the baker, taught letters on elm-boards Morfudd's father sent as payment. Morfudd learned to write well and to bake badly. The Lamp Test lit nothing. She went to the clamps at thirteen instead of her father's workshop, because the clamps were outdoors.","history":"She burned charcoal for forty years and was a good, steady clamp-hand, never a clamp-master. In the dry summer of 1,170, at thirty-eight, she was on the blanket line when the grove caught, and when the fire came for the Grandmother she and five others soaked blankets in the clamp-water butts and climbed the old tree and hung them in her branches, hour after hour, while the grove burned round them. Two of the six fell; one died. Morfudd's hands were burned to the bone on the hot bark. The Grandmother lived. The next morning, hands bandaged, Morfudd hung a clay coal-pot from the Grandmother's lowest branch with a coal from her own hearth inside it, as a Kept Coal for the tree, and the village has treated it as untouchable ever since. She married late, at forty-five, a widower named Cledwyn Ash, a burner with a dry wit to match hers; he died last year. She has no children of her own and claims forty or so anyway, by which she means every child in the village whose elm she has watered.","family":"Her late husband Cledwyn; two stepdaughters who visit; every child in Elmstead, as she counts it.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"Lyria's first lantern was a cracked clay cup. Morfudd's Kept Coal for the Grandmother is in a cracked clay pot. She heard the story from a Spiritveil honey-buyer and laughed for a week."},"band":{"act":"Paper Lantern Funeral","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Doom-gaze, very slow and very sad; she plays it on her late husband's music-box at night with her lamp lit and says it's the only music that goes at the speed of a burn.","rv_vote":"Paper Lantern Funeral, for Cledwyn, who liked them better than she did."},"vote":{"local":"At First Coal she'll refuse the King any order, from anyone, to clear the Grandmother's grove for a furnace; she'll go and stand under the tree with a wet blanket if they try.","edict":"Against; she's too stiff to riot, and she says Lightforge can come and write her name on the Grandmother if it likes, and see what happens to its hands."},"wants":"To be buried under the Grandmother, with the coal-pot.","site":"smeltgate_vf2","town":"Elmstead","realm":"Flameborne"},"shop:stillcity":{"name":"Halvard Skein","age":61,"role":"kit-and-salvage trader at the Auction Hall end of the Long Corridor; retired lamp-tender","look":"Tall and permanently stooped from forty years of low tunnels, a round shiny burn on each palm from wick-tins, a fur hat with the ear-flaps tied up on top like a pair of hands.","voice":"Slow. Lets a sentence hang so long you think he's finished, then finishes it.","greet":"Halla. Bows on the left, bread on the right. Don't lean on that wall. It's older than both of us.","lines":["The brokers moved their tables again. One corridor further from the Seat. That's twenty-two corridors in my lifetime. Give it ten winters, they'll be selling tomorrow off my stall.","The Court says a seen future belongs to the one in it. Fine. Then whose is an hour you paid for and never sat? I've eleven of those.","My knees know the cold's coming before the river-readers do. Probably. Don't go tying that on a cord."],"about":"I tended the blue lamps for thirty-four years. You learn to keep a flame just low enough that the ceiling doesn't weep on you. Now I sell packs to people going somewhere. Same job, near enough. Keep things from melting.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, with a lamp-tender's habit of counting under his breath"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"numbers and three insults, from haggling with Stonehold cheese men"}],"childhood":"Halvard was born in the Lower Galleries, two doors down from the corridor where the ceiling came down on a wedding five hundred winters ago, and his mother made him walk that stretch with one hand on the wall until he was six, in case it remembered. His father tended lamps, and Halvard carried the wick-tin behind him, learning to count by them: forty to a corridor, nine corridors to the Hall. At seven he let one go out on purpose, just to see the dark. His father didn't shout. He sat down on the ice beside the boy and they waited together in the blackness for the length of a whole keeping-soup song before he relit it. Halvard has liked small, quiet dark ever since, and has never told anyone that it frightens him less than crowds.","schooling":"Knot-school in the Hall annex under Old Brannet Usk, who began every term by tying the children's mittens together so they learned undoing before doing. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Chrona lamp flickered and went out; the tester said 'Probably nothing,' which Halvard took as a kindness. He was hopeless at letters until twelve and better than his teacher at ice-counting by thirteen.","history":"He went to the lamp-tenders at thirteen and stayed thirty-four years, learning which tunnels sweat and which ones creak and how to trim a flame by ear. At twenty-six he married Una, a dyer at Halde Orsk's vats, a laughing woman with blue to the elbows who said 'definitely' in front of him on purpose. They had no children and did not much mind. When Una fell ill with a lung fever he went to the Auction Hall and bought a seer's hour from the broker Ama Strell to ask whether she would live, and then stood outside the curtain and could not go in. He bought another the next winter, and another. Una died eight winters ago. He re-tied her cord into the Knotwall himself, without a word, the way it is done. He has kept buying one hour a year on the day she died. Strell writes each in her ledger, unsat. Eleven now. When his knees gave out he sold his lamp-hook, rented a stall cut into the corridor wall, and started trading bows, packs and bread to travellers, and buying back whatever they drag in from the road.","family":"No children. A younger sister, Ragnfrid, twists rope for the stair-cutters in Icewatch and writes him one line a season. Halde Orsk tells him that Una always used the good dye, every time they meet, which is her idea of comfort and, he has come to think, his too.","hero":{"who":"Orrin Who Stayed","why":"Orrin stood still on a cliff for whoever might come back. Halvard sat in a lamp-tunnel for thirty-four years for something like the same reason, though he couldn't tell you who he was waiting for."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Twelve-minute songs that end when they are ready and not before. He says it's the only music that walks at his pace.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters, because he wants to see a Lightforge presenter sit down again."},"vote":{"local":"Tied a knot for Ivo Rimeside at the Warm Chair, because a man who still cries is feeling it now and not later. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting on it, quietly, and wishes the city would stop pretending she never spoke.","edict":"Against. A register of every spark is a Knotwall nobody agreed to tie their knot on."},"wants":"To walk into one of his eleven hours and ask something that isn't about himself.","site":"stillcity","town":"The Still City","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:stillcity":{"name":"Brynja Hollin","age":34,"role":"smith of the Under-Rock forge, the only real forge in the Still City; two-knot Conclave drop-out","look":"Broad through the shoulders, hair cropped to a dark fuzz, and a white streak of frost in her left eyebrow that never melts, even with her face a foot from the coals.","voice":"Fast for Frostgrave. Says 'definitely' on purpose and watches you wince.","greet":"You'll want to keep your coat on. It's warm in here for about two feet in any direction, and you're not standing in them.","lines":["Ice-saw blades, lamp-hooks, chisels for the cutters. Definitely the best in the city. Also the only. Don't tell Halde I said definitely, she'll tie a knot against me.","They want a spark register in Lightforge. Fine. They can come down here and write my name in it themselves. Bring a lamp. Bring two.","That stone by the door is a Stonehold woman's burden-stone. She said she'd be back to lift it. Seven years. I dust it."],"about":"I went to Korrhaven and came back with two knots and no feelings worth the name, so I left. My uncle had a forge nobody wanted. Now I've a forge nobody else could run. I'm better at hitting things than slowing them, it turns out.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, clipped"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, Conclave-taught"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good swearing, fair grammar; learned off a bricklayer"},{"tongue":"Old Spark-speech","level":"the Chrona Tower oath and nothing else"}],"childhood":"The Hollins had cut ice for nine generations and Brynja was the first of them to be bad at it. She cut too fast and too hot, somehow, so her blocks came out glossy and wet when everyone else's were dry and white, and her grandfather called her 'little thaw' until she bit him. What she liked was her Uncle Teit's smoky closet of a forge off the cutters' hall, where he sharpened their saws. At eight she leaned too close and took off her own left eyebrow; it grew back with a white streak in it that the family took as a sign of something. She wanted it to be a sign she'd be a smith. They hoped it meant seer. Both, it turned out, though not the way anyone meant.","schooling":"At the Lamp Test the Chrona lamp answered so bright the tester shaded her eyes: Full Spark. Three Lamp Years under Lamp-reader Oda Venn, who made the class sit in silence guessing the length of a minute until they could do it to a breath. Then the Chrona Tower on Korrhaven at twelve. Two knots on her grey sash. She could slow a dropped hammer to a drift. She left before the third knot.","history":"At fourteen, standing at her grandmother's Re-tying, Brynja noticed she was not crying and could not find the place in herself where crying would come from. The frost had reached her brows a decade early. She walked out of the Conclave that spring, which in Frostgrave counts as a broken word, and the cutters would not take her back. Teit did. She learned the forge the honest way, by burning herself, and when Teit died she took it over and found the ceiling dripped onto the anvil. So she went down. Over three summers she bored the forge into the bedrock beneath the glacier and hired a Kell Deep bricklayer, Tamsa Durrow, to line it with Stonehold firebrick, and a felted chimney up through forty feet of ice. Tamsa stayed two winters, taught her to swear in Holdspeech, left a burden-stone by the door and said she would be back to lift it. Brynja makes saw-blades, lamp-hooks, cutters' chisels and halberd spares for Rimewatch, and turns away seers who want a hammer that rings at a particular pitch.","family":"Her mother still cuts ice and has forgiven her about two-thirds. Two cousins on the cutting gangs. She sends Tamsa Durrow a sledge of birch-sap wine every autumn and gets back, every spring, one carry-loaf, chipped not sliced.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"'Lift. Then talk.' A king who put the crown down and went to work with his hands is the only kind she trusts."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"The drummer's hands are turning to stone and he keeps playing. She thinks that is the most honest thing anyone has ever done on a stage.","rv_vote":"Burden, and she'll shout it, which no one in her row will forgive."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for the motion to read Lyriel's warning back into the Hall's record. Has never tied a knot for or against a Speaker; she says a smith doesn't sit near warm chairs.","edict":"Against, loudly. The Edict would write her two knots in a book in Meridian, and she has spent twenty years making sure her spark lives nowhere but her own two hands."},"wants":"To never be written down anywhere she can't read it.","site":"stillcity","town":"The Still City","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:stillcity":{"name":"Hekla Orsk","age":14,"role":"apprentice knotwright at her great-aunt Halde's dye-vats; rinser of cords, collector of gossip","look":"Small, freckled from the dye-steam, fingers stained blue to the second knuckle, a word-cord at her belt with exactly four knots she will show you whether you ask or not.","voice":"Talks fast, then remembers she's in Frostgrave and slows down mid-word.","greet":"Halla! Sorry. Halla. I'm meant to bow. Have you come about cords? You haven't, have you. Nobody my age gets asked about cords.","lines":["Aunt Halde says the Pellow family buys cheap dye because they're mean. I think they buy it because they don't expect their knots to last. That's worse, isn't it?","My mother clerks for the brokers in the Auction Hall. Aunt Halde won't eat at our table on account of it. We eat at hers.","There's a cord in the Knotwall that's dyed this year but hung with the three-hundred-year-olds. Nobody tied it in front of anyone. I've checked. Twice."],"about":"I'm going to be the best knotwright in the city, probably. That's not a promise, it's a forecast. I rinse cords all day and listen. People talk in front of the vats like dyers are furniture.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, quick"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good street Bridge, learned from traders' kids in the Long Corridor"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"the four funeral words, which she uses wrongly at parties"}],"childhood":"Hekla's first word was 'probably', which her great-aunt Halde has told every customer for fourteen years as though it were a prophecy. She grew up between two rooms that disliked each other: the dye-vats, steaming and blue, where Halde ruled, and her mother's little desk in the Auction Hall, where Jorunn Orsk wrote out the brokers' bills of sale for hours of tomorrow. Hekla learned to walk the corridor between them carrying a pot of keeping soup in one direction and a sealed message in the other, and learned young never to say which room she liked better. At six she tied her baby brother's foot to his cradle with a perfect reef-knot and was praised for the knot and smacked for the brother, on the same afternoon, by the same aunt.","schooling":"Knot-school in the Hall annex under Knot-master Ebbe Rand, who taught the hardest knots by lamplight and then blew the lamp out and made them do it again by touch. At eight the Lamp Test lamps sat dark for her, all eight. She cried the whole walk home. Halde put her to work at the vats the next morning and said that was the end of it, and it was.","history":"She has only fourteen years to tell and tells them all at once. At eleven she was taken on properly at the vats, which means rinsing, wringing, hanging and being shouted at softly. She knows by smell which dye-lot a cord came from and which families buy the cheap madder-blue that fades to grey in a decade, and she has her own theories about why. At twelve she stood in the crowd when Ivo Rimeside sat in the Warm Chair and watched him weep while he dropped his barley into the Long Pot, and decided he was the first grown-up she had seen do something properly. At thirteen she was sent out of a Re-tying for humming a Thaw song under her breath without noticing. She has spent most of this year searching the Knotwall for the new-dyed cord hung among the oldest, the one nobody saw tied. Her mother tells her to leave it. Her aunt tells her to leave it. She has not left it.","family":"Mother Jorunn, broker's clerk; a brother, Pim-not-Pim (his name is Pimmet, and he hates it); father away on the cutting gangs most of the year; great-aunt Halde Orsk, who is the sun her household turns round.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"Edda knotted a vision into a cord so it couldn't be bought or forgotten. Hekla thinks that's what a knotwright is really for, and tells Halde so, which goes about as well as you'd guess."},"band":{"act":"Thaw","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Bubbly, bright, fast enough to dance to, and every elder in the city hates them. That last part is most of it.","rv_vote":"Thaw, and she's already sewn a yellow ribbon on her cord, which is not allowed."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to tie at the Warm Chair. If she could she'd tie for Ivo every time, and for acting on Lyriel's warning, 'obviously, she's a seer, what else is she for'.","edict":"Against, mostly because she doesn't understand it and is annoyed that everyone seems to think that's fine for a fourteen-year-old."},"wants":"To find out who tied the new cord in the old row, and why they did it where nobody would see.","site":"stillcity","town":"The Still City","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:stillcity:b":{"name":"Mattis Grell","age":52,"role":"keeper of the unclaimed cords: he re-ties into the Knotwall the cords of the dead who left nobody to do it","look":"Neat, narrow, clean-shaven, which is rare in the Still City; a long felt coat with a hundred small loops sewn inside it for carrying other people's cords.","voice":"Quiet, unhurried, never uses a person's name until he's sure he'll remember it.","greet":"Halla. You're not dead, so you're not mine. That's good. Is there something you need?","lines":["Eleven unclaimed cords this winter. Twelve last winter. People don't die more. They just die with nobody left.","The brokers sell tomorrow by the hour. My father was one. I carried his hours door to door. Don't buy one. That's all I'll say.","If you see a man by the Knotwall at night with his eyes shut, leave him be. That's how I find the old lines. You feel for the stiff ones."],"about":"I tie the last knot for people who've nobody to tie it. The Hall pays me four crossings a cord and a bowl from the Long Pot on Thaw. It's honest work. Nobody argues with you about it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, very precise"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, learned on the doorsteps"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"enough for a Spiritveil funeral and a polite refusal"}],"childhood":"His father sold tomorrows and Mattis delivered them, folded small in birch-bark, to doors all over the Lower Galleries. From six years old he ran the corridors with a satchel of other people's futures: whether a sledge would come back, whether a marriage would hold, whether the baby would be a girl. He was told never to open one and opened his first at eight, with a hairpin, in the dark behind a lamp. It said the man in question would lose a tooth by spring. Mattis watched him for months. In spring the man lost a tooth, laughing at something in the street, and Mattis ran home and was sick in the snow. He did not open another for seven years. Then he opened a great many.","schooling":"None, to speak of. His father would not spare him from the round, and the knot-school mistress gave up sending for him. He learned his letters by reading the hours he wasn't supposed to read, which made him an odd, careful reader, very good at small hands. He was nine before anyone remembered the Lamp Test; he stood in the ring alone, late, and nothing lit.","history":"At fifteen he opened an hour meant for a woman in the Fourth Gallery. It said her son would fall on the Glass Stair in the year the Lowmere closed early. He did not deliver it. He told his father the woman wasn't home and burned it, and then lay awake for years waiting to hear of a fall. It never came. He has never known whether the seer was wrong or whether he changed it by keeping it back, and he thinks about that more than about anything else in his life. At nineteen he left his father's trade and went labouring for the ice-cutters, hauling blocks, saying nothing. At thirty the Hall of Kept Words needed someone to re-tie the cords of the unclaimed dead, a job nobody wanted, and Ebbe Rand put his name forward because, he said, 'Grell doesn't talk.' He has tied more than four hundred last knots. He married Siv, a felter, at forty, very quietly, in thirty-eight seconds. They have one daughter, grown.","family":"Wife Siv, a felter, who laughs at him for folding his socks. Daughter Ebba, twenty, gone south to work at the inn by the Pale bell-posts. His father, the broker, is ninety and still alive two corridors from the Auction Hall; Mattis takes him soup and they do not discuss the trade.","hero":{"who":"Saelen of the Drift","why":"The Lantern of the Dead, who looked after those nobody else would. Mattis heard the story from a Spiritveil traveller at a Re-tying and thought, for the first time, that his job had a name somewhere."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"The forty-second silence in the middle of their famous song is exactly how long a Re-tying takes if you do it properly.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime, for the silence, and he will not clap during it."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, because the man came to a Re-tying of an unclaimed ferryman once and stood at the back without being asked. On Lyriel's warning he is torn and ties no knot, which in the Still City is a statement in itself.","edict":"For, which surprises everyone who knows him. He thinks a register is only a Knotwall for sparks, and that nobody should be left unclaimed, not even on paper."},"wants":"To know whether keeping back that one hour changed a man's life or did nothing at all.","site":"stillcity","town":"The Still City","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:rimewatch":{"name":"Oddrun Frethe","age":58,"role":"keeper of the gate slop-shop at Rimewatch; sutler, letter-writer, and the castle's unofficial voice to Ironwatch","look":"Short and round in three layers of felt, spectacles of cut ice-glass on a cord round her neck, ink on her right thumb that never quite comes off.","voice":"Brisk, practical, counts things while she talks, and reads anything written down out loud whether you want her to or not.","greet":"Shop's open. Biscuit, bowstrings, mittens, packs. If you're here for the watch-biscuit, I'll sell you the hot wine to dunk it in. I'm not a monster.","lines":["We had a letter from Ironwatch once, asking us to make more noise. I wrote back. Six years ago. We still write. Their second shift sings in the bath, I'm told.","Eighty-one cairns. My Aslak stood to arms for two of them and died of a winter cough in between. The enemy never comes. The cold always does.","Somebody's buying up the old cairn-slates. Collectors from the Still City, they say. What do you want a slate for that says nothing happened?"],"about":"My family's kept the gate shop for five generations. We sell to soldiers who think they're about to die and then don't. It makes them generous and then embarrassed. I've learned to price for both.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent and written; she drafts the castle's supply contracts"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"reads it slowly, from six years of letters; can't say a word of it aloud without laughing"}],"childhood":"Oddrun learned to count on the castle's biscuit barrels, which were stacked in the cellar in fours, so for years she believed numbers went one, two, three, four, barrel. Her grandmother kept the shop then, and kept Oddrun under the counter in a crate lined with fleece, where she listened to soldiers' boots and learned their ranks by the sound. The whole castle went busy and serious, the halberdiers bought socks by the dozen, and then the season passed and everyone ate supper on the ridge in the snow, and she was allowed to stay up. She thought the Cairn Supper was a party until she was ten, when a sergeant at the cairn said what he'd have lost was his daughter, and then looked straight at her, and she went quiet for a week.","schooling":"The garrison knot-school, taught by Halberd-sergeant Ottilie Rann, who drilled sums like marching orders and made the children call out answers at attention. No lamp lit at the Lamp Test, which nobody in her family had expected anyway. She was taught her Bridge letters by the castle's clerk so she could read the supply sledges' bills, and she has read everything put in front of her since.","history":"She took the shop over at twenty-four when her grandmother's eyes went, and married Corporal Aslak Frethe, a large shy halberdier who mended his own socks better than she could, at twenty-seven. They had two sons. In the seventy-ninth cairn season, with the whole garrison stood to arms for an army foreseen coming out of the smoke, Aslak caught a cough in the gate-tower and died of it in nine days, an ordinary death in an extraordinary season. At the Cairn Supper Oddrun was not a soldier and had no right to speak. She stood up anyway and said 'Aslak,' and nobody stopped her, and the garrison has let her speak at every Cairn Supper since. Six years ago a letter came over the hills from Ironwatch, very politely asking the castle to make more noise at night. The Warden laughed and passed it to her. She wrote back: 'We will try. Probably.' The storekeeper who answered signed himself Coyle, Second Shift. They have written every month since.","family":"Two grown sons, both halberdiers. Her late husband Aslak. A sister in the Still City who thinks the letters to Ironwatch are a scandal and asks to read every one.","hero":{"who":"Solvane","why":"A polite young engineer who stood up in front of the whole Council and read Metalcrown's petition clearly while they laughed. Oddrun reads Coyle's letters aloud in the mess, and the soldiers laugh too, and she reads on."},"band":{"act":"Shift Change","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Coyle sent her a broadsheet of their lyrics. Three singers who are never on stage together, one for each whistle, which is the only thing that's made the Ironwatch noise make sense to her.","rv_vote":"Shift Change, because Coyle said Metalcrown has never had one lamp from Lightforge and she'd like them to have one from somewhere."},"vote":{"local":"Tied a knot for Ivo Rimeside, for being a ferryman and not a seer. On Lyriel's warning she ties against acting: Rimewatch has dressed for eighty-one storms, and she's seen what it costs.","edict":"Torn. She asked Coyle what Metalcrown thinks, and he wrote back one line, 'We are smiling,' and she still can't tell what he meant."},"wants":"To meet Coyle once, at the halfway point in the hills, in daylight, with soup.","site":"rimewatch","town":"Rimewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:rimewatch":{"name":"Kolbein Ask","age":40,"role":"castle armourer of Rimewatch; forges halberd-heads for the Stillwatch and keeps the old ones","look":"Long-armed and lean, ginger beard plaited short, a halberdier's cord with three knots and a second, plain undyed cord hanging behind it that he'll tell you is spare.","voice":"Measured, deep, a little formal, as if he's reading off a slate in his head.","greet":"Halla. Edge or haft? I do both, but I'll want to know before I touch it.","lines":["Every foreseen war, I forge forty new heads, in case. Forty heads, three times now. I keep one of each lot. The rest go to sharpening.","Ironwatch whistles all night. The men curse it. I sleep through it like a baby. Don't ask why.","Permafrost played the Conclave last autumn and the whole crowd stood still for an hour. Best thing I've ever not heard."],"about":"I'm the castle smith. I've stood in the line three times and forged for it three times. I'd rather forge. You know where you stand with iron. It tells you when it's ready.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"fluent, from his mother; he hides it"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"understands most of it through the Shiftcant"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Kolbein's mother came over the eastern hills in a snowstorm with a hammer in her coat and no word-cord at her belt. She was from the foundry-town beyond the ridge, she never said why she'd left, and old Gudmund, the castle smith, took her on. Kolbein was born a year later. He grew up with two sets of words for everything: Rime for outside, and at home a clipped, hard language his mother used for tools and swearing and, late at night, for singing. She laid a little hammer in his hands at ten, stamped with his name, and told him never to show it to anybody. He showed it to everybody. She was so angry she didn't speak for three days, and then she was proud, and that was also how she did most things.","schooling":"The Chrona lamp glimmered for him at eight, faint as breath on glass: a Half-Spark. That bought him three Lamp Years in Rimewatch's small lamp-room under Seer-tutor Hild Varre, who had the class predict each week's first snowfall and kept the scores on a slate for three years. He won once. There was no Korrhaven place for a Half-Spark with a foreign mother, and he didn't ask.","history":"He apprenticed to Gudmund at thirteen, beside his mother, and drilled with the halberdiers from sixteen, as every castle-born man does. He stood to arms in the seventy-ninth cairn season, the eightieth and the eighty-first, three foreseen wars, and has a knot on his cord for each. He doesn't know what he promised. Nobody does; it is the custom. Between stand-tos he forged. When Gudmund died he became castle smith at thirty-one, and set his mother up in the corner of the forge with a stool and a small anvil of her own until her eyes went. She died five winters ago. In Frostgrave the dead get a Re-tying; she had no cord to re-tie. So Kolbein laid the little stamped hammer of her own in her hands, the way she had told him her people did it, and then tied a cord for her himself, undyed, with one knot, and wears it behind his own. He forges forty new halberd-heads every time a war is foreseen and keeps one of each lot on a rack in the back, unblooded. Three on the rack. He thinks of them as a set.","family":"Unmarried. Raises his late sister's boy, Runi, twelve, who wants to be a Winter Rider and thinks the forge is boring. His mother, Marra, five winters dead.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"Brask held Korr's Span when holding was the hard thing, and Stormreach riders throw stones at his Stillman to this day. Kolbein thinks the people who hold get the stones, and he's fine with that."},"band":{"act":"Permafrost","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Black metal whose fans stand perfectly still. It's the only music he's heard that sounds like a garrison at stand-to.","rv_vote":"Permafrost, and he'll stand still for the whole set."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside without much thought. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, carefully: 'We stand to arms for every whisper. We can stand to arms for a seer the Council struck out.'","edict":"For, quietly. Frostgrave writes every promise on a wall; he can't see what's so terrible about writing down a spark. He hasn't thought about what it would mean for his mother's people, and he will."},"wants":"For one foreseen war to come, just one, so the forty heads were worth it.","site":"rimewatch","town":"Rimewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:rimewatch":{"name":"Yrsa Brekk","age":29,"role":"handler at the Rimewatch Echo Wolf kennel","look":"Rangy, wind-burned, hair in a thick pale rope down her back, fur cuffs chewed ragged by pups, a whistle on a thong she never blows.","voice":"Loud for Frostgrave, louder for a kennel, laughs with her whole chest and then looks round to see who heard.","greet":"Don't say anything near the pen you don't want to hear again for ten years. Halla. Mind the grey one. He's learned a sneeze.","lines":["The wolves have started doing a whistle that isn't Ironwatch's. Low, long. None of us knows it. They only do it before dawn.","Ironwatch's whistles, three shifts, all night, and Mutter repeats every one. You think the soldiers can't sleep? Try the kennel.","My wife's a farrier's daughter. She proposed by shoeing my boots. Took thirty-nine seconds. I said probably. Best probably I ever said."],"about":"I raise and run the echo wolves. They hear something strange on the ridge, they bring it home and say it back to us. Best sentries in Frostgrave. Worst gossips.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, but she shouts it"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a dozen whistle-words, learned from what the wolves bring back"}],"childhood":"Yrsa was the loudest baby ever born at Rimewatch, and the garrison still swears it stood to arms the night she arrived. Her father was a sledge-maker in the lean-tos below the wall and her mother a halberdier, so she was raised mostly by her father, who didn't mind noise, and by the kennel next door, which repeated her. By three she could set off every wolf in the pen with one shriek, and the kennel-mother used to borrow her to check the pups' ears. She grew up thinking everybody had a choir of wolves that answered them. At seven she shouted a rude word about the Warden, and the pups said it to him at inspection for most of that winter, in her voice. He laughed so hard he had to sit.","schooling":"The garrison knot-school, briefly; she was sent out so often for noise that Kennel-mother Asfrid Lowe took her on instead. Asfrid's rule was never to say a word near a pup you didn't want repeated forever, and she taught by making Yrsa sit silent beside the pens for a whole day. No lamp lit at the Test. She can tie six knots and read slowly.","history":"She apprenticed at the kennel at twelve and has run a pack since nineteen. Her first wolf was Hum, an old grey bitch, who on Yrsa's first winter patrol picked up the crack of an ice-shelf letting go on the far ridge, carried it home, and kept repeating it at the gate until the Warden sent riders to look. They found the shelf ready to come down on the courier road and closed it a day before it fell. Yrsa has never trusted a sound she couldn't trace since. At twenty-two she married Signy, a farrier's daughter, in thirty-nine seconds in the castle yard, and they live in two rooms over the kennel, which nobody else would take. This winter her wolves have started repeating a low, long whistle before dawn that matches none of Ironwatch's three shifts. She has walked the ridge four nights trying to find it, and she has told the Warden, and the Warden has told her to dress for it.","family":"Wife Signy. Father, Bergen, still makes sledges. Mother Ulla Brekk, a retired halberd-sergeant who now keeps the garrison knot-school and has given up teaching Yrsa anything.","hero":{"who":"Hum, her first Echo Wolf","why":"Hum heard the ice-shelf going, carried the sound home and wouldn't stop saying it until someone listened. Yrsa thinks that's the whole job, and the whole of being decent."},"band":{"act":"The Ice Drummers","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Drums cut from lake ice that melt as they're played, so no two shows sound alike. The wolves can't learn them, which she finds restful.","rv_vote":"The Ice Drummers, for melting on purpose."},"vote":{"local":"Doesn't go to tie knots; Signy goes and ties for whatever Yrsa says over breakfast. This year: for Ivo, and for acting on Lyriel's warning, because a warning nobody listens to is just a wolf barking.","edict":"Against, from the gut. She doesn't like anything that wants to write down what you are before you've had a chance to do something about it."},"wants":"To find out what's making the fourth whistle before somebody gets hurt not knowing.","site":"rimewatch","town":"Rimewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:rimewatch:b":{"name":"Arne Tollef","age":71,"role":"retired Stillwatch halberdier; collector of cairn-slates; sits by the gate and offers opinions","look":"Bald, spotted, enormous ears, a halberdier's cord with six knots worn so smooth they shine, and very good boots.","voice":"Cheerful, repetitive, delighted by his own jokes on the third telling.","greet":"Halla, halla. Stood six wars, me. Six. Never saw an enemy. Best record in the castle.","lines":["Eighty-one cairns. Eighty-one times we were ready. You laugh, but nobody's ever come over that ridge, have they? Feel it later, friend. Stand your watch now.","At every Cairn Supper I say what I'd have lost was my good boots. Every time. Fifty-two years. They've stopped laughing. I haven't.","I bought back an old slate from a Still City collector. It's dated forty winters from now. Who carves a cairn before the war?"],"about":"Halberdier fifty-two years, man and boy. Married a laundress, raised three children, buried one wife, kept my boots. That's the lot. Dull as watch-biscuit, my life, and I've loved every bite.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"barracks Bridge, mostly orders"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"the Lonely Line Choir's songs, phonetically"}],"childhood":"Arne's earliest memory is of a sergeant's boot, at eye level, and a voice above it telling him to get out of the way. He was the seventh son of a castle laundress and spent his first years under the drying racks in the long steam room, among the soldiers' smallclothes, where it was the only warm place in Rimewatch and therefore the most popular. He learned to march by watching boots go past the gap under the door. He learned to tell rank from the stitching on the hems his mother mended. He decided at five that he would be a halberdier because they had the best boots, and he did not change his mind once, ever, about anything, which his wife later said was both the best and worst thing about him.","schooling":"The garrison knot-school under a corporal whose name he has forgotten and whose moustache he has not. He learned his drill knots, his letters badly and his numbers well enough to count cairns, and was made to march the yard in his socks for laughing at the moustache. No lamp lit; he says it was the eighth lamp's fault for not trying harder.","history":"He took the halberd at sixteen and kept it fifty-two years. He stood to arms in six foreseen wars, the seventy-fifth cairn through the eightieth, and at every Cairn Supper on the ridge, asked what he would have lost, he said, 'My good boots.' The first time it got a laugh. By the third it got a groan. By the sixth the young halberdiers chanted it with him. He married Hedvig, a laundress like his mother, at twenty-four; they had three children and buried one, a boy, of a fever at four, which Arne does not talk about and never says at a cairn. Hedvig died ten years ago. He retired at sixty-eight and took a stool by the gate, where he greets everyone and tells every recruit the same three stories. In the last year he has started buying back old cairn-slates from Still City collectors with his pension, and one, found under the slumped third cairn, carries the name of an enemy and a date forty winters from now.","family":"Two living daughters, one a castle cook, one married in Glassmere. Six grandchildren, all of whom say 'my good boots' as a family joke. His wife Hedvig, ten winters dead; a son, Leif, dead at four.","hero":{"who":"the Lost Warden","why":"Nobody knows who the Lost Warden was or what they did, and Arne thinks that's the most honest kind of soldier there is: you stand your watch and nobody remembers."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Forty old soldiers singing so low you have to lean in. He heard them once at Hearthward on a supply run forty years ago and has hummed them since, wrongly.","rv_vote":"The Lonely Line Choir, because 'somebody has to vote for the old men'."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside because he heard the man once sold a ferry-fare short to a widow. On Lyriel's warning he ties firmly against: 'We've been ready eighty-one times. You don't wear out the watch on one more whisper.'","edict":"Doesn't much care. Ties no knot. 'The Lightforge folk can count their sparks. I'll count my cairns.'"},"wants":"To see eighty-two cairns before he goes, and for the eighty-second to be as empty as the others.","site":"rimewatch","town":"Rimewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:hoarfen":{"name":"Oddny Reed","age":44,"role":"Hoarfen's trader and eel-smoker; presses hoarfrost feathers for the Still City dyers","look":"Wiry, freckled, smoke-brown to the wrists, with a reed tucked behind one ear that she chews when she's adding up.","voice":"Dry and quick, sells while she talks, and has an opinion about everyone's eel.","greet":"Halla. Packs, arrows, bread, eel. My eel. Not the Moss eel, which tastes of bog, whatever she tells you.","lines":["Stonehold swears the bog moved and the marker didn't. I've lived here forty-four years. The bog's never moved an inch for me.","Halde Orsk up in the city buys my frost-feathers to set her blues. Haggles like I've personally insulted her grandmother. I probably have.","I don't think a ferryman ought to sit in the Warm Chair. Should've been a peat-wife. We know about keeping a fire going."],"about":"I smoke eel, sell kit, buy whatever the road drags in, and press frost into bark for dyers who've never seen a fen. I'm the only one in Hoarfen who's been to Korrhaven. Twice. I'll tell you about it. You can't stop me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, with a Korrhaven market accent she's proud of"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good enough to cheat a Greywater man and be cheated back"}],"childhood":"Every house in Hoarfen says the others' eel tastes of the bog, and Oddny's grandmother, who had a nose like a heron's beak, raised her to prove it. From six Oddny was sent round the village with a basket of the family's eel to trade a strip with each neighbour, 'for friendship,' and bring the neighbour's strip home to be tasted in judgement at the kitchen table. Her grandmother chewed each one slowly and pronounced on it. 'Bog.' 'Bog.' 'Bog and feet.' Oddny believed for years that her family were the only people in the fen who knew how to smoke a fish. At eleven she tasted a strip of the Moss eel on the walk home, out of hunger, and realised it was better than theirs, and kept the secret for thirty-three years.","schooling":"Hoarfen's knot-school is one peat-walled room; Peat-master Hallgrim Tey taught counting in bricks and knots in reed-twine and made the children stand still for a quarter-hour every morning 'to practise'. The Hoarfen children walked to the Still City for the Lamp Test; nothing lit for Oddny, and she spent the walk home buying a honey-sweet off a stall with her mother's coin.","history":"She married Tobbe Reed, a reed-thatcher, at twenty, after he said in front of her whole family that he liked the smell of smoke on her, and she decided a man who could say a thing like that out loud in Frostgrave was worth keeping. They took the village shop off his uncle and added the smokehouse behind it. At twenty-six she rode a peat-cart all the way to the Bridge and then to Korrhaven for the Realm Vision, working her passage as a cart-hand, because she'd heard there would be dancing, and saw Slow Heat play to a crowd that didn't know what to do with them. She went back six years later and they still didn't. In between she worked out that frost-feathers pressed in birch-bark keep their shape, and sold the first sheaf to Halde Orsk's vats for a price she has never got again. She has two sons, both cutters, and a long, quiet arrangement with an old Greywater trader across the boundary marker: Hoarfen peat for Stonehold carry-bread, handed over at night, on the side nobody cuts.","family":"Husband Tobbe, who says very little and always the right thing. Two sons, Gisle and Tor, both peat-cutters. Her grandmother's nose, which she inherited and resents.","hero":{"who":"Ingret Moss, Hoarfen's peat-wife","why":"Three strokes to cut a frozen brick, and she's never said 'definitely' in her life. Oddny says definitely all the time and knows it's a fault, and Ingret's eel is better than hers, and she has never told her."},"band":{"act":"Slow Heat","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Half-speed reggaeton. She saw them twice at Korrhaven, and she dances to them alone in the smokehouse, slowly, among the eels.","rv_vote":"Slow Heat, because nobody else will, and somebody's got to dance."},"vote":{"local":"Tied against Ivo Rimeside at the Warm Chair, the only knot against him from Hoarfen; she wanted a peat-wife. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting: 'A seer warns you, you smoke more eel. That's all acting means.'","edict":"Torn. She doesn't care about sparks, but she's watched the Court try to ban the brokers for years and knows a law about who owns things never stays where it's put."},"wants":"For Hoarfen to admit, just once, out loud, that her family's eel is the best in the fen.","site":"hoarfen","town":"Hoarfen","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:hoarfen":{"name":"Joran Sel","age":66,"role":"Hoarfen's smith: peat-spades, eel-hooks, reed-knives, and the village bell's clapper","look":"Heavy, slow, a white beard he trims with his own shears, and a small, shiny star of scar tissue on the back of each hand.","voice":"Deep and very sparse. Answers questions about iron at length and questions about himself with a single word.","greet":"Halla. What's broken.","lines":["If you hear the chime, be the stone. Don't run, don't swat, don't breathe hard. The last one who ran, we still keep his cord. He'd want you told.","Peat-spade wants a long neck and a short temper. Like a good cutter.","Stone Hour's next week. I'll be at the anvil with a hammer in the air. Held it up the whole hour once. Shoulder's never been right."],"about":"I make spades. My father made spades. I mend the bell when it cracks, which is every few winters, from being rung for Stone Hour. That's me. Not much else to say.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, slow even for Rime"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, all to do with prices"}],"childhood":"Joran was six and standing in the reeds of the Chime-beds when the shardlings rose. He had gone there because his father had told him not to, and he remembers the sound first, a fine high ringing like a hundred glasses touched with a fingernail, and then the air full of light. He remembers his father's voice, very low, saying 'Be the stone, Jory.' He remembers that he couldn't. He started to cry, and then to shake, and the ringing turned towards him. Then his father, Arvid, who had been standing perfectly still forty yards off, moved. Joran does not remember what happened next, only being picked up much later by a peat-wife, and two small cuts on the backs of his hands, and his father's cord being given to his mother.","schooling":"Peat-master Hallgrim Tey's father taught the knot-school then. Joran sat at the back and spoke almost nothing for two years after the swarm, and old Tey let him sit there and tie reed-knots without asking him to say them aloud. At the Lamp Test in the Still City, nothing lit. He was apprenticed to his uncle's forge at ten.","history":"The village, being kind, said Arvid Sel ran. 'The last one who ran,' they say, carefully, and keep his cord on a hook in the meeting-house, and tell every visitor to be the stone. Nobody has ever said, in Joran's hearing, that Arvid moved because his son had moved first. Joran has never said it either. He took over his uncle's forge at twenty-four and has made every peat-spade in Hoarfen for forty years, each with a long neck to reach the deep brick. He married Liss, a reed-cutter, at thirty; they have one daughter, Arvida, who left for the Still City to dye cords for Halde Orsk and comes home on Thaw. Liss died four years ago, of something slow in her chest, and her cord was re-tied by Joran into the Still City Knotwall with a single knot, as is done. At every Stone Hour he stands at the anvil with a hammer raised and holds it there for the whole hour, every month, and he is sixty-six, and his shoulder is ruined, and he does not stop.","family":"Daughter Arvida, a dyer at the Orsk vats, who asks him every Thaw to come and live in the city. His wife Liss, four years dead. His father Arvid, whose cord hangs in the meeting-house.","hero":{"who":"Arvid Sel, his father","why":"He was the stone, right up until his boy needed him not to be. Joran has never said this out loud to anyone, and the hammer he holds up every Stone Hour is the closest he comes."},"band":{"act":"Mason Grey","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A quarry baritone so low you feel it in the anvil. Arvida played him a song on a Greywater man's zither once, and it was the only time she's seen him cry.","rv_vote":"Mason Grey, because he sings like a man who doesn't need to say much."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, because a man who cries in public is braver than he is. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting. He doesn't say why.","edict":"Against. He doesn't understand sparks, but he knows what it is to have a thing written about your family that isn't quite true, and to let it stand."},"wants":"To tell the village, once, that his father didn't run.","site":"hoarfen","town":"Hoarfen","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:hoarfen":{"name":"Tuva Stray","age":10,"role":"village child; reed-gatherer's daughter; has never once lasted a Stone Hour","look":"All elbows and knees, cheeks chapped red, a first word-cord at her belt with no knots on it at all, which she keeps tucking out of sight.","voice":"Talks while she moves and moves while she talks. Can't help it.","greet":"Halla! Have you got grain? We've got hens. Well, two. One's a bit dead. Not dead. Asleep a lot.","lines":["Stone Hour's next week. I'm going to do it this time. I'm going to be so still the shardlings land on me. Ma says please don't.","The lamp lit for me. In the Still City. The blue one. Everyone clapped and Ma cried and then nobody would tell me if that was good.","Old Joran holds his hammer up the whole Stone Hour. The whole hour. I watched him through the window. That's how I got caught."],"about":"I'm Tuva. I pick reeds with Ma and I feed the hens and I'm going to get my first knot. Probably this year. Definitely. No, probably. Ma says probably.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, very fast"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"six words, mostly 'please' and the names of sweets"}],"childhood":"Tuva has never kept still in her life, and the village has made a sort of hobby of her. At Stone Hour, when the bell rings and everybody freezes where they stand, Tuva has lasted four minutes, nine minutes, once nearly twenty, and then sneezed, or scratched, or turned round to see who was looking. The other children got their first knots at six and seven. She is ten and her cord is bare. Her mother picks reeds and her father is away hauling peat to the capital most of the winter, and she has a baby brother who already sits more still than she does, which she thinks is unfair. She is loud, and liked, and every grown-up in Hoarfen has at some point held her by both shoulders and said, very gently, 'Tuva. Stop.'","schooling":"She is at Peat-master Hallgrim Tey's knot-school now and is good at knots, bad at sitting and very good at sums when she can say them while hopping. Last autumn at the Lamp Test in the Still City the Chrona lamp lit for her, steady and blue. She is a Full Spark. She is meant to begin her Lamp Years next winter, and she has asked if she can bring the hens.","history":"Ten years is not much history, but Tuva's has been eventful. At five she followed a fox onto the frozen fen and was found by Esk Holm, sitting in the snow with the fox two yards off, both of them watching each other. At seven she was sent out of the meeting-house for poking Arvid Sel's cord on its hook to see if it would swing, and was told the whole story by Oddny Reed afterwards, standing in the cold, and was very quiet for a day. At eight she climbed the meeting-house roof during a Stone Hour, which is technically allowed because nobody is permitted to move to stop you. At nine she watched old Joran Sel through the forge window holding his hammer up for the full hour and decided he was the strongest person who ever lived. Then the lamp lit. Since then the grown-ups have been talking in low voices about Lightforge, and registers, and audits, and she has worked out that it is about her, and she would very much like someone to tell her plainly what it means.","family":"Mother Gunna, a reed-gatherer; father Ketil, a peat-hauler who is often away; baby brother Lop, who is irritatingly still.","hero":{"who":"the Silent Sentinel","why":"A Gravem master in Stonehold whose jaw has turned to stone and who has to carve slates to talk, and she has heard he hasn't moved from his seat for years. She'd like to ask him how."},"band":{"act":"Kestrel Vane","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A sky-racer pop star who never stops moving. Oddny brought back a torn poster from Korrhaven and Tuva has it on the wall by her bed, a bit upside down.","rv_vote":"Kestrel Vane, because she's fast, and nobody in Frostgrave is fast except the Knot-Cord Brothers, and she's already voted for them in her head."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to tie. She'd tie for whoever let her keep the hens. Asked about Lyriel's warning, she said, 'What did she say?' and nobody could tell her.","edict":"Against, because her mother is, and because it is about her, and nobody asked her."},"wants":"To last one whole Stone Hour and get a knot before they send her away to the Lamp Years.","site":"hoarfen","town":"Hoarfen","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:hoarfen:b":{"name":"Esk Holm","age":37,"role":"peat-cutter; the second-fastest brick in Hoarfen after Ingret Moss; drinks with Stonehold cutters across the marker","look":"Long and narrow as a peat-spade, hair cut in a straight bowl, a cutter's leather apron worn even off-shift, and a chipped Stonehold mile-marker chip on a thong round the neck.","voice":"Flat, wry, never quite smiles at their own jokes. Neither a man nor a woman, and bored of being asked.","greet":"Halla. Not 'sir', not 'madam'. Esk does fine.","lines":["Three hundred years Stonehold's said the bog moved and the marker didn't. I've measured. One of them's lying, and it isn't the bog.","Ingret cuts a brick in three strokes. I do it in three and a half. She says the half is talking. She's right.","I drink across the marker at Greywater some nights. Their ale you can lift. Ours you can see through. Draw your own conclusions."],"about":"I cut peat. I'm good at it, not the best. I've a Stonehold friend I drink with on the side we're not meant to cut, and a mother who'd rather I didn't. That's the whole story. Not much of one, I know.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good, with a Greywater cutter's accent; better drunk"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Esk was named twice, like every Frostgrave child, once in breath and once in the name-ice their mother carved on the day they were born, and spent the next twenty years refusing both names. The ice-name was a girl's name and the breath-name a boy's, because their mother had hoped for one and their father the other and they had agreed, being Frostgrave people, to keep both promises. Esk answered to neither. At eight they took the word for the short bog-knife the cutters carry, esk, and wouldn't answer to anything else, and the village gave in, as Hoarfen tends to with the stubborn. They were the sort of child who measured things. The distance from the meeting-house to the Greywater mile-marker, which they paced every week for a year and wrote on a slate.","schooling":"Hallgrim Tey's father taught them knots and Hallgrim himself taught them sums, and was the first grown-up to call them Esk without being asked. The Lamp Test lamps stayed dark. They learned more on the cutting line from twelve, under Ingret Moss, who taught three things: where to put your feet, when to stop, and never to say definitely.","history":"They cut peat from twelve and have never wanted to do anything else. They are fast and careful and, at thirty-seven, second only to Ingret Moss, whom they love the way you love the person you will never quite beat. At nineteen they went over the boundary on a dare and met a Greywater cutter named Orrik Settle sitting on the far side of the marker eating carry-bread, and he offered them a chip of it, and they have been drinking together on that side of the line ever since. Their mother calls it disloyal. Esk calls it ale. They have never married; they say they keep enough promises at the cutting line. The slate with the distance from the meeting-house to the marker is still on their wall, eighteen years of measurements beside it. Last spring, digging out a frozen drain, Esk found an old iron pin driven into the bog where no pin should be, exactly three strides on the Hoarfen side of the marker.","family":"Mother Runa and three older sisters, all of whom have opinions. Father dead twelve years; his cord is in the Still City wall. A standing seat on the Greywater side of the marker, next to Orrik.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"She led the Unmarked out of Stonehold with nothing and made the ugliest first hammer anyone had ever seen. Esk thinks the ugly first try is the only honest kind."},"band":{"act":"Deep Hold","realm":"Stonehold","why":"'Ask who's asking.' Orrik played it on a Greywater ale-house drum, very badly, and Esk has never heard a line that suited them better.","rv_vote":"Deep Hold. Orrik's voting Frostgrave, out of manners, so somebody has to."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning, against acting, because nobody can tell them what acting would actually mean, and they don't tie knots for fog.","edict":"Against. A law that writes down what you are has never once got Esk right."},"wants":"To find out who drove the iron pin into the bog, and when.","site":"hoarfen","town":"Hoarfen","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:glassmere":{"name":"Kari Tunn","age":50,"role":"Glassmere's trader, and quietly a guide for seers who come to stare into the ice","look":"Squat and strong, a fisher's sealskin coat patched with felt, grey hair cut short by her own knife, and very pale eyes that look at the floor more than at you.","voice":"Low and plain. Never starts a sentence she doesn't know the end of.","greet":"Halla. Kit's on the rack, fish is in the ash, and if you're here to look at the ice, I don't do that. Probably.","lines":["The mere froze last night. No army on the road, no bandits, nothing. You go out and look down, you'll see the camps under the ice. Our camps. Count the fires. There's one too many.","I've been leaving a fish at the shore where the extra fire shows. Every morning it's gone. Foxes, probably.","The post wants test-holes again. Every hole's a crack in a block Highmarch won't pay for. Tell them that. They won't hear it from me."],"about":"I sell kit, buy salvage, and keep my late grandmother's trade going on the quiet. That's leading seers out on the ice and not asking what they see. My first husband went off to Stonehold with an ice-sledge and I'm still waiting, which is a sentence I've said so often it's lost its taste.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent; seers mostly speak it to her"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good, from twenty years of Highmarch buyers"}],"childhood":"Kari's grandmother walked her out to the Underneath Sky when she was four, laid her face-down on the clear ice, and told her to hold on in case she fell into the clouds. Kari held on so hard she tore two fingernails. She remembers the clouds sliding past under her, white and slow, and the sun somehow both above and below, and her grandmother sitting cross-legged beside her eating a fish. After that she could never be frightened of the mere, only careful of it, which her grandmother said was the only right way to be about anything. The Tunns fished, but her grandmother Ase also led the seers who came down from the Still City out onto the ice, and Kari carried the rope. She learned early that seers cry a lot, and say very little, and tip well.","schooling":"Glassmere's knot-school was taught then by the fisher Haakon Brisk, who held lessons in the net-shed and failed anyone who couldn't tie a gill-net knot with their mittens on. No lamp lit for her in the Still City. She left school at eleven when her grandmother's knees went and someone had to carry the rope.","history":"She took over the seer-guiding at fifteen and the village shop at thirty, when the old trader died and nobody else could read. At twenty-three she married Sindre Tunn, a cheerful ice-cutter, in forty seconds on the mere, vowing that she would probably be there when he came back from wherever he went. At twenty-eight he took a sledge of clear blocks to the Highmarch merchants and did not come back. No word, no body, no message. Under Frostgrave law the vow holds until one of them is re-tied; Kari still wears his knot. She has led perhaps three hundred seers out to the Underneath Sky and never once asked what they saw. This winter the mere has frozen at nothing, three times, and when she looked down past her boots she saw the shore camps under the ice and counted the fires, and there was one more than there are. She has started leaving a fish in the ash at the place on the shore where the extra one shows. In the morning it's always gone.","family":"A husband, Sindre, gone twenty-two years, neither dead nor re-tied. A brother on the fishing boats. Her grandmother Ase, whose cord she re-tied in the Still City when she was twenty.","hero":{"who":"Ase Tunn, her grandmother","why":"Sixty years leading seers onto the ice and she never told one of them what she saw herself. Kari has tried to be that close-mouthed and found it costs more than it looks."},"band":{"act":"Isveig","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"She sings the saddest song in the Nine Realms perfectly and feels nothing. Kari understands that better than she'd like.","rv_vote":"Isveig. Kari votes for her every year and has never once said why."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo, because the Warm Chair should go to someone who's lost something on the water. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, and she'd like someone to start with Glassmere's ice.","edict":"Torn. She's led enough seers to know what a spark costs the one who has it, and she thinks Lightforge's lot wouldn't know a cost if it froze them."},"wants":"To know whether Sindre is alive, so that she can keep the vow or let someone re-tie it.","site":"glassmere","town":"Glassmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:glassmere":{"name":"Bjarke Holt","age":45,"role":"Glassmere's smith; forges ice-saws for the fishers and ice-augers for the Rimewatch post, and charges both","look":"Huge, round-bellied, red-faced, beard singed in odd places, laughs so hard his eyes vanish.","voice":"The loudest man on the mere and proud of it. Swears in three languages and apologises in one.","greet":"HALLA! Sorry. Halla. I don't do quiet, friend, the forge took my ears years ago.","lines":["The soldiers want test-holes. The fishers want no holes. I forge the augers and I forge the saws. Best quarrel I've ever been paid for.","The fishers say every hole cut in alarm ice is bad luck. I fell through it at seven. Came up laughing. Explain that.","Kaelis Emberwarden, now there's a councillor. Swears like a forge-hand and tells them all to their faces. Frostgrave could do with one."],"about":"I'm the smith. Augers, saws, hooks, the odd hinge. I'm the only man in Glassmere who can't stand still on the ice for a count of ten, and I've buried two dogs and no family, and I'm happy. Ask anyone. Ask them quietly, mind, I'll be listening.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, very loud"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"swearing, learned off a Flameborne tinker"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a few blunt words"}],"childhood":"Bjarke was a fat, cheerful child who fell through the mere at seven and came up laughing, which the village has never quite forgiven. He'd gone out past the rope-line on a dare from his cousins, stamped, and gone straight down into black water so cold it stopped his heart for a moment, he swears, and started it again, and he bobbed up through the same hole grinning with his hair already freezing. The fishers who hauled him out were white with fright and he couldn't understand why. His mother kept him in bed for a week. He sang the whole week. Since then the village has thought him unlucky and he has thought himself the luckiest boy ever born, and both of those have turned out to be true.","schooling":"Haakon Brisk's knot-school in the net-shed, where he was the worst at sitting still and the best at hauling the nets. At the Lamp Test, nothing lit, and he laughed so loudly in the ring that the tester asked his mother to take him outside. He apprenticed to the Glassmere forge at twelve because the smith was deaf and didn't mind him.","history":"He has been Glassmere's smith since he was twenty-five and the old deaf smith went to live with his daughter in the Still City. He forges the long ice-saws the fishers use to cut clear blocks for Highmarch and the gill-hooks and net-weights and stove-doors, and for twelve years now the Rimewatch post on the shore has paid him handsomely for heavy steel ice-augers, to cut the weekly test-holes the fishers hate. He sells to both and says so, loudly, at every argument, which somehow keeps both sides from punching him. He has never married. He had a long affair with a Flameborne tinker woman who came through every summer for nine years and taught him to swear in Kindle, and then one summer she didn't come. He keeps two dogs at a time and buries each one under the forge floor where it's warm. Last month the post-captain came to him privately and asked him to make an auger that cuts without making a sound.","family":"No wife, no children. A mother who still lives with him and still thinks he's about to fall through the ice. Cousins all round the mere, none of whom have ever been forgiven for the dare.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"Loud, funny, profane, and sits on the Concord Council and tells them so. Bjarke thinks the Council could use more people who can't do quiet."},"band":{"act":"The Bellows","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stadium rock where the crowd pumps a forge. His tinker took him to see them once at Korrhaven and he pumped so hard he broke the handle, and he has been banned from that bellows since.","rv_vote":"The Bellows, every year, at volume."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside because 'he cries and I shout, we're both honest'. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, mainly because the post on the shore would have to buy more augers.","edict":"Against, cheerfully. He'd like to see a Lightforge auditor try to register a man who can't keep quiet long enough to be audited."},"wants":"To keep both sides of the quarrel paying him for another twenty years.","site":"glassmere","town":"Glassmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:glassmere":{"name":"Ide Marn","age":33,"role":"clear-ice block-cutter for the Highmarch merchants; in love with a Stonehold sledge-driver","look":"Square-shouldered, sunburnt round the eyes from the glare off the mere, mittens patched with Stonehold wool in a dull grey-green no Frostgrave dyer would ever use.","voice":"Warm and teasing, drops into Holdspeech when she's embarrassed.","greet":"Halla. You've come from the road? Did you pass a sledge with a grey horse and a big daft man on it? No? Never mind.","lines":["Highmarch pays a crossing a block for clear ice. The ice-houses up the road pay the same for cloudy and complain. I cut what I'm paid for.","The post cut a hole last week right where we'd marked a block. Ruined. Probably on purpose. Definitely on purpose. Don't tell my mother I said definitely.","Brannoc and Hollis built Hearthward together and never married. Five hundred years and people are still arguing whether they should have. I know which side I'm on."],"about":"I cut clear blocks off the mere for the Stonehold cellar-men. The best blocks in the Nine Realms, they say, and they're right. I've a man in Highmarch. Well. Not mine. Not not mine. It's Frostgrave, we take a long time over things.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"near-fluent, with a sledge-driver's vocabulary"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"The Marn girls were sent out on the ice to sell bait to the fishers as soon as they could walk on it, and Ide, the fourth of five, was the one who always came home having given it away. She gave it to the old men because their hands shook, and to the boys because they had no coin, and once to a fox because it looked at her. Her mother despaired. Her eldest sister did her bait-selling for her for a year out of pity and then charged her for it. Ide spent most of her childhood being told she was too soft for the mere, and the rest of it proving otherwise, by learning to cut clear ice better than any of her sisters by the age of twelve, which shut them up for about a fortnight.","schooling":"Haakon Brisk's net-shed knot-school; she was good at knots and better at listening to the Highmarch buyers' wagon-men in the yard outside, which is how she picked up Holdspeech. No lamp lit. Haakon once kept her behind and told her that a soft heart and a sharp saw were a good combination in a woman, which she has never forgotten and never told her sisters.","history":"She has cut clear blocks for Highmarch since she was sixteen: long saw, careful feet, never a crack. The cellar-men of Stonehold say Glassmere ice keeps a cheese through a whole summer, and they ask for her blocks by name. At twenty-four a new driver came for the spring load, a broad, slow, very serious Highmarch man called Karnell, who wouldn't look at her for two whole loads and then on the third asked her, in careful Rime, whether she'd show him the Underneath Sky. She did. He lay down on the ice and laughed so hard he cried. They have been courting, in a way, for nine years: four loads a year, a week each time. He has asked her to come to Highmarch. She has asked him to come to Glassmere. Neither has said yes, and neither has stopped asking. Meanwhile the post's test-holes keep spoiling her best blocks, and she has twice been the fisher sent to argue it in front of the Warden.","family":"Four sisters, a mother who still thinks she gives things away, a father who fishes and says nothing. Karnell, who is not family, and is.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired and Hollis Brask","why":"Two people from two Realms who built a keep that ended a war, and never married. Ide doesn't think that's sad. She thinks it's the most hopeful thing she's ever heard, most days."},"band":{"act":"Warmstone","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Karnell sang her a Warmstone song in the back of the ice-sledge, very badly, about a hot spring. It is the only love song she has ever liked.","rv_vote":"Warmstone, and she's told Karnell to vote Frostgrave, so they'll cancel out, which seems fair."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, and has argued with Karnell about whether Stonehold would ever listen to a Frostgrave seer.","edict":"Against. Karnell says Stonehold will refuse it outright, and she'd like Frostgrave to have the nerve to do the same."},"wants":"For one of them to finally say yes, and for it not to matter which.","site":"glassmere","town":"Glassmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:glassmere:b":{"name":"Haakon Brisk","age":88,"role":"the oldest fisher on Glassmere; said this winter's First Ice Word; former knot-school master","look":"Tiny, bent nearly double, a beard down to his belt that he tucks into his coat, and a word-cord so long he wears it wound three times round his waist.","voice":"Thin, sly, full of pauses, likes to make you wait for the end of a story.","greet":"Halla. Sit down. No, on the ice, it's cleaner than my chair. Have you any grain? Not for me. For the birds. I like to watch them argue.","lines":["I said the First Ice Word this winter. On the ice, out loud. I said we'd find out what the extra fire is. Now we have to. Ha.","Taught half the mere their knots. Taught Kari Tunn, Ide Marn, Bjarke Holt. Bjarke's the only one I failed. He's done best of all of them.","I'm eighty-eight. Old Tamsin's seventy and still kneading dough on stage. Makes me want to get up. I won't. But it makes me want to."],"about":"I've fished this mere eighty winters. Taught its children forty. Been married twice, both good, both gone. Now I sit by the rope-line and the young ones bring me fish I could've caught better. I said this year's Ice Word. That's my last big job, probably.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, old-fashioned and slow"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, if a bit out of date"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a little, from a Spiritveil seer who came every winter for twenty years"}],"childhood":"Haakon's first memory is of his own feet, very small, seen from above through a foot of clear ice, which is impossible, and he has never been able to explain it to anyone's satisfaction, including his own. He was the only son of an ice-fisher and a net-maker, raised in a shore hut with a turf roof that a goat lived on, and he spent his childhood on the mere, mostly on his belly, looking down. At ten he got lost past the end of the old rope-line in a ground-mist and lay down on the ice to wait it out and was found, an hour later, laughing at the clouds underneath him. The fishers took that as a sign he was mad or lucky. He has been comfortably both.","schooling":"The knot-school then was in the old fisher Margit Lav's kitchen, among her cats, and she taught by giving each child a length of cord and a story and making them tie a knot for every promise in it. No lamp lit at the Test. He was so good at knots he took over the school from her at forty and taught it in the net-shed for forty more years.","history":"He fished from twelve, married Brynhild at twenty, buried her at forty-one after she went through the ice at the spring break-up while he was on the far shore, and did not fish for a year. Then he took over Margit Lav's school, which he says saved him, and moved it to the net-shed, and taught every child on the mere their knots for forty years, Kari Tunn and Ide Marn and Bjarke Holt among them. At fifty-five he married Sefa, a widowed net-maker, who laughed at everything he said, including the sad things; she died eight years ago, in her sleep, smiling, which he says was very like her. This winter, as the oldest fisher left, he walked to the end of the rope-line on the morning the mere first froze and said the First Ice Word out loud. He was meant to say something sensible. He said, 'We will find out what the extra fire is.' If the ice holds through the season, so must the promise. The village was not pleased. He was very pleased.","family":"Two marriages, both good, both ended in the cold: Brynhild and Sefa. A daughter in Rimewatch who wants him to move in, and a grandson in the Rimewatch post on the shore, whom he scolds about test-holes every week.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"The Warden who wouldn't cut his citadel's chain when everyone said cut it. Haakon has never cut a promise either, and he thinks there's a stubbornness in it that's better than courage."},"band":{"act":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Seventy years old and kneading dough on the stage while she sings. A seer once brought him a song-sheet. He likes that she's old and still doing the thing.","rv_vote":"Old Tamsin, because the old should stick together, and because Frostgrave's entry this year is 'too young, at forty'."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, who once ferried his first wife's body across the Lowmere for nothing. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, and he says he's been acting on it all winter, out on the ice.","edict":"Against. 'A light that shows everything shows nothing worth seeing.' He has said this at three meetings and nobody knows where he got it."},"wants":"To keep this winter's First Ice Word before the spring break-up, while he still can.","site":"glassmere","town":"Glassmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:icewatch":{"name":"Hedda Strang","age":39,"role":"trader at the top of the Glass Stair; former toll-collector and keeper of Icewatch's cord-box of unpaid crossings","look":"Tall, rope-callused, blonde hair braided tight against the wind off the falls, and a toll-collector's brass tally-ring still on her thumb though she hasn't taken a copper in years.","voice":"Brisk, numerical, cheerful in the way of someone who's been owed a lot for a long time.","greet":"Halla. Did you climb or walk round? Climbed? Good. Soup's at the cutters' hall, kit's here, and the Still City owes us for your ancestors.","lines":["Climb it, don't walk round. Four hundred steps and the river talking under you. Probably the best thing you'll do this winter. Mind the ninetieth step. It's always the ninetieth.","Three hundred years the Still City's paid the old toll rate. One knot per unpaid crossing. The cord-box weighs more than I do now.","I was born in the rope year. Hauled up the falls in a basket at three days old. My mother says I didn't cry once. I say I was saving it."],"about":"I kept the toll for fifteen years and now I keep the shop. Rope, packs, bows, bread, and the best view of the Stair in Icewatch. I can tell you to the copper what the Still City owes us. Most people stop asking after the first gold noon.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, especially for numbers"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a little, learned to read the evidential endings on Lightforge couriers' passes"}],"childhood":"Hedda was born in the rope year, the winter the stair-cutters refused to cut, and at three days old was hauled up the frozen falls in a fish-basket on a rope because her mother would not walk four days round with a newborn. The town tells it as a joke. Hedda tells it as a credential. She grew up in a toll-house at the top of the Stair, under a mother who collected coppers and a father who twisted rope, and learned early that every climber has a story about why they shouldn't have to pay. Her favourite game at six was standing at the top step with her hand out, saying 'One copper, probably,' in her mother's voice, and she made, by her own count, nine coppers that way before anyone noticed.","schooling":"The Icewatch knot-school above the rope-walk, under Knot-mistress Liva Orme, who taught the children to tie a knot for every coin and then made them unpick the lot to show the sum. Nothing lit at the Lamp Test. Hedda could tally faster than her teacher by eleven and was quietly kept back to help with the town's accounts.","history":"She took the toll from her mother at nineteen and held it fifteen years, through ninety thousand climbers by her own tally, which she can still recite. That was when she started opening the cord-box. Icewatch has kept one knot for every Still City courier and seer who crossed at the old rate since the rate was set, and nobody had counted the knots in living memory. Hedda did, over a whole winter, by lamp. She worked out the difference between the old rate and the fair one, and then what it came to over three hundred years, and wrote the sum on a slate in the cutters' hall. It was a great many gold noons. The town cheered and then did nothing, which is how she learned that a sum is not a plan. She gave up the toll-house at thirty-four to her cousin and took the shop at the top of the Stair, where the climbers come in shaking and grateful and buy whatever she puts in front of them. She married a lens-grinder, Arn Strang, at twenty-five. They have two children and an argument about whether the cord-box belongs in their kitchen.","family":"Husband Arn, a lens-grinder who works for Torstein Lisk. A son of twelve and a daughter of nine who both want to draw lots for First Foot. Her mother, the old toll-keeper, who still checks Hedda's change.","hero":{"who":"Brenna Tolle, Icewatch's stair-master","why":"She has cut the Glass Stair thirty-one times and drawn a sketch of every one, none the same. Hedda thinks that's what keeping a record ought to look like: you change it every year and you keep all of it."},"band":{"act":"The Knot-Cord Brothers","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"The only fast act Frostgrave has ever sent, and 'Forty Seconds' is the length of a Frostgrave wedding. She danced to it at her own.","rv_vote":"The Knot-Cord Brothers, and she's backing them in the Sparkbeat Cup too, though she couldn't tell you what a Sparkbeat is."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, on the reasoning that a ferryman understands what a fare is. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, and for sending the Still City the toll sum in the same sledge.","edict":"For, on paper, which surprises Icewatch. She'd like anything that audits the Still City, and she's not sure the Edict doesn't."},"wants":"To see one gold noon of the Still City's debt arrive at the top of the Stair in her lifetime.","site":"icewatch","town":"Icewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:icewatch":{"name":"Torstein Lisk","age":55,"role":"Icewatch's town smith; forges the stair-cutters' chisels and runs a pedal-driven lens-grinding mill he built himself","look":"Stringy and quick, half-moon spectacles of his own grinding, burn-freckled forearms, and a pencil behind each ear in case he loses one.","voice":"Talks in diagrams. Draws in the air. Gets so interested in the answer he forgets the question.","greet":"Halla, halla. Don't touch the treadle, it bites. Chisels on the left, lenses on the right, me in the middle, as usual.","lines":["The ninetieth step. Everyone says it's luck. It isn't. There's a current behind the ice there that runs faster than anywhere else on the falls. I've measured it nine winters running.","The Still City buys my lenses for its lamps and pays for them late. The Still City pays for everything late. It's a sort of tradition.","I was a Hearth Scholar. A Flameborne King paid for me to sit at the Conclave and I got a loaf when I arrived and a loaf when I left. Best bread I've ever had, both."],"about":"I forge chisels for the cutters and grind lenses for the lamp-tenders and, in between, I build things that nobody in Frostgrave asked for. The treadle-mill is my best. The pigeon-loft that folds up was my worst. I've a wife who thinks I'm a fool and is right about half the time.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, Conclave-taught"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"reads it well from Metalcrown engineering notes; speaks it like a book"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it slowly"}],"childhood":"Torstein took his mother's spinning wheel apart at five and put it back together with two pieces left over, and it spun better. She has never forgiven either of them. He was the third child of a rope-maker in the rope-walk under the cutters' hall, and he spent his childhood taking things to pieces: a lamp, a door-hinge, his brother's skates, the town's only clock, which was in the cutters' hall and had been wrong for ninety years and was right, briefly, for a fortnight after he had finished with it. The cutters adored him and hid their tools. He wasn't a talker. He was a boy who stared at the Glass Stair every spring while it was cut, counting the strokes, trying to work out why the steps came out different every year.","schooling":"No lamp lit at the Lamp Test, which upset nobody but Torstein. Then a letter came: the Flameborne King's Hearth Scholarship, for spark-poor children of promise, two years at the Aether Conclave on Korrhaven. A loaf on arrival. A loaf on leaving. Plain arts, letters, numbers and a great deal of the Glass Library annex, where the Metalcrown auditing students let him read their notes, which were the best on the island and which everybody else mocked.","history":"He came home at fourteen full of Metalcrown drawings and was apprenticed to the Icewatch forge, where the old smith, Bergit Holm, made him forge a thousand chisel-blanks before she let him draw a single new tool. He drew the treadle-mill at twenty-two and built it at thirty, a pedal-driven wheel that grinds ice-lenses for the Still City's lamp-tenders six times faster than by hand. The lamp-tenders were suspicious for a decade and are now dependent on it. He took the forge over when Bergit died, and every spring forges the stair-cutters a fresh set of chisels and walks behind them while they cut, with a slate, measuring. He married Saga, a river-reader's daughter, at twenty-eight; she thinks most of his inventions are daft and has sold three of them for him at a profit. Nine winters ago he began measuring the current behind the falls with a weighted line through a drilled hole, and found the ninetieth step sits right over the fastest of it. He told the stair-cutters. The stair-cutters told him they knew, and asked him not to tell anyone else, because climbers who think it is luck mind their feet.","family":"Wife Saga, sharp and practical. A grown daughter who grinds lenses with him and a son who has gone to the Still City to cut ice and never writes. Hedda Strang's husband, Arn, works his mill.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright and Idris Hollowfire","why":"The first Spark Reactor, built by two people who were told it couldn't be done. Torstein read about them in a Metalcrown student's notes in the Glass Library annex, by lamplight, and decided he'd build things too."},"band":{"act":"Long Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Garage rock from a foundry town, played on instruments the band built. He listened to their song-sheets at Korrhaven through the wall of the annex and has been humming wrong versions for forty years.","rv_vote":"Long Whistle, because Metalcrown has never had a lamp from Lightforge and he'd at least like them to have one from Icewatch."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, and has drawn a plan for what acting might look like that nobody has asked to see.","edict":"For, quietly, because he thinks measuring things is usually better than not measuring them. He has not yet thought about what it would be like to be the thing measured."},"wants":"To build something that the Still City has to pay for on time.","site":"icewatch","town":"Icewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:icewatch":{"name":"Nanna Brede","age":10,"role":"rope-maker's daughter; drew the lot for First Foot last spring and took the whole morning to climb the new Glass Stair","look":"Small, solemn, a fur hood far too big for her, mittens on strings, and a word-cord with three careful knots she's very proud of.","voice":"Slow and grave like a little old woman. Thinks before every word. Then says something unexpected.","greet":"Halla. Have you got grain? It's for the stair-birds. They nest on the landings. I know all of them by now.","lines":["I was First Foot. I waved from every landing. Forty landings. My arm was sore for a week. I'd do it again.","Olin Stair carried his sister down the Glass Stair. I'm going to carry my brother up it. Mama says don't say his name in the cutters' hall.","There's a bird on the ninetieth step that won't nest anywhere else. She's always there. I think she knows something."],"about":"I'm Nanna. I help with the rope and I count birds. I climbed the Stair first last year. It took all morning because I kept stopping to look at things. Nobody had looked at them yet, you see. I was the first.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, old-fashioned for her age"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a little, from the climbers"}],"childhood":"Nanna has always been slow, and she is not sorry about it. She walked late, talked late and then all at once in full sentences, and has spent her ten years looking at things for longer than anyone else wants to. Her brother Rafe, three years older, was born with a turned foot and has never climbed the Glass Stair; when the family goes to the Still City he walks the four days round, and Nanna has always walked it with him. Last spring she drew the lot for First Foot. She climbed the new stair alone in front of the whole town and stopped on every one of the forty landings to wave, and look at the birds, and look down, and wave again. It took her the whole morning.","schooling":"Knot-mistress Liva Orme's school above the rope-walk, where Nanna is the slowest at tying and the only child who has never once tied a knot wrong. She sat the Lamp Test in the Still City at eight; nothing lit, and she said, 'That's all right, then,' and asked if she could see the Knotwall instead. Liva has her counting the stair-birds for a tally the cutters keep.","history":"At four she sat on the top step of the Glass Stair for a whole afternoon and counted climbers, and the stair-master, Brenna Tolle, sat down beside her and counted with her, and since then Nanna has believed Brenna is the most important person in Frostgrave. At six she learned about Olin Stair from a climber who should not have told her, a Thawborn man passing through who said that Olin had carried his sister down the frozen falls on his back, step by step, and that some promises were worth putting down. Her mother heard her repeating it and went white. At eight she found the stair-bird that always nests on the ninetieth step and named her Ninety. At nine she was First Foot. Since then she has been planning, very slowly and very thoroughly, how to get Rafe up four hundred steps. She has measured him. She has measured herself. She has asked Torstein Lisk to build her a harness. He is thinking about it.","family":"Mother Kelda and father Bram, rope-makers. Brother Rafe, thirteen, clever, funny, cannot climb. A grandmother who tells her to say 'probably' more.","hero":{"who":"Olin Stair","why":"He carried his sister down the Glass Stair on his back. Nanna doesn't know or care that he was Thawborn. She just thinks he was a good brother."},"band":{"act":"House of Long Vows","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Idols who've sworn to stay together for thirty winters. She likes that they promised so far ahead, and she knows all their names in order.","rv_vote":"House of Long Vows, and she's told Rafe to vote for them too, and he says he's voting for the Knot-Cord Brothers to annoy her."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to tie. She asked her mother what the Warm Chair was and was told 'where the Speaker keeps everyone's promises warm', and she would like to sit in it once.","edict":"Doesn't know what it is. She asked, and her father said, 'A Lightforge thing,' and she has decided that is a sort of lamp."},"wants":"To carry Rafe to the top of the Glass Stair before the thaw.","site":"icewatch","town":"Icewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:icewatch:b":{"name":"Ivar Kjeld","age":63,"role":"Lowmere river-reader of Icewatch; calls the date the river closes for every town downstream","look":"Long, gaunt and grey, with a river-reader's staff of black birch marked in notches, and the stoop of a man who has spent forty years looking down at water.","voice":"Plain, slow, flat as the river in a still week. Hates metaphors. Will tell you so.","greet":"Halla. River's open, edges are thickening, closes in nine days. Probably. You'll want to be on the right bank by then.","lines":["I called the Lowmere early twice in forty years. The pickling families say twice is a habit. They test the ice with their heels now. Heels. Like goats.","Lyriel Frostwraith warned the Council and they struck her from the record. You don't strike out a river-reader for being wrong. You just stop listening. That's worse.","I was born in the Still City, Fourth Gallery. My mother never liked me climbing the Stair. Wouldn't say why. Took it with her."],"about":"I read the river. Black water freezes from the edges in. I watch how fast and call the day it closes. Every town from here to the Pale goes by what I say. Mostly I'm right. When I'm wrong it costs people their summer. I don't forget that.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, precise"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"fair; he likes its evidential endings and thinks Rime should have them"}],"childhood":"Ivar's mother would let him go anywhere in the Still City except the north courier tunnel, which led to the Glass Stair road, and she would never say why. He was raised in the Fourth Gallery, the only child of a widowed knotwright, a careful, frightened woman who tied extra knots on his cord at night and thought he didn't notice. He kept lists. He kept a tally of how many drips fell from the Hall's ceiling in the one wet week of spring, every spring, from six to sixteen, and found it was always within twenty of the year before, and was disappointed. At fourteen he read a Lightforge courier's dropped pamphlet in Clear and fell in love with a language that tells you, at the end of every sentence, how you know.","schooling":"Still City knot-school under Ebbe Rand's predecessor, a severe man named Orm Harl who made the children say aloud how they knew each thing they claimed. No lamp lit at the Test. At twenty he answered a notice for an apprentice river-reader in Icewatch, which his mother forbade, and he went anyway, the first broken word of his life.","history":"Old Sven Kjeld, Icewatch's river-reader, took him on at twenty and adopted him at thirty, and gave him his name and his staff. Ivar learned to read the Lowmere's black water: how fast the edge-ice grows by night, which wind thins it, what a bubble frozen at the margin means. He has called the river's close for thirty-five winters. He has been wrong twice. The first, twenty-two years ago, was an honest mistake, a warm wind he didn't see coming, and the pickling families down at the Farms laid their jars too soon and lost half their summer. The second, nine years ago, he called early on purpose, and has never told anyone why, and has taken the blame like a man standing under a falling roof. His mother died in the Still City while he was on the river; he got to the Re-tying late. He has climbed the Glass Stair every week for forty-three years and never once slipped, and he has never understood why that would have frightened her.","family":"Wife Dagrun, who keeps bees in an ice-house and is the only person who can make him laugh. No children. A daughter-in-all-but-name in Torstein Lisk's wife Saga, Sven Kjeld's granddaughter.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"She warned the Council and they struck her from the record. Ivar has been publicly wrong and he has been privately right, and he thinks being struck from the record for the second is the worse of the two."},"band":{"act":"Plain Speaking","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Honest country songs with no metaphors at all. He thinks it's the only music in the Nine Realms that says what it means.","rv_vote":"Plain Speaking, though he knows Lightforge wins too much, and feels bad about it every year."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, firmly, and has said so at the cutters' hall, which is as close as Ivar ever comes to shouting.","edict":"Torn. He loves the idea of saying how you know things. He hates the idea of being made to."},"wants":"For the pickling families to trust his call again, without his having to tell them why they shouldn't have.","site":"icewatch","town":"Icewatch","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:snowmere":{"name":"Aud Tarn","age":41,"role":"Snowmere's trader; sells brooms, kit and snow-birch wine; the foundling the whole village still argues about","look":"Dark-haired among a village of fair ones, broad-faced, a broom-straw fringe of hair she cuts herself, and a sweeper's mittens worn soft with years of other people's handling.","voice":"Warm, amused, quick to take the joke about herself before you can make it.","greet":"Halla. Brooms, bread, packs, arrows, wine. I'm the cheat's daughter, if anyone's told you. They usually have.","lines":["Look once, if you have to. Don't stand there. The ones who stand there start talking back to it. Say probably, and keep it, that's the rule out on the Fields. Probably that's you in the glass.","The Court pays the sweepers late. The visitors pay by the mirror, on time. You can guess who the village is nicer to.","My mother saw me in a mirror before she ever found me. Half of Snowmere says that's cheating fate. The other half says it's just good planning."],"about":"I was found in a herder's shed in a basket of grey wool. My mother, Ylva Tarn, swept the Line, and she'd seen herself in a mirror that winter with a child she didn't have. In spring she had one. Me. I've spent forty years being a village argument. I run the shop. It's easier than sweeping and you get to sit down.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, from the visitors"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a little, because she was told once she might be from the far side of the Pale and went and learned some"}],"childhood":"Aud has been the subject of a village argument since before she could walk, and by six she could recite both sides. One side: her mother, Ylva, looked into a mirror on the Sweepers' Line one winter, saw herself grey and smiling with a child she didn't have, and in spring went out and found one, which is cheating, because a mirror only shows what might be, and you mustn't go making it be. Other side: Ylva found a baby crying in a herder's shed in a basket of grey wool, and what was she supposed to do, leave it? Aud grew up thinking the argument was a game, played every Thaw over the soup. Then at nine she heard old Bodvar Sorrel say, not laughing, that the child in the mirror might not have been her at all.","schooling":"Snowmere's knot-school is held in the broom-shed by whichever sweeper has a broken leg that winter; Aud was taught mostly by a sweeper called Hjalmar Vik, who taught every lesson with a broom in his hands, sweeping, so the children wouldn't feel watched. Nothing lit for her at the Lamp Test, and the village argued about that too.","history":"She swept the Line beside her mother from twelve to twenty-six. She was good at it but not happy in it; she disliked the mirrors, which she found looked at her too long, and she liked the visitors, the frightened ones especially, and walking them back to the village afterwards and pouring them wine. When the old trader died, Aud took the shop. She was engaged at twenty-nine to a goat-herd's son, and on the morning of the wedding, all forty seconds of it waiting, she couldn't, and walked away, and confessed it the next Thaw over the soup; the village forgave her slowly. Her mother Ylva died at seventy, five winters ago. Aud re-tied her cord in the Knotwall, the person Ylva had kept the most promises to, and spent the walk home realising she'd never once asked her mother if the woman in the mirror had looked happy because of the child, or in spite of it.","family":"Her mother Ylva, five winters dead. No husband, no children, no regrets she'll admit to. Gunnhild Sorrel, her mother's oldest friend, who brings her goat cheese and refuses to talk about the past.","hero":{"who":"Ylva Tarn, her mother","why":"She saw something in a mirror and went out and made it happen, and then raised the child she found as if it had been the plan all along. Aud thinks that's either the bravest thing anyone in Snowmere has done or the most stubborn, and has stopped minding which."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Forty-year love songs from people who live long enough to mean it. Aud is forty-one and has never been in love for forty days, and she finds them extremely funny and then cries in the shop.","rv_vote":"Decades, though she knows they'll come nowhere."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning she ties against acting, which surprises people: 'I'm what happens when you act on what you see in the glass. Ask yourself if you're ready for that.'","edict":"Torn. She doesn't like Lightforge, but she'd quite like a register that told her where she came from."},"wants":"To find out whether the child in her mother's mirror was her.","site":"snowmere","town":"Snowmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:snowmere":{"name":"Rolf Ennes","age":30,"role":"Snowmere's smith; forges broom-irons, skates and goat-bells, and blackens every blade so nothing in the village can show a face","look":"Short and dark-bearded, built like a barrel, black soot worked into every line of his hands, and not one shiny thing on him, not a buckle, not a button.","voice":"Quiet, wry, says the opposite of what he means and waits to see if you notice.","greet":"Halla. Nothing in here shines. Don't take it personally. It's for everyone's birthday.","lines":["Every blade I sell, I blacken. Snowmere can't look at a polished blade on their birthday. So nobody in Snowmere has a polished blade. Simple.","I broke a mirror on the Line when I was eleven. Threw a stone. Swept a whole year for it. Best year of my life, probably. Don't tell anyone.","The seers ride out, read the Fields, complain about our footprints. I made a broom-iron for a seer once. She said it spoilt her reading. It was a broom."],"about":"I'm the smith. Broom-irons, skates, bells, the odd knife, black as the inside of a boot. I went south for four years and learned the blackening. Came home. Never said where I'd been. That's still true.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fair, and he uses it absent-mindedly at the anvil"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Rolf was the boy who broke the mirror. He was eleven and had been told for a whole winter that the mirrors on the Sweepers' Line showed you some way you might turn out, and he had looked, and then he had picked up a stone and thrown it as hard as he could, and the ice-mirror cracked from top to bottom with a sound like a lake going. The village sent word to the Prophet Court. The Court sent a seer, who took away the pieces in a felt bag and said nothing to anyone, and the village set Rolf to sweep the Line, every day, for a whole year, as payment. By the end of the year he'd learned every sweeper's name and joke, and he liked them all, and he never told anyone what he'd seen.","schooling":"He was taught in the broom-shed by the sweeper Hjalmar Vik, who swept while he taught, and who was the one who walked beside Rolf every day of his year of punishment and never once asked about the mirror. No lamp lit at the Test. He left at thirteen for the forge.","history":"He apprenticed to Snowmere's old smith, a woman named Gro, and at twenty took a long-wagon south, which nobody in Snowmere does, and went over the Realm Bridge into Shadowdeep with a letter from a Murmur trader who'd bought goat-bells from Gro for years. He spent four years at a forge on the rope-bridges of Murmur, among smiths who blackened every blade they made so it would never catch a light, and learned the blackening, and learned hand-cant, and learned not to say his full name. He came home at twenty-four with a cart of tools and a habit of silence, and when Gro retired he took the forge. He makes the iron collars that bind Snowmere's brooms, skates for the goat-children, bells for the last few goats and the occasional blade. Every one is black. The village thinks he's being kind, for the Blind Birthdays, and he lets them. He is unmarried and has a long, loving correspondence in hand-cant drawings with a Murmur smith whose half-name is Osk.","family":"Parents both sweepers, both alive, both still faintly embarrassed about the mirror. A younger sister who guides visitors. Osk, in Murmur, who is not family, yet.","hero":{"who":"Veilrend","why":"Shadowdeep's First Blade, whose face nobody knows. Rolf saw what he'd become in a mirror once and threw a stone at it, and he thinks a man who has no face to show is a man who can still choose."},"band":{"act":"Lights Out","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Dark techno played in total darkness. He went to a Lights Out night in Murmur and, for the first time in his life, couldn't see a single reflection anywhere, and danced till dawn.","rv_vote":"Lights Out. They'll come second. They always come second. He'll be glad."},"vote":{"local":"Doesn't tie at the Warm Chair. Says knots are for people who are sure. On Lyriel's warning he says, 'She saw something. Someone should look,' and leaves it there.","edict":"Against. He spent four years with people who know what it means to be written down and he came home with their opinion."},"wants":"For Osk to come north, just once, and see the Fields under snow.","site":"snowmere","town":"Snowmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:snowmere":{"name":"Gunnhild Sorrel","age":67,"role":"one of Snowmere's last goat-herds; refuses to sweep, refuses to guide, refuses most things","look":"Thickset, apple-cheeked, iron-grey hair under a goat-felt hat with the ears still on, and a crook taller than she is.","voice":"Gruff, funny without trying, ends every complaint with a fact about goats.","greet":"Halla. If you're here to look at the mirrors, they're that way, and so's the trouble. If you've grain, the goats are this way. Better company.","lines":["My family kept goats here eleven generations before anybody thought to sweep a mirror. Now there's more brooms than goats. I've counted. Fourteen goats. Two hundred and six brooms.","My husband sweeps. Forty-one years married. He took up the broom at fifty. I said, Bodvar, I married a herder. He said, Probably.","The Court's seers ride out, read for days, complain about footprints. Goats leave footprints. Nobody complains about goats."],"about":"I keep goats. The village is full of people who used to keep goats and now keep brooms. I don't hold it against them. I hold it against the mirrors. Fourteen goats, one husband, no patience. That's me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, very broad"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"stubbornly bad"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"one Kept Coals song, learned off a Flameborne goat-buyer, sung at full volume"}],"childhood":"Gunnhild's family had kept goats on the edge of the Mirror Fields for eleven generations before anyone thought to sweep a mirror, and she has never let the village forget it. She was the youngest of six herders' children in a long turf house where the goats came in at night, and was entirely happy. In those days the village had two hundred goats and nine brooms. She used to drive the herd along the edge of the Fields and the mirrors would show the goats back to themselves, and the goats did not care at all, and Gunnhild thought that was the finest thing about goats. At ten she watched her best friend Ylva look into a mirror for the first time and come away crying, and she decided then that she'd never look properly, and she hasn't.","schooling":"The broom-shed school, though in her day it was a goat-shed school, taught by an old herder named Sorrel Gisle, who was later her father-in-law and whom she adored. He taught counting by goats and knots by tethers. No lamp lit at the Test. She learned everything else on the hills.","history":"She married Bodvar Sorrel, a herder's son, at twenty-six, in thirty-five seconds, beside the goat-pens. They had three children and herded together for twenty-four years while, one by one, the other families sold their goats and took up brooms, because the Prophet Court paid by the season and goats only paid by the cheese. At fifty Bodvar sold half their herd and joined the sweepers. Gunnhild didn't speak to him for most of a winter. Then she started taking him his dinner out on the Line in a pot, every day, so she could tell him what she thought of it, and he started saving the best bit of his day to tell her, and they have been more in love since than before, which annoys her. She was Ylva Tarn's best friend for sixty years. She was standing beside Ylva on the Line the winter Ylva saw the child in the mirror, and she has never told anyone what she saw, including Ylva's daughter Aud.","family":"Husband Bodvar, sweeper. Three grown children, two sweepers and one, to her relief, in the Still City cutting ice. Ylva Tarn's daughter Aud, whom she treats as a fourth.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"She lit the First Hearth in her own kitchen and nobody ever knelt to her. Gunnhild likes a famous person who was just doing the cooking."},"band":{"act":"The Kept Coals","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stomp-folk bakers who sing 'Keep Your Coal Lit'. A Flameborne goat-buyer sang it at her table once and she stamped along so hard the cheese fell off the shelf.","rv_vote":"The Kept Coals, because they sound like people who've done a day's work."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning she ties against: 'You look at the future too long, it looks back. I've watched it happen to half my village.'","edict":"Against. 'Anybody wants to count my sparks can count my goats first. Fourteen. Done.'"},"wants":"To leave the herd to someone in Snowmere who isn't holding a broom.","site":"snowmere","town":"Snowmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:snowmere:b":{"name":"Eilif Nord","age":26,"role":"guide through the Mirror Fields; former sweeper; has a mirror he talks to","look":"Slight and fair, a long sweeper's scarf wound four times round his neck, a habit of walking backwards in his own footprints, and frost always in his eyebrows.","voice":"Light, wandering, a storyteller's rhythm, trails off and then comes back with the ending a minute later.","greet":"Halla. Guide? I'm the cheapest. Not the best. Tove Arn's the best. But I'm the one who'll tell you what to say if it talks back.","lines":["The seers say our footprints spoil the readings. So I walk backwards in my own prints out and in. They still complain. I think they just like complaining.","There's a mirror out past the third bend that I talk to. Every morning. Don't look at me like that. It started it.","I went to Lamp Years in Icewatch. The teacher read us 'The Lamp That Leaned' every Thawday. That's the only reason I can read."],"about":"I guide visitors through the Fields. I used to sweep with Tove Arn until I stood too long in front of one mirror and it started answering, and Tove said that's when you stop sweeping and start guiding. I'm a Half-Spark, so I get a little frost. It's fine. It's mostly fine.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent; the visitors talk"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"reads it slowly; can recite all of 'The Lamp That Leaned' in the original, badly"}],"childhood":"Eilif's father sold the family goats when Eilif was five and bought nine brooms, and Eilif cried for a week over a nanny goat called Bracken until his father took him out on the Sweepers' Line to show him why. Eilif saw himself in the first mirror, grown and laughing with his arm round a man he didn't know, and stopped crying at once and asked to see the next one. He has been asking to see the next one ever since. He was a dreamy, chattering child who told stories to the mirrors and was told off for it, and who got lost on his first Blind Birthday by letting go of his sister's shoulder to follow a sound, and was found three hours later sitting in a drift with his eyes shut, talking happily to nobody.","schooling":"At the Lamp Test the Chrona lamp gave a faint shiver of light: Half-Spark. Three Lamp Years in Icewatch under Lamp-reader Saga Hollen, a Spiritveil-trained woman who read the class Ithiel the Copyist's 'The Lamp That Leaned' every Thawday in the original Lanthic and then in Rime. He couldn't read until she did. No Korrhaven for a Half-Spark from the Fields.","history":"He came back to Snowmere at twelve and swept the Line under Tove Arn, who taught him never to stop moving the broom. He was good. He was talkative. At nineteen, in a hard frost, he stopped moving the broom in front of a tall mirror past the third bend, because the man in it, older, grey at the brows, had stopped too. Eilif said, 'Halla.' The man in the mirror said it a heartbeat before he did. Tove found him there at dusk, still talking, and took the broom off him. Since then he has guided instead, walking visitors through the Fields in his own backward footprints to spare the seers' readings, and every morning before the first visitor he walks to the third bend and talks to the mirror for exactly as long as it takes the frost to form in his eyebrows. He is not in love with anyone yet. He is waiting, quite cheerfully, for the man he saw at five.","family":"Father and mother, both sweepers. An older sister, Rakel, who still won't let go of his shoulder on Blind Birthdays. Several cousins in the Still City who think he's a bit simple and are wrong.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"'The Lamp That Leaned' was the first thing he ever read, and he thinks Ithiel understood that something can show you a version of yourself and still leave you free to be the other one."},"band":{"act":"Echo Bleed","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A DJ who samples dead singers into new songs. 'Somebody Else's Summer' is, Eilif says, what talking to the third-bend mirror sounds like.","rv_vote":"Echo Bleed, and in the Sparkbeat Cup too."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, because he has spent seven years being told by a mirror what's coming and has found it's easier to bear if you do something about it.","edict":"For, which baffles Snowmere. He thinks a register would at least make someone in Meridian notice the Half-Sparks who stand too long in front of things."},"wants":"To meet the man he saw in the first mirror, and to recognise him.","site":"snowmere","town":"Snowmere","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:stillcity_vf1":{"name":"Thyra Bolle","age":36,"role":"trader in mammoth felt, wool and word-cord yarn at the herd-steading; also sells kit to the cutting gangs","look":"Plump, pink-cheeked, wrapped in so much undyed mammoth felt she looks like a small haystack, with a Spiritveil willow-pin holding her hair.","voice":"Soft, unhurried even by Frostgrave standards, finishes everything with a little nod as if agreeing with herself.","greet":"Halla. Felt, wool, cord-yarn, packs. Take your time. Everyone does, out here. You won't have a choice.","lines":["Stay a night by the barn and you'll get up rested and the sun not moved. Don't stay a week, though. Ask Halvor Dun what a week costs.","A Still City cord spun from mammoth wool is supposed to be unbreakable. Supposed to be. I sell a great many of them.","I lived three summers in Willowmere learning to felt from a woman who was four hundred years old. She said I was quick. Nobody's ever said that to me here."],"about":"I sell what the herd grows. Felt for boots, wool for cords, and yarn for the knotwrights in the city. I went south once and came back. The south is lovely. It's just very fast.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, slow"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"good, from three summers in Willowmere; she dreams in it sometimes"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"}],"childhood":"Thyra was born in the loft of the Long Barn in the coldest week of a cold winter, with forty mammoths breathing steam below her, and the midwife said afterwards it was the longest labour she had ever sat through and the shortest she had ever counted. Thyra grew up slow. Everyone at the steading does, a little, and everyone who comes from outside says so. She was a dreamy, comfortable child who could watch wool being carded for a whole morning and come away feeling it had been an hour, and who learned to spin before she could read because her grandmother put a spindle in her hand to stop her sucking her fingers. Her first word-cord, spun from her first Combing wad, was lumpy and thick and has never broken. She still wears it.","schooling":"The steading's knot-school is held in the Long Barn loft in winter, for the warmth, by whoever is too old to haul. Thyra was taught by old Brede Hamm, who could tie a knot so slowly you could see every turn of it and called that the only way to learn. No lamp lit at the Lamp Test, which took place on a day that, she says, felt much shorter in the Still City than at home.","history":"At twenty a Spiritveil wool-buyer came up the Pale road for mammoth felt and took Thyra south with the sledge to learn felting from her aunt in Willowmere, the summer archive-city of willow bridges. The aunt was four hundred years old and looked thirty, and taught Thyra a way of felting in long overlapping sheets that keeps the warmth in twice as well. Thyra stayed three summers. She learned Lanthic, ate a great many honey cakes, saw Vaelora Lanthys cross a willow bridge once, very quietly, with no one beside her, and fell gently in love with a copyist who forgot her within a decade, which is what she had been told would happen. She came home at twenty-three and opened the steading shop with the new felting, and now sells felt to Rimewatch, wool to the city, and cord-yarn to the knotwrights by the sack. The yarn sells faster than the herd can grow it. She married a drover, Lasse Bolle, at twenty-six. They have one son, who is very slow and very happy.","family":"Husband Lasse, a drover who sings to the mammoths. Son Orri, eight. Her grandmother, who taught her to spin and now can't. A Willowmere aunt-in-craft who sends a honey cake north every Long Evening.","hero":{"who":"Vaelora Lanthys","why":"Nine hundred years old and she chose silence. Thyra saw her once on a willow bridge, alone and unhurried, and thought it was the first time she'd seen someone who lived at the herd's pace by choice."},"band":{"act":"Mist-Pear","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Wedding R&B from Spiritveil. The copyist she loved in Willowmere sang Mist-Pear songs to her across the river at night, and she plays them on the barn whistle when no one's in the loft.","rv_vote":"Mist-Pear, out of sentiment, and she'll tell no one."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside because he takes his time. On Lyriel's warning, she ties no knot: 'A warning wants hurrying. I've never been able to hurry.'","edict":"Against, gently. She's lived among Spiritveil people and agrees with them that someone should warn Lightforge before they do something they can't remember why they did."},"wants":"For the herd to grow wool fast enough to keep up with the cords she's promised.","site":"stillcity_vf1","town":"The Still City Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:stillcity_vf1":{"name":"Gorm Lyng","age":48,"role":"steading smith; forges mammoth harness-chain, the great Combing combs and ice-hooks; leader of the herders against night-hauling","look":"Short, enormously broad, a black beard going grey in two stripes, and a hauling-harness scar across his chest he got as a boy pulling sledges for his father's gang.","voice":"Gravelly, emphatic, bangs a fist lightly on whatever's nearest to make a point.","greet":"Halla. If you're from the cutting gangs, I'm closed. If you're not, I'm open. Which?","lines":["The cutters hire our herd to haul and work it past dark. A Chrona Mammoth worked tired goes slow for everyone near it. A whole gang once lost a day and never noticed. My father's gang.","One shift, one vote. Even you. Tibb Anvil said that a thousand years ago in Metalcrown. I put it on the barn door. In Rime. Nobody here's laughed yet.","The Combing comb's my best work. Teeth as long as your hand. Twenty of us at once on one beast. She stands there and lets you. You've never felt anything like it."],"about":"I grew up hauling ice for the gangs in the city. Married a herder's daughter and crossed over. Now I make the harness and tell the gangs no. I like the second part more than I should.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, a cutter's rough version"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a handful of union words from an old Metalcrown pamphlet he's read to pieces"}],"childhood":"Gorm grew up on the other side of this quarrel. His father was a gang-boss on the Still City's ice-cutting crews, a hard, fair, tired man who ran forty cutters and hired the steading's herd every winter to drag the blocks, and from eight Gorm pulled hand-sledges in the tunnels beside the men. He was strong early, and loud, and was the boy the cutters sent for when a block stuck. At eleven he was hauling beside the herd on a long night shift when the whole gang, his father included, came out of the cutting at what they thought was midnight and found it was the following evening. His father checked the tally-cord twice. There was a whole day gone that no one remembered. His father never spoke about it, and hired the herd again the next week.","schooling":"The cutters' children had a knot-school in a side tunnel off the gang hall, taught by a one-handed cutter called Asmund Rye, who taught them the knots for wages, for contracts and for injuries, in that order. No lamp lit. At fourteen Gorm found a crumbling Metalcrown pamphlet in a salvage bin about the old Foundry Commons and taught himself enough Shiftcant to read it.","history":"He hauled for the gangs until twenty-two and then, on a winter haul to the steading, fell in love with a herder's daughter, Inge, who told him to his face that the gangs were killing the herd. He thought about it for a year and agreed, and married her, and crossed over, which his father considered treachery and forgave only on his deathbed. Gorm took over the steading forge from Inge's uncle. He forges the harness-chain the mammoths haul in, light and very strong, and the great Combing combs, and ice-hooks for the drovers. When the gangs began working the herd past dark again six winters ago, Gorm called a meeting in the Long Barn and every herding family tied a knot together, one family, one knot, to refuse any haul that ran after sunset. He painted Tibb Anvil's words on the barn door. The gangs have hired Stonehold oxen twice since, which are slow in the ordinary way, and come back twice.","family":"Wife Inge, a herder who runs half the steading. Three children who all comb the herd at the Combing. His father, dead nine years, who said 'you were right' on the last day and nothing else.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"The last foreman of the Foundry Commons, where every shift had a vote. Gorm read about him in a pamphlet in a salvage bin at fourteen and has been trying to run a herders' strike ever since."},"band":{"act":"The Stampers","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Amapiano where the crowd's applause is the beat. He likes music that only works when everybody joins in.","rv_vote":"The Stampers, because 'you can't win alone and they know it'."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, a working man. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, and thinks the Court should ask the herders first, since they're the only people in Frostgrave who already live with time running wrong.","edict":"Against. 'Count the sparks, count the hands, it starts the same way, with someone deciding which ones matter.'"},"wants":"To get the cutting gangs to sign a knot-contract that the herd never hauls after dark.","site":"stillcity_vf1","town":"The Still City Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:stillcity_vf1":{"name":"Steinar Vall","age":19,"role":"young drover at the herd-steading; grandson of Herd-mother Edda Vall; desperate to leave","look":"Long-legged and restless, fair stubble he's proud of, a Stormreach chain-link earring bought from a travelling pedlar, and boots worn through at the heel from pacing.","voice":"Fast, impatient, interrupts himself, the only person at the steading who taps his foot.","greet":"Halla. Do you come from somewhere fast? Anywhere? Tell me about it. Quickly. Sorry. Quickly.","lines":["Near the herd you get an extra hour a day. Everyone here thinks that's a blessing. I'm nineteen. I'm going to be nineteen for ever.","Gran swears the old bull winks at her. It does. At me too. I think it's laughing.","Seraphine Skyrend stood up in the Free Assembly and shouted down the Edict. Shouted. In public. Here, we'd tie a knot about it next spring."],"about":"I drive the herd. I'm good at it. I hate it. I'm going to Stormreach to ride skyrays, as soon as I've saved enough and Gran stops looking at me like that. Probably. No. Definitely. I'm practising.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few dozen words, learned from a cliff-honey trader, all of them shouted"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair, improving fast on purpose"}],"childhood":"His grandmother says Steinar was the only calf in the Long Barn who never learned to stand still, and she means him. He was born in the loft like half the steading and spent his first years being carried round the herd on someone's hip, and the moment he could walk he walked away from it, out to the road, to watch sledges go by fast. He was the child who timed things. He counted his heartbeats near the herd and far from it and found, at nine, that they were different, and told everyone, and everyone said, 'Yes, Steinar, we know.' At ten a Stormreach cliff-honey trader came through with a tame skyray folded on the sledge, and opened its wings for the children in the yard, and Steinar did not sleep for three nights.","schooling":"The Long Barn loft knot-school, under old Brede Hamm, who tied knots so slowly that Steinar used to scream into a sack of wool at the end of lessons. No lamp lit. He learned Gale from the honey trader, who came twice a year, by following him round the yard asking 'What's that called?' until the man began to answer.","history":"He has been driving the herd since fourteen, hauling for the cutting gangs, leading the mammoths to winter forage and back, and he is good at it in the way a person is good at a thing they've been made to do since before they could choose. He knows every beast by its tread, like his grandmother. He doesn't want to. At sixteen he tried to walk to the Bridge with a pack and got as far as Hoarfen, where Oddny Reed fed him eel and sent a message back, and his grandmother came to get him on the old bull's back, very slowly, without a word. At eighteen he started saving coppers in a felt boot. This winter a chain-yard recruiter from Stormreach came through looking for strong drovers, and Steinar talked to him for an hour by the barn, very fast, in bad Gale.","family":"Grandmother Edda Vall, the herd-mother, who raised him and whom he loves and would like to get away from. Parents both drovers, both content. A little sister, Eir, who wants to inherit the herd and thinks Steinar is mad.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend","why":"The Warden of Skyrend, who speaks for the Free Assembly and opposed the Edict out loud. To Steinar she's the proof that a person can just stand up and shout and mean it."},"band":{"act":"Updraft","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Trance from the sky-citadels. 'Freefall' is the fastest song he's ever heard, and he plays it in his head while driving the slowest animal in the Nine Realms.","rv_vote":"Updraft, in the Sparkbeat Cup and the main contest. Frostgrave can have his lamp when it learns to hurry."},"vote":{"local":"Tied his first knot at the Warm Chair this year, for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting: 'Anything. Do anything. Just do it this year.'","edict":"Against, with Stormreach. He doesn't really know what it does, but anything Seraphine Skyrend shouts against is enough for him."},"wants":"To get on a skyray before he's twenty.","site":"stillcity_vf1","town":"The Still City Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:stillcity_vf1:b":{"name":"Halvor Dun","age":44,"role":"mammoth drover and former Still City ice-cutter; the man who stayed a week by the barn","look":"Ordinary in every particular: medium height, brown beard, a cutter's grey felt coat, and a small notched stick on a cord round his neck that he touches when he's nervous.","voice":"Careful, plain, apologetic. Says things exactly and then checks you understood.","greet":"Halla. I'll be plain. If you're staying by the barn, count the days on a stick. It doesn't help. But count them.","lines":["I stayed a week by the barn. When I got home my girls were taller and my wife had cut her hair. It was four months. Thyra tells everyone. I don't mind. It's true.","I say things plainly now. I used to think I'd have time to say them later. Arcthen Solbrand said say it plainly. He was right.","The Tether Sisters sing 'Cut the Chain'. I've never cut a chain in my life. I like it anyway."],"about":"I cut ice in the city twenty years. Nothing happened. Then I hurt my back and stayed by the herd to mend and came home four months late. Now I drive the mammoths and walk home every night, an hour each way, so I never stay too long. It's a dull life. I chose it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"three phrases from a Lightforge courier, including 'say it plainly'"}],"childhood":"Nothing whatever happened to Halvor Dun until he was thirty-nine, and he would be the first to tell you. He was born in the Still City's Seventh Gallery to two ice-cutters, the middle of five children, and was neither the cleverest nor the slowest, neither the strongest nor the weakest, neither loved best nor least. He had a happy, cold, ordinary childhood of keeping soup, sledding on the tunnel ramps, being told off for shouting in the Hall, and learning the cutters' songs. His great childhood adventure was getting lost for an afternoon in the Lower Galleries, and he was found by a lamp-tender who walked him home without a word. He was good, and steady, and kind to his little sister, and at the time he thought that was enough. Mostly, it was.","schooling":"The Seventh Gallery knot-school, under a mild man named Kalle Ves who never raised his voice and whose only memorable act was keeping the whole class an hour late once to watch the drip from the ceiling freeze into an icicle. No lamp lit. He learned his knots, his sums and his cutting songs and left at twelve for the gangs.","history":"He cut ice for the city from twelve to thirty-nine, in Gorm Lyng's father's gang and then another, with a steady hand and no ambitions. He married Freydis, a lamp-tender's daughter, at twenty-four, and they had two girls. At thirty-nine he wrenched his back hauling a block, and the gang sent him to recover at the herd-steading, where the cold is softer and the days are long, to lie in the loft above the Long Barn for a week. He slept beautifully. He woke rested each morning with the sun not moved. When he walked home at the end of the week, his elder girl was a head taller, his wife had cut her hair short, and the Speaker had changed in the Warm Chair. It had been four months. Freydis had tied a knot for him being dead. He has never got over it and never will, and he decided, the night he got home, that he would never again assume there would be time to say a thing. He now drives the herd by day and walks home every night, an hour each way, so he never sleeps near it.","family":"Wife Freydis, who untied the knot for his being dead and kept the cord. Two daughters, fifteen and twelve, who have not forgiven him and adore him. Four siblings in the Seventh Gallery, all cutters.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"'Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.' Halvor came home to four months he'd missed, and since then he says every true thing the day he thinks it."},"band":{"act":"The Tether Sisters","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Chain-yard harmony folk. His younger girl learned 'Cut the Chain' while he was gone, and sang it to him the night he got back, and he had never heard it before.","rv_vote":"The Tether Sisters, for his girl."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, whom he didn't vote for the first time because he was asleep in a barn. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, now, this season: 'You don't get the time back.'","edict":"Torn. He likes the idea of things being said plainly. He doesn't like anyone else deciding which things."},"wants":"For his daughters to stop counting the months.","site":"stillcity_vf1","town":"The Still City Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:stillcity_vf2":{"name":"Mette Saarn","age":55,"role":"trader of the ice-house village; sells greens, seedlings, kit; negotiates the clear-ice price with Glassmere","look":"Tall and thin, green-stained fingers, a felt apron full of seed-packets, and the cool appraising look of a woman who has priced everything in the room before you finished saying halla.","voice":"Sharp, tidy, ironic. Never wastes a word, and charges for the extra ones.","greet":"Halla. Greens, seeds, packs, bows. If you're from Glassmere, the price of a sledge of cloudy ice is still the price of a sledge of cloudy ice.","lines":["Fresh leaves in the dead of winter, in Frostgrave. The Speaker's table eats our kale. Of course the Seers foresaw it. They foresaw it after we'd done it.","Glassmere sells its best blocks to Stonehold and sends us the cloudy ones at the clear price. I've been arguing it twenty years. It's a hobby now.","My great-grandmother Ragna started this with one pane and a peat-bank because she couldn't pay the knotwright's debts. I think about her every time I cut ice."],"about":"I run the shop and the ice-price, and I'm descended from the widow who started all this, which I'm told I mention too often. The slope feeds the capital every week of the year. Someone has to sell it. I'm good at it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, crisp"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good business Holdspeech, rude but accurate"}],"childhood":"Mette was raised on the story of a widow and a pane of ice, told so often that by seven she could tell it backwards, and once did, for a bet, at the Fallen Day. The widow was Ragna Hult, her great-grandmother, a knotwright's wife left with debts and no trade, who banked peat against a south slope, laid a pane of clear lake-ice over a row of kale, and lived. Everyone in the village is descended from someone who copied her. Mette was descended from Ragna herself, and was not allowed to forget it, and didn't want to. At eight she sold a basket of her parsley to a lamp-house steward on the road and walked home with a crossing in her mitten and never looked back.","schooling":"The village knot-school in the old pane-shed, under Knot-mistress Gudrun Fenn, Aslo Fenn's aunt, who made them tie the knots for every crop and season and could not tell kale from cabbage. No lamp lit at the Test. Mette learned the money side at her father's elbow, counting kale by the crate and crossings by the purse, and never once let him round down.","history":"She took the village's trade over at thirty from an uncle who was too kind to haggle, and has run it with a tight hand since. She sells greens and herbs to the Still City kitchens and lamp-houses every week, seedlings in spring, sour apples in autumn, and goes to the Speaker's own kitchen door once a month with the kale. She negotiates the clear-ice price with Glassmere every autumn and has done for twenty years, and she has never once got a sledge of clear ice at a cloudy price, and has never stopped trying. She married a pane-cutter, Ottil Saarn, at twenty-two; he died young, of a fever, at thirty-one, and she has not remarried and does not intend to. She raised two sons, who now cut panes. Three years ago she found a way round Glassmere: buying back, through a Highmarch cellar-man, the summer-cellar ice Stonehold had already bought from Glassmere, at the end of the season, when it's still clear and nobody wants it.","family":"Two sons, both pane-cutters. Her late husband Ottil. A village full of cousins descended from people who copied her great-grandmother, all of whom she treats as staff.","hero":{"who":"Ragna Hult, her great-grandmother","why":"Left with debts and a slope, she laid one pane of ice over a row of kale and fed a city. Mette thinks that's what a hero looks like: a widow doing sums in the cold."},"band":{"act":"Sunwarm","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Summer Latin pop from the land of endless noon. She finds it ridiculous and plays it in the ice-houses anyway, because the kale grows better to it. She has measured.","rv_vote":"Sunwarm, though she knows Lightforge will win anyway, and resents it."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, who added a crate of her kale to the Long Pot when he sat. On Lyriel's warning she ties against acting: 'The Seers foresee things after we've done them. Let's do them and see.'","edict":"Against, briskly. A Lightforge audit of anyone's books is an audit she'd rather not have."},"wants":"One sledge of truly clear Glassmere ice at the cloudy price, before she dies.","site":"stillcity_vf2","town":"The Still City Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:stillcity_vf2":{"name":"Asta Krog","age":27,"role":"ice-house village smith; forges pane-saws, glazing frames and heating grates; half Lightforge","look":"Fine-boned and dark-eyed, close-cropped black hair, a Lightforge witness-coin pierced on a cord at her throat next to a Rime word-cord, and a burn on her chin she's proud of.","voice":"Precise, slightly formal, ends sentences with how she knows the thing, out of a habit she's trying to break.","greet":"Halla. Aure. I say both, I'm both. What needs mending? You can tell me plainly; I'll know if you don't.","lines":["The Fallen Day's coming. Everyone breaks one pane on purpose and cuts a new one. I forge the saws. Busiest week of my year. I've seen it.","My father's in Meridian. His lot came up for the Assize. He writes that the Edict is just. He writes it in Clear, with the ending that means he saw it himself. He didn't.","Verdict structure every song like a trial. Prosecution, defence, judgement. I find it calming. I don't know what that says about me."],"about":"I'm the smith here. Saws, frames, grates. My father's a Lightforge lens-merchant and my mother's a pane-cutter, and I grew up with a Day-Book in one hand and a word-cord in the other. They don't always agree. I've been trying to work out which one to keep.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"fluent, a Meridian accent from her father"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Asta grew up keeping two records: the Day-Book her father made her write each evening in Clear, what she had done that day and nothing hidden, and the word-cord her mother tied for her in Rime, a knot for every promise kept. By nine she had noticed they did not always agree. On the day she broke her cousin's sled and mended it before anyone knew, the Day-Book said, plainly, 'I broke Sigge's sled and mended it,' and the cord had no knot for it, because she had never promised anything. On the day she promised to look after the baby and fell asleep, the cord was bare and the Day-Book said 'I slept'. She spent her childhood trying to make both say the same thing, and failed, and decided she preferred the cord, and has never told her father.","schooling":"The pane-shed knot-school under Gudrun Fenn, and on the side, her father's lessons in Clear, which ended every sentence with an evidential: seen, told, reasoned. No lamp lit at the Lamp Test in the Still City; her father asked to have her re-tested in Meridian and she was, at ten, under a noon sky, and again nothing lit.","history":"Her father, Davor Krog, came north as a young lens-merchant to buy Icewatch ice-lenses, met her mother cutting panes on the slope, and stayed twelve years. When Asta was fourteen he went back to Meridian, by agreement, kindly, to look after his own ageing mother, and they write. Asta apprenticed to the slope's smith at fifteen and took the forge at twenty-two. She forges the long fine saws for cutting panes from Glassmere blocks, the frames that hold them, and iron grates that warm the ice-houses from beneath without melting them, a design she worked out herself. She is good, and knows it, and says so with an evidential. Last winter her father wrote that his lot had come up for the Assize of Witnesses and that he had voted to elect High Councillor Arath, and that the Edict was just. He wrote it with the evidential ending that means 'I have seen this myself'. Asta has read the letter fifty times. She is fairly sure he hasn't.","family":"Mother Hanne, a pane-cutter, still on the slope. Father Davor in Meridian, on the Assize this year. No partner; a long-running flirtation with Ronja Telle that neither of them will name.","hero":{"who":"Sera Solbrand","why":"The only person ever to use the Judgment of One, and she did it alone, with no one to share the cost. Asta has always wondered what it is like to be the only one who can say whether a thing is just, and thinks her father is about to find out."},"band":{"act":"Verdict","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Symphonic metal where every song is a trial. She saw them at Korrhaven with her father, the last summer before he went south, and they argued all the way home about whether the songs had the verdicts right.","rv_vote":"Verdict, and she'll write to her father that she did, with the right ending."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, and for acting on Lyriel's warning, partly because Lightforge overruled it and she wants to see what happens when someone doesn't let them.","edict":"Torn, painfully. She was raised to believe in saying everything in the light. She also knows the difference between a Day-Book and a cord, and the Edict only has the first."},"wants":"To write her father one letter he can't answer with an evidential.","site":"stillcity_vf2","town":"The Still City Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:stillcity_vf2":{"name":"Bo Hylte","age":58,"role":"grower on the Glittering Slope; bred the village's small sour apple; holds a grudge against Glassmere he calls 'patience'","look":"Stocky, ruddy, a huge grey moustache, apple-wood pipe never lit, and hands so scarred from pane-edges they look embroidered.","voice":"Very calm, very slow, very polite, and you can feel the boil under it like a kettle on a low peat fire.","greet":"Halla. Lovely day. Lovely. Nothing whatever is wrong. Would you like an apple? They're sour. Like some people.","lines":["Glassmere sells us cloudy ice at the clear price. I don't mind. I don't mind at all. I've been not minding for forty years.","My father threw a whole sledge of cloudy ice back down the Glassmere road once. I carried it back up. Somebody had to keep the kale warm.","Torra Cinder was tried in Lightforge for concealed anger. I think about her a great deal. A great deal."],"about":"I grow apples, small and sour, the only apples in Frostgrave that ripen under ice. I bred them myself. I've kept my temper all my life, which my family says is the most frightening thing about me. I'm very fond of everyone.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, slow and very correct"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"one long Ash-Tongue chorus, which he knows by heart and has never sung aloud"}],"childhood":"When Bo was eight his father threw a whole sledge of cloudy Glassmere ice back down the road, block by block, shouting, and Bo spent the rest of the afternoon carrying the blocks back up one at a time so the family's kale wouldn't freeze. He remembers his hands. He remembers his father sitting on the step afterwards, quite calm, saying, 'Well. That's done.' And he remembers deciding, at eight, quite clearly, that he would never in his life throw anything, and that he would carry it back up instead. He was a quiet, watchful, helpful boy who grew things: a row of radishes in a cracked pane at five, a chive pot on the sill at six, and at eleven, a pip from an apple a Lightforge courier dropped on the road, planted under ice, that came up.","schooling":"The pane-shed knot-school under Gudrun Fenn's mother, who was known for kindness and for once tying the whole class's cords together in one long string at the Fallen Day so they had to walk home as a chain. No lamp lit. Bo was a solid, unremarkable pupil who was excellent at knots for patience.","history":"He has spent his whole life on the Glittering Slope. The courier's apple pip grew into a tree that bore small hard sour fruit, and Bo grafted it onto a Frostgrave crab over twenty years until he had an apple that ripens under ice and keeps till spring, and the whole slope grows it now. He is proud of it, quietly. He married Embla, a cheerful pane-cutter, at twenty-eight, and they had four children, all loud, which he enjoys. He has kept his temper through every argument with Glassmere for forty years, every cracked sledge, every cloudy block at the clear price, and the village thinks of him as its calmest man. His children think of him as a peat-fire that has never once been let out. He laughs at their jokes and pays their debts and goes into the oldest ice-house alone some evenings with a mallet and breaks nothing, just holds it, for a while, and comes out smiling.","family":"Wife Embla, who laughs at him and knows. Four grown children, two in the ice-houses, one a lamp-tender, one a Rimewatch halberdier. His father, long dead, who threw the ice.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder","why":"The Banked Fire, tried in Lightforge for 'concealed anger'. Bo has concealed his whole life, carefully, on purpose, and he thinks she was the only one who ever got blamed for doing it well."},"band":{"act":"Ash-Tongue","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Flameborne metal whose singer, Varra Cinder, loses her self-control every show. He heard it through a Flameborne merchant's wagon wall once and has never told anyone it was the best thing he ever heard.","rv_vote":"Ash-Tongue, and he'll vote very calmly."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting: 'She warned them politely and they struck her out. There's only so long you can be polite.'","edict":"Against, mildly. Very mildly. Extremely mildly."},"wants":"For Glassmere, just once, to apologise.","site":"stillcity_vf2","town":"The Still City Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:stillcity_vf2:b":{"name":"Ronja Telle","age":31,"role":"herb-grower for the Still City lamp-houses; keeps the slope's medicinal beds; quietly Thawborn","look":"Lanky, freckled, red hair in a loose knot stuck with sprigs of whatever she's picking, and a word-cord with one gap in the knots that she keeps covered with her hand.","voice":"Gentle, a bit wry, asks you questions back and listens to the answers.","greet":"Halla. Smell this. No, go on. That's frost-mint. It'll clear your head of anything, almost.","lines":["The lamp-houses take my herbs for their tenders' burns and their seers' frost. The seers' frost costs more. Everything to do with seers does.","Some people say a promise can be put down, if keeping it would break you. Some people get thrown out of the Hall for saying it. Those are often the same people.","The Full Name sings her own name aloud to one person. One. In Shadowdeep they'd ruin her for it. I think it's the bravest song I've ever heard."],"about":"I grow herbs for the lamp-houses. My mother promised me to their beds before I could talk, and I grew up in them, and I'm good at it. I don't know if I'd have chosen it. I've chosen it since. That's not the same, is it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"herb-names and some lamp-house Lanthic from Spiritveil tenders"}],"childhood":"Ronja's first knot was tied for her before she could talk, by a mother who promised the Still City lamp-houses a daughter for their herb-beds in return for the medicine that saved her husband's lungs. The promise was kept. Ronja grew up in the herb-beds on the slope, in the steam of the oldest ice-houses, learning frost-mint, lampwort and snow-yarrow by smell before she knew their names. She was happy. That is the thing she always says first, because it is true, and because people expect otherwise. She was a curious, cheerful, rather solemn child who asked why about everything, and at ten asked her mother why she had been promised and not asked, and her mother cried, and Ronja never asked again, which is a thing she has never forgiven herself for.","schooling":"Gudrun Fenn's pane-shed knot-school, and then the lamp-houses' own herb-school, taught in the Still City by a Spiritveil-born tender, Ithrel Saav, three hundred years old, who taught the Lanthic names and how to grow a plant that would forget it was winter, and who cried, once, over a frost-killed bed, and then replanted it the same hour. No lamp lit at the Test.","history":"She took on the medicinal beds at nineteen and has kept them well for twelve years, sending lampwort for burns and frost-mint for the seers' numbness up to the lamp-houses every week. At twenty-four her mother fell ill and asked her, on the last day, whether she would keep the promise when there was no one left to keep it to. Ronja said she probably would. Her mother died. Two winters later, at a quiet meeting in the back of a Still City felter's shop, Ronja heard a man talk about Olin Stair, who carried his sister down the Glass Stair because keeping his word to stay at the top would have killed her. She went home and sat with her cord for a long time and untied her first knot, the one her mother tied, and then went to work at the herb-beds the next morning exactly as before. She is still there. She chose it, this time. Only she knows the difference. She has a long, warm, unnamed thing with Asta Krog, which they discuss by not discussing it.","family":"Father alive and well, lungs and all, and grateful to her in a way that makes her uncomfortable. Her mother, seven winters dead. Asta Krog, who is not family, and who notices her covering the gap.","hero":{"who":"Vey Who Said It","why":"The founder of Shadowdeep's Open-Mouthed, who said her full name aloud when everyone told her not to. Ronja understands saying the forbidden thing, and then going on with your day."},"band":{"act":"The Full Name","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"A singer who sings her full name to one person, which is a scandal at home. Ronja thinks it's the same act as untying a knot: something private done in a way that can't be taken back.","rv_vote":"The Full Name, though they'll come second, being Shadowdeep."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, who is said to cry for people he's never met. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, and for reading the warning aloud, all of it.","edict":"Against. The Edict wants to register what people are. She's spent her life finding out that what you were made to be and what you choose can be different."},"wants":"To be able to show her cord to Asta without covering the gap.","site":"stillcity_vf2","town":"The Still City Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:rimewatch_vf1":{"name":"Kettil Hoar","age":70,"role":"trader of the Sleeper farms; sells kit and 'slow seed'; son of Grandmother Maelis Hoar, who now looks younger than he does","look":"Bent, bald, liver-spotted, a cloud of white beard, and the uneasy habit of glancing over his shoulder at the Titan on the horizon as if checking it's still asleep.","voice":"Deadpan, dry as old straw, delivers the saddest facts like jokes and the jokes like facts.","greet":"Halla. Packs, bread, arrows, slow seed. Don't ask about my mother. Everyone asks about my mother.","lines":["Don't sleep inside the posts. You'll wake up and your horse'll be dead of old age and you won't have missed breakfast. That's the joke. It isn't really a joke.","My mother's looked sixty for fifteen years. I'm seventy. Visitors think she's my daughter. She lets them.","Slow seed lasts longer in the store. Probably. I've sold it for fifty years. Nobody's come back to complain. Nobody's come back at all, some of them."],"about":"I keep the shop. My mother keeps the posts, or sits by them. Between us we've watched the Sleeper take about three hundred steps. That's not as many as it sounds. I'm the oldest man in the village and not the oldest person in my house.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, a slow old farm Rime"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"enough to sell seed to Ironwatch buyers and understand when they're laughing"}],"childhood":"The Hoar farmhouse stands closer to the Sleeper than any other in Frostgrave, and Kettil was raised to treat the Titan's bowed head on the horizon the way other children treat a grandfather asleep by the fire: you go quiet near it, and every so often you check it's breathing. He was the fourth Hoar child and the only one who stayed. He was a solemn, funny little boy with big ears who liked counting the birds nesting in the Sleeper's frost-feathered shoulders and kept a tally on the barn door. Each autumn, when the Titan took its step, he walked behind his mother pulling up boundary posts in silence and carrying them back one stride, and then went home to a loud supper, and he thought, until he was nine, that every family in the world did exactly this.","schooling":"The farms had no school of their own then; Grandmother Maelis, his mother, taught the knot-school in her kitchen and was famous for her slowness, a quarter-hour per knot, so that children came out of her lessons feeling they'd lived a day. No lamp lit for him at the Test in Rimewatch. He learned figures from the castle's grain-buyers and Shiftcant from Ironwatch seed-merchants.","history":"He took over the farm shop at twenty, married Liv, a castle cook's daughter, at twenty-five, and has sold barley, seed and kit to Rimewatch for fifty years. He started selling 'slow seed', grain grown in the Titan's lee, at forty; he believes it is a little better and knows it is not twice as good, and charges double anyway. Liv died at sixty. Their three children grew up and moved away, to Rimewatch, the Still City and Icewatch, partly to get away from the posts. Fifteen years ago his mother Maelis, then about seventy-five, began spending her mornings sitting very close to the boundary posts. She has looked sixty ever since. Kettil, who turned seventy this winter, now looks ten years older than his mother, and visitors to the shop take him for her father. He finds this funnier than anyone else does. He has never once asked her why she sits there, because he thinks he knows, and doesn't want to hear it said.","family":"His mother Maelis, ninety by the calendar, sixty by her face. Three grown children who visit at Stepping Back. His wife Liv, ten winters dead. A great-grandson he has met once.","hero":{"who":"Elder Varyn","why":"The last Titan-speaker. Kettil walked all the way to the Still City at thirty hoping to hear him speak, and never did, and walked home and has thought about the Sleeper differently ever since."},"band":{"act":"Coldread","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Deadpan, numb rap that says terrible things in a flat voice. His grandson played it to him and he laughed so hard he had to sit on a sack of seed.","rv_vote":"Coldread. 'Someone has to vote for a man who sounds like me.'"},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties against acting. 'You can stand next to the future for fifteen years. It doesn't make it come quicker.'","edict":"Against, flatly. 'They'd want to register my mother's age. Good luck to them.'"},"wants":"For his mother to grow old again, properly, before he does it for both of them.","site":"rimewatch_vf1","town":"Rimewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:rimewatch_vf1":{"name":"Sunniva Bratt","age":38,"role":"farrier-smith of the Sleeper farms; shoes the Rimewatch couriers' horses and the Winter Riders' remounts","look":"Big-boned and freckled, copper hair under a leather cap, a farrier's apron cut from an old Winter Rider's saddle, and arms like a Stonehold mason's.","voice":"Cheerful, loud, practical, talks to horses and people in exactly the same tone.","greet":"Halla. Shoe, nail, hinge? Bring it in out of the cold. Not too close to the window. Don't look at the Titan, it puts the horses off.","lines":["The castle wants the posts moved further each year for the courier road. I'm with the castle. I'm the one who has to shoe the horses that age on it.","Some courier comes through here every week and hasn't aged a day in twelve years. Same horse too. Somebody's sleeping inside the posts.","The Hammer-Bearer stood up when the Council laughed at Metalcrown. Only one who did. I'd shoe that one's horse for nothing."],"about":"I'm a farrier. Horses come in from the road and I send them back out. My father shoes the Riders at Rimewatch, my sister married a wolf-handler, and I came out here because the work's steady and I like the quiet. Except the quiet out here's the wrong sort, sometimes.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, loud"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, from the couriers"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"horse words, learned from a Low Reach horse-trader"}],"childhood":"Sunniva's first horse died of old age when she was eleven, though it had been foaled the same spring she turned nine. She was a castle child, the elder daughter of Rimewatch's farrier, Hallstein Bratt, and her father had given her the foal for her own; she broke it to halter herself and rode it bareback along the courier road towards the Sleeper farms, and one summer afternoon she fell asleep with it grazing beside her in a meadow inside the boundary posts. She woke an hour later, by her own count, and the colt was grey round the muzzle. By the next spring it was dead of age. She never told her father where she'd slept. She has never ridden inside the posts again, and she has never stopped being angry about it.","schooling":"The castle knot-school for some years, and then, after the foal, she was sent out to the farms to be taught by Grandmother Maelis Hoar, who taught slowly enough that Sunniva had time to calm down. No lamp lit at the Test. She learned to shoe at her father's forge from twelve, and was better at it than her brother by fifteen.","history":"She worked her father's forge at Rimewatch until twenty-eight, shoeing Winter Riders' horses and courier mounts, and then took the farms' smithy when its old smith retired, because the Rimewatch courier road runs right past it and somebody needs to shoe horses at that end. She does hinges, ploughshares and harrow-teeth too, but horses are what she's good at. She has watched for ten years how fast the courier horses grey on the stretch of road nearest the Titan, and has argued at every Stepping Back that the posts should go further, and the farmers think she's the castle's creature. She is unmarried and lives in two rooms over the smithy with a three-legged cat she calls the Sentinel because it never moves. For twelve years she has shod the same courier's horse every week, a dun mare, and neither the courier nor the mare has aged at all.","family":"Father Hallstein, Rimewatch's farrier. Younger sister Signy, married to the wolf-handler Yrsa Brekk. A brother who failed to become a farrier and became a Winter Rider instead. The cat.","hero":{"who":"the Hammer-Bearer","why":"The only Councillor who stood up when the Council laughed at Metalcrown's ninth petition. Sunniva has stood up at every Stepping Back for ten years and been laughed at too."},"band":{"act":"The Glare","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Arena rock from the land of noon, loud and bright and nothing hidden. She heard it at Korrhaven on a courier's errand and liked that nobody in it had to keep still.","rv_vote":"The Glare. She knows Lightforge wins too much. She doesn't care."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, and has a list of what she'd do first, which starts with moving the posts.","edict":"For, which goes down badly at the farms. She's seen what happens to a body that's near something no one's registered, and she'd like someone to be writing these things down."},"wants":"To get the post-line moved three strides back before another horse greys on the courier road.","site":"rimewatch_vf1","town":"Rimewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:rimewatch_vf1":{"name":"Jorund Ekre","age":49,"role":"barley farmer on the furthest fields from the Sleeper; would like to farm inside the posts, efficiently","look":"Tall and lean as a fence-pole, short-cropped grey hair, an Ironwatch-made pocket-gauge on a chain at his waist, and a permanent squint from looking at things he's measuring.","voice":"Clipped, logical, quietly exasperated with everyone; speaks Rime as if it were a list.","greet":"Halla. Do you know what an extra hour a day is worth over a season? I do. Nobody here wants to hear it.","lines":["Every autumn we move the posts back a stride and lose a stride of the best land in Frostgrave. Then we go home and have a loud supper about it.","Slow barley from inside the posts would be the best grain in the Nine Realms. We could sell it to Metalcrown. We could sell it to everyone. We stand outside a fence instead.","Inefficiency kills children. The Ninth Dominus said that. Everyone here looks at me like I've sworn when I quote it."],"about":"I farm barley at the far edge. My father measured the Sleeper's step every autumn with a rope of a hundred knots, and I've done it since. It's always the same. One step. We could plan around that. We don't. We just step back.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, clipped"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"reads it fairly well; he subscribes, after a fashion, to an Ironwatch engineers' broadsheet"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Jorund's father measured the Sleeper's step every autumn with a rope of exactly one hundred knots, and from the age of five Jorund held the far end. His father was a careful, silent man who wrote the measurement on the barn wall in chalk, year after year, a column of figures nearly all the same, and Jorund grew up believing that numbers were how you kept the world from getting away from you. He told his aunt at a Stepping Back supper that they had moved the posts four inches too far and wasted land, and she put him outside in the snow for the rest of the meal, and he stood there and measured the snow. At eleven he took apart a Rimewatch sergeant's Ironwatch-made pocket-watch to see how it kept time, and could not put it back.","schooling":"Grandmother Maelis Hoar's kitchen knot-school, where he was the only child who complained the knots were tied too slowly. No lamp lit. He taught himself arithmetic out of the castle grain-buyers' tally-books, and a little engineering out of a Shiftcant broadsheet a courier left behind, which he read with a word-list until he didn't need it.","history":"He took over the far fields at twenty-two and has farmed them with a precision the village thinks unhealthy: every furrow measured, every sowing timed by the gauge on his belt. He married Gudny, a quiet castle seamstress, at twenty-seven; they have two daughters, and the elder is as exact as he is and the younger has run off to join the Stillwatch, which he finds incomprehensible. For twenty years he has argued at every Stepping Back that the village should stop retreating from the Sleeper and start using it: sow a measured strip inside the posts, harvest it with a rota so nobody stays long, sell the grain at triple price. Every year the village pulls up the posts in silence anyway, and goes home to a loud supper, and Jorund eats his in silence. Three years ago he wrote to the Ironwatch engineers whose broadsheet he reads, describing the Sleeper's slow time. They wrote back, very politely, with a list of questions, and a request for a sample of soil from inside the posts.","family":"Wife Gudny, who darns his socks and ignores his speeches. Daughters Ragnhild, twenty, who keeps his figures, and Hild, eighteen, a halberdier who thinks he's ridiculous. His father, dead, whose figures are still in chalk on the barn wall.","hero":{"who":"the Ninth Dominus","why":"'Inefficiency kills children. This can be corrected.' Jorund knows that's a frightening thing to say. He still thinks it's true, and he's never heard anyone in Frostgrave say anything as clear."},"band":{"act":"Gearbox","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Engine reggaeton. It keeps perfect time. He likes music where you can count the beat without having to guess.","rv_vote":"Gearbox, because Metalcrown deserves at least one lamp and he can count."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside reluctantly; he'd have preferred someone with figures. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, and has written down what acting would cost.","edict":"For. He thinks Metalcrown is right to smile at it: anything that counts what you've got is better than pretending you don't know."},"wants":"To sow one measured strip inside the posts and prove he's right.","site":"rimewatch_vf1","town":"Rimewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:rimewatch_vf1:b":{"name":"Tilde Ose","age":16,"role":"barley-farmer's daughter; the only person in the village not afraid of the Sleeper, loudly","look":"Small and sharp-faced, black hair chopped short with sheep-shears, a red Stormreach scarf worn to annoy her mother, and a word-cord with no knots she's willing to talk about.","voice":"Quick, provocative, finishes your sentences wrongly on purpose to see what you'll say.","greet":"Halla. You're not from here. Good. Want to see something nobody here'll show you? It's a fence. It's a very exciting fence.","lines":["Everybody says don't sleep inside the posts. I've heard the horse joke nine hundred times. Nobody's actually tried. Not properly.","Ardyn Solbrand wrote a law the whole Concord's fighting about. One person. One law. Everybody arguing. Imagine writing something that mattered that much.","Wahai! play reggaeton with pipes and they never stop moving. You couldn't play Wahai! here. The barley would get upset."],"about":"I'm Tilde. I'm sixteen. I'm going to leave, obviously, probably to the Still City or further, as soon as somebody gives me a reason. In the meantime I annoy everyone. I'm very good at it. I've had practice.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, fast and rude"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, self-taught from couriers to be able to argue with them"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"'Wahai!' and four swear-words"}],"childhood":"Tilde Ose was the only child in the village who was not afraid of the Sleeper, and she made sure everyone knew it, from the age of four, by walking up to the boundary posts and sticking her tongue out at it. She was hauled back by her collar so often that her mother sewed a loop into all her coats for the purpose. She was the youngest of four, by six years, and her cheerful barley-farming father thought her the funniest thing in Frostgrave. At seven she asked at Stepping Back supper why they didn't just ask the Sleeper to go away, and the whole table went quiet, and her grandmother said, 'We did once,' and would not say anything more, and Tilde has been trying to find out what that meant ever since.","schooling":"Grandmother Maelis Hoar's slow kitchen knot-school, from which Tilde was sent home more often than any child in its history, and then the castle knot-school, where she argued with the sergeant. No lamp lit at the Test. She was furious, and asked to be tested again; the tester said the lamps don't change their minds, and she said 'Yet.'","history":"She is sixteen and has done a great deal of arguing. At twelve she ran away to Rimewatch and was brought back by Sunniva Bratt on a courier's horse. At thirteen, on a dare from no one but herself, she slept one night inside the boundary posts, in a hollow by the old Hoar farm, under a blanket, with a candle; she woke at what she thought was dawn and walked home to find she had been gone three days and the village had been searching. She told them she'd been at her aunt's in Rimewatch, and her aunt, who loves her, backed the story. At fourteen she read a copy of the Radiant Edict a Lightforge courier left at the castle, all of it, twice, and decided she didn't care whether it was right, only that one person had written something the whole Concord had to answer. At fifteen she wrote her own, a law for the village about the posts, and nailed it to the barn door, and her father framed it.","family":"Father Erling, a cheerful barley-farmer. Mother Rannvi, worn out. Three older brothers, all farmers. A grandmother who will not explain what 'we did once' means, and an aunt in Rimewatch who keeps secrets.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand","why":"Not because she agrees with him. Because he sat down and wrote something that made the whole Concord stop and argue. Tilde wants to do that, and preferably about something better."},"band":{"act":"Wahai!","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Party reggaeton with pipes, never still, never quiet. It is the opposite of everything about her village, which is the whole point.","rv_vote":"Wahai!, and she'll wear the red scarf on the night."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to tie a knot at the Warm Chair, which she considers a scandal. She'd tie against Ivo, just to be the one who did, and for acting on Lyriel's warning, loudly.","edict":"For, which horrifies her family. She thinks at least it's an answer, and everyone in Frostgrave would rather tie a knot about a thing next spring."},"wants":"To find out what her grandmother meant by 'we did once'.","site":"rimewatch_vf1","town":"Rimewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:icewatch_vf1":{"name":"Hallveig Krans","age":60,"role":"trader of the pickling village; for forty years the crock-carrier who took the jars up the Glass Stair","look":"Short, solid, knees bound in felt, a carrier's back-frame still hanging behind the counter, and a face like a cheerful walnut.","voice":"Chatty, warm, a great one for 'love' and 'pet', complains about her knees as a form of affection.","greet":"Halla, pet. Sit. Have a berry. Have two. Kit's behind me, bread's on the side, and if you're off up the Stair, mind the ninetieth, it's had my knee twice.","lines":["Ever had a berry in midwinter that tastes like the sun's still up? That's us. Don't tell the Still City how easy it is. They think it's a secret. Let them.","Forty years I carried crocks up four hundred steps. Twenty crocks a load. My knees are a quarrel now. They don't speak to each other.","The Flameborne King answers anybody who refuses him with bread. I like a king who's got a loaf ready. I'd refuse him just to see what he baked."],"about":"I carried our jars up the Glass Stair for forty years, love, on my back, twenty at a time. Now I sit here and sell to the young ones who carry them. I've climbed it about four thousand times. I've counted. I've never once looked down.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, warm and broad"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, from forty years of the Stair"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"bargaining Gale, from the cliff-honey trade"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a few warm words from a Flameborne envoy she once fed"}],"childhood":"Hallveig's mother had nine children and one rule: whoever complained carried the crocks. Hallveig was the fifth and the most cheerful and therefore, everyone assumed, the least likely to complain; then at eight, carrying a single small crock down to the Jar-pits in the autumn, she slipped on the bank, broke it, sat in the lingonberries and wailed. Her mother handed her a second crock. She has been carrying them ever since. She was a sturdy, sunny, much-bruised child who learned every family's knot on every pit-lid by the time she was ten, and who could eat more berries in an afternoon than anyone on the bank, and she has never, in sixty years, been anything but very fond of the people around her, even when they're idiots.","schooling":"The village knot-school was held on the riverbank in autumn and in the jar-shed in winter by Knot-mother Sigga Lund, Torvi Lund's grandmother, who made the children taste a berry from each family's pit and tie that family's knot from memory. No lamp lit. Hallveig learned the knots fast and the letters slowly, and was carrying for her mother's pit by eleven.","history":"At twenty she became the village's crock-carrier, taking the full jars up the Glass Stair on a back-frame, twenty at a time, to the Icewatch merchants at the top. She did it for forty years. She married Torleif Krans, a jar-maker who made her frames, at twenty-four; he is alive, deaf now, and still throws pots. They had three children, none of whom carry. In her thirties a Flameborne trade envoy came down the Stair on his way to the Still City and slipped on the ninetieth step, and she caught him by the coat and sat him on a landing and fed him berries for an hour while he shook; he sent her a loaf from Hearthmount every First Coal for twenty years until he died. She gave up carrying at sixty, last spring, when her knees refused, and took the village shop, where she sells kit and bread and berries and talks to everyone who comes in until they buy something to escape.","family":"Husband Torleif, deaf and content. Three grown children, a lamp-tender, a rope-maker and a seer's clerk. Nine siblings, more nephews than she can count.","hero":{"who":"the Flameborne King","why":"A king anyone can refuse, who answers with bread. Hallveig has been refusing to stop climbing for forty years and has only ever been answered with berries, which she thinks is about the same thing."},"band":{"act":"No Knee","realm":"Flameborne","why":"A bakers'-street rap crew at war with Lightforge. Her envoy's grandson sent her a song-sheet. She doesn't understand half of it and likes that a band is named after her knees.","rv_vote":"No Knee. 'For my knees.'"},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, a ferryman, who'd understand carrying things. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, 'and taking some berries along for whoever has to do it'.","edict":"Against. She thinks anyone who wants to count other people's sparks should have to carry twenty crocks up four hundred steps first and see how they feel about counting."},"wants":"To climb the Glass Stair once more without a crock on her back, just to look at it.","site":"icewatch_vf1","town":"Icewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:icewatch_vf1":{"name":"Osvald Tjern","age":44,"role":"smith of the pickling village; forges crock-hoops, pit-lid hinges and the heel-irons the families test the ice with","look":"Gangly, stooped, grey-eyed, deaf in his left ear and always standing to your right, with a tanner's leather apron from somewhere warmer than here.","voice":"Gentle and wry, tilts his head to hear you, speaks a little too softly because he can't hear himself well.","greet":"Halla. Come round this side, I'll hear you better. Hoops, hinges or heels?","lines":["The pickling families don't trust the river-readers. They test the ice with their own heels now. I make the heel-irons. So really they trust me.","I worked five years at the Rim in Lightforge, the only place in that Realm with real dusk. Best years of my life. The dusk was free.","Slag Hill are angry at everyone born with a spark. I'm not angry. But I can see why they are."],"about":"I'm the smith. Hoops for the crocks, hinges for the lids, irons for the heels. I spent five years in Lightforge's Rim at a forge in Lampwick, among the tanners. Came home because my mother asked. I miss the dusk.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, soft"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good, with Rim slang; he can't do the precise evidential endings and the Rim never minded"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Osvald grew up deaf in his left ear and spent his childhood standing to the right of everyone, which in a village of people kneeling along a riverbank every autumn made him the boy at the end of every row. He didn't mind. From the end of the row he could see all of them, the whole bank of families lowering their crocks into the ice in their long patient line, and he used to make up stories about each of them that he never told anyone. His father was the village smith and his mother a picker, and he was an only child. He was not unhappy, just a little apart, and he grew up comfortable being a little apart, which is the most useful thing that ever happened to him.","schooling":"Knot-mother Sigga Lund's riverbank school, where he was quick at knots and slow at hearing the lesson and so learned to read lips. No lamp lit at the Test. He apprenticed to his father at twelve and was taught hoop-work, then hinges, then, at fourteen, to make a nail so fine it could hold a lid without splitting the birch.","history":"At twenty-two, restless, he took a long-wagon south with a lens-merchant from Icewatch and ended up, by a chain of accidents and wagons, in Lampwick on the Lightforge Rim, the town of stag-antler lamps at the shard's edge where the light finally gives way to dusk. He worked five years at a tanners' forge there, among the poor and the unlicensed, making scraping-knives and hooks, and he fell in love with the dusk: an hour each evening when nobody could see anybody very well and everyone was kinder for it. He met Penumbrists there and never joined them, though he went to their suppers. At twenty-seven his mother wrote that his father was dying, and he came home, and stayed. He has been the village smith since. Twelve years ago, after the second early close, he made the first heel-iron, a ringed plate that straps to a boot-heel and tells you by its note how thick the ice is, and every pickling family now uses one to test the edge-ice instead of believing the Icewatch river-readers. He tuned it using the river-readers' own tables.","family":"Mother, a picker, still kneeling on the bank every autumn at seventy-five. Father dead. No partner; a Rim woman in Lampwick he still writes to, who signs her letters with a shaded lamp.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"The first Penumbrist, who said mercy needs shade. Osvald spent five years in the only place in Lightforge where there's any, and he saw what she meant every single evening."},"band":{"act":"Slag Hill","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Industrial drill against spark-born privilege. He heard them through a Lampwick tannery wall, played by Metalcrown labourers, and understood every word without knowing the language.","rv_vote":"Slag Hill, because Metalcrown's never had a lamp from Lightforge and he's lived in Lightforge."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside, for crying. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, and thinks the Lightforge Council that overruled her would not know shade if it bit them.","edict":"Against, from experience. He's seen what lamp-checks do in the Rim, and the Edict makes them law."},"wants":"To go back to Lampwick for one more dusk, once his mother doesn't need him.","site":"icewatch_vf1","town":"Icewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:icewatch_vf1":{"name":"Lova Brenn","age":35,"role":"berry-picker and syrup-trader; carries Frostgrave berry-syrup to Stormreach's cliff-towns for cliff-honey; possibly married","look":"Wind-burned and lean, black hair in a Stormreach life-cord braid with no charge-knots in it, berry-stained fingers, a pack that never seems to come off.","voice":"Quick, bright, prone to Gale exclamations, laughs at herself before anyone else can.","greet":"Halla! Wahai! Sorry, I've been south. Do you want syrup? It's very good. It's mostly good. It's good.","lines":["The river-readers called it early twice. Now we test the ice with our heels. I test mine with my bottom. You sit on it. If you go through, it's early.","Every summer I take our syrup to the cliff-towns and come back with cliff-honey. They eat it standing up there. Sitting down to eat's a funeral. I ate sitting down once. They were very kind about it.","I might be married. In Stormreach. The wind took half his vow. Storm weddings only bind if you both hear. I don't know if I heard."],"about":"I pick berries in summer, lay jars in autumn, and in between I carry syrup south to the Stormreach cliff-towns and honey north. I'm the only person here who speaks Gale. I'm also possibly someone's wife, which is a long story, and I tell it badly.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"good, loud, and no past tense in it, which she says suits her"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Lova's first memory is of a man on the roof of the inn at Icewatch, shouting an apology at the top of his lungs in a language she didn't know. She was three, on her father's shoulders, and she clapped, and the man on the roof saw her and stopped shouting and waved. He was a Stormreach syrup-buyer who had cheated an Icewatch merchant and been caught, and it was the Stormreach way, though she didn't know it. She only knew that somebody far away did things loudly and on rooftops, and from that day she wanted to go there. She was a fidgety, joyful, berry-mad child, the second of three daughters of a picking family, and she was always the one who wandered off down the bank to find the place nobody had picked yet.","schooling":"Knot-mother Sigga Lund's riverbank school, where she learned the families' knots and the taste-test and was famously bad at both. No lamp lit. She learned Gale at fourteen from the Stormreach syrup-buyers at the top of the Stair, following them round asking for words, and has a cliff-town accent that makes Icewatch wince.","history":"At eighteen she went south with the syrup for the first time, over the Realm Bridge and up into the Stormreach cliff-towns, where people live on ledges lashed with chain and eat strike-goat standing up, and she came back with cliff-honey and a sunburn and has gone every summer since. She trades the village's red berry-syrup for honey, and the honey sells well in Icewatch. At twenty-seven, on a cliff-town called Harl's Ledge, a chain-farmer named Joss shouted a storm-wedding vow to her from his roof to hers, across a gap of thirty feet, in a gale, and she shouted hers back. Under Stormreach custom a storm wedding binds if both hear. He heard hers. She heard the start of his, and the end, and the wind took the middle. She has gone back every summer and they have never once discussed it, and he has never married anyone else, and neither has she. This winter he wrote, in bad Rime, that he is coming north.","family":"Parents, pickers; two sisters, one who carries crocks and one who's moved to the Still City. Joss of Harl's Ledge, who may or may not be her husband.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"'No Realm should ever be alone.' He built the first sky bridges. Lova carries syrup between two Realms that think each other mad, and she thinks he'd have liked that."},"band":{"act":"Corran Vale","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Storm-shouted apology rap. It reminds her of the man on the inn roof, and of Joss, and of every loud honest thing she's heard on a cliff.","rv_vote":"Corran Vale. She'll shout it."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, because Stormreach would have acted already and loudly.","edict":"Against, with her cliff-town friends, who are against it so loudly she can hear them from here."},"wants":"To find out, once and for all, what Joss said in the middle.","site":"icewatch_vf1","town":"Icewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:icewatch_vf1:b":{"name":"Folke Holk","age":84,"role":"retired berry-picker and jar-maker; remembers both early closes; tests the ice with his own heel","look":"Gaunt, stooped, wispy white hair, long hands knotted at every joint, and one boot-heel worn down far more than the other.","voice":"Creaky, slow, gives long accounts of the weather sixty years ago and then a short sharp verdict on today.","greet":"Halla, halla. Come to hear about the bad berry years? I've a lot of them. Sit down. It'll take a while.","lines":["I remember both early closes. The first, a warm wind, honest mistake. The second, a still week, perfect ice, and the reader called it closed. Something wrong with that one. Always thought so.","I test the ice with my own heel. Seventy years I've done it. My heel's never called it early.","My Tora laid down a jar the autumn before she died and told me not to open it till I'd forgiven someone. I haven't. It's still in the pit."],"about":"I've picked these bogs eighty years. Made jars sixty. Buried one wife, raised four children, quarrelled with every river-reader Icewatch ever had. I'm the oldest man on the bank. I don't hurry and I don't forgive and I've no plans to start either.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native, old and slow"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, which he uses wrongly on purpose"}],"childhood":"Folke Holk was born in a bad berry year and his grandmother always said it showed. The lingonberries had failed that summer, and the cloudberries, and there was so little to lay down that the village shared its pits, and Folke was carried along the riverbank in a crock-basket, wrapped in a picking-apron, while his mother laid jars that were half empty. He grew into a thin, serious, unsmiling boy who could find a berry where no one else could and who kept count of every good and bad year on a stick. He was the sort of child old people liked and other children didn't. He made his first jar out of Lowmere clay at nine, and it cracked in the kiln, and he made another, and it cracked, and he made a third, which he still has.","schooling":"The riverbank school then was kept by an old picker called Harald Vang, who taught nothing but knots and berries and the sound of ice. No lamp lit. Folke left at twelve, already a better jar-maker than his father, and was bitter for years that the river-readers in Icewatch had been to a school and he hadn't.","history":"He picked the bogs, laid jars in the ice and made crocks from Lowmere clay for sixty years, steady, unspectacular, stubborn. He married Tora, a laughing picker from a family whose pit was next to his, at twenty-three, after nine years of not saying anything to her. They had four children and were married fifty-two years. Tora died at seventy-four, eleven winters ago, and the autumn before she died she laid down one jar of cloudberries in their pit, lidded with her own knot, and told him not to open it until he'd forgiven someone. He knew who. He has not opened it. Twenty-two years ago Icewatch's river-reader called the Lowmere closed early and the village lost half its summer; Folke shrugged that one off. Nine years ago the reader called it early again, in a still week with perfect ice, and Folke has never forgiven him for it, and has said so at every First Jar since.","family":"Four children, eleven grandchildren, three great-grandchildren, most of them pickers. His wife Tora, eleven winters gone. Her jar, still in the pit.","hero":{"who":"Tora Holk, his wife","why":"She laughed at everything, including him, and she was the only person who could make him put down a grudge, and she's been gone eleven years, and he's picked them all back up."},"band":{"act":"The Willow Choir","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"'Nothing Is Lost', a duet with the echo of a singer dead four hundred years. A Pale-road traveller played it at the inn once and he walked out halfway through, and walked back in for the end.","rv_vote":"The Willow Choir, 'for the dead one'."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties against acting: 'I've had readers and seers tell me what's coming all my life. My heel's been right more.'","edict":"Doesn't care. 'Lightforge can count what it likes. I'll count my jars.'"},"wants":"Nothing. He'd tell you that. He wants to open Tora's jar, and he won't.","site":"icewatch_vf1","town":"Icewatch Farms 2","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:icewatch_vf2":{"name":"Rannveig Selvin","age":51,"role":"innkeeper and trader of the last warm house before the Pale; keeper of the Thawing Chair","look":"Broad, red-armed, grey-blonde hair in a felt snood, a knife for carving chair-arms hanging at her belt, and a laugh you can hear from the bell-posts.","voice":"Big, warm, bossy, fond of telling guests what they're about to feel before they feel it.","greet":"Halla! In, in, shut the door. Don't tell me the date. I'll tell you. Sit there. No, there. That's the Chair. You'll want it later.","lines":["Walk with the bells. When you can't hear one, stop and wait. Don't call out. The Pale answers, sometimes, and it never answers in a voice you've heard.","The Spiritveil wardens ring a pattern for every lost name. We ring plain. Meet both in the middle and you'll think there's a wedding and a funeral going on at once.","Pale broth and willow-bark tea in the same cup. Frostgrave thinks it's wrong. Spiritveil thinks it's wrong. Everyone drinks it."],"about":"I keep the inn, the stores and the Chair. I've thawed more frozen travellers than anyone alive, and I've walked the Pale a dozen times myself. Well. I say I have. Feed them, bed them, ask them the date, carve their guess. That's the job.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"good kitchen Lanthic and every Spiritveil word for 'cold'"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Rannveig grew up under the Thawing Chair: her cradle was kept beneath it, in the warmest spot by the inn hearth, and the first words she learned to read were the guesses carved on its arms. 'Third winter of Speaker Hollin.' 'Late spring, probably.' 'I don't know. Is it still the same year?' Her mother kept the inn then, and Rannveig was raised by a rota of half-frozen strangers who came out of the white with silver hair and sat in the Chair and talked to the baby underneath it while they thawed. She learned to tell a Frostgrave traveller from a Spiritveil one by how they held a cup. She learned that the ones who guessed furthest wrong were usually the quietest. She thought, until she was seven, that everyone in the world was a guest.","schooling":"There was no school on the Pale road. A Spiritveil traveller who stayed one whole winter, an old copyist named Lethan, taught her letters by making her copy the Chair's guesses into a ledger, all of them, back to the oldest. No lamp lit at the Test in Icewatch. She has kept the ledger up ever since.","history":"She took over the inn at twenty-six when her mother's chest went, and has run it with a big voice and a soft hand for twenty-five years. She married a bell-warden, Hakon Selvin, at twenty-nine; he walks the Frostgrave half of the bell-line with Ysolde Kael and comes home with his beard white each time. They have two children, one a warden, one in Icewatch grinding lenses. Rannveig feeds, beds and thaws whoever stumbles in out of the Pale, seats them in the Thawing Chair, asks their name, Realm and date, and carves their guess on its arms with the knife at her belt; the Chair is so carved now that the newest guesses are on the legs. She keeps the old ledger too, and tells guests stories from it, and tells them she has walked into the Pale a dozen times herself, which isn't true. She took on a Still City girl, Ebba Grell, as a cook two winters ago; Ebba is quiet, good, and has started walking the bells.","family":"Husband Hakon, bell-warden. Two grown children. Ebba Grell, her cook, whom she has more or less adopted. A mother still living upstairs who corrects her stories.","hero":{"who":"the woman who guessed right","why":"Thirty years ago a Frostgrave fisher-wife came out of the Pale after nine days, sat in the Chair, and guessed the date to the day, and Rannveig, aged twenty-one, carved it. She thinks it's the most remarkable thing she's ever seen, and that the woman was simply paying attention."},"band":{"act":"Retell","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A rapper who repeats every earlier bar in every new verse. That's the Thawing Chair, she says: the same questions asked again and again, and the answers getting longer.","rv_vote":"Retell, because Spiritveil guests leave the inn humming him."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning she ties for acting, because she's seen what the Pale does to people who don't know what's coming and she'd rather they knew.","edict":"Torn. She'd hate a Lightforge auditor at the Chair asking frozen travellers for their sparks. She'd also quite like to know who's walking into her inn."},"wants":"To carve one guess on the Chair that's exactly right, from a traveller who's been gone a whole year.","site":"icewatch_vf2","town":"Icewatch Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"smith:icewatch_vf2":{"name":"Aksel Brun","age":33,"role":"bell-smith of the Pale road; forges and hangs the iron bells on the Frostgrave half of the line; married to a Spiritveil bell-warden","look":"Lean, quiet, dark-bearded, one ear lower than the other from a childhood bell-clapper, and a silver streak in his beard from his first walk to the middle of the line.","voice":"Low and careful, listens before answering, and taps the note of whatever he's talking about on the table with a fingernail.","greet":"Halla. Listen. That's the fourth post. It's flat. I'll be out to it after you've gone. What can I do for you?","lines":["We ring plain, one bell at a time, so the sound walks down the road. My wife's people ring a pattern for every lost name. We have this argument at breakfast.","I hung a bell in the middle of the line, where the two halves meet. It rings both ways. Nobody asked me to. Nobody's taken it down yet.","Two Windows are a Lightforge woman and a Shadowdeep man singing together. They shouldn't work. They do. Same as us, my wife says."],"about":"I make bells and hang them in the mist. My wife Saethra makes bells too, the Spiritveil kind, and hangs them from the other end. We met in the middle of the Pale, both of us lost. We've been arguing about how to ring ever since. It's a good marriage.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"good, married-in; he still gets the long th-words wrong and his wife corrects him fondly"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Aksel learned to tell iron by its ring before he could tell his sisters apart by their faces. His father was the bell-smith on the Pale road before him, and the forge was the loudest place for a day's walk in any direction, and Aksel grew up with a hammer's note in his head the way other children have a song. He was a dreamy, unobservant child about everything except sound. He would walk into a door while listening to a bell four posts away. At six he was knocked flat by a swinging clapper in his father's forge and his left ear swelled up like a mushroom, and when the swelling went down it sat a little lower than the right. He says he hears better on that side. His sisters say he only listens on that side.","schooling":"No school on the Pale road; his letters came from Rannveig Selvin's mother at the inn, who taught them from the Thawing Chair's ledger. No lamp lit at the Test. At fourteen he was sent to Icewatch for a winter to learn proper casting from Bergit Holm, the old town smith, who also taught Torstein Lisk, and who made Aksel hammer the same bell-blank a hundred times until he could hear the one stroke that was right.","history":"He took his father's forge at twenty-three and walked the bell-line from the inn to the middle of the Pale the next winter to check every post, alone, as his father had. In the middle, where the Frostgrave plain bells give way to the Spiritveil pattern-bells, he lost the sound, stood still as he'd been taught, waited, and heard someone else waiting too. It was Saethra Lomm, a bell-warden from Lantern Reach, a hundred and forty years old and looking twenty-five, who had come north to check her half and lost it at the same spot. They stood in the white for an hour by her count and a day by his, not calling out, until a bell came. They married in the Frostgrave way the next spring, forty seconds, and in the Spiritveil way the spring after, which took all evening. She lives at the inn half the year and in Lantern Reach the other half. Last autumn Aksel cast a bell that can ring both ways, plain or patterned, and hung it in the middle of the line without asking either side.","family":"Wife Saethra Lomm, Spiritveil bell-warden, who will outlive him by centuries and has told him so cheerfully. His father, retired, deaf as a post. Two sisters at the inn.","hero":{"who":"Bergit Holm, the old Icewatch smith","why":"She made him hammer one blank a hundred times until he could hear the right stroke, and then told him that most smiths never hear it at all. He has never forgotten the look on her face when he did."},"band":{"act":"Two Windows","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A Lightforge–Shadowdeep couple who sing in two languages that shouldn't fit. He and Saethra sang one of their songs at the Spiritveil half of their wedding and got the words wrong in both tongues.","rv_vote":"Two Windows, for the marriage."},"vote":{"local":"Tied for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning he ties for acting, and has asked Saethra what the Lantern Council would do, and she said 'warn', and he said 'that's what Lyriel did'.","edict":"Against. Saethra's people say Lightforge will forget why it did this within a century, and he'd rather it didn't do it at all than do it and forget."},"wants":"For both halves of the line to agree to ring his middle bell.","site":"icewatch_vf2","town":"Icewatch Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:icewatch_vf2":{"name":"Orthiel Mennath","age":212,"role":"Spiritveil-born cook at the Pale inn; came out of the mist sixty years ago and never went back","look":"Looks twenty-five, fine-featured and pale, hair gone entirely silver from the Pale with three darker grief-streaks in it, and a habit of touching the rim of a cup as if checking it's there.","voice":"Soft, musical, long Lanthic vowels in his Rime, remembers things aloud and corrects himself fondly.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Halla. I say both, I've earned both. Sit. The broth is the way my teacher made it, or nearly. You'll know if I tell you it's nearly.","lines":["The Pale took sixty years off me going north. I guessed the date six years wrong in the Thawing Chair. Rannveig's mother carved it. My guess is still there on the right arm.","Nothing is lost, my people say. Only carried. I left my lamp in Lanthys Vale. Someone's moved it to a back shelf by now. I don't mind. I'm carrying it.","Paper Lantern Funeral sound like the inside of a lamp going out slowly. I find them very cheerful."],"about":"I'm from the Vale. I walked into the Pale a long time ago after a quarrel, and came out here, silver, and stayed. I cook. I make remembered soup from a memory-thread of a cook dead three hundred years. It's the best thing on the road. Don't tell Rannveig.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native, old-fashioned"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"fluent after sixty years, with Lanthic vowels"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good but old-fashioned; traders laugh at his idioms"}],"childhood":"Orthiel was born in Lanthys Vale in a year the Copying Rooms ran out of honey for the sill-cakes, and his mother, a copyist, named his lamp after a cake. He grew up among the Lantern Archives when they were still a hill and not yet a mountain, running errands for copyists who were five hundred years old and still argued like children over who had remembered a thing right. He was a quiet, attentive, greedy boy who sat in the kitchens and watched the cooks make remembered soup, each one holding the thread of some long-dead cook's memory and moving their hands the way the dead cook had moved, and he wanted more than anything to be allowed to try. At twelve he was allowed. The soup was terrible. He tried again for forty years.","schooling":"The Copying Rooms of Lanthys Vale, under a copyist named Elthaine Roe, who made the children copy one page so many times that they could recite it backwards, and then made them copy it again in a different hand. At the Lamp Test, in a Vale lamp-ring, the Veilweave lamp glimmered: a Half-Spark, enough for a cook's thread-work and no more. He did not go to Korrhaven, and did not want to.","history":"He cooked in the Vale for a century, learning remembered soup from the thread of a long-dead cook called Abbeth whose memory he held so often her ghost-habits crept into his: he still wipes his hands on his left hip, as she did. At a hundred and fifty he quarrelled with his younger brother, Ithven, over whether Abbeth's thread should be let go, for the Letting heresy was fashionable then, and Ithven thought it kinder. Orthiel thought it murder. They said things that can't be unsaid in a family where nobody forgets. Orthiel walked north into the Pale that night with Abbeth's thread and a pot and walked for what he thought was a week. He came out at the inn on the Frostgrave side with his hair gone silver and sat in the Thawing Chair and guessed the year six years wrong; Rannveig's mother carved it. He has been the inn's cook for sixty years. He has outlived Rannveig's grandmother and will outlive Rannveig. He has never once gone back south, and every Long Evening he cooks Abbeth's soup and sets one bowl aside.","family":"A brother, Ithven, in Lanthys Vale, who has almost certainly Unlamped him. A mother long dead whose lamp is in the Archive. The Selvins, three generations of them, whom he has watched grow up and grow old.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"Spiritveil's first lantern was a cracked clay cup. Orthiel left everything else behind when he walked into the Pale except a cracked clay cup of his own, and he cooks the soup in it every Long Evening."},"band":{"act":"Paper Lantern Funeral","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Slow doom-gaze that sounds like a lamp burning down. He finds it comforting, the way an old person finds rain comforting.","rv_vote":"Paper Lantern Funeral. Spiritveil votes are always for something slow and he's not going to be the one who changes that."},"vote":{"local":"He ties no knots at the Warm Chair; he'd consider it stealing someone else's custom. If he were in the Vale he would light a lamp for whoever moved to warn Lightforge, and he says Lyriel did exactly that and should be thanked.","edict":"Against, the Spiritveil way: he warns. He tells every Lightforge courier who stops at the inn, very kindly, that in a hundred years nobody will remember why they did it."},"wants":"For Ithven to walk north into the Pale one day and come out at his door.","site":"icewatch_vf2","town":"Icewatch Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"vill:icewatch_vf2:b":{"name":"Gerd Skarre","age":19,"role":"apprentice bell-warden on the Frostgrave half of the Pale line, under Ysolde Kael","look":"Slight, freckled, hair cut short and already silver in one broad streak from the left brow back, and a felt hood with bells sewn round the edge so they can always be heard.","voice":"Quiet, dry, deliberate, a long pause before anything that matters, and laughs in short surprised bursts.","greet":"Halla. You came in out of the white? Sit by the Chair. Don't tell anyone the date. You'll want to guess.","lines":["Ysolde's hair went silver at nineteen. I'm nineteen. Mine's going. She says it happens to the ones who listen properly.","The Spiritveil wardens ring a pattern for each lost name. I've started learning the patterns. Ysolde says don't. I've learned eleven.","Somebody is a pop star in Shadowdeep nobody's ever seen. I like that. You could be anyone, singing that well, and never have to stand in front of it."],"about":"I ring bells in the mist. Ysolde Kael is teaching me. My family farm the last fields on the road, where the Pale comes over the wall in bad winters. I've been listening to it all my life. Now I get paid to.","languages":[{"tongue":"Rime","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a little, and eleven bell-patterns that are sort of words"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Gerd's parents farm the furthest fields on the Pale road, the ones where the mist comes over the drystone wall in bad winters and lies in the yard like spilled milk, and Gerd learned to walk holding a rope tied from the house door to the byre. They were the youngest of three and the only one who wasn't frightened of the white. On mist mornings their brothers stayed in by the fire. Gerd went out along the rope with a bucket and came back an hour later, by the house clock, having been, they insisted, gone only a minute, with frost on their eyelashes and the bucket full. They once spent an entire afternoon lying in the hay counting the bells on the line, which you can hear from the farm on still days, faint and far off.","schooling":"They walked to the inn for letters, which Rannveig Selvin taught from the Chair-ledger as her mother had, and they learned more from the guests than from the ledger. No lamp lit at the Test in Icewatch; Gerd was relieved. At fifteen they asked Ysolde Kael to take them on and she said no for two years, and then yes.","history":"They have walked the Frostgrave half of the bell-line with Ysolde for four years, at first only to the third post, then the tenth, then, last winter, all the way to the middle where the plain bells end and the Spiritveil pattern-bells begin. The silver streak in their hair came on that walk. Ysolde says the Pale takes something from those who listen properly and that a streak is cheap. Gerd keeps the posts, rings in turn, guides travellers through when the inn asks, and in the evenings has been teaching themself the Spiritveil bell-patterns from Aksel Brun's wife Saethra, who thinks it's charming and Ysolde thinks is dangerous. Gerd is neither a man nor a woman, has never said so in words, and the inn has simply never asked; Rannveig calls them 'pet', which is what she calls everyone. They have a quiet friendship with Ebba Grell, the inn's cook, who walks the bells with them now.","family":"Parents and two older brothers at the end farm, all of whom are a little afraid of Gerd and very proud. Ysolde Kael, who is not family and is the most important person in their life.","hero":{"who":"Ysolde Kael, bell-warden","why":"She has walked the Pale a hundred times and went silver at nineteen and still goes out. Gerd wants to be exactly that, and is nineteen, and is going silver, and is going out."},"band":{"act":"Somebody","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"An anonymous pop star nobody has ever seen. Gerd loves that a voice can be completely known and the person behind it never have to be.","rv_vote":"Somebody, and they hope Somebody comes second, the way Shadowdeep likes."},"vote":{"local":"Tied their first knot at the Warm Chair this year, for Ivo Rimeside. On Lyriel's warning they tie for acting: 'Someone heard something in the white. You don't ignore that. You walk towards it, slowly, with the bells.'","edict":"Torn. They don't know enough about sparks to say. They do know that the Pale doesn't care what's written about you."},"wants":"To walk the whole line, both halves, all the way to Lantern Reach, and ring every bell.","site":"icewatch_vf2","town":"Icewatch Farms 3","realm":"Frostgrave"},"shop:dawnwatch":{"name":"Hessa Ropewright","age":47,"role":"post-trader at the Rim gate; former mist-guide on the edge-paths","look":"Short and wind-chapped, a coil of hemp always over one shoulder, hair cropped to the scalp with one silver stripe the Pale left in it; she walks with a slight roll since she lost two toes.","voice":"Measures words like rope: gives you exactly the length you need, then stops.","greet":"Rope's sold by the true length. I measure it in front of you. Hold your arms out, love, you're my yard.","lines":["Lamp-checks start on the Rim next month. Clerks with a list, door to door at dusk, seeing whose lamp is lit. Ours will be. Theirs'll be the ones that get counted.","Meridian lads eat at the left table, Rim lads at the right. Same dusk-pala on both. The bread doesn't know.","Two toes, left foot. The Pale had them. I wrote it that night: 'Came back with eight.'"],"about":"I sell what gets you down the edge-paths and back. Twenty years I walked them myself, cairn to cairn into the mist. Now I sell the rope and tell people which cairn to turn back at. They mostly don't listen, and then they buy more rope.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"enough to haggle and ask the way at a cairn"}],"childhood":"Hessa was born in the rope-walk under Dawnwatch's east wall, fourth of six, to a mother who twisted hemp and a father who never stayed past a season and wrote, honestly, in every Day-Book he kept, that he did not mean to. She learned her numbers walking backwards down the walk with a strand in her fist, counting paces. Rim evenings frightened her brothers. Hessa liked them, because when the light went her mother stopped working and told stories with her hands still twisting nothing. At nine she climbed to the Dusk Gallery on a dare and was caught by a Sentinel who, instead of hauling her down, let her stand at the rail and watch the red go out. Nobody has ever asked her his name, so she has never said it.","schooling":"The Rim lamp-school under the old toll-house, kept by Master Corlan Wix, who taught reading by making each child write a Day-Book entry on a slate and then read someone else's aloud to the room. Lamp Test at eight: not a flicker from any of the eight. Spark-poor. She left at eleven for the rope-walk and can write a clean entry and a bill of sale, which she considers plenty.","history":"At seventeen she carried coils for an old mist-guide called Bennet Harl, and when his knees went she took his route at twenty-two. For two decades she walked lantern-traders and Lightforge buyers along the edge-paths into the Spiritveil mists, reading the cairns, turning people round when the air went silver. Nine years ago a Meridian wax-buyer insisted on going past the seventh cairn into the Pale; she went after him, brought him back by the collar with his hair gone white at the front, and lost two toes to the cold on the way. After that she bought the Dawnwatch post from the quartermaster's widow. She sells bows, packs, arrows, lamp-oil and dusk-pala to soldiers and walkers, buys salvage off the edge-paths, and pays the wax toll on Candlewick candles twice over in effect, which she complains about to both sides. This season a man with no Day-Book on him paid her to walk him to the third cairn. Her entry reads: 'Took a walker to the third cairn. Came back alone. He did not want to come back.' Every word of it is true.","family":"Never married. Lives with her younger brother Tobbe, a cook at the Rim-born table, and argues with him every evening about whose turn it is to put out the kitchen lamp. Her father's four Day-Books sit on her shelf; she has read them once, all the way through, and not since.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"He wouldn't cut his citadel's chain when they begged him to. She has been asked, twice, to cut a rope with somebody still on the far end of it."},"band":{"act":"The Willow Choir","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Lantern-traders sing 'Nothing Is Lost' at the fifth cairn to keep together in the mist. She heard it fifty times before anyone told her it had a name.","rv_vote":"The Willow Choir, because the traders will ask her at the fifth cairn and she'd like to say yes."},"vote":{"local":"If her lot came up she'd stand, be bad at it, and do it anyway. She would not back Arath: not because he's wrong, but because he looks very sure for a man who says nobody wants this.","edict":"Torn. Registers she can live with, having nothing to register. Lamp-checks on the Rim she can't: dusk is the one thing the Rim has that Meridian hasn't."},"wants":"To buy the old toll-house where she went to school and run the post from there, nearer the head of the edge-path.","site":"dawnwatch","town":"Dawnwatch","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:dawnwatch":{"name":"Ottar Kelbrand","age":61,"role":"garrison armourer and town smith; Meridian-born, forty years on a two-year posting","look":"Big, stooped, white beard singed short on one side, a Sentinel's old brass gorget worn as a forge apron-clasp; eyes gone pale from forty years of looking into the coals.","voice":"Slow and formal, Citadel vowels worn down by the Rim; always finishes a hard thing with 'and I'd say it to them too.'","greet":"Aure. Blade, buckle or hinge? I mend all three, and I'll tell you which one you've been neglecting.","lines":["They've asked me to cast brass seals for the lamp-checks. I've said no, and why, in writing. And I'd say it to them too.","The Meridian lads won't sit with me. Forty years and I'm still the one who changed tables.","Dusk-pala heels. First month I wouldn't touch them. Forty years on I hide them from the cook."],"about":"I came out to Dawnwatch at twenty-one as an armourer's mate on a two-year posting. I'm sixty-one. You can do the sum. I eat at the Rim table, I shoe the Rim's horses, and I've not been back to Meridian since my mother's naming in the Grief Room.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, Citadel accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, learned from the garrison rolls"}],"childhood":"Ottar grew up in the Weighhouse quarter of Meridian, where every front door faced another front door and the back windows lined up so neatly you could see three houses deep down the row. His father checked market scales for the Assize and came home every evening and read his own Day-Book aloud at supper, as if the family were a court. Ottar was the quiet one, tall too early, sent on errands because he could carry more. He loved the Citadel armoury's noise more than anything in the city, and spent his eleventh year standing outside its door until a journeyman gave him a bucket of rivets to sort just to make him useful. He sorted them by size, then by colour, then by maker, and was asked back.","schooling":"The Weighhouse district school under Mistress Ilvane Corsbrand, who once made the class describe a cup for a whole morning without using the word 'white'. Lamp Test at nine: the Radiance lamp blinked once, as though checking he was there, and went dark. Half-Spark; his name went in the old register and nothing came of it. He was apprenticed to the Citadel armoury at thirteen and learned plate, mail and the Sentinel's long glaive.","history":"His posting to Dawnwatch at twenty-one was meant to toughen him. It did something else. The Rim-born half of the garrison treated the armourer's mate as a pair of hands, which nobody in Meridian had ever done, and the evening light made the forge glow in a way that the noon never allowed. At twenty-four he wrote home asking to be rotated back. No answer came. He wrote in his Day-Book, 'Decided to stay,' on the day he gave up waiting, and he has never quite settled whether that line was true. He moved to the Rim table at twenty-six, which the Meridian half has never forgiven, and became master armourer at thirty-eight. His lot came up for the Assize at forty-four; he went to Meridian for a year, sat through land cases and two Witness Court appeals, did it properly, and came back thinner. When he relined the forge chimney nine years ago he found a mouse-eaten Day-Book wedged behind the bricks, with a captain's name on the cover. He has read it many times. This season the captain asked him to cast brass seals for the Rim lamp-checks. He refused in writing, with reasons, which in Lightforge is the loudest a man can be.","family":"Married Bettrin, a Rim-born bowyer, at thirty; she died eleven years ago and he named her in the Dawnwatch Grief Room. Their son Haldor is a Sentinel in Meridian and writes every month in a careful hand that never once asks him to come home.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"'Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.' He tells the Meridian table exactly what he tells the Rim table, and it costs him at both."},"band":{"act":"Verdict","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Every song is built like a trial with the verdict in the last bar, and in thirty years of listening he has never once guessed it right.","rv_vote":"Verdict, because a man should vote for the thing that still surprises him."},"vote":{"local":"Served his lot once and would again, without complaint. He backs Arath: 'I sat beside men like him. He'll do it properly. That's what worries me.'","edict":"For, in principle; his spark has been in a register since he was nine and it never did him harm. Against the lamp-checks, and against making the seals for them."},"wants":"To forge one blade without a single flaw before his eyes go, and give it to Lise Ambry when she makes corporal.","site":"dawnwatch","town":"Dawnwatch","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:dawnwatch":{"name":"Lise Ambry","age":17,"role":"garrison recruit, youngest on the wall; keeps the Lamp-Down watch every evening","look":"Thin, sunburnt on one side of her face only from standing the same post, tanner's brown still in the creases of her knuckles; a borrowed helmet stuffed with wool to make it fit.","voice":"Quick and flat, says the plain thing first and the feeling never; laughs through her nose.","greet":"Seen. You're not from the Rim, you're squinting. It's only evening, it won't bite.","lines":["They put my mum's name on the spark register first week. Clerk wanted every time she'd used it since she was nine. She's forty-two. They were there till dark.","Captain Varbrand takes Lamp-Down herself on the longest night. I've asked to sit it with her. She hasn't said no.","Grain? For the Rim table? Give it here, Tobbe'll cry. He cries at gifts."],"about":"I'm from Rushbrook, I signed at sixteen, and I'm the youngest so I put the lamp out. Every evening. Then I sit beside it till the light comes back. That's my job. It's a good job.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, Rim drawl"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting; mostly garrison orders and swear-words"}],"childhood":"Lise grew up in Rushbrook, two doors from the Shade Shed, the middle child of a curer and a Half-Spark mother who scraped hides and kept her spark folded away like a good shirt. The brook was her whole world: she fell into a bark-pit at six and came out brown to the eyebrows and was called Pit for a year. At Dusk Supper she sat at the bottom of the long table where the little ones were put, and she loved the rule that nobody could speak of what they'd done that day, because it meant the grown-ups talked about other things, about what they'd like, and that was the only time she ever heard them do it. She has never been scared of the dark. She has spent a long time trying to work out why everyone else is.","schooling":"The Rushbrook lamp-school, one room above the currier's, kept by Mistress Faye Orrick, who wore gloves to teach because the tanning had eaten her hands and who made the children read Day-Books aloud with their eyes shut, from memory, to prove they'd paid attention. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp answered, then seemed to think better of it. Half-Spark, like her mother. She left at fourteen, good at sums, bad at sitting still.","history":"At fourteen she went to work in the pits like everyone, and hated the smell less than the sameness. At sixteen a recruiting sergeant came down to Rushbrook for the Rim half of the garrison and she was first in the line, before her mother had finished saying no. She's been at Dawnwatch eighteen months. She's a fair shot, a poor marcher, and the best sentry on the wall because she can see in the red light better than anyone. Because she's youngest, Lamp-Down is hers: every evening she puts out the one lamp on the Dusk Gallery and sits beside it until the light comes back. The Meridian half of the garrison think it is a hardship and bring her blankets. She lets them. In her Day-Book the entry for it is always the same two words, 'Lamp-Down. Sat.', because writing the rest feels like handing it over. This season her mother's name went on the new spark register and a clerk came to the house with a list of questions that took the whole of one evening.","family":"Mother Haska, father Jemm, an older brother in the pits and a little sister who wants to join the garrison and whom Lise has told, at length, not to.","hero":{"who":"Captain Adra Varbrand (Adra Who Sat Up)","why":"The captain came to Dawnwatch at sixteen to stop being afraid of the dark. Lise came at sixteen because she never was, and wants to know what the captain found here."},"band":{"act":"Lights Out","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"She has never heard them. She has only heard that they play in total darkness and the crowd can't see each other's faces, and that is enough.","rv_vote":"Lights Out, whatever they sound like."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up, mostly to see Meridian. She's against Arath because he signed the paper that sent the clerk to her mother.","edict":"Against. She watched her mother answer questions for a whole evening about a spark she's used to warm her hands in winter."},"wants":"To make corporal before she's twenty, and to keep Lamp-Down anyway.","site":"dawnwatch","town":"Dawnwatch","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:dawnwatch:b":{"name":"Teodric Lumbrand","age":33,"role":"Meridian Sentinel-lieutenant on a two-year posting; Radiance Full Spark; compiling the Rim garrison's spark register","look":"Tall, scrubbed and very upright, Sentinel white kept spotless even on the Rim; a grey Conclave sash with four knots folded in his breast pocket; eyes that never quite rest.","voice":"Careful and complete; uses the evidential endings of Clear exactly, even in jokes, and never lowers his voice.","greet":"Aure. You're carrying something under the left strap; it's fine, I'm not asking. I just can't not see it.","lines":["Three months left on my posting. Then Meridian, and white walls, and nobody eating dusk-pala. I'm told I'll miss it. I'm told that by everyone.","The Rim half are 'local help' in the reports. I write the reports. I've started writing 'soldiers' and crossing it out. I leave the crossing-out in.","I keep a list now. It is the first thing I've ever written that I don't want anyone to read."],"about":"I'm a Radiance Sentinel, Meridian-born, four years at Korrhaven. I'm here because every officer does the Rim. I was told it would be a lesson in what the light is for. It has been a lesson.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, formal"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"Conclave-fluent"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a little, from a Stormreach roommate at Korrhaven"}],"childhood":"Teodric was the eldest son of a Lumbrand house that has sent Sentinels to the Citadel for eleven generations, and he grew up in rooms so bright that his younger brother used to hide under the dining table just to know what shade looked like. Teodric never hid. He was the child who read his own Day-Book aloud at the Unshadowing without being asked, and the one who told his tutor, at seven, that her ribbon was crooked. His mother called him 'a clear morning' as praise. His grandmother, who had used Radiance for forty years and could not forgive a dropped spoon, watched him with something he took for pride.","schooling":"Lamp Years at the Noon Hall's lower school under Master Rell Ostbrand, who taught history by making each child stand on a pane of witness-glass and recite it. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp went so bright the tester shaded her eyes. Full Spark. Four years at the Aether Conclave, Radiance Tower, grey sash with four knots; he hated the porridge with real precision and passed drill and law at the top of his year.","history":"He took his Sentinel oath at eighteen, served at the Sentinel Gate and on the Pillar Road, and was decorated for spotting a forged pass at sixty paces. The price of Radiance has been working on him since his Conclave years: first his privacy, so that every thought seems to arrive already said; lately his doubt, so that decisions come easily and cold. His Rim posting began twenty-one months ago. He expected to be bored and instead found a garrison split down the middle at mealtimes and a cook, Tobbe Ropewright, who fed him broth for four days when the Rim fever took him and never wrote it down as a favour. This season the captain handed him the Edict's order: compile a spark register for every Rim-born soldier at Dawnwatch, with the dates and uses of each spark. He has done all but one. The one is Tobbe, a Half-Spark, whose name is not on the list. Teodric cannot feel any doubt about leaving it off, and that is the part that frightens him.","family":"Parents and grandmother in Meridian; a younger brother, Ansel, who borrowed his good boots three years ago and returned them scuffed. Teodric has noticed lately that he cannot forgive Ansel for the boots, and he knows exactly why.","hero":{"who":"Sera Solbrand","why":"She used the Judgment of One, once, and then had to live as the one person who'd done it. He thinks about what it took to be sure."},"band":{"act":"The Noon Chorale","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Everyone is sick of them, and he isn't, and he refuses to pretend otherwise; their harmonies were sung at his Sentinel oath.","rv_vote":"The Noon Chorale again, and he will say so to the garrison, and they will throw dusk-pala heels at him."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and would expect to be good at it. He backs Arath, firmly, and has said so in his reports.","edict":"For, until he started the list. Now he is for the Edict and against what it has done to his own hand, and cannot make those two fit."},"wants":"To finish his posting without ever having to choose between the list and the cook.","site":"dawnwatch","town":"Dawnwatch","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:brightmere":{"name":"Romilly Cray","age":54,"role":"Pillar Road trader for walkers and riders; widow of a Stormreach rider","look":"Broad-hipped and quick, grey plait pinned up with a Stormreach chain-link, apron pockets full of glassfruit peel; she stands to eat, always.","voice":"Warm, fast, a little Gale bounce in it from twenty years of marriage; ends things with 'go well' when she means it.","greet":"Wahai — sorry, Aure. Habit. Packs on the left, arrows on the right, bread's yesterday's but honest about it.","lines":["Tempest Hold riders still cut through the rows at a gallop. I sell them the cracked fruit. They tip. Brightmere doesn't like that I don't mind.","My Jarrow's cousins send the arrows down. This month there's a note in the bundle. In Gale. About 'the Assembly.' I can read about half.","I eat standing up. Twenty years with a rider. Sitting down at supper still feels like somebody's died."],"about":"I keep the stall at the bend where the walkers stop. I was married to a Stormreach rider for twenty-one years, I buried him the Stormreach way, which isn't burying at all, and I came home to the orchards. I sell what I know about, which is packs and fruit and arrows.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"good spoken, poor written"}],"childhood":"Romilly was a picker's daughter on the east bank, the one who always fell out of the trees and never broke anything, which the village decided meant she'd be lucky and she decided meant she could keep falling. She grew up on salt fish over glassfruit, on Stump-Tellings where great-uncles said one true thing each about a tree and the true things were usually about kissing someone, and on the Leaning Pillar, which she climbed to the ninth carved ring at eleven and was fetched down by her furious mother. Her mother wrote it in the Day-Book: 'Romilly climbed the Pillar. Romilly was wrong to.' Romilly added, underneath, 'It was very good though.'","schooling":"The Brightmere orchard school in the packing barn, taught between harvests by Master Tolly Brand, who graded fruit and children by the same rule, 'clear, cloudy or cracked', and meant it kindly. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She left at thirteen for the picking, can reckon a crate's price in her head faster than the buyer can, and still writes with her tongue between her teeth.","history":"Her lot came up for the Assize at thirty, and she spent a year in Meridian hearing land and trade cases. One was about a Stormreach horse sold lame on the Pillar Road, and the witness for the seller was a rider called Jarrow Skyle, who shouted his evidence at the court because he could not do otherwise and then shouted an apology for shouting. She found for the buyer. He waited outside to tell her she'd been right. They were married by storm-vow on the Tempest Hold road six months later, shouting it field to field. She lived on the Stormreach side for twenty-one years, rode a little, ate standing up, learned to shout an apology from a roof. Every charge Jarrow spent put grey in his hair; at forty-nine he was white and slow, and he died the next winter. They let him go into the wind off a cliff above the Low Reach. She came home to Brightmere with two saddlebags and took over the stall at the bend. His cousins send arrows to sell, and she sends money back, and the arrangement has never been written down by either side because it doesn't need to be.","family":"No children. A sister, Gwenna, still picks the east bank and thinks Romilly married beneath the orchards. Jarrow's people in Tempest Hold count her as theirs and shout for her at festivals.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"'No Realm should ever be alone.' She lived both sides of a border for twenty-one years and thinks he was right and that it got him knifed."},"band":{"act":"Kestrel Vane","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Jarrow loved her and played the songs off-key on a pipe in the saddle; Kestrel ages every season like he did.","rv_vote":"Kestrel Vane, every year, and she doesn't care who in Brightmere reads it."},"vote":{"local":"Served her lot at thirty and says it was the best year of her life, for reasons that had nothing to do with the law. She's against Arath; she's seen what the riders say about him.","edict":"Against. Her husband's people are already in the Assembly shouting about it, and she agrees with every shout she can translate."},"wants":"To make the trip to Tempest Hold this autumn for Jarrow's cousins' storm-wedding without Brightmere noticing she's gone.","site":"brightmere","town":"Brightmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:brightmere":{"name":"Garet Pinbrand","age":38,"role":"village smith; ladder-irons, pruning hooks, hoops for fruit-crates; the man who won't brace the Leaning Pillar","look":"Short, thick-armed, beard cut square; carries a plumb-bob on a string in his shirt and has a habit of holding it up to things.","voice":"Gruff, says 'Right' before every sentence, uses Stonehold words he picked up at Hearthward without noticing.","greet":"Right. Set. Sorry, that's a Hearthward thing. What's broke?","lines":["Meridian sent a clerk again with drawings for an iron collar round the Pillar. Seventh time. I said no again. I'll say no an eighth.","Riders from the Hold bent three of my ladders galloping through. Bent them like they meant to. I've started charging the Hold for the iron.","My plumb-bob says the Pillar's further out this year. A thumb, since I was a boy. I've written it down. Nobody's asked."],"about":"I make the iron that keeps the orchards up: ladder-hooks, crate-hoops, pruning knives. I trained a year at Hearthward under a Stonehold smith who never once told me I'd done well. I didn't need telling by the end.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a dozen smithing words and the greetings"}],"childhood":"Garet's father was Brightmere's smith before him, a thin, patient man who mended fruit-ladders and wrote in his Day-Book in a hand so small it had to be read with a glass. Garet was the noisy child of a quiet house, always banging something to see what note it made. His favourite thing was the Leaning Pillar: he'd lie on his back in the grass beside it and line up its edge with a fixed point in the noon sky, and he swore at seven that it moved. Nobody believed him, so he started measuring. His father gave him the plumb-bob on his ninth birthday and said, 'Then prove it.' He has been trying ever since.","schooling":"Two years at Master Tolly Brand's barn school, then his father's forge. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered. At nineteen he walked to Hearthward and asked a Stonehold smith, Durra Vell, to teach him to set iron into stone. She said 'Lift,' handed him an anvil horn, and he held it for an hour. He stayed a year, learned to pin a crack in granite, and came home talking like a mountain.","history":"He took over the forge at twenty-four when his father's eyes failed. Brightmere wanted him to make ladder-hooks, and he did, well. But the Pillar kept pulling him back. The year after he came home, Meridian sent its fifth offer to straighten it, and the village asked Garet, now that he could set iron in stone, what he thought. He said it could be braced, and that bracing it would be a lie about what it had done. The village has quoted him ever since. He married Pernet, a fruit-sorter, at twenty-eight; they have two boys who bang things. He has measured the Pillar every Unshadowing for thirty years and every year it has crept outwards a little. His notes are all in his Day-Book, open to anyone. This season the seventh Meridian clerk came with drawings for an iron collar and a fee large enough to pay for a new forge. Garet sent him off and then sat up the whole of the next shift redoing the sums, and the sums came out the same: at this rate the Pillar will not last ten more Unshadowings with walkers going under it barefoot.","family":"Wife Pernet, sons Brin and Oswy (eleven and eight). His father Tavish is alive, nearly blind, and asks every evening, 'How far out?'","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"'Lift. Then talk.' His Hearthward teacher said it every morning, and he thinks most of Brightmere's arguments would end if people carried something first."},"band":{"act":"Landslide","realm":"Stonehold","why":"He heard them at a Hearthward fair the night before he came home and stood at the front so long his ears rang for a week.","rv_vote":"Landslide, because Stonehold never gets Lightforge lamps either and he thinks it's petty."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, and would bring his plumb-bob. Torn on Arath: a man who wants things done properly ought to want the Pillar done properly too.","edict":"Torn. He doesn't care who has a spark. He cares that the same Citadel that wants to register every spark can't be bothered to read his Pillar notes."},"wants":"For somebody in Meridian to read the thirty years of measurements before the Pillar decides for them.","site":"brightmere","town":"Brightmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:brightmere":{"name":"Lissel Whitbrand","age":10,"role":"picker's child and granddaughter of Lenna Whitbrand; a new Full Spark waiting for her Lamp Years","look":"Gap-toothed, freckled across the nose, plaits tied with fruit-twine; always holding a glassfruit up to the noon to count its rings like her gran does.","voice":"Very loud, very sure, asks two questions for every answer.","greet":"Are you a rider? You're not a rider. Do you want to know how old this fruit is? It's four. I counted.","lines":["A man from Meridian came to look at my spark. Twice. He wrote on a board. Gran made him tea and didn't give him any fruit.","A Stormreach rider ate sitting down on the bank. Sitting! He thought we were pale because of the fruit. We're pale because of the sun, everybody knows that.","Gran can tell how old a fruit is. I can tell how old a person is. You're about forty. Am I right? I'm right."],"about":"I'm Lissel. My gran is Lenna Whitbrand and she's the best picker on the Road. My lamp went off like a slap and now I have to go to the Lamp Years. I don't want to, because there's no orchard there. I asked.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, fast"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, mostly 'how much' and 'go away'"}],"childhood":"Lissel has had ten years of being the loudest Whitbrand in a family of quiet pickers. She was born in harvest and spent her first summer in a fruit-crate lined with sacking at the end of her gran's row. At four she bit into a Citadel-grade glassfruit from the clearest crate and was made to write her first Day-Book entry about it, in her gran's hand guiding hers. At six she followed a hawk from Goldfield for a whole bell and got lost in the next village's barley. She has a tree of her own, a young one beside a stump with a white stone on it, where her grandfather's tree stood, and at his Stump-Telling she said the one true thing was that he snored in the shade, and everyone laughed and then cried.","schooling":"One year at the packing-barn school under Master Tolly Brand, now very old, who still grades everything 'clear, cloudy or cracked' and calls Lissel 'cracked, in the best way'. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp went off so hard the tester dropped her chalk. Full Spark. She is meant to begin the Lamp Years in Meridian next season and is fighting it with every argument a ten-year-old can find.","history":"Most of Lissel's life has happened in the last two years. The Lamp Test made her the first Full Spark in the Whitbrand line in four generations, and Brightmere threw a supper where people kept touching her head. Then the Edict talk started. This season a Meridian clerk came out to measure the strength of her lamp-answer for the new children's register, and then came back a month later to measure it again, because the first number looked high. Her gran made him tea both times and didn't offer fruit, which Lissel understood was the worst thing her gran could do to anyone. Lissel has started practising holding her spark small, the way she's seen her gran hold her temper. She doesn't know if that's allowed. She has asked three adults and got three answers. She is also, as of last week, in disgrace: she ate a fruit from the clear crate, didn't write it down, and then wrote it the next day with 'yesterday' in front, and she is not sure if that counts as a lie.","family":"Lives with her gran Lenna and her mother Fia, a sorter; her father picks for a Meridian estate and comes home every tenth day. Her little brother Dob, four, is her sworn enemy and best audience.","hero":{"who":"Her gran, Lenna Whitbrand","why":"Gran has never once told a buyer a fruit was younger than it was, and people come from the other end of the Road to buy from her because of it."},"band":{"act":"Sunwarm","realm":"Lightforge","why":"The pickers sing their summer song in the rows, and she knows all the words and some that aren't the words.","rv_vote":"Sunwarm, and she'll make Pim vote for them too, even though he can't."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for the lot. She says if it came up she'd stand and make everyone in Meridian peel their own fruit.","edict":"'Gran says it's for people like me. And Gran's face went funny saying it.' She is for whatever lets her stay in the orchard."},"wants":"To be allowed to stay in Brightmere and be a picker, spark and all.","site":"brightmere","town":"Brightmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:brightmere:b":{"name":"Haskel Merrow","age":79,"role":"retired orchard-keeper; the last living coin-walker from the dry year","look":"Bent like a ladder, white tufts over each ear, a coin on a string round his neck worn smooth on one face; walks the mere-bank every day with a stick he uses mainly to point.","voice":"Slow, dry, says everything twice in slightly different words in case you missed it the first time.","greet":"Aure. Seen you. Seen you before, I think. Or someone with your walk.","lines":["When I was nine the mere went to a puddle and we found a hundred years of coins in the mud. We walked every one home. Every one. Nearly.","I've had my lot come up three times. Three. I know exactly how dull the Assize is, and I'd tell Arath so.","They want to straighten the Pillar. Leave it. Some things lean. I lean. Nobody's straightening me."],"about":"I kept the north-bank trees for sixty years and my son keeps them now, wrongly. I'm the last of the children who walked the coins home in the dry year. I talk about it too much. I know I talk about it too much.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, old Pillar Road dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting, and he refuses to improve"}],"childhood":"Haskel was nine the summer the mere shrank to a puddle the size of a threshing floor. Before that his childhood was ordinary orchard work: picking, peeling, falling asleep in the packing barn. After it, everything was measured from it. The white mud was full of coins, a century of witnesses dropped by walkers at the Unshadowing, and his grandmother Beda organised the village children into walking parties to carry each coin back to the house whose assayer had signed it. Haskel's party walked to Gleamford, to Meridian, to farms whose names they had to ask twice. He saw more of Lightforge in that month than in the seventy years since. He remembers the coins being cold in his hand in a place where nothing is ever cold.","schooling":"The barn school under old Mistress Hester Brand, grandmother of the present teacher, who taught letters from coin-stamps because the coin-walk had made every child in the village able to read an assayer's mark. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. He left at twelve and has never regretted it, and says so twice.","history":"He took over his father's north-bank trees at twenty, married Edna Clove from the south bank in a wedding that both banks still argue about, and grew the most consistent fruit on the Road for sixty years: never the clearest, never cracked. His lot came up for the Assize at thirty-one, fifty and sixty-eight; he served all three years without distinction and with growing impatience, and at the last one he asked the presiding councillor whether the cases had always been this slow or whether it was him. Edna died nine years ago. At her Stump-Telling, when the tree they'd tended together was felled, he said only, 'She was right about the pruning,' which for Haskel was a speech. Since then he walks the mere-bank daily and looks into the water. He tells people he's checking the level. He is looking for the coins they missed.","family":"Son Jory keeps the trees; daughter Mab married into Wheatley and bakes pala he calls 'nearly pale enough'. Five grandchildren, one of whom reads to him.","hero":{"who":"His grandmother, Beda Merrow","why":"She organised a whole village of children to walk a century of strangers' coins home, and never once let them keep one. Nearly never."},"band":{"act":"Plain Speaking","realm":"Lightforge","why":"No metaphors. He has spent seventy years being told that the Pillar is 'a symbol' and the mere is 'a mirror' and would like one song that's just about a cart.","rv_vote":"Plain Speaking, and he'll say why, twice."},"vote":{"local":"Would stand again if his lot came up, and would complain the whole year. Against Arath, on the grounds that Arath looks like the councillor who was slow the last time.","edict":"Doubtful. He's never had a spark, but he walked coins back to a hundred houses and learnt that most people don't hide anything worth finding."},"wants":"To see the mere shrink once more before he dies, so he can look for the ones they missed.","site":"brightmere","town":"Brightmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:candlewick":{"name":"Oriel Lampwright","age":31,"role":"village trader and Spiritveil go-between; vat-master Merin Lampwright's daughter","look":"Tall, sooty at the cuffs, a Spiritveil willow-bark hairpin she claims was a gift from a customer; smells of honey and tallow in equal measure.","voice":"Brisk, wry, slips into Lanthic when she's counting so nobody can follow the sum.","greet":"Aure, or 'lamp's lit', depending which way you came in. Bread, packs, bows, candles, mist-pears. The pears are new. Don't ask what I paid.","lines":["Dawnwatch taxes our wax on the way up. The only light the Rim makes for itself, and they put a toll on it. Write that down, I have.","The lantern-keepers pay in honey half the time. You can't pay a wax toll in honey. I've tried. The sergeant ate it.","My father says he can tell a lie by how a candle gutters. I've never once lit a candle near him on purpose."],"about":"I run the trade for Candlewick: our candles up to Dawnwatch and over the mist-path, honey and pears and lantern-oil back. My father renders, I sell. We agree on almost nothing except the price of tallow.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"good; reads it better than she writes Clear"}],"childhood":"Oriel grew up under the Dipping Wheel, the only child of a vat-master who loved his vats and a mother, Rhosa, who loved the mist-path and walked it as a pedlar when Oriel was small. Her First Wick burned every birthday on the moot-house sill beside the door with the Spiritveil merchant's lamp-name painted on it, and she spent her childhood asking who the merchant was and getting a different, fond, wrong answer from every adult. When she was eight her mother took her over the mist-path for the first time, and a lantern-keeper gave her a honey cake off a copying-room sill and told her it was for the ghosts but they wouldn't mind. At her First Telling at twelve she snuffed her wick and said she meant to go where the honey came from. The village laughed, and then gave her the told name she still has, Oriel Who Went Over, two years later, when she did.","schooling":"The Candlewick lamp-school in the moot-house, kept by Master Davin Chell, who taught arithmetic with wick-counts and made every child dip one candle a week and sell it to him, honestly priced. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. At fourteen she spent a winter in a Spiritveil copying-room as a paid runner, learning Lanthic by carrying it.","history":"From fourteen she worked the mist-path with her mother, carrying candles over and honey back, until her mother's knees failed and Oriel took the trade alone at twenty. She learned which lantern-keepers paid in coin and which in pears, and which would talk about the old merchant on the moot-house door and which would only smile and tilt their heads. She built Candlewick's Spiritveil trade from three customers to forty. At twenty-six she fought Dawnwatch over the wax toll in a letter so precise the captain wrote back to thank her for it and still didn't lower the toll. This season the Edict has made the mist-path harder: lamp-checks mean every carrier's lantern is counted going over, and a Meridian clerk now sits at the head of the path with a ledger. Her Day-Book, open on the shop counter, is now written half in Lanthic. Nobody in Candlewick can read that half. Everyone in Candlewick has noticed.","family":"Father Merin, mother Rhosa (housebound, sharp), no siblings. She is courting a lantern-keeper of Spiritveil, Ilthen, who is two hundred and forty and looks twenty-five, and her father does not know.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"The first lantern was a cracked clay cup. Oriel sells candles to people who keep lamps for nine hundred years and thinks that beginning was the best part."},"band":{"act":"The Lamplit","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"The same idols for three hundred years; Ilthen saw their first show and says they've hardly changed, and she finds that funnier every time.","rv_vote":"The Lamplit, mostly to make Ilthen laugh."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and would bring every wax-toll ledger with her. Against Arath, against the toll, against the clerk at the path-head.","edict":"Against. The lamp-checks have turned the only honest trade road on the Rim into a queue."},"wants":"To bring Ilthen to Candlewick for the Dipping Wheel lighting without her father guttering any candles.","site":"candlewick","town":"Candlewick","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:candlewick":{"name":"Dunstan Rendle","age":44,"role":"village smith; Dipping Wheel axles, vat-hooks and wick-irons; left the Conclave in his second year","look":"Lean, ink-dark hair going grey at the parting, soot in the lines of his face; a Conclave sash with two knots used to tie back his apron.","voice":"Quiet, deliberate, asks more than he answers; his laugh comes late, after he's thought about it.","greet":"Seen. Something to mend? Or something you'd like to ask without asking? Both are fine at this forge.","lines":["The Wheel's axle was forged by my great-grandmother. I've rebuilt it three times. It's still hers. That's how axles work.","I went to Korrhaven for two years. I came home. People ask why. I tell them: I wanted to keep my doubts.","A man who can't doubt can't forgive. That's not heresy. That's the price of Radiance, written in the Conclave's own book."],"about":"I keep the Wheel turning and the hooks sharp. I was a Full Spark once, the real kind. I walked away from it. I'd do it again.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"Conclave-fluent"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"reads a little, from mist-path traders"}],"childhood":"Dunstan was born to a line of Candlewick smiths and a mother who rendered, in a house whose back window looked straight onto the Dipping Wheel, so that he grew up thinking the whole world glowed orange once a day. He was a solemn child who asked his grandmother, at five, whether candles minded being lit. She said they did, and that was the point. At his First Wick lighting each year he watched the flame rather than the cake. He had his first Rim fever at seven and lay for a week in the dark with a damp cloth, and remembers it as the happiest week of his early life, because nobody needed him to see anything.","schooling":"Candlewick moot-house school under Master Davin Chell, then a shock: the Radiance lamp at his Lamp Test lit like a struck match. Full Spark. Three Lamp Years at the Meridian lower school, then two years in the Radiance Tower at Korrhaven. He was good at it. By the second year he noticed his doubts going, small ones first, and then he caught himself unable to forgive a friend for a lie that hadn't happened yet. He walked out with two knots in his sash.","history":"Leaving the Conclave without leave cost his family a fine and him a reputation. In Meridian it was recorded as 'withdrew'; in Candlewick it was recorded as 'came home', which is kinder. He took his grandmother's forge at twenty and rebuilt the Wheel's axle the first of three times. He married Jenet Wick, a chandler, at twenty-eight; she died of a lung fever six years later and he named her in the Rushbrook Grief Room, the nearest. He raised their child Kit alone, badly and well. Somewhere in his thirties he began to go to the Penumbrist readings that the Rim has always half-hidden, and found that the people there had arrived, by a different road, at the same thing he had learned in the Radiance Tower: that mercy needs shade. He has read at them himself for six years. His Day-Book records each one as 'Forge. Late.', which is true. This season the Edict outlawed the Penumbrists by name, and his teacher, an old reader who once slept three nights in Gisla Fenn's back room, has gone over the mist-path and not come back.","family":"Child Kit, twelve. A sister, Orla, renders at the vats and thinks he should have stayed at Korrhaven and 'made something of it'.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"The first Penumbrist stood in the Rim's dusk and said mercy needs shade. He'd felt the noon taking his mercy away and walked home to find she'd said it first."},"band":{"act":"Two Windows","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A Lightforge–Shadowdeep couple singing to each other through two windows that don't quite line up; he and Jenet used to sing it, badly, across the forge yard.","rv_vote":"Two Windows, for Jenet, who would have."},"vote":{"local":"If his lot came up he would stand, and would vote against every Edict case put before him, in writing, with reasons. Against Arath.","edict":"A secret Penumbrist, and against it with everything he has; he's already on the old Conclave register, and the memory audits will want the years since he left."},"wants":"To bring his teacher back over the mist-path, or at least to learn where she stopped.","site":"candlewick","town":"Candlewick","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:candlewick":{"name":"Kit Rendle","age":12,"role":"smith's child, chandler's apprentice at the Wheel; told name Kit Who Asked Back","look":"Small, all elbows, singed eyebrows, a snuffed wick-stub on a thread round their wrist against all custom.","voice":"Answers a question with a question, deliberately, and then grins as if they've won something.","greet":"Seen. Did you come for candles? Or did you come for the cakes and you're pretending it's candles?","lines":["Merin Lampwright says he can tell a lie by the gutter of a candle. I lit one next to him and said I'd done my chores. It didn't gutter. I hadn't. So.","My First Telling was last month. I snuffed my wick and said I mean to learn what the dark is for. Everybody went quiet. Then they gave me a told name.","Dad goes quiet when the Edict comes up. So I've gone quiet. We're very quiet in our house now."],"about":"I'm Kit. I'm twelve as of the Telling. I work the Wheel with the dippers and my dad's the smith. I'm Kit Who Asked Back, because when the moot asked what I meant I asked them what they meant first.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few phrases from mist-traders"}],"childhood":"Kit's mother died when they were six, of a lung fever that took her in a week, and their childhood since has been their father and the forge and the Wheel and a great deal of being looked after by the whole village in turns. They were a quiet small child and became a loud medium one. They have always had a temper, a hot sudden one, and from about eight they learned to sit on it: to go out to the brook at dusk and throw stones until it passed. At ten they were found dipping their own wicks at the vats at night and instead of being punished were taken on, because the dip was good. They have never been scared of the dark. They have always been interested in why the adults talk differently in it.","schooling":"The moot-house school under Master Davin Chell, who taught them to price a candle and read a contract and who caught them, once, correcting his arithmetic on the slate and made them teach the next lesson. Lamp Test at eight: nothing, in any of the eight lamps. Spark-poor. Kit was relieved in a way they didn't understand at the time and understand better now.","history":"At their First Telling, a month ago, Kit stood on the moot-house step at dusk while their First Wick burned for the last time, snuffed it with their own fingers, and said, 'I mean to learn what the dark is for.' Candlewick is a Rim village and has heard bolder things, but not usually from a smith's child this season, with a Meridian clerk sitting at the mist-path head. There was a silence. Then old Gisla Fenn said, 'And what do you think it's for?', and Kit said, 'What do you?', and the moot laughed, and they had their told name before the cakes came round. Since then Kit has done something nobody does: read their father's Day-Book from the first page, years of it, the way the law says anyone may and nobody ever bothers. They found the evenings, one a month, written only as 'Forge. Late.' They have said nothing. They have started keeping a second notebook, about other people's evenings.","family":"Father Dunstan; aunt Orla at the vats; a cousin, Bree, who is ten and follows Kit everywhere and is not allowed to.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder","why":"She was tried in Lightforge for 'concealed anger.' Kit has been sitting on their temper since they were eight and wants to know when that became a crime."},"band":{"act":"Calder Glass","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A battle rapper who cannot lie, so every insult has to be true, and Kit thinks that's the hardest rule anyone's ever had to rhyme in.","rv_vote":"Calder Glass, and Kit has written a verse back to him that they'll never send."},"vote":{"local":"If their lot came up they'd stand, and ask everyone on the Assize what they meant by 'properly'.","edict":"'Ask my dad.' Then, quieter: against, because of how he goes quiet."},"wants":"To be told the truth about the evenings by their father, without having to ask.","site":"candlewick","town":"Candlewick","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:candlewick:b":{"name":"Gisla Fenn","age":66,"role":"tallow-cake fryer at the moot-house; widow; the woman with the back room","look":"Round, red-cheeked from the fryer, arms freckled with old spatter-burns, a Spiritveil honey-pot always tucked in the crook of one elbow.","voice":"Loud, cheerful, completely indiscreet; says the dangerous thing first and the cake price second.","greet":"Warm cakes, love. Four for a copper. Meridian folk look at them like they're a lie and then eat four.","lines":["A Penumbrist reader slept in my back room three nights last season. I wrote it in my Day-Book plain as you like. Nobody from the castle came to read it. Make of that what you will.","I've bought three coils of rope off Hessa at Dawnwatch this month. I've no use for rope. Don't ask me why. Ask me in a month.","First skimming of the vats, that's the only fat for a cake. Second skimming's for candles. Third's for Meridian."],"about":"I fry. I've fried at the moot-house for forty years, and before me my mother, and before her a woman who wasn't my mother but might as well have been. My husband's dead, my children are grown, my back room's empty. Mostly.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, broad Rim"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"poor, with confidence"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"'honey', 'thank you', 'too dear'"}],"childhood":"Gisla was raised by the moot-house fryer, a big quiet woman called Annet Hob who took her in at four when her own mother went over the mist-path to sell and stayed in Spiritveil for six years. Nobody called it abandonment, because her mother wrote every week, and Gisla read the letters aloud at the fryer to whoever was queuing. She grew up thinking everybody's mother lived in the mist. She learned to fry before she could read, standing on a crate, and she learned the first skimming from the second by smell at seven. When her mother came back at last, older and silver-haired from the Pale, Gisla was ten and already had a trade. They were kind to each other, and careful, ever after.","schooling":"Master Davin Chell's predecessor, Mistress Wenna Lisk, at the moot-house school, who taught letters from Gisla's mother's letters because they were the only long writing in the village anyone wanted to read. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. She left at eleven to fry full time. Mistress Lisk kept a cake tin of her own on the desk and gave a tallow-cake to any child who could read a whole letter aloud without stopping; Gisla won it most weeks.","history":"At twenty she married Dannel Harrow, whom everyone in Candlewick calls Old Harrow though he died at fifty, a dipper at the Wheel who sang through every shift and was terrible at it. They had three children, all grown and gone up the Rim to Dawnwatch and Rushbrook. She took the fryer from Annet Hob at twenty-six and has made the same tallow-cakes, dusted with the same Spiritveil honey, for forty years. She has stood her Assize lot twice and voted, both times, against every case the Citadel sent and for every case the Rim sent, and has been told this is not how it works. Harrow died nine years ago and her back room has stood empty since. Last season a reader came to the door at dusk, an old woman with a Penumbrist's grey shawl, and asked for three nights. Gisla gave her four, the fourth being for the rain. On the last morning the reader asked her to carry a message to the trader at Dawnwatch, 'about rope'. Gisla has been up to Dawnwatch four times since. She has bought three coils of rope. She has not yet said the words.","family":"Three grown children up the Rim; seven grandchildren. Her mother's letters from Spiritveil, two hundred and eleven of them, are in a cake tin under her bed.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"She lit the First Hearth in her own kitchen, and nobody ever knelt to her. Gisla thinks most of the important things in the Nine have happened next to somebody's fryer."},"band":{"act":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Seventy and kneading dough on stage. Gisla saw her once on a trip to Hearthward and cried into her cake.","rv_vote":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens, and anyone who calls that disloyal can buy their cakes in Meridian."},"vote":{"local":"She's stood her lot twice and would stand again. Against Arath, loudly.","edict":"Against, openly, to anyone who'll listen and several who won't. 'I've written it down. Let them come and read it.'"},"wants":"To find the nerve to say the reader's message to Hessa Ropewright before the lamp-checks start.","site":"candlewick","town":"Candlewick","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:prismport":{"name":"Oswin Tumbler","age":42,"role":"harbour-mole trader; ex-glassblower with spent lungs; deals with Metalcrown buyers","look":"Barrel-chested and wheezing, forearms glossy with old burn-shine, a sunwine tumbler of his own blowing hung on a cord round his neck as a pendant.","voice":"Cheerful and short of breath, sentences come in two halves with a breath in the middle.","greet":"Aure! Kit, packs — arrows, bread. And if you want a tumbler — I've got the seconds. No. Joke. There are no seconds. Cracking Day.","lines":["Metalcrown buyers on the mole every week now. Pay in iron bars. Polite. Ask for lens blanks ground to a curve I've never seen on any Lightforge instrument.","Blew glass for twenty years. Lungs went at thirty-five. Now I sell boatmen rope and argue with gulls.","Kiln-perch off our slab is the best in town. Every glassworks says that. Ours is true."],"about":"I was a blower at the Hollin works for twenty years. My lungs gave out and I came down to the mole. I sell kit to boatmen and walkers, I buy salvage off the beaches, and I talk to the Metalcrown buyers because nobody else will.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, Prismport dock speech"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a few dozen words, picked up on the mole"}],"childhood":"Oswin was born in the cottage behind the Hollin glassworks, where his mother kept the furnace logs and his father blew sunwine tumblers, six hundred a shift, each one a little different and each one perfect. Oswin's earliest memory is sitting on an upturned bucket watching his father turn a gather on the pipe while the whole room glowed white. He was a happy, greedy child who ate kiln-perch off the slab at the furnace mouth whenever he could steal one and who blistered his fingers at five trying to catch a falling bubble. On Cracking Day he loved the smashing more than anything, and at eight was found sneaking a cracked bowl home because it was the prettiest thing he'd ever seen. His father made him carry it to the mole himself and throw it. He cried. He was right to cry, his father wrote, but it was still flawed.","schooling":"The Prismport kiln-school, where children learned their letters from the marks on the glassworks' crates, under Mistress Ama Hollin, who once stopped a lesson to make the whole room watch a sunset reflection in a lamp-chimney because 'you'll never see it out there'. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He went into his father's works at twelve as gatherer's boy.","history":"He gathered at twelve, blew at sixteen and was a master blower at twenty-five, known for tumblers so even that buyers stacked them twelve high to show off. The furnace air did to his lungs what it does to blowers' lungs, sooner than most. At thirty-five he couldn't finish a gather without coughing. He left the works with a pension of one tumbler a week for life and took a stall on the harbour mole. For seven years it was boatmen's rope, bread and salvage. Then, two seasons ago, the Metalcrown buyers began to come: men and women in black coats with tally plates at their wrists, polite as a closed door, paying in iron bars. Prismport doesn't like them and doesn't like refusing iron. Oswin likes them; they talk to him about pressure and heat as if he were still a blower. They have started asking him to find lens blanks ground to a curve no Lightforge instrument uses. He has found some. He has written every sale in his Day-Book, curve and all.","family":"Married to Bryn, a net-mender from Sunmere, who thinks the Metalcrown buyers are trouble and says so in his Day-Book in the margin. Two daughters, both blowers' apprentices.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright and Idris Hollowfire","why":"They built the first Spark Reactor out of nothing in a Realm with no spark. He's spent his life making something out of sand and fire and thinks they're the only people who'd understand a furnace."},"band":{"act":"Long Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Garage rock about shift-ends; a Metalcrown buyer hummed it on the mole and Oswin made him sing the whole thing.","rv_vote":"He'll give it to Long Whistle and be the only Prismporter who does."},"vote":{"local":"He hopes his lot never comes up and would stand if it did. Shrugs at Arath.","edict":"Indifferent, and a little ashamed of it. 'It's not glass. I don't understand anything that isn't glass.'"},"wants":"One more season of blowing; his doctor says no, his daughters say no, he still has his pipe on the wall.","site":"prismport","town":"Prismport","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:prismport":{"name":"Ysa Pontilwright","age":35,"role":"Prismport's tool-smith; blowpipes, pontils, jacks and shears for every glassworks on the shore","look":"Tall and rangy, hair scraped back under a leather cap, both forearms scarred in neat parallel lines from pontil-heat; walks very fast.","voice":"Clipped and certain, loves a rule, quotes the Prismport charter word for word.","greet":"Seen. If it's a blowpipe, I'll know whose works it came from by the bore. If it's a weapon, fine, but I'll want it back for the iron.","lines":["My lot came up last year. I sat on the Assize. I voted for Arath. I'd vote for him again tomorrow, and I'll tell you why if you've a bell to spare.","The Courts sent three panes back this month. Glass doesn't take sides. But somebody poured them, and somebody should check.","Every pontil in this town is mine or my mother's. You can tell. Hers are better. I'm not saying it twice."],"about":"I make the tools the glassworks can't make for themselves. My mother made them before me and made them better. I served my Assize year last year and I believe in it, which I'm told is unfashionable.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, very exact"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent; she learned it to read the Concord trade law"}],"childhood":"Ysa was Brigge Pontilwright's only daughter, and Brigge was the finest tool-smith Prismport has had in a century, a woman who once made a blowpipe so true that the Hollin works framed it. Ysa grew up in her mother's shadow, which in Lightforge is an odd thing to say, and in her mother's forge, which was loud and hot and full of men who deferred to Brigge and treated Ysa as an apprentice from the age of four. She liked rules early. At seven she drew up a chart of which glassworks owed the forge money and pinned it on the door, and Brigge left it there, which Ysa took as the highest praise she ever got. She was not a warm child. She was a fair one.","schooling":"The Prismport kiln-school under Mistress Ama Hollin, who praised her handwriting and worried about her. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp flickered twice and settled. Half-Spark. She was sent to the Meridian district school for two years at her own insistence to learn law properly, and came back able to quote the glassmakers' charter, the Witness Court rules and the Concord trade code clause by clause.","history":"She went into her mother's forge full time at fifteen and was making blowpipes good enough to sell by eighteen. At twenty-two the Great Lens needed a new iron mount, the first in sixty years, and Brigge gave Ysa the commission. Ysa failed it: her mount sat a hair out of true, and the white spot it threw onto the harbour wandered. Brigge said nothing, reforged it herself over three nights, and let Ysa's mark go on the finished piece. Ysa found out from her mother's Day-Book, months later. She has never corrected the mark and never forgiven either of them. Brigge died four years ago; Ysa named her in the Grief Room and took the forge. Last year her lot came up for the Assize. She went to Meridian gladly, was diligent, and voted for High Councillor Arath to enforce the Edict because the law had been written and someone had to do it properly. She sat on one of the first spark-register hearings. The case was a Prismport furnace-hand who hadn't registered a Half-Spark, and she voted to fine him, and she still sees him on the mole.","family":"Unmarried. A half-brother, Coll, works the Great Lens crew and thinks she's insufferable; she thinks he's lazy; they eat together every tenth day.","hero":{"who":"Her mother, Brigge Pontilwright","why":"Brigge made a blowpipe so true it hangs in a frame. Ysa has spent thirteen years trying to make one that deserves to be next to it."},"band":{"act":"The Glare","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Arena rock with no subtlety at all, and she likes music that does exactly what it says.","rv_vote":"The Glare, because Lightforge deserves to win when it's best, and it usually is."},"vote":{"local":"Served her lot gladly and voted for Arath. Would do both again.","edict":"For. 'The law was written. It should be kept. If it's wrong, write a better one.' She still sees the furnace-hand she fined."},"wants":"To make one tool her mother would have framed.","site":"prismport","town":"Prismport","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:prismport":{"name":"Quillon Panewell","age":58,"role":"witness-glass pourer at the Clearwater Works; poured the three panes the Courts sent back","look":"Thickset, bald and shining with sweat, no eyebrows and singed lashes, a pane-pourer's long leather gauntlet always tucked into his belt.","voice":"Gentle and stubborn; calls everyone 'love'; never raises his voice even when he's furious.","greet":"Aure, love. Mind the hot-stones, they don't look hot. Nothing here looks the way it is, except the glass.","lines":["The Courts sent three panes back this month. Said they showed a witness flinch who swore she never flinched. We poured them true. Glass doesn't take sides, love.","Forty years on witness-glass. You learn to read a pane like a face. Every one I've poured, I'd stand on.","Cracking Day, I always take the first swing. It's not anger. It's just that someone has to start."],"about":"I pour the glass the witnesses stand on. Forty years. Every pane in the Courts across the water has a Prismport pourer's mark on the edge, and about a third of them are mine.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent; he reads the Courts' Bridge-language orders"}],"childhood":"Quillon was the youngest of nine in a lime-burner's family, the last of the old kiln-families before the kilns became glassworks, and he grew up white with lime-dust from the knees down. His grandmother told him the old story of the apprentice who first melted lake-sand clear, and he decided at six that he'd be the next one to find something. He found mainly blisters. He was a slow, sweet, stubborn boy who could stand at a furnace mouth longer than anyone his age without flinching, and the pourers noticed. At ten he was let into the Clearwater Works to sweep. At eleven he stood beside a master pourer through a full witness-pane casting and didn't blink once, and the master wrote in her Day-Book that night: 'The Panewell boy has eyes.'","schooling":"The kiln-school under Mistress Ama Hollin's mother, Mistress Tilde Hollin, who made the children write their Day-Book entries on slate and then hold the slates up to a lamp, 'so you can see if anything's behind it'. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. He went into the Works at twelve. He learned to write a fair hand and to read a pane's casting-mark, and he failed geography outright, because he could not believe there were places without glass in them.","history":"He apprenticed under Mistress Hanne Clare, the pourer who said he had eyes, and became a journeyman at eighteen and a master pourer at twenty-nine. Witness-glass is the hardest thing Prismport makes: the pane must be perfectly flat and perfectly clear, so the Courts can watch a witness's reflection in it for the smallest flinch. Quillon has poured more than three hundred. He married late, at forty, to Inge, a lens-grinder, and they have a son who wants to be a boatman. This season the Witness Courts sent three panes back, the first returns in his working life, with a note that the glass had shown a witness flinch who swore under oath she had not. Quillon had the panes carried to the Works and examined every one with Aure Glass's best lens. They are flat. They are clear. They are true. He believes the witness flinched. He believes the Courts want the glass to agree with her, and he has said so, very quietly, in a letter the Works master has not yet sent.","family":"Wife Inge, son Nils (sixteen). Seven living siblings scattered round the shore; he is 'named-uncle' to nineteen of their children.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"The first Lightwarden wrote the Radiant Laws to make truth something you could stand on. Quillon pours the thing they stand on."},"band":{"act":"Somebody","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"An anonymous pop star nobody has ever seen. He spends his life on glass that shows everything and finds it restful.","rv_vote":"Somebody, and he'll write 'I don't know who' in his Day-Book next to it."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up. Against Arath, since the Courts started sending panes back.","edict":"Doubtful. He's for honesty; he's beginning to think the Edict is about something else."},"wants":"For the Courts to stand the witness on a fourth pane, in front of him.","site":"prismport","town":"Prismport","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:prismport:b":{"name":"Thea Lensman","age":71,"role":"keeper of the Great Lens on the harbour mole; cleaner, gull-chaser, spot-watcher","look":"Tiny, brown and wrinkled as a walnut, a straw hat tied under her chin, a gull feather in the band and a soft cloth in each hand.","voice":"Chatty, sing-song, talks to the Lens as if it's a slow relative.","greet":"Mind the spot, dear! It's on the water this bell, but it wanders. Like me.","lines":["Gulls go round the Lens. Always round. They know. Smarter than half the pilots.","Once a year, at a certain angle, the spot lands on the Witness Court windows across the lake. I've seen things in those windows. I've written them down.","When the Lens is wrong I see seven colours at the edge. Like that Prism song. It's never wrong for long."],"about":"I keep the Great Lens. I clean it at the first bell and the sixth, I chase off the gulls, and I watch the spot so the pilots don't have to. My mother kept it before me, and her mother.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, old harbour dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words for boatmen"}],"childhood":"Thea grew up on the mole. Her mother, Lensman by name and by trade, kept the Great Lens and kept her daughter beside it from the day she could walk, tied to the rail on a long cord so she wouldn't fall in. Thea's whole childhood was the spot of white fire on the water and the gulls going round. She thought every child in the Nine Realms had a Lens. At seven she was told that the Lens had been ground over eleven years by a man who went nearly blind doing it, and she cried for a whole afternoon for the man, and her mother wrote: 'Thea cried for the grinder. I didn't stop her.' She learned to clean the Lens before she learned to write, and still holds a pen like a cloth.","schooling":"A season at the kiln-school, which she hated because it was indoors. Mistress Tilde Hollin let her sit by the window. Lamp Test at eight: nothing, though she swears the Radiance lamp leaned towards her. Spark-poor. Her real school was her mother and the Lens. She learned enough letters to keep the Lens log, which her mother had kept in pictures, and she still draws a small gull beside any entry she thinks matters.","history":"She took the Lens at twenty-six when her mother's hands went stiff. She has cleaned it twice a day for forty-five years. She married a pilot, Joss Lensman, who took her name because it was the better trade; he steered by her spot for thirty years and drowned in a squall within sight of it, which she has written about once and never since. She kept on. Her eyes started failing at sixty. Now she tends the Lens mostly by feel, and her grand-niece Pella reads the spot for her when it wanders. Once a year, in high summer, when the Lens is set a certain way, its spot falls across Clearwater onto the tall windows of the Witness Courts, and for most of a bell anyone on the mole with good eyes can see into the great hall. For forty years she has written down what she saw on that day. She has stopped seeing it herself, and this year Pella will look for her.","family":"Widow. No children. Grand-niece Pella, fifteen, who wants to be a pilot like the uncle she never met.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"'The Lamp That Leaned.' She's spent her life with a light that wanders a little and thinks the copyist understood that some lights have to lean to land true."},"band":{"act":"Prism","realm":"Lightforge","why":"'Seven Colours'. When the Lens is off true she sees seven colours at its edge, and the song sounds exactly like that looks.","rv_vote":"Prism, for the Sparkbeat Cup and the lamp both, and she dances to it badly on the mole."},"vote":{"local":"She has stood every time her lot came up. Torn on Arath: a nice face, she says, but she's seen nice faces in those windows.","edict":"Torn. She has nothing to register, and forty years of watching the Courts through a window tells her they don't always do things properly."},"wants":"For Pella to see the Court windows clearly on the high-summer day and write it down the way she did.","site":"prismport","town":"Prismport","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:gleamford":{"name":"Emeric Sheen","age":50,"role":"ford-side outfitter and changer for Stormreach traders; a former Hearth Scholar","look":"Lanky, greying at the brows, a rider's leather coat over a Lightforge white shirt; keeps a rock-hard Conclave loaf on the shelf behind him like a trophy.","voice":"Easy and amused, half his sentences in Gale-shaped Bridge; counts aloud in three languages without noticing.","greet":"Wahai, or Aure, whichever gets you through the door. Packs, bows, arrows. I'll change your crossings too, but count them after me.","lines":["Stormreach folk pay in crossings and shout the sum at you from the door. You get used to it. Count it twice, though. Wind's not the only thing that moves up there.","That loaf? The Flameborne King gave it me the day I left Korrhaven. Thirty-two years old. Harder than the ford stones.","Tempest Hold riders water their horses upstream of the crayfish beds. Every season the broth goes cloudy for a week, and every season we write them a letter."],"about":"I outfit the Stormreach trade: packs, bows, crossings changed. I'm spark-poor and I went to the Conclave anyway, on the Flameborne King's bread. Best four years of my life and the worst porridge.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"Conclave-fluent"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"fluent, shouts well"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a little, from Hearth Scholar friends"}],"childhood":"Emeric's mother swept the Piercing Hall and his father carted silver down from the eastern hills, and the boy grew up between the bench and the ford with grey ore-dust in his hair. He was clever in a way that annoyed the assayers' children: he could weigh a coin in his palm and guess it to a grain, and he did it for pocket-copper at the ford until his mother found out. Stormreach traders loved him. One, a woman with a white streak who sold strike-goat off a wagon, taught him Gale insults when he was eight and paid him in thunder-milk to shout them at her husband. He thought for years that was what Stormreach marriage was. He wasn't far wrong.","schooling":"The Gleamford ford-school under Master Ivor Kesbrand, who set sums in coin-weights and once made the class assay his own lunch. Lamp Test at eight: dark across the ring. Spark-poor. At twelve Master Kesbrand put his name forward as a Hearth Scholar, one of the spark-poor children the Flameborne King pays to send to Korrhaven. He was given a loaf on the day he arrived and another the day he left four years later, and he has never eaten the second.","history":"At the Conclave he sat in on Radiance law and Tempest drill, learned Bridge properly, and roomed with a Stormreach boy and a Flameborne girl who argued so beautifully he stopped sleeping to listen. He came home at sixteen to a Lightforge that thought a Hearth Scholar from Gleamford had been given ideas, and he had. He clerked for a changer for nine years, then opened his own table at the ford, on the Stormreach side of the street, because that was where the noise was. He married a Gleamford dyer, Rosalind Pake, at twenty-eight, divorced amicably at thirty-five, and the two of them still have supper on Short-Coin Day. He's known up and down the eastern road as the Lightforge man who'll take your crossings and tell you you're shouting. He is also, he admits, lie-blind: raised where nobody lies, he cannot tell when a Stormreach trader is doing it, and he relies on the second count to catch what his face doesn't. This season the eastern road is fuller than he's ever seen it, and the traders are shouting about the Edict more than about prices.","family":"Ex-wife Rosalind, a friend. No children. A younger sister, Petra, assays at the bench and thinks he is a bad influence on the whole street.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend","why":"Speaker of the Free Assembly, opposing the Edict at the top of her lungs. He has heard her shouted at the ford by people who've never met her and thinks that's what a Warden's for."},"band":{"act":"Wahai!","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Party reggaeton with pipes; the traders play it on their wagons coming down the eastern road and he can tell they're near before he sees dust.","rv_vote":"Wahai!, and he'll shout it at the ford the night the lamps are counted."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and expects to be hated by both halves of the Assize. Against Arath, with reasons he'll give you in three languages.","edict":"Against. He spent four years at Korrhaven with spark children from every Realm and can't see a list of them as anything but a list of targets."},"wants":"To ride the eastern road all the way to Skyrend once, and hear the Assembly shout.","site":"gleamford","town":"Gleamford","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:gleamford":{"name":"Ermilde Punchwright","age":63,"role":"punch-smith of the Piercing Hall; cuts every assayer's mark-punch and forges the chains that hold them to the bench","look":"Square and short, steel-grey braids pinned in a crown, a jeweller's loupe on a chain, fingertips flattened from fifty years of fine filing.","voice":"Dry, exact, never wastes a word, and calls everyone by their mark rather than their name.","greet":"Seen. Show me your coin. No, the other side. Whose mark? Ah. Fourteenth chair. Good hand.","lines":["Every punch on that bench is mine. Every mark on every witness in Lightforge started on my file. I keep a book of all of them. All of them.","Short-Coin Day is next month. They nail up the false coins and dance under them. I don't dance. I check the marks.","My brother signed a short coin once. Once. He hasn't signed his own Day-Book since. That's the law, and the law is right, and I make him tea."],"about":"I cut the punches the assayers sign with. Forty chairs, forty marks; when an assayer dies, I break the punch and cut a new one for the next. I've cut a hundred and nine in my life. I remember all of them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, with the assayers' clipped endings"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, for the eastern-road silver buyers"}],"childhood":"Ermilde was a punch-smith's daughter in a family of punch-smiths going back nine generations, and from four years old she sat on a stool beside her father at the Piercing Hall's end bench, filing practice marks into lead. She was a fierce, silent child. Her brother Aster, two years older, was the charming one, the one who'd talk to assayers while Ermilde cut. At nine she cut her first real mark, a small three-pointed star, for an apprentice who'd lost hers, and the old head assayer held it up to the glass roof and said, 'Who did this?' Ermilde said, 'I did,' and was given the job of cutting every apprentice mark in the Hall from then on. She has never once been late with one.","schooling":"The Gleamford ford-school, briefly, under Mistress Dorrit Ash, who despaired of her handwriting and admired her drawings of coin-faces. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Her real schooling was her father, who taught her that a punch is a promise, because every coin it signs is a person saying 'this is true'.","history":"She took the punch-bench at twenty-five when her father's hands began to shake. Aster took a chair at the assay bench the same year and was good, quick and popular for twenty years. Then, at forty-seven, he signed a short coin. He has always said a clerk switched it; he has never been able to prove it; the law makes no allowance either way. He lost the right to sign anything, coin or ledger or his own Day-Book, and Ermilde took him in. For sixteen years he has dictated his Day-Book to her every evening and she has written it in her hand with his name crossed through at the top, as the law requires. She has cut a hundred and nine punches and kept a book with an impression of every mark the Hall has used for three centuries, including those of assayers struck off. The oldest pages of that book are her great-grandmother's, and one page has been glued shut since before Ermilde was born.","family":"Unmarried. Lives with her brother Aster, who makes her laugh every evening and has not signed his name in sixteen years. A niece, Seline Marrbrand's mother, is a cousin by marriage.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"'One shift, one vote. Even you.' She cuts the same size punch for the head of the Hall as for a first-year apprentice, and thinks that's the only fair thing in Gleamford."},"band":{"act":"Courtbell","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Amapiano built on court bells, and she can tell every bell they use by its maker; she likes music you can check.","rv_vote":"Courtbell, and she'll argue at the bench that it's better than the Noon Chorale."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and would be dreadful company. For Arath, because he's thorough.","edict":"For, with exceptions she writes in the margin. She believes in marks. She doesn't believe a person's spark should be stamped like a coin."},"wants":"To open the glued page of her great-grandmother's book before she dies, and to be sure she wants to.","site":"gleamford","town":"Gleamford","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:gleamford":{"name":"Seline Marrbrand","age":27,"role":"assayer at the Piercing Hall bench, ninth chair; currently serving her Assize year by lot","look":"Neat and pale, dark hair cut level with her jaw, ink on the second finger of her right hand, a pierced witness worn as an earring; looks tired in a way she would never say.","voice":"Earnest and precise; uses every evidential ending properly and apologises when she forgets one.","greet":"Aure. Sorry, I'm only home for the tenth-day. I've been in Meridian. On the Assize. The lot came up. It does, you know.","lines":["My lot came up this year. I sit on the Assize until next spring. I voted for Arath at the crisis sitting. I believed it then. I believe it — mostly — now.","Memory audits need an Assize signature. Three of us sign. I've signed four. I read every page first. I read them twice.","The bench is very quiet without me, they say. I doubt it. Nobody at the bench talks."],"about":"I'm an assayer, ninth chair; I sign about two hundred witnesses a day. This year I'm also one of the Assize of Witnesses, chosen by lot, which means I help decide how the Edict is done. I'm twenty-seven. Nobody asked if I was ready. That's the point of the lot.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, exact"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good; she's studying Concord law in it at night"}],"childhood":"Seline was a cousin of a minor house and grew up in the assayers' row, where every back window looks into the Piercing Hall's glass roof and you can watch the bench from your bed. She was a dutiful, worried, clever child who did her chores before she was asked and then fretted that she'd done them wrong. At her First Telling, standing on the ford steps at twelve, she told the town that she was afraid of making a mistake that couldn't be undone, and the town, which lives by the law of the short coin, nodded very seriously, and she was given the told name Seline Who Checked. She has checked everything since, twice. She was happy in a quiet way, and still is, mostly.","schooling":"The ford-school under Mistress Dorrit Ash, top of every class, then three years as an apprentice on the bench under the old ninth chair, Hendrick Vale, who taught her to feel a short coin by its ring on the stone. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She was relieved, then guilty for being relieved.","history":"She took the ninth chair at twenty-one when old Vale died, and Ermilde Punchwright cut her mark, a small closed eye, which Ermilde said suited her. She signed coins. She was very good. At twenty-six her lot came up for the Assize of Witnesses, the body of ordinary Lightforge people chosen by lot to serve a year, and she went to Meridian. It was meant to be a year of land disputes. Instead it was the year of the Edict. She sat in the crisis sitting that chose High Councillor Arath to enforce it, and voted for him because he said nobody wanted it and that it had to be done properly, and she believed that and still mostly does. Since then she has been one of the three Assize signers who must authorise each memory audit, in which a Radiance reader examines a person's recollection for concealed spark use. She has signed four. She reads every page of every request twice. The fourth was for a Gleamford child of eleven, the son of a woman she went to school with.","family":"Parents in the assayers' row; an older brother who left for Meridian and writes rarely. She is engaged to a Twos paper-maker, Brann Mill, whom she hasn't seen in three months.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand","why":"The Lawbringer wrote the Edict. She has read it eleven times and thinks it is the most carefully made law she's ever seen, and she can't stop reading it."},"band":{"act":"Noonday","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A perfect pop star who's never been seen in shadow. She used to sing Noonday at the bench under her breath; lately she's noticed the songs are all about being watched.","rv_vote":"Noonday, out of habit; she's thought about changing it and hasn't."},"vote":{"local":"Her lot is up now; she is serving. She voted for Arath and would, she thinks, again.","edict":"For, sincerely, and more troubled every week; she believes in the law and has started to dream about the boy's audit."},"wants":"To finish her Assize year without signing a fifth audit.","site":"gleamford","town":"Gleamford","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:gleamford:b":{"name":"Barret Fold","age":45,"role":"fiddler and dance-caller for Short-Coin Day; mends harness the rest of the year","look":"Wiry and loose-jointed, curly hair going white in patches, a battered fiddle slung across his back on a harness strap, one tooth missing at the front.","voice":"Fast, rhythmic, tells everything as if it's a story with a tune under it; laughs at his own jokes first.","greet":"Aure, Aure! You're here for the dance? You're early. Eleven months early. Stay anyway.","lines":["Short-Coin Day, they nail the false coins up and read the makers' names between tunes. I play the tunes. I play them fast after the names. You'd be surprised what a fast tune does to a name.","My father's name got read out when I was eight. He danced anyway. Front row. I play for him every year.","The false coins this year are good. Too good. Somebody's very proud of their work."],"about":"I mend harness for the carters, and once a year I play the dance under the short coins. My father was a coiner, and caught, and then a harness-man. I've got his tools and his fiddle and his name, and I don't mind any of them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, fast"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"songs and swearing"}],"childhood":"Barret's earliest memory is his father standing on the Piercing Hall steps on Short-Coin Day while his name was read out beside a clipped witness nailed to the door. Barret was eight. Everyone in the crowd turned to look at the boy. His father, Brannan Fold, walked down the steps, took Barret's mother by the hand and danced, front row, through the whole tune, and Barret understood that something terrible had happened and that his father had decided it wasn't going to be the last thing about him. They lived poor after that. His father mended harness, played the fiddle in the evenings, and wrote every single day's work in his Day-Book with a care that bordered on rudeness, so anyone who wanted could check it. Barret learned the fiddle on the back step.","schooling":"The ford-school under Mistress Dorrit Ash, who once caught him playing the fiddle under his desk with the bow held in his teeth and, instead of taking it, asked him to play it properly for the class. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp sparked once and went out. Half-Spark, registered and forgotten. He left at thirteen to work harness with his father.","history":"At sixteen he played his first Short-Coin Day dance, standing in for a fiddler with a broken arm, and the town noticed that after each maker's name he struck up so fast and so loud that nobody could stand there gawping at whoever had been named; they had to dance or get out of the way. He has played every one since, twenty-nine years. He calls it 'giving them a tune to hide in', which in Gleamford is very nearly a dangerous sentence. He married Wenna Shale, a crayfish-wife, at twenty-five; they have three loud children and a house with the shutters always open. His father died six years ago and was named in the Grief Room with his coin-maker's past spoken as plainly as his fiddling. This season the short coins being found round Gleamford are unusually fine: clipped so evenly, re-milled so well, that even the bench nearly misses them. Barret has handled one. He knows the hand that did that milling. He learned harness-tooling beside it.","family":"Wife Wenna, children Nessa, Rowan and Tallis. Mother alive, deaf, dances anyway.","hero":{"who":"Vey Who Said It","why":"Founder of the Open-Mouthed in Shadowdeep, who said her full name aloud when everyone hides it. His father said his own name on those steps by dancing under it, and that's close enough for Barret."},"band":{"act":"Corran Vale","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Storm-shouted apology rap, the loudest sorry in the Nine. He learned the rhythm off traders and plays it on the fiddle at the dance to make Stormreach folk cry.","rv_vote":"Corran Vale, and he'll shout the apology bit at the lamp-count himself."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, and would play at the Assize if they let him. Against Arath.","edict":"Against. 'Short coins get nailed up. People shouldn't.'"},"wants":"To play Short-Coin Day this year without having to read a name he knows.","site":"gleamford","town":"Gleamford","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:sunmere":{"name":"Dace Rackham","age":39,"role":"village trader at Rack Seventeen; nets, hooks, bread, bows and salvage","look":"Brown, bandy-legged from wading, sleeves always rolled to the shoulder, salt dried white in his eyebrows; keeps a ledger in an oilskin.","voice":"Steady, wry, a fisher's patience; he answers in numbers whenever he can.","greet":"Aure. Net-twine's three coppers the length. Hooks are five. The salt fish is free if you can tell me what the Courts pay for it.","lines":["The Courts' fish contract hasn't changed its price in ninety years. Their clerks quote it at us word for word. I quote it back. We're very well-read in Sunmere.","Prismport smashes its bad glass on the mole and sweeps it in the lake. Our nets come up glittering. Pretty, if you don't have to mend them.","Mol says I count everything. I say someone in this village should. We're poorer than Prismport by about four witnesses a family a season. I counted."],"about":"I run the shop at Rack Seventeen with my partner Mol. We sell what fishers need and buy what the lake gives up. I write the prices on a board at the door so nobody has to ask.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, shore accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, for the carters"}],"childhood":"Dace was a racks child, raised among the white-painted drying racks on their stilts where every post carries a family name, and his family's post, Seventeen, was the one that leaned. His father fished, his mother salted, and Dace counted: fish on the hooks, hooks on the rack, racks to the shore. At six he worked out that Sunmere was poorer than Prismport by reading both villages' prices on carters' boards, and was so upset he cried, and his mother wrote in her Day-Book, 'Dace has discovered money.' He fished well enough and hated being wet. He spent the Seen Catch every season wading by himself, slowly, taking only what he could see, and loved it more than any day of the year.","schooling":"The Sunmere rack-school, held on the shore under the racks themselves, taught by Master Hob Linnet, who wrote lessons on the white rack-posts in charcoal and made the children wade out to read them. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at twelve to salt and keep accounts for three families.","history":"At twenty-four he and Mol Tench, a salter's daughter with a better head for buying than his, went in together on the shop at Rack Seventeen after old Hessel Rack retired. They have run it for fifteen years. They have never married. Sunmere has asked, by every means short of the Assize, and they have both said 'we work together' in the tone people use when they mean the conversation is over. His lot came up for the Assize at thirty-one; he served, was assigned to a trade panel, and spent the whole year trying to get the Witness Courts' fish contract onto the list for review, and failed, and came home with a copy of the contract he can now recite. He sells nets, hooks, bows for the shore birds, Wheatley bread brought down on carts, salvage and glass-cut repairs. He keeps the Cracking Day tally: how many nets cut, how many hours mending, at what price, every year for twelve years.","family":"Parents dead, named in the Prismport Grief Room. A younger brother fishes Rack Twenty-Two. Mol has a son, Tobin, nine, whom Dace is teaching to count.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired and Hollis Brask","why":"They built Hearthward together and never married. Sunmere thinks this is the strangest thing in history. Dace doesn't."},"band":{"act":"The Tether Sisters","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Chain-yard harmony folk; the chain-wrights are the only Stormreach folk who stay in one place, and he likes them for it.","rv_vote":"The Tether Sisters; their 'Cut the Chain' sounds like mending nets."},"vote":{"local":"Served his lot, would serve again, would bring the fish contract both times. Doubtful of Arath, who has never mentioned fish.","edict":"Doubtful. 'It's a law about sparks. We haven't got any. We've got a contract from ninety years ago nobody'll look at.'"},"wants":"To get the fish contract reviewed before he's fifty.","site":"sunmere","town":"Sunmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:sunmere":{"name":"Una Holloway","age":52,"role":"village smith; fish-hooks, rack-irons, net-weights and the iron rings on every rack post","look":"Long-faced and weathered, iron-grey hair under a fisher's knitted cap, a pair of Frostgrave word-cords at her belt she's never explained.","voice":"Low and slow, pauses before she promises anything, like someone from further north than Sunmere.","greet":"Seen. A hook? I'll have it by the sixth bell. Probably.","lines":["One bell, the shallows froze. The only frost ever written on the Noon Plains. My great-grandmother wrote it. I've read it a hundred times. She saw someone on the shore.","They say Frostgrave had a hand in it. I say a Frostgrave woman had a hand in my family. Draw your own sums.","People laugh at 'probably'. I've never broken a promise in my life. Probably's why."],"about":"I make the iron the racks hang on. My family's smithed in Sunmere for five generations, and one of those generations came from somewhere colder. I say 'probably' too much. I keep what I say.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few words and a nursery song, from her grandmother"}],"childhood":"Una grew up in the forge at the shore end of the racks, where the hammering carried over the flat water to Prismport and came back off the glass. Her grandmother, Ysel, was the daughter of a Frostgrave woman who had come south and married a Sunmere smith, and Ysel sang Una to sleep in a language nobody else in the village knew, soft and long with a pause before certain words. Una grew up saying 'probably' about everything, which other children mocked, and keeping every promise she made, which other children came to rely on. At ten she promised to forge her own fish-hook and spent a whole season failing until she did. At her First Telling at twelve she told the village her great-great-grandmother was from Frostgrave, which everyone knew, and that she was proud of it, which nobody had expected.","schooling":"Master Hob Linnet's rack-school, where she was quiet and good at fractions. Lamp Test at eight: the Chrona lamp, not the Radiance lamp, gave a faint cold blink and went out. Half-Spark in Chrona, the only one anyone in Sunmere can remember; the tester wrote it twice to be sure. She never went further with it. Her father taught her the forge.","history":"She took the forge at twenty-three when her father's back failed and has kept it for twenty-nine years: hooks, net-weights, the iron rings on the rack-posts, and mending everything the Cracking Day glass cuts. She married Fenn Hollo — the village joke is that she married him for the name — a quiet net-mender who died of a bad cut gone septic eight years ago; she named him in the Prismport Grief Room and wears his word-cord, which she made for him, with her own. Her great-grandmother's Day-Book is kept in the forge in a tin box. The entry for the one frost reads: 'Shallows froze one bell. Fish stood still in the ice. A woman on the shore in a grey coat, very still too. She bowed to me. I bowed back.' Una has read it many times. This season, with Lyriel Frostwraith struck from the Frostgrave record for her warning and Lightforge the Realm that overruled it, Una has started tying a knot on her own cord each day she says nothing about it.","family":"Widow; a son, Halvor, fishes Rack Six; a daughter apprenticed to Ysa Pontilwright in Prismport, which Una regrets for both of them.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"The Seer who knotted a vision so it would be kept. Una's family kept a frost-bell in a Day-Book for a hundred years and thinks that's the same work."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Throat-singing with a famous forty-second silence. Her grandmother hummed something like it at the forge, and Una holds her breath through the silence every time.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime, because nobody in Lightforge will, and somebody should."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up, and say probably when they asked. Doubtful of Arath.","edict":"Doubtful. She can't forget that the Realm that's registering every spark is the same one that overruled Lyriel's warning."},"wants":"To walk to Frostgrave and see the Hall of Kept Words once.","site":"sunmere","town":"Sunmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:sunmere":{"name":"Cadoc Clearwater","age":15,"role":"fisher's apprentice at Rack Nine; Ilse Clearwater's grandson and reader","look":"Gangly, sunburnt, hair bleached white at the tips, a slingshot in his back pocket and new cuts across both palms from net-glass.","voice":"Loud, cocky, and not half as sure as he sounds; swears in Kindle he learnt off a cart-driver.","greet":"Aure. Grain? Gran'll want to know who from. She writes it down. She writes everything down.","lines":["Gran's been writing one line about Prismport glass in her nets every day for eleven years. She's going to read the whole lot out at the Unshadowing. All of it. We'll be there till next noon.","Water's so clear here you can watch a man drop his coin and then decide whether to tell you. Most do. True as light, most do. Gran says that. I say most.","Kaelis Emberwarden swore at the Council and they wrote it down word for word. Imagine having your swears in the record."],"about":"I'm Cadoc. I fish Rack Nine with my uncle and I read Gran her lines because her eyes are going. I'm fifteen. I'm getting out of Sunmere. Maybe. Probably not.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, shore slang"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"exactly eleven swear-words"}],"childhood":"Cadoc's mother left Sunmere for a Meridian kitchen when he was three and his father went out on the lake one morning in a squall and didn't come back, and he was raised by his grandmother Ilse at the end of Rack Nine among nets. It sounds sad and mostly wasn't. Ilse was funny and sharp and let him run wild on the shore, and the racks were a perfect climbing-frame, and he had his father's rack-name on the post to point at. He was the kind of boy who got stuck up a rack every summer and had to be fetched down, and who brought home every odd thing the lake gave up. At nine he found a whole Prismport tumbler, unbroken, in the shallows, and Ilse made him take it back to the glassworks, and the glassworks smashed it on the mole in front of him because it had a bubble.","schooling":"Master Hob Linnet's rack-school, which he skipped for the lake as often as he could; Master Linnet once came and taught the day's lesson to him standing in the shallows, waist-deep, to make a point. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. He left at thirteen and can read well enough to read his gran her lines, which is the reading he cares about.","history":"At thirteen he went out on Rack Nine's boat with his uncle Bram. He's a fair fisher with a good eye and no patience. Since his gran's eyes started going two years ago, he's been the one who reads her Day-Book back to her each evening, including the line she writes every day about Prismport glass in her nets: how many cuts, how many hours, what it cost. Eleven years of them. At the next Unshadowing, when everyone in Lightforge says one hidden thing aloud, she means to stand up in Sunmere and read the whole lot. Cadoc has been helping her rehearse. He's also found his own way to get even: for a year he's been cutting the big clear glass shards out of her nets before he mends them, taking them up to the Prismport mole, and selling them to the trader Oswin Tumbler as beach salvage. He has written this in his own Day-Book. He has also, three times, quietly added a line of his own to his gran's.","family":"Grandmother Ilse; uncle Bram; a mother in Meridian who sends money and a letter on his birthday.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"Loud, funny and profane at the Concord Council table, and they had to write down every word. Tam wants to be that loud somewhere that matters."},"band":{"act":"No Knee","realm":"Flameborne","why":"A bakers'-street rap crew at war with Lightforge. He's learned all the words from a cart-driver and does them on the racks to annoy his uncle.","rv_vote":"No Knee, and he'll tell anyone, which in Sunmere counts as rebellion."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for the lot. Says he'd stand and 'put the fish contract on fire'.","edict":"Against, loudly, at fifteen, for reasons he's still collecting."},"wants":"To see Hearthmount, the Cinderway and a real riot, in that order.","site":"sunmere","town":"Sunmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:sunmere:b":{"name":"Marro Finch","age":81,"role":"retired fisher; the oldest living rack-name in Sunmere","look":"Small and leathery, toothless, brown spotted hands that still knot net without looking; sits on a rack-step every day in a white coat that used to be his father's.","voice":"Thin and courteous, very old-fashioned Clear, calls the Witness Courts 'the honourable Courts' without irony.","greet":"Aure, Aure. Sit. No — stand, if you're from the Courts. Sit if you're not.","lines":["The honourable Courts have bought our fish for ninety years at the same price. It is a very good contract. My great-grandfather signed it. He was a very good man.","When I was small, my father showed me where the shallows froze. Just there. He wasn't born. His father told him. We keep it like that.","I've had a long life. Fish, mostly. I'm not complaining. I am very slightly complaining."],"about":"I am Marro Finch of Rack One. I fished seventy years. My family's name is on the first post, and the first post is on the contract. I believe in the Courts. I believe in salt.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, old formal"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words he says very politely"}],"childhood":"Marro was a Finch of Rack One, the oldest post in Sunmere, the one the village says the Courts' first clerk tied his horse to, and he was raised to believe that Rack One had obligations. He got up before the first bell to help his father rig, he soaked the salt perch in sunwine twelve bells by the sand-glass, he sat with his grandmother at the Seen Catch giving away the hand-caught fish to whatever house had buried someone that year. He was a careful, gentle boy who cried when other boys threw stones at gulls. His great-grandfather's signature on the Courts' contract hung framed in the house, and at seven he could recite the whole contract by heart. He was very proud of that. He still is.","schooling":"The rack-school under Master Linnet's grandfather, Master Corbet Linnet, who taught the children to read using the Courts' fish contract, since it was the only document every family in Sunmere owned. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at eleven for the boat. He learned to write beautifully, in the old looped hand, and still writes his Day-Book in it, so slowly that each entry takes him most of a bell.","history":"He fished Rack One for seventy years, married Ellin Shoal at nineteen, had six children, buried two. His lot for the Assize came up at forty-two; he served on a panel hearing Witness Court appeals and thought the honourable Courts were fair in every case, and said so. He has a deep, unshowy loyalty to Lightforge's law, and is the only person in Sunmere who has never complained about the contract price. When Dace Rackham at Seventeen got the contract onto a review list and failed, Marro wrote him a kind letter saying the Courts must have had their reasons. He gave up the boat at seventy-eight. He sits on Rack One's step now and watches the young ones wade the Seen Catch. Ellin died last spring. That year the Seen Catch went to the Finches, and he couldn't eat it, and gave it on to Ilse Clearwater, who'd lost a cousin, and nobody said anything about the rule.","family":"Four living children, fifteen grandchildren, two great-grandchildren who climb him. Wife Ellin, named in the Prismport Grief Room last spring.","hero":{"who":"Saelen of the Drift","why":"The Lantern of the Dead. Marro has given the Seen Catch to the bereaved every year of his life and thinks Saelen's work was the same, only further."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Forty-year love songs. Ellin liked them; he thought they were too long. He listens to them now every evening.","rv_vote":"Decades, for Ellin, who would have said they were exactly the right length."},"vote":{"local":"Served his lot at forty-two. For Arath; the Courts are honourable and so must the Assize be.","edict":"Loyal. He has nothing to register and trusts the law. He is a little hurt that anyone thinks otherwise."},"wants":"To give the Seen Catch to someone himself, one more time, wading.","site":"sunmere","town":"Sunmere","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:goldfield":{"name":"Imma Hoodwright","age":33,"role":"Goldfield's trader; hawk-hoods, jesses and gauntlets sewn from Rushbrook leather, plus packs and bows","look":"Compact and quick-handed, black hair in a net, a hawk's leather hood worn as a pendant, needle-callouses on every finger.","voice":"Sharp and funny, a Rim twang she hasn't lost; talks while she sews and never misses a stitch.","greet":"Aure. Hood, jess, glove? Packs and arrows if you're the walking sort. Everything stitched by me, and I'll show you the stitch.","lines":["Hawks won't fly into Stormreach weather. You see one turn back over the Hold, get your washing in. And your children.","The Assize has asked for twenty more courier hawks for the register work. Twenty. They took every hawk Goldfield had once. It took us twenty years to buy them back.","My leather comes from Odra's pits at Rushbrook. Best on the Rim. Smells like a funeral. Hawks love it."],"about":"I'm Rim-born, Rushbrook stock, married into Goldfield. I sew the gear the hawks wear, and I sell the kit walkers need, and I listen to the handlers argue. I'm very good at the first two.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, Rim accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"twenty signs, taught her by a Shadowdeep leather-buyer"}],"childhood":"Imma was a Rushbrook currier's daughter, raised on the brown stream with the smell of bark in everything she owned, and she was the child who sat at the Dusk Supper and, when nobody was allowed to speak of the day, told enormous made-up stories about what she would do tomorrow instead. She was clever with her hands: by seven she was stitching the edges of hides for her mother, by ten she was making doll-sized saddles and selling them to Goldfield handlers' children. A Shadowdeep leather-buyer who came every season taught her hand-cant at nine, a sign at a time, because she was the only child who never stared at him. She still uses it to talk to him across a crowded yard.","schooling":"Mistress Faye Orrick's room above the currier's in Rushbrook, where the children read Day-Books aloud with their eyes shut; Imma was the best at it and the worst at sitting still. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She was apprenticed to a Rushbrook saddler at twelve and learned the stitch she now calls 'the Rim lock'.","history":"At nineteen she came to Goldfield to deliver forty hawk-hoods and met Corwen Fairweather, a handler's son with a bad laugh, who asked her to stay for the Feather-Letting that evening. She stayed eleven years and counting. They married at twenty-one in the Long Mews. She opened the trading post at twenty-five in a lean-to at the end of the mews, and now sells every hood, jess and gauntlet Goldfield needs, plus bows and packs for walkers and travellers, and buys hawk-feathers and old Assize hood-seals as salvage. She still buys all her leather from Odra Brookwater's pits, and still stitches every piece herself. This season the Assize has asked Goldfield for twenty more courier hawks for the Edict's register work, couriering spark lists between towns, and the village is in a quiet uproar. They remember the last time.","family":"Husband Corwen; a daughter, Lark, six, who wants a hawk. Her mother and three brothers still at Rushbrook.","hero":{"who":"Amon Greycloak","why":"The first Shardwatch, who stood at an edge before anyone knew why it needed standing at. Imma grew up at the Rim's edge and likes anyone who went first."},"band":{"act":"Five Signs","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Idols who perform in hand-cant. She's the only one in Goldfield who can follow the lyrics, and she does it at the fair just to watch people's faces.","rv_vote":"Five Signs, and she'll sign it to her leather-buyer across the yard."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and would sew through the whole year. Torn on Arath.","edict":"Torn. She has no spark to hide, but she remembers Goldfield's stories, and twenty hawks is how it started last time."},"wants":"For Goldfield to keep its hawks this time without having to buy them back.","site":"goldfield","town":"Goldfield","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:goldfield":{"name":"Lothar Bellbrand","age":70,"role":"bell-founder and smith; hawk-bells, jess-rings, swivels and the courier hawks' message-tubes","look":"Huge, slow and bald, with white whiskers to his collarbone; wears a ring of tiny brass hawk-bells on his wrist that chime when he moves.","voice":"Rumbling and kindly, a big laugh, always tells you what he's making before you ask.","greet":"Aure! You hear that? Bells. That's me coming. Hawks hear me three fields off and get ready to complain.","lines":["Every courier hawk in Lightforge wears a bell I cast. Every one. The Assize thinks it owns the hawks. I own the bells.","Metalcrown's never got a single lamp from Lightforge at Realm Vision. Not one. In all the years. I mean to be the first. I've told everyone. They're appalled.","Gleamford takes a cut on every hawk-letter that carries coin. I put a little extra brass on those message-tubes. Makes them heavier. Makes them a little slower to Gleamford."],"about":"I cast the bells. Hawk-bells, mews-bells, the handbell for the Feather-Letting. My mother taught me. I've been at it sixty years and I still get excited when one rings true first time.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"his mother's words, for metal and for cursing"}],"childhood":"Lothar's mother, Hanne, came to Goldfield from Metalcrown as a young bell-caster with a tally plate at her wrist and nothing else, and married the village smith, and nobody minded much, because she cast the best hawk-bells anyone had heard. Lothar grew up in a forge where his father hammered and his mother poured, where he heard Shiftcant words for heat and failure and luck before he heard their Clear ones. She taught him to stamp a tiny 'I made it' inside every bell, as the Metalcrown smiths do, where only the clapper would touch it. He loved the mews, the hawks, the light along the perches. At nine he was sent to cast a hawk-bell alone and it came out cracked, and his mother laid it in his hand and said, in Shiftcant, 'Now you know.'","schooling":"The Goldfield school in the end stall of the Long Mews, taught by Mistress Corra Hay, who made children write letters to carry by hawk and wouldn't send any with a mistake. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor; his mother said she was glad. He left at twelve for the forge.","history":"He took the forge at thirty when his father died and his mother, already old, stepped back to pour only the bells she liked. For forty years he has cast every hawk-bell, swivel, jess-ring and message-tube Goldfield uses, and every bell on every Assize courier hawk in Lightforge. He married twice: first Elspeth, who left for Meridian and is still a friend; then Rhian Mown, who died eleven years ago and whose handbell he still rings himself at every Feather-Letting. He became, without meaning to, Goldfield's link to Metalcrown: buyers from the Iron Throne City come to him for brass, and he gives them supper and lets them talk. Every year at Realm Vision Lightforge gives no lamp to Metalcrown, and Lothar has decided that this year he will be the first. He has announced it at every supper since spring.","family":"Widower; son Arno, a hawk-handler; granddaughter Hala, twelve, who rings the Feather-Letting handbell. His mother's tally plate hangs over the forge.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"She led the Unmarked to the slag-hills and made an ugly first hammer that worked. His mother told him the story every night, and he still thinks an ugly tool that works is the finest thing in the Nine."},"band":{"act":"The Bench","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"A family trio on homemade instruments; his mother would have loved them, and they sound like his forge on a good day.","rv_vote":"The Bench, so that Metalcrown finally gets one lamp from Lightforge, and he'll write it in his Day-Book in red."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and would bring brass bells for the whole Assize. Against Arath; the Council laughed at Metalcrown's ninth petition, and he took it personally.","edict":"Against. 'Register sparks today, bells tomorrow, and they'll want my mother's plate off the wall.'"},"wants":"To hear one of his bells rung at Korrhaven at Realm Vision before he dies.","site":"goldfield","town":"Goldfield","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:goldfield":{"name":"Averil Whitlow","age":24,"role":"hawk-handler at the Long Mews; hawk-master Oren Whitlow's daughter","look":"Rangy and windburnt, a gauntlet always on her left hand, dark hair cropped short after a hawk got tangled in it, a skyray-feather pin she doesn't explain.","voice":"Clipped, hawk-like: short phrases, then a sudden flood when something matters.","greet":"Aure. Mind the near perch. That one bites. Not the hawk. The handler on it. Me.","lines":["The Assize wants twenty more hawks for the register couriers. My father hasn't said a word. He wrote a long bit in his Day-Book about rabbits. That's how I know he's angry.","Stormreach skyrays spook our hawks off their lines. We've written to the Free Assembly eleven times. Eleven loud replies. I read them all. Some were funny.","My father writes the hawks' side of every conversation in his Day-Book. Mine he leaves out. I've checked."],"about":"I handle hawks at the Long Mews. My father's Oren Whitlow, the hawk-master, and he talks to the birds more than to us. I talk to the birds too. And to someone in Tempest Hold, by bird.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"fair, improving fast and suspiciously"}],"childhood":"Averil grew up under the Long Mews with a hawk on her shoulder before she could walk, and a father who called her by the name of his best falcon for the first four years of her life by accident. Her mother, Tessaly, ran the mews accounts and made sure Averil was fed, schooled and occasionally spoken to. She was a quiet, watchful child who learned the hawks' moods before she learned her letters, and who sat for hours on the mews roof watching the yellow grass for movement. At nine she trained her first kestrel without telling anyone and sent it to Gleamford with a note that said, 'This is my bird, Averil Whitlow.' Gleamford sent it back with a reply. Her father wrote in his Day-Book that night, in the bird's voice, 'She did well.'","schooling":"Mistress Corra Hay's school in the end stall, where she wrote the clearest hawk-letters in the village and refused to write anything else. Lamp Test at eight: the Tempest lamp, not the Radiance one, gave a tiny spark. Half-Spark in Tempest, registered and ignored. She has never once used it, and has two grey hairs anyway, which she says are from her father.","history":"She took a handler's perch at fifteen and her own string of eight hawks at nineteen. She is very good: her birds carry sealed letters from Goldfield to Meridian and Gleamford without loss, and once flew a Gleamford exchange note through a Stormreach squall that had grounded everything else. Three years ago she was out on the Tempest Hold edge chasing a hawk spooked by a skyray, and the skyray's flyer, a Stormreach woman named Briet, landed the great glider in the yellow grass and shouted an apology at her for a full minute. Averil shouted back. They have been writing ever since, by hawk. Briet's letters come in Gale, shouted on paper, and Averil's Gale is now better than anyone else's in Goldfield. This season the Assize wants twenty of Goldfield's hawks for courier work on the Edict's spark registers, and five of them are Averil's.","family":"Father Oren, mother Tessaly, a brother, Kip, who wants nothing to do with hawks and keeps bees instead.","hero":{"who":"Annick, the herd-girl who founded Goldfield","why":"She tamed a Radiant Hawk that kept stealing her lunch and taught it to carry notes. Averil thinks every good thing about Goldfield started with a girl being annoyed."},"band":{"act":"Updraft","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Trance named for the rising air the skyrays ride. Briet sent her the words of 'Freefall' by hawk, one verse a letter.","rv_vote":"Updraft, in the Sparkbeat Cup and the lamps, and Goldfield can think what it likes."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up, and bring a hawk to the Assize. Against Arath.","edict":"Against: it's taking her birds to carry lists of people's sparks between towns, and she'll have to fly them."},"wants":"For one of her hawks to carry her all the way to Tempest Hold, which is impossible, so the next best thing.","site":"goldfield","town":"Goldfield","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:goldfield:b":{"name":"Agathe Mown","age":88,"role":"keeper of the unmown patches; Goldfield's oldest resident and its unofficial mower","look":"Tiny and bent, white hair in a long plait, a scythe she uses as a walking stick, and a dozen hawk-feathers stuck through the brim of her hat.","voice":"Cheerful and vague, wanders off mid-sentence and comes back to it a minute later exactly where she left off.","greet":"Aure, dear! Don't step there. That's Fennel's patch. He died in the spring. The hawk, not my husband. My husband's over there.","lines":["When a hawk dies, its handler lets one feather go from the mews roof. Wherever it lands, no mowing for a year. I keep the list. I've kept it sixty years.","They want twenty hawks for the Assize again. Last time they took six, for a border muster. I was seven. Four came back. My mother cried in the mews. She never cried.","I asked to be struck off the lot at eighty. They said I could. I was a bit offended they said yes so fast."],"about":"I mow. And I don't mow, which is the important part. I keep the list of every unmown patch, every feather, every hawk. Sixty years of it. I'm eighty-eight. I'm not dead yet, whatever my knees say.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, very old Goldfield dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"she says she knows it; she does not"}],"childhood":"Agathe was born in the yellow grass during haymaking, and her mother finished the row before she lay down, which Agathe has always considered a good start. She was a mower's child and a mower's grandchild, and her first memory is the sound of a scythe in long grass, steady as breathing. Every Goldfield child is told how, early in the Concord, the Assize took every hawk in the village and it took twenty years to buy the pairs back. Agathe thought it was a story until she was seven, when the Assize came for six hawks for a border muster on the Tempest Hold side. She remembers the handlers standing in the Long Mews in silence and her mother, who never cried, crying. Four came back. Agathe wrote the two that didn't into the Feather-Letting list herself, in letters an inch high, and that was the first page she ever kept.","schooling":"The end-stall school under Mistress Corra Hay's great-aunt, Mistress Moll Hay, who taught her to read from the Feather-Letting list, because it was the longest thing in the village. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. She left at ten to mow. Mistress Hay read the list aloud each spring and let the children pick which hawk's story to copy out. Agathe always chose the ones nobody else wanted, and still knows them by heart.","history":"She married Willem Mown, a hawk-handler, at twenty-five, and took on the job nobody else wanted: keeping the list of unmown patches from the Feather-Letting, so the mowers knew which grass to leave and when it could be cut again. She has kept the list for sixty years. She has three children, nine grandchildren, a great-great-grandson, and a clear memory of every hawk that died in Goldfield since she was twenty-eight. Her daughter Rhian married Lothar Bellbrand and died eleven years ago. Willem died thirty years ago. His hawk, a big grey called Sorrow, died the same winter, and its feather landed at the edge of the far field. Agathe has never let that patch be mown. Not for a year. For thirty. The list says it's due. She has written it down every year as 'still due', which is true. Nobody has said anything.","family":"Three children, nine grandchildren, many greats. Son-in-law Lothar Bellbrand, whom she adores and bullies.","hero":{"who":"The Lost Warden","why":"Nobody knows who they were, and she thinks that's the best thing a person could be remembered for: being remembered without anyone knowing the details."},"band":{"act":"Isveig","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"She sings the saddest song perfectly and feels nothing. Agathe thinks that's restful at her age and says so to anyone young enough to be shocked.","rv_vote":"Isveig. Or the nice boys with the bells. She'll decide on the day."},"vote":{"local":"Struck off the lot at her own request at eighty. Thinks Arath has a kind face and sad eyes and doesn't trust either.","edict":"Doesn't much care. 'I've no spark. I've a scythe. Come and register that.' But she remembers the hawks."},"wants":"To mow the far field patch herself before she dies, when she's ready.","site":"goldfield","town":"Goldfield","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:luminant_vf1":{"name":"Piers Cartwell","age":46,"role":"cart-master and Wheatley's trader; runs the Meridian bread carts and sells kit, bread and packs from the depot","look":"Stocky and red-faced, flour to the elbows, a carter's whip coiled through his belt that he has never used on a horse; carries a tally-stick and a sand-glass.","voice":"Brisk, practical, punctuates everything with the time: 'third cart's gone, fourth cart's late.'","greet":"Aure. Fourth cart's just gone, fifth's loading. Bread, packs, arrows. Make it quick or ride with the pala.","lines":["Grain's dear this season. Carts go north to Tempest Hold full and come back with nothing but shouting. Somebody's feeding an army, or getting ready to.","There's no clock in Wheatley. There's the carts. If the carts stop, time stops. I'm very important.","The Citadel kitchens want overnight baking again. Overnight. I've asked them to send the night down with the order."],"about":"I run the carts. Every few bells, a cart of pala goes to Meridian, and I make sure it does. Wheatley keeps time by my carts. I sell what travellers need from the depot while the bread's loading.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, mostly about weights and roads"}],"childhood":"Piers was a carter's son, born in the depot loft above the horses, where the bread-smell came up through the floorboards and the cart-wheels went out every few bells like a heartbeat. He learned to count by carts before he learned to count by numbers: first cart, second cart, Pale Day. He loved the horses, the road, the way the Oven Row flashed in the corner of his eye as the cart pulled out. He hated being still. At eight he hid in a bread-cart and rode all the way to Meridian, and was found by a Citadel cook under three hundred pala, and was sent home with a note pinned to his shirt in the cook's hand: 'Delivered. Slightly squashed.' His father framed it.","schooling":"The Wheatley school at the end of the Oven Row, taught by Master Elden Rye, who timed lessons by the carts and stopped mid-sentence whenever one went out. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at twelve to drive. He learned to read road-signs, weigh-tickets and the Citadel's delivery orders, which Master Rye called the only literature Wheatley needed, and he never once finished a book.","history":"He drove the Meridian carts from twelve to thirty, took over the depot from his father at thirty-one, and has run the cart schedule for fifteen years without one late cart he couldn't account for. He married Bettany Oake at twenty-four, a baker's daughter; they have four children, two of whom drive. He started selling kit from the depot six years ago because travellers kept asking, and now sells packs, bows, arrows and bread to anyone going north to the Tempest Hold border. This season he has been counting the grain carts going north, as he counts everything, and the numbers don't match the grain orders the Assize has posted on the depot door. More is going than is being bought. He has written the difference in his Day-Book every day for two months.","family":"Wife Bettany, four children. His father, retired, still counts the carts from a chair outside the depot.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"The anchor-master who held Korr's Span; Stormreach riders throw stones at his Stillman. Piers holds a schedule that the whole village hates on a slow day, and thinks someone has to."},"band":{"act":"The Trough","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Amapiano played on kneading troughs. It's the only music that sounds like the bakehouse at full shift, and his drivers sing it on the road.","rv_vote":"The Trough, because a bread band deserves a bread vote."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, if someone else ran the carts; nobody could. For Arath, on the grounds that he seems punctual.","edict":"For, mostly; he wants things counted and lists kept. He wishes someone would count the grain."},"wants":"To know where the extra grain is going before someone asks him why he didn't ask.","site":"luminant_vf1","town":"Wheatley","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:luminant_vf1":{"name":"Sabine Brassbrand","age":40,"role":"oven-smith; mends and polishes the forty brass sun-ovens of the Oven Row and half the Realm's besides","look":"Tall and broad-shouldered, red-gold hair in a long tail, brass polish ground permanently into her knuckles; wears dark smoked lenses on a cord because the Row is too bright to look at.","voice":"Low and wry, says the uncomfortable thing calmly and then waits.","greet":"Seen. Don't look at the Row without lenses, you'll see spots for a week. Here, borrow mine. I'll know if you keep them.","lines":["The forty-first oven at the end, the dull one. My great-great-great-great-grandfather's. He browned his loaves to hide bad flour. I'm the only smith in Wheatley who's never polished it. Never been asked.","Pale Day's coming. Any loaf with a brown patch goes to the hens and the baker buys the ale. Jarvis Oake buys the ale every year. Ask him why.","Meridian wants its ovens running overnight. There's no night. I've told them. I've told them in writing. They've written back."],"about":"I keep the Row. Forty ovens, polished every day, mended when they crack, adjusted to the noon. I'm a Brassbrand, which means I'm descended from the man who shamed the village. Nobody holds it against me. They just mention it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"tool-words, from a Metalcrown smithing manual she taught herself"}],"childhood":"Sabine grew up knowing she was a Brassbrand, which in Wheatley means growing up beside the forty-first oven. It stands at the end of the Row, dull and unpolished, exactly as it was the day her many-times-great-grandfather was caught browning his loaves to hide bad flour. Every child in Wheatley is told the story. Sabine was told it with a look. She was a stubborn, curious girl who decided early that if her family was going to be known for an oven, she would know more about ovens than anyone else alive. At eight she took apart her mother's kitchen oven and put it back together. At ten she was found inside the forty-first with a lamp, reading the maker's marks.","schooling":"Master Elden Rye's school at the end of the Row, where she corrected his sums about oven angles. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. At fourteen she apprenticed to Wheatley's old oven-smith, Gregor Pane, who said she'd be better than him and was right by twenty. Old Gregor taught her to read a brass seam by the colour of its heat and to listen to an oven cooling, and gave her his own smoked lenses when he retired.","history":"She took the Row at twenty-four. Since then every one of the forty ovens has been rebuilt at least once under her hand, and half the brass ovens in Lightforge's towns have been sent to her for mending. She taught herself to read Shiftcant tool-words out of a Metalcrown smithing manual a trader left, because Metalcrown understands brass under heat better than anyone. She has never polished the forty-first. Five years ago, cleaning out its firebox for the first time in a century, she found a Day-Book wrapped in oilcloth: her ancestor's, from the year he was caught. It says he browned the loaves because the flour the Citadel sent that season was bad, that he reported it, and that nobody read the report. She has not shown it to anyone. She has written in her own Day-Book that she found it, which is true and which nobody has read.","family":"Married to Hal Tanner, a miller's son from Halden Fallow's mill; a son, Edric, eleven. Her mother, alive, still won't walk past the forty-first.","hero":{"who":"Solvane","why":"The young Metalcrown engineer who read the ninth petition to the Council politely while they laughed. Sabine knows what it's like to be descended from a joke and stand there anyway."},"band":{"act":"Gearbox","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Engine reggaeton. It's the only music that sounds like an oven cooling, ticking as it contracts.","rv_vote":"Gearbox; she'll split her vote with Lothar's Metalcrown campaign if she ever meets him."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up, and would bring her ancestor's Day-Book to the Assize. Torn on Arath.","edict":"Torn. She's for honesty and records; she has proof that records nobody reads protect nobody."},"wants":"To polish the forty-first oven once, in front of everyone, with her ancestor's Day-Book open on top of it.","site":"luminant_vf1","town":"Wheatley","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:luminant_vf1":{"name":"Jarvis Oake","age":28,"role":"baker at the Oven Row, oven twenty-three; has bought the Pale Day ale four years running","look":"Short, round and floury, dark curly hair under a cloth, a burn-scar across one cheek from an oven door; always faintly smells of something toasted.","voice":"Warm, apologetic, laughs at himself before anyone else can.","greet":"Aure! Pala? Warm. Very pale. Paler than usual. I'm trying.","lines":["Pale Day's coming. Any loaf with a brown patch goes to the hens and the baker buys the ale. I've bought the ale four years running. The hens know me by name.","Halden at the mill weighs every sack in front of you and then says what he thinks of you. He called me 'cheerful'. I think it was a complaint.","The Citadel says our pala isn't as pale as it was. It is. I hold every loaf up to the noon. Most of them."],"about":"I'm Jarvis, oven twenty-three. I bake pala for Meridian, a hundred loaves a shift. I'm good at it, mostly. I buy the ale on Pale Day. Everyone's very understanding.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a little"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"bread-words, from a Flameborne baker at a Hearthward fair"}],"childhood":"Jarvis was a baker's son on the Oven Row, third of five, the one who was always somewhere near the food. He grew up in the brass glare, squinting, eating warm pala with hind's-milk butter off the oven ledge, and he loved it with his whole heart. He was cheerful in a way that made his mother worry he'd never be taken seriously. At nine he was found in the back kitchen at home with a loaf he'd browned on purpose on the hearth, eating it with his eyes shut, and his mother said, 'Don't let anyone see you,' which is the closest a Wheatley mother comes to hiding something. He never forgot the taste. He never forgot the look on her face either.","schooling":"Master Elden Rye's school, where he was well liked and inattentive. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at twelve to work his father's oven, and at eighteen spent a summer at a Hearthward fair with a Flameborne baker who taught him slapbread on hot stone and told him he had good hands and a cowardly heart.","history":"He took oven twenty-three from his father at twenty-two. He bakes well. He bakes very well, by any Realm's standard but Wheatley's, because his loaves now and then come out with the faintest touch of colour on one edge, and on Pale Day, when every baker holds a loaf to the noon on the green, his has a patch. He has bought the ale four years running. The village is fond of him and mildly embarrassed. What nobody knows is that at home, in his own kitchen, with the shutters open because he's Lightforge, he bakes himself a browned loaf every tenth day and eats it at his table. It isn't hidden. Anyone could walk past and see. He writes it in his Day-Book as 'baked for myself', which is true. This season he has started to wonder whether he'd rather be a baker in Flameborne, and has written that down too.","family":"Parents retired; four siblings, two baking; he's courting Mira Mill's apprentice at the Twos, a papermaker named Della, who likes his bread brown and says so.","hero":{"who":"The Flameborne King","why":"A King who answers refusals with bread. Jarvis has never wanted to rule anything, but he thinks bread as the answer to an argument is the best idea anyone's had."},"band":{"act":"The Kept Coals","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stomp-folk bakers. He saw them at Hearthward the summer he learned slapbread and stomped till his shoes split.","rv_vote":"The Kept Coals, and he'll bake a brown loaf for the lamp-count."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, but would worry the whole year. Against Arath, gently.","edict":"Against, quietly. 'It's like Pale Day for people. Everyone held up to the light, and the ones with a patch go to the hens.'"},"wants":"To stop buying the ale, or stop minding.","site":"luminant_vf1","town":"Wheatley","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:luminant_vf1:b":{"name":"Winifred Fallow","age":60,"role":"miller's wife and the mill's bookkeeper; writes the sack-weights and Halden's opinions","look":"Thin and upright, iron-grey bun, spectacles on a ribbon, flour in the seams of her dress she's given up brushing out; a pen behind each ear.","voice":"Dry, gently amused, finishes her husband's sentences more politely than he started them.","greet":"Aure. Halden will weigh your sack and then tell you what he thinks of you. I'll write down the weight. And something kinder.","lines":["Halden has said what he thinks of every buyer for thirty years. I've written it all down. Somewhat edited.","Lyriel Frostwraith warned the Council and they struck her from the record. Our Lightforge seat helped. I keep a page for her. In my book, not theirs.","The carts go north full and come back empty. Piers counts them. I count the sacks. Our numbers agree. The Assize's don't."],"about":"I keep the mill's books. I've been married to Halden Fallow for thirty-eight years. He's honest. I'm honest too. I'm just honest more slowly.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, very precise"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, for the flour trade"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"she's learning it from a book, very slowly"}],"childhood":"Winifred was a clerk's daughter in Meridian, raised in a house where every word was weighed, and she was a careful, observant girl who liked to read other people's Day-Books, which is allowed and which nobody does. By twelve she had read every Day-Book on her street and knew more about her neighbours than they knew about each other. At her First Telling she said, 'I've read all of yours,' and the street went silent, and then old Mistress Brey laughed so hard she had to sit down, and Winifred was given the told name Winifred Who Read Them. She learned that knowing a great deal and saying a little was a kind of power, and also a kind of kindness.","schooling":"The Meridian Weighhouse district school, then two years copying for the Assize registry, where she learned the shape of the law from its paperwork. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She was very good at copying and very bored. The registry taught her that every official page has another page behind it, the draft, the note in the margin, the copy that went somewhere else, and that the second page is usually the truer one.","history":"At twenty-two she came to Wheatley to deliver registry papers to the mill and met Halden Fallow, who weighed the papers in front of her and told her she'd been sent with too many. She agreed, which nobody had done before. They married within the year. For thirty-eight years she has kept the mill's books: every sack, every weight, every buyer. Halden says aloud what he thinks of each buyer, and Winifred writes it down in her own Day-Book, which buyers sometimes read, but in her words, which are kinder. Customers who stay with Halden for life usually stayed after reading Winifred. This season she has started a page in her Day-Book for Lyriel Frostwraith, the Frostgrave Seer whom the Council struck from the record after her warning, with Lightforge's voice among those who overruled her. Winifred writes a line on it every day. She is learning Rime from a book, slowly, to read Lyriel's words if she ever finds them.","family":"Husband Halden; two sons, one milling, one an Assize clerk in Meridian whom she writes to every tenth day.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"She warned of collapse and was struck from the record. Winifred has spent her life keeping the true record behind the official one and thinks Lyriel deserved a better clerk."},"band":{"act":"Mason Grey","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A quarry baritone soul singer. Halden snores in a baritone, and she finds it comforting after thirty-eight years; Mason Grey is the waking version.","rv_vote":"Mason Grey, because Halden won't, and somebody in the house should."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and would read every document twice. Doubtful of Arath.","edict":"Doubtful. She's a record-keeper. She's watched records used to strike people out."},"wants":"To find a copy of Lyriel's warning in her own Realm's archive, and read it.","site":"luminant_vf1","town":"Wheatley","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:luminant_vf2":{"name":"Cassia Reedman","age":37,"role":"Marrowfen's trader; sells packs, bows and reed goods, and carries the fen's antlers to Lampwick herself","look":"Long-legged and mud-booted to the knee, reed-pale hair in two plaits, a Luminar antler tine hung at her belt that glows faintly whenever she steps into shade.","voice":"Quick and businesslike, a laugh like a reed whistle; she always tells you the Lampwick price as well as hers.","greet":"Aure. Bows, packs, reed mats, custard if you're lucky. And antlers. Fen price, not Lampwick price. I'll tell you both.","lines":["The Rim antler-traders buy at fen prices and sell at Lampwick prices. So I walk them to Lampwick myself. Two days each way. Worth every blister.","The stags shed early this year. All of them, in one bell. Sorel says the fen books say they did the same the year of the Burning of the Back. I'm only telling you what she says.","Lamp-checks on the Rim count your lamp-oil. They don't count antlers. An antler lights a room and burns nothing. I've noticed. Others have noticed."],"about":"I trade for the fen. I got sick of watching Rim middlemen double our antler price, so I started walking the antlers to Lampwick myself. Now the middlemen hate me and the fen buys me ale. Fair trade.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, fen accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"}],"childhood":"Cassia was born the year after the herd came home from the Rim, into a village still a little giddy with having survived, and she grew up on the stories of the walk: who carried whom, who sang the hinds along, how little Sorel Fenwick walked the whole way at five without being carried once. Cassia wanted a story like that and the fen kept not giving her one. She was a restless, bargaining child who sold reed whistles to Pillar Road walkers at six and doubled her price for Meridian accents at seven. Her mother cut reeds and her father milked; neither of them ever haggled in their lives. When she was ten she went down to Lampwick with a Rim antler-trader as his carrier and saw him sell a fen antler for five times what he'd paid, and walked the two days home without saying a word.","schooling":"The fen-school in the milking shed, taught by Mistress Garra Sedge, who used custard baskets to teach fractions and made every child learn the Hind-Calling notes before their letters. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She was good at sums and bad at patience, and left at twelve to cut reeds.","history":"She cut reeds until nineteen and saved every copper. At twenty she bought a cart and an old mule called Plainly and walked the season's antlers to Lampwick herself, selling them at the stag-antler lamp-makers' doors for what the middlemen got. The Rim traders tried to undercut her for three years and then gave up and started buying from her instead. She took over the fen's little trading post at twenty-eight and now sells packs, bows, reed mats and brooms to travellers, buys salvage, and walks the antlers down twice a season. She has never married and has a long, cheerful arrangement with a Lampwick lamp-maker called Dorran which both of them write about in their Day-Books in the same words. This season she's noticed something new at the Lampwick doors: Rim households asking for antlers, not lamps. Unworked tines, a dozen at a time. An antler lights a room in the Rim's dusk and needs no oil, and the new lamp-checks count oil.","family":"Parents still milk; two younger sisters, both cutters. Dorran in Lampwick, on and off for nine years.","hero":{"who":"Sorel Fenwick (Sorel Who Followed)","why":"She walked the whole drought road at five. Cassia was born just after and has spent her life trying to walk somewhere that far on her own legs."},"band":{"act":"Willowroot","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Amapiano played on willow harps; she heard it drifting over the mist-path the first time she walked to Lampwick and has looked for it on every walk since.","rv_vote":"Willowroot, because it sounds like reeds in wind."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up, and would bring a cart of antlers to Meridian to sell between sittings. Torn on Arath.","edict":"Torn. She doesn't mind lists. She minds that the Rim is being counted by its oil and not by anything that matters."},"wants":"To sell the fen's antlers at Lampwick price without walking two days to do it.","site":"luminant_vf2","town":"Marrowfen","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:luminant_vf2":{"name":"Ewart Hindley","age":67,"role":"fen smith; reed-sickles, pail-hoops, milking-stool irons and the pins that hold the Antler Hedge together","look":"Bent and bow-legged, white hair standing up like reed-down, enormous hands with a wedding ring worn on a cord because it won't fit his knuckle any more.","voice":"Soft and sing-song; he hums the Hind-Calling under everything he says, slightly flat.","greet":"Seen, seen. Hold that pail a moment. No, the other way up. There. Now we're friends.","lines":["When I was seven the springs failed and the herd walked off towards the Rim, and we followed it. My gran stayed behind. Somebody had to keep the fen for when we came back.","The Hind-Calling's been changed twice in six hundred years. The second time was me. By accident. I was sixteen and I sang flat. The hinds came anyway. The village voted to keep it.","Meridian drains a little more of the fen each generation for white streets. I've pinned the Hedge back nine times. One day there'll be nothing to pin."],"about":"I keep the Hedge pinned and the pails round. I was born here, walked away at seven with the herd, walked back at eight, and haven't left since except for one wedding and two Grief Rooms.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, old fen speech"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a dozen words he refuses to pronounce properly"}],"childhood":"Ewart was seven when the springs failed. Before that he remembers a fen thick with reeds, milk in every pail, and his grandmother Hesk teaching him to whistle the hinds in with two fingers. Then the water went brown and thin and the herd stood with their heads up for a day and walked off towards the Rim, and the village packed and followed. His father carried him the first day, then he walked. He remembers the Rim's dusk for the first time, and being terrified of it, and Sorel Fenwick, smaller than him, holding his hand in it. His grandmother would not come. She said someone had to be there when they came back, so the fen would know it was still theirs. When the rains brought them home a year later, she was in the milking shed, thin as a reed, and had kept every pail scrubbed.","schooling":"The milking-shed school under Mistress Garra Sedge's mother, Mistress Elin Sedge, who taught the children to write by having them copy the fen books, the old herd records going back centuries, so that every fen child can name the year of every early shedding. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at eleven for his uncle's forge.","history":"He apprenticed to his uncle at eleven and took the forge at twenty-five. At sixteen he sang the third note of the Hind-Calling flat for a whole season because his voice was breaking, and the hinds came to the flat note as readily as the true one, and the village met, argued for a month, and voted to keep it, the second change in six hundred years. He married Ilsa Brook, a custard-setter, at twenty-two; they had three children and forty-one years together before she died of a wasting illness, and he named her in the Meridian Grief Room because the fen has none and walked home the same day. He has pinned the Antler Hedge back nine times as Meridian's drainage crews took another strip of fen, each time moving the pins a few yards inward. He keeps a ledger of every move. This season the stags shed early, all in one bell, and Ewart was the one who stood in the shed beside Sorel Fenwick while she read the old fen book, the same book he copied at nine.","family":"Three children, two in the fen, one a Sentinel. Seven grandchildren. His grandmother Hesk, named in Meridian fifty-eight years ago.","hero":{"who":"Orrin Who Stayed","why":"The first Anchorite stayed still on a cliff for the ones who'd come back. Ewart's grandmother stayed in the fen for a year so it would still be theirs. He thinks Orrin would have liked her."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Forty veterans who never sing above a murmur. He heard them once at Hearthward and said it was the only choir he'd ever heard that sang the way the fen sounds at the eighth bell.","rv_vote":"The Lonely Line Choir. They'll never win. He likes that about them."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and would bring the Hedge ledger to show the Assize. For Arath, because Arath looks like someone who'd read a ledger.","edict":"Loyal, mostly; he has nothing to hide and trusts the law. He'd like the law to stop draining the fen."},"wants":"To pin the Hedge one last time and have it stay.","site":"luminant_vf2","town":"Marrowfen","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:luminant_vf2":{"name":"Linnea Calloway","age":22,"role":"hind-milker at the Antler Hedge; the one who wants to change a note","look":"Short and strong, freckled to the elbows, a reed-cut scar through one eyebrow; always has a milking stool hooked over her arm like a handbag.","voice":"Fierce and cheerful; argues by asking you to sing something, then pointing out you did it wrong.","greet":"Aure! You're just in time for the eighth bell. Stand there. Don't sing. Unless you can. Can you?","lines":["The Hind-Calling's four notes have been changed twice in six hundred years. I want to change the fourth. The hinds don't like it. I've watched. The whole village has to vote.","Meridian eats our custard cold and plain. Calls the honey a sauce. A sauce! It's honey. Honey doesn't hide anything. It just sticks.","The Councillor without a Realm stood up when they laughed at Metalcrown. One person. Standing. I think about that when I'm arguing with the moot."],"about":"I milk the hinds, I set the custard, and I've asked the whole fen to vote on changing the fourth note of the Hind-Calling. They think I'm mad. I've written down why I'm not. It's very long.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, fen accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting, enthusiastic"}],"childhood":"Linnea was the fifth daughter of a milking family, and the noisiest, and she grew up on a milking stool at the Antler Hedge with her ear pressed to a hind's flank, listening to it breathe. She learned the four notes of the Hind-Calling at three and sang them at the wrong bell for a week, bringing the whole herd to the Hedge at odd hours, and her mother wrote: 'Linnea has called the hinds. Again.' She has always noticed things other people didn't bother to: that the old hinds turned their heads on the third note and the young ones on the fourth, that one hind came only when a particular milker sang. She kept a notebook of hinds by six. She still keeps it.","schooling":"The milking-shed school under Mistress Garra Sedge, who once let her spend an entire term writing down the Hind-Calling as numbers instead of notes, to see if it made sense that way. It did. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She left at twelve to milk. Mistress Sedge wrote on Linnea's last report, 'Will change something in this village, and we will all have to sit through it,' and Linnea has the report pinned above her bed.","history":"She took her own string of hinds at sixteen and has milked them six years. She is the most observant milker the fen has had in a generation, and the most exhausting. For four years she has watched the young hinds hang back on the fourth note of the Calling and come in late, and has written down every bell: which hinds, how late, how much milk lost. Her conclusion is that the fourth note has drifted, sung slightly differently by each generation, until the young hinds no longer recognise it. She wants to change it back, or change it forward, depending on whom you ask. Changing a note of the Hind-Calling is a matter for the whole village and has been done twice in six hundred years, once by Ewart Hindley by accident. This season she put the motion to the moot. The moot has asked her to bring her evidence in writing. She has brought it: two hundred pages. They are reading it, slowly, and some of them are reading it in Ewart's hearing on purpose.","family":"Four older sisters, all married and all weary of her. Parents milking still. A sweetheart, Rafe, who cuts reeds and claims to be tone-deaf to stay out of it.","hero":{"who":"The Hammer-Bearer","why":"The Councillor without a Realm was the only one who stood when the Council laughed at Metalcrown's petition. She is, at the moot, often the only one standing."},"band":{"act":"Echo Bleed","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A DJ who samples dead singers. 'Somebody Else's Summer' sounds to her like a Hind-Calling sung by a dead milker, and she wants to know who.","rv_vote":"Echo Bleed, in the Sparkbeat Cup and the lamps, loudly."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and argue with the whole Assize about procedure. Against Arath: 'properly' is what the moot says to her every week.","edict":"Against. 'They want to hold everyone up to the noon. Some things only show at the eighth bell.'"},"wants":"To win the vote on the fourth note before the next shedding.","site":"luminant_vf2","town":"Marrowfen","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:luminant_vf2:b":{"name":"Lambert Drain","age":50,"role":"reed-cutter and ditch-mender; formerly a Meridian street-layer who helped drain the fen","look":"Tall and stooped, a city man gone to seed in the fen, white street-layer's dust still ground into old boots he won't throw out; ears red from the sun.","voice":"Apologetic and precise, a Meridian accent the fen has spent twelve years trying to soften.","greet":"Aure. I'm the one who drained it. Some of it. Best you hear that from me first.","lines":["Meridian drains a little more of the fen each generation to lay new white streets. I laid some of those streets. Three of them. You can walk on them. I don't.","I married into the fen. My wife's family took me in. Her father shook my hand and then made me cut reeds for a year before he'd sit with me. Fair.","The stags shed early this year. All in one bell. I was there. I've never seen anything so frightening that made no noise."],"about":"I was a street-layer in Meridian for twenty years. I drained part of this fen for white streets. Then I came out to see what I'd drained and met my wife, and I stayed. I cut reeds now. I try to put back what I can.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, Meridian formal"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, from guild contracts"}],"childhood":"Lambert grew up in Meridian's Lime Quarter among street-layers who whitewashed the roads every season, so that the whole city stays the white of new bread. His father laid stone; his mother mixed lime; Lambert was the child who sat on the curb drawing roads in chalk. He loved straight lines and clean edges and the way a new street looked under the noon, perfect and empty. At nine he walked into the Assize registry and asked for a copy of the city's street plan, and was given one, and slept with it under his pillow. His mother wrote, 'Lambert wants to make the whole world flat.' He would have said yes.","schooling":"The Lime Quarter district school under Master Telford Brand, who taught geometry with chalk on the street outside and made the children walk their proofs. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. At fourteen he joined the Street-Layers' Guild and swore its oath: 'Every road true, every edge clean.' The Guild school after that taught him surveying, levels, drainage and the cutting of a clean ditch, and he was its best student in a decade.","history":"He laid streets for twenty years and was good at it. At thirty-two he joined a survey crew for a new district on the fen side of Meridian, where the land had to be drained before it could take white stone. He drove stakes and cut ditches through reeds and watched the water go. At thirty-eight, on a rest day, he walked out along his own drainage line to see where the water came from, and found Marrowfen, and the Antler Hedge, and a milker named Bethan Reed who told him exactly what he'd done, without raising her voice. He went back to Meridian, released himself from the Guild oath in writing, which no one had done in a century and which caused a small scandal, and came back to the fen with one bag. Bethan's father made him cut reeds for a year before he'd sit with him at table. They married the next spring. He mends ditches now, and quietly blocks the old drainage cuts one by one. He has blocked eleven.","family":"Wife Bethan, two children, eight and ten. His mother in Meridian, who writes every month and has never once mentioned the fen.","hero":{"who":"Olin Stair","why":"The Thawborn who believed a promise could be released, and who carried his sister down the Glass Stair. Lambert released an oath, and carries reeds, and thinks Olin would understand both."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Twelve-minute post-rock; he listens to it while cutting reeds because it takes exactly as long as a row.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters, which Bethan says is the only thing about him that's still from Meridian, and she's wrong."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, and would sit very straight. Doubtful of Arath; he knows what 'properly' looks like on a survey map.","edict":"Doubtful. He's watched the Citadel plan a fen out of existence in clean lines and believes it can do the same to people."},"wants":"To block the last drainage cut before Meridian notices the fen coming back.","site":"luminant_vf2","town":"Marrowfen","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:dawnwatch_vf1":{"name":"Rennick Barkley","age":29,"role":"Rushbrook trader; sells Rim beer, leather goods, packs and bows, and wants to modernise the pits","look":"Lean, quick and restless, brown to the wrist from the pits, a Metalcrown-made brass pressure gauge hung round his neck like a medal; boots too good for Rushbrook.","voice":"Fast and persuasive, a pitch in every sentence; uses Metalcrown words like 'yield' and 'efficiency' and enjoys the faces it gets.","greet":"Aure. Beer's the colour of the brook and tastes better. Packs, bows, a good belt. And if you've got an idea for making this place less poor, I'm buying.","lines":["Out here we say mercy needs shade. Up there they write it down and send someone. I say: fine. Let them write. And let's get a pump.","Dawnwatch buys our best saddle leather cheap and still sends Sentinels twice a year to measure the Shade Shed. Twice a year for a hand's width. Imagine if they measured our wages that carefully.","A Metalcrown man showed me a pump that could empty a soaking pit in a bell. A bell! We do it by bucket in a day."],"about":"I trade for Rushbrook: beer, leather, kit. I was born in the pits and I'm sick of being poor in a Realm that has endless noon. I've got plans. Some of them come from Metalcrown. People here don't like that. People here don't like much.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, Rim drawl he's trying to lose"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a working vocabulary of engines and money"}],"childhood":"Rennick grew up in a currier's house so poor that the back window, the one every Lightforge house must have, had no glass in it, only oiled hide. He spent his childhood at Dusk Supper, eating bark-smoked stag at the long table with every household in the village and nobody allowed to speak of their day, and he hated that rule from the first time he understood it. He wanted to talk about his day. He wanted his days to be worth talking about. At eight he built a waterwheel out of bark-strips in the brown brook and it turned for an afternoon before the current took it. He spent the next five years building better ones. Nobody in Rushbrook asked him why.","schooling":"Mistress Faye Orrick's room above the currier's, where he talked through every lesson and once, asked to read a Day-Book aloud with his eyes shut, recited instead a plan for draining the soaking pits with a wheel. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at twelve for the pits.","history":"He worked the pits from twelve to twenty and hated it, and was good at it, which made it worse. At twenty he took over the village trade from an old currier who wanted to stop, and has since doubled Rushbrook's leather sales, mainly by walking samples to Goldfield, Tempest Hold's riders and the Pillar Road carters himself. Two years ago a Metalcrown engineer came through on the edge-road heading for Lampwick and stopped to buy a belt, and stayed three days, talking pumps. Rennick has not stopped talking pumps since. Last season he took a loan from a Metalcrown trading house, iron-backed, to buy one pump and the fittings for it, and the pump is now standing, wrapped in tarp, in the back of his shop. Half of Rushbrook thinks it's a fine idea. The other half thinks Metalcrown never lent anything to anybody without wanting something back. Both halves eat at the same long table and say nothing about it at Dusk Supper.","family":"Mother and two sisters at the pits; father dead of a lung fever. Odra Brookwater is his named-aunt and his fiercest critic.","hero":{"who":"The Ninth Dominus","why":"'Inefficiency kills children. This can be corrected.' Rennick watched his father die poor and thinks the Dominus is the only ruler in the Nine who says the plain thing about poverty."},"band":{"act":"Third Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Industrial metal; 'I Made It' is the only song he's ever heard about being proud of your own work instead of your family's.","rv_vote":"Third Whistle, and he's already told the long table, which was a Dusk Supper rule broken, and nobody stopped him."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and take a Metalcrown pump drawing to Meridian. For Arath: someone needs to run things properly.","edict":"For, pragmatically: 'If they want a list, give them a list. Then maybe they'll stop measuring our shed and start measuring our wages.'"},"wants":"To get the pump running before the next Sentinel inspection, so they have something real to measure.","site":"dawnwatch_vf1","town":"Rushbrook","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:dawnwatch_vf1":{"name":"Rhoswen Knapp","age":48,"role":"Rushbrook's smith; fleshing knives, scrapers, beam-hooks and pit-irons","look":"Broad and quiet, hair cropped to grey stubble, forearms stained brown to the elbow like every tanner, and not one maker's mark on any knife she's ever made.","voice":"Spare and low; answers in as few words as will do and lets silence finish the rest.","greet":"Seen. Knife? Let me see your hands first. Then I'll know which knife.","lines":["Every smith in Lightforge marks their work. I don't. A good blade shouldn't need a name on it. Tanners know mine.","The Shade Shed missed needing a licence by a hand's width. A hand's width. My grandfather built that end wall. Draw your own lines.","Dusk Supper's my favourite meal. Nobody says what they did. Nobody asks what I made."],"about":"I make the knives the tanners use. Fleshing, scraping, splitting. I've made them thirty years. None of them carries my mark, and every tanner on the Rim could tell you whose they are.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, Rim drawl"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"poor"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"a few signs, from a Shadowdeep hide-buyer"}],"childhood":"Rhoswen grew up in the forge at the downstream end of Rushbrook, where the brook was browner than anywhere and the smell was strongest, and she spent most of her childhood in the Shade Shed's long dim end, playing among the soaking pits because nobody minded and nobody could see her. Her grandfather Idwal had built the shed's end wall when he was young and told her, once, that a wall is only as long as the man who measures it says it is. She didn't understand him then. She was a silent, solid girl who could sit still for a whole bell watching her father grind an edge, and who at seven ground her first scraper so true that a tanner paid her for it. She hid the copper. Then, because she was Lightforge, she wrote down where.","schooling":"Mistress Faye Orrick's room above the currier's, three years, where she learned letters and that she didn't like being looked at. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp flared and died almost at once. Half-Spark, registered at the time and never used since. Her father taught her the forge from nine.","history":"She took the forge at eighteen when her father's lungs failed, as Rim lungs do. She has made every fleshing knife and beam-scraper in Rushbrook for thirty years, and half of Lampwick's, and she has never once stamped a mark, in a Realm where assayers sign every coin and smiths sign every blade. Meridian clerks have fined her for it twice. She pays and still doesn't mark. She married late, at thirty-six, to Garrow Pit, a currier with a booming laugh she says she can't stand; they have one son, twelve, who wants to be a Sentinel. Her grandfather Idwal died when she was twenty. Clearing his things she found his Day-Book for the year he built the Shade Shed end wall, and the entry for the day the Sentinels first measured it. It reads: 'Measured. Short by a hand. As built.' She has read that line many times.","family":"Husband Garrow, son Davyd (twelve). Her sister married into Candlewick and dips at the Wheel.","hero":{"who":"Veilrend","why":"Shadowdeep's First Blade, whom no one has ever seen the face of. Rhoswen thinks the best blades don't need a name on them, and nor did the best blade-hand."},"band":{"act":"The Hollows Kids","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"No singing, only blades on stone and hand signs. It's the only music that sounds like her forge, and the Shadowdeep hide-buyer took her to see them at a Lampwick fair.","rv_vote":"The Hollows Kids, without telling anyone, which she knows is not how Day-Books work."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and say nothing all year unless asked. Against Arath.","edict":"Against. 'They want every spark marked like a coin. I won't mark a knife.'"},"wants":"To be left alone with her grindstone until her own lungs go.","site":"dawnwatch_vf1","town":"Rushbrook","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:dawnwatch_vf1":{"name":"Alder Brookwater","age":74,"role":"retired pit-master; Odra Brookwater's uncle; keeper of the long table at Dusk Supper","look":"Gaunt and very tall, brown to the shoulders from sixty years of dye, white hair to his collar; walks with a stick of black Rim oak and never carries a lamp.","voice":"Slow, deep and kind; leaves long pauses, as if expecting you to fill them with something true.","greet":"Seen, seen. Sit if you like, the table's long. But the Day-Books stay shut till the light comes back.","lines":["Out here we say mercy needs shade. That's an old saying. Older than the Edict. Older than the Sentinels who keep measuring my niece's shed.","At Dusk Supper nobody speaks of what they did that day. Not because it's secret. Because the day is over and the evening is for something else.","Sentinels searched Rushbrook three times for Penumbrists in the old days. Found only tanners. We say that proves they can't tell the difference. Perhaps there isn't one."],"about":"I worked the pits for sixty years and I keep the long table now. I set it out at dusk and take it in at the light. I'm an old man, and I've been an honest one, and I've learned that honest isn't the same as loud.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, old Rim speech"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"poor"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a few funeral words from the mist-path"}],"childhood":"Alder grew up in the Brookwater house at the head of the brook, where the long table for Dusk Supper had always been kept in the yard, folded against the wall by day and set out every evening by whoever was eldest. When he was small, the eldest was his great-grandmother Morwen, who was blind and set the table by touch and knew every plate. He was a tall, gentle, slow-speaking boy who other children found odd, because he listened to them for longer than they expected. At Dusk Supper, where no one may speak of their day, he loved the way the adults talked instead about what might be, or what had been long ago, or what they were afraid of. He decided at nine that this was the best talk in the world. He has never changed his mind.","schooling":"The Rushbrook lamp-school above the currier's, under a teacher before Mistress Orrick, Master Ewan Tanner, who read the children stories in the Rim's dusk without a lamp, from memory. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at eleven for the pits. Master Tanner never once wrote anything on a board. Every lesson was told aloud in the dusk, and Alder can still recite the whole of the Rim's coast-road from Dawnwatch to Lampwick in the order the teacher walked it.","history":"He went into the pits at eleven and was pit-master by forty. He married Seren Dale at twenty-three; she died at fifty, and he named her in the Rushbrook Grief Room, the one licensed dark room on the Rim, where he has since named his brother, two friends and a great-niece. When the eldest at the long table died, he became the one who set it out. That was thirty years ago. In those thirty years, on certain evenings, after the plates are cleared and before the light comes back, someone at the far end of his long table has read aloud from a Penumbrist book, in the dark, to whoever stays. Alder has never read himself. He sets the table and keeps the silence. This season the Edict named the Penumbrists as outlaws, and on the first evening after the news came from Meridian, more people stayed at the long table than ever before. A dozen new fen antlers lit it, unrecorded by any lamp-check.","family":"Widower; no children. His niece Odra, the pit-master, whom he raised from twelve after her parents drowned in the brook flood, and who fights everyone except him.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"The first Penumbrist said mercy needs shade, here on the Rim, in the dusk. He has spent his life setting a table where that could be said."},"band":{"act":"Under Haskerry","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Whispered folk. It's the only music that fits Dusk Supper, and a Shadowdeep hide-buyer sang it once at the far end of his table.","rv_vote":"Under Haskerry, which he'll write down in plain words, in the light."},"vote":{"local":"He's stood his lot twice and would again, and would say very little and listen to everything. Against Arath, quietly.","edict":"A secret Penumbrist host. He has never read, but he has set the table for thirty years, and the Edict has made the table a crime."},"wants":"For the long table to be set out by someone else after him, at the same hour, with the same rule.","site":"dawnwatch_vf1","town":"Rushbrook","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:dawnwatch_vf1:b":{"name":"Tilla Ambry","age":14,"role":"pit-girl at the soaking pits; Lise Ambry's little sister","look":"Small, wiry and quick, brown dye to the wrists already, hair hacked short with a fleshing knife, a dusk-moth pinned to her collar like a badge.","voice":"Breathless and bold; talks fast, laughs loud, and goes very quiet whenever an adult she doesn't know comes near.","greet":"Seen! Are you from the castle? Do you know my sister? Lise. She puts the lamp out. She's the best one on the wall.","lines":["I want to join the garrison like Lise. Lise says no. She says it a lot. She's said it in three letters.","They put Mum's name on the spark register. The clerk drank all our tea. Mum warmed her hands on her spark after he left. On purpose.","I'm small and I'm fast. Everyone says. Small and fast is good for lots of things."],"about":"I'm Tilla. I work the soaking pits. I'm going to be a soldier like Lise, or a runner, or something that isn't pits. I'm fourteen and I can run to Candlewick and back before the light comes.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, broad Rim"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Tilla was the youngest Ambry, three years behind Lise and spoiled by everybody, and she spent her childhood running: down the brook, round the Shade Shed, up the Dawnwatch road to watch the soldiers, back before Dusk Supper. She was never still. She fell in a soaking pit at five, the same pit Lise had fallen in, and was called Pit Two for a year. She adored her sister with a ferocity that annoyed both of them. When Lise left for the garrison at sixteen, Tilla, eleven, ran behind the recruiting cart for a mile and a half until the sergeant stopped and sent her home on a mule. She wrote it in her Day-Book in letters so big they took three pages.","schooling":"Mistress Faye Orrick's room above the currier's, where she read Day-Books aloud with her eyes shut faster than anyone in the class and got half the words wrong. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp gave a faint flicker, then went out. Half-Spark, like her mother and sister. She left at thirteen for the pits and hates it.","history":"She has worked the soaking pits for a year. She is a poor tanner and an excellent messenger, and Rushbrook has started sending her to Candlewick, Dawnwatch and back with anything that needs to get there fast. This season the Edict's clerks came and put her mother's name on the spark register, and sat in the Ambry kitchen until dark asking every time her mother had used her spark since she was nine, and Tilla sat on the stairs and listened to every word. Two weeks later old Alder Brookwater asked her, at the long table after Dusk Supper, whether she could carry a wrapped parcel to the Candlewick smithy, quickly, without stopping, and give it only to Dunstan Rendle. She did. She has done it four times since. She has not asked what's in it. She has written each run in her Day-Book as 'ran to Candlewick for Alder', which is true.","family":"Mother Haska, father Jemm, an older brother in the pits, and Lise, whom she writes to every week and who writes back telling her not to join the garrison.","hero":{"who":"Her sister, Lise Ambry","why":"Lise signed up at sixteen when their mother said no, and she puts out the only lamp anyone at Dawnwatch puts out on purpose. Tilla thinks that's the bravest job in the world."},"band":{"act":"Telt","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Punk so loud it's banned at home in Shadowdeep. A hide-buyer's son taught her the chorus, and she shouts it running the brook road.","rv_vote":"Telt, because they're banned and she isn't."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for the lot. Says she'd stand and 'make them sit in our kitchen till dark, see how they like it'.","edict":"Against, fiercely, since the kitchen."},"wants":"To be allowed to join the garrison and stand Lamp-Down beside her sister.","site":"dawnwatch_vf1","town":"Rushbrook","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:prismport_vf1":{"name":"Rosmund Tare","age":55,"role":"keeper of Second Farm's scales and its shop; weighs every sack of potash before Prismport does","look":"Short and very upright, iron-grey hair in a tight knot, a brass scale-weight on a chain round her neck; wears the cleanest apron in a farm covered in ash.","voice":"Clipped and formal, every number said twice: once to you, once to the scale.","greet":"Aure. Packs, bows, bread, arrows. I'll weigh anything you buy. Twice. Once for you, once for me.","lines":["Prismport weighs our potash on its scales. We don't trust its scales. So we weigh it on ours first. Then they weigh it on theirs. Then we argue.","Prismport's buying potash like there's no next season. They say Metalcrown is taking glass by the wagon. Glass for what, I'd like to know.","Third Farm's goats got into the coppice again. I've weighed the shoots they ate. I have a figure. I'll send it with the bill."],"about":"I keep the scales at Second Farm. Every sack of potash we send to Prismport goes on my scale first, and I write the weight in the farm Day-Book in front of the whole crew. I also run the shop, because someone has to and the scales are in it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, careful"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good; she reads Prismport's contracts in both tongues"}],"childhood":"Rosmund was born at Second Farm in a hut beside the old weighing-shed, the daughter of the scale-keeper before her, a large, slow man called Hobart Tare who could balance a feather against a feather. She grew up with brass weights for toys and the sound of the beam settling as the only lullaby she remembers. She was a strict, small, serious child who told the other children when they'd miscounted, and who at eight caught a Prismport carter shorting the farm by a sack and stood in front of his cart until he paid. Her father wrote that night: 'Rosmund stopped a cart. She was right.' It is the entry she is proudest of in all his books.","schooling":"The Second Farm school in the charcoal shed, taught between burns by Master Leofric Ash, who wrote sums on the shed wall with charcoal and made the crew children count stools in the coppice aloud. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She left at twelve to learn the scales from her father.","history":"She took the scales at twenty-six. For twenty-nine years every sack of Second Farm potash has gone on her beam before it goes to Prismport, and the weights are written in the farm's shared Day-Book in front of the cutting crew. She also took over the shop when the old keeper died, and now sells bread, packs and bows to carters and travellers on the Prismport lane. Her lot came up for the Assize at forty; she served a year on a trade panel, and spent most of it explaining weights to Meridian councillors. She married Arn Colley, a charcoal-burner, at thirty; he is cheerful and absent-minded and she loves him in a way she writes about only on his birthday. Eleven years ago, testing her own beam against a Gleamford standard weight borrowed for a dispute, she found that the Second Farm scale read a hair heavy. A hair. In the farm's favour.","family":"Husband Arn; a daughter, Elsbet, who cuts in the coppice; a son in Prismport's glassworks. Her father Hobart, named in the Prismport Grief Room eighteen years ago.","hero":{"who":"Sera Solbrand","why":"The only one who ever used the Judgment of One. Rosmund signs every weight alone, with no second scale behind her, and has always thought that's the hardest thing in Lightforge."},"band":{"act":"Deep Hold","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Slow heavy-flow rap: 'Ask who's asking.' Stonehold sand comes in when the lake sand runs thin, and a Stonehold carter played it on a horn at the gate.","rv_vote":"Deep Hold, because they're careful, and careful is rare."},"vote":{"local":"She's served her lot and would again. For Arath; he wants things weighed.","edict":"For, in principle; she believes in records and checking. She is very quiet about it lately."},"wants":"To fix the scale without telling Prismport it was ever wrong.","site":"prismport_vf1","town":"Prismport Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:prismport_vf1":{"name":"Felix Ashdown","age":31,"role":"coppice smith; billhooks, felling axes, pit-rakes and the iron lids of the potash pits; told name Felix Who Came Cold","look":"Slight and pale under a permanent dusting of grey ash, dark eyes, a slate and chalk hung at his belt in place of a purse.","voice":"He stammers badly aloud and writes very fluently; speaks the first word, then reaches for the slate.","greet":"S— (he writes:) Seen. Welcome. Axe, hook or rake? I'll write the price.","lines":["(on the slate) I was born the month the pit fire took half the wood and every furnace in Prismport went cold. That's my told name. I didn't choose it. I like it.","(on the slate) The Silent Sentinel speaks by carving slates. I've never met them. I think we'd get on.","(aloud, slowly) The cutters lose count. I never do. Can't talk. Count."],"about":"(on the slate) I make the iron the coppice runs on. My father made it before me. I can't say long things out loud, so I write them. People here wait for me. That's a kind thing about Second Farm.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native; writes beautifully, speaks with a severe stammer"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"reads and writes well"}],"childhood":"Felix was born at Second Farm in the coldest month the farm has known: half the coppice had burned in a pit fire, every Prismport furnace had gone out for lack of potash, and his mother went into labour in a charcoal hut with no fire because there was no wood to spare. He came out small and grey and screaming. The midwife wrote 'came cold'. He grew up slow to speak, and when he did speak the words stuck, the first sound repeating and repeating until he gave up. Other children waited, or didn't. His father, the smith, handed him a slate and chalk at five. He wrote 'thank you' on it, perfectly spelled, and his father had to go outside for a while.","schooling":"Master Leofric Ash's charcoal-shed school, where he wrote every answer on the slate and Master Ash let him, and read his slate-answers aloud to the class in a dramatic voice that made Felix laugh so hard he stammered. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. At his First Telling at twelve he wrote his true thing on a slate and his father read it out: 'I would rather write than speak, and I'm not ashamed.' He was given the told name Felix Who Came Cold, from his birth, which he thought was fair.","history":"He went into his father's forge at thirteen and took it at twenty-four when his father's hands stiffened. He makes billhooks, felling axes, pit-rakes, and the heavy iron lids of the potash pits, and keeps all of them in better order than any smith Second Farm can remember. He courts slowly and badly: two years ago he wrote a letter to Elsbet Colley, Rosmund Tare's daughter, a cutter, and she wrote back, and they have been writing letters across the farmyard ever since, which the whole farm reads, as is everyone's right, and pretends not to. The crew takes his written counts at the Cutting Count, because he never loses one. This season, with Prismport buying potash faster than ever and Metalcrown wagons on the lake road, he has started writing down every potash cart that leaves and comparing it to Rosmund's weighing-book. The numbers match. But the carts are coming back for more sooner every month.","family":"Father Bran, retired; mother Annis, a burner. Elsbet Colley, in letters.","hero":{"who":"The Silent Sentinel","why":"The Stonehold councillor whose jaw has turned to stone and who speaks by carving slates. Felix speaks by chalk on slate, and has never once felt it was less than speaking."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A doom trio whose drummer's hands are turning to stone. He likes music that keeps going when it shouldn't be able to.","rv_vote":"Burden, and he'll write the vote on a slate and hold it up."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, and write every word he had to say at the Assize. Torn on Arath.","edict":"Torn. He'd lose nothing; he's spark-poor. But he knows what it's like to be asked to say a thing aloud that you can only write."},"wants":"To say one whole sentence to Elsbet aloud, without the slate.","site":"prismport_vf1","town":"Prismport Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:prismport_vf1":{"name":"Wat Lister","age":42,"role":"coppice cutter at Second Farm; famous for losing count at the Cutting Count","look":"Huge, ruddy and cheerful, beard full of wood-chips, an axe always on his shoulder; owes more rounds of ale than anyone in the farm's history.","voice":"Loud, warm, rambling; starts a story, loses the thread, finds three others.","greet":"Aure! Don't talk to me, I'm counting. No, I've lost it. Talk to me.","lines":["Cutting Count: every stool counted aloud by the whole crew, written down. Lose count, buy the round and start again. I've bought four hundred and twelve rounds. I counted those.","Edrin Collier counts heartbeats for his burns. Exact. He's never once lost count. I don't trust a man like that. I love him, but I don't trust him.","Third Farm's goats strip our new shoots. I chased one a mile once. It won. Goats always win."],"about":"I cut the coppice. I'm good with an axe and bad with numbers, which at Second Farm is the worst combination you can be. I've bought more rounds than any man alive. I'm proud of it. Somebody has to be.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, enthusiastic"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, loudly"}],"childhood":"Wat was a cutter's son, the biggest baby Second Farm had ever weighed, a fact Rosmund Tare's father wrote in the farm book and which Wat quotes to this day. He grew up in the coppice, swinging a child's hatchet at five, climbing stools, chasing Third Farm goats out of the new shoots with a stick. He was a happy, distractible boy who could never finish a story without starting another. At the Cutting Count, where the whole crew counts every stool aloud, he lost count at eight, nine, ten and every year after, and his father bought the rounds for him until Wat was old enough to buy his own. He was never once ashamed of it. His mother wrote: 'Wat lost count. Wat was happy anyway.'","schooling":"Master Leofric Ash's charcoal-shed school, where he was beloved and hopeless, and where Master Ash once let him tell the whole class a story for an entire morning just to see if he would ever finish it. He did not. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at eleven for the coppice.","history":"He has cut the Second Farm coppice for thirty-one years and is one of the strongest cutters the farm has ever had. He was eleven the year of the great pit fire and remembers chopping firebreaks beside his father for three days with no sleep, and the cold month after, when every Prismport furnace went out and Second Farm ate pit potatoes for every meal. He married Willa Dun at twenty-five, a Third Farm goat-keeper, which caused an argument between the farms that is still going on, chiefly about goats. They have four children, all of whom can count. At the Cutting Count he has lost his place four hundred and twelve times. The farm keeps the tally. He keeps it too, and never loses that one, which he says proves he's selective. This season he's started counting other things: the Metalcrown wagons on the lake road, the potash carts, the Sentinels riding through on the Edict's business. He hasn't lost count of any of them.","family":"Wife Willa from Third Farm; four children; a mother-in-law, Calla Kidwell's cousin, who reckons his descent at him nine generations back whenever he visits.","hero":{"who":"Vaelora Lanthys","why":"The Spiritveil councillor who chose silence. Wat has never chosen silence in his life and thinks it must be the hardest thing anyone could do."},"band":{"act":"The Knot-Cord Brothers","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"The only fast Frostgrave act: 'Forty Seconds'. He's tried to count the beats in it and lost count every time, and loves it more for that.","rv_vote":"The Knot-Cord Brothers, and he'll buy the round when they lose."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, and the Assize would never get anything done. Doubtful of Arath, because Arath looks like a man who's never lost count.","edict":"Doubtful. 'They want to count everybody. Counting's harder than they think. Ask me.'"},"wants":"To get through one Cutting Count without losing his place, just once, and then never again.","site":"prismport_vf1","town":"Prismport Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:prismport_vf1:b":{"name":"Sidony Twofold","age":63,"role":"retired potash-burner; keeper of the Number Stone; descendant of the couple who couldn't agree a name","look":"Tall and gaunt, grey hair cut in a fringe, a burner's scarred hands; walks every day to the lane end to wipe the Number Stone clean with her sleeve.","voice":"Measured and wry, very fond of a pause; never finishes an argument, only postpones it.","greet":"Aure. Second Farm. Pending agreement. As it has been for three hundred years.","lines":["The Number Stone says '2' and underneath, 'pending agreement'. My ancestors couldn't agree a name. The clerk wouldn't write one that wasn't true for both. I have a name in mind. My husband has another.","I mean to propose a name at the next Unshadowing. The whole farm will have to agree. My husband will disagree. I've written down what he'll say. I'm ready.","The Lawbringer wrote the Edict down properly, word by word. Whatever else, it's written properly. Our stone was written properly too, and look how long that's lasted."],"about":"I'm Sidony Twofold. My name is a joke three hundred years old. My ancestor the glassmaster and his wife couldn't agree on a name for this farm, and the clerk wrote '2, pending agreement.' I've decided it's time. My husband hasn't.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Sidony was raised in the old house at the lane end, the one the first glassmaster and his wife built and could not agree to name, and every generation of her family since has carried the surname Twofold, given them by the farm as a joke and kept as a badge. She grew up hearing the story told both ways: that the glassmaster wanted to call the farm after his mother and his wife after the coppice, or the other way round, depending on which side of the family was telling it. She was a serious, argumentative child who took sides early and changed them often. At ten she painted a name on the Number Stone in whitewash, and the rain washed it off, and her grandmother wrote: 'Sidony named the farm. The farm declined.'","schooling":"Master Leofric Ash's father, Master Aldric Ash, at the charcoal-shed school, who taught history from the farm's own Day-Books and made the children argue both sides of the naming every year. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She left at twelve to burn potash. She was the best arguer in the shed and lost every vote she ever called, which Master Ash said was the most useful lesson the school had to give.","history":"She burned potash for forty years, mixing beech-ash, leaching it, boiling it down to the white salt Prismport's furnaces eat, and was the best burner on the farm for twenty of them. She was twenty-eight the year of the great pit fire and helped dig the firebreaks, and has always said the coppice growing back thicker was the farm's best argument. She married Tobiah Brack, a cutter from a Meridian family, at twenty-four. They have agreed on almost everything for thirty-nine years except the name of the farm, which they have disagreed about every Unshadowing since their wedding. This season she has decided to propose a name aloud at the coming Unshadowing, when every Lightforge household says one hidden thing, and ask the farm to agree it. Her name is 'Regrowth'. Tobiah's is 'Collier's', after the first burner. They have each written their case in their Day-Books, at length, and each has read the other's.","family":"Husband Tobiah; two daughters, one in Prismport, one burning; five grandchildren who have each been asked to pick a side and have refused.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand","why":"The Lawbringer, who writes everything properly, word by word. She admires anyone who can get a thing written down so that it stays, whatever she thinks of the Edict itself."},"band":{"act":"The Full Name","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"A singer who sings her own full name to one person, which is a scandal at home. Sidony thinks a name you choose is worth a scandal.","rv_vote":"The Full Name, which her husband says is a political statement, and it is."},"vote":{"local":"She has served her lot once and would again. For Arath, as she'd be for anyone who promised to finish something.","edict":"Loyal, with reservations she hasn't finished writing."},"wants":"For Second Farm to have a name before she dies.","site":"prismport_vf1","town":"Prismport Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:prismport_vf2":{"name":"Ferris Goatley","age":44,"role":"Third Farm's gate-trader; sells brine cheese, kids to Stormreach drovers, and packs and bows to the lake road","look":"Lanky and sun-browned, a straw hat with a goat-bite out of the brim, a white kid usually following him like a dog; forearms smelling faintly of brine.","voice":"Earnest and slightly anxious, apologises for prices before he says them; admits things nobody asked him to.","greet":"Aure. Cheese at the gate, kids in the pen, packs on the hook. Sorry about the price. It's honest. That's the problem with it.","lines":["Our goats know when weather's coming off Stormreach. They all face one way and stop eating. They've been facing east a lot this season.","I'm lie-blind, everyone says. Can't tell when a drover's having me on. So I let the goats judge. If they won't eat near a buyer, I double-count his coin.","Second Farm says our goats strip their coppice. Our goats say nothing. They're goats. I've apologised on their behalf. Twice. In writing."],"about":"I keep the gate at Third Farm. I sell what we make and the kids we can spare, mostly to Stormreach drovers who shout at me in a friendly way. I'm terrible at haggling and very good at goats.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"numbers, goats and 'sorry'"}],"childhood":"Ferris was the soft one of a hard-working goat family, the boy who named every kid and cried when they went over the border with the drovers. He grew up in the white: white goats, white brine, white cheese, white tubs, a lime-washed farmhouse, and the dark cloth tied over his eyes every time he went into the Brine House because the glare made him sneeze. He was trusting to a degree his mother found alarming; Stormreach drovers loved him because he believed every tall story they shouted at him, and at nine he gave away a kid to a drover's daughter who told him it would be a skyray's best friend. His mother wrote: 'Ferris gave away a kid. The drover paid for it later. People do, with Ferris.'","schooling":"The Third Farm school in the Brine House loft, under Mistress Harriet Curd, who taught reading from the Name-Wheel rinds, every child's name pressed into a cheese, so that the first word most Third Farm children can read is their own. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at thirteen to herd.","history":"He herded until twenty-five, took the gate when his uncle died, and has kept it since. He's known on the lake road as the most honest trader in the Prismport farms and the easiest to fool, and he knows both of those things. Twenty years ago he sold a Stormreach drover a kid that coughed, knowing it coughed, because the drover was in a hurry and Ferris didn't want to make him wait. He wrote it in his Day-Book that evening. The kid died before it reached the border and the drover never came back. Ferris has kept a coin aside for him every season since and asks every Stormreach drover who comes to the gate if they know a man with a yellow scarf called Briet, of the skyray yards — and none of them have, until this season, when one laughed and said, 'Which one? There's a sister too.' He married Joan Tallis, a cheese-setter, at thirty; they have a daughter, Rue, who haggles for him.","family":"Wife Joan, daughter Rue (fourteen), and a great-aunt, Calla Kidwell, who has worked out his descent nine generations back and is disappointed in about three of them.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"'No Realm should ever be alone.' Ferris sells goats across a border every week and thinks Stormreach and Lightforge would get on fine if they'd just stop shouting and lying respectively."},"band":{"act":"Thunderhead","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Power metal; a drover played it on a pipe at the gate and the goats all turned to face him at the chorus, which Ferris took as a sign.","rv_vote":"Thunderhead, because the goats liked it."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up, and apologise to everyone in the Assize at the start of each sitting. Torn on Arath.","edict":"Torn. He's spark-poor and trusts the law, but he's lie-blind, and he doesn't trust that the Assize can tell who's lying either."},"wants":"To find the drover he sold the coughing kid to and give him back his money.","site":"prismport_vf2","town":"Prismport Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:prismport_vf2":{"name":"Ysenne Brinewright","age":58,"role":"Third Farm smith; cheese-press screws, brine-tub hoops, curd knives and the iron racks of the Brine House","look":"Stout and square-faced, grey hair in a coronet, burn-scarred hands and a very good nose; wears a Stonehold carved-stone bead on a cord.","voice":"Gruff, says what she thinks and then explains it slowly; treats every question as a fair one.","greet":"Seen. If you've got a press that sticks, I've already fixed one like it. If you've got a cheese that sticks, that's not my department.","lines":["Forty years ago a cheese-house passed off Stonehold cheese as ours. The Witness Courts settled it by tasting. Eleven days. I was one of the tasters. I was eighteen. I've never been the same.","Third Farm cheese is the best on the Witness Plate. Stonehold cheese is the best in the world. Both of those are true. Don't tell anyone here I said the second.","The Courts argued once that our cheese should count as evidence, because it never changes its story. They pay so slowly, the cheese ages into a different one."],"about":"I make the iron the cheese depends on: presses, hoops, knives, racks. I've done it thirty years. And I was one of the tasters in the Eleven Days, which on the lake road is like saying you were at a famous battle.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"cheese-words and greetings, learned on purpose"}],"childhood":"Ysenne was the smith's daughter at Third Farm and grew up with brine in her nose and iron in her hands; her father made presses and she sat under the bench sorting screws. She was a gruff, honest, greedy child who ate the cheese-ends off the press-boards and was always caught, because her breath gave her away. At twelve, at her Name-Wheel cutting, she shared her cheese with the neighbours and told them her true thing: that she thought Third Farm cheese was a little too salty. There was a long silence. Old Calla Kidwell, then a young woman, burst out laughing. Ysenne's told name, Ysenne Who Tasted Honestly, came out of that afternoon, and it was why, six years later, the Witness Courts asked for her.","schooling":"The Brine House loft school under Mistress Harriet Curd's predecessor, Master Peregrine Whey, who used taste to teach adjectives and once made the class describe sunwine for an hour without using 'clear' or 'nothing'. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. Her father taught her the forge from ten. Master Whey's tasting lessons were the only ones she ever paid attention to, and she can still name every cheese on a Witness Plate by smell from the door.","history":"When she was eighteen a Prismport cheese-house was accused of selling Stonehold cheese stamped as Third Farm's, and the Witness Courts, unable to settle it by witnesses, settled it by tasting. Seven tasters were chosen: three from the Courts, two from Prismport, two from Third Farm. Ysenne, Who Tasted Honestly, was one. For eleven days they tasted every cheese from every press involved, blind, cloth over their eyes, in front of the Courts, and Ysenne picked out every Stonehold cheese correctly, and also wrote, in her taster's statement, that the Stonehold cheese was better. The Courts entered it in the record. Third Farm has never quite forgiven her. The Stonehold cheese-maker, a woman from Kell Deep, sent her a carved-stone bead and a whole wheel. She took the forge at twenty-eight. She has never married. She has corresponded with the Kell Deep cheese-maker's daughter for thirty years, and once a year a Stonehold wheel arrives at the forge, and Ysenne eats it alone, at the bench, slowly.","family":"Unmarried; a brother, a herder; three nephews who think she's the most interesting person at Third Farm, and they're right.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"'Lift. Then talk.' She met a dozen Stoneholders during the Eleven Days and none of them said a word until they'd carried something; she's tried to live like that and mostly failed."},"band":{"act":"Warmstone","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A sultry hot-spring dance duo. The Kell Deep letters mention them every year, and she finally saw them at a Hearthward fair and was embarrassed by how much she liked it.","rv_vote":"Warmstone, to Third Farm's alarm."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and would say exactly what she thought in the record, as she did at eighteen. Against Arath.","edict":"Against. 'They had me taste for eleven days to find a lie. Now they want to taste everybody.'"},"wants":"To visit Kell Deep and eat Stonehold cheese where it's made, before her nose goes.","site":"prismport_vf2","town":"Prismport Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:prismport_vf2":{"name":"Rafferty Kidwell","age":11,"role":"goat-boy at Third Farm; Calla Kidwell's great-grandson, waiting for his Name-Wheel cutting","look":"Small and serious, white-blond, a herding crook taller than he is, a goat-bite scar on one thumb he shows to everyone.","voice":"Grave and formal, like a little old man; recites goat lineages unasked.","greet":"Aure. That's Bramble. Out of Sedge, out of Clover, out of Mab the Second. Do you have goats? You should.","lines":["My Name-Wheel's in the brine. It's got my name pressed in the rind. Next year I cut it and share it with the neighbours I tell my true thing to. I've picked my true thing. I'm not telling you.","Great-Gran can recite every goat's descent nine generations back. I can do seven. She says I'll get there. She says I'll get there in a tone.","The Witness Courts pay slow. Gran says they think the cheese is evidence. Cheese isn't evidence. It's lunch."],"about":"I'm Rafferty Kidwell. I herd the young goats. I'm eleven. My Name-Wheel is in the brine and it has a crack, and I'm not supposed to know that, so don't say.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, oddly formal"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"'goat', 'how much' and 'no'"}],"childhood":"Rafferty has spent eleven years being the youngest Kidwell in a family that has kept the same goat line for longer than anyone can remember. His great-grandmother Calla, who can recite the descent of every animal nine generations back, has been his teacher, his nurse and his conscience since he could walk. He was a quiet toddler and a talkative small boy, mostly about goats. At five he named every kid born that spring after Lightforge laws, and the whole farm still calls the oldest nanny 'Third Statute'. At eight he got lost in the Brine House without his eye-cloth and came out squinting so hard he had to lie in the dark for an afternoon, and he liked it, and was confused about liking it.","schooling":"The Brine House loft school under Mistress Harriet Curd, where he can read every Name-Wheel rind on the racks below and has started reading the older ones, rinds of people long dead, for fun. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. His great-grandmother said 'good', then wrote it down, then crossed it out and wrote 'well', which he has thought about since.","history":"His life is short and so far mostly goats. He was given his first kid at six, Bramble, and has raised her into the best milker on the young string. His Name-Wheel, the cheese set with his name pressed into the rind the week he was born, has been ageing in the white brine for eleven years. Next year, at his First Telling, he'll cut it and share it with the neighbours he tells his one true thing to. Last month, sent into the Brine House to fetch a tub, he saw his own Name-Wheel on its rack and saw a crack along the rind, through the 'R'. He told no one, and then, because he is Lightforge, he wrote it in his Day-Book, and then he worried that writing it was the same as telling, and then he decided that if nobody reads it, it isn't. He has since asked three grown-ups whether a cracked Name-Wheel means anything. They all said no. All three of them looked away when they said it.","family":"Parents both cheese-makers; two older sisters who tease him; great-grandmother Calla Kidwell, who is the centre of his world and pretends not to know it.","hero":{"who":"His great-grandmother, Calla Kidwell","why":"She has kept the same goat line for sixty years and can tell you the descent of anyone who stands still. He wants to know that much about something, someday."},"band":{"act":"The Citadel Nine","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Idols. His sisters like them; he pretends not to and knows all their names and their drills.","rv_vote":"The Citadel Nine, because it'll annoy his sisters to have to agree with him."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for the lot. Says if it came up he'd make the Witness Courts pay for their cheese on time.","edict":"For, mostly because his teacher said a law is a promise and he likes promises. He's not sure what it's for."},"wants":"For his Name-Wheel to be fine when he cuts it.","site":"prismport_vf2","town":"Prismport Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:prismport_vf2:b":{"name":"Magda Saltash","age":49,"role":"keeper of the Brine House; turns, brines and grades every cheese in it","look":"Tall and thin, a strip of dark cloth always tied over her eyes even outside, salt-cracked hands, white headscarf; navigates by touch and the smell of brine.","voice":"Low and dry, very exact, and has a sudden filthy laugh nobody at Third Farm expects.","greet":"Aure. I can't see you, the white took my eyes years ago. Mostly. Tell me who you are, then tell me again so I believe you.","lines":["The Brine House is all white. White cheese, white brine, white tubs, lit through Prismport roof-panes. I worked it twenty years without a cloth. Now I can't take the cloth off.","The Courts argued once that our cheese should count as evidence, since it never changes its story. I took them at their word. I submitted a cheese. In my divorce.","Second Farm says our goats strip their coppice. They're not wrong. I've watched. With my nose."],"about":"I keep the Brine House. I turn every cheese, brine it, grade it, and know it by touch and smell. I'm half blind from the white, which is the joke of Third Farm, and I'm the best cheese-grader in Lightforge, which isn't.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, lake accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, for buyers"}],"childhood":"Magda grew up at the edge of Third Farm, in a cottage too small for her family of seven, and spent her childhood trying to be anywhere else. The Brine House was the only quiet place she knew: cool, white, smelling of salt, with nobody in it but the cheeses. She'd hide in it and read, which you're meant not to do because of the glare, and by twelve her eyes ached by the end of every afternoon and she didn't care. At her Name-Wheel cutting she shared her cheese and told the neighbours her true thing: that she wanted to live in the Brine House. They laughed. She meant it. At fifteen the keeper took her on.","schooling":"The Brine House loft school under Master Peregrine Whey, who let her read under the roof-panes and later blamed himself. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She left at fifteen for the Brine House and learned grading from the old keeper, Mistress Agnes Rind, who could tell a cheese's age to the season by tapping it.","history":"She took the Brine House at twenty-four and worked it for twenty years without an eye-cloth, out of pride, until the white took most of her sight at forty-four. She now grades by touch, smell and the sound a cheese makes when you tap it, and she is better at it than she ever was by eye. She married Hennig Croft, a Prismport kiln-man, at twenty-six, and divorced him at thirty-eight after he sold three of her best wheels without telling her and denied it. The Witness Courts, who once argued half in jest that Third Farm cheese should count as evidence since it never changes its story, found themselves presented by Magda with a cheese, one of the three, bought back from the buyer, with her grading mark still in the rind. The Courts accepted it. The divorce went her way. The cheese is still in a Court cellar, entered as evidence. She thinks this is the funniest thing that has ever happened in Lightforge, and she may be right.","family":"Divorced; no children; four sisters, all at Third Farm, all a little in awe of her. Hennig still sends a pane of roof-glass every Cracking Day, as an apology, and she still uses them.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"She lit the First Hearth from her own kitchen fire and nobody ever knelt to her. Magda thinks the best things in the Nine have always been made in rooms like hers, by women nobody asked."},"band":{"act":"Permafrost","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Black metal whose fans stand perfectly still. She stands still in the Brine House listening to the cheeses, and when she heard Permafrost she said that was it, exactly.","rv_vote":"Permafrost, standing very still."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up, and would want to grade the Assize by smell. Against Arath.","edict":"Against. She's watched the Witness Courts accept a cheese as evidence and doesn't think they should be trusted with people."},"wants":"To taste the Eleven Days' Stonehold cheese, the oldest cheese in Lightforge, before it's thrown out.","site":"prismport_vf2","town":"Prismport Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:gleamford_vf1":{"name":"Brann Mill","age":30,"role":"the Twos' trader; sells blank Day-Books, ledgers, packs and bows; frame-master Mira Mill's son","look":"Pale, tidy and long-fingered, flax-fibre in his hair, paper-cuts on every knuckle; carries a Day-Book of his own making that's thicker than most.","voice":"Courteous and careful, says 'I think' a great deal, and means it.","greet":"Aure. Day-Books, ledgers, a good blank page. Packs and bows too, though I'm told I sell those like a paper-maker.","lines":["The Assize ordered twice the usual Day-Books this season. Twice! Either everyone's learned to write, or somebody means to start reading.","Gleamford's assayers weigh every ledger before they pay. They say it's fair. We say it costs us a sheet a ream. It's both, I think.","I'm engaged to an assayer who's serving on the Assize. I've seen her twice in three months. She writes very short letters. She signs them in full."],"about":"I sell what the Twos make: paper, ledgers, the blank Day-Books half of Lightforge starts its life with. My mother makes them, I sell them. I'm engaged to Seline Marrbrand at Gleamford. She's serving her lot. I'm waiting.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good; he binds Concord ledgers"}],"childhood":"Brann was born in the Drying Meadow in the middle of a pegging, his mother Mira insisting on finishing the line, and he has spent his life among white sheets so still in the noon that he thought they were clouds until he was four. He was a gentle, watchful, slightly solemn child who liked to sit under the lines and read the shadows of the sheets, which weren't there, because there's no shade at noon, which he worked out at six and found very upsetting. His mother made him his first Day-Book from flax she grew herself, as she'd done her own. At his First Telling the Twos gave him the new Day-Book with its first page written by his parents, as is the custom: one true thing about the day he was born. It read: 'He came during a pegging. We finished the line.' He has always found this both funny and correct.","schooling":"The Twos' frame-school in the pressing shed, under Master Osbert Flax, who taught letters by having the children write on the wet pulp with their fingers before it was pressed. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He spent two years as a clerk's runner in Gleamford and learned assaying from the outside, and that he didn't want to sit at a bench.","history":"He came back to the Twos at sixteen and took over the trading from his uncle at twenty-two. He sells Day-Books to every district clerk in Lightforge and ledgers to Gleamford's assayers, and he met Seline Marrbrand at nineteen when she weighed one of his ledgers in front of him, found it a sheet short, apologised for finding it, and bought it anyway. They have been engaged two years. This year her lot came up for the Assize and she went to Meridian. He writes every tenth day; she writes back short letters, careful and correct, signed in full. This season the Assize ordered twice the usual number of blank Day-Books, and his mother has been at the frames every bell. Last month a Gleamford woman he went to school with came to the Twos and asked him to carry a request to Seline: a copy of the signature page of her son's memory audit, which the law says she may have. He has carried it. He hasn't had an answer.","family":"Mother Mira, the frame-master; father Garth, who retts flax and sings; Seline, in Meridian.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"The first lantern was a cracked clay cup. He thinks the first Day-Book was probably a scrap of something nobody would have bought, and he likes that."},"band":{"act":"Paper Lantern Funeral","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Doom-gaze, slow and drowned in reverb. He heard it played at a Spiritveil buyer's camp and it sounded like paper drying.","rv_vote":"Paper Lantern Funeral, which Seline will find funny, he thinks."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and would bring blank paper for everyone. Torn on Arath; Seline voted for him, and Brann trusts her.","edict":"Torn. He makes the paper the registers will be kept in. He can't stop thinking about what's going to be written on it."},"wants":"For Seline to come home in the spring and for the boy's mother to have her answer before then.","site":"gleamford_vf1","town":"Gleamford Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:gleamford_vf1":{"name":"Wilmot Deckle","age":52,"role":"Twos frame-smith; makes the wire moulds, deckles and watermark wires for every paper frame","look":"Small, bald and bright-eyed, a magnifying glass strapped to his forehead, fingertips scarred from fine brass wire; wears a Twos sheet folded in his hat to keep the sun off.","voice":"Precise and gleeful about technical matters, flustered about everything else.","greet":"Seen! Careful of the frames, they look strong and they're not. A frame's a promise you can see through.","lines":["Every Twos sheet carries a watermark. Wire, sewn into the mould. I sew them. Fifty years ago an apprentice sewed a shut eye in as a joke. The Assize spent a year on it. I was two.","The Assize has sent me a drawing. A new watermark for the register paper. Tiny. You'd only see it if you held it up to the noon. I haven't sewn it yet.","Gleamford weighs our ledgers. I've offered to weigh the assayers. They didn't laugh."],"about":"I make the frames paper's made on: the wire mesh, the deckle edge, the watermark wire sewn into the mesh. Every Day-Book in Lightforge passes through one of my frames. I find that marvellous and a bit frightening.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"}],"childhood":"Wilmot was a frame-smith's boy, born the year after the famous shut-eye watermark, and he grew up hearing the story so often he thought he'd been there. His father sewed watermarks into paper moulds with brass wire finer than hair, and Wilmot was given a needle and a scrap of mesh at four and told to sew his initial. It took him a season. It was crooked. His father framed it anyway. He was a fussy, delighted, nervous child who loved small things: beetles, wire, the grain of flax, the threads in a sheet held up to the noon. At nine he found he could see the watermark in any sheet without holding it up, just by the way the light sat on it, and he spent a whole summer telling people what was in their paper.","schooling":"Master Osbert Flax's frame-school in the pressing shed, where he was quick at drawing and slow at reading because he kept looking at the paper instead of the words. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp flickered faintly. Half-Spark, registered and ignored. His father taught him wire-work from ten. Master Flax gave up on his spelling at eleven and let him draw the watermark designs for the frame-house instead; two of them are still in use.","history":"He took his father's bench at twenty-six and has made or mended every frame at the Twos for twenty-six years. Each frame carries the Twos' mark, a small flax-flower in wire, and each mark is his. He married Esther Pond, a Gleamford dyer, at thirty-four, late and happily; they have no children and a great many cats. He's been on good terms with the Assize all his life; they buy his paper and never question his frames. This season a Meridian clerk brought him a drawing: a new watermark, very small, to be sewn into every mould that makes paper for the Edict's spark registers. A mark only visible held up to the noon. A mark, the clerk said, that would let the Assize tell a true register sheet from a false one. Wilmot looked at the drawing for a long time. It is not a security mark. It is a numbered code, different for each frame, so that every sheet could be traced to the bench and the bell it was made on. He has not sewn it yet.","family":"Wife Esther; eleven cats. His father, retired, sews watermarks into handkerchiefs for the grandchildren he doesn't have.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"The Warden who wouldn't cut his citadel's chain. Wilmot has been asked to sew a mark he doesn't want to sew, and wants to be the kind of person who doesn't."},"band":{"act":"Retell","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A rapper who repeats every earlier bar in every later one. He says it's exactly how a watermark works: the same thing, in every sheet, forever.","rv_vote":"Retell, and he'll explain why at length to anyone."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and would be very nervous. Against Arath, since the drawing.","edict":"Against, now. He was neutral until he understood what the watermark was for."},"wants":"For the clerk never to come back for the frames.","site":"gleamford_vf1","town":"Gleamford Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:gleamford_vf1":{"name":"Esmé Crane","age":66,"role":"retired paper-maker; the apprentice who sewed the shut-eye watermark; told name Esmé Who Winked","look":"Small, round and merry, white hair in a loose bun stuck with pens, wire-scarred fingers, a twinkle she's never bothered to hide; always slightly ink-stained.","voice":"Light, teasing, delighted with herself; laughs at the end of every sentence, sometimes at nothing.","greet":"Aure! You've a face like a fresh sheet. Don't worry, it'll get some marks on it.","lines":["Fifty years ago I sewed a shut eye into a paper mould as a joke. The Assize spent a year deciding if a joke in the paper counted as hiding something. It did not. I got a told name out of it.","They've asked for twice the Day-Books this season. Someone means to start reading. If I were them I'd start with mine. It's very funny in places.","I hear Wilmot's been sent a drawing. Poor Wilmot. He's got the face of a man who's been asked to sew a lie."],"about":"I made paper at the Twos for forty years. I'm Esmé Who Winked: the shut eye in the watermark, the year-long Assize hearing, all of it. I'm sixty-six and I've never regretted a stitch.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"one sign, which she won't show you"}],"childhood":"Esmé was the cheeky daughter of two serious flax-retters, a girl who sang in the retting pond and was told to stop and kept going. She grew up among the white sheets of the Drying Meadow and the slap of wet pulp on frames, and she loved making things that other people would carry and never notice: the deckle edge, the grain, the faint watermark you only see held to the light. At ten she sewed her own initial into a scrap of mesh and her mother was so pleased she wrote it in the Day-Book in red. At her First Telling at twelve she told the Twos her true thing: that she found the noon very boring. The Twos were shocked. She didn't care.","schooling":"Master Osbert Flax's father, Master Ewald Flax, at the pressing shed, who taught her wire-work early because she was clever with her hands and impossible with books. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She went into the frame-house at thirteen as an apprentice. Master Ewald let her stay late to sew practice marks into scrap mesh, and caught her twice sewing small faces into them, and said nothing either time.","history":"At sixteen, apprenticed at the frame-house, she sewed a tiny shut eye into the corner of a paper mould. She did it on a dare, and also because she was in love with a Gleamford boy named Aurel Fyne who had winked at her on Short-Coin Day and she wanted to put it somewhere. The paper from that mould went out as Day-Books all over Lightforge before anyone noticed the mark. The Assize spent a year deciding whether a joke in the paper counted as hiding something, which in Lightforge is a serious question, and in the end ruled that a mark visible to anyone who held a page up to the noon was not hidden, merely unnoticed. Esmé was given the told name Esmé Who Winked. She married Aurel two years later. She made paper for forty years and was one of the best frame-workers the Twos ever had. Aurel died four years ago and she named him in the Gleamford Grief Room, and when she was asked to say a true thing about him, she held up a Day-Book from that first mould to the lamp. This season she has heard about Wilmot's drawing.","family":"Widow; two sons, one at the frames, one an Assize registry clerk in Meridian; four grandchildren who want to be told the shut-eye story every visit.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder","why":"She was tried in Lightforge for 'concealed anger.' Esmé was nearly tried for concealed humour, and thinks she and Torra would have liked each other, after they'd had a drink."},"band":{"act":"Thaw","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Bubbly pop that Frostgrave elders hate. Esmé is an elder and loves it, mainly because of who it annoys.","rv_vote":"Thaw, loudly, and she'll dance at the Twos when they lose."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and would make the Assize laugh at least once. Against Arath, cheerfully.","edict":"Against. 'If they want to hide marks in the paper now, they'll find I've done it before.'"},"wants":"To sew one more mark into a mould before her hands go.","site":"gleamford_vf1","town":"Gleamford Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:gleamford_vf1:b":{"name":"Mattias Rett","age":35,"role":"retting-pond keeper at the Twos; champion of the Twos in the stream quarrel; sings at weddings","look":"Broad and cheerful, sun-red, with a flax-fibre beard you'd swear was straw, wet to the knee always; carries a tin cup of stream water everywhere he goes.","voice":"Big, warm, theatrical; sings when he can't find the words, which is often.","greet":"Aure! Have a sip. It's from the stream below the ponds. Go on. It's clean. I drink it every day. Look at me. Fine.","lines":["The Thirds say retting flax fouls the stream that runs into their vines. It did. Did. I rebuilt the ponds. It's clean now. I drink it every day to prove it. Nobody believes me. Yet.","Gleamford's assayers weigh every ledger before they pay. A sheet in every ream. We've counted. I've written a song about it. It's a long song.","Weddings, I sing at. Twos weddings, Thirds weddings, even Gleamford ones. If we're at war over the stream, at least we sing at each other's weddings."],"about":"I keep the retting ponds, where the flax soaks until it can be beaten. I rebuilt them so they don't foul the stream. I'm also the man who sings 'Mist-Pear' at every wedding in the valley, so I've a reputation.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, loud"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"the words of three wedding songs, pronounced creatively"}],"childhood":"Mattias was a retter's son, born to the smell of soaking flax, which smells like a pond that's given up, and he loved it, which nobody could understand. He grew up in and around the retting ponds, wading, catching frogs, being told off for both. He had a huge voice from early on and sang at everything: weddings, Stump-Tellings in Brightmere when his mother took him, the Thirds' First Pour once at six, to general embarrassment. At eight he noticed that the stream below the ponds ran green and the Thirds' basins downstream sometimes smelled, and asked his father why. His father said that's how retting is. Mattias said that didn't seem fair to the Thirds. His father wrote: 'Mattias took the Thirds' side. He'll grow out of it.' He did not.","schooling":"Master Osbert Flax's frame-school, where he sang his times tables, and spent an extra year because he kept wading out of lessons into the ponds. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at thirteen for the ponds. Master Flax let him lead the morning songs in exchange for staying in his seat until noon, a bargain Mattias kept about half the time.","history":"He worked the ponds with his father until twenty-five, when his father retired, and then spent five years doing something no retter had done in three centuries: redesigning them. He dug settling basins between the ponds and the stream, planted reed beds to drink the green, and diverted the run-off through a gravel bed, borrowing the idea, he admits, from the Thirds' own Clearing Stair. The water that reaches the stream now is clean. He knows it's clean. He drinks a cup of it every day at the Twos' boundary stone, in full view of the Thirds, to prove it. The Thirds say it's a trick. The quarrel goes on, out of habit as much as anything. He also sings at every wedding in the valley, Twos, Thirds and Gleamford alike, and is beloved at all of them, which he says proves the quarrel is mostly theatre. He's unmarried, has never been very interested, and is 'named-uncle' to half the valley's children.","family":"Parents retired; two sisters at the frames; a great many named-nieces and nephews across the valley.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"She warned the Council of what was coming and they struck her from the record. He's been telling the Thirds their stream is clean for five years and thinks she'd understand being right and unbelieved."},"band":{"act":"Mist-Pear","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Wedding R&B. He sings their songs at every wedding in the valley, with words he's guessed at in Lanthic, and couples cry.","rv_vote":"Mist-Pear, and he'll sing it at the lamp-count."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and sing in the Assize corridors. For Arath: 'Somebody has to do the dull part properly.'","edict":"For. He's spark-poor and believes in checking things. He just wishes the Thirds would check his stream."},"wants":"For the Thirds to drink a cup of his stream water at the boundary stone, once, publicly.","site":"gleamford_vf1","town":"Gleamford Farms 2","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:gleamford_vf2":{"name":"Clemence Vint","age":41,"role":"the Thirds' trader; sells casks, packs, bows and travel goods; arranges the witness for every cask sale","look":"Sharp-featured and handsome, black hair streaked white at one side from a childhood fall, vine-leaf green apron; carries a witness-bell to ring when a Flameborne buyer comes.","voice":"Brisk, ironic, fond of a sharp line; drops into Kindle slang when she's angry, which is often.","greet":"Aure. Sunwine by the cask, packs, bows, a good cup. If you're Flameborne, I'll ring the bell and we'll wait for the witness. If you're not, we'll wait anyway.","lines":["They're asking for our sunwine at the Council table now. Not the Citadel's own, ours. Somebody up there wants a drink they can see clean through.","No Thirds cask goes to a Flameborne buyer without a witness standing by. Over one man's cinder-wine trick, twenty-three years ago. I've rung the bell four hundred times.","The Twos say their stream's clean now. Their retter drinks a cup every day at the stone. Show-off. It probably is clean. Don't tell him."],"about":"I sell what the Thirds make, which is sunwine, and what travellers need, which is everything else. I ring a bell for Flameborne buyers and wait for a witness, which is the rule. I think it's a stupid rule. I keep it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good, especially the rude parts"}],"childhood":"Clemence was raised by her grandmother on the top terrace of the Clearing Stair, among the basins and the chained tin cup and the endless raking, and fell off the eleventh basin at seven, which gave her the white streak in her hair and a reputation for never looking where she was going. She was quick, sardonic and restless, and she loved the Flameborne buyers who came every season for casks: loud, warm, quarrelsome people who argued with her grandmother for an hour and then hugged her. When she was eighteen one of those buyers coloured a Thirds vintage and sold it on as cinder-wine, and three of the Thirds rode to Hearthmount to complain, and after that every Flameborne buyer came with a witness. Clemence missed the hugs.","schooling":"The Thirds' school in the cask-shed under Mistress Prue Basin, who taught by the Stair: first basin letters, fortieth basin philosophy, and once made the class follow a single drop of wine from the top to the jar to understand patience. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She left at fourteen for the cask trade.","history":"She hauled casks from fourteen, ran the Thirds' cask-shed at twenty-five, and took over the trading post at thirty. She sells casks to the Citadel and to Stormreach traders who drink it standing on their wagons, sells packs and bows and travel goods to the eastern road, and rings a bell for every Flameborne buyer so a witness can come from the vineyard and stand by the sale. She has rung it four hundred times. She married a Stormreach cask-hauler, Teague, at twenty-eight; he rides the eastern road and comes home every fortnight shouting. Over the years she has quietly made friends with half the Flameborne cask-buyers on the Coal Road. She thinks the witness rule is an old grudge dressed as law. She keeps it to the letter, and has found a way round it entirely within the letter.","family":"Husband Teague, two children; her grandmother, ninety-four, is Old Ysmay, who does the First Pour.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"Loud, funny and profane, Flameborne's voice at the Council. Clemence wishes Lightforge had someone at the table who'd swear at it."},"band":{"act":"Sabra Hearth","realm":"Flameborne","why":"The pop star who refused to kneel to the King at a festival. A Flameborne buyer gave her a song-sheet with Sabra's face on it, and it's pinned up behind the counter.","rv_vote":"Sabra Hearth, in red ink, with the bell rung for good measure."},"vote":{"local":"She'd stand if her lot came up and would put the witness rule on the agenda first. Against Arath.","edict":"Against. 'The Thirds spent twenty-three years making every Flameborne buyer stand next to a witness. I've seen how that goes. It doesn't end.'"},"wants":"To sell a cask to a Flameborne buyer with a handshake and no bell, once, in the open.","site":"gleamford_vf2","town":"Gleamford Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"smith:gleamford_vf2":{"name":"Peregrine Gravel","age":60,"role":"Thirds smith; basin rakes, cask hoops, the chain for the tin cup; one of the three who rode to Hearthmount","look":"Rangy and grizzled, a short white beard, a hacking cough he's had for twenty-three years; wears a Flameborne ember-bead on his wrist he says he won at cards.","voice":"Gravelly and easy, tells a good story badly and a bad story well; coughs at the best bits.","greet":"Seen. Rake, hoop or chain? I made the chain on that tin cup. Twenty-three years. Never broke. Unlike me.","lines":["Twenty-three years ago three of us rode all the way to Hearthmount to complain to a man's face about his cinder-wine trick. We came back with an apology and a cough. I've still got the cough.","My cousin Tavin is the official first taster. Caught drinking from the Stair at twelve. I was there. I was fourteen. I drank too. Nobody caught me.","Hearthmount is the loudest place in the Nine. Everyone argues. Everyone hugs after. I've never been so happy and so scared at the same time."],"about":"I make the iron for the Stair: rakes, hoops, the chain for the cup. I've been to Hearthmount, which almost no one at the Thirds has, and I came back with a cough and a story. I tell the story. I can't stop the cough.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a fair amount, mostly from one long night"}],"childhood":"Peregrine was born to the Gravels, the family that has raked the Clearing Stair since the first vines took in the gravel, and he grew up on the terraces with a child-sized rake. His cousin Tavin was two years younger and a born taster; Peregrine was a born mender, the boy who fixed the broken basin edges with a trowel at nine because nobody else was going to. At fourteen he and Tavin crept to the bottom of the Stair before First Pour and drank from the jar, and Tavin was caught and given his told name and the job of first taster, and Peregrine, who'd ducked behind the fortieth basin, was not. He has never decided whether he got away with it or was left out.","schooling":"The cask-shed school under Mistress Prue Basin's mother, Mistress Ermilde Basin, who taught arithmetic with basin levels and made the children predict how long a drop would take to reach the jar. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He apprenticed to the Thirds' old smith at twelve. Old Mistress Basin let him mend the cask-shed's broken bench as his final lesson, and it is still the bench the children sit on.","history":"He took the forge at twenty-six. Twenty-three years ago, when a Flameborne buyer coloured a Thirds vintage and sold it as cinder-wine, Peregrine, Tavin and their old aunt Marit rode the length of the Coal Road to Hearthmount to complain to the man's face. It took three weeks. Hearthmount was terraced round a caldera lit from below by the Cinderway, full of people shouting, and the buyer, when found, shouted back for an hour, then broke down, then fed them slapbread and pit lamb and apologised so loudly the whole street heard. They came home with the apology in writing and a cough from the ash-falls that Peregrine has never shaken. He has told the Hearthmount story at every First Pour since. He doesn't tell the part where, on the last night, a Flameborne cask-wright named Marra Ember took him to the Bellows, the wind-caves lovers go to. He was thirty-seven and married. He wrote it in his Day-Book. His wife read it. She stayed. It took them two years.","family":"Wife Bettrys; two grown children; his cousin Tavin, the stair-keeper, his oldest friend.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"He's seen the First Hearth, lit from her kitchen fire, with a crowd standing round it and nobody kneeling. He thinks it's the best thing he's seen in his life, and he's seen the Clearing Stair at First Pour."},"band":{"act":"The Bellows","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stadium rock where the crowd pumps a forge. He heard them in Hearthmount, the night of the wind-caves, and has never told anyone that's why.","rv_vote":"The Bellows. Bettrys knows why. She's let it go."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and would cough through every sitting. Torn on Arath.","edict":"Torn. He believes in honesty. He also remembers a Flameborne street that shouted the truth at him for an hour and then fed him, and thinks that's better than a register."},"wants":"To go back to Hearthmount once before his lungs give out, with Bettrys, and show her the First Hearth.","site":"gleamford_vf2","town":"Gleamford Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:gleamford_vf2":{"name":"Ysmay Thirdling","age":94,"role":"the oldest person in the Thirds; holds up the first jar at every First Pour","look":"Tiny and translucent, white hair like spun sugar, eyes pale but sharp; carried to the Stair on a chair and holds the jar with both hands, very steady.","voice":"Clear and thin, very old Gleamford speech, says less every year and means more of it.","greet":"Aure, child. Come where I can see you. Closer. No, I see you. I see everything. That's my job.","lines":["First Pour: I hold the first jar up to the noon in front of everyone. If I see anything in it at all, the whole vintage goes back to the top of the Stair. I've sent it back four times in thirty years.","They're asking for our sunwine at the Council table now. I've always known it was good. It took them nine hundred years.","Sunwine tastes of nothing. That's harder to make than anything that tastes of something. My father said that. I've said it since. Someone else can say it next."],"about":"I'm Ysmay. I'm ninety-four. I'm the oldest person in the Thirds, so I hold up the jar. I've done it thirty years. I've sent four vintages back. This year I sent it back again, and nobody else could see why.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, very old Gleamford speech"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"once good, now forgotten mostly"}],"childhood":"Ysmay was born in the Thirds ninety-four years ago, in a house at the foot of the Clearing Stair, and she has lived in that house her whole life. She remembers the vineyard when it was smaller, before the eastern terraces, and her grandfather raking the top basin in a straw hat, and the first time she saw First Pour, at five, held up by an old woman with hands like twigs who looked into the jar for a long time and said, 'Clear.' Ysmay thought it was the most important word anyone had ever said. She was a quiet, steady, observant child who could watch a drop of wine travel from basin to basin for a whole bell and not get bored. Other children thought her strange. She was.","schooling":"The cask-shed school under a teacher so long dead that Ysmay is the only person living who remembers her name, Mistress Alys Rake, who made the children hold a cup of water to the noon and describe what they saw until they could see nothing, which took most of them a year. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. She left at twelve to rake.","history":"She raked the Stair for fifty years, married Owain Thirdling at twenty, and had nine children, of whom five are living; her eldest is seventy-three. Owain died thirty-one years ago. The year after, she became the oldest person in the Thirds and was handed the first jar of the vintage at First Pour, and has held it up every year since, before the whole vineyard, looking for anything at all. She has sent the vintage back four times: twice for sediment, once for a hair, once for what she called 'a cloud' that no one else saw, and which a week later the Stair-keeper found in the twentieth basin. This year she held up the jar and saw, at its very edge where the glass meets the noon, a faint grey line, like a frayed edge of nothing, there and then not. She sent the vintage back. Nobody else saw anything. They sent it back anyway, because that is the rule, and because it's Ysmay.","family":"Five living children, twenty-two grandchildren, eleven great-grandchildren, one great-great; granddaughter Clemence Vint runs the trading post and looks after her and argues with her daily.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"The first lantern was a cracked clay cup. Ysmay has held the same glass jar up to the noon for thirty years and thinks the vessel never mattered, only the looking."},"band":{"act":"Mirel of the Last Lamp","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Symphonic rock; every show burns a memory from the crowd. A great-grandson took her to Korrhaven once and she lost a memory there, and doesn't know which one, and found that rather wonderful.","rv_vote":"Mirel of the Last Lamp, because she has plenty of memories to spare."},"vote":{"local":"She asked to be struck from the lot at ninety. Neither for nor against Arath: 'I've seen a lot of High Councillors. They all looked tired.'","edict":"She doesn't think it will last long enough to matter to her, and says so."},"wants":"To hold up one more vintage and say 'clear'.","site":"gleamford_vf2","town":"Gleamford Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"vill:gleamford_vf2:b":{"name":"Elric Rake","age":19,"role":"basin-raker on the Clearing Stair; next in line as first taster; famous for last Unshadowing's confession","look":"Tall, thin and freckled, sunburnt neck, a basin rake over one shoulder; blushes very easily and very visibly.","voice":"Shy and blunt at once: says the dreadful thing in a mumble and then can't take it back.","greet":"Aure. Sorry. Hello. You've probably heard about me. Everyone has.","lines":["At the last Unshadowing I said my one hidden thing aloud in front of the whole vineyard. I said I don't like sunwine. It was very quiet after.","Tavin's going to make me first taster when he steps down. I'm next in line. I'm the only one who can tell the forty basins apart by smell. Which is funny. You'll see why.","They say the Twos' stream's clean now. I could tell you. I've smelled it. I'm not allowed to say."],"about":"I'm Elric. I rake the Stair. I'm next in line to be first taster. I said I don't like sunwine at the Unshadowing, which is true, and the Thirds have been very kind about it, mostly.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native, mumbled"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Elric was the third son of a raking family, and was raised on the Stair, which for a Thirds child means raking basins from six, being told to be patient by everyone, and being fed sunwine-steamed crayfish at every feast. He was a gentle, anxious, sniffly child with an astonishing nose: at seven he could tell which basin a cup of wine had come from with his eyes shut. The vineyard thought it was a gift. At thirteen he had a bad bout of Rim fever caught off a Rushbrook carter, and spent a month in bed, and when he got up the world had gone flat. Salt fish, crayfish, honey, sunwine: all the same pale nothing. His nose still worked. His tongue didn't.","schooling":"The cask-shed school under Mistress Prue Basin, who called him her 'best nose' and once set him a test against the Stair-keeper that he won. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He left at fourteen to rake full time. He learned to read and write well enough, and to keep a basin log, which he does in a neat, careful hand, and in which he has never written the word 'taste'.","history":"He has raked the Stair for five years and is the finest raker the Gravels have trained in a generation, which is why Tavin Gravel, the stair-keeper and official first taster, has named him next in line. Last Unshadowing, when every Lightforge household lights every lamp and each person says one hidden thing aloud, Elric stood in front of the whole vineyard and said, 'I don't like sunwine.' There was a silence that went on for some time. Then old Ysmay laughed, and then everyone did, and Tavin clapped him on the shoulder. The Thirds have treated it since as a charming joke. It wasn't a joke; it was true, as far as it went. He didn't say that since the fever he can't taste anything at all, and that he means to be first taster anyway, by nose alone, and nobody will ever know.","family":"Parents and two older brothers on the Stair; a sweetheart in the Twos, Bree Retter, who knows about the fever and nothing else.","hero":{"who":"His mother, Nesta Rake","why":"She has raked the top basin every day for thirty years, the one nobody sees the result of, and says that's the important one."},"band":{"act":"Coldread","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Deadpan, numb rap. He found it after the fever, when everything tasted of nothing, and it was the first thing that sounded right.","rv_vote":"Coldread. He's told no one why."},"vote":{"local":"He'd stand if his lot came up and would say almost nothing. Torn on Arath.","edict":"Torn. He's spark-poor, and has his own thing he hasn't said aloud, and understands both sides more than he'd like."},"wants":"To be first taster and never be found out.","site":"gleamford_vf2","town":"Gleamford Farms 3","realm":"Lightforge"},"shop:irontown":{"name":"Garnet Spindle","age":52,"role":"trader and salvage-buyer at the foot of Refusal Row; former long-wagon brake-girl on the Hearthward run","look":"Short and broad, a grey plait pinned up with a bent rivet. Two front teeth of polished steel that she shows on purpose, often.","voice":"Fast and cheerful, prices first and opinions after; calls everyone 'shift' whatever the hour.","greet":"Shift. You've a traveller's walk, so you'll want a pack before you want manners. I've both. The pack's cheaper.","lines":["Ninth chimney's still in scaffold. Old Ermengarde Clinker hasn't named it yet, and half the crest is leaving pies on her step.","Lightforge buys our iron through Hearthward and won't say whose it is. Fine. Their money isn't shy.","These teeth? A bridgewarden's mule on the Coal Road. I kept the old ones in a tin. Couldn't tell you why."],"about":"I buy what's broken and sell what isn't, and I've done it at the foot of Refusal Row for twenty-two years. Before that I rode the long-wagons down to Hearthward and came back with other people's coin. Born-whole bar the teeth. It's loud enough here that nobody hears you think, and I like that in a city.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good (wagon drivers)"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a few words, all of them prices"}],"childhood":"Garnet grew up in the Spindle tenement on the fourth terrace, where her mother wound bobbin-wire for the cable shops and her father sang badly on third shift so the whole stairwell knew when he was home. She was the child who sold things. At seven it was slag marbles to the Smeltway swimmers. At nine it was chimney names scratched on tin, which she swore were official copies. She kept her accounts in chalk on the stairwell wall, columns of who owed her a bun, and every spring her mother whitewashed the lot and Garnet started again from the top step. She says that wall taught her more about debt than anything with a teacher in it, because she learned that every debt goes white eventually and you'd best collect before the brush comes.","schooling":"Shift-school at the Bobbin Street hall under Master Ulla Corve, who taught sums by making the class weigh their own lunches and argue the difference. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered, though the Gravem lamp coughed out a puff of dust, which the tester said was the room. First Tool at ten, a brass tally-hook stamped SPINDLE G. She left at thirteen, quick at figures and hopeless at sitting.","history":"At fifteen she went onto the long-wagons as a brake-girl, riding the back axle down the Ember March to Hearthward with the iron, and by twenty she was keeping the wagon-master's book because she was the only one who could. On that road she learned which Concord buyers wanted Metalcrown iron and which wanted to pretend they didn't, and she learned to sell to both. At twenty-four she married Dace, a wheelwright on the Stonehold side of Hearthward, who would not move east any more than she would move west; they lived apart three seasons of every four for nine years and then stopped pretending, without a raised voice between them. At twenty-eight a mule took her front teeth on the Coal Road and a Rivet Hollow fitter set the steel ones, the first line struck off her tally plate. At thirty she bought the stall at the foot of Refusal Row with her wagon savings. She has watched four chimneys go up from that counter and painted each refusal's date on her shutters, and the newest coat of paint is still tacky.","family":"Dace still sends a letter every First Coal, which she answers within the week. Her son Lorne, thirty, works the Reactor Halls and had his left arm fitted last winter; she has not yet decided how she feels about it, and keeps asking him how it grips a cup.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"Orra's first hammer was ugly and it worked. Garnet keeps a drawing of it over the till, where other traders keep a mirror."},"band":{"act":"Oil & Ivory","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Dace courted her to the crooner's songs in a Hearthward inn; she claims she only likes the voice.","rv_vote":"Oil & Ivory, because you lamp your own, and because Lightforge never will."},"vote":{"local":"A Dominion woman for the trade: the Dominus brought the Hearthward tolls down, which the Commons never managed. She has signed all nine petitions and kept a copy of each.","edict":"Smiling. Every spark Lightforge registers needs a bracket, a lock and a ledger-box, and she knows who casts brackets."},"wants":"To sell Lightforge the iron for its own spark registers, and this once to sell it under its real mark.","site":"irontown","town":"Iron Throne City","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:irontown":{"name":"Brannick Sledge","age":34,"role":"crest smith below Refusal Row; casts the iron foot-plates for the refusal chimneys","look":"Big through the chest, right arm fitted to the shoulder in black steel with a Rivet Hollow knuckle, the left one born and burn-mottled. Tally plate on the left wrist.","voice":"Even and practical; now and then drops the word 'I' and builds the sentence around the gap.","greet":"Shift. Forge is hot, the queue is short. What needs making?","lines":["The ninth chimney's foot-plate is cast and the face is blank. Waiting on Ermengarde. She says she'll know the name when she hears it.","The arm was a choice. Rivet Hollow makes a good joint. Better than the old shoulder made, frankly.","Used to say 'I made it' after every blade. Now the stamp says it. Saves breath."],"about":"Smith on the crest, under Refusal Row. The arm was fitted at twenty-two, the rest is born. The Dominus says inefficiency kills children, and the old way of hammering was killing smiths, so it got corrected. That's all there is to it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"workable"}],"childhood":"Brannick was born the night the crest lit the chimney now called Second Helping, which his mother said was why he was always hungry; it was a joke the family wore smooth. His father Odo ran the crest forge with a right shoulder ground down by forty years of the same swing, and by the time Brannick was eight Odo could no longer lift him onto the anvil block to watch. So Brannick climbed up himself. He carried quench water from the Smeltway standpipe before school, ate two breakfasts, and spent his evenings learning to strike on the off-beat of his father's hammer so Odo's arm could rest a fraction between blows. Nobody told him to. He noticed the arm and did the arithmetic.","schooling":"Shift-school at Crestside under Teacher Absalom Nail, who kept a drawer of First Tools belonging to children who had died before they reached ten, and once a year let each pupil hold one and read the name. Brannick was good at the bending exercises Nail set and bad at letters. Lamp Test: nothing. First Tool at ten, a straight-peen hammer head too heavy for him; he grew into it by fourteen.","history":"He apprenticed to his father at twelve and by nineteen he was running the forge outright, because that was the year Odo's shoulder simply stopped. At twenty-one his own right shoulder started clicking on the upstroke. That spring he stood in the crowd at a Proving and heard the Ninth Dominus's line about inefficiency and children, and he took it as a sentence about his father. At twenty-two he walked to Rivet Hollow and had the arm fitted, the old joint. Seven years ago, after the Proving accident, he went back and had it refitted with the Hollow's new knuckle, and he made the trip in one day each way so the forge wouldn't go cold. He works double now and doesn't tire in the arm. He courted a Reactor Halls clerk named Imke for two years; she ended it, saying he'd stopped saying 'I' and hadn't noticed. He told her that was an exaggeration, and heard himself say it without the word. Since last spring he has had the commission to cast the foot-plates for Refusal Row, and he poured the ninth one alone, at night, with nobody watching.","family":"Odo, seventy, sits in a chair at the forge door, born-whole and bitter about it, and argues with his son about the arm every day in a way both of them enjoy. His mother Bettany died when he was twenty; he says her name at the Long Whistle and at nothing else.","hero":{"who":"The Ninth Dominus","why":"Gave up a name and the word 'I', and the crest got cheaper bread. Brannick thinks that's a fair trade and says so more often than anyone asks."},"band":{"act":"Unit Nine","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Rumour says they're fitted, and he likes that nobody on that stage looks ashamed of it.","rv_vote":"Unit Nine, and he'll tell you the vote isn't sentiment, it's quality control."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion man, all the way. The Commons is a story old men tell; the Synthesis Council paid for half his arm.","edict":"Smiling. Let Lightforge count its sparks. Metalcrown sparks don't need registering; they come with serial numbers."},"wants":"To cast the foot-plate for a chimney the city never has to raise.","site":"irontown","town":"Iron Throne City","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:irontown":{"name":"Ermengarde Clinker","age":81,"role":"retired crane-chain greaser; the oldest born-whole worker on Shift One, and so the one who must name the ninth chimney","look":"Small and bandy-legged, white hair cropped close against grease. Bare wrists, no fittings anywhere, and a tally plate rubbed so smooth the one word on it barely shows: ALL.","voice":"Slow and dry. Stops halfway through a sentence to make you wait, and enjoys it.","greet":"You're not here about the chimney. Good. Everyone else is. Sit.","lines":["They leave pies on my step. Shift Two pie, Shift Three pie. Bribes, every tin. I eat the lot and promise nothing.","My gran heard Tibb Anvil speak once when she was a girl. Said he had a voice like a kettle. That's the Commons. Nobody pretty. Everybody heard.","Eighty-one and born-whole. Plate says ALL. Some mornings it's lying."],"about":"Greased crane chains on the crest for fifty-three years. Never had a part fitted, never wanted one, never been offered one I'd trust. Now I'm the oldest whole body on Shift One, so I name a chimney. I've named a goat and married two husbands. How hard can it be.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"understands the old words, from her gran"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"The Clinkers lived in two rooms under the crest chain-house, where the great hoist chains came down on their rollers to be greased, and Ermengarde learned to walk holding a link as tall as she was. The house ran on her gran Thessaly, who had heard Foreman Tibb Anvil speak once as a small girl and never stopped telling it. Thessaly made the family vote at supper, one mouth one vote, on what went in the pot. Ermengarde had four brothers and lost every vote; turnip, again. When she cried about it Thessaly said, 'That's the Commons, lump it,' and handed her the ladle anyway. Ermengarde says that was the most useful thing anyone ever did for her, and that she still hates turnip.","schooling":"Shift-school at the Chainhouse under Teacher Mabry Coil, who cast the letters in lead and made the children read them by touch with the lamps out, in case the lamps ever went. Ermengarde reads slowly to this day and perfectly in the dark. Lamp Test at eight: nothing, and she told the tester his ring was dusty. First Tool at ten, a grease paddle stamped CLINKER H. She left at twelve to work the chains.","history":"She greased her first chain at twelve and her last at sixty-five, and in between she knew every link on the crest by the noise it made dry. At twenty she married Fenwick Bray, a crane-driver who called her 'boss' for thirty-two years and meant it; he died of the cough when she was fifty-two and she took his name off nothing. At sixty she married again, Japhet Morrow, a widowed clockmaker who ran the crest's clockwork theatre and its Iron Man Who Wanted a Heart; they married because he made her laugh at a funeral, and he made her laugh until he died at seventy-four. She has refused fitting three times: when her knee went and she started walking bandy, when her eyes clouded and she started squinting, and when the Synthesis Council offered every pensioner 'a courtesy part'. She sent the courtesy part back in its box with a note. When word came that she was the oldest born-whole body on Shift One, she sat down on her step and laughed, and the pies started arriving the next morning.","family":"Two sons, Rolf and Abner, both fitted to the elbow and working the Reactor Halls, who think she should name the chimney after their father. A daughter-in-law who brings her slag-tea. Seven grandchildren, each with a candidate name written on her wall.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"Because of her gran's stories. When a grandchild tries to get out of a family vote, Ermengarde says 'Even you,' and they know where it comes from."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Japhet took her to hear them at Hearthward. Forty old soldiers who never sing above a murmur, the only band quiet enough she can hear herself hum underneath.","rv_vote":"The Lonely Line Choir. Someone has to give old soldiers a lamp, and it won't be the young."},"vote":{"local":"A Commons woman. She'd bring back one shift, one vote tomorrow, though she'll admit the Dominus fixed the crest water and she drinks it.","edict":"Against, mostly. Counting people's sparks, and next they'll count your parts — and then she looks at her own wrist and laughs."},"wants":"To give the chimney a name that a Lightforge envoy will one day have to read out loud in Council.","site":"irontown","town":"Iron Throne City","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:irontown:b":{"name":"Wystan Tinplate","age":13,"role":"message-runner for the Reactor Halls and the crest's best scaffold climber","look":"Small and all elbows, soot to the ears. His First Tool, a tin-snip, hangs on a string round his neck instead of on a belt.","voice":"Quick, a mimic, does voices, finishes your sentences for you and gets them wrong.","greet":"Shift! Want to hear the Ninth Refusal? I do the Lightforge voice. Costs nothing. Costs a bun.","lines":["From the top of the new chimney's scaffold you can see the Smeltway all the way to the Warm. Don't tell Mum I said top.","The ninth refusal on the Hall doors still shines. Someone polishes it at night. Not me. Probably not me.","Chief Engineer Solvane said thank you to me once. To me. I was carrying his ink."],"about":"I run messages for the Reactor Halls, third shift mostly, and nobody on the crest climbs better. My First Tool's a tin-snip because Mum's a tinsmith, and I wear it round my neck so I don't lose it again. When I'm grown I'm going to read the petition at Korrhaven like Solvane did, and they won't laugh.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"school-good, and he can do a Lightforge accent in it"}],"childhood":"Wystan was born in a tinsmith's flat on Kettle Stair, the fourth of five, and he was the one who kept getting lost. At four he went missing for a whole shift and was found asleep in the Hall of Named Engines, curled against a retired pump called Auntie Grue, and Engine-keeper Marrow Fenn walked him home and told his mother he'd only been visiting family. At six he taught himself to climb the drain-chains on the tenement wall. At eight he went up a chimney scaffold for the first time behind the older Kettle Stair children and came down long after them, alone, because he had stopped near the top to count the lights along the Smeltway and lost count at four hundred and wanted to start again.","schooling":"Shift-school at the Kettle Stair hall under Teacher Perpetua Sallow, who reads the Concord's refusals aloud each year as a spelling lesson and gives a prize to whoever finds the most words in them that mean no. Wystan has won three years running. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered, and he asked if he could have another go with his eyes shut. First Tool at ten, the tin-snip. Still at school mornings.","history":"At eleven Wystan got the runner's place by being the only child who could find his way up the Reactor Halls' back stairs without a lamp, and since then he has carried ink, chits, tea, and once a sealed brass tube he was told not to open. He was on the Hall steps the day the ninth refusal came back from Korrhaven. He tells it the same way every time: Solvane walked in from the wagon, nailed the paper to the doors himself with four nails, and went to his desk without a word to anyone, and Wystan has been practising reading aloud ever since because of the four nails. Nights he climbs the ninth chimney's scaffold with the Kettle Stair crowd and chalks the height they reached on a girder. This season he learned the whole of the ninth refusal by heart in a Lightforge accent he copied off a Rim pedlar, and he does it at the slag-oven pie stalls for buns; Shift Two pays best. His mother has threatened to have him fitted with a quieter mouth.","family":"His mother Clemency, tinsmith, born-whole but for a fitted left ear. His father Roswell is a warder at the Kiln and never talks about work at the table. Three sisters and a baby brother who copies everything Wystan does, including the accent.","hero":{"who":"Solvane, Chief Engineer","why":"The Council laughed at the petition, not at Solvane, and Wystan has decided that is a different thing and worth getting right."},"band":{"act":"Slag Hill","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Industrial drill against spark-born privilege; he knows every bar and his mother leaves the room when he starts.","rv_vote":"Slag Hill, because they'd make the Lightforge judges cover their ears."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion because the Halls pay runners on the nail, and Commons because Ermengarde Clinker told him about it. He hasn't noticed those two don't fit together.","edict":"Smiling, because the whole crest smiles at it. He does the Edict in the Lightforge voice too, and that one gets two buns."},"wants":"To read the tenth petition at Korrhaven himself, so well that nobody laughs.","site":"irontown","town":"Iron Throne City","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:slagmoor":{"name":"Gertrud Dross","age":39,"role":"keeper of the pit-store and of Slagmoor's second scale; former hewer with a fitted bellows-lung","look":"Tall, stooped from eleven years of low roofs, with a ribbed brass panel showing at her left collarbone that hisses faintly when she laughs.","voice":"Short breaths, short sentences, and a laugh that comes out as a whistle.","greet":"Shift. Pasties are hot, packs are dry, prices are fair. Unlike some scales I could name.","lines":["I weigh every wagon before it rolls down to Pitmouth. My book against their beam. Twelve years of it.","Old Grandmother carries a hundred and six plates now. I polish the ones nobody else comes for.","The lung? Rivet Hollow bellows-work. Breathes better than the one I was born with. Bit loud at night, my husband says."],"about":"Mined eleven years till the dust settled in. Now I run the store and keep the second scale, so Pitmouth can never say we're guessing. I've a brass lung and a long memory, and both of them work.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"fluent (the family still counts in it)"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"passable"}],"childhood":"The Drosses dig the Stonehold way and count the Stonehold way, out loud, in Holdspeech, so Gertrud learned her numbers before her name. Her father set props in the main shaft and made her count each one as it went down the cage, and she went on counting everything afterwards: stair-treads, pasty crimps, the strokes of the Last Up triangle, the coins in other people's hands. At eight she watched the old store-keeper, Sefton Wale, hand a pit widow short change in shift-scrip, and she walked to the triangle at Last Up and announced it to the whole village before anyone could stop her. It cost the family a winter of cold looks from the Wales. It also meant that nobody in Slagmoor ever again counted change in front of Gertrud without counting it twice.","schooling":"The pithead school in the old lamp-room, under Teacher Hulda Maul, who would not let a child open a primer until they could strip a miner's lamp and rebuild it blindfold. Gertrud did it on the first try and was insufferable for a month. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered. First Tool at ten, a tally-awl for scratching counts on tin, which she still uses at the counter.","history":"She went down at fourteen as a sorter, then hewed coal-slag and spark-ore on the second level for eleven years, and she was a good, careful, unexciting miner. She wasn't on the drowned shift and lost nobody in it closer than a neighbour; what she did was help hang the plates on Old Grandmother afterwards, and count them, because someone had to. At twenty-five the dust took most of her left lung. A Rivet Hollow fitter built her the bellows-panel, the first line struck off her tally plate, and she came up for good. At twenty-seven she took over the store when Sefton Wale retired, which he hated. Her first act was to build a weighbridge out of a scrapped Pitmouth beam, and she has weighed every ore wagon that left Slagmoor since, in a book now four volumes thick. The book shows Pitmouth's beam reading light, about one load in twenty. It also shows something else, which she has told no one.","family":"Married to Halloran Tusk, who works the wheel at Old Grandmother with Wenna Pike and took the Dross name because it was the better-known one. Two children, Cass, twelve, and Toller, nine, who are both counting already.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright and Idris Hollowfire","why":"Somebody had to work out how spark-ore takes a charge, or her pit would just be a hole in a hill."},"band":{"act":"Rivet Gun","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"You can dance to it with half a lung; she's checked.","rv_vote":"Rivet Gun, and she'll tell you the exact count of lamps they got last year."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion, from plain arithmetic: the Synthesis sent the fitters, and the Commons, from all she's heard, sent speeches.","edict":"A mild smile. It doesn't weigh anything, so it isn't her business."},"wants":"For Slagmoor and Pitmouth to weigh on one beam set in the middle of the hill road, with both books open on it.","site":"slagmoor","town":"Slagmoor","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:slagmoor":{"name":"Torvald Pickhaft","age":61,"role":"pick-smith to the pits; forged the iron cap over the flooded shaft","look":"Thick and grey-bearded, born-whole and stubborn about it. His left shoulder sits lower than his right from a lifetime of lifting the same stone.","voice":"Holdspeech underneath everything, stress dropped on the last syllable, few words and none wasted.","greet":"Set. You'll want a pick, or a prop-iron, or you're lost. I can help with two of those.","lines":["Old Grandmother turned slow this morning. Wenna heard it before I did. Nobody goes down till she's listened.","Pitmouth's scales, Slagmoor's water. My grandfather had the same argument. Different beards.","They want me fitted. My arms do the work. Why swap a good worker for a new one?"],"about":"Pick-smith to the pit, forty years. My people walked east out of Stonehold with no markers and a great many opinions. I kept the opinions. A pick is an ugly thing. It works.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"fluent; the tongue of his house"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting, and he doesn't mind"}],"childhood":"The Pickhafts kept the old ways closer than anyone else in Slagmoor. Every soul in the house lifted a burden-stone morning and night, the bread was double-baked and chipped, not sliced, and on Marker Day they took a picnic up to the slag-hills where the Stonehold road runs out. When Torvald was seven his grandfather Aldo walked him to the last marker on the Eastern March, where the carving stops halfway through a dead man's initial, and had him put his palm flat on the blank stone beyond it and say nothing at all for as long as it took the old man to finish his pipe. Torvald's own childhood stone was a lump of black slag the size of a loaf. He still has it. He still lifts it.","schooling":"Barely any. He went to the pithead school for a single season before Aldo pulled him out, because the teacher then called Holdspeech 'a dead man's mouth'. After that his grandfather taught him letters from charcoal rubbings of mile-markers, so Torvald learned to read on the initials of strangers. Lamp Test at eight, from a city cart that came up the hill once a year: nothing. His First Tool came from Aldo, not the school, a pick-haft gauge.","history":"He apprenticed to Aldo at the pit forge and took it over at twenty-four, and he has made every pick, prop-iron and cage-hook in Slagmoor since, ugly and sound. He married Sarra, a sorter with a loud laugh, and they had two daughters. Sarra died at forty-nine from a fall on the pithead steps on a dry, ordinary afternoon, which he still finds harder to forgive than if it had been the pit. Nineteen years ago, when the third shift drowned and the village voted to cap the shaft, Torvald forged the cap: a round plug of iron the width of a wagon, riveted in four rings, and he would not let another man swing on it. It took him eleven days. Both daughters live in the city now, both fitted, both sending money he puts in a tin and doesn't open. He has refused fitting three times; his knuckles look like walnuts. Nights, lately, he has been walking up to the slag-hills with a chisel.","family":"Daughters Tamsa and Hilde in Iron Throne City, five grandchildren he sees at the Proving. He and his sister Gunna haven't spoken since she had both legs fitted and called his stone 'a lump of pride'.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"'Lift. Then talk.' Torvald thinks that's the whole of good manners, and has never met a Synthesis man who does it."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Heard them once at a Hearthward fair; the drummer's hands are going to stone and he plays slower each year, and Torvald hammers at that tempo.","rv_vote":"Burden, for as long as the drummer can still hold the sticks."},"vote":{"local":"Commons by blood and Unmarked by grudge. He signs the petitions, but says a Concord seat matters less to him than Stonehold saying sorry.","edict":"Against. Stonehold refuses it, good on them; Metalcrown smiles at it, more fool us."},"wants":"A mile-marker on the Stonehold road carrying his grandfather's initials, carved by Stonehold hands.","site":"slagmoor","town":"Slagmoor","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:slagmoor":{"name":"Corisande Glaze","age":26,"role":"lamp-glass blower working the moor's slag-sand; sells most of her glass on the Spiritveil road","look":"Freckled with tiny burn scars up both forearms, hair tied back with a twist of wire, a green glass bead in one ear that she blew at nine.","voice":"Bright, interrupts herself, shapes the air with her hands as if she's turning a gather on the pipe.","greet":"Shift! Mind the crate, it's lamps. Well. It's going to be lamps.","lines":["Spiritveil buyers pay double for slag-glass. Say it holds a flame steadier. I think they just like the green.","Pitmouth isn't all cheats. Some of them are only rude. One of them is — never mind.","At Last Up I hold my breath from the first strike to the third. Everyone does. Don't tell anyone everyone does."],"about":"I blow lamp-glass from the moor's sand, like my mother did, and hers. Mine's better. Most of it goes down the Spiritveil road to people who'll keep a lamp burning four hundred years, which is a nice thought, isn't it. A bit of Slagmoor outliving the hill.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a few polite words from buyers ('Lamp's lit')"}],"childhood":"Corisande grew up in the glass-shed at the edge of the moor, under a roof of cracked green panes that dripped when it rained, and her mother's rule was that nobody touched a blowpipe until they had swept sand for a year. Corisande swept from five to six, then from six to seven again because she broke a chimney-glass on purpose to see how it would go. Her father, a pit carpenter, made her toys out of prop offcuts: a cage that went up and down on a string, a tiny Old Grandmother with a wheel that turned. Her great childhood terror was not the pit but the annealing oven, which she was certain ate cats, because the shed cat vanished one winter. It turned up in Pitmouth a year later, fat, and wouldn't come home.","schooling":"The pithead school under Teacher Hulda Maul. Corisande failed the blindfold lamp test twice, then passed the third time by smelling which part had the oil on it, and Maul let it stand because it worked. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered, but she told the tester the Veil lamp's glass had a seam in it, and he wrote that down and thanked her. First Tool at ten, a pair of jacks, the glassblower's tongs.","history":"She took the furnace at nineteen when her mother's eyes went, and ground the lenses for her mother's fitted sight herself, which took a winter and three tries. She sells some lamp-glass to the pit, but the money is on the Spiritveil road: at twenty she met a Spiritveil buyer called Oth at a Marches fair, a man of three hundred and something who haggles like he has all the time in the world because he does, and he has bought half her glass every season since. Two years ago a weighing at the hill road turned into a shoving match between Slagmoor and Pitmouth, and a Pitmouth smith with a fitted lens for an eye pulled Corisande out from under a tipping wagon. They argued about the scales for four hours over flue eggs. They have been arguing ever since, in a hayloft halfway between the two villages, twice a week. Nobody knows. In Slagmoor or in Pitmouth it would be a scandal of the very best kind.","family":"Her mother Ysa, sharp-tongued behind her ground lenses; her father Corwen, who helped build the frame for the shaft cap. A younger brother, Ned, who works the pit and is quieter than he used to be.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"The first lantern was a cracked clay cup. Corisande thinks about the crack, and that it held a flame anyway."},"band":{"act":"The Willow Choir","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Oth hummed 'Nothing Is Lost' while he haggled, and she knocked a crossing off the price so he'd finish it.","rv_vote":"The Willow Choir, so Oth has something to be smug about."},"vote":{"local":"A quiet Heartwright sympathiser: she thinks people who build for others shouldn't be hanged as 'inefficient', and keeps that thought inside the glass-shed.","edict":"Against, softly. Oth says the lamp-checks will come for lanterns next."},"wants":"To bring Marit to Last Up and have the whole village look at the two of them and then go in to supper.","site":"slagmoor","town":"Slagmoor","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:slagmoor:b":{"name":"Jet Tamper","age":50,"role":"prop-setter on Old Grandmother's shaft and, most nights, the Last Up who rings the triangle","look":"Wiry, bald under a leather cap, the creases of the face grey for good. Their right wrist is fitted, so the tally plate hangs on a cord at the throat instead.","voice":"Low and unhurried; says 'we' for the shift and saves 'I' for mistakes.","greet":"Shift. You'll hear the triangle soon. When you do, stop. Everyone does here.","lines":["Gertrud says Pitmouth's light. Tibbet says we're wet. The ore doesn't care, and nor does the pit.","The capped shaft breathes when the weather turns. You can hear it from my door. Company, really.","I'm Last Up most nights. Somebody has to be last, and I like it down there with everyone gone."],"about":"I set props in Old Grandmother's shaft, thirty-eight years now. I'm usually last up, so I've rung that triangle more than anyone living. People think it's a sad job. It's the best one in the village. I'm the one who gets to tell them it's supper.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good; props are still counted in it"},{"tongue":"Pit knock-code","level":"fluent, through rock or iron"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Jet was the middle of seven Tampers in a sod-roofed pit house, and their mother ran the children by the shift whistles, washing on first, lessons on second, bed on third. The game Jet loved was props: digging tunnels into the soft slag dunes behind the house with the younger ones, shoring them with bean-sticks, and then seeing who dared crawl furthest in. They had a gift for knowing which stick was carrying the most. At eleven Jet told the family at supper that they were neither a boy nor a girl and wouldn't be pretending otherwise. Their grandmother said, 'Then pass the swede,' and that was the whole of it in the Tamper house. The village took a year longer, and the year was mostly fine.","schooling":"A few winters at the pithead school under old Teacher Jem Prowse, before Hulda Maul's time, who taught the pit knock-code alongside letters and rapped 'good' on a desk with his knuckle instead of saying it. Lamp Test at nine, late, because the city cart broke an axle on the hill the year before: nothing. First Tool at ten, a tamping bar too long for them to carry. Down the pit at twelve.","history":"Jet worked the cage at twelve, packed props at fifteen and set them by twenty, and became the person the overmen asked when a roof sounded wrong. At twenty-seven a prop slipped and crushed their right hand, and a Rivet Hollow fitter rebuilt it from the wrist, which is why the plate moved to a cord. At thirty-one Jet was Last Up on the second shift the night before the third shift drowned; they rang the triangle, the village went to supper, the next shift went down, and the next morning nobody rang anything. Since then Jet has taken Last Up whenever they can, and has never once rung before checking every drift twice. Almost never. Eight years ago they rang, went home, ate, and then sat up thinking about the side-drift on level four, went back down alone with a lamp, and found Ned Glaze, sixteen, asleep against the wall after a double. Jet woke him, walked him up, and said nothing to anyone. They still share a house with their sister Moll's family, and have never wanted a Link of their own.","family":"Sister Moll and her husband and four children, in whose house Jet has the back room and the last word on props. Five other siblings scattered between the pit and the city.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"He held what he was told to hold, and Stormreach riders still throw stones at him. Jet thinks that's about what holding costs."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"The forty-second silence is the best part. It's the gap between the second strike of the triangle and the third.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime, and Jet will sit through the silence without moving."},"vote":{"local":"Commons. One shift, one vote; the pit is a shift, and no Dominus has ever been down one.","edict":"Torn. Sparks aren't their business, but anything that counts people instead of props makes them uneasy."},"wants":"To retire without ever once ringing the triangle while someone is still below.","site":"slagmoor","town":"Slagmoor","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:pitmouth":{"name":"Lisle Flux","age":58,"role":"village trader and flue-egg seller; a former melt-watcher, taken off the furnaces after the Second Sister cracked","look":"Long and loose-jointed, ginger hair gone white, eyebrows singed thin for good. His fitted right knee clicks when he bends for the bottom shelf.","voice":"Rambling and self-mocking; slips into his mother's Kindle endearments, everything ending in '-ren'.","greet":"Warm hearth — sorry, shift! My mother's tongue. Flue eggs, packs, string. Everything a body needs and a few things it doesn't.","lines":["Slagmoor sent a load yesterday that dripped on the beam. Tibbet stood there and counted the drips. Forty-one.","Second Sister cracked thirty-one winters back. I was on watch. I was asleep. There. Now you know all of it.","Flue eggs keep a week. Mine keep a day and a half, because I eat them."],"about":"My mother walked up the Coal Road from Flameborne with a coal in a tin and married a Pitmouth melter. So I'm half warm and half iron, and all of me's in this shop. I used to watch furnaces. Now I watch the door, which is safer for everyone.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good; his mother's"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a little"}],"childhood":"Lisle's mother Tella came from a forge-town on the Cinderway and brought her Kept Coal east in a tin lined with ash, and every First Coal she relit her Pitmouth kitchen from it while the neighbours stood at the window watching as if it were clockwork theatre. Lisle grew up in a house where his mother shouted because she loved you and his father's family went silent because they were hurt, and he spent his first ten years translating between the two. At nine he refused his father to his face over the iron-soled school boots every Pitmouth child wore, a First No four years early by his mother's reckoning. His father laughed for a week, and Lisle went to school in his mother's slippers and was mocked for a month.","schooling":"The ridge school under Teacher Dunmore Slake, a young man then, who taught reading in the warm room and sums in the cold room on the theory that cold sharpens the mind. It didn't; nobody on the ridge does sums well. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered, and his mother cried a little, not because he had no spark but because she'd hoped for Ember. First Tool at ten, a melt-hook. Left at thirteen.","history":"He went on the furnaces at fourteen as a melt-watcher and he was good at it, the boy who could see the colour turn a breath before anyone else. At twenty-seven, on the night the Second Sister cracked, he was on watch alone, and by morning three houses on the slope were gone under melt, along with a dog called Biscuit. Nobody died, because Sorcha Pell was up with a teething baby and screamed the whole row awake. The furnace-masters said Lisle had dozed on the bench after a double shift, and he let them say it. He was taken off the furnaces for good. He spent a year sullen, and then his mother set him up selling her slapbread and flue eggs from a trestle, and the trestle grew into the village shop. He sells chips of the Second Sister's old iron band as keepsakes, which is in poor taste and sells well. At forty-four he married Dorcas, a glasshouse widow from down the slope, and has been happier since than he'd thought a man with his reputation was allowed.","family":"Dorcas, who calls him 'my disaster' fondly; her daughter Imelda, who runs a glasshouse and buys furnace heat from Bartholomew Clinch. His mother died at eighty, and her Kept Coal still burns in the shop stove.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"Her kitchen fire became a whole Realm's hearth. Lisle keeps his mother's coal in a shop stove and says that counts for something."},"band":{"act":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"She kneads dough on stage the way his mother kneaded, elbows out, swearing under her breath.","rv_vote":"Old Tamsin, for his mother's sake."},"vote":{"local":"Commons, lightly. The Commons would have voted on whether to sack him, and he'd have liked a vote.","edict":"Against. Half of him is Flameborne, and his mother would have refused it with a loaf to the face."},"wants":"For one person in Pitmouth to forget the Second Sister before he dies.","site":"pitmouth","town":"Pitmouth","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:pitmouth":{"name":"Marit Tongs","age":31,"role":"smith to the Seven Sisters: flue doors, hooks, bands; re-forged the Second Sister's iron patch","look":"Lean, black hair hacked short, her left eye a smoke-grey lens in a brass rim that ticks when it focuses.","voice":"Clipped and competitive. Says the number before the thing.","greet":"Shift. Nine hundred on the Fourth Sister today and my forge is hotter. What do you need?","lines":["Tibbet Gauge checks the beam every morning with his mother's brass block. I cast its case. It's a good block. Probably.","Not everyone up the hill's a cheat. One of them owes me four hours and a flue egg.","Fifteen years old, I came out the top of the Fourth Sister first. Still the record. Nobody's touched it."],"about":"Smith for the Sisters: hooks, doors, bands, the patch on the Second. The eye's fitted and it reads heat like you'd read a face. I won the Sweep at fifteen and I'll tell you about it if you stand still.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few words, from a Stormreach chain-wright who trades for her hooks"}],"childhood":"Marit was born the spring after the Second Sister cracked, the third daughter of a furnace-door hanger, in one of the houses rebuilt behind the new slag wall, and the wall was her first playground. She learned to judge iron by spitting on it. Her mother Rhoswen timed everything in the house with a sand-glass, baths, chores, tempers, and Marit grew up racing the sand. At eleven she tried to climb an unbanked Sister for a bet with a Corbel boy and got as far as the first flue door before her mother hauled her out backwards by the belt. The heat that came out of that door is the clearest thing she remembers from being a child, clearer than any face, and four years later it was why she won.","schooling":"The ridge school under Teacher Dunmore Slake, old by then and teaching from a chair by the warm-room door, who played a game of guessing the heat of things by touch, a spoon, a stone, a slate left in the sun. Marit never lost. Lamp Test at nine: nothing answered. First Tool at ten, a pair of small furnace tongs stamped TONGS M.","history":"At fifteen she raced the Sweep and came out of the top of the Fourth Sister so fast the judges thought she'd skipped a flue; she shouted down the shaft and the village shouted back her grandfather's name, Haddo Tongs, and she still says it was the loudest she's ever heard Pitmouth. She apprenticed to old Pernell at the ridge forge at sixteen. At twenty-two a flue-door blowback took her left eye, and she chose a Rivet Hollow lens that turns heat into colour; she calls it the best decision she's made, and she has stopped asking anyone else's opinion. She took the forge at twenty-six. Three years ago she re-forged the Second Sister's band, which is why it still looks new. Two years ago she pulled a Slagmoor glassblower out from under a tipping wagon and has not been the same since. Lately she has been looking at what the fitted can do and thinking about a second fitting, a hand, and has told no one but the glassblower.","family":"Her mother Rhoswen, still timing things; two older sisters in the city who think she should marry. No partner anyone in Pitmouth knows of.","hero":{"who":"The Hammer-Bearer","why":"The only one who stood when the whole Council laughed at Metalcrown. Marit has decided that when everyone's laughing, you stand first."},"band":{"act":"Third Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"'I Made It' is scratched inside her leather apron, where she'll see it every time she ties it on.","rv_vote":"Third Whistle, and she'll be at the front."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion, mostly: the lens works, and the Synthesis paid half of it.","edict":"Smiling, but only because a Lightforge busy counting its own sparks has less time to sneer at Metalcrown's."},"wants":"To race the Sweep one more time and beat her own record.","site":"pitmouth","town":"Pitmouth","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:pitmouth":{"name":"Bartholomew Clinch","age":74,"role":"heat-meterer: bills the glasshouses down the slope for furnace heat by the hour","look":"Square and bald, in a felt waistcoat with a dozen pocket gauges clipped to it. Born-whole bar two fitted toes he never mentions.","voice":"Flat and thorough; gives more detail than you asked for and knows it.","greet":"Shift. I'll warn you now, I'm the dull one. Ask me anything about ducts.","lines":["Heat runs down the slope in clay ducts to fourteen glasshouses. I meter all fourteen. Gripping stuff.","Flue eggs don't keep a week. I've tested it. Day five they go strange. Nobody wants to hear it.","Lisle Flux slept through the Second Sister. Lovely man. Still wouldn't trust him with a kettle."],"about":"I meter furnace heat to the glasshouses and bill them by the hour. Fifty-one years. Never had an adventure, never wanted one. My wife says I'm the most reliable man on the ridge, and she says it as a complaint.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Bartholomew was the only child of two meter-readers, and he will tell you, cheerfully, that nothing happened to him. His father kept an hourly heat log for the glasshouse ducts, and Bartholomew learned to read from it, columns of numbers with the odd word like 'draught' or 'Tuesday'. His mother quizzed him on duct joints at supper and he loved every question. The single scandal of his childhood came at twelve, when he slipped through a glasshouse roof and landed on the only melon vine on the ridge, which its owner, a widow called Grizel Pane, had been coaxing for nine years. He paid her back by carrying her water for two summers. She left him the vine's last seeds in her will, and he has never managed to grow them.","schooling":"The ridge school under Teacher Ortolan Brisk, before Slake's day, who gave every child a job for the term; Bartholomew was made keeper of the stove and never gave it up. He stayed until fourteen, two years past the usual, and is still the only child on the ridge who stayed on because he wanted to. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a duct-gauge to his father's own design.","history":"He worked as his father's assistant from fifteen and took the meter house over at twenty-three, and he has logged the heat to fourteen glasshouses every hour since. At twenty-six he married Philippa Grate, a glasshouse daughter who said yes because he was the only man on the ridge who never lied about anything, including how dull he was. They had three children; one meters heat in the city, one rides the long-wagons, one married into Stormreach and sends shouted apologies by letter, in capitals. When the Second Sister cracked he was forty-three, and the melt came down the slope straight at his meter house, the only building in its path that didn't burn, because he'd insisted on clay walls twenty years before and been laughed at. He has never let anyone forget this. Lately the glasshouses want to buy heat by the day instead of the hour, which would make his log pointless, and he is resisting with a patience nobody on the ridge can match.","family":"Philippa, who teases him and checks his sums; three grown children and six grandchildren, none of whom want the meter house.","hero":{"who":"Wilmet Clinch, his mother","why":"She designed the clay duct joint everyone on the ridge uses and never put her name to it. He thinks that's the most admirable thing a person can do and is a little sad nobody knows."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Long, even, no surprises. He timed one track at twelve minutes eleven seconds and was delighted.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters, and he will time the set."},"vote":{"local":"Commons, in a quiet way; he likes anything that's counted fairly, and one shift, one vote is a count.","edict":"Mildly for. It's a register, and he likes registers. He'd like it less if it were his."},"wants":"For the meter house to outlast him, and for someone to read the log after he's gone.","site":"pitmouth","town":"Pitmouth","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:pitmouth:b":{"name":"Posy Sootwell","age":16,"role":"clinker-sorter on the ridge; racing the Sweep for the third time","look":"Short and strong, hair cropped, knees permanently scabbed from flue practice, a chimney brush pushed through her belt like a sword.","voice":"Breathless and cheeky; keeps score of everything, including you.","greet":"Shift! I'm racing the Sweep this year. Third go. I'll win. Ask me why I'm worried.","lines":["If I win, the village shouts the name of my dead. I haven't got any. Everybody's alive. It's a disaster.","Marit Tongs holds the record. She timed me once and said 'nearly'. I'll take nearly.","Someone's been sweeping the Fourth Sister at night. Not me. The soot's gone exactly where I'd put my feet."],"about":"I'm sixteen and I sort clinker for my uncle and I train for the Sweep every night. My family is enormous and nobody in it has died for forty years, which my gran says is lucky and I say is a problem.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Posy is the eleventh of fourteen cousins living in three knocked-through houses behind the slag wall, and the Sootwells are known up and down the ridge for not dying. Her childhood was one long contest. Every First Tool day the cousins race up the slag wall for the Sootwell Cup, a dented ladle, and at nine Posy beat three older cousins to the top and broke her wrist coming down. Her great-grandmother Agathe, then ninety, set it on the kitchen table with two spoons and a strip of sheet, born bone, no fitting, and told her she was an idiot and a credit to the family in the same breath. Posy wore the ladle on a string for a year. She has not, so far, lost to a cousin at anything since.","schooling":"The ridge school under Teacher Iolanthe Clamp, who traded for a Stormreach chain-ladder and hung it in the yard so children could climb it during sums. Posy did all her times tables upside down. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered, and she tried to climb the lamp stand. First Tool at ten, a flue-brush, which is the one in her belt.","history":"She left school at fourteen to sort clinker for her uncle Hob on the furnace floor and that spring raced her first Sweep and came third. At fifteen she came second, beaten by a hand's width by Fitch Corbel, who shouted his dead aunt's name down the Fourth Sister and made half the village weep. That was when Posy understood the problem. If she wins, the ridge waits for a name, and she has nobody. She spent the winter interviewing great-grandmother Agathe for dead relatives; Agathe produced three, one of whom turned out to be alive and living in Furnace Row. So Posy has a plan. When she comes out on top she is going to shout 'Biscuit', the dog that went under the melt the night the Second Sister cracked, before she was born. She's told nobody but the dog's old owners, the Pells, who laughed until Sorcha Pell had to sit down, and then said yes.","family":"Parents Hob and Linnet Sootwell, clinker-firers; thirteen cousins; great-grandmother Agathe, ninety-seven, alive and extremely rude.","hero":{"who":"Kestrel Vane","why":"The sky-racer ages every season she races, and she races anyway. Posy's poster is folded so Kestrel's face fits the back of her door."},"band":{"act":"The Fitted","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"'Unplated' is what she dances to on the flue ledges, and they're in the Sparkbeat Cup.","rv_vote":"The Fitted, because nobody from home has ever won the Cup."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to sign anything. She'd vote for whoever let the over-twenties race the Sweep, so she could race forever.","edict":"She doesn't really know what it is, only that the grown-ups smile when it's mentioned, so she's learned to smile too."},"wants":"To win the Sweep and hear the whole ridge shout 'Biscuit'.","site":"pitmouth","town":"Pitmouth","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:rivethollow":{"name":"Hendry Burr","age":45,"role":"keeper of the Hollow store; buys failed fittings from all over Metalcrown; former Proving stagehand; keeps goats","look":"Barrel-chested and soft-footed, in a coat sewn with dozens of small pockets, each one holding a different rivet. Two fingers of his right hand are fitted.","voice":"Gentle and careful; never once says the word 'smuggle'.","greet":"Shift. Packs on the left, bread on the right, broken fittings in the bin by your foot. I buy those.","lines":["Steelgate owns the canyon road. Nobody owns the goats' road. Goats are very honest animals.","I was on the Proving stage when the joint tore. I held the lad's other arm. Ask Aurek about it; he tells it better.","Every apprentice drives a rivet in the Wall. Mine's the crooked one by the drain. I was nervous."],"about":"I keep the Hollow's store and buy every failed fitting I can find, so the smiths here can see exactly what broke. People call it ghoulish. It's how the joint got fixed. I'm also very fond of goats.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"passable, from Stonehold wagon drivers"}],"childhood":"Hendry was born to a burr-grinder, the man who takes the rough edges off rivets, and grew up in a house that hissed with grinding from first whistle to last. He was a timid child in the loudest canyon in the Realm. He hid from the hammers by going up, and up the cliffs he found the Hollow's goats, who come down at dusk to lick the salt off the forge banks. By twelve he knew every goat track out of the canyon, which cracks led to the slag-hills, and which led to the old vents that breathe warm air from somewhere underneath. His father wanted him a fitter. At thirteen Hendry watched a fitting being set into a man's shoulder and fainted onto the shop floor, and that settled it.","schooling":"Hollow shift-school under Teacher Gilda Swarf, who wouldn't let a child near the Rivet Wall until they'd driven a hundred rivets straight into a plank. Hendry drove a hundred and three because he lost count, and his Wall rivet still went in crooked. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a burr-file, which lives in the pocket over his heart.","history":"He couldn't stomach fitting, so at seventeen he went to the city as a Proving stagehand, hauling machines on and off the yearly show stage, and he did it for twenty years. Seven years ago he was behind the lift-frame when Aurek Spline's joint tore at the shoulder, and Hendry was the one who held the boy's good arm until the fitters came. He went home to the Hollow that winter and bought the dead store, and then began buying failed fittings from every fitting-house in Metalcrown, by the bin, so the Hollow's smiths could see what broke and how. Hepzibah Punch built the new knuckle joint off his bins. Now that Steelgate pays less for fittings and owns the only road out, Hendry walks loads up the goat tracks on his own back to buyers in the city, and calls it taking the goats for exercise. He lives with his sister Marigold and her three children, and keeps eleven goats, each named for a famous fitting failure.","family":"His sister Marigold and her children, who think he's the softest man alive; their father, the burr-grinder, long gone and still said at the Long Whistle.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"Korr built bridges so no Realm would ever be alone. As far as Hendry is concerned his goat track is a bridge, and Steelgate's road is a wall."},"band":{"act":"Gearbox","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"The stagehands danced to it in the wings at every Proving, while the engineers sweated.","rv_vote":"Gearbox, for the old crew."},"vote":{"local":"He signs the petitions and doubts the Dominion quietly. He'd vote for whoever let the Hollow sell straight to the city, which so far is nobody, so he votes with goats.","edict":"An indifferent smile. Metalcrown sparks come with their list already stamped on the housing."},"wants":"For Steelgate to buy the goat track and pave it, so that he can stop climbing it at forty-five.","site":"rivethollow","town":"Rivet Hollow","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:rivethollow":{"name":"Hepzibah Punch","age":66,"role":"master riveter; quietly redesigned the knuckle joint every fitted arm in Metalcrown now wears","look":"Tall and thin as a drawn rod, white hair in a net, forearms like rope. Her tally plate reads ALL and is polished bright.","voice":"Precise and teacherly; answers a question with a better question.","greet":"Shift. What's broken, and how did you break it? Tell me the second part first.","lines":["We make the joint. Who wears it, and why, is the other side of the canyon. I used to believe that more than I do.","The month we stopped work, the Hollow went so quiet the goats came down to see who'd died.","Steelgate takes my best apprentices, then sends me their mistakes to mend. It's a living."],"about":"I've riveted in the Hollow fifty-four years. I make joints; I don't wear them. Somebody has to remember what an arm does on its own. Seven years ago I redesigned the knuckle the whole Realm wears now, and nobody knows that, and I'd like it kept that way.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"some, picked up underground"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"understands a little, speaks none"}],"childhood":"Hepzibah was the last of nine children in a rivet-header's house and the one who took the clocks apart. At seven her grandfather's old fitted hand seized in the night, the fingers locked in a claw, and while the adults argued about the cost of a fitter she opened the housing with a hairpin, found the bent pawl, and straightened it on the kitchen step. He opened and closed his hand ten times and then gave her his own First Tool, which is not done; a First Tool goes into your hands at death, not a grandchild's. Her mother made him take it back. He waited until the funeral of a neighbour, when everyone was busy, and gave it to her again. She has it still.","schooling":"Hollow shift-school under Teacher Brigid Swarf, Gilda's mother, who made every child draw the same hinge every morning for a month until they could see how it moved without moving it. Hepzibah drew it in her sleep. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a rivet-set, and that made two.","history":"She apprenticed at twelve and was a master at thirty, and for most of her life she made exactly what she was asked to. She never married; for twenty-five years her companion was Agnete, a wages clerk at the Steelgate end of the canyon road, and they walked to each other on alternate nights until Agnete died at sixty. In her forties she met a man called Wilm Gannet, a Heartwright, who built fittings for people who couldn't pay; he was hanged as inefficient in the old Dominus's time, and his design book came to Hepzibah in a sack of scrap with no note. Seven years ago, after the Proving accident, she stood up in the canyon and said the Hollow would not drive another rivet until the joint was right. They stopped for a month. She worked through Hendry Burr's bins of failures and drew the new knuckle from Gannet's old pages and her own, and gave the drawing to the Hollow as a whole. Twice a season now she goes up the goat tracks and down an old vent into the Warm, under the city, and fits the unaugmented for nothing.","family":"Seven living siblings, most of them fitted, most of them proud of her in a way she finds embarrassing. Agnete's name at the Long Whistle every dusk.","hero":{"who":"Wilm Gannet, Heartwright","why":"He fitted her grandfather's second hand for nothing, years before she knew what that would cost him."},"band":{"act":"Long Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Loud and rough and built out of whatever was lying around, which is how she'd build if nobody checked her.","rv_vote":"Long Whistle, and she'll deny it."},"vote":{"local":"A Heartwright sympathiser in all but name. She votes by not signing the Synthesis work-orders that say 'efficiency' on them.","edict":"Against. It starts with sparks, then it's parts, then it's who deserves them."},"wants":"To train one apprentice who will do the Warm work after her and never be caught.","site":"rivethollow","town":"Rivet Hollow","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:rivethollow":{"name":"Aurek Spline","age":28,"role":"test-wearer and carrier for the Hollow; the young man whose fitting tore on the Proving stage","look":"Rangy, dark curls, a fitted left arm from the shoulder carrying the Hollow's new knuckle, a scar like a seam where steel meets collarbone.","voice":"Wry, sick of his own story, tells it anyway; wants plain talk back.","greet":"Shift. Yes, I'm the arm. No, it doesn't hurt. Yes, you can look.","lines":["They stopped the whole Hollow for a month for me. I owe a canyon. You try paying that back.","Hendry buys broken fittings. I sold him mine. Funny afternoon, that.","Steelgate wants the Hollow's best. They can have me. I'm not a smith. I'm an example."],"about":"I tested fittings on the Proving stage. One tore. The Hollow fixed it so it'd never tear again, and I wear the fix. Everyone here is very kind to me all day long, and I'd like one of them to be rude.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a few phrases, and the evidential endings, from letters"}],"childhood":"Aurek grew up in Steelgate proper, among the mill quarters, son of a rolling-hand and a wages clerk, in a family that loved a stage. His uncle walked fitted legs at the Proving every year to cheering, and Aurek wanted cheering. At nine he played the boy in the clockwork theatre's Iron Man Who Wanted a Heart, the boy who hands the Iron Man the key, and he did it so earnestly that a mill foreman cried and gave him a copper toll. He kept the toll in his shoe for a year. He was born-whole and vain about it, and he practised looking modest in the washroom mirror.","schooling":"Steelgate mill-school under Teacher Rennart Coke, who marched his class across the rolling floor once a year so they'd know what had killed their grandfathers. Most children hated it; Aurek loved the noise and walked slowly to be seen. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a spline key.","history":"At nineteen he signed on with a city fitting-house as a test-wearer, paid to wear new work and show it off, and at twenty he had his left arm fitted for the job, the old joint, and was proud of it. At twenty-one, on the Proving stage, lifting an iron beam to show what the arm could hold, the joint tore and took his arm at the shoulder in front of the whole city. A stagehand held his other arm. A Lightforge witness who'd come to watch, which was itself a scandal, sat by his cot afterwards and asked him plain questions, and he found that he liked them better than sympathy. The Hollow stopped for a month and Hepzibah Punch's crew fitted him with the first of the new knuckle. Steelgate looked at him differently after that, so he moved down the canyon. Now he wears-in every new joint the Hollow makes, carries loads, and is everybody's favourite example. He writes every month to the witness, Lenna Glass of Clearwater, in his bad Clear, and she writes back in better Shiftcant.","family":"His parents in Steelgate, who are hurt that he left; his uncle, who stopped walking the Proving after the accident.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"Lenna quoted him at the cot: 'Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.' Aurek wants everyone to talk to him like that."},"band":{"act":"Calder Glass","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Lenna sent him the battle rapper who can't lie, and he likes hearing someone who couldn't flatter you if he tried.","rv_vote":"Calder Glass: a lamp from Metalcrown to Lightforge, the first anyone in the Hollow has heard of, partly out of spite."},"vote":{"local":"Unsure. The Synthesis paid for the new joint and then asked for the torn one to be destroyed, and he can't make those two things sit together.","edict":"Against. Lenna's people will be audited, and he doesn't like anything that makes her write more carefully."},"wants":"For one person to treat him as just a man with an arm.","site":"rivethollow","town":"Rivet Hollow","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:rivethollow:b":{"name":"Lark Swage","age":19,"role":"second-year apprentice fitter under Hepzibah Punch; leader of the Hollow's piglet raids","look":"Small and ferocious, brown hair in two knots, apron stiff with forge-black, a burn on her chin shaped like a comma.","voice":"Loud from canyon habit; swears creatively without ever swearing.","greet":"SHIFT! Sorry. Canyon voice. What're you after?","lines":["I've lost the Echo Count six times this season. Six rounds. It was Dov's hammer every time. It's always Dov's hammer.","We steal Steelgate piglets on a dare, and we give them back. Mostly. Barnaby Mangle knows what he did.","Mab Ferrule won't let anyone touch her hand but her. I want to be that stubborn when I'm old."],"about":"Second-year apprentice in the Hollow under Hepzibah Punch. I drove my Wall rivet last spring and it's the straightest on the left side, ask anyone. I'm going to be the best fitter in Metalcrown, or the loudest, whichever comes first.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"passable"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"good, from Shadowdeep children in the Warm"}],"childhood":"Lark wasn't Hollow-born. She grew up in the Warm, the pits under the city's foundries where the unaugmented live, in a hot dark that never fully goes dark because the furnace floors glow through the gratings above. Her mother sold slag-tea from a barrow, and Lark ran the cups. The Warm sits against Shadowdeep's tunnels, and the Shadowdeep children who came through trading glim taught her hand-cant before she could write, mostly the rude signs. She fixed things. A trader's lamp at eleven. At twelve she mended her mother's leaking kettle on the barrow in front of a tall old woman from the Hollow who'd stopped for tea, and the woman asked her name, drank two cups, and came back the next season to ask her mother if Lark could come down the canyon and keep house for her.","schooling":"A Warm school in a disused flue, under Teacher Abelard Ash, who had no tools to teach with so drew them in chalk on the brick and made the class name every part. When the foundry sent no First Tools for Warm children he carved them out of wood. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Her First Tool at ten was a wooden mallet; Hepzibah gave her an iron one at fourteen.","history":"She came to the Hollow at twelve to keep Hepzibah Punch's house and spent three years being told to keep out of the shop and not keeping out of it. At fifteen Hepzibah gave up and apprenticed her. At eighteen she drove her rivet into the Wall, dead straight, and the whole canyon watched because they'd heard she'd been practising on Hepzibah's shutters. She is good at joints and impatient with drawings. She loses the Echo Count constantly and buys rounds she can't afford. She runs the Hollow's piglet raids on the Steelgate farms, and last month got a piglet as far as the canyon road before Barnaby Mangle caught up with her, mysteriously slowly. Every month she sends half her pay down to the Warm, and every month her mother sends a quarter of it back with a cup of slag-tea leaves wrapped in cloth. Lark has no idea that Hepzibah ever goes down there.","family":"Her mother, Dilly Swage, still on her barrow in the Warm with a hip that's grown crooked; no father anyone mentions.","hero":{"who":"Mab Ferrule","why":"A fitter who built her own hand and lets no one else touch it. Lark thinks that's what being a fitter means."},"band":{"act":"No Name","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"The Shadowdeep kids in the Warm whispered No Name's bars through the gratings, and it's the sound of home.","rv_vote":"No Name, so they can come second, the way they like."},"vote":{"local":"Nobody in the Warm votes for anything, so she's for whoever sends Warm children iron First Tools.","edict":"Against, loudly, without knowing much about it."},"wants":"To fit her mother's crooked hip with a joint she made with her own hands.","site":"rivethollow","town":"Rivet Hollow","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:furnacerow":{"name":"Mina Damper","age":70,"role":"keeper of the Row store; sells ration bread to Ironwatch's billeted soldiers","look":"Round and quick, a black shawl fastened with brass buttons cut from her husband's old work coat. Her tally plate has one line struck through: right hip.","voice":"Brisk and mothering, calls everyone 'pet', talks politics while she counts your change.","greet":"Shift, pet. Bread's from the Row, packs are from the city, and the gossip's free.","lines":["Ironwatch's lads buy half my bread. I want them gone, and I want their money. I'm seventy. I'm allowed both.","We sit in our family's seat on the Bench. Ours is the Damper seat, fourth from the north end. My nan wore that dip into it.","Somebody pulls the paper street names down at night. Ada Lathe knows them all anyway, so who's it hurting?"],"about":"Store on the Row for forty years, and my mother's before me. I sell bread to the soldiers I want out of my street. That's Furnace Row, pet. We'll feed anyone, and then we'll argue with them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few words, from a Stormreach drover who reads speeches aloud"}],"childhood":"Mina's father made the dampers, the iron plates that choke a furnace's flue, and he had fitted every damper on the Row, so she grew up able to tell each house's fire by the note its flue made. She was the child sent down the Long Pot every morning with a bowl, to walk the whole street and report which hundred yards tasted best that day. She took it more seriously than anything since. She kept a slate of rankings by the door and the families argued with it. At eleven she declared the Tuyere stretch best for nine mornings running, and the Gudgeons stopped speaking to the Dampers for a season, and her father told her that was what honesty cost on the Row and he was proud of her, and also to stop it.","schooling":"The Row school, held on the Commons Bench itself in summer and in the Cope furnace-house in winter, under Teacher Hob Cokeley, who taught history by sitting each child in a different family's original seat and making them argue as that furnace would have. Mina was the Anvil seat for a whole term and has never got over it. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a damper key.","history":"She married Tobias Chock at twenty, a wheel-chock maker, and he took her name because the Dampers had a seat on the Bench and the Chocks didn't; on the Row, the seat-name wins. She took over her mother's store at thirty and has run it for forty years on a ledger, a long memory, and the principle that everybody eats. Her right hip went at sixty-two and a city fitter set the new one; she walked to the Bench the day after and sat in her seat to show it. Tobias died when she was sixty-five, of nothing anyone could name, just tired, and she says his name at the Long Whistle with her hand on his old coat buttons. This season Ironwatch billeted soldiers in the Row's cold furnaces and drilled them in the street. She sells the quartermaster bread at a fair price and the soldiers bread at a less fair one, and she has also been quietly paid by the same quartermaster to tell him what's said on the Bench, which is a joke he hasn't noticed yet.","family":"Two daughters on the Row, one son who married into Ironwatch's garrison families and has gone very quiet about it, and a great many grandchildren who come to the store for broken biscuits.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend","why":"A Stormreach drover read out her speech against the Edict on the Row, and Mina had it copied twice. Someone loud who governs by shouting is the nearest thing to the Commons she's heard of in her lifetime."},"band":{"act":"The Bench","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"She's certain they're named after the Commons Bench. They are probably not.","rv_vote":"The Bench, because family trios should stick together."},"vote":{"local":"Commons, without a second's doubt. She votes Ada Lathe as street speaker every year and would vote the Commons back tomorrow.","edict":"Against. If they'll count sparks, they'll count bread, and then they'll count who's had too much."},"wants":"To see the soldiers march out of the Row on a morning she's already baked for them, so they leave fed.","site":"furnacerow","town":"Furnace Row","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:furnacerow":{"name":"Silas Tuyere","age":40,"role":"smith at the Tuyere family furnace; pans, hinges, the odd blade for Ironwatch","look":"Heavy and slow-moving, his fitted knees hiss when he kneels at the furnace mouth. A strand of copper wire braided into his beard.","voice":"Quiet and considered, slow as a Stonehold road; his hands never stop while he talks.","greet":"Shift. Pots, pans, hinges, horseshoes. If it's iron and it's small, the Tuyere fire's done it.","lines":["There's a soldier sleeping in my grandad's old furnace next door. Nice lad. Snores like a bellows. I'm meant to hate him.","Ironwatch says it's for a season. They said that last season.","Knees are fitted, Rivet Hollow. Took two months to stop falling over and a year to stop being cross about it."],"about":"Tuyeres have run a fire on the Row since the first Commons sat. I cast pans, mostly, and now and then a blade for the castle I don't enjoy making. My knees are iron. The rest of me's slow, but born.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"fair"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"}],"childhood":"Silas was raised in the Tuyere house by his mother Odile and her wife Hanne, a casting-mistress who had come over from the Steelgate side, with his grandfather in the back room, and the house had two furnaces. One was the family fire. The other, next door, was his grandfather's, cold since the old man's hands gave out, and it was Silas's whole kingdom: a castle, a ship, a Reactor Hall, a mine. He kept his treasures inside it on a ledge above the firebars, a Stormreach feather, a cracked glass eye from somebody's fitting, a carving he'd made of the Commons Bench with an extra seat on the end for himself. He'd crawl in with a candle and sit there for hours while Hanne's hammer rang through the wall.","schooling":"The Row school on the Bench under Teacher Hob Cokeley, middle-aged by then, who had the children elect a class speaker every week by shouting. Silas never shouted and never won, until the week everyone else shouted themselves hoarse and he got it by being the only one who could still speak. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a tuyere gauge, the nozzle that feeds air to a furnace.","history":"He apprenticed to Hanne at thirteen and took the family fire at twenty-eight when her eyes dimmed. Decades of kneeling at the furnace mouth ruined his knees, and at thirty-four he had both fitted; he fell over in the street for two months and the Row laughed with him, then stopped laughing when he went back to work twice as fast. He has courted three people and kept none, and is the Row's favourite uncle to children who aren't his. Ironwatch orders blades from him under the billeting levy, and he makes them well and doesn't stamp 'I made it' on them; that goes on his pans. This season they billeted Private Daw Rammer, nineteen, in the cold furnace next door, and on the third night Silas found the boy sitting by candlelight looking at the treasures on the ledge, and Daw said he hadn't touched them, only moved the feather back where it had fallen from. Since then they eat together most nights. Last month Silas did something nobody on the Row would forgive.","family":"Odile and Hanne, both alive and both opinionated, in the back room where his grandfather used to sit. No partner, no children, a great many children who aren't his and call him Uncle Si anyway.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"Pell wouldn't cut his citadel's chain. Silas won't cut the Row loose either, though he couldn't tell you what that would mean in iron."},"band":{"act":"Mason Grey","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A quarry baritone, slow and warm, which is exactly the speed Silas thinks at.","rv_vote":"Mason Grey."},"vote":{"local":"Commons, and Ada Lathe for street speaker, every year.","edict":"Torn. He doesn't like registers, but he likes Lightforge being busy with something that isn't Metalcrown."},"wants":"For Daw to leave the Row as a friend, not as a soldier.","site":"furnacerow","town":"Furnace Row","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:furnacerow":{"name":"Crispin Cope","age":11,"role":"schoolboy; last year's child Speaker of the Row","look":"Skinny and serious, hair cut by his sisters with shears. He wears last Speaker's Night's ribbon pinned to his jumper every day.","voice":"Grave and formal, using grown-up phrases slightly wrong.","greet":"Shift. I was Speaker. Last year. You may approach the Bench.","lines":["I ruled that the soldiers have to share their sweets with the Row. They did. Ironwatch didn't say anything. That means it's law.","Ada Lathe says I'm the best speaker since her sister. Her sister ruled no bedtimes. That's hard to beat.","I can say all the old street names. Anvil Lane, Ballot Turn, Short Bellows, Even-You Row. Ada taught me."],"about":"My name is Crispin Cope and I'm eleven and I was Speaker of the Row for one night. I settled forty-one arguments. Thirty-eight were about sweets. I'm going to be speaker again, a real one, when I'm grown.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, from the soldiers"}],"childhood":"The Copes make the iron hoods that sit over furnace mouths, and Crispin is the youngest of three in a narrow house with a hood-forge in the front room. He was born with a stammer that came out worst when he had something important to say. At six he discovered by accident that if he stood up on the Commons Bench, on the Cope seat, the stammer went quiet; nobody knows why, least of all Crispin. From then on he climbed onto the Bench to say anything that mattered, that he'd broken a cup, that he wanted the last potato, that a cat had died. His mother lets him. The whole Row has grown used to a small boy standing on the iron bench announcing things in a careful voice, and stops to listen, because it's only polite.","schooling":"The Row school under Teacher Ida Cokeley, granddaughter of old Teacher Hob, who teaches sums with the Long Pot ('if every fourth house adds a turnip and the Gudgeons add two…') and keeps the tally on Speaker's Night. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered, and he asked the tester, very politely, for a recount. First Tool at ten, a small cope-hook, which he has polished nearly flat.","history":"Last Speaker's Night the soldiers were newly arrived, and a sergeant had put his boots up on the Damper seat to lace them. Crispin climbed onto the Bench and told him to take them off, and the sergeant did, and that afternoon the children shouted for Crispin louder than anyone has shouted on the Row in years. He sat the Bench from dusk to dawn. He settled forty-one arguments, wrote down every ruling on a slate, and fell asleep on the Cope seat at sunrise with the slate in his lap. Ruling seventeen said the soldiers must share their sweets with the Row. The next morning Sergeant Ulfric Bolt walked down the street with a tin of Ironwatch boiled sweets and handed them round 'by order of the Speaker', and the Row has not stopped telling it since. Crispin has been practising rulings on his sisters all year for the next Speaker's Night.","family":"His father Oswin, night shift at the city furnaces; his mother Hetty, who makes the hoods; sisters Dulcie, fifteen, and Rae, thirteen, who cut his hair badly and defend him savagely.","hero":{"who":"Ada Lathe, street speaker of the Row","why":"She knows every seat on the Bench and who sat in it before. Crispin wants to know as much as she does by the time he's old, which he thinks is about thirty."},"band":{"act":"House of the Seven Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"His sisters love them, so he decided to as well, and then found he actually did.","rv_vote":"House of the Seven Ovens, because his sisters made him promise."},"vote":{"local":"Ada Lathe forever. He wants the Commons back because then everyone would get a Speaker's Night every night.","edict":"He ruled against it on Speaker's Night, ruling twenty-nine, which nobody has enforced."},"wants":"To be elected street speaker at the youngest age ever allowed; when he asked Ada what that was, she said, 'Older than you.'","site":"furnacerow","town":"Furnace Row","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:furnacerow:b":{"name":"Petra Gudgeon","age":35,"role":"head baker at the Row's ration-bakehouse, which feeds Ironwatch; runs the Long Pot rota; prints the old street names","look":"Tall and red-armed, flour to the elbow, a voice built to carry. Her tally plate hangs on a leather strap with one line struck: left ear.","voice":"Loud and nearly profane; laughs at her own jokes before you've heard the end.","greet":"Shift! Mind the trays! If you're buying, buy. If you're gawping, gawp at the bread, it's earned it.","lines":["I print the old street names and nail them up. Someone pulls them down. I print them again. I've more ink than they've got nights.","The Long Pot's best at the Gudgeon stretch, because I put bacon in. Don't tell the Damper end.","Ironwatch says the soldiers are only here a season. Lovely. Which season? They've never said."],"about":"I bake the loaves Ironwatch eats, so I'm feeding the lot drilling in my street. I also run the Long Pot rota and print the old street names. I've a loud mouth and a fitted ear, and the ear doesn't help.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good, from three years at Hearthward"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Petra grew up a gudgeon-turner's daughter, gudgeons being the iron pins that hinges and wheels turn on, in a house where you got fed by speaking up; the quiet Gudgeon children were the thin ones. Her uncle salvaged a broken playbill press from the clockwork theatre when she was eight, and she fixed it with a gudgeon pin and began printing the family's arguments as broadsheets: GRAN SAYS NO, and UNCLE DEN WRONG ABOUT TURNIPS. She sold them up and down the Row for a copper toll each. The Row bought them. By ten she was taking requests, and by eleven the Cokeleys had banned her from printing anything about the Cokeleys.","schooling":"The Row school under Teacher Hob Cokeley in his later years. He made the class keep a street paper, and Petra was editor four years running and once printed, with measurements, that the Damper seat on the Bench was two inches wider than the others. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a pin-punch.","history":"At sixteen the Row's bakehouse sent her down the Ember March to Hearthward to learn real bread from a Flameborne baker on the black-stone side, and she spent three years learning slapbread, Kindle, and that you can shout at someone you love and still hand them the first loaf. She came home at nineteen with a temper, a starter dough in a crock, and a habit of arguing with ovens. At twenty-four a mill whistle blew its housing beside her and burst her left eardrum, and a city fitter gave her the ear; she says it hears bread rising. She took over the ration-bakehouse at twenty-seven and turned out a better loaf than Ironwatch had any right to. Her partner Fen Cordwain mends boots and is as quiet as she isn't; their son Nico is eight. When the paper street names began going missing, she started reprinting them on her old press and nailing them back. Ironwatch's quartermaster pulls them down, because his soldiers keep getting lost following old-name directions, which Petra considers the whole point.","family":"Fen and Nico; a mother who still lives in the Gudgeon house; a feud with the Damper end over the Long Pot that both sides enjoy too much to end.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"She heard one of the Emberwarden's speeches repeated in a Hearthward bakehouse and laughed so hard she dropped a full tray, and has wanted to be that loud in public ever since."},"band":{"act":"No Knee","realm":"Flameborne","why":"The bakers'-street rap crew at war with Lightforge; she learned the words with her hands in dough at Hearthward.","rv_vote":"No Knee, and she'll shout their name from the Bench."},"vote":{"local":"Commons, loudly, and Ada Lathe for speaker.","edict":"Against. Flameborne riots against it, and she'd riot with them if the Row would hold her coat."},"wants":"To bake a single loaf the length of the whole Long Pot, from the Gudgeon end to the Damper end, and make them share it.","site":"furnacerow","town":"Furnace Row","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:irontown_vf2":{"name":"Ilsabet Peck","age":33,"role":"village store-keeper in the pipe-fields; born Ilsabet Whitlow, a tanner's daughter from Lampwick on the Lightforge Rim","look":"Pale and freckled the way only noon-raised people are, sooted now at the hairline. A little Lampwick antler-chip lamp hangs at her belt, never lit.","voice":"Precise; she still ends sentences with how she knows a thing, the way Clear does: 'I saw it', 'so I'm told'.","greet":"Shift. That's right, isn't it? Eleven years and I still check. Potatoes, packs, bread. What do you need?","lines":["The Reactor Halls took more heat again. The far rows are cold; I walked them this morning, so I know.","At home they'd call this place dark. It isn't. It's lit from underneath. You get used to light coming up instead of down.","Everyone here smiles about the Edict. I'm from Lightforge. I could tell you what it's like when they come to your door."],"about":"I was a Whitlow, from Lampwick on the Rim, and I married a potato farmer from the pipe-fields because he made me laugh at a Hearthward fair. Now I keep the store. I still keep a Day-Book. Nobody here reads it, which is very restful.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"good, with an accent the children copy"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Ilsabet grew up in a tannery family in Lampwick, where the Rim has a real dusk, and every Lightforge house has a back window so you can see right through it. Hers looked out over the tanning pits and past them to the scrubland, and from that window, most evenings, she watched lamps being shaded and people gathering in the half-dark who weren't supposed to. Her grandmother kept a Day-Book with whole pages left blank, which in Lampwick is nearly the same as a confession, and nobody ever asked her about them. At her First Telling at twelve Ilsabet stood in front of the neighbours and said she was frightened of noon. They gave her a told name for it, Ilsabet Who Likes the Evening, and her mother didn't speak to her for two days.","schooling":"The Rim lamp-school under Teacher Corin Lampwright, who taught letters by the shadows his hand made on the wall, which wasn't strictly allowed. She learned Clear's evidential endings beautifully and failed geometry twice. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered. Lampwright let her stay after lessons and make shadow-letters of her own on the back wall, and never wrote it in his Day-Book. She left at fourteen for the tannery vats.","history":"She worked her family's vats until twenty-one, when she went down to the Hearthward fair with a cart of tanned bellows-hides and came back engaged. Dunstan Peck had been selling pipe-baked potatoes from a brazier and telling the worst jokes she had ever heard, and she laughed at every one. They were Linked at twenty-two and she came east, eleven years ago, the winter after the Feast, so she has heard the Feast story perhaps four hundred times and never once eaten a potato from it. She learned Shiftcant by shopping. At twenty-eight she took over the village store from Dunstan's aunt. She still writes her Day-Book every night, in Clear, and keeps it on the counter where anyone could read it, and in eleven years only Lazlo Riddle ever has. This season her mother's letters from Lampwick have got shorter: the lamp-checks have reached the Rim, the Penumbrists are being registered, and her cousin Merrit was audited and has stopped writing at all.","family":"Dunstan, who still makes her laugh; their daughters Clemmie, nine, and little Aure, six. Her widowed mother is still in Lampwick and won't come east.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"The first Penumbrist, who said mercy needs shade. Ilsabet grew up watching from a back window the people who believed it."},"band":{"act":"Two Windows","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A Lightforge girl and a Shadowdeep man singing together. She knows the song isn't about her, and plays it as if it were.","rv_vote":"Two Windows, and she'll lamp them from Metalcrown with a straight face."},"vote":{"local":"She has never worked out the Metalcrown system; she signs what Dunstan signs, and reads it first.","edict":"Against, quietly and completely. She is a Penumbrist in a village that thinks the word is a kind of potato."},"wants":"To get her mother east before the lamp-checks reach the tanneries.","site":"irontown_vf2","town":"Iron Throne City Farms 3","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:irontown_vf2":{"name":"Maddox Flange","age":49,"role":"pipe-fitter and welder for the waste-heat lines; smith for whatever else breaks in the fields","look":"Wide and ruddy from pipe-heat, a fitted right foot after a scald, heavy gloves he never takes off.","voice":"Hearty and anecdotal; laughs before the punchline; tells the Feast story too often and too well.","greet":"Shift! Cold morning, so you've come to the warm end. Sit on a valve. They don't mind.","lines":["The Feast, twelve winters back. Pipe went, the whole crop baked in the ground, we ate for a month. Best winter of my life. Don't ask me which pipe.","Reactor Halls are taking heat for their new hall. Far rows went cold Tuesday. Hal's raging. Hal's always raging, but this time he's right.","Foot's fitted. Scald off a seam. Tally plate says 'right foot' with a line through it. Best line I own."],"about":"I fit and weld the heat lines under the fields, and I smith whatever else breaks. I've been under these rows more than I've been on them. Hal Brazier turns the wheels; I mend what's behind them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"}],"childhood":"The Flanges were flange-makers on the city crest who came down the hill when the city first began burying its spent heat in iron pipe, and Maddox grew up in the pipe-workers' huts at the edge of the steaming rows, digging in warm earth in the middle of winter. His father laid pipe and taught him to listen to it with an iron rod against the ear, the other end on the line. At seven Maddox heard a hiss nobody else could, under Row Four, and when they dug there was a pinhole leak exactly where he said. After that the crews called him the fields' little ear and lifted him onto their shoulders to walk the rows. He believed for years that the ground talked only to him.","schooling":"The fields' school in the old pump-house, under Teacher Orla Hackett, who held every winter lesson outdoors on whatever patch of the rows was warmest that day; the warm patch wandered, so the class wandered with it, carrying their slates. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a listening rod, which he still carries.","history":"He laid pipe at fourteen and welded by twenty. At twenty-six a seam blew back on him and scalded his right foot to the bone, and he walked out of a city fitting-house on an iron one a season later, telling jokes about it within the week. At thirty he married Bryony, who grows the fields' seed potatoes, and they have three children. Twelve winters ago he made the junction weld under Row Nine at the very end of a shift, cold and quick, because his first daughter had been born that afternoon and he wanted to be home. It burst a week later and cooked the whole season's crop in the ground. The village ate baked potatoes for a month and called it the Feast, and Maddox has told the story every winter since as the luckiest thing that ever happened to the fields. Now, with the Reactor Halls taking more heat and leaving the far rows cold, he and Hal Brazier spend their nights working out how to lay a new line to the far end before the city takes the rest.","family":"Bryony and their three children; the eldest, Lilly, born the day of the bad weld, is twelve and asks for the Feast story every Valve Day.","hero":{"who":"Hal Brazier, the valve-master","why":"Hal has turned every wheel in the fields by hand and knows which ones stick, and so, says Maddox, does Hal's temper."},"band":{"act":"Shift Change","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Three singers who never share a stage, like him and Bryony: one works days, one works nights, and the children are the chorus.","rv_vote":"Shift Change."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion, loosely; the Dominus pays for pipe. The Reactor Halls taking the heat has him doubting for the first time.","edict":"A smile. He doesn't much care."},"wants":"To lay one more line to the far rows before the Reactor Halls take all the heat.","site":"irontown_vf2","town":"Iron Throne City Farms 3","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:irontown_vf2":{"name":"Elspeth Stover","age":88,"role":"retired; widow of the man who laid the first heat lines; winner of the warmest patch on Valve Day for thirty years running","look":"Tiny and bent, a walking stick topped with a little brass valve-wheel, white hair under a wool cap. Born-whole; her plate says ALL, and someone has scratched 'near enough' beneath it.","voice":"Sly and whispery, a crackling laugh; pretends to be deafer than she is.","greet":"Eh? Oh, shift. Come closer, I don't bite. I've not got the teeth for it.","lines":["Valve Day's coming. Thirty years I've found the warmest patch. It's the feet. Old feet know.","Far rows are cold. Hal says the Reactor Halls. I say Hal left the wheel on Row Twelve a quarter-turn shut. Ask him.","I like that Deep Hold lot. 'Ask who's asking.' I ask it at my door every day."],"about":"Eighty-eight. I came down to these fields when they were only pipes. My husband laid the first lines and I carried his tea. I pick first every harvest and they all think I cheat. I don't. I remember.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Knock-taps","level":"fluent, through rock or pipe"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Elspeth was born a Tapley, eldest of five in a stove-mender's family on the city's lower terraces who had never quite stopped being Stonehold: they knocked messages to each other through the walls in the old rock-tap code and said 'Set' at the door. When she was eight there wasn't enough to feed five, so her parents sent her down the Ember March to a great-aunt who kept lodgings on the granite side of Hearthward. She stayed four years. She learned to cook food heavy enough that guests apologised for leaving, to lift her great-aunt's burden-stone without being told, and to hear a thing knocked through a wall from three rooms away. When she came home at twelve she had a Stonehold walk and a habit of tapping on anything iron, and she never lost either.","schooling":"Two schools. At Hearthward, a Stonehold road-school taught on the march between the mile-markers by Teacher Harl Dunnet, who set reading lessons in the initials carved on the stones and made each child carry their own slate the whole way. Then a season at a city shift-school at twelve, where she was older than everyone and bored. Lamp Test at eight, at Hearthward: nothing. First Tool late, at twelve, a stove-lid lifter.","history":"At nineteen she married Corwin Stover, and his trade-name became hers. He was one of the first crew laying the city's heat lines under the slag, and he couldn't draw a straight line to save his life, so Elspeth drew the layouts for him, in chalk and then scratched into the backs of old stove lids, which were the only flat iron in the house. She carried his tea out to the rows every noon for twenty-nine years. They had three children, and now there are nine grandchildren and two great-grandchildren working the fields. Corwin died when she was fifty-eight. The first Valve Day after, she walked the rows at dawn with everyone else and stood on the line she knew he'd laid first, and it was the warmest patch, and she has found it every year since. When the pipe burst for the Feast she told everyone it was the Row Nine junction before a spade went in, and Maddox Flange has been bringing her potatoes ever since without being asked.","family":"Three children, nine grandchildren, two great-grandchildren; the eldest great-grandson, Pip, plays her Deep Hold until the walls shake.","hero":{"who":"The Silent Sentinel","why":"A jaw of stone, and every word carved on a slate. Somebody who only says what's worth cutting into rock. Elspeth's stove lids are her slates."},"band":{"act":"Deep Hold","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Slow heavy-flow rap; her great-grandson plays it, and 'Ask who's asking' is what her great-aunt used to say at the Hearthward door.","rv_vote":"Deep Hold, and she'd like it written down that she's the oldest fan they have."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion, Commons, she's seen both argued about for eighty years. She votes for whoever fixes Row Twelve.","edict":"Metalcrown smiles at it, they tell her. She's too old to smile because someone tells her to."},"wants":"To give the stove lids to someone who'll use them well before Hal Brazier finds out she has a better map than he does.","site":"irontown_vf2","town":"Iron Throne City Farms 3","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:irontown_vf2:b":{"name":"Lazlo Riddle","age":22,"role":"slag-rye thresher for the ration-loaf mills; a Half-Spark who writes everything down","look":"Long and narrow, slag-rye dust in his eyebrows, ink on the inside of his wrist where the tally plate rubs, spectacles mended with wire.","voice":"Eager and digressive; adds footnotes to his own sentences out loud.","greet":"Shift. Sorry, I was counting. Not the rye. Something else. What can I do for you?","lines":["Metalcrown students at Korrhaven can only audit, and they say we take the best notes. I'm going to take the best notes anyone has ever taken.","The far rows are two degrees colder since the new Reactor hall lit. I've written it down. Nobody asked me to.","At my Lamp Test the Gravem lamp flickered. Half-Spark, the tester said. Half of what, I asked. She didn't know either."],"about":"I thresh slag-rye for the loaf mills and I write down everything: temperatures, yields, who said what on the Valve Day walk. I tested Half-Spark, which in Metalcrown mostly means a story at weddings. I want to go to Korrhaven and sit in the Glass Library annex and be mocked for my notes.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"reads it well, speaks it stiffly"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads a little, taught by Ilsabet Peck"}],"childhood":"The Riddles make riddles, the big round sieves that sort slag-rye from grit, and Lazlo's childhood was one long sorting. Grain from stones, buttons from nails, words from other words. At six he was making lists of everything in the house and pinning them to the wall, and his family, large and cheerful and mostly unable to read, admired the lists enormously without knowing what was on them. At eleven he sat his mother down at the threshing table every evening for a winter and taught her to read from the stamped marks on ration-loaves, and she read her first whole sentence off a flour sack and then made him read it back to check. He still has the sack.","schooling":"The pump-house school under Teacher Orla Hackett, very old by then, who gave him her own battered Concord primer in Bridge and told him to steal more books than he was given. At his Lamp Test at eight the Gravem lamp flickered, and the tester called it Half-Spark. Nobody in the pipe-fields knew what to do with one. He did a single year of Lamp Years in the city before his father's back went and he came home. First Tool at ten, a riddle-frame.","history":"He came home at thirteen to sieve, and threshed from fifteen, and has filled forty-one notebooks in nine years, mostly about rye and heat. At twenty he wrote to the Glass Library annex at Korrhaven asking to audit and had a reply in a clerk's hand: Metalcrown auditors are accepted with a sponsor's seal. He is saving for the journey, but his pay is in shift-scrip good only at the foundry store, so he's turning it into coin through Garnet Spindle at the foot of Refusal Row, at a rate she calls generous and he has calculated is not. Ilsabet Peck caught him reading her Day-Book on the counter, the only person who ever has, and instead of being cross she began teaching him Clear letters. Last winter, hoping for a sponsor, he started sending his heat notes to a clerk at the Synthesis Council, hour by hour, row by row, beautifully kept.","family":"His father Tobit, the sieve-maker, whose back is bad; his mother Sib, a proud late reader; four sisters, all older, all of whom call him 'the Notebook'.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"'The Lamp That Leaned': a copyist who kept what other people threw away. Lazlo thinks keeping things is a kind of work nobody pays for."},"band":{"act":"Echo Bleed","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A DJ who samples dead singers. To Lazlo it's the most beautiful thing anyone has ever done with an archive.","rv_vote":"Echo Bleed, in the Sparkbeat Cup."},"vote":{"local":"He is the only person in the fields who has read both the Synthesis Council's charter and the old Foundry Commons rules, and he is writing a comparison nobody will read.","edict":"Torn. A register is a kind of notebook. He loves notebooks, and he is afraid of this one."},"wants":"A sponsor's seal for the Glass Library annex at Korrhaven.","site":"irontown_vf2","town":"Iron Throne City Farms 3","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:ironwatch_vf1":{"name":"Benedikt Cullet","age":55,"role":"village store-keeper; buys the Yard's broken slag-glass for the glasshouses; sells sour cabbage by the crock to Stonehold-road drivers","look":"Stooped but wide in the shoulder, fingernails tinted green from forty years of glass dust. A fitted left ear with a small brass horn.","voice":"A grumbling haggler, warm underneath; Holdspeech from the drivers has crept into his stresses.","greet":"Shift. Cabbage in the crock, packs on the hook, bread's yesterday's and priced like it.","lines":["The Yard used to give us its broken glass. Now it charges us. For broken glass. I've asked them to charge us for their smoke too. Fair's fair.","Stonehold drivers buy my cabbage by the crock. They say it's the only thing east of the slag-hills heavy enough to be honest.","You can see through our houses. Doesn't mean you can see in. That's not a saying. It's a warning."],"about":"I keep the store and haggle with the Yard over broken glass, which makes me the most hated man at Ironwatch and the most loved one here. I sell crocks to the Stonehold drivers. I like drivers. They leave.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"}],"childhood":"Benedikt was a cullet-carter's boy, cullet being broken glass, and when the Yard still gave its spoiled panes away for nothing his father carted them down the slope twice a day in an open wagon. From the age of five Benedikt rode on top of the load, sitting on a heap of jagged green glass, and he never once cut himself. The village decided this was luck and started paying his father extra to bring the boy along, as if he kept the glass in one piece. He grew up between the Yard and the glasshouses and belonged to neither: too much glass for the soldiers, too much Yard for the growers. He talked to the drivers instead, because they were the only ones who'd treat a boy on a glass heap as ordinary.","schooling":"The village school, held in a glasshouse every winter, under Teacher Morwen Lintel, who grew a seedling for each child and made them keep its diary. Benedikt's died in the second week, and he wrote its funeral diary, day by day, for the rest of the term; she gave him top marks. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a glass-pick.","history":"He carted cullet until twenty-five, when the Yard first began charging and the work dried up, and at thirty he took over the store and turned himself into the village's negotiator with the Yard, which nobody else wanted to be. He has fought the price for fifteen years. One season he paid entirely in sour cabbage, crocks of it stacked in the Yard-master's office until the soldiers' breath could strip paint. At twenty-eight he Linked with Gudrun, a Stonehold driver's daughter, who tried the glass village for six years and then went back to the road; they opened their Link chain by agreement and have written every month since. He has an understanding with the Stonehold drivers: they carry his crocks down the road and bring back news, and he knows more about the Hold-Senate's arguments than anyone at Ironwatch. Lately, the glass he buys has been strangely good for glass that's meant to be broken.","family":"Gudrun, on the Stonehold road, by letter. No children. A sister in the city who thinks he's wasted on cabbage.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired and Hollis Brask","why":"Two people who never married and built something that's stood twelve hundred years. He likes to think he and Gudrun have built something like it out of letters."},"band":{"act":"Warmstone","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Gudrun's favourite. He plays it badly on a comb when the store is empty.","rv_vote":"Warmstone, so he can tell Gudrun he did."},"vote":{"local":"A Dominion man who hates the Yard: he wrote the petition to the Synthesis Council against Ironwatch's Yard-master himself, and collected every signature in the village.","edict":"Smiling. Lightforge will need glass for all those lamps, and somebody has to sell it."},"wants":"Free broken glass again, for the village, the way it was when he rode the wagon.","site":"ironwatch_vf1","town":"Ironwatch Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:ironwatch_vf1":{"name":"Ragna Muntin","age":42,"role":"the glasshouse smith: forges the muntins, the bars between panes, and most of the Long Glass's frame","look":"Broad, sun-blotched from working under glass, hair under a green scarf. Born-whole but for a fitted right thumb.","voice":"Deadpan; reads bits of her brother's letters aloud for effect and doesn't laugh at them.","greet":"Shift. I make the bars that hold the glass. If you need a hinge, a hook or a horseshoe, I make those too, under protest.","lines":["My brother's pane in the Long Glass is broken. He's in Stormreach marrying a woman on a roof. He wrote 'don't fix it yet'.","The Yard's put the broken glass up again. Ben's furious. Ben's furious in a very organised way.","Nell Vise scratched someone else's name into a pane once. I've seen the pane. I'm not telling you whose."],"about":"I'm the glasshouse smith. I forge the muntins, the bars between the panes, and I've forged most of the Long Glass's frame. My brother Ottar left for Stormreach and writes me rude letters. I keep his pane's frame empty. That's all the sentiment you'll get out of me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"halting, entirely from her brother's letters"}],"childhood":"Ragna and her brother Ottar, three years younger, were the muntin-maker's children, and the village called the Muntins its bones, because without their bars the glass would fall in. Their favourite game was Titans. They had heard from a Frostgrave carter that the Titans take one step a season, so they would cross the whole village as slowly as they could bear, one foot an hour, while their mother timed them with a pot of water left to boil. Ottar always cheated and ran the last stretch. Ragna never did. Once, at nine, she took a whole afternoon to cross the yard, and their mother brought her supper where she stood and fed it to her without a word, which Ragna still counts as the best meal of her life.","schooling":"The village glasshouse school under Teacher Morwen Lintel, who by then had her pupils keep weather diaries instead of seedling diaries. Ragna's diary, at sixteen, recorded a grey-white wall of cloud on the Frostgrave road an hour before the great hail, and she ran to ring the yard bell. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a bar-bender. Pane Day at ten, her pane near the east end.","history":"She rang the bell the night of the hail, twenty-six years ago, and so the village had an hour to carry seedlings indoors instead of none; she does not tell this part, and nobody else remembers it was her. She apprenticed to her father and took the forge at thirty. At thirty-five a frame-press closed on her right thumb and a Rivet Hollow fitter replaced it, which she says improved her grip and her vocabulary. She never married and doesn't intend to. Ottar left at twenty-four to work the Chainyards in Stormreach, where chain-wrights are the only people allowed to stay put, and by custom his pane in the Long Glass was broken the day he went. He writes every month, in Bridge with Gale shouted through it, about storm-chains and the woman he's courting on a roof. His last letter said he was marrying her in a storm wedding, and then, at the bottom, in smaller letters: 'don't fix it yet'. Ragna has read that line more times than she would admit.","family":"Ottar, in Stormreach. Their parents are both dead and said at the Long Whistle; their mother's water-pot is on Ragna's stove.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"A Seer who knotted down what she saw coming. Ragna wrote down a hailstorm once and rang a bell. She likes people who write things down and are mostly ignored."},"band":{"act":"The Tether Sisters","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Ottar sent her the words to 'Cut the Chain' copied out in his awful hand.","rv_vote":"The Tether Sisters, for Ottar's sake and to annoy him."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion, drily. The Dominus answers letters; the Yard doesn't.","edict":"Smiling, mostly because Ottar's Stormreach is rebelling against it and his letters about it are the best he's ever written."},"wants":"To fit Ottar's pane back into the Long Glass herself, on the day he walks in.","site":"ironwatch_vf1","town":"Ironwatch Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:ironwatch_vf1":{"name":"Cuthbert Putty","age":67,"role":"beet and cabbage grower; famous for saving the seedlings in the great hail","look":"Small, round and rosy, beard like a scrubbing brush. Born-whole, smells of soil and vinegar.","voice":"Happy and chatty; interrupts himself to point at plants.","greet":"Shift, shift! Mind the beet, it's sulking. Have you eaten? You look like you haven't eaten.","lines":["Twenty-six years ago the hail came off the Frostgrave road. Seedlings went into my bed. I slept on the floor. Thomasina saw. That was that.","The Yard wants coin for broken glass now. When I was a boy you were paid to take it away.","Nell Vise's pane? Ask her. She'll give you a look that takes a fortnight to wear off."],"about":"I grow beet and cabbage and I'm married to the best woman at Ironwatch Farms, and I've had a very good life, which I gather isn't the fashion. Sixty-seven. Nothing hurts much. Have a pickle.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Cuthbert was the putty-maker's son, and putty is what holds a pane in its frame, so he grew up smelling of linseed and chalk and being chased out of glasshouses for leaving thumbprints on the glass. At six he started talking to seedlings, crouched over the trays telling them about his day, and the adults laughed at him until they noticed his trays came up thicker than theirs. By eight, growers were asking him to come and talk to their beds, and paying him in apples. He took it entirely seriously. He would give each tray a little speech about weather and patience and what he'd had for breakfast, and once, notoriously, a long one about a dog he didn't like.","schooling":"The village school before Lintel's day, under Teacher Grisel Tacker, who kept a hen in the schoolroom and settled every dispute by which slate it pecked first. Cuthbert won a great many disputes by keeping grain in his pocket. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a putty knife. Pane Day at ten.","history":"He worked the glasshouses all his life, steady and cheerful, and by forty-one the village had decided he would never marry, which didn't trouble him. Then came the hail. Half the Long Glass broke in a night, and in the scramble Cuthbert carried tray after tray of seedlings into his own house and filled his bed with them and slept on the floor beside them for three weeks while the panes were replaced. Thomasina Strop, a widow who mended the Ironwatch soldiers' coats and had never once looked at him, came by on the fourth morning with a coat to return, saw a grown man asleep on the boards next to a bed full of cabbages, and laughed until she cried. They were Linked that summer. They had two sons late, both in the city now. He has grown the best beet at Ironwatch for twenty-five years, has never been fitted, has no objection to it, and has never needed it.","family":"Thomasina, sixty-four, who still mends coats and manages him; sons Petrus and Gil in the city, who visit at Valve Day and leave with crocks.","hero":{"who":"Thomasina Putty, his wife","why":"She laughed at him on the floor and then married him. He thinks that's the bravest thing anyone in the village has done."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Forty-year love songs, and they dance to them in the kitchen.","rv_vote":"Decades."},"vote":{"local":"He votes how Thomasina votes, and she votes Dominion, so he does, and is very happy about it.","edict":"He hasn't followed it. He's heard it's about sparks, and he grows beet."},"wants":"To reach forty years married, like the songs; he needs fourteen more.","site":"ironwatch_vf1","town":"Ironwatch Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:ironwatch_vf1:b":{"name":"Evander Spall","age":38,"role":"glasshouse hand; sixteen years a spark-tender in the Reactor Halls, back home two years and learning to say 'I' again","look":"Tall and quiet, both arms fitted to the shoulder in dull Reactor-Hall grey. A faint ember-orange glow shows at the seams under his shirt in the dark.","voice":"Careful and slow; catches himself saying 'this one' and corrects it to 'I' with visible effort.","greet":"Shift. This one— I. I work the glass now. What do you need?","lines":["My pane's back in the Long Glass. Third row from the east end. Nell cut it. Crooked on purpose, I think.","In the Reactor Halls you stop saying 'I' and don't notice. Here they notice. I'm grateful. Mostly.","The Yard's glass costs coin now. I could tell you why. I used to work for the people who want the furnace heat."],"about":"I left at twenty, went to the city, and tended reactor sparks for sixteen years. They fitted me a piece at a time, because it was efficient. I came home two years ago. I'm learning to say 'I' again. It's harder than glass.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"a few words, from Warm workers in the Halls"}],"childhood":"The Spalls break up the big slabs of slag-glass into usable chips, a loud, dusty trade, and Evander was the dreamy one in a family of practical people. He climbed onto the roof of the Long Glass at dusk to look north at the city's chimneys and the orange line of the Smeltway, and he'd be fetched down by a sister and climb straight back up. He wanted to be up there, where the lights were. On his Pane Day at ten he scratched his name into his first pane so hard that a hair-crack ran from the S to the corner. His grandmother looked at it for a long time and said that meant he'd leave. He was delighted.","schooling":"The village glasshouse school under Teacher Morwen Lintel, who told him he was good at sums but held his pencil 'like you're trying to escape with it'. He spent most lessons looking north through the glass roof at the city's chimneys, and Lintel let him, so long as he could tell her afterwards how many were smoking. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a slab-breaker's hammer.","history":"He left at twenty for the Reactor Halls, and by custom his pane was broken the week he went. He tended synthetic sparks in the long halls, and was good at it. At twenty-four the heat took his left arm and it was fitted; at twenty-seven, the right; at thirty-one a plate went into his chest to help a scarred lung. Each fitting made the work faster, and he took each one as a promotion. His letters home got shorter, and his mother noticed before he did that they had stopped having the word 'I' in them. At thirty-five, standing at a console, he heard himself tell a junior that 'this one will see to it', and he couldn't remember the last time he'd said anything else. He finished the shift and walked home, all the way, two days. At the village edge he asked Nell Vise for a new pane. She cut it that week. He has worked the glasshouses since, and goes back to the city twice a year to have his parts tended, and each time he comes home a little quieter and takes a week to get his 'I' back.","family":"His mother Gunhild, who kept every short letter; three sisters in the glass trade who correct him gently whenever he slips, and two nieces who correct him much less gently.","hero":{"who":"The Lost Warden","why":"A hero nobody can say anything certain about. For years nobody could say anything about Evander either, including Evander."},"band":{"act":"Lights Out","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"He went to one of their sets once, in total dark, and for an hour nobody could see his arms.","rv_vote":"Lights Out."},"vote":{"local":"He was a Dominion man without thinking. Now he's trying to work out an opinion that has 'I' in it, and hasn't yet.","edict":"Against, carefully: he distrusts anything that counts what you are instead of what you choose."},"wants":"To get through one whole day without saying 'this one'.","site":"ironwatch_vf1","town":"Ironwatch Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:steelgate_vf1":{"name":"Marjory Saltbox","age":60,"role":"salter of pork and keeper of the farms' store; sells salt meat down the Marches","look":"Spare and leathery, sleeves rolled over salt-cracked forearms. A fitted left knee she calls 'the new girl'.","voice":"Bone-dry and slow; every sentence stops sooner than you expect.","greet":"Shift. Ham, lard, bread, packs. I'd say the ham's good. It is. Saves time.","lines":["Steelgate wants the sheds moved further from the gate. The smell. I said the smell's money. They didn't laugh.","Rivet Hollow brats came for piglets again Tuesday. I gave them a ham to go away. Works every time. Bad parenting.","Gerda Mangle's furious about the hog-naming. Every year. Never seen a woman so cross at a compliment."],"about":"I salt pork. My mother salted pork. I've a knee that's fitted and a mouth that isn't. Sixty years. Not much to tell. Plenty to buy.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few words, from Frostgrave fur-traders"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"reads it well, signs nothing; nobody knows"}],"childhood":"Marjory grew up in the salting house, among barrels and hanging hams, and says her childhood was packed in salt: tasted fine, kept forever. Her father had been born without speech and talked with a set of hand-signs he had made up himself as a boy, and the family learned them before they learned Shiftcant. Her mother did the talking for all of them, at the counter, at the mill, at the Long Whistle, and talked enough for three. So Marjory learned early that words in that house were her mother's and hands were her father's, and kept very few of either for herself. At nine she won a bet with her brothers by going a full week without saying a word, and her father signed that he was proud of her, and her mother didn't notice for four days.","schooling":"Steelgate's mill-school, years before Rennart Coke, under Teacher Belva Quench, who made pupils answer in as few words as possible and docked a mark for every word too many. Marjory won every term, and once answered a whole history question with 'Mill. Fire. Rebuilt.' and got full marks and a long look. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a salting fork.","history":"She took the salting house at twenty-five when her mother's heart slowed, and she has salted Steelgate's pork for thirty-five years since. At twenty-seven she married Edwin, a drover on the Frostgrave road who left every autumn with salt meat and came back every spring with furs and news; it suited them both. At fifty-five he died of a stopped heart three days north on the road, and the Frostgrave fur-traders he'd drunk with for twenty years carried him home on their own sled and would not take a crossing for it. She still sells to them, at cost. Her knee went at fifty-two and the new girl has been fine since. Nine years ago she was at the mill delivering lard for the rollers when the sow got loose and ran the length of the rolling floor, and she laughed so long and so loudly that the rolling-master, Gerda Mangle, has never forgiven her, which Marjory considers a fair price.","family":"Two brothers, both fitted at the mill, both talkers. No children. Edwin at the Long Whistle every dusk, and her father too, in his own hand-sign.","hero":{"who":"Vaelora Lanthys","why":"She chose silence, in Council. Marjory respects anyone who can manage it in company."},"band":{"act":"Permafrost","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"The fur-traders took her to a Permafrost set; the fans stand absolutely still, and so did she, for the first time in a crowd without wanting to leave.","rv_vote":"Permafrost."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion. The Dominus doesn't talk much either.","edict":"For, briefly. Fewer surprises."},"wants":"To sell salt pork to Lightforge, who refuse sauce but surely eat ham.","site":"steelgate_vf1","town":"Steelgate Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"smith:steelgate_vf1":{"name":"Lucan Plate","age":37,"role":"farm smith: crackling sheets, shed gates, troughs, and exceptionally good latches; once a Steelgate millhand","look":"Lanky and fair, burn-freckled, a fitted left thumb in bright steel he fiddles with constantly. Laughs too loud.","voice":"Nervous, over-explains, laughs at the wrong moments.","greet":"Shift! Sorry. Loud. Mill habit. What're you after? Sheets, hooks, gates. Good gates. Gates that shut.","lines":["'Pig on the floor,' they say at the mill when something goes wrong. Funny phrase. I've no idea who started it.","Steelgate wants the sheds moved. I'll move them if they pay. Iron doesn't mind the smell and neither do I.","I like The Glare. Yes, Lightforge. Yes, arena rock. Leave me alone about it."],"about":"I was a millhand at Steelgate till nine years ago. Now I smith for the farms: the crackling sheets, the shed gates, the troughs. Best gates in Metalcrown. Latches that hold. I'm very particular about latches.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fair"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a few words from The Glare's choruses, mispronounced"}],"childhood":"Lucan was the youngest of five loud boys in a mill family three generations deep in the plate-rollers, and the phrase of his childhood was 'Lucan, the door!' He left every door open. Oven doors, coop doors, the front door in winter. He'd be thinking of something else and simply walk through. When his mother worked nights he slept in the mill canteen under his father's coat, and he loved the mill's sound so much that he couldn't sleep in the quiet farmhouse of an aunt he was sent to one summer, and was sent home early. He grew up gangly and eager to please and was always half a step behind his brothers, and he laughed loudest at their jokes so they'd keep him around.","schooling":"Steelgate mill-school under Teacher Rennart Coke, who made Lucan door-monitor for a full year to cure him of leaving them open. It didn't take. He liked the annual march across the rolling floor and once stopped in the middle to look at the rollers until Coke came back for him. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a plate gauge.","history":"He went on the mill at fourteen and the rolling floor at twenty. At twenty-five a plate edge crushed his left thumb and the mill paid for the steel one, which he still finds fascinating. At twenty-eight, on a night shift, he propped the scrap-yard gate open with a brick so he could slip out and meet Hesta, a pig-girl from the farms, and when he came back the gate was wide and a sow was halfway down the rolling floor with the night shift chasing her. Nobody was hurt. The mill stopped for half a shift. The rolling-master investigated for a month and never found out who. Lucan left the mill a month later 'for the farm air', became the farms' smith, married Hesta, and has made the most over-engineered latches in the Realm ever since. They have two small children. Every time someone at the mill says 'pig on the floor', which is daily, he laughs too loud.","family":"Hesta, who knows, and finds it hilarious; their children Bettony, five, and Wat, three. Four brothers at the mill who still call him 'the door'.","hero":{"who":"Veilrend","why":"Shadowdeep's First Blade, with no known face. Lucan would very much like to have done one thing in his life without his face attached to it."},"band":{"act":"The Glare","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Loud, shiny, a bit stupid, and everybody sings. He's embarrassed and he doesn't care.","rv_vote":"The Glare, and he knows exactly what the mill will say."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion by habit. 'Inefficiency kills children,' says the Dominus; Lucan once stopped a whole mill with one gate, and he's been very efficient ever since.","edict":"Smiling, because that's what you do."},"wants":"For one year to pass in which nobody at Steelgate says 'pig on the floor'.","site":"steelgate_vf1","town":"Steelgate Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:steelgate_vf1":{"name":"Merrow Currier","age":45,"role":"tanner of bellows hides and roller belts for Steelgate's mill","look":"Compact and shaven-headed, both hands fitted to the wrist in blackened steel with leather grips, an apron stiff as board.","voice":"Matter-of-fact and efficient; sometimes leaves 'I' out without noticing.","greet":"Shift. Hides on the rack are for Steelgate's bellows. Not for sale. Anything else, ask Marjory.","lines":["Tanning eats hands. Mine went at thirty. These don't burn. Three times the hides my mother managed. Not a boast. Arithmetic.","Steelgate wants the sheds moved. The smell is the price of bellows. Steelgate likes bellows.","Lightforge's Edict? Good law. Somebody's written down what everyone has. Metalcrown did it first, with tally plates."],"about":"Tanner. Bellows hides for the mill, belts for the rollers. Hands were fitted at thirty; the vats had the old ones. Work's better since. People here think that's a sad story. It isn't one.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it slowly; has studied the Edict's text"}],"childhood":"Merrow was born beside the tanning pits at the bottom of the farms, where the hides from the pig sheds and the mill's old bellows go to be cured, and the first job they ever had was rubbing salve into their mother's hands at night. Dulcibel Currier's fingers were already yellow and split by then, and Merrow, at six, worked the salve into every crack. At the vats nobody ever asked whether Merrow was a boy or a girl, and Merrow never offered; it simply didn't come up among people who all looked the same in a leather apron. What Merrow loved was counting: hides in, hides out, days to cure. At ten they chalked a chart on the tannery wall of hides per day for a whole year, and their mother left it there until it flaked.","schooling":"The farms' school in the lard-house under Teacher Ulrica Tallow, who taught letters by having the children read the brand-marks on hides, so Merrow learned the alphabet as Steelgate's stock marks and could name every farm on the Marches by its brand before they could spell their own name; Tallow thought that was the right order. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. First Tool at ten, a fleshing knife.","history":"Into the vats at thirteen, and good at the work from the first week. By thirty the tanning liquor had eaten Merrow's hands the way it had eaten their mother's, and the Synthesis Council was offering half-price fittings to trades it judged efficient. Merrow took both hands, and within a season was tanning three times the hides with none of the pain. Dulcibel refused the same offer, kept her own ruined hands, and died at sixty-one unable to hold a cup; Merrow thinks of that as pure waste, and says so, and cried at the Long Whistle for a year. Since then Merrow has been a Dominion loyalist with a reason. Last spring a Lightforge hide-buyer left a copy of the Radiant Edict's text at the vats, and Merrow read it six times and found it beautiful: a clean mechanism for knowing what everyone has. They are writing a proposal to the Synthesis Council for a spark-line to be added to every Metalcrown tally plate.","family":"No partner. A very old dog called Bellows. Two cousins in the mill who think Merrow works too hard.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand","why":"He wrote a law that will actually work. Merrow doesn't have to like Lightforge to admire a good mechanism."},"band":{"act":"Seismic","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Bass you feel instead of hear. The fitted hands pick it up through the table.","rv_vote":"Seismic, in the Sparkbeat Cup."},"vote":{"local":"Dominion, signs every petition, and would sign a tenth tomorrow.","edict":"For. A good register prevents waste, and waste is the only thing Merrow truly hates."},"wants":"For the Synthesis Council to accept the proposal for a spark-line on every tally plate.","site":"steelgate_vf1","town":"Steelgate Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"vill:steelgate_vf1:b":{"name":"Barnaby Mangle","age":20,"role":"pig-man at the Warm Sheds; son of Steelgate's rolling-master, and the reason the heaviest hog is named after her","look":"Huge, soft-spoken, pink-cheeked, nearly always with a piglet tucked under one arm. Born-whole, sunburnt across the nose.","voice":"Gentle and slow, deadpan funny, devoted to pigs.","greet":"Shift. Mind Gerda, she nips. The pig. Not Mum. Well.","lines":["Heaviest hog this year'll be a big one. I've been feeding her apples. Mum's going to hate it.","Rivet Hollow sends apprentices to steal piglets. Lark Swage got one as far as the canyon road last month. I let her get that far. It was a good run.","The mill says 'pig on the floor' when something goes wrong. I was about that night. I like pigs better than floors."],"about":"Pig-man at the Warm Sheds. Twenty. My mum's the rolling-master at Steelgate, and the heaviest hog of every year gets named after her, which she says is my fault. It is my fault. I like pigs and I like my mum, so it seemed nice.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few words, all from Wahai! songs"}],"childhood":"Barnaby grew up in the Steelgate mill quarters with his mother Gerda, who was a rising rolling-hand then and raised him alone, strictly, on a timetable posted by the door. He was a big, slow, gentle child in a place built for speed, and the mill kept startling him. Every summer she sent him to his uncle at the farms, and he spent those summers lying in the straw of the Warm Sheds with the sows, who didn't startle him at all. At eleven he asked to stay for good. Gerda read his request, which he'd written out properly, and signed it at the kitchen table with a face like a rolled plate, and drove him down herself, and didn't speak the whole way, and hugged him at the shed door so hard he dropped his bag.","schooling":"Steelgate mill-school under Teacher Rennart Coke until eleven; Coke let him stand by the door during the march across the rolling floor. Then the farms' lard-house school under Teacher Ulrica Tallow, where he did well at everything involving animals and nothing else. Lamp Test at eight: nothing, and he asked whether pigs ever took it. First Tool at ten, a pig-board.","history":"He walked down to the farms for good on a night nine years ago, carrying his bag past the mill's scrap-yard, and saw a man he knew by sight prop the yard gate open with a brick and slip away. He thought nothing of it until the next morning, when the whole valley was laughing about a sow on the rolling floor. He has never told anyone. At fourteen he helped with his first autumn Weighing, and when the heaviest hog came off Steelgate's plate-scale he wrote GERDA on her tag in chalk, as a joke, because the hog was large, determined and impossible to move. The farms made it custom on the spot. His mother has been furious about it for six years and has come to every single Weighing. He runs the piglet defence against the Rivet Hollow apprentices, and lets Lark Swage get further every time, and nobody on the farms is fooled.","family":"His mother Gerda, rolling-master at Steelgate, who sends him a letter a week with the timetable for the coming week attached; his uncle Hamm at the farms.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"She warned them and was struck from the record. At ten Barnaby told the mill the scrap-yard latch was bad, and nobody listened to the big slow boy either."},"band":{"act":"Wahai!","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Party reggaeton with pipes. The pigs like it; he's tested.","rv_vote":"Wahai!, because they're fun and nobody else on the farms will lamp them."},"vote":{"local":"He'd vote for whoever his mum isn't voting for, then feel bad and bring her crackling.","edict":"Torn, because he doesn't understand it. He has asked several people whether pigs have sparks."},"wants":"For his mum to come to a Weighing and laugh.","site":"steelgate_vf1","town":"Steelgate Farms 2","realm":"Metalcrown"},"shop:unmadecity":{"name":"Wenn","age":47,"role":"kit-and-bread trader on the second terrace; retired Faceless","look":"Tall, long-jawed, a stranger-friendly face she was trained to wear; grey wrapped in a plum scarf that is the only colour on her stall.","voice":"Warm, easy Bridge with outsiders, the vowels of three Realms in it; drops to two-word Undertongue the second a local walks up.","greet":"Ess. Or wahai, or aure, whatever you're used to. Bread's fresh, arrows are straight, and I won't remember your face. Professional habit.","lines":["The Drowned Tunnel token's a month late. The Warden used to send it up every seventh day like a heartbeat. Somebody still knocks back, though. Nobody wants to be the one who opens that hatch.","Murmur says it carved the bottom step of the Stair. Murmur also says its bridges never sway. Walk one and tell me.","Twenty years talking to strangers for Hush. Now strangers just want arrows. It's restful."],"about":"For twenty years I was one of the faces Hush sends up, so no Hush face sticks in a foreigner's head. You learn to be pleasant and forgettable at once, which is harder than it sounds. Now I sell bread and quivers and keep my own face for myself.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, polished"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; can read slowly"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"trade phrases and every curse"}],"childhood":"Wenn was the fourth daughter of a glass-cutter on the second terrace, and the only one of the four who liked to be looked at. Her sisters ducked under the stall when buyers came; Wenn climbed onto it. Her mother, mortified, tied a bell to her ankle so she could be found and pulled back down. It did not help. By eight she could tell a Murmur buyer from a Hush one by the way they tested glass with a thumbnail, and she would say so, loudly, which in Hush is very nearly a crime. A Keeper two doors up, an old man who smoked dried glim-cap, told her mother the girl was not broken, just pointed the wrong way, and that the city had a use for faces pointed outward.","schooling":"Well-school under a hand-cant teacher called Sabb, who made the class sit on their hands for the first week so they would feel how much they wanted to talk with them. Wenn's Lamp Test lit nothing; she remembers being relieved. At fourteen she was taken into the Faceless school above the market, where an instructor called Rhee taught her Bridge, Clear manners, and how to be liked without being memorable. She failed memory drills twice. She passed everything else.","history":"For twenty years Wenn met strangers at the top of the Ledger Stair and in the guest-halls on the first terrace, so that envoys and merchants went home remembering a pleasant woman and nothing else about Hush. She did it well. She negotiated glass tariffs with Murmur, salt with Stormreach, and once spent six days teaching a Lightforge clerk to stop writing down everything she said. He wrote to her afterwards, in Clear, through three hands. She still has the letter and has only ever read half of it. At forty-one she began to notice strangers coming back and greeting her by sight, which meant the job had worn off her; a Faceless who is remembered is retired the same week. She took her pay as a stall on the second terrace, sells kit to anyone going down the Stair or out to the Veil Roads, and has discovered she likes being bored. She owes one debt she will not discuss, cut into a low step of the Stair at her own expense.","family":"Three sisters, all in glass, who still think she shows off. Never married; she says the Faceless school ruins you for people who only know one of your faces.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"\"No Realm should ever be alone\" was the first thing a Stormreach envoy ever shouted at her, and she spent twenty years quietly agreeing with a dead Stormreacher she'd never be allowed to quote."},"band":{"act":"Two Windows","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A Lightforge–Shadowdeep couple singing to each other through a wall. She plays it on the stall's cap-box when trade is slow and tells nobody why.","rv_vote":"Two Windows, because one of them is a Shadowdeep voice and someone ought to vote for it in the open."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Ledger Well, the old debt-route families of the second terrace, by keeping her stall shut on the days their rivals hold market.","edict":"Against. Three Faceless she trained are now 'misplacing' the Lightforge register forms on the guest-hall tables, and she taught them how to look sorry about it."},"wants":"To read the second half of that letter, and to be the kind of person who doesn't need to.","site":"unmadecity","town":"Hush","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:unmadecity":{"name":"Corv","age":62,"role":"rune-floor smith and debt-carver of the Ledger Stair","look":"Short and wide, forearms speckled with old glass-burn; wears a leather bib cut from a Metalcrown furnace apron, stamped with someone else's foundry mark.","voice":"Low, gravelly Undertongue with Shiftcant swearing stuck in it; talks to his tools more kindly than to customers.","greet":"Mind your hands near the quench. What needs mending? Don't say a name. I don't want one.","lines":["Every rune in Hush's floor started as a chisel off this anvil. You walk on my work and never look down. Good. That's the trade.","The Murmur lot claim the bottom step. Tell you what, the bottom step's cut with a left-handed chisel and Hush never had a left-handed carver. Don't repeat that.","Ugly tools last. Pretty ones get stolen. Mine have never been stolen."],"about":"I make the chisels that cut light into the floor, and some days I cut debts into the Stair myself, for those who'll pay. I learned the hot trade in the Warm under Metalcrown, which upsets people here. I don't mind upsetting people. I mind blunt edges.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"good, mostly foundry words and Warm-talk"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent but stiff-fingered"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"workable"}],"childhood":"His father cut rune-floors and his mother cooked ink-eel for the terrace, and the two trades fought over the same hearth for Corv's whole childhood. The floors needed the fire hot and the eel needed it low. He grew up being handed hot iron in one hand and a ladle in the other and learning to tell, by the smell, which parent was about to win. He was a silent child even for Hush, so silent that his Well-school teacher sent home a slate asking if he could hear. He could. He just preferred listening to the hiss a chisel made going into black glass, which he could pick out from three terraces away. At ten he cut his first rune into the kitchen floor without asking. It still glows, faintly, under his sister's table.","schooling":"Well-school on the third terrace under Tamb, a teacher with no thumbs who taught hand-cant anyway and made it look easy; Corv learned to sign before he could write his letters. Lamp Test: nothing lit, not even a flicker, and he walked out whistling. No further schooling at home. At seventeen his father sent him down the Underways to the Warm beneath Metalcrown's foundries, where a Shiftcant blacksmith called Gant taught him steel properly for six years.","history":"In the Warm, Corv learned two things Hush could not teach: how to temper a chisel so it holds an edge in glass for a month, and that people who had nothing still argued about who had made what. He came home at twenty-three with a Metalcrown apron, a stack of ugly, perfect chisels and a habit of saying \"Make it\" under his breath. The rune-cutters laughed at the apron and bought every chisel. Within ten years half the floors laid in Hush were cut with his tools. At forty he was given the right to carve on the Ledger Stair, the old carver having gone blind, and he has cut perhaps three hundred debts since, always a mark, never a name. He has looked closely at the lowest steps, as no carver is meant to, and has an opinion on the Murmur quarrel he keeps for his sister's kitchen. He works alone, sleeps above the forge, and leaves the quench barrel uncovered at night so the hiss of cooling iron keeps him company.","family":"An older sister, Hab, who still cooks eel and won't let him near her fire. A son in Murmur he sees twice a year and argues bridges with on purpose.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"Gant kept a drawing of her ugly first hammer nailed over his forge in the Warm, and Corv decided anyone whose first tool was that ugly and still founded a Realm was worth copying."},"band":{"act":"Third Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"He heard them rehearsing through a vent in the Warm and the drums were the exact rhythm of a drop-hammer. He works to it in his head.","rv_vote":"Third Whistle, and he'll bet anyone that Lightforge gives Metalcrown nothing again."},"vote":{"local":"Backs no Well; carves for all of them, which is the nearest thing to a vote he trusts. When Mael read the Edict decree he carved a blank step that night and has not said why.","edict":"Against, but he thinks pocket sabotage is childish. If Hush means to wreck the lamp-checks, he'd rather they did it with a proper tool."},"wants":"To find a smith in Hush worth teaching before his hands go.","site":"unmadecity","town":"Hush","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:unmadecity":{"name":"Ilt","age":11,"role":"eel-scrap seller and Well-school pupil, carving her second Tidegiving stone","look":"Small, sharp elbows, a fringe she cuts herself with glass; ink-eel stains on both palms and a carving knife she isn't meant to have.","voice":"Fast, whispered, full of questions, always halfway into hand-cant before she remembers you might not know it.","greet":"Ess! Want eel ends? They're the best bit, the grown-ups just don't know. Two coppers. One if you don't tell my aunt.","lines":["Tidegiving's soon. You carve a secret on a stone and drop it in the deep pool. Mine's going to be so good nobody could guess it. Not that anyone reads them. They don't. Do they?","Osk's my Keeper. She hums when she walks so you think she's not listening. She's listening. She's always listening.","If you've got grain to spare, my aunt says the terrace remembers who feeds it. I'll remember too. I'm very good at remembering."],"about":"I sell the eel scraps from my aunt's pot and I go to Well-school when I can't get out of it. I've got a full name, and Osk has it, and nobody else ever will. I'm going to be a Keeper. Or a Faceless. Or the one who sweeps the stones back into the sea.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent and fast, uses kids' slang signs"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting, learned from buyers"}],"childhood":"Ilt's mother went down the Veil Roads to work a toll-gate when Ilt was four and sends coin and a carved bead every season, one bead for every year, threaded by her aunt onto a cord Ilt wears under her shirt. She lives with that aunt, who cooks ink-eel on the fourth terrace, and with three cousins who all go silent when she walks into a room because she repeats things. Ilt is not cruel with it; she simply cannot see why a thing heard should not be passed on. At seven she told the whole terrace which neighbour turned their glass bowl upside down before finishing. The neighbour stopped buying eel for a month. Ilt was given to Osk for keeping that same year, partly, the aunt admits, so someone would teach her to hold her tongue.","schooling":"Still at Well-school on the fourth terrace, under a teacher called Rusk who taught the class hand-cant by holding hands with each child under the table and signing into their palm. Ilt can sign faster than Rusk now and gets told off for slang. At her Lamp Test, last year, the shadow-glass lamp gave one weak flicker and went out; the examiner wrote 'Half, perhaps' and Ilt has been insufferable since.","history":"Ilt's life so far is short and busy. She sells eel scraps at the foot of the fourth terrace stairs every evening, keeps a hidden ledger of who buys what in scratch-marks on the underside of her stool, and has twice been caught following Faceless up to the guest-halls to study how they smile. Last Tidegiving she carved her first secret stone, about a cousin, and dropped it in the deepest pool with great ceremony. The next morning she got up before the oldest Keeper came to sweep and picked over what the tide had thrown back. She found a stone that was not hers, read it, and put it back. She has not been the same about Tidegiving since: half thrilled, half sick, and very, very careful not to mention it to Osk, who she is fairly sure knows anyway. This year she is carving her second stone and keeps changing what it says.","family":"Mother on a Veil Road toll-gate, far off; aunt and three cousins on the fourth terrace. A father she's never asked about, which in Hush is good manners.","hero":{"who":"Osk, her Keeper","why":"Osk holds forty full names and has never written one down, and Ilt cannot imagine anything harder or better. She practises humming while she walks."},"band":{"act":"Somebody","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Nobody knows who Somebody is, so it could be anyone, so it could be her, one day. She has a torn poster with the face scratched off on purpose.","rv_vote":"Somebody, and if Somebody comes second like they're meant to she'll cry anyway."},"vote":{"local":"Children don't vote, but at the table when her aunt asked who backed the Ledger Well she said nothing, very loudly, and her aunt laughed.","edict":"Against. Writing down who has a spark sounds like writing down a name, and everyone knows you don't do that."},"wants":"To be taken on as the youngest Keeper Hush has ever had.","site":"unmadecity","town":"Hush","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:unmadecity:b":{"name":"Yse","age":74,"role":"cloth-bearer to the Empty Chair, and mender of the Hollow Market awnings","look":"Thin and slightly stooped, hair cropped to grey fuzz, always carrying a folded square of black cloth; wears neither a man's wrap nor a woman's but a mender's apron with forty pockets.","voice":"Slow, dry, precise; leaves long pauses that are not for effect and calls everyone 'traveller' regardless of where they're from.","greet":"Ess, traveller. Sit if you like. Nobody stands about the market for long without someone wondering what they're selling.","lines":["Every year Hush sends a cloth to dust the Empty Chair. Thirty-one of those cloths were carried by these hands. The Chair is always clean already. Someone else dusts it too. Nobody admits it.","Last petition Metalcrown sent, the hall laughed. Every seat but one. The Hammer-Bearer stood. Thought you'd want to know someone did.","Down the Drowned Tunnel they knock back. If it's the Warden, why no token? If it's not the Warden, who learned the knock?"],"about":"I mend awnings in the Hollow Market and once a year I carry Hush's cloth to the Council floor and dust a chair nobody sits in. I'm neither a he nor a she; Hush is relaxed about that, it's names it's fussy about. I've watched the Concord argue for thirty-one years. It's mostly very boring.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, old-fashioned forms"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, formal"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"understands it well; won't speak it"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a few courtesy phrases"}],"childhood":"Yse was raised by two awning-menders who worked the Hollow Market roofs, and spent their early years mostly overhead, sitting in the rigging while their parents stitched. From up there the market was a map: who met whom, who left by which stair, which buyer came every week and never bought. Yse learned that you could know a great deal about people without hearing a word, and that nobody ever looks up. At nine they dropped a needle into a buyer's hood by accident, and the buyer, a hooded woman from the Umbral Council's own house, sent up a note by hand-cant asking for it back. Yse climbed down and returned it, and the woman signed 'neat stitches' and left. They have kept both the habit of looking down on markets and the memory of that note.","schooling":"Well-school under a teacher called Morl, who believed children should be taught to wait; the class sat in total silence for an hour each morning and learned letters only after. Yse liked it. The Lamp Test: no lamp lit, and the examiner, a tired Lightforge woman, apologised, which Yse found very strange. No more schooling after twelve; the awnings needed hands.","history":"Yse took over their parents' awnings at twenty and have stitched the Hollow Market roof ever since, every seam of it. At forty-three they were chosen, without being asked, to carry the year's cloth to the Empty Chair, because the previous bearer died and Yse's stitches were known to be neat. Nobody told them who chose. They have gone every year since, up the Veil Roads in a sealed carriage with two silent escorts, into the Council floor at an hour when it is empty, to dust a chair that is always already clean. Over thirty-one journeys they have seen envoys come and go, the Edict argued, and, this very season, the ninth Metalcrown petition laughed down while Yse waited at the back with their cloth folded. They do not have opinions in public. In private they keep a careful list of who on the Council looks at the Empty Chair while they talk, and for how long, which they deliver to nobody.","family":"A partner of fifty years, Gell, who ran a glim-house and died two winters back. No children. A nephew who keeps asking Yse to retire.","hero":{"who":"The Hammer-Bearer","why":"Yse was at the back of the hall when the Council laughed at Metalcrown's ninth petition, and saw one person stand. That was enough to make a hero of someone after seventy-four years of not having one."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"The famous forty-second silence. Yse sat through it once at Korrhaven, during a Council recess, and thinks it was the only honest thing anyone said that week.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime, and they'll fold the lamp-slip into a very small square to do it."},"vote":{"local":"Votes by how they fold the cloth: lengthways in a year they approve of the Council's silence, corner to corner in a year they don't. This year it went corner to corner.","edict":"Against. Yse watched Ardyn Solbrand argue it and noticed he never once looked at the Empty Chair."},"wants":"To find out who else dusts the Chair.","site":"unmadecity","town":"Hush","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:hollowgate":{"name":"Drav","age":58,"role":"quartermaster-trader at the hatch; the skyray rider who fell into the courtyard forty years ago","look":"Long-limbed and restless, hair striped grey from the eyebrow back in the Stormreach way; a wrist rope with a knot in it he will never let you touch.","voice":"Says too much, too quickly, then remembers where he is and stops dead mid-word, like Gale with the brakes on.","greet":"Wahai — sorry. Ess. Forty years and the mouth still lands first. What do you need? Kit's good. Bread's Frane's, so it's better.","lines":["The skyrays fly low over us on purpose. They like to hear us not answering. Mine certainly did.","They buy Stormreach chain offcuts for tunnel ladders here. Finest ladders in the Nine. My own Realm's chain, holding up Shadowdeep holes. Nobody back home would find it funny. Shame.","Tash eats standing up. Took it off drovers. Half the garrison thinks it's a code. It's not. It's just the right way to eat."],"about":"I came down in a gale on a skyray named Long Shout and broke her across the courtyard. She died; I didn't. Hollowgate never sent me home and I never asked them to. Now I sell recruits their first good boots and pretend I don't miss the wind.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, rusty"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"fluent; still sounds like a foreigner"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, a bit too emphatic"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Drav was born on a cliff-farm citadel near the Low Reach, lashed to the rock with chain his grandfather forged, and he took the First Fall at ten so eagerly he jumped before his mother had finished tying the net. He was the kind of child who ate standing up even by Stormreach standards, who ran across chain-bridges in gales to see if he could, and who shouted apologies from rooftops for things he had not done yet. At twelve he was bonded to a young skyray, a big grey female who called back to him across the steppe. He named her Long Shout because she would not stop. They flew everywhere together. Nobody in his family thought that would ever end any way except well.","schooling":"A riding-school on the Low Reach under a one-legged instructor called Corrow who taught the children to fall properly before she let them fly. Drav fell properly about two hundred times. At his Lamp Test the Tempest lamp flared bright for a moment and then dimmed to an ember; Half-Spark, the examiner said. He used his charge twice in his whole life, both times in the gale that ended his riding.","history":"At eighteen, flying a message run across the gulf, Drav and Long Shout were caught in a storm-tear and thrown down towards Shadowdeep. He spent both his charges trying to hold her up; the grey streaks above his ears date from that night. She hit the courtyard of Hollowgate and broke across it, and he woke in a dark room with a stranger signing at him. For three days a Free Assembly envoy stood under the walls shouting an apology, and Hollowgate said nothing, which he learned later was how they accepted it. When the shouting stopped, nobody came for him. He stayed. He was given a half-name, a Keeper, and a job sorting chain offcuts, and over forty years he became the keep's quartermaster, then its trader: boots, packs, arrows and bread for recruits going down to the lower halls. He has been home to Stormreach once, at forty, and found he could not sleep in the noise. Long Shout's bones hang in the gatehall. He greets them every morning, very quietly.","family":"A Shadowdeep wife, Hesk, who signs at him to lower his voice and laughs when he can't. Two grown daughters, both Nightblades, who sound nothing like him. A Stormreach family who stopped writing after he didn't come home.","hero":{"who":"Orrin Who Stayed","why":"The first Anchorite chose to stay still for those who come back. Drav didn't choose it, exactly, but he's been still for forty years, and he likes having someone to blame it on."},"band":{"act":"The Tether Sisters","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Chain-yard harmonies from the place his grandfather forged. When \"Cut the Chain\" comes through on a cap-recording he leaves the stall and stands with his back to the wall.","rv_vote":"The Tether Sisters, though he'll tell Hesk he gave it to a Shadowdeep act."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Veil Road toll families who gave him work, by keeping their ledgers straight and saying nothing of their skimming. Silence is the only Shadowdeep vote he's any good at.","edict":"Against, for Stormreach's sake more than Shadowdeep's. His old Realm is shouting rebellion and he's scared for every cousin he hasn't spoken to in eighteen years."},"wants":"To fly once more, on anything, before he can't.","site":"hollowgate","town":"Hollowgate","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:hollowgate":{"name":"Mosk","age":39,"role":"keysmith and ladder-forger of Hollowgate's lower halls","look":"Compact and muscled, scalp shaved, a burn shaped like a keyhole on one cheek that she says she got on purpose and won't explain.","voice":"Clipped and dry; signs while talking, like she doesn't trust either alone to carry the whole of it.","greet":"Ess. I don't do locks for strangers and I don't do keys for anyone. Everything else, ask.","lines":["Three keys open the Hollow Gate. Three sentries hold them. None knows the other two. Who made the keys? Don't ask that one either.","Stormreach chain offcuts. Look at the grain on this link. Struck by lightning before it was ever forged. You can't buy that. Well, you can, from Drav.","Quietwater keeps sending notes about our drill echo. The drill's secret, so we can't discuss where it is. That's true. It's also very funny."],"about":"I forge for the keep: hinges, ladders, the odd blade for a recruit who's lost theirs. I also cut the three keys of the Hollow Gate when they wear down, which is every eleven years. I don't know who carries them. That's the point of me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, garrison dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"shouting phrases only, learned from skyray riders overhead"}],"childhood":"Mosk's mother was a Nightblade who came home rarely and quietly, and her father ran the garrison laundry, so she grew up in the steam rooms of Hollowgate folding grey wraps for soldiers she was told never to look at. She looked. By eight she knew every sentry by the way they folded their wrap and could tell, from a pile of laundry, which of them had been on night watch. She once told her father which sentry had lost their watch-pebble a week before the keep found out. He made her promise never to tell anyone else anything she noticed, and she kept it so well that her mother, on one of her rare visits, called her the best-trained child in the keep. Mosk took that as permission to start picking locks.","schooling":"The keep's own children's school under an old sentry, Hult, who taught them to count pebbles in the dark before he taught numbers in the light. Lamp Test: no lamp answered. Her mother looked at the dark lamps for a long time and said nothing. At fifteen Mosk asked to apprentice in the forge instead of the Nightblade halls, and the old keysmith, Durn, took her because her hands never shook.","history":"Durn trained Mosk for twelve years and in the eleventh showed her the one job that mattered: re-cutting the three keys of the Hollow Gate. The keys go to the forge in sealed boxes, one at a time, weeks apart; the smith files each to its pattern and returns it without ever seeing who brings it. When Durn died, Mosk inherited the job, the forge, and his conviction that a smith who wonders about the sentries is a smith who will be replaced. She has cut the keys once herself, four years ago. The rest of her work is plainer: hinges that do not squeak, ladders forged from Stormreach chain offcuts, blades for Nightblade recruits training in the lower halls. She is well liked, rarely invited anywhere, and spends her evenings on the high wall listening to skyray riders shout greetings overhead, which she will not admit she enjoys.","family":"Mother long gone into the Nightblade Orders and probably dead; nobody has said. Father retired, still folding everyone's laundry. No partner; she says the keep is enough people to not talk to.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend","why":"Someone has to be loud so the rest of us can be quiet. Mosk has listened to the Warden's speeches shouted across the gulf and thinks Shadowdeep owes her a drink."},"band":{"act":"Thunderhead","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Power metal, as loud as anything in the Nine. She forges to it, in her head, when a key is on the anvil and her hands must be still.","rv_vote":"Thunderhead, for the noise, and because nobody in Hollowgate will ever guess."},"vote":{"local":"Backs no Well; keysmiths are not allowed to. She votes by what she declines to forge, and she has declined three requests from Hush this year.","edict":"Against. She has been 'mis-cutting' the brass seals the Lightforge envoys want struck for lamp-check certificates, very slightly, so they'll never quite sit flat."},"wants":"To meet one of the three key-holders, once, without either of them knowing.","site":"hollowgate","town":"Hollowgate","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:hollowgate":{"name":"Rell","age":19,"role":"Nightblade recruit in the lower halls, failing slowly","look":"Lanky, bony wrists out of his training wrap, a cut lip from drill that never quite heals; looks constantly as if he has just been caught.","voice":"Eager, apologetic, too many words; uses training slang wrong.","greet":"Ess! Sorry. I'm not meant to talk to visitors in the halls. You're not in the halls. Are you? Good. Hello.","lines":["Changing of Hands is at an hour nobody announces. You press a pebble into the next sentry's palm. If one's missing at dawn the whole keep goes silent. Two days, once. Very long two days.","The drill echoes down to Quietwater. They want it moved. We can't discuss where it is. I don't actually know where it is. I'm in it.","If you've grain, give it to the cook, not me. Frane feeds us. I just eat."],"about":"I'm in my second year in the lower halls, learning to be a Nightblade. I'm not very good. I'm very good at the climbing and very bad at the rest, which is most of it. I've a full name; my Keeper's a cousin in Hush who keeps sending me socks.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"good, garrison dialect, sloppy"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"school-level"}],"childhood":"Rell grew up in Murkwater, which matters more than he lets on: he was raised among the old retired casters on the Door Wall, carrying their fish and reading their chalk names back to them on bad mornings. One of them, a gaunt man with no use left in his left hand, taught Rell to climb the cistern walls blind and told him stories of the Veil Roads with all the good parts missing. Rell decided at seven that he would be a Nightblade, and has never wanted anything else as plainly. His parents, fish farmers, were proud and uneasy in equal measure, because they had watched the Door Wall every morning of their lives. His mother made him promise to chalk his name every day once he was grown. He has not missed one.","schooling":"Well-school in Murkwater under a teacher called Gorse who could not read and taught letters by having the casters chalk them. At his Lamp Test the shadow-glass lamp lit, faintly but steadily: Half-Spark. He was over the moon. He went to Hollowgate's lower halls at seventeen and is learning drill, silent movement, Veil Road maps by memory, and, very badly, inversion.","history":"Rell's two years at Hollowgate have been a series of small disasters handled with great enthusiasm. He fell through a skylight on his first week. He got lost in the Veil Road below the keep and was found by a toll family who fed him and sent him back with a note. He has climbed every wall in the lower halls, including two nobody had climbed before, and was punished for one and quietly praised for the other. Last winter, on his first Changing of Hands, his pebble went missing; the keep went silent for two days while sentries searched every crack, and Rell, sick with dread, found it in his own boot on the second night. He slipped it into the count before dawn and has told no one. His instructors think he has promise and no instinct. He thinks he has instinct and no promise. Both are probably right. He chalks his name on the inside of his locker every morning.","family":"Parents fish-farming in Murkwater, who send dried murk-fish and anxious notes. A younger sister, Lom, who reads the Door Wall now in his place.","hero":{"who":"Veilrend","why":"Shadowdeep's First Blade, with no known face. Rell stands under the hooded carving in the gatehall before every drill and asks it, silently, to make him less clumsy."},"band":{"act":"Knife Dance","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Dark reggaeton the recruits play on smuggled cap-boxes after lights-out. He can't dance either, but nobody can see.","rv_vote":"Knife Dance, and he hopes they come second properly this time, because last year they nearly won by accident."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to back a Well, and Nightblades are meant to be above them. He votes by how hard he trains on the days Mael's decrees are read; this season, very hard.","edict":"Torn. Everyone hates it. But a Lightforge register would at least write down that he has a spark, and nobody back home in Murkwater has ever quite believed it."},"wants":"To pass his third year and be sent down a Veil Road on his own.","site":"hollowgate","town":"Hollowgate","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:hollowgate:b":{"name":"Frane","age":66,"role":"cook of the Hollowgate pot","look":"Broad, short, forearms like hams, a wrap tied up with string, one eyebrow permanently raised; smells of inkroot and smoke.","voice":"Brisk, bossy, kind underneath; refuses all questions about food with a single raised spoon.","greet":"Ess. You're thin. Sit. No, don't ask what's in it. Eat it.","lines":["Whatever came up the Veil Road this week went into the pot. Rude to guess aloud. Ruder not to finish.","Tash eats standing up. Forty years of soldiers watching her do it, trying to read it. She just likes her back straight.","Grain? Bless you. I'll put it in Thursday's. You won't know which bit. That's the joy."],"about":"I've cooked the Hollowgate pot for thirty-eight years. The garrison eats what I give it and doesn't ask. Best job in the keep; nobody knows what I'm doing and everyone's grateful.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, kitchen signs only, mostly rude"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"just enough to bargain with drovers"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"food words"}],"childhood":"Frane was born in a toll family on the Veil Road below the keep, the youngest of nine, and the only one who would eat anything. Her brothers and sisters gave her everything they would not touch, and she ate it all, from boiled moth to Stormreach thunder-milk that a drover had left to sour. By six she had opinions about strike-goat. By eight she was cooking for the toll-gate guards on a brazier her father let her keep under the gate arch. They paid her in stories of what was coming up the road, and she began to plan meals around what she heard: a Spiritveil trader due Tuesday meant willow-leaf; a Flameborne one meant, if she was lucky, a pinch of emberseed. Her mother called her greedy. Her father called her a quartermaster.","schooling":"A toll-gate school of three children taught by Vosh, a retired gatekeeper who made them learn every route marker on the Veil Road by touch. Frane hated sums and loved maps. At the Lamp Test nothing lit, and she asked if the lamps could be eaten. No other schooling; she went into the keep's kitchens at fourteen as a pot-scrubber.","history":"For fourteen years Frane scrubbed, peeled and stirred under the old cook, a silent man called Brack who never once told her what went into the pot either. When he died she took the ladle, and a quartermaster asked her, as a formality, what she intended to cook. She raised her spoon at him and he went away, and that was her appointment. For thirty-eight years she has fed the garrison of Hollowgate, its Nightblade recruits, and any drover who comes up the tunnel hungry. She has fed three gate-captains, including Tash, whom she adores and teases. She has cooked through two floods in the lower halls, the year a skyray crashed in the courtyard (she sent up broth for the boy), and the season the Quietwater gardens went dark and the village sent the keep a bill, which she paid out of the kitchen money because she thought the officer deserved it. She will retire when she is dead.","family":"A husband, Ott, a ladder-climber, dead twelve years. A son in Hush who sells glim and doesn't cook, which she has forgiven.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"Seryn lit the First Hearth in her own kitchen fire and nobody ever knelt to her. Frane says that's the only way to run a kitchen."},"band":{"act":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"A seventy-year-old who kneads dough on stage. Frane saw her once at Korrhaven, the only time she left the keep, and has never forgiven anyone for talking through it.","rv_vote":"Old Tamsin, every year, because one of these years she won't be there to vote for."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the old toll Well she was born into, mostly by giving its drovers the best bowls. That's how Hollowgate votes, she says: with portions.","edict":"Against, on the grounds that anyone who wants to write down what's in a person wants to write down what's in a pot, and she'll not have it."},"wants":"For Tash to sit down to eat with her once, just once.","site":"hollowgate","town":"Hollowgate","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:quietwater":{"name":"Lune","age":35,"role":"silent trader in glow-caps, kit and miners' supplies","look":"Slight, very straight-backed, glow-cap dust making her fingertips faintly green; her hair is pinned with wooden pins so nothing chimes.","voice":"Mostly hand-cant; when she must speak, it's in a near-whisper Bridge with no wasted words.","greet":"(signs, then whispers) Ess. Quietly, please. The beds. What do you need?","lines":["(whispers) Speak at the Third Bed and it goes dark for a week. It's been dark for years. So someone keeps speaking to it. Think about that.","(whispers) Hush buys our spores and can't get their gardens to glow as bright. They think it's the spores. It's the whistling.","(whispers) The keep above drills in the lower halls. The echo comes down the shaft. We've asked. They can't discuss where the drill is. So we sign rude things up the shaft. Kept."],"about":"(whispers) I came from Hush ten years ago to find out why our gardens there were dim. The answer was: Hush can't keep quiet. I stayed. Now I sell glow-stock, boots and bread without saying much, which suits me better than I expected.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, Quietwater's silent dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, but whispers it"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a few miners' words"}],"childhood":"Lune grew up in the Quiet Gardens of Hush, the small public ones on the sixth terrace, where her parents were under-gardeners. The gardens there glowed weakly, a sickly green that barely lit a hand, and every gardener blamed the spores. Lune, a watchful, bossy little girl, kept a slate of which beds glowed brightest on which days and found the pattern before she was nine: the beds lit up whenever the head gardener was away. He whistled. Nobody would tell him. She tried, at ten, and was sent home for a week for rudeness. She spent the week sitting by the dimmest bed with her hands over her mouth, and it brightened, and she understood that being right was not the same as being listened to.","schooling":"Well-school on the sixth terrace under a tiny, fierce teacher called Ann, who let the class sign her half-name as a joke-shape, which was itself scandalous. Ann taught hand-cant through riddles. At the Lamp Test no lamp lit for Lune, which she still considers the examiner's fault for talking. At sixteen she became an under-gardener herself, and at twenty-five she walked to Quietwater to learn properly.","history":"Quietwater did not want her at first. She was a Hush girl, she whispered too loudly, and she asked questions in a village that speaks in hand-cant. Pim, the oldest grower, took one look at her slate of dim beds and bright days and let her stay. Lune spent three years learning the beds, a silent year in which she said not one word aloud, and found she could do it. Then she took over the village's trading, because Quietwater needed someone who could bear to talk to outsiders, and she was the only one who did not mind. She now sells glow-caps to lamp-houses and miners, spores to Hush, kit and bread to anyone passing down from the keep, and handles all the letters with Hollowgate about the drill echo. She has written fourteen. She keeps copies, which in Shadowdeep is close to an act of war.","family":"Parents still in Hush's sixth-terrace gardens, still dim. A partner in Quietwater, Wae, a grower who has never spoken a word to her aloud and never needs to.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"The first lantern was a cracked clay cup. Lune likes that the brightest thing in Spiritveil started as something dim and broken that someone wouldn't throw away."},"band":{"act":"Under Haskerry","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Whispered folk. The only music anyone in Quietwater can play without darkening the beds, and she learned all the hand-signs to it.","rv_vote":"Under Haskerry, and she hopes they come second, because winning would mean noise."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the growers' own small Well, the Quiet route under the lake, by refusing to trade with families who send noisy buyers.","edict":"Against. A Lightforge lamp-check in Quietwater would mean someone with a lamp shouting for an audit, and the beds would go dark for a year."},"wants":"For Hollowgate to answer one of her fourteen letters with a straight answer.","site":"quietwater","town":"Quietwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:quietwater":{"name":"Teff","age":44,"role":"the silent smith of Quietwater, who cold-works iron behind three doors","look":"Big, soft-spoken in movement, hands wrapped in felt bands; a shaved head with a ring of old burn scars around the ear like a crown.","voice":"Talks only in his forge-cave, low and slow, as if each word is a hammer-blow he is pricing.","greet":"(through the third door, low) Come in quick. Shut it. Now we can talk. Ess.","lines":["A forge is the loudest thing a village can own. Mine's three doors deep and I still hear the beds complain.","That officer who shouted for a lamp, years back? I handed it to him. I was his armour-bearer. Gardens went black a month. Village sent the keep a bill. I stayed to pay it off. Still here.","Lift, then talk. That's Garron. That's also how you shoe a pit-mule."],"about":"I was an armourer up in Hollowgate once. Now I'm Quietwater's smith, which means I mend spades and wrap my hammer in felt so the gardens don't hear. I came down because of a lamp and never went back up.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, garrison and Quietwater both"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a few smiths' words from a Stonehold master"}],"childhood":"Teff was born to a garrison family in Hollowgate, son of a sentry and a ladder-forger, and grew up big enough that everyone assumed he would be a Nightblade. He was clumsy, kind, and loud, the worst possible combination in that keep. His mother, gently, steered him into the forge, where noise was allowed. He loved it: the ring of iron, the shouting over the bellows, the right to make a racket. At eleven he made his first knife and banged it on the forge wall in triumph, and a passing gate-captain made him stand in silence in the courtyard till dusk. He did not understand the punishment then. He understood it twenty years later, standing on the shore of the lake below, watching a garden go black.","schooling":"The keep's school under the same old Hult who taught Mosk, two years ahead of her; Hult taught him to count pebbles and despaired of him. Lamp Test: nothing. At fourteen he was apprenticed to the garrison armourer, a Stonehold woman named Bresk, an exile, who taught him in Holdspeech that heavy work done well needs no talking, and that you lift first.","history":"Teff became the keep's armourer's second, then armour-bearer to a Hollowgate officer whose job was inspecting the villages below. One season they rode down to Quietwater to inspect the Quiet Gardens. The officer, cross in the dark, shouted for a lamp, and Teff, who had it, lit it and handed it over. Every bed on the lake went black. For a month the village lived in darkness and its glow-stock trade collapsed. Quietwater sent Hollowgate a bill; the officer refused it. Teff went to Pim and offered to work it off. He was meant to stay a year. That was seventeen years ago. He built a forge three doors deep into the rock, felted his hammers, learned to cold-work iron and rivet instead of hammer, and now makes every spade, hook and drying-rack in Quietwater. The bill was paid off in year four. He did not tell anyone he'd finished.","family":"A husband, Sorl, a cap-drier, who taught him hand-cant properly and laughs without sound. No children. His mother in Hollowgate, who visits and talks too loud on purpose to annoy the beds.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"Bresk taught him \"Lift. Then talk.\" in Holdspeech, and it's the only rule that has never failed him, including the day he should have kept the lamp in his pocket."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A doom trio whose drummer's hands are turning to stone. His cousin heard them at Korrhaven and hummed the riff in his cave for an hour; Teff felt it in his teeth.","rv_vote":"Burden, for the drummer, who's playing slower every year and still won't stop."},"vote":{"local":"Backs Quietwater's growers' Well because they took him in. He has never voted any other way and doesn't see why he'd start.","edict":"Against, and quietly working on it: he's forged three hooded lamp-caps that fit over a Lightforge audit lamp and look, from a distance, like part of it."},"wants":"For his mother to stop shouting at the beds.","site":"quietwater","town":"Quietwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:quietwater":{"name":"Ib","age":9,"role":"apprentice grower to Pim, who cannot stop humming","look":"Tiny, round-cheeked, hair in a stubby tail tied with cap-stalk; usually has one hand clamped over her own mouth.","voice":"Signs in big, eager sweeps; whispers when caught out; hums without noticing.","greet":"(signs, then a whisper) Ess! You're new. Are you quiet? Pim says new people are never quiet. Prove her wrong.","lines":["(whisper) Speak at the Third Bed and it goes dark for a week. Fesk did the day his girl left. Eleven years. That's longer than me.","(whisper) Pim's beds are the brightest because she can't hear herself and stopped humming. Mine are the darkest because I can hear myself and I can't stop.","(whisper) Grain? For the growers? That's kind. The whole lake will know. Not by talking. By looking at you."],"about":"(whisper) I'm learning to grow glow-caps from Pim. She's deaf and the best. I hum. Everyone says so. I'm trying very hard to stop and I can't, and the beds go dim, and I just want one bed of mine to light up.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, child's signs, very fast"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words: 'hello', 'quiet', 'buy'"}],"childhood":"Ib is the daughter of two drying-shed workers and the great-niece of nobody in particular; Quietwater calls every grower's apprentice 'Pim's' regardless. She was born on the Dark Day itself, which the village considers lucky and her mother considers unfair, since labour without shouting was not her idea. From the start Ib was noisy in the only way a Quietwater child can be: humming. In her cot, at the table, in her sleep. Her parents tried everything, from sitting her by the beds to see the dimming for herself, to letting her hum into a jar. She liked the jar. At seven she wandered down the shore alone to the edge of the Third Bed and stood there, humming, and nothing happened at all, which frightened her more than she can explain.","schooling":"Quietwater's tiny Well-school under a grower called Fenn, who teaches only hand-cant until the age of ten and makes the children sit by a lit bed every morning to learn stillness. Ib's bed is always the first to dim. She took the Lamp Test this past season; one lamp, the shadow-glass, gave a slow, faint glow, and the examiner wrote 'Half'. Ib has been worrying it means she'll have to leave.","history":"There is not much yet, and Ib would like you to know she is working on it. Last spring Pim, who has not heard a sound in twelve years, chose Ib from the children as her apprentice, for reasons nobody can work out; Ib is the noisiest child on the lake. Pim teaches by touch. She takes Ib's hand and lays it flat on the bed until Ib feels the faint warmth of the caps under her palm, and then takes Ib's other hand and lays it on Ib's own throat, so she can feel the hum. Ib has learned more about stopping in six months of that than in nine years of being told. Her bed, the smallest on the near shore, glowed for nearly an hour last week. She cried, silently, which she was very proud of.","family":"Mother and father in the drying sheds; an older brother who teases her by humming back. Her Keeper is Pim, which is unusual and which everyone is too polite to mention.","hero":{"who":"Pim","why":"Pim's beds are the brightest on the lake and she can't hear a thing. Ib thinks that means being quiet is something you can do on purpose, even if you're broken in the noisy direction."},"band":{"act":"Five Signs","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Idols who sing in hand-cant, so nobody has to make a sound. She knows every dance and can do them sitting down.","rv_vote":"Five Signs, because they're the only act she can watch without humming along."},"vote":{"local":"She's nine. But on the Dark Day she sat next to a loud visitor, by choice, and got the salt fine. Her mother says that's a vote.","edict":"Against, because Pim's against, and because a lamp-check would mean someone shouting, and she knows what that does."},"wants":"For one of her beds to glow all night.","site":"quietwater","town":"Quietwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:quietwater:b":{"name":"Sorn","age":30,"role":"returned cap-reader's assistant, now drying glow-caps; the girl who left","look":"Tall and sun-starved, a Duskhollow cap-reader's grey hood she won't take off, ink rubbed into the cuticles from eleven years of listening work.","voice":"Speaks aloud more than any Quietwater person would, then catches herself; her hand-cant has a Duskhollow accent the village pretends not to notice.","greet":"(signs, then very softly) Ess. Yes, I'm the one. Don't ask. Buy something.","lines":["(softly) They tell you Fesk spoke at the Third Bed the day his girl left. That was me, the girl. What he said, I've never heard. I may have it in my pocket.","(softly) Eleven years in Duskhollow listening to other people's caps. You come back and the silence here sounds like everyone holding their breath for you.","(softly) Fish come down from Murkwater into Duskhollow's sluice and get sold. Murkwater calls a fish a debt that swims. That's the most Shadowdeep thing I've ever heard."],"about":"(softly) I grew up here, left for Duskhollow at nineteen to read caps, and came home this spring. I dry glow-caps now. Everyone's very polite, which in Quietwater means they're furious. I've got a full name. Lately I'd like to say it, which isn't something you should tell a stranger.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, Duskhollow accent now"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it; learned from caps of Lightforge buyers"}],"childhood":"Sorn was the restless one in a family of very still growers, the child who kept running up the shaft towards the keep to listen to the drill echo because it was the only loud thing she knew. She loved sound the way other children loved sweets. She would press her ear to the lake's rock walls to hear water moving, sit at the mouth of Teff's forge — before Teff, the old smith, who let her — and wait for each hammer-blow like a heartbeat. At twelve she met Fesk, a grower's son who could hold a bed lit for a whole night by sheer stubbornness, and they spent years sitting side by side in the dark, saying nothing, and being perfectly happy about it, which was the problem.","schooling":"Quietwater Well-school under Fenn's mother, who taught the same silence drills and expelled Sorn twice for humming under the lake at night. At her Lamp Test nothing lit; she was secretly glad, because it meant she could leave without anyone needing her. She taught herself letters from a Bridge almanac a miner left behind.","history":"At nineteen Sorn walked to Duskhollow, told Fesk she was going two days before she went, and did not say goodbye, which in Shadowdeep is either cowardice or great respect. She became a cap-sorter's assistant, then a junior reader in the Listening Wood, and spent eleven years pressed to stone-fungus caps hearing strangers' voices: lovers, merchants, Hush collectors, children counting, an old man reciting recipes. She was good at it. She learned to separate voice from drip faster than anyone in her year, and was beginning to answer in other people's tones the way the chief reader Sill does. That frightened her home. She came back to Quietwater this spring to dry caps, which is the least listening a person can do. Fesk is still here. They have not spoken. Before she left Duskhollow she cut one cap from the edge of the Wood nearest the Quietwater road, because it had a voice she knew in it, and she has not dared listen to it again.","family":"Parents, both growers, who welcomed her home in total silence and fed her for a week. A brother in Hollowgate's garrison. Fesk, who is not family and never was.","hero":{"who":"Vey Who Said It","why":"The founder of the Open-Mouthed, who said a full name aloud. Sorn learned about Vey from a heretic's cap in the Wood and has not been able to stop thinking about it."},"band":{"act":"The Full Name","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"A singer who sings her full name to one person, a scandal. Sorn has a cap-recording and listens to it under her hood when the beds are being tended far off.","rv_vote":"The Full Name, openly, which got her the coldest looks of the season at the lamp-count."},"vote":{"local":"Backs no Well; she's been away too long for any to want her. Her vote this year was to sit at the Dark Day with her hood down, which nobody missed.","edict":"Against, but with a twist: she thinks Shadowdeep sabotaging Lightforge's registers is fine, and also that Shadowdeep keeps its own register in every Keeper's head and nobody calls that an Edict."},"wants":"To hear what Fesk said at the Third Bed, and to decide whether to tell him her full name.","site":"quietwater","town":"Quietwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:nightfen":{"name":"Quell","age":58,"role":"dye-trader on the border road; sells black to Lightforge for mourning sashes","look":"Neat, narrow, clean-shaven, nails scrubbed nearly pink except for a black rim he can never shift; wears a Lightforge-cut coat with the buttons swapped for bone.","voice":"Courteous, exact Bridge, slipping into Clear with evidential endings when he wants to make a Lightforger uncomfortable.","greet":"Ess, or Aure, depending which side of the road you woke up on. Dye, kit, bread. All honest. Mostly.","lines":["They plant a new lamp on the road, we bring a new sack. Forty days they gave us. We've got longer than that. That's not me talking. That's the whole fen.","Every mourning sash in Lightforge is dyed with Nightfen inkroot. Their dead go into the Grief Rooms wearing our black. Nobody has ever written that in a Day-Book.","The patrols cut the sacks off the posts. Where do you think the cut sacks go? Somebody buys them. Somebody's me."],"about":"I sell black dye to Lightforge cloth-merchants across the border road, and boots and bread to anyone in between. My grandfather came up out of the Undercroft blind. I sell to the people who did it. I don't find that strange. I find it lucrative.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"fluent, uses evidentials on purpose"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Quell grew up in a cellar three rooms deep, the son of an inkroot grower and a woman who boiled dye for the border trade, and was the first child in his family to stand at the edge of the border road in daylight. He did it at six, on a dare, and stood there blinking at the lamp-posts while a Lightforge patrol watched him from across the gravel. One of them, a young woman with a ledger, crossed over, crouched, and asked him in Bridge what he was called. He gave his half-name. She wrote it in her Day-Book and showed him the page, and he was so offended and delighted that he refused to speak for a day. From then on he wanted to know everything about the people across the road, mostly so he could beat them.","schooling":"Nightfen's cellar Well-school under Grisk, a woman who had been a child in the Undercroft and taught hand-cant before letters because \"letters need light and hands don't.\" At the Lamp Test no lamp answered. He then paid, out of dye-money, a Rim lamp-school teacher across the road to teach him Clear on market days, and learned it in three years.","history":"Quell took over his mother's dye trade at twenty-two and turned it from a sideline into the fen's main coin. He found the Lightforge cloth-merchants of the Rim, who needed black for mourning sashes and hated buying it from Shadowdeep, and made himself the only seller they could bear: clean nails, exact Clear, receipts signed and dated. He trades across the road twice a week and has never once raised his voice. He also runs the village's stall, selling kit to travellers. Thirty years ago he began buying the cut sacks off the Lightforge patrols, who bin them, and selling the sacking back to Nightfen families for new hoods. The patrols think it is a joke. Nightfen thinks it is a joke. Quell thinks it is a margin. His secret pleasure is a Lampwick cloth-merchant, Merin Whitlow, who has bought from him for twenty-six years and whom he has never once seen in shade.","family":"A wife, Tesk, who grows inkroot and thinks he's too fond of Lightforgers. A daughter, Lis, studying dye-making in Hush. His grandfather's blindness, which the family does not discuss at table.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"\"Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.\" Quell trades on both sides of the road and believes it's the only rule that keeps a man honest and in business."},"band":{"act":"Plain Speaking","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Honest country with no metaphors. Merin Whitlow gave him a cap-recording as a joke twenty years ago and he's worn it thin.","rv_vote":"Plain Speaking, quietly, and he'll tell the fen he backed Telt."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Border Well, the families who hold the cellar routes under the road, by handing them the receipts he copies from Lightforge buyers.","edict":"Torn, publicly against. A Lightforge register means Lightforge clerks across the road with more ink and less patience, and he sells ink."},"wants":"To see Merin Whitlow's face in shade once, just to know he would.","site":"nightfen","town":"Nightfen","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:nightfen":{"name":"Aun","age":71,"role":"blind-handed smith who forges the hood-frames for the Sack Posts","look":"Small, hunched, hands like gnarled roots, eyes clouded pale; she wears a strip of grey bandage round her neck like a scarf.","voice":"Sharp, sardonic, swears in Undertongue with an old Undercroft accent; laughs at her own jokes before she finishes them.","greet":"Ess. Don't wave. Speak. My eyes stopped arguing with me years back.","lines":["Every hood on those lamp-posts has an iron frame inside it. Mine. Patrols cut the sack, the frame stays on. Takes them a week to notice.","No Lightforge court's ever named the Burning in a Day-Book. Forty days of light. They named the price of a cheese that week, I'd bet.","I make good hooks. If you're a patrol, I make bad ones. You'll never know which."],"about":"My mother came up out of the Undercroft as a girl with her eyes ruined, and I came out of her with eyes that started going at forty. I forge iron by feel. I like it better. You don't get distracted by what the work looks like.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native, old Undercroft accent"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent; reads it by touch now"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"enough to insult a buyer"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"understands it; refuses to speak it"}],"childhood":"Aun's mother had been a child in the Back of Noon when the mirror-light came, and her eyes never recovered; she ran a Nightfen household by touch and temper, and Aun grew up as her mother's eyes. She read her mother the inkroot harvest, the colour of the eel-mash, the faces of guests. She learned to describe things precisely and without kindness, because her mother wanted the truth of a pot. 'Burnt,' she would say. 'Grey. He's lying, he's touching his ear.' By nine she had a reputation as the most honest child in the fen, which in Shadowdeep is not a compliment. She was also the child who climbed the first lamp-post Lightforge planted on the border road and pulled a sack over it, a year before the fen took up the custom.","schooling":"The cellar Well-school under Grisk, the same teacher Quell had later; Grisk taught hand-cant by touch from the first day, which would serve Aun better than anyone knew. No lamp lit at her Lamp Test. At thirteen she was apprenticed to the fen's smith, Dask, a quiet ex-Nightblade, who let her work because she never asked to be thanked.","history":"Aun became Nightfen's smith at twenty-six when Dask's hands gave out, and for fifteen years made eel-traps, cellar hinges and inkroot forks like any village smith. At forty her sight began to cloud, as her mother's had, and she spent one bad winter sure she would have to give up. Instead she learned to forge by feel: the heat on her face, the sound of the strike, the vibration up the hammer handle. By fifty she was better than she had ever been sighted. That was also when the Sack Posts war began properly, after Lightforge planted a long line of new lamp-posts along the road, and Aun invented the hood-frame: an iron cage that clamps round the lamp-head so a sack stays on even when cut. Patrols cut them off with saws now. Aun makes new ones every month. She has been warned by Lightforge envoys twice and by the Umbral Council once, for being too obvious. She took the Council's warning as praise.","family":"Her mother, dead thirty years. Never married; had a long, cheerful understanding with a Hollowgate eel-buyer who visited every harvest till he died. Her nephew Pask digs cellars and checks on her daily, which she pretends to hate.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder","why":"The Banked Fire, tried in Lightforge for 'concealed anger'. Aun says she has been concealing anger at Lightforge for seventy-one years and would like to see them try her for it."},"band":{"act":"Telt","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Punk, banned at home. She can't see them but she's heard them twice through a cellar wall in Hush, and liked that they were loud in a Realm that isn't.","rv_vote":"Telt, if they're let in, and she hopes they come first by mistake."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Border Well, and has made it known, which is very un-Shadowdeep of her; she says she's old enough to be impolite.","edict":"Against, flatly and noisily. She has started fitting hood-frames with a catch that jams Lightforge lamp-check lanterns if they're clipped on top."},"wants":"To outlive one more line of Lightforge lamp-posts.","site":"nightfen","town":"Nightfen","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:nightfen":{"name":"Midge","age":16,"role":"inkroot picker who wants, very badly, to see noon","look":"Wiry, black-lipped half the year, hands stained to the wrist, an old Noonday poster folded so small in a pocket it's gone soft as cloth.","voice":"Quick, ironic, half-whispered; uses Bridge slang picked up from border-road drovers and wears it like a coat.","greet":"Ess. You've come from the light side? What's it like? No, don't tell me. Yes. Tell me.","lines":["The patrols cut the sacks off. We tie new ones on by morning. I've tied about four hundred. I'd like to see one lit, just once, before I cover it.","Black Lip Night's after harvest. Everyone eats a raw root, so nobody can tell who's been kissing whom. I'm only saying. In case you were wondering.","Give the fen grain and they'll say you're all right. They won't say it to you. They'll say it to each other."],"about":"I pick inkroot. I've picked it since I could hold a fork. I want to walk the Pillar Road at noon, barefoot, and see what everyone over there is so proud of. Everyone here thinks I'm a traitor. I think they're scared I'll like it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, slangy"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a little, self-taught from a dropped Day-Book"}],"childhood":"Midge was born in the deepest cellar in Nightfen, a cramped warm place where three generations of inkroot growers slept head to toe, and grew up sure the world was low, wet and black. Their family is cheerful, loud by fen standards, and terrible at secrets. Midge, the youngest, was the opposite: a child who watched. At eight they found a Lightforge Day-Book a patrol had dropped on the border road, smuggled it into the cellar under their shirt, and taught themselves Clear letters from it by glow-cap light. The book was a young patrolman's: what he ate, how far he walked, a girl he liked in Lampwick, a dead dog he missed. Midge read it until the spine broke and still knows the dog's name.","schooling":"The cellar Well-school, still taught by old Grisk, now nearly a hundred, who taught Midge hand-cant by touch and despaired of their spelling. Midge's Lamp Test: the Radiance lamp glimmered, faint and yellow, which nobody in Nightfen talks about and which Midge will not shut up about. The examiner wrote 'Half? Unusual.' No further school. The harvest needed hands.","history":"Midge has picked inkroot every harvest since six and is the fastest picker of their year, mostly because they pick while thinking about somewhere else. At twelve they tied their first sack on a Sack Post and were caught by a patrol, a boy not much older, who cut it off and then, after a long look, retied it himself and walked on. Midge has not stopped thinking about that. They tie sacks every week with the rest of the fen, and every week they pick a different post and look for him. Two Black Lip Nights ago they saw him at the edge of the road, out of uniform, mouth black, and neither of them said a word. They have a plan to walk to the Pillar Road for the Unshadowing and stand barefoot at noon, and have saved four crossings towards it in a jar in the cellar wall. Midge uses no he or she, and the fen, mostly, has got used to it, though Grisk forgets.","family":"Two parents, three grandparents, four siblings, all in one cellar, all of whom know about the jar. A great-aunt, Aun, the smith, who calls Midge 'the sunflower' to annoy them.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"The first Penumbrist, who said mercy needs shade. Midge thinks a Lightforger who wanted shade is the same as a Nightfen kid who wants noon, and they've never met anyone else who'd understand."},"band":{"act":"Noonday","realm":"Lightforge","why":"The perfect pop star never photographed in shadow. Midge loves her completely and ironically and also not ironically at all, and the soft poster in their pocket is of her.","rv_vote":"Noonday, which will make the fen furious, and they'll deny it at the lamp-count."},"vote":{"local":"Says they'd back the Border Well, but only so they could cross the road more.","edict":"Against, because the fen is, and because a spark register would mean their glimmer on the Radiance lamp gets written down somewhere, and then someone in Lightforge might come for them, and Midge wants to go there on their own feet."},"wants":"To stand on the Pillar Road at noon and not be afraid.","site":"nightfen","town":"Nightfen","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:nightfen:b":{"name":"Pask","age":45,"role":"cellar-digger and keeper of the fen's under-rooms","look":"Stocky and stooped from forty years of low ceilings, a permanent dirt-line on the crown of his head where he ducks; laughs with his whole body.","voice":"Friendly, rambling Undertongue-flavoured Bridge, always talking about depth and soil as if they're people he knows.","greet":"Ess, friend. Mind your head. No, lower. There. Nightfen's bigger than it looks. Most of it's under you.","lines":["They plant a new lamp on the road, we bring a new sack. Forty days they gave us. We've got longer than that. My mum said it, and her mum. It's in the soil now.","Inkroot mash needs a spoon of eel fat at the end. Every family swears theirs is the right amount. Ours is. I've tasted the rest.","If you've grain, the fen'll remember. We remember everything down here. It's cool and dry. Good for remembering."],"about":"I dig cellars. All of Nightfen's under-rooms, most of them, are mine or my dad's. I like the soil. It doesn't argue and it holds what you put in it. My aunt's the smith, Aun. She argues for both of us.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, a digger's dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, chatty"}],"childhood":"Pask was the son of a cellar-digger and grew up underground in the most literal sense, carried down the shafts on his father's back before he could walk and put to bed in half-dug rooms on a pile of sacking. His earliest memory is the smell of fresh-cut fen soil, a black, peppery smell like inkroot. He was a happy child, a slow one at letters and a fast one at shovels, and he had a gift: he could tell by tapping a wall how far the next cellar was and whether water was coming. By ten his father let him choose where to dig. By twelve he had found an old Undercroft tunnel nobody had known ran under Nightfen, and the fen elders made him promise to forget it. He has not forgotten it.","schooling":"The cellar Well-school under Grisk, who gave up on his letters at eleven and let him tap the walls instead, which she said was a kind of reading. Lamp Test: nothing lit; he was relieved because sparks seemed like hard work. No more school. He went into his father's trade full-time at twelve.","history":"Pask has dug, by his own count, two hundred and nine cellars in Nightfen, and cleared, shored or rebuilt most of the others. The village is three times the size underground that it is above, and he knows all of it by touch. He took his father's spade at twenty-three when the old man's back went, married a fen-eel trapper called Noll at twenty-six, and has spent his life cheerfully underground. The border road has been the backdrop to all of it: the lamps, the sacks, the patrols. Pask has no feelings about Lightforge he can name, which worries him at fen gatherings. What he does have is a tunnel. When he was twenty, digging a new under-room for a family near the border, he broke through into a narrow, very old passage running east under the road. He bricked it up. Nine years ago he unbricked it and walked it. It goes a long way. He has told no one, not even Noll, not even Aun.","family":"His wife Noll, an eel-trapper who works alongside Hask; two children, one already digging. His aunt Aun, whom he checks on daily and who calls him 'the mole'.","hero":{"who":"His mother, Gret","why":"She dug the first cellar Nightfen had with a drain that worked, at sixty, after everyone said it couldn't be done, and then went back to bed."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Forty veterans who never go above a murmur. He heard them through a cap a drover brought and said it was like music for underground.","rv_vote":"The Lonely Line Choir, because they'll never win and he likes backing things that keep going anyway."},"vote":{"local":"Backs no Well in particular; he digs for all of them, and every Well in Nightfen owes him a room. Owing is his vote.","edict":"Torn. Everyone's against it, and he is, but he also thinks if Lightforge is busy registering its own sparks it'll stop planting lamps on the road, and that would be nice."},"wants":"To dig a cellar so good the whole fen comes to see it and nobody can find the door.","site":"nightfen","town":"Nightfen","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:duskhollow":{"name":"Thrum","age":50,"role":"trader in the honeycomb walls; sells kit by day and listening-caps before Hush's collectors arrive","look":"Heavy, loose-limbed, good teeth, a cap-reader's grey hood worn pushed back as if to say he's off duty; ringed fingers he takes off before shaking hands.","voice":"Easy, joking, persuasive Bridge with Kindle slang he picked up from a Flameborne partner; laughs at the start of sentences.","greet":"Ess! Come in out of the Beam. Packs, boots, arrows. And if you've ears for it, something better. Ask nicely.","lines":["That cap on my counter's been saying the same word for a hundred years. No, I won't tell you what. Nobody in this town will. Go unseen.","Hush wants every listening-cap sent up to the Council unheard. Unheard! Like wine sent up uncorked. The good ones go before the collectors get here.","We're the richest town after Hush, and Hush is not pleased. That's my favourite sentence. Say it with me."],"about":"I run the kit shop at the bottom of the east stair and do a little cap-trade on the side. All right, a lot. I had a Flameborne wife for twenty years, so I'm loud for Duskhollow. I sell silence for a living and talk all day. Somebody has to.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, salesman's"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good, warm, rude"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Thrum's father sorted listening-caps for the old chief reader, and Thrum spent his childhood on a stool in the sorting halls, handed caps and told to sort them by weight and nothing else, never to listen. He listened. He heard a woman laughing in one, a dog in another, a man reciting a list of debts in a voice so sad that Thrum, aged seven, put the cap down and cried. His father caught him and did not punish him; he sat down next to him and said that was why they sold the caps, so somebody far away could cry instead. Thrum decided then that he would sell them himself one day. He also decided he would rather make people laugh.","schooling":"Duskhollow's Well-school in the honeycomb under a teacher called Fray, who taught arithmetic by having children price imaginary caps and fined them in pear-stones for bad sums. Thrum won every week. No lamp lit at his Lamp Test; he asked if he could buy one. At fifteen he apprenticed to a kit-trader, which he says taught him everything he knows about smiling.","history":"Thrum took over the kit shop at twenty-eight, married a Flameborne caravan cook called Ressa at thirty, and spent twenty happy, noisy years with her in the eastern stairs. She taught him Kindle, arguing, and how to tell a buyer to their face they were being a fool. When she died three winters ago, of a fever she caught on the Coal Road, Thrum closed the shop for one day and opened it the next. Most of his money comes from the cap-trade, which runs through the back room: caps the chief reader grades as 'voice' that the Umbral Council's collectors want sent up to Hush unheard. Thrum gets the best ones out the night before the collectors come and sells them to buyers in Murmur, Stormreach and once, very carefully, Lightforge. Duskhollow considers him a scoundrel and a civic treasure in roughly equal measure.","family":"His wife Ressa, dead three winters. A son, Hoth, in Hush, who has gone very proper and Council-minded and won't visit. Ressa's Flameborne family, who still send him coal cakes at First Coal.","hero":{"who":"Sera Solbrand","why":"The only person ever to use the Judgment of One. Thrum admires anyone who does something once that nobody else dares do at all, and then lives with it."},"band":{"act":"Hot Corner","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Ressa's favourite, a reggaeton crew from her home street. He plays it in the back room while he sorts and pretends he's not crying.","rv_vote":"Hot Corner, for Ressa, every year, no matter how they sing."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Wood Well, the Duskhollow families who hold the eastern tunnels, by paying them a cut of every cap he sells before Hush sees it.","edict":"Against, cheerfully. Lightforge auditors in Duskhollow would want to hear the caps, and Thrum doesn't sell to people who won't pay."},"wants":"For his son to come home and argue with him the way Ressa used to.","site":"duskhollow","town":"Duskhollow","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:duskhollow":{"name":"Oat","age":28,"role":"cap-knife smith; one year at Korrhaven, one knot on her sash","look":"Rangy, sunburnt from the one hour of Beam she stands in on purpose, a grey Conclave sash with a single knot tied round her forearm as a sweatband.","voice":"Fast, defensive, very funny, mixes Conclave words into Undertongue and then dares you to notice.","greet":"Ess. Yes, that's a Conclave sash. One knot. Don't ask about the second. What needs sharpening?","lines":["Cap-readers need blades that cut a cap off its stem without a sound. A ringing knife ruins a voice. I make the quiet ones. I'm the only one who can.","That cap on the trader's counter's been saying the same word for a century. Kip knows what it is. Ask her. She'll lie. Beautifully.","Korrhaven porridge offends every Realm equally. That's the one thing they get right there."],"about":"I went to the Aether Conclave on a spark the lamps gave me at eight. I lasted a year and came home. Now I forge cap-knives and hooks for the readers, and every month someone asks why I left. I'm a smith. I'm a good smith. That's why.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, Conclave-taught"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"reasonable, from Metalcrown friends at the Glass Library annex"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"some, mostly insults"}],"childhood":"Oat was the cap-sorter's daughter who wouldn't stay out of the Beam. While every other child in Duskhollow pressed back against the walls when the noon light came down into the square, Oat walked into it, stood in the pear-orchard, and looked straight up the shaft at the sky. Her mother dragged her back by the collar every day for two years. At seven she was banned from the square at noon. At eight the Lamp Test lit the shadow-glass bright and full, and everyone decided the Beam had cooked her. Oat says she was just curious what the town was hiding from. She has a scar on her shin from a pear branch she climbed to get closer, and is very proud of it.","schooling":"Duskhollow Well-school under Fray, then the Lamp Years in Hush, three years of letters, drill and plain arts, and then, at thirteen, the Aether Conclave on Korrhaven. She lasted a year. She was brilliant at theory and terrible at the cost: the first time inversion slipped her reflection in a practice mirror, she put her fist through it. She spent her free hours in the Glass Library annex with the Metalcrown students, who were mocked for taking the best notes, and liked them more than anyone else there.","history":"Oat came home from Korrhaven at fifteen with one knot on her sash, a cut hand and a refusal to explain. Duskhollow, mortified on her behalf, did not ask. She went into the forge of an old cap-knife smith, a woman called Wyth, who had been making silent blades for cap-readers for fifty years and who did not care about Conclaves at all. Wyth taught her to forge blades with felt-damped tangs and edges so thin they could cut a stone-fungus stem without the cap hearing it. When Wyth died, Oat took the forge. She now makes every cap-knife in Duskhollow and half the hooks, and sells blades to Hush readers who pretend not to know she's a dropout. Two of her Metalcrown friends from the annex write to her. One, now a junior engineer, was in the hall when the ninth petition was laughed down. Oat read the letter in the Beam, at noon, where nobody would come near enough to see her face.","family":"Her mother still sorts caps and has never once said the word Conclave aloud. A younger brother, Pent, who has no spark and is relieved. No partner; she says she's waiting for someone who'll stand in the Beam without being married to.","hero":{"who":"Solvane","why":"The polite young engineer who read the ninth petition and was laughed at by the whole Council. Oat knows exactly what a hall laughing sounds like and thinks finishing the reading was braver than any spell."},"band":{"act":"Slag Hill","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Industrial drill against spark-born privilege. Her annex friends played it for her in the Glass Library at night, and she realised she'd left the Conclave for the same reasons they rap about.","rv_vote":"Slag Hill, and she'll tell Duskhollow it's to get a lamp to Metalcrown since Lightforge never will."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Wood Well, because the readers are her customers, and because it annoys Hush, which she enjoys.","edict":"Against, with feeling. She was a registered spark at Korrhaven and hated being a line in someone's ledger. The Edict is that ledger, for everyone."},"wants":"For someone at the Conclave to admit she was right to leave.","site":"duskhollow","town":"Duskhollow","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:duskhollow":{"name":"Seld","age":23,"role":"pear-orchard keeper of the Beam, about to stand in it at his wedding","look":"Pale even for Duskhollow, nervous hands always pruning something, a pear-wood comb in his hair that his intended carved.","voice":"Soft, polite, sweetly anxious; trails off mid-sentence and finishes with a hand-sign.","greet":"Ess. Sorry. I was counting pears. There aren't many. There never are.","lines":["When the Beam comes down, everyone steps back against the walls. Only couples on their wedding day walk out into it. Mine's in three weeks. I'm told I'll be fine. Everyone is told they'll be fine.","That cap on the trader's counter's been saying the same word for a hundred years. My gran says it's a name. My gran says a lot.","Beamcider? There's never enough. If I give you a cup, remember it. That's the whole point of it."],"about":"I keep the pear trees at the foot of the Beam. They're the only things in Duskhollow that grow towards the light. In three weeks I stand in it with Vash, in front of the whole town, the one time in my life I'll agree to be seen. I've been sick twice this week.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, uses lovers' signs"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Seld was a timid child in a family of loud cap-traders, and the only place he ever felt easy was the pear orchard at the foot of the Beam, where nobody went except at the one noon hour, and then only to look away. He spent his mornings there from age five, hiding among the twisted trunks while the shaft of light crept across the square. He talked to the trees, which in Duskhollow is not unusual, and the old orchard-keeper, a crooked man called Wuld, let him water them in exchange for silence. Seld gave it gladly. The first time Seld stood in the edge of the Beam was by accident, at nine, chasing a dropped pear, and the whole square went quiet. He ran. He still dreams about it.","schooling":"Duskhollow Well-school under Fray, who was patient with his stammer and let him answer in hand-cant. At the Lamp Test nothing answered, and he says he was too nervous to notice. He learned the trees from Wuld, who could not read but could tell a sick pear-root by the smell of the bark.","history":"Seld became orchard-keeper at nineteen when Wuld died in the orchard in his sleep, and he has kept the Beam pears alive since: a dozen stunted trees, about a barrel of hard little fruit a year, pressed to the beamcider the whole town treasures. It is a small, important life, and he likes it small. Two years ago a Duskhollow Farms 3 baker's daughter, Vash, came to buy pears for a spiced loaf and stayed to argue with him about pruning. She wins the arguments. He has never been happier, which terrifies him, because the wedding means walking out into the Beam with her while all of Duskhollow watches, and Seld has spent his entire life arranging not to be seen. He practises at dawn, standing in the empty square where the Beam will fall, counting to sixty. Some mornings he reaches forty.","family":"A loud family of cap-traders who adore him and can't understand him. Vash, his intended, and her baker family, who already treat him like a son and keep giving him loaves with no stone in.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired and Hollis Brask","why":"They built Hearthward together and never married. Seld thinks that sounds very peaceful and has said so to Vash, who threw a pear at him."},"band":{"act":"Mist-Pear","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Wedding R&B named after a pear, which he thinks is a sign. Vash says it's just a fruit. They're dancing to it in the Beam.","rv_vote":"Mist-Pear, for the wedding, and because Vash told him to."},"vote":{"local":"Doesn't back a Well; says the trees are his Well. Gave the orchard's beamcider to the Wood Well's elders this year as his whole vote.","edict":"Doesn't really care, which he knows is shameful. If the Lightforgers want to count sparks they can count his: none. He's more worried about the Beam."},"wants":"To stand in the Beam for the whole hour and not look away from Vash once.","site":"duskhollow","town":"Duskhollow","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:duskhollow:b":{"name":"Kip","age":82,"role":"retired cap-sorter; the town's memory of cheap caps","look":"Tiny and quick, cropped white hair, rings on every finger she says are all paste; a sorting-apron she still wears though she's not sorted a cap in twenty years.","voice":"Brisk, gossipy, a grandmother's Undertongue with old words in it; changes the subject the moment it gets interesting.","greet":"Ess, dear. Sit by me, out of the Beam. Did they sell you the cider? Overpriced. When I was a girl a cup cost a nod.","lines":["That cap on the counter's been saying the same word for a hundred years. I know what it is. No, I won't. I'm eighty-two. I'm allowed to keep something.","Hush's collectors come every season for the caps. They get the drips. They always get the drips. Someone ought to tell them. Not me.","Grain? You're a good one. This town's got a long memory. Mine's longer."],"about":"I sorted listening-caps for sixty-one years, voice from drip, good from rubbish. I've heard more of other people's lives than anyone in Duskhollow, including Sill, who's only been at it forty. I remember when nobody bought caps at all. I remember the man who started it. I won't say his half-name. He'd hate it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native, old forms"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, old-fashioned"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a little, from Spiritveil buyers who came for old voices"}],"childhood":"Kip was the daughter of a mushroom-gatherer in the Listening Wood back when the caps were picked for soup, not sold. She grew up in the Wood, which was not yet anything special, among caps that whispered faintly if you held them to your ear, and nobody much minded. She used to sit with a basket of caps and listen to them like other children listened to stories. By ten she had favourite voices. By twelve she had noticed that the same voice turned up in caps from different years, and that a cap from the far side of the Wood held her own grandmother singing. She told the gatherers. One of them, a quick, clever young man, listened very hard to her for a whole afternoon, and within five years the caps were being sold.","schooling":"A Well-school in the Wood itself, held in a clearing by a teacher called Hobb, who believed children learned best in the dark and taught letters by touch on wooden boards. No lamp lit at her Lamp Test. She never went further; the Wood was school enough, and the young man who started the cap-trade hired her as his first sorter at fourteen.","history":"For sixty-one years Kip sorted listening-caps, first in a shed, then in the great sorting halls that the trade built. She trained most of the town's sorters, including Sill, the present chief reader, whom she remembers as a small, solemn girl who answered too quickly. She has heard thousands of voices: merchants, lovers, children, a Hush envoy rehearsing a lie, a Stormreach rider shouting an apology on a cap that came up from the gulf tunnels. She married twice, outlived both, and kept a little fortune in caps she bought cheap fifty years ago and has never sold. She retired at seventy-six and now sits by the Beam's edge every noon with whoever will sit with her, telling stories in which she is never quite the hero. The hundred-year cap on Thrum's counter came through her hands when she was twenty-two. She sorted it as 'voice' and has said nothing about it since.","family":"Two husbands, both dead. A daughter, Nerra, a reader in Hush. Grandchildren in Duskhollow who call her 'Old Drip' and come for coin.","hero":{"who":"Vaelora Lanthys","why":"The Spiritveil councillor who chose silence. Kip has known the value of keeping your mouth shut about a good voice since she was twelve, and thinks Vaelora does it beautifully."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Forty-year love songs. She first heard them on a cap from a Spiritveil buyer sixty years ago and their songs are still about the same couple.","rv_vote":"Decades, who'll lose, as she has voted for them losing every year since she was twenty."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Wood Well; she was one of its first members, and some say she still chooses its elders by who she sits beside at noon.","edict":"For, which shocks everyone. She thinks it will keep Lightforge's clerks so busy listening to their own Realm for ten years that they'll want to buy caps of everyone else's."},"wants":"To hear one more new voice in a cap before her ears go.","site":"duskhollow","town":"Duskhollow","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:murkwater":{"name":"Brisk","age":41,"role":"trader, chalk-seller and keeper of the old casters' coin","look":"Wiry, close-shaved, a chalk-dust thumb on every surface he touches, grey fish-scales in his sleeve cuffs; a well-mended coat with seven inside pockets.","voice":"Organised, a bit pompous, keen; loves the words 'motion' and 'meeting', which nobody else in Murkwater uses.","greet":"Ess. Kit, bread, chalk. Chalk is important here. You'll see why.","lines":["Don't bring a mirror in here. Not for fear. It's just bad manners. I sell you chalk instead. Better use.","Fish swim from our ponds down to Duskhollow's sluice and get sold. A fish is a debt that swims. I've written to Duskhollow eleven times. They write back 'it is a fish'.","Every family that boards an old caster here sends coin and doesn't visit. I keep the coin. I'm the one who visits."],"about":"I run the village shop and keep the accounts for the old casters on the Door Wall: who's paid, who's owed, who needs boots. I tried to start a village meeting once. One shift, one vote. Nobody came. I hold it anyway, every month, by myself.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, bureaucratic"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"decent, from a Metalcrown pen-friend"}],"childhood":"Brisk was born to two fish farmers in Murkwater who were clever, poor and disorganised, and he spent his childhood making lists to stop the family going under. At six he kept the household pond records on a slate. At eight he caught his father selling the same fish twice. At ten he organised the children of Murkwater into a rota to carry fish to the old casters on the Door Wall, with a chalk tally on each door, and it worked so well the adults took it over and forgot he'd invented it. He was hurt, and then he was proud, and then he made another list. His hero arrived at eleven, on a leaflet blown down from a Metalcrown petition-carrier passing through the tunnels: a drawing of a foreman with a slogan. He has the leaflet still, framed.","schooling":"Murkwater's Well-school under Gorse, the teacher who could not read and had the old casters chalk the letters; Brisk learned letters from twenty different hands and has the most irregular writing in Shadowdeep. No lamp lit at his Lamp Test; he asked to see the examiner's register and was refused. He taught himself accounts from a Metalcrown ledger-primer bought off a drover.","history":"Brisk took over the village shop at twenty-four and immediately made it the most orderly building in Murkwater, which was not hard. He sells boots, packs, arrows, bread and grey chalk, and from his twenties he has kept the accounts for the casters on the Door Wall, whose families far off send coin and do not visit. He is scrupulous. He visits every one of the thirty-odd old casters monthly, writes down what they need, and buys it. He writes to Duskhollow about the fish quarrel with exhausting regularity and once walked there to read his letter aloud in their square, during the Beam, which nobody had ever done and which got him laughed out. At thirty he tried to start a monthly village meeting, one person one voice, after his foreman hero. Nobody came. He has held it alone every month for eleven years, minutes and all. Last year Nell came. Brisk wrote her attendance down in red.","family":"His parents, still fish-farming and still disorganised. Unmarried; says the casters are his family, which embarrasses everyone. A Metalcrown pen-friend, Holt, who writes from the Warm.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"\"One shift, one vote. Even you.\" Brisk has the old petition leaflet framed over his counter and reads it aloud to himself on meeting days."},"band":{"act":"Shift Change","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Three singers, one per shift, never on stage together. Brisk thinks it's the most organised act in the Nine and has a timetable of who sings which verse.","rv_vote":"Shift Change, and he's written to Holt to coordinate their votes."},"vote":{"local":"Backs no Well; thinks Wells are undemocratic. He votes in his own monthly meeting, unanimously, and sends the minutes to Duskhollow.","edict":"Against, on procedural grounds. He read it twice and says it has no appeal clause, and any law without an appeal is a debt nobody can repay."},"wants":"For three people to come to his village meeting at the same time.","site":"murkwater","town":"Murkwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:murkwater":{"name":"Hent","age":77,"role":"retired Nightblade, now the village's small-forge smith","look":"Gaunt and very upright, white hair cropped close, a scar through one eyebrow; he never looks at the forge's quench-water, which gives back nothing anyway.","voice":"Slow, careful, oddly formal Undertongue; sometimes stops mid-sentence and asks you to say his half-name back to him.","greet":"Ess. Before you ask for anything, would you read my door? There. Thank you. Yes, that's mine. Now, what do you need?","lines":["Don't bring a mirror in here. Not for fear. It's just bad manners. Some of us like not knowing.","Tull chalks his name each dawn. I chalk mine. Some days the hand that writes it doesn't feel like mine. The iron always knows my hammer, though.","The Lost Warden. Nobody knows who. Nobody's meant to. Very restful, that."],"about":"I was a Nightblade for forty years and I've been a smith for twenty. I can't always read my own handwriting now. But I know my hammer-marks on iron better than I know my face. The iron doesn't forget whose it is. That's why I took it up.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, old Order forms"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, military"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good once; slipping"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"some, from Veil Road work"}],"childhood":"Hent was born to a Murmur rope-bridge family and spent his first years scrambling over the chasm on bridges his parents mended, without a thought for the drop. He was strong, quiet and greedy for skill: if someone could do a thing, he wanted to do it better, quietly, and then not mention it. At six he could walk a slack bridge-rope in the dark. At eight he could tie seventeen knots one-handed. At nine the shadow-glass lamp blazed for him so brightly the examiner stepped back. His family were proud and sorry at once, because in Murmur a Full Spark child meant a Nightblade, and a Nightblade meant someone who came home less every year. His mother made him a rope bracelet with his half-name tied into it in knots. He still has it.","schooling":"The Lamp Years in Hush, then the Aether Conclave on Korrhaven, where he earned three knots on his grey sash and was quietly considered the best shadow student of his year; then the Nightblade lower halls at Hollowgate, under an instructor called Varr who taught that the cost was the craft, not a side of it. He remembers every lesson. He doesn't remember Varr's face.","history":"Hent served the Nightblade Orders for forty years. He went down the Veil Roads more times than he can count, which is literally true now. He worked in the Undercrofts under three Realms, and he has kept every secret he was given, including several he no longer remembers. Inversion took him the usual way: his reflection first, in his thirties, then his handwriting, in his fifties. At fifty-seven he came to Murkwater to retire like so many before him, and found he could not sit still. The old village smith was dying, so Hent took the forge and taught himself the trade, slowly, with one rule: always the same hammer, always the same strike, so that every piece he makes carries the same mark. He makes fish-hooks, paddle-blades and pond-gates. He chalks his half-name on his door each morning and sits and checks it. Lom, the cistern girl, reads it to him on bad days, and lately there have been more of those.","family":"His Murmur family, long dead. He was married once, to another Nightblade, Dess, who died on a Veil Road thirty years ago; he remembers her voice but not her face. No children.","hero":{"who":"The Lost Warden","why":"Nobody knows who the Lost Warden was. Hent has spent his old age forgetting who he was, and finds it comforting that someone was a legend without ever needing a name."},"band":{"act":"The Turning","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Goth metal, a singer whose identity is eroding. Hent heard them once in Hush and was the only one in the crowd who knew exactly what the singer was doing, and wept.","rv_vote":"The Turning, while the singer still knows the words."},"vote":{"local":"Backs no Well; Nightblades never do. He votes by what he forges: this year he refused to make hooks for a Hush family that send no coin to their old caster.","edict":"Against, without fuss. He thinks the Orders will see to the registers, and he hopes no one asks him to help."},"wants":"To make one thing at the forge so good he remembers making it.","site":"murkwater","town":"Murkwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:murkwater":{"name":"Lom","age":13,"role":"door-reader for the old casters, and fish-carrier","look":"Gangly, freckled grey with pond-silt, a chalk stub tucked behind each ear; her brother's old climbing gloves, three sizes too big.","voice":"Calm, matter-of-fact, very patient, like an old nurse in a girl's body; reads aloud in a careful sing-song.","greet":"Ess. If you're looking for someone on the Door Wall, I can take you. I know them all. Some of them know themselves.","lines":["Don't bring a mirror in here. Not for fear of anything. It's just bad manners. Tell your friends.","Tull chalks his name each dawn. When he doesn't know the letters, he sits on the step till a child comes by and reads it. Usually that's me. Sometimes I'm late on purpose so someone else gets to.","Grain? For the casters? That's good. They'll forget you gave it. I won't."],"about":"I read the doors. Every morning I walk the Door Wall and read the chalk names back to the old casters who can't tell any more if their hand is theirs. I carry their fish. My brother Rell's a recruit at Hollowgate. I used to do this with him; now I do it on my own.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, speaks the old casters' Order forms too"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a little"}],"childhood":"Lom was born in Murkwater, carried past a stirred pond as a baby so her first sight of water showed her nothing, and grew up following her older brother Rell up and down the Door Wall with fish baskets. While Rell climbed and chattered and listened to the old casters' stories, Lom watched how they looked at their chalk. She learned early that the right way to read a name to an old caster is not to read it at once, but to wait until they ask, and then read it plainly, not kindly. At eight she noticed that one caster, Hent, sometimes wrote a different half-name, and that he never noticed. She did not tell him. She started a list on the inside of her fish basket lid instead, and has kept it for five years.","schooling":"Murkwater's Well-school, still taught by Gorse, who can't read; the casters chalk the lessons. Lom can read every caster's hand in the village, which is a skill nobody else has. At her Lamp Test nothing lit. She told the examiner it was fine, someone in the family had to stay home.","history":"Since Rell left for Hollowgate two years ago, Lom has done the Door Wall alone. She walks it at dawn, every day, thirty doors, reading names to anyone who asks. She has learned the casters' moods, their old Order slang, which ones want company and which want to be left on the step. She brings Tull his fish and reads his name three mornings in five. She takes Hent his bread and reads his door, and on the days the chalk says something else, she reads it as Hent anyway, and adds the other name to her list. She has become, without meaning to, the person Murkwater trusts to tell the old casters who they are. The adults are a little afraid of her. Brisk, the trader, asked her to minute his village meeting; she went once, wrote 'Brisk. Nell. Me.' and left.","family":"Parents, fish farmers; brother Rell at Hollowgate, who writes badly-spelled letters she corrects and sends back. Her Keeper is an old caster on the Wall, Varn, which is very unusual for a child.","hero":{"who":"Tull","why":"Tull sits on his step until a child reads his name, and never gets cross when they're late. Lom thinks that's the bravest thing she's ever watched anyone do."},"band":{"act":"The Hollows Kids","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Blades on stone, hand signs, no singing. She can do every sign, and she likes that nobody can tell whose voice it is, because there aren't any.","rv_vote":"The Hollows Kids, because Rell wants Knife Dance and someone has to disagree with him."},"vote":{"local":"Too young. She'd back the casters' own Well if they had one. She's thinking of starting one.","edict":"Against, though she asked Hent what it was and he forgot halfway through explaining, and she thinks that's what an Edict would do to all of them."},"wants":"For every caster on the Wall to recognise their own name one more time each.","site":"murkwater","town":"Murkwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:murkwater:b":{"name":"Nell","age":36,"role":"midwife and pond-keeper; does the Clouding at every birth","look":"Solid, red-cheeked, grey silt to the elbows, a long paddle carried everywhere like a walking stick; neither man's wrap nor woman's but a midwife's smock.","voice":"Warm, frank, unfussed by anything; uses 'they' for themself and corrects nobody else's mistakes about it.","greet":"Ess. Mind the paddle, it's not a stick, it's a job. Don't bring a mirror in here. Not for fear. Just manners.","lines":["When a baby's born here I stir the pond before they're carried past. First sight of water shows them nothing. Best gift Murkwater gives.","The old casters come here because the water won't show them their own faces. You'd think that was sad. Mostly it's restful. Mostly.","Grain's welcome. Fish is better. Coin's best. Kindness is cheapest and I'll take that too."],"about":"I deliver babies and ease the old ones out, and in between I keep three ponds of blind-fish. Murkwater's a birth-and-death sort of place. I'm neither he nor she; the babies don't mind and the old casters can't remember to.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, midwives' gentle forms"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a few death-rite phrases from a Spiritveil midwife"}],"childhood":"Nell was the child of a Murkwater midwife and a dredger, and grew up between births and silt. Their mother took them to their first delivery at seven, to hold the lamp and keep quiet, and they did both perfectly until the baby cried and Nell laughed out loud with joy. Their mother sent them out to stir the pond, and so Nell did their first Clouding by accident, a child with a paddle stirring grey water while a new baby was carried past. They have done it at every birth since. They were a cheerful, unbothered child who picked up stray fish, injured bats and lonely old casters, and brought them all home, and their father built them a shed for the bats and gave up arguing about the rest.","schooling":"Murkwater's Well-school under Gorse, then nine years at their mother's side, which they call the real school. Lamp Test: nothing. At twenty they walked to Spiritveil's border with a midwives' caravan and spent a season with a Lanthic-speaking midwife, Thessaly, who taught them how to sit with the dying as well as the born.","history":"Nell took over their mother's work at twenty-four and has delivered most of the children in Murkwater under the age of twelve, including Lom, who they're fond of. They keep three blind-fish ponds for coin, dredge silt for potters when the ponds need clearing, and spend more nights than they'd like by the beds of old casters on the Door Wall, holding hands and telling the dying their own names. Murkwater considers them a necessity, like salt. They had a long, happy love affair with a Duskhollow cap-sorter that ended without a fight when the sorter moved to Hush, and they still send each other fish and caps. Last year, out of pity, they went to Brisk's lonely village meeting and found to their horror that they liked it. They have been to every one since and now chair the second half.","family":"Their mother, retired, who criticises their stirring. A brother in the dredging trade. The Duskhollow cap-sorter, Rhen, in Hush, who still sends caps.","hero":{"who":"Saelen of the Drift","why":"The Lantern of the Dead. Thessaly told Nell about Saelen on the Spiritveil border, and they decided someone who kept the dead company was a better hero than anyone with a sword."},"band":{"act":"Paper Lantern Funeral","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Doom-gaze, slow and kind. They play it by deathbeds, very softly, when the family says yes.","rv_vote":"Paper Lantern Funeral, because nobody else in Murkwater will."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the dredgers' small Well, the one that holds the silt-channels, because it pays for the casters' coffins.","edict":"Torn. Nell thinks a midwife should know what a child is born with, spark or not; they also think nobody in Lightforge should know it, ever, and can't make the two halves meet."},"wants":"To deliver a baby at the same hour an old caster dies, so the two can be named together.","site":"murkwater","town":"Murkwater","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:unmadecity_vf1":{"name":"Fid","age":34,"role":"salt-shore trader; sells the cheap grey salt to Stormreach and sleeps fine","look":"Round-faced, cheerful, a salt-crust on his eyebrows he never quite washes out; a Stormreach drover's scarf he won at dice and wears to put buyers at ease.","voice":"Jolly, rapid, a born haggler; drops in Gale exclamations to make Stormreachers feel at home, and gets them slightly wrong.","greet":"Wahai! Ess! Both! Come in, salt, kit, bread. Best black salt in the Nine, or the second best, depending who's asking.","lines":["Up the terraces they think salt is simple. Taste a slab from the Roost side and you'll know it isn't. Or rather, don't. Theirs is worse.","The stake on the boundary moves a hand's width every winter. The tide does it. The tide's very partial to whoever's telling the story.","Stormreach buys our grey salt by the barrel and calls it storm-salt. It's floor-sweepings. They're happy. I'm happy. That's trade."],"about":"I run the shop on the salt shore and sell to the Stormreach drovers who come round by the sea tunnels. I'm not ashamed of anything I've sold. Ask anyone. Well, ask anyone from here.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, haggler's"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"enthusiastic, inaccurate"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Fid was the only child of two salt-cutters who both wanted him to cut salt and were both disappointed, gently, every day. He could not hold a chisel straight. He could, however, sell anything, and at seven he sold his father's broken cutting-chisel to a Hush buyer as an 'heirloom' for more than a new one cost. His father was so impressed he forgot to be angry. Fid spent the rest of his childhood running errands for the village trader, an old woman called Reth who taught him that a buyer who leaves happy has not been cheated, whatever they paid. He believed her completely. He also believed, from about eight, that he was the funniest person on the shore, and nobody has had the heart to argue.","schooling":"The salt shore's two-room Well-school under Brann, a retired cutter who taught sums with salt crystals and hand-cant with salt in your palm so you'd remember the grit. No lamp lit at Fid's Lamp Test; he tried to sell the examiner a slab on the way out. He never cared for letters but learned Bridge and Gale numbers from drovers by fifteen.","history":"Fid took over Reth's shop at twenty when she retired to her cousin's on the Roost side, which the salt shore considered treason. He has been selling ever since: black salt to Hush for fish, kit to anyone heading out along the sea tunnels, and coarse grey salt to Stormreach traders, who come round by sea in the dry season and buy it as 'storm-salt' for their strike-goat. He doesn't know who first told them it was special and doesn't intend to correct it. He has a mild, ongoing feud with the Roost side over the boundary stake and the strip of shore between the villages, mostly conducted by leaving salt on their doorsteps at night. Two years ago a Stormreach buyer found out the salt was cheap, came back, stood on the cliff and shouted a furious apology at Fid for having been fooled, then bought twice as much. Fid has never understood Stormreach and loves it.","family":"Both parents still cutting, still disappointed, still proud. A wife, Hessa, who cuts salt better than either of them and thinks he's ridiculous. A baby daughter who had the salt on her tongue at last season's first cut and spat.","hero":{"who":"The Flameborne King","why":"A king you can refuse to his face, and who has to answer with bread. Fid thinks that's the best bargain anyone's ever struck, and would like to haggle with him once."},"band":{"act":"Wahai!","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Party reggaeton with pipes. The drovers play it on the shore in the dry season and Fid dances to it worse than anyone has ever danced.","rv_vote":"Wahai!, because the drovers will ask, and a happy buyer's a buyer."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Salt Well, the shore families who hold the old sea tunnels, by keeping the drovers' tolls cheap so nobody looks too hard at what else comes in.","edict":"Torn. Against it in principle, he says, but if it means more Stormreachers fleeing round by sea, he'll need more grey salt."},"wants":"To sell salt to someone from every Realm in the Nine, and he's missing Frostgrave.","site":"unmadecity_vf1","town":"Hush Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:unmadecity_vf1":{"name":"Ruk","age":55,"role":"salt-chisel smith; forges each family's slant","look":"Big-shouldered, close-cropped grey hair, salt-cracked knuckles, a chisel worn on a cord round her neck like a pendant.","voice":"Flat and slow, every word stressed at the end like a hammer-blow; dry jokes you only get later.","greet":"Ess. Chisels. Hooks. Hinges. That order. What's yours?","lines":["Look at the Cutting Shelf. A thousand years of tool-marks, every family a different slant. I make the slant. Get it wrong, you've cut someone else's salt.","The boundary stake moves every winter. Tide does it. Or a cousin on the Roost side with a mallet. One of the two.","The storm tide that took the lower shelf? I was nine. We rebuilt without a word. I've not stopped hammering since."],"about":"I forge salt-chisels. Every family on the shelf has its own angle, and I keep the patterns in my hands. My aunt raised me after the tide took the lower shelf. She was a smith. So am I.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"plain"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a little, from a Stonehold drover who sold her ore"}],"childhood":"Ruk was nine when the storm tide came up over the lower shelf at night and took the pans, the huts, and her parents, who had gone back for the salt. She was asleep in her aunt's hut on the higher ground. In the morning there was nothing below but black water and the tide-line, and her aunt, a smith called Gorn, took her by the wrist and walked her to the forge and gave her a hammer. They did not speak of it. Nobody on the shelf did; the families rebuilt higher without a word. Ruk spent the rest of her childhood at the forge, beating out chisels for the rebuilding, and found that the noise filled the space. She was a fierce, solid girl, quick to fight, and Gorn let her.","schooling":"The salt shore's Well-school under Brann, who taught her hand-cant with salt in her palm; she preferred the forge and was there more than at school. No lamp lit at her Lamp Test. Gorn taught her the slants: each family's chisel angle, kept only in the smith's hands, never drawn, never written.","history":"Ruk has forged every chisel on the Cutting Shelf for thirty years, since Gorn's hands went. She holds about forty family slants in her memory, the exact angle at which each cuts the black salt so their lines run their own way across the cliff, and when she forges a new chisel for a family she does it with her eyes shut. She also makes hooks for the fish-salters, hinges for the drying sheds and, for coin, the occasional heavy door for Hush. She has opinions about Stonehold steel, which she buys from a drover who comes round by the sea tunnels once a year, a Holdspeech-speaking man called Durrin who taught her the word 'karn' and laughs at her accent. Ruk has never married, has many friends who are afraid of her, and once a season goes down to the tide-line below the rebuilt shelf and hammers one plain stake into the black sand where her parents' hut stood. The tide takes it every time.","family":"Her aunt Gorn, now very old, who lives with her. Her parents, taken by the storm tide. A cousin on the Roost side she hasn't spoken to in twelve years, over the stake.","hero":{"who":"Her aunt Gorn","why":"Gorn handed her a hammer the morning after the tide and never once asked how she felt. Ruk thinks that was the kindest thing anyone has ever done for her."},"band":{"act":"Deep Hold","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Slow, heavy-flow rap: 'Ask who's asking.' Durrin played it for her on a cap and she liked that it was like hammering, every word landing hard at the end.","rv_vote":"Deep Hold, and if Stonehold doesn't win she'll blame Lightforge."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Salt Well, firmly, and made the chisels for its new tunnel-door for free.","edict":"Against. She'd like to see a Lightforge auditor try to measure a spark on someone who's been holding a hammer since the morning after a storm tide."},"wants":"For one of her tide-line stakes to still be standing in the morning.","site":"unmadecity_vf1","town":"Hush Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:unmadecity_vf1":{"name":"Tamm","age":10,"role":"youngest of a cutting family; salt on the tongue this season","look":"Skinny, salt-white knees, a gap where a front tooth was, a carved salt-crystal on a string that he says is a charm and absolutely isn't.","voice":"Loud for Shadowdeep, excitable, interrupts himself; does impressions of grown-ups.","greet":"Ess! I'm having the salt this year! On my tongue! At first cut! I can't swallow till the slab's free. I've been practising with a spoon of water. I've swallowed it every time.","lines":["Up the terraces they think salt is simple. Old Vek licks a slab and says what year it was laid. He's wrong one time in three. I counted.","The stake moves every winter. My gran says the tide does it. My uncle says the Roost lot do it. I say I'm going to sit on it all night and see.","Grain? For us? I'll tell my gran. She'll pretend she doesn't care. She cares."],"about":"I'm Tamm and I'm the youngest in my family so I get the salt at first cut this season. I've got a full name too, my gran's my Keeper, and I'm not telling you, not even the first letter. Ask me something else. Ask me about Updraft.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"good, fidgety"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words from drovers, mostly swears"}],"childhood":"Tamm is the youngest of seven in a cutting family, which means he has been the baby for ten years and is sick of it. His family's slant on the Cutting Shelf runs steeply left, and he can pick their old lines out of the cliff from the shore, which he does to anyone who will stand still. He follows old Vek about, the oldest cutter, and watches him lick slabs and name the year, and keeps a tally on a shell of how often Vek is right. He has been sent home from the shelf three times for climbing it. He has a pet cliff-crab called Fid, after the trader, which the trader finds hilarious and his mother does not.","schooling":"The salt shore's Well-school under Brann, now very old, who still makes the children hold salt in their palms while they sign. Tamm is bad at hand-cant because he can't keep still. He took the Lamp Test last season: the Tempest lamp, of all things, gave a tiny blue spark and went out. The examiner shrugged. Tamm has told every drover since that he's 'part Stormreach'.","history":"This is the season. At first cut, when the families gather on the Cutting Shelf and the oldest cutter makes the season's opening line, Tamm's gran will lay a pinch of black salt on his tongue and he must not swallow until the first slab is lifted free. It can take a long time. His older sister swallowed hers and the family has never let her forget it. Tamm has practised for months, with spoons of water, with pebbles, with his own spit, and failed every time. He has decided the trick is to think of something else, and the something else he has chosen is the Updraft song 'Freefall', which he sings in his head from start to finish, all eleven minutes. He has also, privately, decided that if he manages it he will ask Vek to teach him to lick salt properly, because one in three wrong is not good enough.","family":"Parents, six siblings, a gran who is his Keeper, and a cliff-crab. An uncle who swears the Roost lot move the stake and has been banned from saying so at meals.","hero":{"who":"Vek, the oldest cutter","why":"Vek can taste what year salt was laid. Tamm thinks being wrong one time in three is still amazing and wants to be wrong one time in four."},"band":{"act":"Updraft","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Trance, 'Freefall', which a drover played on the shore once and Tamm hasn't stopped humming since. It's his salt-on-the-tongue song.","rv_vote":"Updraft in the Sparkbeat Cup, obviously, because he's part Stormreach now."},"vote":{"local":"He's ten. He backs the Salt Well because his gran does, and because he wants to go in the old sea tunnels one day.","edict":"Against, mostly because he thinks if Lightforge registers his tiny blue spark they'll take him to Stormreach, and actually, now he thinks of it, that might be good. So: torn."},"wants":"To hold the salt on his tongue until the slab is free.","site":"unmadecity_vf1","town":"Hush Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:unmadecity_vf1:b":{"name":"Gyle","age":68,"role":"retired cutter and keeper of the boundary stake's 'true' place","look":"Bent, sinewy, salt-white braids pinned up with fish-bone, a walking stick notched with forty winters of stake measurements.","voice":"Precise, pedantic, extremely pleased with herself; says 'technically' a great deal and enjoys it.","greet":"Ess. You'll have come about the stake. Everyone comes about the stake. Sit, it's a long story and I've got the notches.","lines":["The tide moves the stake a hand's width every winter, always in the direction that suits whoever's telling it. Technically, I'm the one telling it. So.","Up the terraces they think salt is simple. Taste a Roost-side slab and you'll know it isn't. It's worse. Technically worse.","Black-salted blind-fish keeps for years. I've a slab older than the trader. Slice it thin, a little glim. That's living."],"about":"I cut salt for fifty years and now I measure the stake. Every winter I walk down, notch my stick, and write it in my head. I know exactly where the boundary was in my grandmother's day. The Roost side knows I know. That's why they bring me moth fritters.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native, pedantic"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few words: 'probably', 'kept', from a Frostgrave salt-buyer long ago"}],"childhood":"Gyle was the clever, annoying child of two salt-cutters, the one who corrected her elders' sums and won. She liked rules, edges and exact places: where one family's slant ended and another's began, which pan belonged to whom, where the stake had stood on which date. Her grandmother, a famous cutter who could split a slab along a single layer, took her down to the boundary every winter from the age of five to measure the stake, and taught her that a boundary was not a line but a memory, and whoever remembered best won. Gyle took this very seriously. At eleven she won an argument with the Roost side's eldest by reciting the stake's position for nineteen consecutive winters. The eldest gave her a jar of glim and told her she'd be trouble.","schooling":"The salt shore's Well-school under a teacher called Mesk, before Brann, who taught by contradiction and was delighted to be contradicted back. Gyle adored her. No lamp lit at the Lamp Test; she asked the examiner how the lamps were calibrated. Afterwards she learned the slants and the stake from her grandmother, which she counts as her real schooling.","history":"Gyle cut salt for fifty years on her family's slant, one of the shallowest on the shelf, and was known for slabs as even as glass. She married a quiet fish-salter, Werr, who let her be right about everything and died, she says, of agreeing too much. She retired from the shelf at sixty and turned full-time to the stake. Every winter she walks down to it, notches the old stick that was her grandmother's, and adds the year's position to the forty-odd she holds in memory. She is consulted by both villages and trusted by neither, which suits her. She keeps a running correspondence by moth fritter and salt with the Roost side's elders, who send her treats to keep her favourable and get, in return, exact and devastating corrections. She has noticed, privately, that the stake did not move with the tide at all one winter, nearly forty years back, but moved two hands at once. She has never said so. She is waiting to see who confesses.","family":"Her husband Werr, dead fifteen years. Two daughters, both cutters, who think the stake is ridiculous. A Roost-side cousin she likes far more than she admits.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"A Frostgrave Seer who knotted a vision so it couldn't slip. Gyle heard the story from a salt-buyer and thought: that's what I do with the stake."},"band":{"act":"Slow Heat","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Half-speed reggaeton. Gyle says it's the only dance music that respects an old woman's knees.","rv_vote":"Slow Heat, technically for the knees."},"vote":{"local":"Backs neither Well that claims the strip, on principle, and makes sure both know she could tip it either way.","edict":"Against, with a footnote: she thinks it's badly drafted, and says so in long hand-cant sentences to anyone who brings her fish."},"wants":"For whoever moved the stake forty years ago to confess to her, specifically.","site":"unmadecity_vf1","town":"Hush Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:unmadecity_vf2":{"name":"Vosk","age":46,"role":"Roost trader; second hand in the guano chain to Lightforge","look":"Lean, sharp-nosed, bat-leather gloves he never takes off, neat as a clerk; smells faintly of guano no matter what he does.","voice":"Smooth, amused, very clean Bridge; enjoys a joke more when the buyer doesn't get it.","greet":"Ess. Packs, bows, bread, moth fritters if you're brave. Next time you eat a glassfruit, think of us.","lines":["The finest orchard dung in the Nine comes off our cave floors. Lightforge's growers on the Pillar Road buy it through three sets of hands. They've no idea. We've never laughed so much.","Dree keeps the guano ledger. There's a column in it with only a sun at the top. Ask her what it means. She'll smile. I know what it means. I'll smile too.","The salt shore says we move the stake. We say the tide does. We also say the salt shore's salt is gritty, which is true."],"about":"I sell kit and moth fritters, and I'm the second pair of hands between our dung and the Lightforge orchards. I take the sacks to the Hollow Market broker, the broker takes them to a Rim merchant, the merchant sells to the Pillar Road. I've never been to Lightforge. I don't need to. They eat what we give them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, polished"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; reads Day-Books for fun"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Vosk grew up on the cliff face of the Roost, in a family of guano diggers, and spent his early childhood in a sack: his mother dug with him tied to her back in a guano sack so the bats wouldn't nip his ears. He came out of it with a strong stomach, a good nose and no fear of anything that smells. A sly, quick child, he liked to know what people wanted before they did. At nine he worked out that the Hush broker who came for the guano paid more on days when the bats' outflight had been thick, and started telling his mother which days to sell. At eleven he bought a Lightforge Day-Book off a Hollow Market stall and read it cover to cover, laughing, and has been reading Lightforgers' private days ever since.","schooling":"The Roost's Well-school under a teacher called Thask, who held classes at the cave mouth during the outflight so children learned to keep their nerve and their signs steady in the noise. No lamp lit at Vosk's Lamp Test. He taught himself Clear from Day-Books and, later, from receipts, and still reads it better than he speaks it.","history":"Vosk became the Roost's trader at twenty-five, and the second hand in the guano chain at thirty, when the old broker's runner retired and recommended him. It was the old runner who told him the truth: that the guano went, via a Hush broker and a Rim merchant, to the glassfruit growers of the Pillar Road, and that nobody along the chain had ever told the growers. Vosk thought it was the funniest thing he had ever heard. He still does. He runs the sacks down through the sea tunnel under the disputed strip at night, which is why the Roost side will never give up the strip, and he sells moth fritters, kit and bat-leather goods in the village shop by day. He reads the Rim merchant's receipts and occasionally the Lightforge growers' published Day-Books, which mention their excellent new dung and thank 'a reliable supplier'. He has kept every one. He has a small collection of glassfruit stones he plants on the cliff, which never grow.","family":"His wife Tarra, a moth-netter, who thinks his joke has gone on too long. Two children who can count bats better than him.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"The Stormreach Warden who wouldn't cut his citadel's chain. Vosk likes people who hold on to something absurd past the point of sense, and thinks guano to Lightforge is his chain."},"band":{"act":"Corran Vale","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Storm-shouted apology rap. Vosk finds apology funny, as a concept, and Corran Vale shouts them better than anyone.","rv_vote":"Corran Vale, because Stormreach never apologises to Shadowdeep in the right direction and someone ought to reward the effort."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Roost Well, which holds the sea tunnel, by running its cargo and taking its share without argument.","edict":"Against, though he says it's funny: Lightforge is about to register every spark in the Nine and still doesn't know where its dung comes from."},"wants":"To be there, in person, the day a Pillar Road grower finds out.","site":"unmadecity_vf2","town":"Hush Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:unmadecity_vf2":{"name":"Ebb","age":31,"role":"smith of net-frames, tanning knives and lock-soft bat-leather tools","look":"Slight and long-fingered, hair dyed with inkroot in stripes, bat-leather apron soft as skin; neither man nor woman and dresses like a clever thief.","voice":"Quick, technical, enthusiastic about mechanisms; explains how things work whether you ask or not.","greet":"Ess! Do you have a lock that sticks? I love a lock that sticks. No? A net? A knife? I'll take anything with a hinge in it.","lines":["Bat-wing leather makes Nightblade gloves soft enough to work a lock in. I make the knives that tan it, and I test the gloves on my own door. My door's tired.","The Count's coming: sit on the cliff at outflight and count the bats. Nobody ever finishes. I tried building a counting-wheel once. It finished before I did. Disqualified.","Next time you eat a glassfruit, think of us. Everybody says that. I say it because I'm proud of the dung. Is that strange? It's very good dung."],"about":"I'm the Roost's smith, though really I'm a tinkerer who got stuck with a forge. I make net frames, tanning knives and little tools for the leather. I'd like to make a machine one day. Shadowdeep isn't keen on machines. Metalcrown is. Make of that what you will.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, uses fiddly technical signs"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"good, self-taught from Metalcrown manuals"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Ebb was born to a moth-netter and a bat-leather tanner, and was the child who took apart everything in the house to see how it worked. They dismantled their father's tanning frame at five and reassembled it so it worked better. At seven they took a Hush lock apart and could not get it back together and the whole family had to sleep with the door propped. Their parents, who expected a netter, got an inventor, and handled it with tolerance and many spare hinges. Ebb never cared about being a he or a she; the Roost gave them the half-name early and stopped there. At ten a Metalcrown drover passing through the sea tunnel left a broken clockwork moth at the shop, and Ebb fixed it in a week. It still flies, badly, in their forge.","schooling":"The Roost's Well-school under Thask, at the cave mouth in the outflight; Ebb spent most lessons watching the bats' wings rather than Thask's hands. No lamp lit at the Lamp Test, and Ebb asked to take a lamp apart. Afterwards they apprenticed to the old Roost smith, Grell, and taught themselves Shiftcant from smuggled Metalcrown engine manuals.","history":"Ebb became the Roost's smith at twenty-four when Grell's eyes went, and immediately began improving everything: net frames with folding joints, moth nets on spring-poles, tanning knives with angled blades that scrape bat-wing leather thin enough to see through. Their bat-leather lock-gloves, made with the tanner families, are bought by Nightblade quartermasters, including Drav at Hollowgate, and are the best in the Nine. Ebb has a growing, unsanctioned interest in Metalcrown: they read engine manuals bought through the Hollow Market, keep a jar of synthetic-spark filings they will not explain, and once tried to build a bat-counting wheel for the Count, which was disqualified for finishing. They have written, twice, to the Metalcrown Synthesis Council asking to visit a Reactor Hall. The second reply came back unopened, stamped USEFUL, which Ebb has decided is encouraging.","family":"Both parents, still netting and tanning, still finding Ebb's parts in the soup. A sister, Shen's mother, who is the Roost's best netter. No partner; Ebb says machines are less trouble and is wrong.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright and Idris Hollowfire","why":"They built the first Spark Reactor. Ebb thinks making a spark where there wasn't one is the greatest trick anyone ever pulled, and wants to know how it works more than anything."},"band":{"act":"The Fitted","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"A half-synthetic voice singing 'Unplated'. Ebb is fascinated by how the voice is made and has listened to the cap so many times it's gone thin.","rv_vote":"The Fitted, in the Sparkbeat Cup, against Lights Out, which has made Shen furious."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Roost Well, because it buys their gloves, and slips an extra pair to its elders before every season's decision.","edict":"Against, and interested: Ebb wants to take a Lightforge lamp-check lantern apart to see how it measures a spark, and has a drover looking for one."},"wants":"To stand inside a Metalcrown Reactor Hall and watch a spark being made.","site":"unmadecity_vf2","town":"Hush Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:unmadecity_vf2":{"name":"Shen","age":14,"role":"moth-netter and the Roost's champion bat-counter","look":"Compact, wiry, moth-dust on her cheeks like freckles, hair cropped very short so nothing catches in a net; counts on her fingers without knowing she's doing it.","voice":"Intense, fast, numerical; uses exact figures for everything, including her moods.","greet":"Ess. Sorry, I was counting. Forty-one moths in that net, eleven too small. What do you need?","lines":["The Count's at outflight. Every child sits on the cliff and counts. Nobody ever finishes. Last year I got to three hundred and eighteen thousand before I fell asleep. I'll beat it.","Dree's ledger has a column with only a sun on it. I've counted the marks in it. Four hundred and two. I don't know what they are. I want to.","Grain's good. The Roost counts kindness, too. We just don't say the number."],"about":"I net moths and I count bats. I'm the best counter in the Roost, which isn't bragging, it's arithmetic. I'm going to finish the Count one day. Nobody believes me. Lyriel Frostwraith warned everyone about something once, and nobody believed her either.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, uses number-signs very fast"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"school-level"}],"childhood":"Shen was born the year the Roost's outflight was the thickest anyone could remember, and her mother, a netter, says she came out counting. She counted everything from the beginning: steps, moths, the lumps in her porridge, the seconds between bats. At six she could estimate a net-full of moths to within five. At eight she worked out how fast the outflight left the Roost and tried to calculate how many bats lived there, and the number frightened her so much she cried. Ebb, her mother's sibling, sat with her and showed her how to break a big number into parts. She has trusted Ebb above all adults since. She dislikes surprises, loves rules, and has never once lost a game of chance on the cliff, which the other children consider suspicious.","schooling":"The Roost's Well-school under Thask, during the outflight; Shen is the only child in living memory to sign her sums faster than the bats left. At her Lamp Test the Chrona lamp, of all things, gave one slow pulse, which made the examiner frown. Half-Spark, unexpected. Shen calculated afterwards the odds of a Chrona flicker in Shadowdeep and says they're very poor.","history":"Shen has been in the Count every year since she was four. She has improved every year: forty thousand, eighty, a hundred and fifty, and last year three hundred and eighteen thousand, before she fell asleep on the cliff and was carried home. The record, held by an old netter called Brist, is three hundred and forty thousand. Shen means to beat it this season. She has prepared like a soldier: sleeping by day, training her eyes on falling ash, counting in groups of ten using a hand-cant trick Ebb taught her. She also nets moths for fritters with her mother and keeps the tally for the net families. She has started, on her own, counting the sun-marks in Dree's guano ledger, which she glimpsed once and has memorised; there are four hundred and two, and they do not match any guano delivery she can find. She has not told anyone. She is waiting till she has a pattern.","family":"Her mother, a netter; a father who digs guano and falls asleep at every Count; Ebb, the smith, her mother's sibling and her favourite person in the Nine.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"She counted what was coming and warned the Council, and they struck her from the record. Shen thinks being right and not believed is the worst thing that can happen to a person, and wants to be right anyway."},"band":{"act":"Lights Out","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Dark techno played in total darkness, 'No Faces'. Shen likes that nobody can see anybody and she can count the beats without being looked at.","rv_vote":"Lights Out in the Sparkbeat Cup, against Ebb's Fitted, and she's not speaking to Ebb about it."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for the Wells, but she has calculated which Well wins most often and backs that one, which her mother says is cheating.","edict":"Against. She heard the registers would count every spark, and she thinks counting people isn't the same as counting bats, and she's angry she can't explain why."},"wants":"To finish the Count, or at least beat Brist's three hundred and forty thousand.","site":"unmadecity_vf2","town":"Hush Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:unmadecity_vf2:b":{"name":"Cray","age":59,"role":"guano digger who once went to Lightforge and ate a glassfruit","look":"Broad, stooped, very strong, a back like a stone wall, a guano-stained scarf over his nose and mouth that he lowers to talk; one Lightforge pierced-silver witness coin on a thong round his neck.","voice":"Slow, solemn, oddly formal Bridge; tells jokes completely deadpan and waits to see if you've noticed.","greet":"Ess. You'll want to step back. Not from me. From the smell. That's the Roost. You get used to it in about forty years.","lines":["Next time you eat a glassfruit, think of us. I've eaten one. In Lightforge. Off the Pillar Road. I thought of us. Tasted fine.","The salt shore says we move the stake. We don't. We've no need. The tide's on our side. It's a reasonable tide.","Give grain to the Roost and the Roost remembers. We dig dung for a living. We know about things that last."],"about":"I dig guano. Fifty years of it. Once, at thirty, I walked to Lightforge to see where it went and stood on the Pillar Road at noon with dung under my nails, barefoot, and ate a glassfruit. Best day of my life. Don't tell them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, formal"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting, learned for the trip"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Cray was the eldest son of the eldest digging family on the Roost and was handed a short shovel at five, a real one at eight, and his father's at fourteen. He liked the work. He was slow and strong and patient and did not mind the smell, and he found the cave floor a peaceful place: dark, warm, the bats chittering overhead. He was not a curious child; he did not want to know where the guano went, or why the broker paid so much. Then, at twelve, an old runner told him, as a joke, that it went to Lightforge, to the most famous orchards in the Nine, and Cray, who had never been curious about anything, could not stop thinking about it. He dug for eighteen more years and saved.","schooling":"The Roost's Well-school under the teacher before Thask, an old woman called Wem, who taught letters by scratching them in guano and made every child clean the board. No lamp lit at his Lamp Test; he thanked the examiner. He learned Clear at twenty-nine from a Hush tutor, for the trip, slowly, in two hundred lessons.","history":"At thirty Cray took his savings, his Clear and a clean shirt and walked out of Shadowdeep along the trade roads, through the Rim and up the Pillar Road to the glassfruit orchards. He told nobody why. He walked the road barefoot at noon with the Lightforgers at Unshadowing, because a woman at an inn told him it was the custom. He stood in the orchards. A grower, a cheerful old man, gave him a glassfruit off a tree and asked, in his Day-Book way, what had brought him so far, and Cray, who could not lie in Clear, said he had come to see the orchards' dung. The grower laughed and wrote it down. Cray came home with a witness coin, a full belly and a happiness he has never quite explained. He has dug guano for twenty-nine more years since, married late to a widow, Asha, and has never told the Roost that he once told a Pillar Road grower the truth, to his face, and was not believed.","family":"His wife Asha, a moth-netter, whom he met at the Count, both too old to win. Two stepchildren, grown. His father, long dead, whose shovel he still uses.","hero":{"who":"High Councillor Arath","why":"\"Nobody wants this. That's why it has to be done properly.\" Cray heard the line quoted by a drover and thought it was the truest thing ever said about digging dung, whatever else the man is doing."},"band":{"act":"The Glare","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Arena rock he heard at an inn on the Pillar Road the night after the glassfruit. He associates them with the happiest day of his life and can't help it.","rv_vote":"The Glare, quietly, and he'll bear it if the Roost finds out."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Roost Well, as his family always has, by digging an extra sack for it every season without being asked.","edict":"For, which nobody in the Roost knows. He thinks Lightforge means it honestly and is wrong, and that being registered is no worse than being the man who said the dung out loud."},"wants":"To go back to the Pillar Road once more with Asha, and buy her a glassfruit.","site":"unmadecity_vf2","town":"Hush Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:hollowgate_vf1":{"name":"Wist","age":52,"role":"rope-walk trader; sells silent cord and chases Murmur's late payments","look":"Tall, rangy, flax-burned palms, a coil of grey climbing cord slung over one shoulder like a sash, and a list of Murmur debts knotted into it.","voice":"Brisk, impatient, very funny when cross, which is often; ties knots while she talks.","greet":"Ess. Cord, kit, flatbread. If you're from Murmur, you owe me. If you're not, you're welcome.","lines":["Ask anyone in Murmur where the bridges come from. They'll tell you Murmur. Kept. They also pay late. Also kept.","Hollowgate borrows the Long Walk for drills and tangles the twist. Our rope, their boots. Guess who apologises. Not them. Not me either.","Silent cord's what Nightblades climb on. Doesn't creak, doesn't sing, doesn't tell. Best thing we make. Murmur would claim that too if they could."],"about":"I sell the village's rope and cord and chase Murmur for what it owes. I know every bridge in Murmur by the length it took on the Walk. I've a knot in this cord for every late payment. It's getting heavy.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, rope-walk signs"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"some, from chain-yard buyers"}],"childhood":"Wist was the third child of two spinners on the Long Walk and spent her childhood walking backwards, feeding flax into the twist beside her parents, until she could walk the half-mile backwards blind without a stumble. She was a cross, restless child who hated slowness. The Walk is slow. She was sent, at nine, to carry the finished rope to Murmur with the old carters, and fell in love with the bridges, swaying over the chasm on her rope, and then, at the end, watched a Murmur bridge-master tell an inspector the rope was 'Murmur-laid'. She shouted at him. The carters took her home and her mother laughed till she cried and said the girl would make a trader.","schooling":"The rope-walk's Well-school, held on the Walk itself under a teacher called Thren who made the children sign letters while walking backwards so they learned both at once. Wist was best at both. No lamp lit at her Lamp Test; she said good, she'd no time for it. She learned accounts from her mother's knotted cords, the old way.","history":"Wist became the village's trader at twenty-six and its unofficial debt-collector at twenty-seven, after Murmur paid for a whole season's cable with a promise and a sack of moths. She keeps the village's accounts in knots on a climbing cord over her shoulder, one knot for every late payment from Murmur, and she visits the city four times a year to stand on its bridges and recite them. She has known every Murmur bridge-master of the last twenty-five years and been thrown off three bridges, metaphorically. She also handles the village's quarrel with Hollowgate over the Long Walk drills; the garrison borrows it for running practice and tangles the drying twist, and Wist sends Hollowgate a bill every season that Lune at Quietwater advised her how to write. Wist and Lune write to each other often; they have never met. Wist married a quiet spinner, Fosk, who walks backwards beside her every day and never says a word about the knots.","family":"Her husband Fosk, a spinner. A son on the Walk, a daughter in Murmur married to a bridge-master, which Wist considers a betrayal and enjoys enormously.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"The anchor-master who held Korr's Span. Stormreach riders throw stones at his Stillman, and Wist thinks that's what happens to anyone who holds a rope for someone else."},"band":{"act":"The Kept Coals","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stomp-folk bakers singing 'Keep Your Coal Lit'. The stomp is the exact beat of spinners walking backwards and she can't hear it without her feet moving.","rv_vote":"The Kept Coals, because they're loud and Murmur hates loud."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Rope Well, the spinning families who hold the cord-routes up to Murmur, by withholding Murmur's cable until its debts clear.","edict":"Against. Her silent cord climbs every Nightblade wall in Shadowdeep; she considers the Edict a customer threat."},"wants":"For Murmur to say, out loud, once, that its bridges come from the Long Walk.","site":"hollowgate_vf1","town":"Hollowgate Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:hollowgate_vf1":{"name":"Jot","age":63,"role":"smith of twist-hooks, whirls and rope-walk wheels","look":"Short, thick-necked, very bald, a grey beard plaited into a rope; tiny precise hands for a big man.","voice":"Quiet, thoughtful, careful; speaks in short complete sentences as though each must be load-bearing.","greet":"Ess. Hooks. Whirls. Wheels. I make what turns. What do you need turning?","lines":["Every rope on the Walk turns on a hook I forged. If a rope fails, they look at the spinner. Nobody looks at the hook. I look at the hook.","Murmur pays late and calls our rope its own. I don't mind. A thing doesn't care who claims it. It holds or it doesn't.","Hollowgate drills on our Walk and tangles the twist. I'd forge them a ladder to drill on elsewhere. Nobody's asked."],"about":"I forge the hooks and whirls the spinners twist on. Forty years at it. I don't talk much; my hooks talk for me, by not breaking. I gave up my full name to my Keeper long ago and I sometimes think about that Metalcrown fellow who gave up his entirely.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"careful"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a little, from the Warm"}],"childhood":"Jot was an only child born to old parents, a spinner father and a smith mother who had both given up on children, and he grew up spoiled by quiet. He was the most careful child on the Walk: he lined up stones, sorted flax by length, and never broke anything. His mother let him into the forge at seven and found him, at eight, examining every hook on the Walk for wear and marking the bad ones with chalk. He was right every time. He was also lonely, and he dealt with it by finding a friend in the next spinning family's boy, Rind, who was loud and clever and twisted rope faster than anyone, and who would talk for both of them. They have been friends for fifty-five years.","schooling":"The Walk's Well-school under Thren, the backwards-walking teacher; Jot was slow but never wrong. No lamp lit at the Lamp Test. He apprenticed to his mother at twelve and learned hooks, whirls and the great twisting wheels at the head of the Walk, and spent two winters in the Warm under Metalcrown learning bearings from a Shiftcant engine-keeper.","history":"Jot has forged every hook, whirl and wheel on the Long Walk for forty years. He came back from the Warm with bearings in his head, and the twisting wheels at the head of the Walk now run smoother than any in the Nine. He is the village's quiet authority on everything that turns. When the Murmur bridge failed and the rope was traced to Rind's twist, Jot went to the Walk that night and examined every hook Rind had used that season. None were faulty. He then examined the length of the failed rope that came back from Murmur, which nobody else wanted to look at, and found the strands at the break were not frayed but clean on one side. He told Rind. Rind told him not to say it, because accusing Murmur would cost the village its contract. Jot has kept the strand in a box at his forge for nine years.","family":"His wife Lenn, a flax-grower, dead five years. A daughter, a Hollowgate laundress. Rind, who is not family and is the closest thing he has.","hero":{"who":"The Ninth Iron Dominus","why":"A man who gave up his name and hasn't said 'I' in eleven years. Jot, who gave his full name to a Keeper at twelve and never wanted it back, thinks that's the only thing a stranger ever did that he understood completely."},"band":{"act":"Landslide","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Hard rock. He heard it played in the Warm, on a cap, and liked that it sounded like a wheel coming loose and still somehow holding.","rv_vote":"Landslide, and he hopes Metalcrown gets one lamp from somewhere."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Rope Well, by building its new wheel for free, which in the Walk is as loud as a vote gets.","edict":"Torn. He doesn't like Lightforge writing things down, but he thinks something ought to be checked properly, and nobody checks the hooks."},"wants":"For someone to look at the strand in his box and say what he already knows.","site":"hollowgate_vf1","town":"Hollowgate Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:hollowgate_vf1":{"name":"Mott","age":20,"role":"new spinner, hunting Murmur for his first coloured thread","look":"Round-faced, earnest, flax in his hair, a stained bandage on one palm from twist-burn; a bright orange thread tied round his wrist to match the one he spun in.","voice":"Earnest and wordy, eager to explain, slightly too loud; laughs at himself first.","greet":"Ess! Do you know Murmur at all? Have you seen an orange thread in any of the bridges? No? Worth asking.","lines":["Every new spinner ties a thread of their own colour into their first bridge-rope and walks to Murmur to find it once it hangs. I went. Looked at four hundred bridges. Haven't found it. It's there. Somewhere.","Ask anyone in Murmur where the bridges come from. They'll tell you Murmur. Kept. I asked forty people. Forty times Murmur.","Lisk won't let a rope leave the Walk until she's slept a night beside it. I slept beside mine too. On the other side. She told me to move."],"about":"I'm the newest spinner on the Walk. My first bridge-rope went to Murmur this spring with an orange thread twisted into it. I walked there to find it. I've been there four times. I'll go again. Lisk thinks I'm a fool. I'm a fool with a thread.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"good, rope signs"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Mott was not born on the Walk; his family were moth-netters from the Roost who moved to the rope village when he was six, after his father fell from the cliff and could not net any more. He arrived a stranger, smelling of guano, among children who had walked backwards since birth. He fell over a lot. He was teased, and he dealt with it by being so relentlessly cheerful the other children gave up. He spent his evenings, alone, walking the Long Walk backwards in the dark after the spinners had gone, falling, getting up, until at eleven he could do the whole half-mile without stumbling. Nobody saw. The next morning he walked it beside the others as though he'd always been able to, and nobody teased him again.","schooling":"The Walk's Well-school under Thren, who caught him practising once in the night and said nothing. No lamp lit at his Lamp Test; his father looked relieved and he never knew why. He apprenticed to the rope-master, Lisk, at fifteen and learned the twist, the lay and the silent cord, and has burned his palms more than any apprentice in a decade.","history":"Mott spun his first bridge-rope this spring, under Lisk's eye, and twisted into it a single orange thread, a colour he chose because nobody else on the Walk uses it and because it is the colour of the Metalcrown reactor glow, which his father once saw. Lisk slept beside the rope a night and passed it. It went to Murmur with the carters and was hung in a new bridge somewhere in the city. Every new spinner walks to Murmur to find their thread, and most do in a day. Mott has been four times. He has walked the bridges of Murmur from end to end, asked bridge-masters, looked at every new span. He has not found it. Each time he comes home he is a little quieter. On his last trip a Murmur bridge-master told him, kindly, that sometimes a rope is cut down and relaid in a different bridge under a different name. Mott has started to wonder whether his rope was really hung at all.","family":"His father, who can't net any more and twists cord for Wist; his mother, a netter who never got used to the Walk. A grandmother on the Roost who sends moth fritters and asks if he's found it yet.","hero":{"who":"Lisk, the rope-master","why":"She sleeps beside every rope before she lets it go. Mott thinks that's what caring about your work looks like, and wants to be like that so badly it hurts."},"band":{"act":"The Rainmakers","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A drum collective. He likes music where nobody's the star and every drum matters, and he plays along on the Walk's wheel housing.","rv_vote":"The Rainmakers, because they never win and he knows how that feels."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Rope Well because Lisk does, and because they pay for the carters who take him to Murmur.","edict":"Against, though honestly he's not sure what it means for the Walk. He thinks it might mean Lightforgers coming to check ropes, and they'd tangle the twist like Hollowgate."},"wants":"To find his orange thread hanging in a Murmur bridge.","site":"hollowgate_vf1","town":"Hollowgate Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:hollowgate_vf1:b":{"name":"Rind","age":61,"role":"spinner whose rope failed; still spins, never for bridges","look":"Lean and weathered, a long grey braid, twist-calluses like horn on both palms; walks backwards even on flat ground, from habit.","voice":"Once loud, now careful; dry, self-mocking, with a long pause before anything about the bridge.","greet":"Ess. You'll have heard about me. I spin cord now. Good cord. Not for bridges. Buy some if you like.","lines":["A bridge once failed in Murmur and the rope was traced back to one spinner's twist. That's me. I still work. Nobody buys my rope for bridges. They buy it for washing lines. Very strong washing lines.","Ask anyone in Murmur where the bridges come from. They'll tell you Murmur. Except that one bridge. That one, they'll tell you, came from me.","Lisk sleeps beside every rope a night before it leaves. She started that after mine. I don't blame her. I'd sleep beside them too."],"about":"I was the fastest twist on the Walk for thirty years. Then a bridge in Murmur failed and they traced the rope to me, and nobody died, which I count every day. I spin washing-line cord now, and climbing rope for Hollowgate that nobody tells me goes to Nightblades. I think about that bridge every morning.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, old Walk dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a handful of words from a Frostgrave buyer of rope who visited for years"}],"childhood":"Rind was the loud child on the Walk, the one who sang while spinning, talked over the elders and walked backwards faster than anyone his age. He was the son of the best twist on the Walk and wanted, from the moment he could stand, to be faster than her. By ten he was. He had a friend, the smith's careful boy Jot, who said almost nothing, and Rind talked enough for both of them and covered for him when the other children were cruel. He was quick-tempered, generous and showy, and he never once thought of the bridges in Murmur as anything but proof of how good he was. He used to lie on the high bridges with his ear to the rope and tell Jot he could hear his own twist humming.","schooling":"The Walk's Well-school under Thren, whom he exasperated; he was quick at everything and finished nothing. No lamp lit at his Lamp Test; he thought the lamps were broken, and said so to the examiner. His mother taught him the twist herself, from six, and by sixteen he was the Walk's fastest spinner.","history":"For thirty years Rind's rope was in more Murmur bridges than anyone's. He spun fast and well, and was proud, and Murmur, which never thanked the Walk, sent word through the carters that his cable was the best. Then, nine years ago, a span over the chasm parted at midday with eleven people on it. They were caught by the safety nets beneath, and nobody died, but the rope was traced back to his twist by the lay. Rind went to Murmur and stood in front of the bridge-master and said nothing, which is the most Shadowdeep thing he has ever done. He has not spun bridge-rope since. He spins washing-line cord, climbing rope and nets, and he walks to Murmur once a year and stands under the span his rope failed in. Jot has told him the strand was cut. Rind told Jot not to say so, because the Walk needs Murmur's contract, and because he would rather be the man who failed than the man who called Murmur a liar and could not prove it.","family":"A wife, Hal, who left him the year after the failure and came back two years later without explanation. A son who spins for bridges, which Rind is fiercely proud of. Jot.","hero":{"who":"Olin Stair","why":"The Thawborn who carried his sister down the Glass Stair. Rind thinks a man who carries something heavy down a dangerous stair and doesn't drop it is a hero, and he'd like to have been one."},"band":{"act":"Isveig","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"She sings the saddest song perfectly and feels nothing. Rind would very much like to feel nothing about one particular bridge.","rv_vote":"Isveig, because the Frostgrave rope-buyer told him she always loses to Lightforge and he likes her more for it."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Rope Well silently, and the Well backs him silently in return, which is why he still has work.","edict":"Against, and he says the Murmur bridge-masters should be audited before any spark."},"wants":"To spin one more bridge-rope and walk across it himself.","site":"hollowgate_vf1","town":"Hollowgate Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:duskhollow_vf1":{"name":"Haff","age":39,"role":"grease-trader; sells hinge-grease by the pot to every Realm with a lock","look":"Plump, shiny-cheeked, always faintly glistening from the grease; a Metalcrown engine-man's cap he wears to irritate Metalcrown buyers.","voice":"Bouncy, loud for Shadowdeep, swears inventively in four tongues; laughs like a door that needs his product.","greet":"Ess! Grease? Course you need grease. Everyone needs grease. Listen. Hear that? Nothing. That's my grease.","lines":["A loud door in Shadowdeep means someone wants you to hear it. Think about that before you go knocking. Then buy a pot.","Metalcrown buyers say their own oil does the job. Their oil smells of hot pennies and squeaks in a month. Ours smells of snail and lasts a lifetime.","The cap-readers say our slime ruins their caps when they're stored together. Then don't store them together! Simple. They never listen. Funny, for listeners."],"about":"I sell the village's grease. Spiritveil buys it for archive doors. Stormreach buys it for chain-winches. Lightforge buys it and pretends it doesn't. Metalcrown refuses and then buys it quietly. I like Kaelis Emberwarden. She's the only one on the Council who swears more than me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, merchant's"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"good, mostly insults"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"swearing and haggling only"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Haff was born in the snail cellars and raised among the steps of wet stone where tens of thousands of lamp-snails glow under sacking. His mother rendered grease and his father farmed snails, and both of them were very quiet people who produced, to their bafflement, the noisiest boy in the village. Haff talked to the snails. He sang to them. He invented races, betted on them, lost and claimed fouls. At seven he sold his first pot of grease to a passing Stormreach drover by demonstrating it on the man's own creaking boot-buckles, and the drover laughed so hard he bought three. From then on Haff was the one sent to sell. His mother says he could sell a pot of grease to a hinge.","schooling":"The village's tiny Well-school under a soft-voiced teacher called Breth who taught letters on wet slate with a snail-trail, which faded, so you had to learn fast. No lamp lit at Haff's Lamp Test; he said the lamps needed greasing. At sixteen he went to Duskhollow to work for a kit-trader, Thrum, who taught him that a buyer remembers how you made them laugh longer than what they paid.","history":"Haff came back to the village at twenty-two and took over the grease trade from his mother, who was delighted to stop talking to strangers. He made it into the village's fortune. He found buyers in every Realm with a lock: Spiritveil archive-keepers who want silent doors in the Lantern Archives, Stormreach chain-yards for winches, Frostgrave for ice-gates, even Lightforge clerks who want doors that open without a sound and would never say so in a Day-Book. Metalcrown buyers scoff, say their own oil works, and order through a Hush broker so no one sees. Haff travels the Veil Roads four months a year with a pack of pots, and comes home loud, sunburnt and full of other Realms' gossip. He handles the village's quarrel with the Farms 3 bakers over the smell in the shared cellars, mostly by sending them pots of grease, which they find insulting, and the quarrel with the cap-readers by never storing their caps with his slime, which they don't believe.","family":"His mother, retired, very happy not to talk. His father, still farming. A partner, Lell, a Duskhollow cap-sorter, which both villages find scandalous given the quarrel.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"Loud, funny, profane, and sitting on the Council among people who whisper. Haff saw her once from a distance on a trade trip and decided that was who he was meant to be."},"band":{"act":"Gearbox","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Engine reggaeton. He heard it in a Metalcrown buyer's shop while arguing about oil and lost the argument because he was dancing.","rv_vote":"Gearbox, because Metalcrown's never had one lamp from Lightforge and he'd like them to have one from Shadowdeep."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Cellar Well, the snail families who hold the eastern damp-routes, by greasing their doors for free all year, not just on the Night of Doors.","edict":"Against. A Lightforge register would mean lamp-checks at every door, and he doesn't want anyone checking whose doors are quiet."},"wants":"To sell a pot of grease in every one of the Nine Realms' capitals, and Metalcrown's Iron Throne City is the last.","site":"duskhollow_vf1","town":"Duskhollow Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:duskhollow_vf1":{"name":"Dun","age":48,"role":"hinge-smith and maker of snail-pins","look":"Tall, gaunt, a heavy jaw she rarely moves; hair tied back with a strip of bat leather; wears slates on a cord round her neck and writes on them instead of talking.","voice":"She writes more than she speaks; when she speaks it's in a few heavy, well-chosen words.","greet":"(writes on a slate: 'Ess. Hinges. Pins. Ask.') (then, aloud, low) Ask.","lines":["(slate) A loud door means someone wants you to hear it. My hinges are silent. Unless you pay me extra.","(slate) The bakers say our smell gets in their crust. Their crust is good. Maybe it's the smell.","(aloud, low) Pins for snails. Silver, if you're fancy. Iron, if you're honest."],"about":"(slate, then aloud) I forge hinges and pins. I write more than I talk; I like the time it gives me to choose. The Silent Sentinel of Stonehold does the same, they say, with a stone jaw. Mine's just a jaw.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native, rarely spoken"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, written better than spoken"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"some, read from Stonehold smithing slates"}],"childhood":"Dun stammered so badly as a child that her mother taught her to write before she could reliably speak, on a slate tied round her neck, and Dun found that she liked the slate better than her own mouth. She was a solemn, deliberate child who wrote very short notes: 'No.' 'Yes, later.' 'That snail is ill.' She was always right about the snails. Other children found her strange, and she found them noisy, and they left each other alone. At nine she watched the village smith, an old man called Tarr, forge a hinge from a bar of iron in total silence, and wrote him a note: 'Teach me.' He wrote back on the same slate: 'Yes, later.' Later turned out to be the next day.","schooling":"The village Well-school under Breth, the snail-trail teacher, who let her answer on her slate. No lamp lit at her Lamp Test; she wrote 'Thank you' to the examiner. Tarr taught her hinges for twelve years, in silence, and she learned Holdspeech from Stonehold smithing slates he'd bought, which taught her more about heat than any person.","history":"Dun took Tarr's forge at twenty-five and has made every hinge in the village since, and many for Duskhollow and Hush. Her speciality is a hinge she forges already greased, the pin sealed in snail-grease inside a hollow knuckle, so it never needs doing again; Haff sells them in every Realm. She also makes the pins the village eats snails with, which she forges in fine iron for honest families and silver for show-offs. She is respected, a little feared, and courted by nobody, which she says suits her. She has a long friendship by slate with a Stonehold smith she has never met, who carves his replies; they have exchanged two hundred slates over fifteen years about heat, iron and very little else. When the Silent Sentinel was spoken of at Duskhollow, a Stonehold councillor with a stone jaw who speaks by carved slates, Dun walked out of the square and went to her forge and stood there a long while.","family":"Her mother, who taught her the slate, dead ten years. A brother in Murkwater dredging silt. Her Stonehold slate-friend, whose half of the correspondence fills a shelf.","hero":{"who":"The Silent Sentinel","why":"A Stonehold elder whose jaw turned to stone and who speaks by carved slates. Dun has done the same since she was five for a much smaller reason, and has never felt more seen by someone she'll never meet."},"band":{"act":"Mason Grey","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Quarry baritone soul. Her slate-friend sent a cap of it, and the voice was so deep and slow she says it was like hearing a slate written aloud.","rv_vote":"Mason Grey, written on her lamp-slip in a very small hand."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Cellar Well, by slate, which in the village is binding.","edict":"Against. She writes 'No.' on a slate whenever anyone mentions it, and has hung one on her forge door."},"wants":"To meet her Stonehold slate-friend once, and say nothing to each other for an hour.","site":"duskhollow_vf1","town":"Duskhollow Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:duskhollow_vf1":{"name":"Prue","age":27,"role":"hinge-greaser and snail-farmer; keeps one door loud on purpose","look":"Short, quick, freckled, a snail-grease shine to her hands, a battered tin grease-pot on her belt; a laugh that turns heads in a village that doesn't laugh much.","voice":"Bright, teasing, a little reckless; tells stories with her hands as much as her mouth.","greet":"Ess! Listen. Hear my door? Squeaks. On purpose. I grease every hinge in Duskhollow and I keep mine loud. Want to know why? Ask nicely.","lines":["A loud door in Shadowdeep means someone wants you to hear it. Think about that before you go knocking. My door is very loud.","On the eve of the Night of Doors we grease every hinge in Duskhollow for free, house by house. Nobody thanks us. The custom is nobody admits their door was ever loud. Some of them were very loud.","Nim's got a silent door and a bell on the inside. I've got a loud door and no bell. Between us we've covered the whole problem."],"about":"I farm snails and grease hinges. Every year on the Night of Doors I'm in every house in Duskhollow, and every year nobody says thank you, and I love it. I keep my own door squeaking so people know I'm home. My gran used to tell a story called The Lamp That Leaned and never finished it. I'm still waiting.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, playful"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent, picked up from Haff"}],"childhood":"Prue grew up in a snail family that was very good at the work and very bad at fun, and she decided at about six to supply the fun herself. She rode the snail-sledges down the wet steps, named every glowing snail after a Hush councillor, and was sent to grease hinges in Duskhollow at eight because it was the only job that kept her out of the cellars all day. She loved it: every house, every door, every life glimpsed for a moment through a gap. Her gran, who sat by the snail steps and told stories, had one she never finished: The Lamp That Leaned, about a copyist and a lamp. She always stopped at the same place, and Prue, who hated not knowing, begged for years. Her gran died at the same place in the story.","schooling":"The village Well-school under Breth, who wrote with snail-trails; Prue learned letters fast to read them before they faded, and then mostly learned mischief. No lamp lit at her Lamp Test. She learned the greasing trade from her father, who taught her that a hinge tells you who uses a door and how often.","history":"Prue has greased hinges for nineteen years, and for the last six she has led the village's Night of Doors round, when they grease every hinge in Duskhollow, house by house, unthanked. She knows more Duskhollow doors than anyone, and who opens which, and when; she keeps it all to herself, mostly. She farms snails the rest of the year with her brothers, and is the village's best racer of snail-sledges, a game she invented and nobody else plays well. She keeps her own door unoiled. The squeak tells the whole cellar row when she's in, and when she's out, and Prue has discovered that a loud door brings more visitors than a silent one, because people like to know they're expected. Every year on the Night of Doors she leaves one hinge in Duskhollow ungreased on purpose: the same one, in the eastern stair. She has never told anyone why.","family":"Two brothers who farm snails with her; parents in the cellars; her gran, dead eight years, whose unfinished story she tells to children and also never finishes.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"From her gran's story, The Lamp That Leaned. Prue has never heard the end and has decided the copyist must be the hero of it, because her gran always smiled at the name."},"band":{"act":"Retell","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A rapper who repeats every earlier bar. Prue says it's like her gran's story: you keep coming round to the same part, and one day it'll go on.","rv_vote":"Retell, because she wants to hear how the set ends."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Cellar Well, and votes by which hinges she greases first on the Night of Doors; this year she started with the Wood Well's elders, which was noticed.","edict":"Against. She's been inside every door in Duskhollow and thinks nobody should have a register of who's behind them."},"wants":"To hear the end of The Lamp That Leaned.","site":"duskhollow_vf1","town":"Duskhollow Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:duskhollow_vf1:b":{"name":"Grue","age":88,"role":"oldest snail-farmer; descendant of the founding family","look":"Very small, bald, wrinkled as a dried cap, sitting on the Snail Steps under wet sacking, glowing faintly green from the snails crawling on his sleeves.","voice":"Whispery, slow, cheerful, very proud; refers to snails by family lineage like old houses.","greet":"Ess, young one. Mind the steps. These are the Pale Shell line, these the Old Damp line. Don't tread on history.","lines":["A loud door in Shadowdeep means someone wants you to hear it. My great-grandmother said that. She meant it as a warning. Now it's a slogan on our pots.","The bakers complain our snails get into their crust. They've complained for sixty years. Their bread's been very good for sixty years. I've told them. They don't believe me.","Snails in inkroot butter, a hot slate and a pin. Outsiders refuse them. Outsiders are wrong. I'll eat yours if you don't."],"about":"I'm the oldest snail-farmer in the village and the last of the first family. My great-grandmother farmed lamp-snails for the table before anyone thought of grease. When the Night of Doors came along, she was the one who rubbed slime on a squeaking hinge to stop her husband hearing her come home. That's the whole village, right there.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native, very old forms"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, archaic"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, refuses to learn more"}],"childhood":"Grue was born on the Snail Steps, literally; his mother was turning sacking when he came and refused to stop. He grew up among snails as other children grew up among dogs, and he knew every line of them, which snail had which mother, which strain glowed brightest and which gave the best slime. His great-grandmother, very old then, taught him the story of the first greasing: a sly, laughing woman coming home late through a squeaking door, and the snail slime on her fingers. She told it to him every night and changed a detail each time, and when he caught her she laughed and said that was the point. He was a slow, happy child who never wanted to leave the cellars and never has.","schooling":"No school; there was none in the village then, only family. His great-grandmother taught him his letters on a wet slate with a snail-trail, the method Breth later took up. No lamp lit at his Lamp Test, which he took in Duskhollow, the only time he has ever been there. He says it was too bright.","history":"Grue has farmed snails on the same steps for eighty years. He married a Duskhollow cap-sorter, Fen, who moved down to the cellars for him and never complained of the smell, and they raised six children who now run half the village's grease trade. He has outlived Fen, three of his children and every other snail-farmer of his generation. He is the village's memory of itself: the man who remembers when there were twenty families, then forty, the year the snails sickened and he saved the Pale Shell line by sleeping beside them under wet sacking for a month, and the day the Farms 3 bakers first complained of the smell, sixty years ago, a complaint he has rebutted personally every season since. He does not travel. He has not been to Duskhollow since his Lamp Test. He sits on the steps every day, handles snails, and tells his great-grandmother's story to whoever sits with him, changing a detail every time.","family":"His wife Fen, dead twenty years. Three living children, eleven grandchildren, many great-grandchildren including Prue's cousins. Haff, he says, is not his grandson, but behaves like one.","hero":{"who":"His great-grandmother, who greased the first hinge","why":"She stopped a door squeaking so she could come home late, and built a village out of it. Grue says that's more than most legends manage."},"band":{"act":"The Stampers","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Amapiano where applause is the beat. He likes that the audience makes the music. A grandchild played him a cap and he clapped along, slowly, off the beat.","rv_vote":"The Stampers, because the great-grandchildren asked him to and he wants them to visit."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Cellar Well, which his family founded; his vote is whatever he says at the steps on the morning of a decision, and the elders come to hear it.","edict":"Against, mildly, because he's too old to be registered and too old to care, but he doesn't like strangers counting things."},"wants":"To live to see a snail line he started reach its hundredth generation.","site":"duskhollow_vf1","town":"Duskhollow Farms 2","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:duskhollow_vf2":{"name":"Scal","age":57,"role":"bakery trader; sells loaves, kit and genuine marked stones to other bakeries","look":"Thin, floury, sharp-eyed, a magnifying lens on a chain he uses to inspect stones; a pocket that clicks with them when he walks.","voice":"Dry, connoisseurial, slightly snobbish about stones; enjoys long technical words and short insults.","greet":"Ess. Bread, packs, arrows. And if you're a baker, stones. Genuine Farms 3 marked stones. Don't buy forgeries from Hush.","lines":["If you find the stone, don't say. Just finish the slice. That's what manners are. Also: don't swallow it. That's what teeth are.","Other bakeries buy our marked stones so their Liar's Bread looks genuine. The mark's a little scratch. Copying it is easy. Copying it well isn't.","Brem's bitten the stone four years running. The rest of us are trying not to look pleased. I'm not trying very hard."],"about":"I sell the bakery's loaves and kit, and I handle the stone trade: every stone we mark and sell to other bakeries goes through my hands. I can tell a Farms 3 stone from a forgery by the scratch. I'm not proud of much. I'm proud of that.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"bakers' words only, from Flameborne bakeries that buy stones"}],"childhood":"Scal was a river-stone collector's son from the deep tunnels below Duskhollow, where his father gathered smooth stones from underground streams and sold them to bakeries for Liar's Bread. He grew up with stones in his pockets, his mouth, his bed. At five he could tell a good baking stone from a bad one by weight on his tongue. At eight his father took him to the Farms 3 bakery to deliver a sack, and an old baker showed him the little mark scratched on each stone so the village's loaves could be known, and let him scratch one. Scal's first mark was crooked. The baker kept it in the Stone Jar as a joke. It is still there, and Scal has never stopped trying to make up for it.","schooling":"A deep-tunnel Well-school taught by a stone-gatherer called Krell, who taught hand-cant by water-sounds and letters by scratching them on wet stones. No lamp lit at Scal's Lamp Test. He apprenticed to the bakery at fourteen and learned the marking from the old baker, who made him scratch a thousand practice stones before he let him mark one for a loaf.","history":"Scal marked stones for the bakery for twenty years before he took over its trading at thirty-four. He turned the stone trade from an occasional favour into a real business: bakeries across Shadowdeep, and then in Flameborne and Stonehold, buy Farms 3 marked stones so their festival loaves look genuine, and Scal sells them by the sack with a certificate in his own spidery hand. He is ruthless about forgeries; Hush bakeries scratch fake marks and he has a collection of their mistakes he shows to buyers. He also sells the bakery's everyday loaves and some kit to travellers. He was the one who sent out the loaf with two stones in it, years ago; he marked both, and doesn't know how. He has never said so. He sits with the Stone Jar most evenings and takes out stones, examines the marks under his lens, and puts them back.","family":"A wife, Mira, who bakes everyday bread and thinks he loves stones more than her. Two grown sons who left for Hush and buy forgeries out of spite. His father, the stone-gatherer, long dead.","hero":{"who":"Amon Greycloak","why":"The first Shardwatch. Scal knows almost nothing about him and likes it that way; he says a man remembered for one title and nothing else must have done a very careful job."},"band":{"act":"No Name","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Masked whisper-rap that comes second on purpose. Scal thinks deliberately coming second takes more skill than winning, like a stone nobody bites.","rv_vote":"No Name, to come second, as is proper."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Rye Well, the bakery families who hold the terraces and the deep-tunnel starter, by giving its elders the first-pick stones.","edict":"Against, because Lightforge auditors will want to know what's hidden in things, and his entire trade is things hidden in bread."},"wants":"For his crooked first stone to be taken out of the Stone Jar.","site":"duskhollow_vf2","town":"Duskhollow Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"smith:duskhollow_vf2":{"name":"Wick","age":42,"role":"oven-smith; forges oven doors, peels and the iron of the rye mills","look":"Wide-hipped, bare-armed, oven-reddened face, hair a cloud of grey frizz tied with string; neither man nor woman and dresses in a baker's apron burned through at the knees.","voice":"Hearty and generous, loud for Shadowdeep but somehow never annoying; calls everyone 'loaf'.","greet":"Ess, loaf! Warm in here, isn't it? Ovens never sleep. Neither do I. What needs forging?","lines":["If you find the stone, don't say. Just finish the slice. I've bitten more stones than Brem. Nobody counts the smith.","The snail folk next door, the smell gets in the crust, so the bakers say. I say a little snail never hurt a crust. I say it quietly.","The starter came up from the deep tunnels before Hush was built, they say. I've seen the crock. Very old crock. Very sour starter. I believe it."],"about":"I forge the oven doors, the peels and the mill irons, and I keep the ovens running all year, which is more work than anyone thinks. My grandmother was the bakery's smith before me. I'm neither he nor she; the bakery calls me 'the smith' and the children call me 'loaf' back.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good, from a Flameborne oven-builder they apprenticed with"}],"childhood":"Wick's grandmother forged the bakery's ovens and raised Wick in the forge next door to them, in the warmest place in Shadowdeep, where Wick learned to walk holding on to an oven door and burned their hands so often their grandmother wrapped them in wet bread dough to cool them. Wick loved the heat, the smell of rye, the roar of the ovens at night, and grew up big, warm and unafraid of fire. They were loud, generous and liked by every baker, who fed them crusts. At nine their grandmother set them a test: forge a peel, the flat shovel that loads loaves, that a baker would use. Wick's first peel was too heavy. The bakers used it anyway, to be kind. Wick found out and was so ashamed they made a better one in a week.","schooling":"The bakery's own Well-school, taught by an older baker named Hebe, who taught letters by scoring them on loaves before baking. No lamp lit at Wick's Lamp Test. At sixteen Wick went to Flameborne for two years to learn oven-building from a Kindle-speaking master, which is how they learned that fire can be argued with.","history":"Wick came back from Flameborne at eighteen with a new oven design, a Kindle vocabulary, and a deep admiration for Flameborne bakers, and spent the next decade rebuilding every oven in the village. The ovens now run all year, the rye mills turn on iron Wick forged, and the bakery's output has doubled. Their grandmother died at ninety in the forge, sitting by the warmest oven. Wick took over properly, and became the bakery's quiet centre: the one who fixes things, feeds the apprentices, and keeps the night ovens going. They are close to Brem, the head baker, whose four-year streak of biting the stone at the Biting they consider a hilarious tragedy, and they have twice bitten the stone at the Biting themselves without anyone counting it, because smiths aren't counted. Wick has one sorrow they don't speak of: a Flameborne oven-builder they loved, who did not want to come to a place without daylight.","family":"Their grandmother, dead twelve years. Parents who were bakers. Brem, whom they treat as a brother. A Flameborne oven-builder, Kerran, far away.","hero":{"who":"Their grandmother, the old oven-smith","why":"She forged the bakery's first year-round oven and died sitting by it at ninety. Wick says they'd like to do exactly the same."},"band":{"act":"The Bellows","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stadium rock where the crowd pumps a forge. Wick saw them in Flameborne at seventeen, pumped the bellows, and has never felt so happy.","rv_vote":"The Bellows, every year, because Kerran will too and it's the nearest they get to being in the same room."},"vote":{"local":"Backs the Rye Well with the bakers, and their vote is whose oven gets rebuilt first, which is a powerful vote in a bakery.","edict":"Against, loudly, which annoys the bakers. Wick learned in Flameborne what a Lightforge court does to angry people and says Shadowdeep should be angrier."},"wants":"For Kerran to come down the Coal Road and the Veil Roads to see the ovens.","site":"duskhollow_vf2","town":"Duskhollow Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:duskhollow_vf2":{"name":"Bex","age":15,"role":"apprentice baker who badly wants to bite the stone at the Biting","look":"Small, round, flour to the eyebrows, a burn on one thumb she shows everyone; her sister Vash's old apron, too long.","voice":"Fierce, competitive, very funny about herself; talks with her mouth full.","greet":"Ess! Want a loaf? It's Liar's Bread. One slice has a stone. Don't say if you find it. Just finish. That's what manners are. I didn't make that rule, but I love it.","lines":["At the Biting the bakers bake one loaf for themselves, and whoever bites the stone has to bake alone all night to start the year. Brem's bitten it four years running. I want it so much. It's not fair.","Once a loaf left here with two stones in it. Nobody knows how. The table it reached is still arguing. There's a man here now who was at that table. He keeps looking at the Stone Jar.","Grain for the bakery? Yes. We'll make bread of it and someone will find a stone in it and won't say. You'll never know. That's the best kind of thank you."],"about":"I'm an apprentice baker. My sister Vash is marrying Seld from Duskhollow; they're standing in the Beam next month and Vash is pretending not to be terrified. I want to bite the stone at the Biting more than anything. Brem keeps getting it. I keep thinking he's doing it on purpose.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"good, bakers' signs"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"Bex is the youngest of four sisters in a baking family, which means she has had to fight for everything, starting with the crusts. She was born small and stayed small and decided, at about three, to make up for it by being loudest. She bakes by temper: her loaves are lopsided, ferociously flavoured and rise like they're angry about something. At seven she hid a stone in her sister's breakfast bread, not a festival loaf, just an ordinary one, and her sister bit it and chipped a tooth, and Bex was banned from the bakery for a month. She sat outside the door every day of the month and watched. When she came back she baked her first proper Liar's Bread and hid the stone so well nobody found it until the last slice.","schooling":"The bakery's Well-school under Hebe, who scored letters on loaves; Bex learned to read so she could eat the lessons. No lamp lit at her Lamp Test; she said she was too busy anyway. She apprenticed to Brem at twelve and is learning the cave-rye starter, the oven timings and how to hide a stone so the slice doesn't sit wrong.","history":"Bex has been an apprentice for three years and is already, by Brem's grudging admission, the best stone-hider in the bakery. She can bury a stone in a loaf so that no slice looks heavier than another, which matters at a Shadowdeep table where people watch each other's knives. She has entered the Biting twice, and both years Brem bit the stone and baked alone all night, and both years Bex sat outside the bakery door in the dark, listening to him, burning with envy. She has begun to suspect that Brem, the most competitive man in Duskhollow, knows exactly where the stone is and bites it on purpose, because he likes the night alone with the ovens. This year she intends to hide the stone herself. She has also taken a strong liking to the old man, Olm, who came to the village asking about the two-stone loaf, and she brings him crusts and they argue about it.","family":"Parents who bake; three sisters, including Vash, about to marry Seld the pear-keeper in the Beam. A grandfather who bites every stone he finds and swallows it out of spite.","hero":{"who":"Brem, the head baker","why":"He's bitten the stone four years running and she can't stand it and she can't stop watching how he does everything. She'd die before telling him he's her hero."},"band":{"act":"House of the Seven Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Seven idols with fire choreography. Wick taught her the dance in the forge, and she does it badly with a peel.","rv_vote":"House of the Seven Ovens, because Wick said so and because bakers should stick together."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for the Wells, but she has already announced she'll back the Rye Well, which the Rye Well found very funny.","edict":"Against, because Wick's against and Wick shouts about it, and she likes anything Wick shouts about."},"wants":"To bite the stone at the Biting and bake alone all night.","site":"duskhollow_vf2","town":"Duskhollow Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"vill:duskhollow_vf2:b":{"name":"Olm","age":70,"role":"retired Duskhollow cap-grader who has moved to the bakery to find out about the two-stone loaf","look":"Tall, stooped, gentle, a long grey coat and a cap-grader's thin gloves; carries a crust in one pocket and a notebook of hand-cant shorthand in the other.","voice":"Mild, courteous, persistent; asks the same question many ways and is never rude.","greet":"Ess. Forgive me. May I ask, were you here the year the loaf went out with two stones? No? Then you'll not mind me asking someone else.","lines":["If you find the stone, don't say. Just finish the slice. That's what manners are. At my table, years ago, two people found one each. Neither said. Then both said. Then nobody spoke for a year.","The Stone Jar holds every stone ever bitten and sent back. The two from our loaf are in there, tagged with my house. I visit them.","The bakers say the snail smell gets in their crust. I'm a cap-grader; I've listened to that crust. It's fine."],"about":"I graded listening-caps in Duskhollow for fifty years. Years ago a loaf of Liar's Bread came to my table with two stones in it, and my brother and my wife both bit one, and the argument that followed broke something in my house that's never mended. When I retired, I came here to find out how it happened. I've been here two years. The bakers are very patient.","languages":[{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent, grader's shorthand"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a little, from listening to Frostgrave voices in the caps"}],"childhood":"Olm was born in Duskhollow into a family of cap-graders, the quiet, exact people who decide which caps hold voices and which drips. He was a gentle, solitary child who liked rules and patience. He spent his childhood in the grading halls, sitting on a stool beside his mother, learning to tell a cap's contents by the faint resonance against his ear. He was good at it early and loved it, because it felt like being trusted. At ten he graded his first cap alone: a voice, an old woman counting coins. His mother checked it and said nothing, which was the highest praise in their family. He also had a younger brother, Tenn, and they were close and quarrelled constantly about who was better at everything.","schooling":"Duskhollow's Well-school under Fray's predecessor, a man called Hoss who taught sums by having children count drips. No lamp lit at Olm's Lamp Test. His real school was the grading halls, where his mother taught him for twelve years, and where he learned that what matters about a voice is not what it says but whether it means it.","history":"Olm graded caps in Duskhollow for fifty years and was, for most of them, one of its most trusted graders. He married a cap-sorter, Jess, a lively, sharp woman, and they lived in a house in the honeycomb with his brother Tenn. Twenty-six years ago, at the Night of Doors supper, a loaf of Farms 3 Liar's Bread came to their table. Jess bit a stone. Tenn bit a stone. By custom, whoever finds the stone says nothing; both said nothing, and then both saw the other, and then both spoke at once. The argument that followed, about who had been meant to find it and what that meant, was never settled. Tenn moved to Hush within the year. Jess and Olm stayed married and never quite spoke of it again. She died four winters back. When Olm retired he packed a small bag and walked to Duskhollow Farms 3, took a room above the mill, and began asking how a loaf could leave the bakery with two stones. He visits the two stones in the Stone Jar every day.","family":"His wife Jess, dead four winters. His brother Tenn in Hush, who has not written in twenty-five years. No children.","hero":{"who":"His wife Jess","why":"She stayed, after the loaf, when she could have gone. He says that's the bravest thing he ever saw, and he graded caps for fifty years."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Twelve-minute post-rock. He says it's the only music slow enough to let a man think, and Jess hated it, which he finds he misses.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters, and he hopes the presenter sits down."},"vote":{"local":"Backs no Well since he left Duskhollow, which nobody minds, as he's considered a guest; his vote is to bring the bakery crusts back from the mill each morning.","edict":"Torn. He thinks writing things down settles arguments, and he would give anything to have had the loaf written down. He also knows exactly what Lightforge would do with a register."},"wants":"To know whether the second stone was a mistake or a message.","site":"duskhollow_vf2","town":"Duskhollow Farms 3","realm":"Shadowdeep"},"shop:lanterncity":{"name":"Hesper Vantelle","age":212,"role":"outfitter at the foot of the Gallery steps; ex-fog-harbour fisher; holds eight decades of cart deposits for a shelf move that never happens","look":"Broad in the shoulder and brown from the harbour, two silver streaks above the left ear, fish-scale rings on three fingers she never takes off.","voice":"Brisk and haggling; counts everything aloud, including her own change, and laughs at her prices before you can.","greet":"Lamp's lit. You've got the look of someone walking to Willowmere. Everyone has, this year.","lines":["Council's put the shelf vote off again. Eighty-one years now. I've had carts booked for the move since my hair was dark.","Lightforge wants every spark counted. Let them count. They'll lose track somewhere round the third mountain.","My mother steered home by the Mountain's windows. I still count them every dusk. Four thousand and some. I always lose it at the crooked row."],"about":"I sell what walkers forget to pack and buy what they drop on the way back. Before that I gutted fish on the harbour, and before that I was nobody in particular. I'm honest about money. It's the only honesty a trader can afford.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a few words, mostly carters' swearing"}],"childhood":"Hesper grew up on her mother's trawler, the Seventh Window, in the fog harbour under the city, where the mist sat so thick that nobody steered by stars. You steered by the Lit Mountain. Her mother made her stand at the bow and count the rows of windows aloud as they came on, and the count told you how far east you'd drifted. Her father salted fish on deck and sang worse than anyone on the water. At eight she miscounted on a black night and the boat put its nose on the Lamp Rocks, and they lost the season's big net. Her mother never raised her voice about it. She just took Hesper out on the jetty every dusk that winter and made her count again, out loud, until she could do it with her eyes half shut.","schooling":"Harbour school, held under three upturned hulls on the shingle, kept by Orlo Fenn, a one-eyed net-mender who taught sums with fish heads and fined you a head for every wrong answer. Hesper paid almost nothing. At the Lamp Test in the city, aged eight, not one of the eight lamps stirred, and she asked if she could go again. Spark-poor. Brilliant at figures; couldn't read a Bridge contract until she was past twenty, and still moves her lips.","history":"When she stopped ageing at twenty-five she stopped fishing too. The Seventh Window went to her brother, and she spent her share on a handcart and sold dried fish on the willow bridges to people waiting for the Mountain to light. The fish became packs, the packs became lamp-oil and boots for Archive pilgrims, and the cart became a stall at the foot of the Gallery steps that she has kept for a hundred and sixty years. At sixty she went halves with a Metalcrown carter who taught her block-and-tackle; she rigged iron pulleys to haul stock up the steps until the copyists complained the clanking ran through the Evening Names, and she sold the pulleys on to the harbour at a profit. Eighty-one years ago a councillor's clerk came down and told her, quietly, to book carts: the oldest shelves were going to Willowmere within the season. She paid a cartwright's deposit. The vote was put off. She has paid a new deposit every decade since, on principle. She married a glassblower, Teodor, who fell from the scaffold on the Mountain face; his lamp sits in the crooked row. She married again, a wick-braider named Ianthe, and they argue happily about money in two languages.","family":"Wife Ianthe, a wick-braider; brother Corm still fishes the Seventh Window and calls her 'the shopkeeper' as an insult. Her first husband's lamp is on the crooked row, which is the row where she always loses count.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright & Idris Hollowfire","why":"Two people from no great family built a thing that worked, and everyone has used it since. Hesper has sold a great deal and built nothing, and she'd like to shake their hands for it."},"band":{"act":"Gearbox","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Her old carter partner played Gearbox off a wind-up whistle-box on the harbour wall, and she still dances to it badly when nobody from the Copying Rooms is looking.","rv_vote":"Gearbox, because Metalcrown never gets a lamp from anyone and somebody ought to start."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for Councillor Ebba Lanmore's motion to carry the oldest shelves down-river to Willowmere, and tells anyone who asks that she'd get the haulage, so they can weigh her vote accordingly.","edict":"Against, the Council's warning line. A register is a ledger nobody agreed to keep. She'll still sell the Lightforge assessors a pack each when they come."},"wants":"To see the shelf vote taken in her lifetime, either way, so she can stop paying deposits.","site":"lanterncity","town":"Lanthys Vale","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:lanterncity":{"name":"Caddoc Vire","age":41,"role":"shelf-iron smith to the Lantern Archives; makes brackets, hinges, uprights and the odd blade; a Half-Spark who left the Conclave","look":"Lean, looks about twenty-six, forearms grey with soot, cracks his knuckles one at a time in an order that clearly isn't his.","voice":"Short sentences. Dry. Ends nearly every thought with 'right'.","greet":"Forge is hot. If you want a blade, say what for. If you want a bracket, I'm already busy. Right.","lines":["Archive's ordered three hundred brackets this year. For a Council that can't decide to move a shelf, they're very keen on holding them up.","Lightforge wants to register every spark. Had mine registered once, at Korrhaven. Look where it got me. Right.","The knuckles. Yes. Not my habit. My tutor's. He's dead and I'm still cracking them for him."],"about":"I went to Korrhaven with half a spark and came back with somebody else's hands. Now I make iron that holds up the dead. Pays steady. Nobody argues with a bracket.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent (Conclave)"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"halting; the swearing is fluent"},{"tongue":"Old Spark-speech","level":"a handful of oath-words"}],"childhood":"His parents ground ink in the lower workshops of the Copying Rooms, his mother soot-black, his father oak-gall brown, and Caddoc came up with both colours under his nails. He was not supposed to play in the service passages behind the stacks. He did. At seven he got lost back there for a whole night and was found at breakfast asleep against an upright from one of the first-century shelves. He remembers the iron being cold and pitted under his cheek, and thinking, the way a child does, that it was poorly and somebody ought to tell it to lie down. By nine he could name every ink in the building by smell. His father made him an ink-pot of his own with his initial scratched in the bottom, and he still has it, full of forge grease.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight: the Veil lamp guttered on and off like it couldn't make up its mind. Half-Spark. Lamp Years in the Vale under copyist-teacher Maud Thornel, who made every child write their own name a hundred times and burn ninety-nine. Then the Veil Tower at Korrhaven, two knots on the grey sash, good at drill, poor at weaving. At fifteen his tutor's knuckle-cracking and head-tilt began turning up in Caddoc's own body, and he asked to go home.","history":"He came back ashamed. His father said nothing at all; his mother walked him to the nearest forge and stood him in front of a hammer. The forge belonged to Karrick Dunsett, a Stonehold smith who had married into the Vale and never stopped doing the Lift each morning with a burden-stone on the doorstep. Karrick taught him that heavy is honest and that you can hear a flaw in iron if you tap it and shut up. When Karrick went home to the Long Roads, Caddoc kept the forge and took the Archive contract: every new shelf needs uprights and brackets, and he has made several thousand. Three years ago the copyists started calling him up to the oldest shelves to brace a sag here and there. He tapped, and listened. The first-thousand-year uprights are rotting from inside, lamp-smoke and mountain damp working at them for centuries. He has quietly braced forty. He has written a report to the Lantern Council saying how long the rest have. It sits in his apron drawer, folded twice, unsent. On Restdays he plays stones with the son of a senior copyist and loses on purpose so he can ask about the stacks.","family":"Parents still grinding ink downstairs, still not mentioning Korrhaven. No partner; he says the forge is jealous. Writes to Karrick twice a year and gets one slate back.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"Karrick used to say 'Lift. Then talk.' every time Caddoc started explaining instead of working. A king who gave the crown back and picked up a stone is the only kind Caddoc trusts."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"The drummer's hands are turning to stone and the trio keep playing anyway, out loud, in front of everyone. Caddoc knows a bit about paying for a thing in your hands.","rv_vote":"Burden. Right."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for neither shelf motion. He wants the iron fixed first and the arguing after, and he says so at the forge door to anyone in a councillor's coat.","edict":"Against. He'd back Councillor Ostrith Wane's motion to refuse outright. He was registered at Korrhaven once, and knows what a register remembers about you that you'd rather it didn't."},"wants":"For one councillor to come down to the oldest shelves and put a bare hand on the iron.","site":"lanterncity","town":"Lanthys Vale","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:lanterncity":{"name":"Maelis Corrow","age":11,"role":"wick-braider's daughter who writes down every name read at the Evening Names","look":"Small, ink on her chin, a satchel holding a copybook almost as long as her forearm, one sock always down.","voice":"Fast; asks a question and answers it herself before you can.","greet":"Lamp's lit! Are you here for the Names? You should be. It's nearly dusk. Well, it's always nearly dusk somewhere, isn't it. Yes.","lines":["A reader got a name wrong last week and four people on the bridge corrected her at once. She went pink. I wrote that down too.","Lightforge wants to count sparks. I count names. Mine's more useful. Mum says don't say that to a Lightforger.","I've got two thousand and eleven names in my book. I don't know any of them. Gran says that's the point of it."],"about":"I'm Maelis. I'm going to remember every name they read on the steps until I'm as old as the High Seer. I'm eleven, so I've got ages.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words; she can count to a hundred in it"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"two words, 'Aure' and 'Seen', which she uses on everyone"}],"childhood":"She was born over her mother's braiding room on Thread Lane, in a house that always smells of beeswax and hot thread. Her father blows lamp-glass on the night shift, so she mostly sees him at breakfast with his eyebrows singed off. When she was six her great-gran's lamp went into the stacks and her name was read on the Gallery steps, and Maelis was furious, in a way nobody could talk her out of, that she didn't know any of the other names read that night. 'They all had grans too,' she said, and wouldn't be moved. She started chalking the names on the bridge rail. The bridge-keeper scrubbed them off. Her gran bought her a copybook, and that was that.","schooling":"She goes to the copying-room school in the lower rooms of the Archive, taught by Reader Sunniva Drake, who reads the Evening Names some nights and lets one pupil hold the list. At her Lamp Test at eight nothing answered. She asked why there was an empty bracket in the ring; nobody answered that either, so she wrote it down. Spark-poor, and doesn't mind. Good at letters. Terrible at blotting.","history":"For five years she has been on the Gallery steps nearly every dusk, satchel open, pencil going, writing each name in as it's read and putting a small mark beside any name nobody seemed to be standing there for. Her mother walks her down; lately she's allowed to go alone if she holds a bridge-keeper's lantern. She has been to Mournhaven once, for her great-gran's Night on the Water, and remembers that the wake cook gave her two pears without asking, and that the second one was the good one. Last winter she had a fever and missed a single night, and the names from that dusk aren't in her book. She cried about it for longer than about the fever. Reader Sunniva wouldn't give her the list: 'They were heard. That's enough.' Maelis disagrees with this completely. She has been asking strangers on the bridges, one by one, whether they were there that night and what they heard. So far she has seven names out of an unknown number, and one man who swears there was a name read twice.","family":"Mother Brisca braids wick for the Archive; father Tamm blows lamp-glass; Gran Ottoline, around three hundred and very sly, bought the copybook and pays for the pencils.","hero":{"who":"Copyist Nerys Quill","why":"She's copied one family's lamps for three hundred years and still tells them off when they get their own gran's laugh wrong. Maelis wants to be that, but for everybody."},"band":{"act":"The Relay Runners","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"She sings 'Nothing Worth Keeping' with the words in the wrong order because she thinks it's funnier, and it is.","rv_vote":"The Relay Runners, because they're daft and nobody ever gives daft songs a lamp."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to light a lamp for a motion, so she puts a candle-end in her window for whichever side Gran didn't pick, so it's fair.","edict":"Against. If Lightforge counts everybody's sparks they'll need a book like hers, and they'll do it badly."},"wants":"To fill in the one missing night.","site":"lanterncity","town":"Lanthys Vale","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:lanterncity:b":{"name":"Orlaith Vensel","age":563,"role":"retired copyist; reads the Second Copy aloud each anniversary, including the famous passage where she is wrong","look":"Looks thirty-five, but her hair has gone silver from the crown back in a hood of grief-streaks. Spectacles on a cord. Ink in the cracks of both thumbs, though she hasn't copied in six years.","voice":"Slow and amused; corrects you, then corrects her correction.","greet":"Lamp's lit, dear. No, don't tell me your name yet. I'll only want to spell it.","lines":["The shelf vote. Eighty years. I remember when it was only forty and we thought that was a scandal.","Move the oldest lamps to Willowmere? Willowmere floods. I've seen it flood. Twice. Once with a goat on the roof.","In the Second Copy I said a woman's eyes were green. They were brown. They've read that out for five hundred years. I've mostly stopped blushing."],"about":"I copied lamps for nearly five hundred years and I was wrong in front of the whole Realm exactly once, and that's the bit they kept. I don't mind. It means they're listening.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native, and the old court Lanthic of the first shelves"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"reads the funeral words"},{"tongue":"Old Spark-speech","level":"reads the oldest lamp-runes, slowly"}],"childhood":"She was born in a lock-keeper's house on the river between Willowmere and the Vale. Her father worked the lock gates; her mother traded eels from a punt and could out-shout anyone on the water. The lock wall was painted with flood-marks, each one a line with a date and the names of who had drowned that year, and Orlaith learned to read off that wall before she ever held a book. At nine she sat on the roof of the lock-house with her father and a neighbour's goat while Willowmere's lower bridges went under, and watched an archive barge turn slowly over in the brown water with its lamps still lit inside. Afterwards she copied the new flood-mark onto the wall herself, very neatly, because her father's hands were shaking.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight in Willowmere's lamp-house: nothing. Spark-poor. At fourteen she went to the Copying Rooms as apprentice to Copyist Benedek Strand, who never once raised his voice and made her write every mistake in red beside its correction, so she would always see both. She failed fine hand twice. She was the best of her year at old runes, and smug about it.","history":"She copied the lamps of the river families, then the fog-harbour lines, for the better part of five centuries. She was sixty-seven, and looked twenty-five, when the fire in the Concord's seven-hundredth year burned two thousand copies in one night, her whole section among them. In the Second Copy she sat at the long table with the families and argued, warmly and for three days, that the grandmother Ysmay Harland had green eyes. Three cousins said brown. Orlaith was certain. Forty years later she found Ysmay's own lamp-memory and the eyes were brown, plain as bread, and because Benedek had taught her to keep her errors in red, the wrong passage is still there in her hand, and is read aloud at every anniversary. She now reads it herself, with a flourish. She retired at five hundred and fifty-seven, she says, because she'd grown tired of being right. For three hundred years she has shared lunch every day with Davin, a fishmonger on the Lower Bridge, and they have never married or discussed it.","family":"No children. Two nephews' lines she has copied herself, which she says is cheating. Davin, of the daily lunch.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"Every apprentice learns 'The Lamp That Leaned' first, and Orlaith has always believed it leans on purpose, so you'd see the crooked bit and copy it anyway. She has built a life on that reading."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Forty-year love songs. She says she's on her fifth, and won't say whose.","rv_vote":"Decades, as every year since they started."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp against Councillor Lanmore's motion to move the oldest shelves, as she has every time it has nearly been called, which is eleven times.","edict":"Warn, the Council's line. Lightforge should be told plainly why it is foolish and then left to be foolish."},"wants":"To be in the Gallery when the shelf vote is finally taken, and to win or lose it gracefully in person.","site":"lanterncity","town":"Lanthys Vale","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:wickhaven":{"name":"Tobin Marrowick","age":37,"role":"trader under the castle walls; imports cinder-wine and charcoal up the Flameborne track; sells kit and arrows to the road","look":"Barrel-chested, an ember-orange Flameborne scarf at his throat, one palm burn-shiny from a wine-vat, laughs with his whole face.","voice":"Loud and warm; calls everyone 'friend', swears in Kindle whenever prices come up.","greet":"Warm hearth! Ah, no. Lamp's lit, I mean. I switch. Come in, friend, everything here's honest except the prices.","lines":["Old Ammon at the gate has forty-one entries under my name marked 'made up'. This week I told him the plain truth and he marked that too.","Flameborne's rioting over the Edict and here we're writing a polite warning with a ribbon on it. My stepdad would throw bread at the lot of us.","Knight's pears in cinder-wine. It's a waste of wine. Don't tell the castle. Don't tell Millbrook I like it."],"about":"I grew up in Millbrook, came back to my mother's people at twenty, and never quite stopped shouting. I sell wine, charcoal, arrows and opinions. Opinions are free, which is why I'm poor.","languages":[{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"native (childhood)"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"fluent, with a Millbrook roll on the th"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a few words for the Lightforge wick-buyers"}],"childhood":"His mother Elys was a knight's daughter from the road town under Wickhaven's walls, and when Tobin was two she married Garrow Dunt, a Flameborne vintner from Millbrook, and took the boy down the track with her. So he grew up treading cinder-grapes barefoot in hot stone vats, in a house where everyone argued at dinner as a way of saying they loved you and the door was never shut. At his First No, aged thirteen, he stood up at the table and refused his stepfather's plan to make him a vintner. Garrow put down his cup and said, 'Good. You'd ruin it.' They laughed for most of an hour. Tobin still counts it as the best night of his life.","schooling":"Millbrook's slate-school in the press-house, under Dama Ressel, who taught sums on the ends of wine casks and paid correct answers in grapes. Lamp Test at eight in Millbrook: no lamp answered, though he will tell you the Ember lamp thought about it. Spark-poor. Fine at Kindle letters, hopeless at Lanthic ones until his twenties, when Alun made him read the gate-book aloud for practice.","history":"At twenty his mother wanted to come home, and Tobin came with her. She runs The Bale, a road inn under the walls; he set up the stall next to it and started bringing Garrow's cinder-wine up the track for the castle's knight's pears, which Flameborne finds faintly insulting and still sells. Wine led to charcoal, charcoal to arrows and packs, and now the stall is a shop with a door and a bad sign. He married Alun, a candle-maker from the castle farms, who is quiet and reads and is the only person who can make Tobin lower his voice. Every week at the gate he tells Gatekeeper Ammon Sael one thing he saw on the road, and for seventeen years they have been invented: a three-legged heron, a Lightforge man who lied by accident, a cart pulled by goats in hats. Ammon writes each down and marks it. Last month, coming up through the fog on the Millbrook track, Tobin saw a strip of mist that didn't move with the wind, greyish at its edges, and told Ammon so, plainly. Ammon marked that one 'made up' as well. Tobin has not got over it.","family":"Mother Elys at The Bale; stepfather Garrow in Millbrook, who sends a cask and an insult every season; husband Alun.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"A woman lit her own kitchen fire and a whole Realm decided that was the thing worth keeping, and nobody ever knelt to her. Tobin likes a hero you could have had dinner with."},"band":{"act":"Hot Corner","realm":"Flameborne","why":"He plays them in the shop loud enough that the knights on the wall complain, and he dances behind the counter while he weighs charcoal.","rv_vote":"Hot Corner, obviously; he's only half Spiritveil when he's sober."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for Councillor Ostrith Wane's motion to refuse the Edict outright. 'Warning is what you do to a dog.'","edict":"Against, loudly and at the counter. If Spiritveil only warns, he says, he'll go down to Millbrook for the riot."},"wants":"For Ammon Sael to write one of his stories in the gate-book without the mark.","site":"wickhaven","town":"Wickhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:wickhaven":{"name":"Halcyra Dunmoor","age":334,"role":"castle armourer and gate-hinge smith; once a squire and knight of the Lantern Knights; was on the walls the night the eleven held","look":"Square and solid, a silver plait to her belt, an old burn across the jaw she claims came from a frying pan.","voice":"Gravelly and unhurried; tells stories sideways and never finishes the funny ones.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Mind the anvil, it bites. You want armour, or you want to hear about the gate? Everyone wants to hear about the gate.","lines":["Lightforge farmers say the Wick Tower looks like a fence. It is a fence. That's what a hundred and six people make, stood in a row.","Flameborne keeps selling us cinder-wine and complaining we cook with it. Of course we cook with it. You can't drink that stuff on watch.","I was there the night the eleven held. I carried water up the stair. Fourteen trips. Nobody puts the water girl on a lamp, and quite right."],"about":"Squire, then knight, then a smith when my knees said so. Three hundred and some years of the same gate. It's a good gate. I've rehung it four times.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good; 'learned it off a war-band, which is a story'"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"She was born in the castle's laundry court, the daughter of a laundress and the castle cooper, and was underfoot from the first day she could walk. The knights let her carry mail shirts down to the sand-barrels to be scoured. At nine she took a sword off a rack to see how heavy it was, got caught, and was set to scour every hauberk in the armoury as punishment. She was meant to hate it. She loved it: the smell of oiled rings, the rasp of sand, the way a rusty shirt came up silver if you kept at it. By ten she could tell every knight on the wall by the sound of their boots on the stair, and she still can, with the living ones.","schooling":"The castle gate-school, children taught in the gatehouse by the gatekeeper of the time, Imelda Sael, who made them read the oldest pages of the gate-book aloud and gave a cup of pear-wine for the hardest one. At the Lamp Test the Veil lamp flickered once. Half-Spark. The castle had no coin to send her to Korrhaven and she didn't ask. She learned letters, knots and drill, and how to keep her mouth shut in a gatehouse.","history":"At fifteen she squired for Knight Maudry Fenn. At thirty-four came the night of the Flameborne war-band. Eleven knights held the gate; Halcyra ran water up the tower stair, fourteen trips, and on the last one Maudry was lying on the landing and didn't need it. Near dawn the band below were tired and singing. Halcyra went down the postern stair with a basket of knight's pears and two jars of cinder-wine out of the kitchen, walked into their camp and told them, in bad Kindle, that the pears were wasted on knights. They ate. They argued about the wine. They laughed at her accent and went home. The story in the gate-book says they got bored. She has never corrected it. She was knighted at forty, served on the walls for two centuries, and went to the forge at two hundred and fifty when her knees gave out on the stair. She has rehung the gate four times, mends the garrison's mail, and every autumn polishes the eleven iron brackets over the gate arch until they shine.","family":"Never married. A grand-nephew in the garrison who brings her his mail to mend so he has an excuse to visit.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired & Hollis Brask","why":"They ended a war by building one wall out of two Realms' stone. Halcyra knows what it costs to end a fight sideways, and what it costs to let other people get the song."},"band":{"act":"The Kept Coals","realm":"Flameborne","why":"The war-band below the gate sang a stomping song all that night, and the Kept Coals sound exactly like it. She goes to hear them and stands at the back with her arms folded.","rv_vote":"The Kept Coals."},"vote":{"local":"Lights her lamp for whatever motion the Lantern Knights' own councillor brings, and argues with him afterwards.","edict":"Warn. Tell Lightforge once, kindly. Then shut the gate."},"wants":"To rehang the gate one more time before her hands go.","site":"wickhaven","town":"Wickhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:wickhaven":{"name":"Fennick Ostrel","age":96,"role":"master wick-braider for the castle and the Lightforge bale-buyers; amateur historian of the braided-wick quarrel","look":"Narrow, stooped from the braiding bench, fingers shiny with wax, a pencil behind each ear.","voice":"Precise; footnotes his own sentences, then apologises for footnoting.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Do you know anything about Candlewick? Anything at all? Sorry. Good evening first. Good evening.","lines":["Candlewick's Day-Book entry is nine hundred years old. Our lamp is older. I've measured the soot. Probably older. I'm checking.","The Edict wants everything written down and signed. I'd normally like that. I find I don't. That's annoying.","My grandmother braided four strands. I braid five. Nobody can tell the difference but me and the flame."],"about":"I braid wick for the castle and for the bale-buyers over the border, and in the evenings I am trying to settle a nine-hundred-year argument. My wife says I could just pick a side like everyone else.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; he reads Day-Books and adores the evidential endings"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Four generations of Ostrels had braided in the sheds by the castle farms before him. He was a fussy child who sorted beeswax by shade and wept when other children mixed the piles. At ten he was given the job of cleaning the family's oldest lamp, the one that is said to remember the woman who first braided wick. He lit it to check the trim and, for a moment, felt her hands working, three strands over and under, and the smell of a kitchen he had never stood in. He sat on the floor of the shed until supper. His father told him the Candlewick story that night as a villain tale, and Fennick, who already preferred evidence to villains, asked how anyone knew.","schooling":"The castle gate-school under Bryony Sael, Ammon's aunt, who gave a cup of pear-wine to whoever read the gate-book's hardest page; Fennick won it so often she stopped offering it to him. Lamp Test: spark-poor. He learned Clear off a Lightforge bale-buyer's son who spent summers at the castle. He failed drill so badly the knights still mention it.","history":"He braided from fourteen and was a master by forty, the only braider in Wickhaven who does five strands. He married Gwen Halley, a castle cook, at thirty, and they raised three children who all left braiding, which he took better than anyone expected. At sixty he began writing to the keepers of Candlewick's Day-Books, very politely, asking questions about soot and dates. They wrote back, very politely, with evidential endings that he read aloud to Gwen for their beauty. At eighty-eight he went to Candlewick on the excuse of selling a bale and asked to see the old entry. A Day-Book keeper named Davora Glasswright let him read it. It records that a Spiritveil wick-seller stopped one night in a Candlewick kitchen, and that 'we braided until the lamp needed none'. He came home and lit his family's lamp again and felt the kitchen around those first hands, and this time he noticed a second pair, younger, across the table. He has told no one in either town.","family":"Wife Gwen, who calls the braided-wick quarrel 'your other wife'. Three grown children in the Vale, Mournhaven and Lantern Reach.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"'Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.' Fennick has the plain thing to say, and an enemy on each side of the border, and he hasn't managed it yet."},"band":{"act":"Plain Speaking","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Honest country with no metaphors. After a day of footnotes it's a rest.","rv_vote":"Plain Speaking, which will annoy everyone in Wickhaven, and he finds he wants that."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for the Council's warning motion, after reading all three drafts.","edict":"Torn. He loves a record and dislikes being one."},"wants":"To find a way to tell Wickhaven and Candlewick the truth on the same day, in the same room.","site":"wickhaven","town":"Wickhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:wickhaven:b":{"name":"Ottilie Brennick","age":23,"role":"Lantern Knight in her first year, assigned to tend the hundred and six lamps of the Wick Tower; a Full Spark of the Veil","look":"Tall, cropped hair, a knight's grey surcoat too big in the shoulders, rubs her left thumb across two fingertips like someone counting coins.","voice":"Earnest and quick; catches herself and starts the sentence again more formally.","greet":"Lamp's lit, traveller. I'm on tower duty, so if it's about tolls, the gate's, no, sorry. How can I help?","lines":["There's a lamp in the tower, the forty-third, that gutters on the same night every year. The knights say it's a draught. There's no draught on that side.","Lightforge says the tower looks like a fence. Good. That's the idea.","The thumb thing. It isn't mine. I picked it up at Korrhaven from a stranger's memory. I've stopped trying to stop it."],"about":"I went to the Conclave for the Veil Tower and came back three knots later with a sword and a habit I don't own. I fill the tower lamps. All hundred and six. I know every one of them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent (Conclave)"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"some swear-words from a dorm-mate"}],"childhood":"Youngest of five, daughter of the castle farrier and an orchard woman from the pear village down the hill, Ottilie spent her early years between hoof-trimmings and the Grandmothers' Row, where her nan's tree leans. At six a knight let her hold his sword for the length of one breath and she decided everything. At eight she climbed the Wick Tower without permission, counting lamps all the way up, and came down white-faced with a hundred and five. She was sure a knight was missing and wouldn't be calmed until someone showed her the lamp being cleaned in the armoury. At every Pear Moon her mother made her eat her own pear slowly, with the family watching, which she found unbearable and now misses.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight: the Veil lamp answered clean and bright. Full Spark. Lamp Years in the Vale, then the Veil Tower at Korrhaven from thirteen, three knots. Her tutor, Imrie Lathom, began every student's weaving on a borrowed memory of a stranger's breakfast, so the first thing you ever spent would be something nobody needed. Ottilie's was a woman counting coins at a table; the thumb habit came from that. She came home at sixteen.","history":"She squired at the castle from sixteen and was knighted at twenty-two, then given tower duty, which knights think of as the job for the ones who aren't trusted on the gate yet. She loves it. Every evening she climbs with an oil-can and trims a hundred and six wicks and knows each knight from the tower book: who they were, where they stood, how they fell. The forty-third lamp belongs to Knight Cassian Rell, recorded as lost holding the gate a hundred and eighty years ago. It gutters every year on the anniversary. Last spring she put her hand to it and wove ghostlight, against every rule, and spent something of her own to do it; she can no longer remember her eleventh birthday at all. What the lamp showed her was a man in a knight's coat walking away down the Lightforge road in daylight, alive, with a book under his arm. She hasn't told her captain. She doesn't know whether he ran or was sent, and she can't decide which would be worse to say out loud.","family":"Four older brothers and sisters, a farrier father, an orchard mother; a nan whose pear tree she visits before every long watch.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"A Warden who wouldn't cut his citadel's chain. Ottilie doesn't fully know what that cost him and doesn't need to; staying is what knights do."},"band":{"act":"Mirel of the Last Lamp","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"She saw them once at Korrhaven. Every show burns a memory from the crowd, and she has no idea what it took from her, which is somehow why she loves them.","rv_vote":"Mirel of the Last Lamp."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for Councillor Wane's motion to refuse the Edict.","edict":"Refuse. A Full Spark knight would be among the first registered and audited, and she knows what an audit of a memory is like from the inside."},"wants":"To find out where Knight Cassian Rell went.","site":"wickhaven","town":"Wickhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:echomere":{"name":"Penhallow Strake","age":71,"role":"village trader and keeper of the Calling Stone ledger; a bridge-weaver until his hands curled","look":"Looks about twenty-six; willow-cutter's hands that won't close all the way, a ledger tied to his belt with a strip of eel-skin.","voice":"Gossipy and discreet in the wrong order: tells you the gossip, then says he shouldn't have.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Bed for the night, or the Stone? The Stone's a crossing a night and you bring your own blanket. We're ashamed of it. Do you want it?","lines":["Flintmoor's knocking came through so loud at dawn a girl's vow sounded like a cart going down stairs. She's trying again tonight. Don't tell her I told you.","Lightforge wants to write down everyone's sparks. I write down everyone's vows. The difference is mine stay in a drawer.","My hands went at forty. Twenty-five years weaving bridges and one spring they just wouldn't close round the withy. I can still hold a pen. Badly."],"about":"I wove willow for the Vale's bridges until my hands quit, so now I keep the book for the Calling Stone. I hear every dawn there is. I've never called across myself. Not the type.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"a few words, chiefly 'knock it off' in Durm"}],"childhood":"He was the middle of seven in a coppicer's house in the willow-beds, all nine of them in one room that smelled of sap and wet boots. Penhallow learned early that if you lay awake by the window you could hear the dawn echoes come back off the mere, and that grown people called the most extraordinary things across it at dusk. By eight he knew half the village's business and sold it to his brothers for trout. His mother found out. She walked him to the Calling Stone that evening and made him shout an apology to the whole village, by name, one by one. At dawn the mere gave it all back in his own squeaking voice, and everyone was at the water to hear it. He has not called anything across since.","schooling":"The willow-school in the coppice under Old Gethin Rowe, who taught letters by scratching them on peeled withies that then went into baskets, so half the baskets in Lanthys Vale have Penhallow's spelling mistakes woven into them. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. Good at weaving, bad at sums, very good at remembering who said what.","history":"He went on the bridge crews at fifteen and spent twenty-five years on the willow bridges of the Vale, hanging off scaffolds above the river, weaving next to Faelin Rook, who was faster than him and said so. At forty the willow-claw took his hands, as it takes most weavers sooner or later, and the village gave him the Calling Stone ledger on the grounds that he already knew everyone's business and might as well be paid for it. Thirty-one years he has kept it: who rented the Stone, which night, and whether what they called came back clear. He started the rule about bringing your own blanket. He started selling bread and kit out of the same hut, then bows to walkers heading for the Pale, then trout at dawn to the night-renters with Hobb Wimmery, which is the closest thing to a business partnership Hobb has ever had. He sits up at the Stone most dawns with the ledger on his knee. Twenty-seven years ago he wrote down a vow as clear that had come back with a stammer in it, so a couple could marry before the bride's mother found a reason to stop it. They have been married ever since, and happily.","family":"Six brothers and sisters scattered between the coppice and the Vale; never married; nineteen nieces and nephews, every one of whom thinks he knows their secrets. He does.","hero":{"who":"Vey Who Said It","why":"Somebody in Shadowdeep said their own full name aloud, once, on purpose. Echo Mere says everything aloud and still nobody says the real thing. Penhallow includes himself."},"band":{"act":"Retell","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A rapper who repeats every earlier bar back at you. 'It's the mere with a beat.'","rv_vote":"Retell."},"vote":{"local":"Lamp lit for the Council's warning.","edict":"Warn, and then warn again next morning, louder, the way the mere does."},"wants":"To call one thing across the water before his hands lose the pen as well.","site":"echomere","town":"Echo Mere","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:echomere":{"name":"Ansel Tarrock","age":45,"role":"village smith; makes the bill-hooks and coppice knives; trained in Stonehold's Flintmoor and reads its seismic taps","look":"Thick-necked, looks thirty, quarry dust in his eyebrows that never washes out, taps his fingers on everything he touches.","voice":"Few words, heavy at the end, drops into Durm when he's annoyed.","greet":"Set. Lamp's lit, sorry. Picked that up over the hill. You want a blade or a hook?","lines":["The village wrote Flintmoor another letter. Flintmoor tapped back. I know what it said. I'm not saying.","Lightforge counting sparks? Stonehold'll refuse it. Good. We should too. A warning's only a slow yes.","I tap when I'm thinking. A hammer's a kind of tap. Don't read anything into it."],"about":"I learned the hammer in Flintmoor, over the hill, because nobody here would take a boy who broke things. Came back. Make hooks for the coppice. Some nights I go up on the ridge and talk to the rock.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"good; fluent in the quarry's seismic taps"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"His father fished trout and his mother made baskets, and Ansel broke things: withies, an oar, a neighbour's bench, his own front tooth on a gatepost. He couldn't sit through the long quiet evenings by the mere when everyone waited for dawn. At nine, after a row at home he no longer remembers the cause of, he ran over the hills towards the noise of Flintmoor's quarry and fell asleep in a cutting. The quarrymen who found him fed him carry-bread, and he chipped another tooth on it, and they laughed at him without any meanness at all. He sat in the cutting all afternoon listening to them knock to each other through the stone and decided that was the best sound in the Nine Realms.","schooling":"The coppice school under Gethin Rowe, who gave up on his letters within a year and let him hammer withy-stakes instead. Lamp Test: spark-poor. At thirteen his parents gave in and sent him to Flintmoor to Smith Bertolf Karnsett, who taught him iron, carry-bread and the taps for come, stop, fire, food and 'your mother's here'.","history":"He spent ten years in Flintmoor and did his own Shouldering at fifteen with the quarry lads, a marker-stone a whole mile, though nobody had asked a Spiritveil boy to. He came home at twenty-three when Echo Mere's old smith's lamp went up the road, and took the forge. Twenty-two years since, making bill-hooks and slashers for the coppice, mending traps and bridge-irons. In the Flintmoor quarrel he is the only person in Echo Mere who can read Flintmoor's replies. When the village sends a letter complaining about the knocking on the lovers' vows, Flintmoor taps its answer through the ridge, and Ansel puts his palm on the forge's footing stone and translates. Loosely. He has never once passed on a Flintmoor insult exactly. Most dusks he climbs the ridge and taps news back and forth with his old friend Brannick Durr, a quarry foreman, through half a mile of rock. Brannick's son Tollan is marrying Saffi Marrell next month. Ansel is supposed to keep the peace at the wedding. He is not optimistic.","family":"Parents still in the willow-beds. Unmarried. Brannick Durr is the closest thing he has to a brother, and they have stood in the same room perhaps a dozen times in twenty years.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"The anchor-master who held the bridge everyone else wanted dropped, and Stormreach riders still throw stones at the Stillman. Someone always throws stones at the man holding the middle."},"band":{"act":"The Stampers","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Amapiano where the applause is the beat. He saw them in Flintmoor; the whole hall stamped and the floor answered.","rv_vote":"The Stampers."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for Councillor Wane's motion to refuse the Edict.","edict":"Refuse outright, like Stonehold. He thinks the Council's warning is a habit, not a decision."},"wants":"For the two villages to sit at one table for Saffi and Tollan's wedding without anyone throwing a trout.","site":"echomere","town":"Echo Mere","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:echomere":{"name":"Saffi Marrell","age":26,"role":"willow-coppicer and bridge-weaver; marrying a Flintmoor quarryman in three weeks","look":"Freckled, willow-sap stains to both elbows, a wedding ribbon already tied in her hair three weeks early.","voice":"Bright and nervous; laughs at the wrong moment, asks you to say things again.","greet":"Lamp's lit! Sorry, I was practising. Not my vow. Just saying things over water. Do you want a trout?","lines":["My Late Answer's in three weeks. If Flintmoor knocks over it, I'll go over the hill and knock back. With a mallet.","Lightforge wants to register people. They'd have to register Tollan as Stonehold and me as Spiritveil and the wedding as a border incident.","I've written my vow eleven times. It gets shorter every time. It's down to nine words."],"about":"I cut willow for the Vale's bridges like my mum did. I'm marrying a quarryman from Flintmoor, which half the village thinks is a joke and the other half thinks is a declaration of war.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"halting but improving; 'I learned the swearing first'"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"}],"childhood":"She was the Marrells' only child, raised in the coppice with sap on everything, and told from the cradle that her parents' Late Answer had come back off the mere 'clear as a bell'. She and the other children played a game called Stealing Echoes: dare each other to shout something rude at dusk, then hide at dawn and try not to laugh when it came back. At eleven she shouted that Faelin Rook had a beard. At dawn the whole village was at the water for someone else's wedding and heard it in Saffi's voice. Faelin didn't scold her. She walked Saffi to the bridge-sheds that afternoon and put a withy in her hands and said, 'If you're that loud, you can be useful.'","schooling":"By her time the willow-school had moved into the basket shed, taught by Mistress Ondra Fell, who taught letters by making the children write love letters to the mere and read them out. At the Lamp Test the Veil lamp hiccupped. Half-Spark. Her family needed her in the coppice, and she has never been sorry; she has seen what the weaving costs the Lantern Knights who come through.","history":"She coppiced with her mother from fourteen and joined Faelin's bridge crew at twenty, and has woven on two of Lanthys Vale's bridges. At twenty-two she met Tollan Durr on the ridge, where Flintmoor's quarry and Echo Mere's willow-beds come within a stone's throw. He was cutting a marker-stone on the slope she was cutting withies on, and they had a shouting match about whose slope it was. He tapped 'sorry' on a rock. She didn't understand it and threw a stick at him. He came back a week later with a slate on which he'd written out thirty taps and their meanings in careful Bridge. He proposed a year ago with a very good rock, as they do over the hill. They will have a Hearthstone wedding in Flintmoor and a Late Answer at Echo Mere, both, and both families are pretending to be calm. Her father keeps saying 'well' and going out to the coppice.","family":"Parents Jory-Ann and Cal Marrell, coppicers; fiancé Tollan Durr; a future father-in-law, Brannick, who taps very loudly when moved.","hero":{"who":"Faelin Rook","why":"Faelin gave a rude eleven-year-old a withy instead of a scolding, and has woven three of the Vale's bridges. Saffi wants to weave a fourth before Faelin does."},"band":{"act":"Mist-Pear","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Wedding R&B. She has Mist-Pear stuck in her head every waking minute and has stopped fighting it.","rv_vote":"Mist-Pear. It's her wedding year; she's allowed."},"vote":{"local":"Hasn't lit a lamp this season. She's saving it for the shelf vote, if the Council ever holds one.","edict":"Torn. She'd warn; Tollan's family would refuse; they argue about it in bed and both enjoy it."},"wants":"For the mere to give her vow back clear the first time, with no knocking over it.","site":"echomere","town":"Echo Mere","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:echomere:b":{"name":"Hobb Wimmery","age":402,"role":"trout fisher; steams trout in willow at dawn and sells them to the Calling Stone's night-renters","look":"Looks forty because he squints; hair wholly silver, cut with his own gutting knife; smells of willow smoke.","voice":"Slow, short and pleased; ends most stories with 'that's all'.","greet":"Morning. Or evening. I lose track out on the water. Lamp's lit. Trout?","lines":["They say Flintmoor's taps are getting louder. I've fished through four hundred years of dawns. They were louder before.","The Edict. They argue it up at the Vale. Down here we fish. That's all.","Four hundred and two. Never called anything across the mere. Didn't need to. My wife said everything for the both of us. That's all."],"about":"I fish trout and steam them in willow at dawn. I've done it a long while. It's a dull life and I like it very much. Ask me about trout instead. There's more to say about trout.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"prices only"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few funeral words, from his wife's mother"}],"childhood":"His first memory is his father holding him up over the side of a stilt hut at first light so he could hear the echoes coming home across the water, 'like rain going back up'. He caught his first trout at five, tickling it out from under a bank with his fingers the way his father showed him. Other children thought he was slow because he would sit for an afternoon in a coracle and not say anything. He wasn't slow. He liked waiting, and the mere was the best thing to wait beside. His mother cooked trout in willow and over-salted it every single time, and he learned to do it himself mainly so somebody would.","schooling":"Not much, he says. The willow-school was there, kept then by Coppice-mother Haldis, and he went in winter because summer was for fish. Haldis let him sit by the door to watch the water, and he learned enough letters to sign his name and read a price. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. Spark-poor. He doesn't remember minding.","history":"He has fished the same bank for nearly four hundred years. At sixty he married Morra Tansey, a talker from the Pale edge, who did all the calling across the mere either of them ever needed. Their Late Answer came back clear the first time; he likes to point out that this is less common than people think. They had two children, a son who weaves baskets in the Vale and a daughter, Ilsabet, who copies the Echo Mere families' lamps at the Archive. Morra's lamp went up the road ninety years ago. Twice in his life he has seen the mere freeze. Once, a century back, the echoes came home in the wrong order one morning and the village argued about it for a year; he slept through the morning in question and has never been sorry. His only new idea in four centuries was Penhallow's, really: selling steamed trout at dawn to whoever had sat up all night on the willow bench. It's done well. People waiting for their own voice want something hot.","family":"A son in the Vale, a copyist daughter in the Archive who disputes his memory of her mother's laugh, seven grandchildren, and Morra's lamp, which he visits twice a decade.","hero":{"who":"Orrin Who Stayed","why":"The first Anchorite stayed put for those who come back. Hobb has stayed put. Nobody's come back, he says, but he's caught a lot of trout."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Twelve-minute post-rock. He can land a fish during one song and still hear the end.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp once, about the bridge tolls, two hundred years ago. Hasn't needed to since.","edict":"Warn, he supposes. Whatever the Vale says. They're cleverer up there."},"wants":"Another hundred years on the same bank.","site":"echomere","town":"Echo Mere","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:graveglass":{"name":"Iselda Whitlow","age":58,"role":"village trader and go-between with the Lightforge assayers; a Rim-born widow; a Penumbrist in secret","look":"Grey-blonde, the only properly lined face in Graveglass, a Day-Book with a cracked spine always under one arm.","voice":"Clear-speech habits she can't shake: tells you how she knows a thing, 'I saw', 'I was told', 'I think'.","greet":"Aure, Lamp's lit. Forgive me. Thirty years and I still say both. Kit, bread, arrows? I saw you looking at the bows.","lines":["The assayers came last week with a new form: every shard named, every crate signed. I was told it's the Edict. I think it's Assessor Pallant being Pallant.","In Meridian everything is lit. Here a shard can hold a dead child's face and nobody has to read it out. I know which I'd choose, now.","I still keep a Day-Book. I just don't write the true things in it anymore. I write those on the back pages."],"about":"I came from the Rim of Lightforge, married a Graveglass miner, and stayed when he went up the road in a lantern. I'm the one who stands between the crates and the assayers. Both sides think I'm on the other side.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"fluent, with a Rim accent the children imitate"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"a few breathy words from Rim tannery smugglers"}],"childhood":"She grew up in Lampwick on the Rim, a tanner's daughter, in the one part of Lightforge that gets a real dusk. The tan-pits stank all day; in the evening the stag-antler lamps came on along the lanes and the light went soft and brown, and Iselda thought it was the most beautiful thing in the world. At her First Telling, aged twelve, she stood before the neighbours and told them her true thing: that she was frightened of noon. They gave her a told name for it, Iselda Who Liked the Dark, and her parents never quite forgave the neighbours. She used to sit on the tannery roof in the dusk hour with her knees up, until her mother came out with a lamp and stood beside her without a word.","schooling":"Rim lamp-school in Lampwick under Teacher Hesk Lampwright, who taught letters only in the dusk hour and ended every lesson by putting the lamp out and letting the children sit in the dark for a count of ten. Lamp Test at eight: the Radiance lamp warmed under her hand but never lit. Spark-poor. She left with perfect Clear grammar, a clerk's arithmetic, and a habit of counting to ten in the dark when frightened.","history":"She clerked at the tannery until twenty-six, when she rode with a leather shipment to Graveglass and met Dunstan Pryor, a miner who hummed even when he was eating. She married him at twenty-eight and moved to the hill, learned Lanthic badly and then well, and was distrusted for years as the Lightforger who'd come to count things. She made herself useful the only way she could: she was the one who could talk to the assayers in their own tongue and still not sign a crate. She took over the store, then the whole business of the trade. Six years ago a gallery came down in the third shaft and Dunstan's lamp went up the road at the age of eighty, which in Graveglass is very young. She is fifty-eight and will be the only person on the hill who grows properly old. She joined the Penumbrists in Lampwick at nineteen, at a reading in a cellar, and has kept the back pages of her Day-Book dark ever since. Under the Edict that is now a crime in the Realm she was born in.","family":"Dunstan's lamp is in the Archive; his brothers treat her as a sister and also as a foreigner, often in one sentence. Her mother still writes from Lampwick, every letter beginning 'I saw'.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"The first Penumbrist said mercy needs shade. Iselda has spent thirty years standing in a shed full of shade and calling it trade."},"band":{"act":"Two Windows","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A Lightforge-Shadowdeep couple singing together is the only Lightforge act that admits there's a back to noon.","rv_vote":"Two Windows."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for Councillor Wane's motion to refuse the Edict. Nobody on the Council checked where she was born.","edict":"Against, and a secret Penumbrist; in Lightforge terms, the very thing the Edict was written to find."},"wants":"To have a lamp named for her in the Archive when she goes, though she was never named to one at birth.","site":"graveglass","town":"Graveglass","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:graveglass":{"name":"Corvin Lathe","age":140,"role":"smith of shard-chisels and pick-heads; a miner for fifty years until the day he stopped humming","look":"Looks twenty-six, a gap where a front tooth was, hums under his breath without stopping, even at the anvil.","voice":"Gentle and hesitant; breaks off mid-sentence to hum a bar, then carries on.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Mind the, hm-hm, the bucket. Chisels are on the left. Don't touch the blue ones, they're still cooling.","lines":["Elowen at the Sorting Shed hasn't sold a child's shard in thirty years. The Archive's started asking why the back wall's so full.","Lightforge wants every shard named. You can't name a shard. You can only, hm, look at it.","I stopped humming once. Underground. Ninety years ago. Something went into the glass. I don't know what. That's the trouble with it."],"about":"I cut glass for fifty years, and now I make the chisels instead. You can't think the wrong thing into an anvil. Mostly you can't.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a few words: 'crate', 'sign', 'refuse'"}],"childhood":"He was the youngest of nine in the miners' row, and the Humming Down tune was the first thing he ever heard; his mother hummed it over his cradle, his father came home humming it with glittering dust on his hands. At eight Corvin crept into the Sorting Shed after dark and stood in front of the back wall, where the shards nobody will sell are shelved, and tilted one to the lantern. A baby laughed at him out of the glass, silently, gums and all. He ran the whole way home and wouldn't say why. He kept a jar of sparkling shard-dust under his bed for years, shaking it to watch it settle, and believed for a long time that it was full of tiny people.","schooling":"The hum-school in the winding-house, kept by Old Pellam Cray, an ex-miner deaf in one ear, who taught the tune eight different ways and letters as an afterthought. Lamp Test: spark-poor. Corvin hummed perfectly and spelled terribly, and Pellam said that was the right way round for Graveglass. Corvin still hums Pellam's slow third version when he is frightened.","history":"He went down the shaft at fourteen and cut glass for fifty years. Somewhere in his late forties he was courting. He knows that much. He has a bundle of letters in a hand he doesn't recognise, each signed with a single letter, A, full of plans to meet at the Vale market. When he was fifty-one there was a rumble in a seam and the timbers groaned, and for perhaps twenty breaths, in the dark, he forgot to hum. The others hauled him up at once. Afterwards there was a clean gap in him where a person had been. He couldn't place the letters. He didn't answer them. After a while they stopped. Nobody in Graveglass knew who A was; he had never brought her home. He left the shaft, apprenticed himself to the old smith, Brig Halvard, and took the forge at ninety. His blue-steel chisels are the best on the hill. He hums while he tempers them, so the steel takes the tune. On slow evenings he goes to the Sorting Shed and asks Elowen if anything new has come up out of his old seam.","family":"Eight brothers and sisters, most still mining, all of whom have stopped asking about the letters. Never married, as far as he knows, which he says is the joke.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"She led the Unmarked to the slag-hills and made an ugly first hammer that worked. Corvin makes ugly chisels that work and thinks that's the most honest thing a person can do."},"band":{"act":"Paper Lantern Funeral","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Doom-gaze slow enough that he can hum the Graveglass tune under it without it clashing.","rv_vote":"Paper Lantern Funeral."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for Councillor Lanmore's motion to move the oldest shelves to Willowmere. Memories should go where there's room for them.","edict":"Refuse. If Lightforge audits his memory they'll find a hole and want to know what was in it."},"wants":"To learn the name that goes with the letters signed A.","site":"graveglass","town":"Graveglass","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:graveglass":{"name":"Bryn Halloway","age":13,"role":"miner's son who can't hold the Humming Down tune; sweeper and lantern-holder at the Sorting Shed","look":"Gangly, ears that stick out, shard-glitter in his hair from sitting too close to the sorting tables.","voice":"Complaints in bursts, then sudden enormous enthusiasm.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Don't ask me to hum. Everyone asks me to hum.","lines":["The Lightforge assayers came in a carriage with glass windows. Glass windows! For carrying glass! I said that to one of them and he didn't laugh.","Everyone says the Edict's bad. Nobody's told me what it is. I think it's mostly forms.","I'm going to be a Shardwatch or something. Something where you watch. You don't have to hum to watch."],"about":"I'm Bryn. Dad mines, Mam sorts, my sister cuts. I'm supposed to go down next spring and I can't hold the tune. So that's me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"three words: 'crate', 'sign here', 'no'"}],"childhood":"Bryn was born singing flat, his mam says, and never improved. Middle of three, between a sister who cuts glass beautifully and a baby brother who already hums in tune in his sleep, which Bryn takes personally. At seven he hummed along in a game and an old miner pulled a face like he'd heard a crack in a seam, and the other children made the face at him for a year. At nine he watched his sister go down the shaft for the first time, humming perfectly, and went straight home to the woodshed and practised until dark. His mam came out with a lamp and stood listening for a long time and then said, 'Well.' That was all. He has a Kestrel Vane poster a carter gave him nailed inside the woodshed door.","schooling":"The hum-school in the winding-house, now taught by Mistress Ennor Cray, old Pellam's granddaughter, who teaches the tune off a ringing tuning-shard. Bryn fails it every single term. Ennor gave up and made him keep the shard tallies instead, and he turns out to be the best counter the school has had in years. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. He asked if there was a counting lamp.","history":"At eleven he started sweeping the Sorting Shed, and Elowen Tamsy began to let him hold the lantern while she logged the faces. He has seen thousands: a farmer, a bride, a soldier on a horse with a lance up, which made him decide he would see the world and not just the inside of a hill. At twelve the family agreed he'd go down the shaft next spring if he could hum by then. He has been trying. He has also invented his own way: tapping the Humming Down rhythm on his breastbone with two fingers instead of humming the notes. The miners have split over whether rhythm counts if the pitch doesn't, and the winding-house is supposed to settle it at the next meeting. Last summer he ran away as far as the Lanthys road and came back in time for supper because it was trout. He didn't tell anyone he'd gone. His mam knew anyway.","family":"Dad Ruel, a miner; Mam Tesni, a sorter; older sister Carys, a cutter who hums him to sleep when he's upset, which he denies; a baby brother who is too good at everything.","hero":{"who":"Amon Greycloak","why":"The first Shardwatch watched the edge. You don't have to hum to watch an edge, Bryn has decided, so it counts."},"band":{"act":"Kestrel Vane","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A sky-racer who gets older every season and keeps racing. The poster's on the woodshed door. He knows every word and sings them all flat.","rv_vote":"Kestrel Vane."},"vote":{"local":"He's thirteen. He put a candle in his window for 'anything that keeps me out of the shaft'.","edict":"Against, because Mam is, and Mam reads things."},"wants":"For the winding-house to rule that tapping counts.","site":"graveglass","town":"Graveglass","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:graveglass:b":{"name":"Gwyth Morrow","age":205,"role":"cook of the Morrow family's remembered soup and of the shaft-head canteen","look":"Looks twenty-eight, a broad cook's apron over a miner's jacket, a burn on her wrist shaped like a ladle-hook.","voice":"Brisk and motherly; measures out her words like salt.","greet":"Lamp's lit. You look like you missed supper. Everyone who comes up this road has. Sit down.","lines":["They say an Archive copyist came asking after a face on the back wall. Wouldn't say whose. Stayed three days. Went home without it.","The assayers want our shards named. Name the one in my kitchen, then. I'd love to know.","My nan's soup has too much salt in it. It always did. That's how I knew it was her. Or I thought it was."],"about":"I cook the remembered soup for my family and for anybody who turns up, off the shard my great-grandfather cut. Same broth, same salt, a hundred and eighty years. Lately I've had questions about the hands in it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent; she's fed carters for two centuries"}],"childhood":"The Morrow kitchen had a shard on its own shelf over the stove, wrapped in a napkin, and in it were the hands of Gran Ebrel, who had died before anyone now living was born. Gwyth's mother would take it down, tilt it to the window, and copy every pinch and stir. At six Gwyth was allowed to tilt it herself. She saw the hands roll salt between finger and thumb, a big pinch, then another, and laughed out loud. She talked to the shard as a child the way other children talk to a cat: told it about her day, her fights, which boy she hated. When her little brother knocked it off the shelf she caught it in her apron, and nobody in the family has ever let her forget it.","schooling":"The hum-school in the winding-house, under a teacher called Ilar Venn, who cared more about soup than spelling and let the girls who could cook out early to run the canteen. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. She can read and write a recipe and a bill, and does not see what else letters are for.","history":"She cooked in the canteen from fifteen and took it over at forty. She married Alder Morrow, a cutter, at fifty, and has cooked the remembered soup for four children, eleven grandchildren and every miner who ever came up cold. Twenty years ago her granddaughter, apprenticed to the Sorting Shed, borrowed the family shard to show Elowen Tamsy, who held it up to the lantern for a long minute and said, quietly, that the hands were wearing a ring. A ring of plaited willow, the kind they give at an Echo Mere Late Answer. No Morrow, no Ebrel, nobody in Gwyth's line was ever wed at Echo Mere. Gwyth went home and looked. It is there, plain as anything, and she had never once noticed it in two hundred years of watching those hands. She has written to the trader at Echo Mere, asking whether anyone there had a grandmother who couldn't stop salting. She hasn't had an answer yet.","family":"Husband Alder; four children, eleven grandchildren, one shard; and a stranger's hands she has cooked beside every day of her life.","hero":{"who":"The woman in the shard","why":"She doesn't know who she is. She has cooked with her every day for two hundred years, and she can't think of anyone she knows better."},"band":{"act":"Willowroot","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Amapiano on willow harps: the washers at the canteen tubs sing it all day, and it keeps the stirring arm moving.","rv_vote":"Willowroot."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp against moving the oldest shelves. Things should be kept where they were put. She hears herself say it and winces.","edict":"Warn. Then send Lightforge a bowl of soup and ask them to name what's in it."},"wants":"To know whose hands she has been cooking with.","site":"graveglass","town":"Graveglass","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:mournhaven":{"name":"Sennet Oriel","age":266,"role":"trader at the landward end of the Long Jetty; salvager of sunk lanterns; the town's unofficial weather-reader of the Drift","look":"Weather-dark for a Spiritveiler, three silver streaks forked like lightning, a brass glass-barometer on a strap across her chest.","voice":"Dry and ominous by habit, then undercuts herself before you can laugh at her.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Wind's coming round from the Pale. Probably nothing. Buy a cloak anyway.","lines":["Glass has dropped three nights running. Last time it did that the jetty lost forty hooks. I told the harbour-reeve. He told me to sell cloaks.","The Council's warning Lightforge. Nobody listens to a warning. I've given eleven. Two came true. They remember the other nine.","I fish broken lanterns off the bottom of the Drift. Not to sell. To mend. Then they go up the road with their families."],"about":"I sell cloaks and kit to mourners and I watch the weather on the Drift. Two hundred and forty years of watching. I've been right about storms more often than not, and nobody remembers that part.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"good; a Frostgrave weather-reader taught her one winter"}],"childhood":"Her father built lantern-boats and her mother wove fog-nets, and Sennet grew up in a boat-shed full of half-finished hulls, sawdust and the smell of pitch. When she was seven a squall came off the water at night and turned over a family's boat halfway out to the jetty; she watched from the shed door as the lantern went under, still lit, and its light went green, then gone. After that she started keeping a weather book in chalk on the shed's back wall: wind, cloud, the colour of the water at dusk. Every spring her father limewashed the wall. Every spring she started again. He stopped limewashing it the year she turned twelve, and never said why.","schooling":"Mournhaven's wake-school, children taught in the back room of an inn between funerals by Teacher Aldyth Moor, who made each pupil tell a story about a stranger's dead before they were allowed home. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. At twenty-two she spent a winter at Coldwater Step in Frostgrave with an old weather-reader who taught her the brass glass and enough Rime to argue with him.","history":"She built boats with her father until she was eighty and then took the store at the jetty's foot, selling cloaks, rope, lantern-wicks and, lately, bows to walkers on the Pale road. She never stopped watching the Drift. At a hundred and fifty she warned of a great squall and the harbour-reeve cleared the jetty on her word. Nothing came. Thirty families lost their Night on the Water for a breeze, and several didn't speak to her for a decade. At two hundred and ten she warned again, and was ignored, and that night the wind took forty hooks and twelve lanterns off the jetty and into the black. She spent the next month diving for them with a weighted line and a lamp in a glass bell, and brought up eleven. Eleven families came for theirs. She mended each, relit each, and the families carried them up to Lanthys Vale a month late. The twelfth lantern nobody claimed. She never married. She lives over the store with her sister's grown children and a barometer she taps every hour without noticing.","family":"Her sister's three children, all past a hundred, all of whom tap the barometer when they walk past it now.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"A seer who warned of a collapse and was struck from the record for it. Sennet doesn't know the details and doesn't need to: she knows what the face of a room looks like when you've warned it."},"band":{"act":"Isveig","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Isveig sings the saddest song perfectly and feels nothing, which is exactly how Sennet tells people a storm is coming.","rv_vote":"Isveig."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for Mournhaven's own motion, that its families' lamps be shelved in Mournhaven and not sent up the road.","edict":"Warn, but loudly, with a date, and a second warning ready."},"wants":"To be wrong about the glass this week.","site":"mournhaven","town":"Mournhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:mournhaven":{"name":"Ruan Teague","age":33,"role":"smith of the Long Jetty's lantern-hooks and the boat-builders' nails; a Stormreach exile","look":"Wiry, never sits, a single white streak above his left ear, eats standing up at the anvil.","voice":"Loud for Mournhaven, fast, ends his sentences with a hard stop the way Gale does.","greet":"Wahai! Lamp's lit, I mean. Don't sit, there's nowhere to. I eat standing anyway.","lines":["Stormreach's Assembly shouted the Edict down so loud they heard it on Korr's Span. Here they wrote a letter. With a seal on it.","Still is dead, we say at home. Then I came to a town where everyone sits up all night on purpose. They're not dead. Took me ten years to see it.","One streak. Got it at my First Fall. Charged without meaning to. My mother said 'worth the grey'. You only say that about someone else. She said it to my face. Still annoyed."],"about":"Stormreach boy, came down to Spiritveil for a reason I'll tell you after a drink. I make the hooks on the Long Jetty. Every lantern out there hangs off my iron. Somebody has to keep still enough to make them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"good, spoken too fast"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"understands a fair bit, thanks to Gale"}],"childhood":"He was born on a chain-lashed cliff-farm on a citadel's underside below Skyrend, the son of strike-goat herders, and he ran everywhere, as all Stormreach children do, eating trail-cake on the move and sleeping like a dropped coat. At his First Fall, aged ten, he stepped off the edge into the wind over his family's net and panicked halfway down, and something in him went off like a struck chain. He landed in the net crackling, with his hair standing up and one lock gone white as goat's milk. Nobody had known he had a spark at all. His mother laughed until she cried and then said the thing you only say about other people.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight: the Tempest lamp flickered and fizzed. Half-Spark, but his parents needed him with the goats and never sent him on. Later he went to a chain-yard school in the Chainyards, the only place in Stormreach where people are allowed to stand still, under Chain-wright Dorrell Fast, who taught letters by stamping them into links. He learned forging there, and failed riding outright.","history":"At nineteen he dared his best friend, Dashen, to race a skyray off the citadel's lip in a crosswind. Dashen fell into the Spill. Stormreach lets its dead go into the gulf and moves on, standing up. Ruan sat down. He sat down for a week, on the cliff-farm steps, which in Stormreach is what you do at a funeral and nowhere else, and his neighbours stepped round him like a rock. Then he heard a carter talk about a town in Spiritveil where whole families sit up all night beside their dead on purpose, and he walked the bridges down to Mournhaven with a chain-wright's hammer in his pack. The old jetty smith took him on. Eleven years later the forge is his. He makes the hooks along both sides of the Long Jetty, the nails for the lantern-boats, the iron cord-ring on the statue at the jetty's end. Every year on the anniversary he ties a small lantern with no name on it to a hook at the far end and sits beside it all night, which at home would make him a stranger and here makes him a neighbour.","family":"Parents and two sisters on the cliff-farm, who write once a year in shouted capitals. No partner. Half of Mournhaven's grandmothers are trying to fix that.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"'No Realm should ever be alone.' He built the first bridges. Ruan walked them all the way down here to find out what that meant."},"band":{"act":"The Nine Storms","realm":"Stormreach","why":"White-haired at twenty-five and singing 'Worth the Grey'. He plays it at the forge and nobody in Mournhaven understands why he grins at the chorus.","rv_vote":"The Nine Storms."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for Councillor Wane's motion to refuse the Edict, and shouted it at the window too, from habit.","edict":"Against. At home he'd be on the rooftops with Seraphine Skyrend's people."},"wants":"To bring his mother down the bridges to sit one night on the jetty with him.","site":"mournhaven","town":"Mournhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:mournhaven":{"name":"Bettany Coil","age":88,"role":"wake cook at the Heron's Rest; decides who gets one poached pear and who gets two","look":"Round-faced, looks twenty-five, sleeves always rolled, a ladle on a cord round her neck like a necklace.","voice":"Teasing and blunt; reads your face out loud to you.","greet":"Lamp's lit, love. Two pears for you. Don't argue. I can see it.","lines":["Quillan Mere's sung the heron song eleven thousand times. Last night he forgot the second verse. Nobody said a word. Everyone noticed.","The Council's cart comes every dawn for our lamps. I give the carter a pear. He's from here. It's not his fault. It's a bit his fault.","One pear, you'll be fine. Two, you need sitting with. I've never given anyone three. I've wanted to."],"about":"I poach pears for the dead's last night and for whoever's sitting with them. I'm good at faces and bad at everything else. Ask my husband. No, don't, he'll agree.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a few words, all swearing, from a Flameborne pastry cook"}],"childhood":"Her aunt ran the Heron's Rest, a wake inn on the shore, and Bettany was carrying trays out onto the jetty by five, bossy and sure of herself. Her aunt taught her the trade by hand-signal from the kitchen door: one finger, one pear; two fingers, two. At six Bettany got it wrong for the first time. A man was laughing with his brothers by his father's lantern, and she gave him one. Two hours later he was crying so hard his brothers held him up. Bettany ran a second pear down the quarter-mile of jetty at midnight in her nightdress, and stood there while he ate it. Her aunt met her at the shore, wrapped her in a shawl and said, 'That's the job. Now you know.'","schooling":"Wake-school in the inn's back room under Teacher Efa Brack, who taught counting with pears and wouldn't let anyone eat the sums. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. She learned to read faces long before she learned to read Bridge, and her handwriting is still so bad the inn's orders go out in her husband's hand.","history":"She cooked under her aunt from fourteen and took over the kitchen at fifty, when her aunt's hands started to shake. In between she married Garan Coil, a lantern-boat builder with a quiet laugh, and they had a daughter, Wenna, now forty and a rower. Bettany has decided pears for something like fifty thousand mourners and keeps no record of it, except a small book of faces she thinks should have had three. Last spring a Lightforge visitor sat on the jetty with a stranger's dead and asked for his pear plain, no sauce, because sauce hides things. She nearly tipped him in the Drift. She gave him two instead and watched him come apart over the second like a wet loaf. Her best friend since childhood is Emrys Dunlin, the Council's dawn-cart driver, whom half the town won't speak to; she brings him a pear every morning on the road and stands with him while he loads.","family":"Husband Garan; daughter Wenna, a rower; her aunt, retired and supervising from a chair by the stove.","hero":{"who":"Quillan Mere","why":"Eleven thousand lanterns rowed out and the heron song every time. Bettany thinks doing the same small kind thing eleven thousand times is braver than anything in the Archive."},"band":{"act":"Sunwarm","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Summer Latin pop. She can't cook to sad music; she'd drown in the pot.","rv_vote":"Sunwarm, and she'll hear about it from every Mournhaven cook at the market."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for Mournhaven's motion to keep its own families' lamps in town.","edict":"Warn. And send them a pear."},"wants":"To meet someone who needs three, and give them three.","site":"mournhaven","town":"Mournhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:mournhaven:b":{"name":"Emrys Dunlin","age":151,"role":"driver of the Lantern Council's dawn cart, which collects Mournhaven's lamps for the Archive; a Mournhaven man and a Full Spark","look":"Tall, stooped at the shoulders from the cart bench, keeps his Council badge in a pocket instead of on his coat.","voice":"Quiet and apologetic; gentle with horses, flat with people.","greet":"Lamp's lit. I'm not taking anything of yours. Just passing through. I always am.","lines":["Council's sending two carts from next season. More lamps. Same road. Nobody here's going to like that.","Mournhaven says the Drift had the dead first. It did. The Archive has room. That's all I know. I drive.","My mam's lamp went up the road on my cart. I drove it myself. Wouldn't let anyone else do it."],"about":"I was born here and I take the town's dead away every morning. You can imagine how that goes at parties. I don't go to parties.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Old Spark-speech","level":"the carters' road-oath and a few words more"}],"childhood":"He grew up over a cooperage on Dyers' Row, the son of a cooper and a mourning-cloth dyer, with river-silver stains on his hands from the dye vats every summer. Even at six he was the boy who sat with strangers on the jetty: he'd wander out at dusk, find a family with a lantern, sit down at the end of their row and listen until he fell asleep, and someone would carry him home and his mother would thank them without surprise. He liked the old people best, because they told the longest stories and corrected each other the most. He was proud of Mournhaven in the way only children are, and believed the Drift was the centre of the Nine Realms.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight: the Veil lamp lit bright and steady. Full Spark. The town made a fuss of it. Lamp Years in the Vale, then the Veil Tower at Korrhaven, four knots, under Tutor Damaris Fell, who made every student tell her one memory they were willing to lose before each weave, so they would never spend one by accident. He learned ghostlight. The first memory he gave up was the taste of his mother's blackberry cake.","history":"He came home with his spark and could have been a Lantern Knight or an archivist. He chose the Council's carriage-house in the Vale because he liked horses and liked quiet. At sixty the Council gave him the Mournhaven dawn route, on the theory that a local man would soften the old quarrel. It made him the most awkward person in town for ninety years. Every dawn he loads the lanterns that sat their last night on the jetty and drives them up the Lanthys road, and the families watch from their doors. In thick fog he weaves a thread of ghostlight to see the road, and spends a small memory each time; he keeps a list in the back of his route-book of what he's spent. Thirty years ago he drove his own mother's lamp. That night Bettany Coil gave him three pears. Twice this year, at the Archive gate, he has found a lantern on his cart with no tag that nobody loaded, and by the time the clerks came out it was gone.","family":"Mother's lamp in the Archive; father's lamp in the Archive; no siblings; a horse called Settle whom he loves more than he admits.","hero":{"who":"Vaelora Lanthys","why":"She chose silence, in a Realm that talks about everything all night. Emrys drives a cart through a town that talks about him all day, and he respects her more than he can say."},"band":{"act":"The Lamplit","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"The same idols for three hundred years. He likes a thing that's there every morning.","rv_vote":"The Lamplit."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp against Mournhaven's motion to keep its own lamps, on the Council's side, which the town will not forgive.","edict":"Warn, and stop there."},"wants":"To drive the cart one morning with nothing on it.","site":"mournhaven","town":"Mournhaven","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:lanternreach":{"name":"Odile Fenwarren","age":179,"role":"keeper of the stair-foot store and of the walker-card cabinet; has never walked a Path","look":"Looks thirty, round spectacles, a leather pencil-case on her belt with nine labelled pencils, one for each Path.","voice":"Warm and organised; speaks in lists and asks one question too many.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Going up or come down? Either way, sit. Going up, I'll want your name and three things you love. Come down, I'll tell you them.","lines":["Frostgrave rang plain all week again. I sent them a nice letter. It said 'noise in a fog' is a rude thing to call a dead person's name.","Lightforge's Edict wants to audit memories. We've audited memories here for centuries. It's called Telling Back and you get a cake.","I've never been up a Path. Somebody has to stay at the bottom with the drawers. That's my excuse, and I'm keeping it."],"about":"I run the store at the stair-foot and I keep a card on every walker who's gone up in my lifetime. Forty thousand of them. If you lose yourself up there, I've probably got you in a drawer.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"She was a granddaughter of the stair-foot family that first fed walkers coming down, and grew up in a house where supper ended with a game: everyone told the person beside them three true things about themselves. Odile was a sorter. She kept her mother's buttons in rows by who had sewn them on, and could tell you which button came off which coat in which year. At ten a walker came down the Sixth Path weeping and the grown-ups were all busy, so she sat beside him on the stone and read him what he'd said going up: that he loved his dog, Pip, and his brother's bad singing, and pickled pears. He stopped crying and laughed at 'bad singing'. She went to bed that night feeling about nine feet tall.","schooling":"The note-school in the inn kitchen, taught by her grandfather, Grandsire Tobyn Fenwarren, whose one rule was handwriting: 'If they can't read it coming down, you've robbed them twice.' Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. She learned Rime from the Frostgrave mule-drivers on the far side of the bell-line and filing from nobody; she invented most of it.","history":"She took over the notes at sixty and threw out the old ledgers in favour of cards: nine drawers, one per Path, every walker filed by date and name with their three loves and whatever else they let slip. The Council has asked twice to copy the cabinet for the Archive; she has said no twice, politely, on the grounds that walkers told those things to the Reach. She runs the stair-foot store too: packs, rope, walking-staves, stair cakes, bows for the Pale road. She has been engaged twice. Both times her intended asked her to walk a Path with him, for the closeness of it, and both times she found a reason to break it off within a month. She has a long, prickly correspondence with the Frostgrave inn across the Pale about the bells, which she enjoys a great deal more than she lets on, and she has started teaching the note-school herself now that Tobyn's lamp is in the Archive.","family":"A large stair-foot family of guides and bakers; Wenlow Ashgrove, her best pupil, whom she treats exactly like a niece and never praises.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"The Seer who knotted a vision, so it would still be there after the Seer couldn't hold it. Odile has spent her life doing that for other people with cards and a pencil."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Throat-singing with the famous forty-second silence. She once filed a whole drawer during it.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime, partly so Frostgrave stops sulking about the bells."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for the Council's warning.","edict":"Warn. If Lightforge got its way it would want her drawers, and it isn't having them."},"wants":"To go up one Path, once, and come down to a card in her own hand.","site":"lanternreach","town":"Lantern Reach","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:lanternreach":{"name":"Garreth Lune","age":52,"role":"village smith and bell-founder for the Spiritveil half of the Pale bell-line","look":"Looks twenty-seven, wool stuffed in both ears, frost-burn on his cheekbones, one streak of silver in his hair from a night in the Pale.","voice":"Talks slightly too loud from deafness; kind; laughs at his own mishearings.","greet":"LAMP'S, sorry. Lamp's lit. It's the bells. You get used to them. Or deaf. I went with deaf.","lines":["Frostgrave's bell-warden sent the winter complaint early this year. Different handwriting. Rounder.","The pattern for the lost names, that's ours. They ring plain because plain's honest. Both right. Don't tell either side I said so.","This streak? Not grief. Cold. I walked into the Pale at twelve and came out with it. My mum hasn't forgiven the fog."],"about":"I cast and mend the bells on our half of the line. When the mist's thick I go out and ring them myself. There's always somebody out in it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"good, mostly written"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"His mother was the Reach's bell-ringer and the family lived in the bell-house at the very edge of the village, where the lantern-posts walk off into the Pale. He fell asleep most nights to the pattern for the lost names, three short, one long, a pause, which his mother rang for every name read that week in the Vale. At twelve he followed the line out into the mist on a dare, lost the posts, and sat in white nothing for a whole night listening for her bell. She rang until dawn without stopping. He walked out along the sound, half-frozen, with one lock of hair gone silver. She hugged him so hard he thought she'd crack a rib, then didn't speak to him for a week.","schooling":"The note-school in the inn kitchen under Grandsire Tobyn Fenwarren, who despaired of his handwriting: 'A walker would come down and think you'd written in a hailstorm.' Lamp Test at eight: the Veil lamp flickered. Half-Spark; no Conclave. At sixteen he went to the Vale to a bell-founder named Aldric Tey and learned to cast.","history":"He spent ten years at Aldric's foundry in Lanthys Vale, casting bells for the Archive and the river towns, and came home at thirty with a cart of moulds and his hearing already going. He took over the village forge and the bell-line: twelve bells on posts out to the mid-point of the Pale, where the Frostgrave line takes over. He mends clappers, recasts cracked bells, and on bad nights walks out along the posts ringing by hand. Every winter Lantern Reach and the Frostgrave inn on the far side send each other the same complaint about the ringing, and it was Garreth who first wrote on the back of one, in his clumsy Rime, a riddle. The old Frostgrave warden wrote an answer and a riddle back. That went on for fifteen years. Two winters ago the handwriting on the Frostgrave side changed, rounder and quicker, and the riddles got better. The old warden's daughter has the bells now. They have never met. The Pale lies between them and neither has crossed it.","family":"His mother, retired, still wakes at every bell; a younger brother who guides on the Second Path. No wife, he says, 'unless you count a fog'.","hero":{"who":"Olin Stair","why":"He carried his sister down the Glass Stair. Garreth has walked into the Pale for strangers on bad nights and thinks of Olin every time his boots go through the crust."},"band":{"act":"Permafrost","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Black metal where the fans stand perfectly still. It's the only gig where nobody jostles a deaf man.","rv_vote":"Permafrost."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for the Council's warning.","edict":"Torn, leaning towards refuse. He'd say more if he were sure he'd heard the question right."},"wants":"To walk across the Pale on a clear day and ring her bell from her side.","site":"lanternreach","town":"Lantern Reach","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:lanternreach":{"name":"Merric Hale","age":44,"role":"handyman at Lantern Reach; a Mournhaven tanner who walked the Fourth Path and came down without his reason for going","look":"Looks twenty-six, a notebook strapped to his forearm with a leather cuff, a stair cake in his coat pocket two years old and hard as slate.","voice":"Courteous and uncertain; checks the notebook before he says anything about himself.","greet":"Lamp's lit. I'm Merric. It's in here, look. And I'm fairly sure I'm pleased to meet you.","lines":["A walker came down off the Seventh yesterday and asked for her mother. Her mother was sitting right next to her. They're working it out.","Lightforge wants to audit memories. They should come and stand at the bottom of this stair for a day. They'd stop wanting to.","I went up the Fourth two years ago. Came down with my name, my trade, my mother's face. Not why I went. Nobody wrote it down. I made sure of that, apparently."],"about":"I was a tanner in Mournhaven. Probably still am, by trade. I went up a Path for a reason I've lost, and I've decided to stay here until somebody tells me what it was.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"What he has of it comes partly from memory and partly from his mother's telling, and he writes both down in different inks. He grew up by the tan-yards at the Mournhaven end of Dyers' Row, the second son of a tanner, in a house that smelled of bark and lime. He remembers mud, his older brother's honking laugh, a dog called Ferret who stole gloves. He collected heron feathers and kept them in a boot. On Nights on the Water he used to crawl under the Long Jetty's boards and lie on the cross-beams in the dark listening to strangers tell stories about their dead overhead, and he still knows several of those strangers' grandfathers better than his own.","schooling":"Mournhaven's wake-school under Teacher Efa Brack, who taught counting with pears; Merric remembers the pears and almost nothing else. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. He left at twelve to work the tan-pits with his father, and could read well enough for orders and badly enough for anything else. Efa once kept him behind to tell him he listened better than anyone in the room; that, oddly, he remembers word for word.","history":"He tanned hides for thirty years, unremarkably, by his own account and his family's. Two years ago, at forty-two, he arrived at Lantern Reach alone, dodged the note-sitters by saying he'd been interviewed already, and went up the Fourth Path. He came down three days later and sat on the Fourth Toll Stone with his hands between his knees. Wenlow Ashgrove, who was fifteen, sat beside him and told him the only three things anyone had heard him say on the way up: that the bread was good, that it was 'for her', and that he'd be quick. He did not know who 'her' was. His family came from Mournhaven to fetch him and said that he had been quieter than usual that year, and nothing more useful. He stayed. He mends the Toll Stones, re-hangs gates on the bell-line, splits wood for the inn, and keeps a list in the forearm notebook of women he might have meant, crossed out one by one as each turns out not to be.","family":"Mother, father and brother in Mournhaven, who visit at Long Evenings and try not to look worried; no wife or children, as far as anyone has told him.","hero":{"who":"The Lost Warden","why":"Nobody knows who the Lost Warden was, and it's still a hero. Merric finds that hopeful in a way he can't quite explain."},"band":{"act":"Echo Bleed","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A DJ sampling dead singers, and 'Somebody Else's Summer' is how Merric thinks of the year before the stair.","rv_vote":"Echo Bleed, in the Sparkbeat Cup and out of it."},"vote":{"local":"Lit no lamp this season. He doesn't remember what he used to believe and doesn't want to guess.","edict":"Torn."},"wants":"To learn who 'her' is.","site":"lanternreach","town":"Lantern Reach","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:lanternreach:b":{"name":"Wenlow Ashgrove","age":16,"role":"apprentice note-writer who sits with walkers before they climb; the best hand in the Reach","look":"Ink to the wrist, hair in two plaits pinned up with pencils, cheeks pink from the Pale wind.","voice":"Quick and opinionated, then suddenly shy when you're sad.","greet":"Lamp's lit! Going up? Sit. I'll need three things. Real ones. Not 'my mum'. Everyone says 'my mum'.","lines":["Merric said 'it's for her' on the way up and I wrote it down. Worst note I've ever taken. I think about it every day.","Seraphine Skyrend shouted the Edict down in Skyrend. Here we write letters. I'd shout. I'm practising.","I've never been up. Mum won't let me till I'm twenty-five and stop growing. She says the stair takes more off you if there's less of you to start with."],"about":"I'm Wenlow. I write down what walkers love before they go up, so we can tell them after. I've got the best hand in the Reach. Odile says so, and she's never said anything nice about anything.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good; she practises on every traveller"}],"childhood":"Her father guides on the Second Path and her mother bakes the stair cakes, and from the age of four Wenlow pressed the thumbprints into them, a tray a morning. Once she pressed her own initial into one instead, a little W, to see what would happen. A walker found it in his pocket coming down, couldn't remember who W was, and loved it anyway, and told everyone in the inn. She grew up hearing grown people cry on the Toll Stones every day of her life, and it never frightened her. It made her nosy. She wanted to know what they'd lost, and what they'd kept, and which things they'd fight the notes about.","schooling":"The note-school in the inn kitchen under Odile Fenwarren, who made her rewrite one card forty times until it could be read by a lantern in the rain. Lamp Test at eight: the Veil lamp warmed under her hand. Half-Spark. She'll decide at eighteen whether to train, and has decided already, twice, both ways.","history":"She started sitting with walkers at thirteen and did her first Telling Back alone at fourteen, to a huge Stonehold man down from the Ninth, who cried and then picked her up off the ground like a sack of meal. At fifteen she sat with Merric Hale and wrote down the only three things he said. This year Odile has sent her to Lanthys Vale twice to learn copying hand from the Archive's readers, and there, on the Gallery steps, she met a girl called Maelis Corrow who writes down every name read at dusk; the two of them now compare lists by letter every week. She also writes, by the bridge post, to a girl in a Stormreach chain-yard who sends her shouted poems in Gale that Wenlow can only half read. She wants to be the reader who stands on the Gallery steps.","family":"Father a guide, mother a baker, two small brothers who steal the cakes she's thumb-printed. Odile, whom she would never call family to her face.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend","why":"A woman of thirty-one who stood up in front of a whole Assembly and shouted no. Wenlow is sixteen and has been told to lower her voice her whole life."},"band":{"act":"The Knot-Cord Brothers","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"'Forty Seconds' is proof that Frostgrave can hurry when it wants to, and she likes proof.","rv_vote":"The Knot-Cord Brothers, and the Sparkbeat Cup too."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for a lamp; she put a candle on her sill for Councillor Wane's refusal so the street could see it.","edict":"Refuse."},"wants":"To be the reader of the Evening Names on the Gallery steps.","site":"lanternreach","town":"Lantern Reach","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:lanterncity_vf2":{"name":"Rhosyn Abernay","age":120,"role":"village trader; sells turned memorywood bowls, packs and kit; a former Archive lamp-maker who came to argue and stayed","look":"Looks twenty-seven, fine sawdust in her eyebrows, a lamp-makers' guild ring worn on a string round her neck instead of her finger.","voice":"Crisp and wry; a Vale accent she's working to lose and can't.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Bowls, kit, packs, the odd bow. Everything here's grove wood except the arrows. Don't ask the arrows where they're from.","lines":["The lamp-makers sent another letter asking for spring pruning. Tavi read it out to the Speaking Trunk. The tree didn't answer. It doesn't do letters.","The city's right that the Archive's filling faster than these trees grow. I worked it out. I was right. I left anyway.","I used to cut these branches for lamp-stands. Now I turn them into bowls and sell them to visitors. My old guild calls it treason. It's bowls."],"about":"I made lamps in the Vale for seventy years. I came up here to argue the foresters into spring pruning. They put my hand on the big tree and I stayed. Now I run the store and lose arguments with my old friends by post.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; she sold lamp-stands over the border for decades"}],"childhood":"She grew up in the lamp-makers' quarter of the Vale, where every yard is ankle-deep in shavings, her father turning lamp-stands at a treadle lathe and her mother etching names into lamp-glass. She was fiercely competitive and won the children's turning race at the Long Evening two decades running. At nine her father put a grey memorywood branch on her lathe for the first time and, as the chisel bit, she heard a man somewhere very far off counting, quite cheerfully, 'eleven, twelve, thirteen'. She assumed all wood talked and was furious, the next week, to find that pine didn't. She went back to the memorywood offcuts in her father's bin and listened to them by the hour.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. Guild-school of the lamp-makers under Master Cadfael Hearne, who checked every apprentice's work with brass calipers and praised nothing for six years, then told her on her last day that her stands were 'nearly adequate', and she has never been prouder. She learned turning, guild accounts and how to read a timber ledger. She failed etching.","history":"She was a guild member for seventy years and a master for forty. Being good at sums, she worked out what nobody had wanted to: so many new lamps go up the valley each year, so many stands the grove can give in an autumn prune, and the gap grows every decade. Thirty years ago the guild sent her up to the memorywood village to argue for spring pruning. Tavi Lorne listened politely, then walked her to the Speaking Trunk, put her palm low on the bark, and she heard a man counting, cheerfully, 'eleven, twelve, thirteen': her father, who had pruned there as a young man before she was born. She stayed three days, then three weeks, then for good. She runs the store, turns bowls in the back, and sends her old guild careful letters with her latest sums, which they read and answer and ignore. She is the only person on either side of the quarrel whom both sides trust and neither listens to. She and Daveth Orme play cards on Restdays and cheat each other openly.","family":"Father's lamp in the Archive, standing, she checked, on a stand she turned herself. Mother still etching in the Vale and writing to ask when she's coming home.","hero":{"who":"Solvane","why":"A polite young engineer read out a petition to a room that laughed. Rhosyn has read sums to rooms like that and has some idea what that face was doing."},"band":{"act":"The Fitted","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"A half-synthetic voice singing 'Unplated'. She likes a made thing that sounds like it's alive.","rv_vote":"The Fitted, in the Sparkbeat Cup."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for Councillor Lanmore's motion to carry the oldest shelves to Willowmere; it would ease the shortage of stands for years.","edict":"Torn. She likes counting things and dislikes counting people, and she knows the difference is thinner than she'd like."},"wants":"For the lamp-makers' guild and the foresters to read her sums together, in one room, out loud.","site":"lanterncity_vf2","town":"Lanthys Vale Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:lanterncity_vf2":{"name":"Daveth Orme","age":214,"role":"village smith; makes the pruning saws and hooks; the forester's boy whose carved name the Speaking Trunk still says","look":"Looks twenty-five, sheepish, burn-scarred knuckles, takes his hand off any tree the moment he notices it's there.","voice":"Self-mocking and rushed; winces at the sound of his own name.","greet":"Lamp's lit. If you've been at the big tree, yes, that was me. No, I don't want to talk about it. Saws are over here.","lines":["A Lightforge visitor put her hand on my name and asked the tree if I was single. It said my name. That's all it says. She took that as a yes.","The lamp-makers want more wood. They can have the bit of bark with my voice in it. Cut that out. Please.","Two hundred years I've heard myself at fourteen saying my own name like I invented having one. Every day. Every single visitor."],"about":"I'm Daveth. You've probably heard me already. I was a forester's boy who carved his name in the Speaking Trunk, and the tree's been saying it ever since. I make saws now. Funny how that turned out.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"He was a show-off, the forester's youngest, always first up a trunk and loudest at supper. At fourteen his father gave him his own folding knife, a good one with a horn handle, and Daveth went straight up the grove and carved D-A-V-E-T-H at shoulder height into the oldest memorywood tree there is, saying each letter aloud as he cut it, very pleased with himself. The next morning the first forester to put a hand to the Speaking Trunk heard a boy's voice say 'Daveth' with enormous satisfaction. The village was appalled. His grandmother, Eiry Orme, walked him back up that afternoon and made him stand under the branches and say something kind out loud. Then the next day. Then every day for a year.","schooling":"The grove school under Old Teilo Penn, who taught letters by having the children trace them in bark-dust on a slate and never, ever on a tree. 'Which I'd have known,' Daveth says, 'if I'd been listening.' Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. Fine at sums and woodcraft, hopeless at sitting still.","history":"After the carving the foresters kept him off the pruning crews, in case the trees learned anything worse from him, and at sixteen he was apprenticed to the village smith, Gwilym Dace, a gentle man who said only that iron doesn't remember. Daveth took the forge at seventy and has made every pruning saw, hook and bark-knife in the village for a hundred and forty years. He married Nia Cadwal, who teases him without mercy, and they have children and grandchildren who go up the grove on purpose to touch his name and shout 'Grandad!' at the tree. For two hundred years he has kept going back, most mornings, to say something kind under the Speaking Trunk's lower branches, long after his grandmother stopped making him. Visitors who put their hands just below his name now hear, faint and layered, a young man telling the tree it looked well, that the frost had been hard, that he was sorry. They still hear the name first.","family":"Wife Nia; three children, nine grandchildren, all of whom think the tree is the funniest thing in the world. Grandmother Eiry's lamp in the Archive.","hero":{"who":"His grandmother, Eiry Orme","why":"She made him go back every day and give the tree something better, and never once let him off. He has never met a Realm hero who'd have bothered."},"band":{"act":"Long Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Garage rock loud enough to drown out his own fourteen-year-old voice in his head.","rv_vote":"Long Whistle. Metalcrown needs a lamp; Lightforge won't give them one; he will."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp, sixty years ago, for the foresters' petition on autumn-only pruning, which the Council passed and nobody has enforced since. Lit it again this year to remind them.","edict":"Refuse. Imagine Lightforge auditing the memory of the boy who carved his name in a tree."},"wants":"For the tree to say one of the kind things to visitors first, before his name.","site":"lanterncity_vf2","town":"Lanthys Vale Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:lanterncity_vf2":{"name":"Ondine Pryce","age":27,"role":"grove guide for visitors; keeps a ledger of every voice in the memorywood and is hunting the laughing girl","look":"Just stopped ageing and pleased about it; muddy boots, a battered ledger of voice-notes, a sprig of memorywood in her buttonhole that she shouldn't have.","voice":"Excited and technical; says 'actually' a great deal.","greet":"Lamp's lit! Have you heard the big tree yet? Low down, left side. Actually, let me show you. People always touch it too high.","lines":["An Archive copyist came up last month to hear the laughing girl. Said the laugh's in the old court style. Which means she's at least seven hundred years old. Actually, older.","The lamp-makers say the trees grow too slowly. They haven't measured. I have. They grow exactly as fast as trees grow.","I've touched the big one nine thousand times. I've got the laugh down to the half-breath. I'm going to find her."],"about":"I guide visitors through the grove, and in between I'm working out who the laughing girl in the Speaking Trunk was. Everyone says nobody knows. I think nobody's asked properly.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native, reads old court Lanthic"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting, from Lightforge visitors"}],"childhood":"Her father keeps the hives at the edge of the grove, and Ondine was stung so often as a small girl that she stopped noticing. She was a list-maker before she could write: she kept pebbles in rows for the trees she'd touched, one pebble each. At seven her father lifted her to the Speaking Trunk, low down, and she heard the laugh for the first time, a girl's, high and helpless, as if someone had just fallen in a pond. Ondine laughed straight back at it, the first time in her life she had laughed without knowing what at, and then made her father bring her back every day for a month. She has the month's pebbles still, in a honey jar on her windowsill.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. Three years at the copying-room school in the Vale, because her mother wanted her to be a copyist, under Copyist Bethan Sorle, who let her copy old court Lanthic from the first-century lamp-tags and told her she had the worst patience and the best ear in the room. She left at fifteen to guide in the grove, which her mother has accepted with a great deal of sighing.","history":"She has guided visitors through the grove since sixteen: copyists, Lantern Knights on leave, Lightforge couples who think it's romantic, Stonehold walkers who put their palms flat and go very quiet. Ten years ago she began the voice-ledger: every tree, every height, every voice she can find, with a note of words, accent and mood. Four hundred and twelve voices so far. She sends a copy to the Archive each spring. This year she caught, after the laughing girl's laugh, something nobody had written down before: half a word, swallowed, two syllables ending in a soft th. A name, she thinks, or the start of one. She fell out with Daveth Orme last winter for asking to record his voice for comparison, and has made it up since with a jar of her father's honey. Tavi Lorne, who pretends not to approve of her, leaves the grove gate unlatched for her before dawn.","family":"Father a beekeeper; mother in the Vale, still hoping for a copyist; a younger brother who is allergic to bees, which the family regards as a tragedy.","hero":{"who":"The laughing girl in the Speaking Trunk","why":"Nobody knows her name. She has made thousands of strangers laugh for longer than the Concord has stood, and Ondine would like to thank her by name."},"band":{"act":"Prism","realm":"Lightforge","why":"'Seven Colours'. She maps the voices in her ledger by colour and has done it to Prism for ten years.","rv_vote":"Prism, in the Sparkbeat Cup."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for the foresters' autumn-only pruning, again.","edict":"Against. Lightforge would want the grove signed and named, every voice, and some of them don't want to be."},"wants":"To find the laughing girl's lamp on a shelf in the Archive.","site":"lanterncity_vf2","town":"Lanthys Vale Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:lanterncity_vf2:b":{"name":"Hewel Marrin","age":388,"role":"retired forester; sits each morning under one young branch on the grove's eastern edge, saying kind things","look":"Looks thirty-five, hair silver as birch bark, carries a folding stool everywhere and sets it down with great care.","voice":"Very few words, chosen slowly; long silences that aren't unfriendly.","greet":"Lamp's lit. ... Good boots. Somebody looked after those.","lines":["Tavi talks to the big tree every morning. I talk to a small one. Mine's less famous. It knows worse.","Lightforge says say it in the light. Fine. Some things are better not said anywhere.","Three hundred and forty years I've been sitting under the same branch. I said one thing there once. I'm still paying it off."],"about":"I pruned this grove for two hundred years. Now I sit. ... I'm working on something. It's slow.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"'Set', 'carry them', and a few others from a Stonehold forester he worked beside"}],"childhood":"He was the chief forester's son and a clever, sharp-tongued boy, the kind who found the soft spot in other children within a minute and pressed on it to see what happened. His father was strict and silent, and Hewel learned the grove from him tree by tree, by name, until he could walk it blindfold. At nine he climbed the tallest memorywood in the grove, far higher than allowed, and saw the Lit Mountain come on in the dusk across the valley, row by row, and he was so happy up there that he sang at the top of his voice. Nobody below could hear him. He has never told anyone about that either.","schooling":"The grove school under Forester-mistress Rhian Coed, who made every child plant one sapling and keep it alive for a year; Hewel's lived, and stands on the eastern edge. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. He was quick at everything and kind about nothing, and Rhian told his father so, in writing.","history":"He went on the crews at fifteen and became the best pruner in the grove; he could tell from the grey of a bough whether a tree had let it go. At forty-eight, under the young branch of his own sapling on the eastern edge, he said something cruel to his younger brother Iolo. He has never told anyone what. Iolo left the village that week, went east across the bridges and never came back; word came, decades later, that he was living in Stormreach. The branch kept it, of course. For three hundred and forty years Hewel has gone to that branch most mornings, set down his stool and said something kind there, to give it other things to hold: the weather, the bees, the names of good dogs, the small news of the village. Thousands of things. The branch says them now in his voice, a quiet murmur under the hand. The one thing is still underneath. He retired from pruning at two hundred and fifty, and never married.","family":"His brother Iolo, alive somewhere in Stormreach, whose lamp still stands on its front shelf in the Archive. Hewel checks it every Long Evening.","hero":{"who":"The Silent Sentinel","why":"Someone who stopped speaking and kept their seat, and says what must be said by carving it into slate. Hewel has thought about that for most of his life."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Forty veterans who never sing above a murmur. It is the only music that doesn't make him flinch.","rv_vote":"The Lonely Line Choir."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for the foresters' autumn-only pruning.","edict":"Warn."},"wants":"To hear from his brother before one of them goes up the valley.","site":"lanterncity_vf2","town":"Lanthys Vale Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:wickhaven_vf0":{"name":"Mabyn Treloar","age":91,"role":"village trader in honey, wax cakes, mead and kit; the bee-telling child of eighty years ago","look":"Looks twenty-five, small and freckled, a straw skep-hat pushed back on her head, a list of names pinned inside her coat.","voice":"Cheerful and quick, but checks every list twice, then a third time.","greet":"Lamp's lit! Honey, wax cakes, mead, arrows? Wax cakes keep a season. The knights swear by them. The knights swear by most things.","lines":["Candlewick's tallow is in the road inns again. Smells like a sheep died in your lamp. Ysra says the bees can smell it from up the hill.","Lightforge wants to register every spark. We tell the bees every death. One of those is the polite way to keep count.","I was the telling child for six years. Knock three times, say the name, say how. I'm very good at lists now."],"about":"I sell honey and kit for the village and buy whatever the road drops. I was a telling child once. I take lists very seriously. Ask anyone. No, don't ask anyone.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Her mother kept forty skeps on the lower terraces and Mabyn was chosen as the village's telling child at five, because she had the loudest clear voice in the hive-school and didn't cry at names. For six years she went hive to hive after every death with a knocking-stick: three knocks, the name, how they went. She was proud of it in the fierce private way of small children with a job. At eleven came the spring of the sickness. An old wick-seller who lived alone over the far side of the hill died that week, with no family and no one to put him on the list. Mabyn told the bees every other name. She didn't know about his. By midsummer half the village's hives were dead in their straw.","schooling":"The hive-school in the wax-shed under Keeper Annis Brae, who taught letters by having the children paint names on old skeps, then scrub them off and paint them again. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. Mabyn loved letters and loathed arithmetic until she started trading and discovered that sums are only lists that argue.","history":"The old keepers blamed the village for carelessness in general, and nobody ever blamed a child. Mabyn gave up the knocking-stick that autumn and went quiet for nearly twenty years. She helped in the honey trade, then ran the wax-press, then at forty took over the store, which she runs with a ruthlessness the village finds restful. She keeps the books in three colours and a list of every death she can learn of for a day's walk in any direction: travellers who die on the road, farmers over the Lightforge border, carters, a Lantern Knight's horse. Late at night she goes up the terraces alone with an old knocking-stick and tells the hives those names too. Nobody has asked her to. She married Knight Bevin Treloar of the Wickhaven garrison at fifty; he is away on patrol half the year and brings her back road-gossip for the list.","family":"Husband Bevin, a Lantern Knight; two sons, one a beekeeper, one a hopeless poet; her mother's lamp in the Archive.","hero":{"who":"Saelen of the Drift","why":"The Lantern of the Dead kept the dead before there were shelves. Mabyn keeps the names nobody else is keeping, a few at a time, at night."},"band":{"act":"The Willow Choir","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"'Nothing Is Lost', sung with a four-hundred-year-dead singer's echo. She plays it while she does the accounts and finds it makes her honest.","rv_vote":"The Willow Choir."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for the Council's warning.","edict":"Warn. She dislikes any list kept for the wrong reasons."},"wants":"To find the name of the wick-seller she missed, and paint a skep for him.","site":"wickhaven_vf0","town":"Wickhaven Farms 1","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:wickhaven_vf0":{"name":"Lowen Kitto","age":60,"role":"hive-iron smith: skep stands, smoker pots, bee-knives and wax-pans; the village's quiet arbiter. Neither man nor woman; Lowen to everyone.","look":"Looks twenty-six, close-cropped, cracked knuckles, a beeswax-cloth apron, sets every tool down with exaggerated care.","voice":"Level and very calm; anger shows only in how slowly they put a thing down.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Smoker pots on the left, stands on the right. If something's broken, I'll look at it. If someone's broken, Ysra's up the hill.","lines":["A Candlewick carter knocked over three skeps on the road last week. Said sorry. Ysra says the bees heard it. I say he should've heard them.","Flameborne's rioting over the Edict. I understand a riot. I just don't think you should hold one outside your own door.","I keep my temper on a shelf, like a jar of honey. It doesn't go off. It keeps."],"about":"I make the ironwork for the hives and mend everyone's pans. I'm calm. People find it restful, or alarming. Either is fine.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good; five years in a Flameborne forge-town"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Lowen was a furious child, the kind who threw spoons and bit cousins. Their father kept bees and their mother cooked at a road inn under Wickhaven, and both had given up. At seven, in a tantrum over nothing, Lowen kicked over a skep and was stung forty times. They lay in a dark room for two days, swollen and shaking. An old keeper from the top terrace came and sat in the dark and said, 'Now you're quiet, I'll talk,' and talked about bees for most of a night: how a hive cools itself, what the guards do, how a swarm decides. Lowen didn't throw anything for a month. Then a year. They still think of that voice when the shelf feels heavy.","schooling":"The hive-school under Keeper Annis Brae. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. At sixteen Lowen went down the track to Flameborne and apprenticed in a forge-town on a lava-vein to Smith Orrel Ashkettle, who taught heat, temper and that arguing at dinner is how you love people. Lowen liked the forge and hated the arguing, and learned both anyway.","history":"Five years in Flameborne, in a town where doors stay open and everyone shouts. Lowen met some of the Banked there, quiet people with a bad name who set their anger aside instead of burning with it, and for the first time felt understood without having to explain. They took the Banked vow at nineteen, which in Flameborne you don't advertise. They came home at twenty-five, took the village forge at thirty, and have made every skep stand, smoker pot and wax-pan on the hill since. Somewhere along the way the village began bringing its quarrels to the forge instead of the meeting-house: two families over a swarm that crossed a wall, a widow and her son over a press. Lowen listens, works the bellows, and says one sentence at the end. It usually holds. They live alone with a very old dog and a great many jars.","family":"Parents both up the valley now, in lamps; a sister in Wickhaven who sends knight's pears every festival; no partner, by choice, they say, and mean it.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder","why":"The Banked Fire, tried in Lightforge for 'concealed anger'. Lowen has a shelf of concealed anger and has never hurt anyone with it, and that trial frightens them more than any battle story."},"band":{"act":"Ash-Tongue","realm":"Flameborne","why":"The singer loses all self-control on stage every show. Lowen goes once a year, stands at the back with arms folded and watches someone else lose it for them.","rv_vote":"Ash-Tongue."},"vote":{"local":"Lit their lamp for the Council's warning, and then put it out and lit it again for Councillor Wane's refusal, and has told no one which one they meant.","edict":"Against. Lightforge once put someone on trial for being angry quietly, and Lowen knows where lists like that go."},"wants":"To go back to the forge-town and thank Orrel Ashkettle without the two of them ending up in an argument.","site":"wickhaven_vf0","town":"Wickhaven Farms 1","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:wickhaven_vf0":{"name":"Hedra Wynn","age":10,"role":"the village's current telling child, who knocks on the hives to tell the bees each death, birth and wedding","look":"Gap-toothed, a knocking-stick taller than she is with three notches worn into it, a skep-hat that falls over her eyes.","voice":"Solemn about the bees, giggly about everything else.","greet":"Lamp's lit. I'm the telling child. If anyone's died, tell me first. Then I tell the bees. That's the order.","lines":["Old Mostyn's repainting the names again. He did my great-grandad's and spelt it right this time.","Lightforge uses tallow. Tallow is sheep. Bees don't like sheep. That's all you need to know about Lightforge.","Knock, knock, knock, name, how. I practise on the woodshed. The woodshed doesn't mind."],"about":"I'm Hedra and I tell the bees. I've done eleven deaths, two weddings and a baby. The baby was my cousin. The bees liked that one best.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"'honey', 'how much', 'go away'"}],"childhood":"She was born on the middle terrace, the daughter of a candle-maker and a Lantern Knight who is on long patrol more than he's home, and is raised mostly by her grandmother, who keeps twelve skeps and a great many opinions. At five she followed the old telling child, a solemn boy called Owain, from hive to hive at every death, copying his knocks on her own fist. At six she was stung on the lip, cried so hard the whole terrace came out, and then went back to the hive and apologised to it out loud, because she'd been told bees die when they sting and she felt responsible. When Owain turned fourteen and gave up the stick, she asked for it before anyone else could.","schooling":"The hive-school, now taught by Keeper Senna Tregaron, who lets the children paint names on practice skeps and marks them on neatness. Hedra's letters lean; Senna says that's character. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered. She asked the Veil lamp very nicely and it still didn't. She is learning to paint names and is failing patience.","history":"Her first telling, at eight, was for Gramma Clow from the top terrace. Hedra got the name right, knocked perfectly, and forgot to say how she died. She was halfway home before she realised, ran all the way back up in the dark and did the whole row again. Since then: eleven deaths, two weddings, her baby cousin. Last month a Lightforge pedlar died on the road near the bottom of the hill and Mabyn Treloar told her to tell him too. Hedra wasn't sure a Lightforger counted; Mabyn said everyone counts, so she did it, and spelled his name the Lightforge way, slowly, off a card. She keeps a telling book with every name in pencil. She has been asking whether she should tell the bees about the Edict, and nobody will give her a straight answer.","family":"Grandmother Ysolt; mother, a candle-maker; father, Knight Collen Wynn, on patrol; several cousins and one new baby.","hero":{"who":"Ysra Hollin","why":"She can tell which hive is grieving by the sound and has never once been stung. Hedra has been stung four times and considers this a personal failing."},"band":{"act":"House of the Seven Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Seven idols doing fire with their hands and nobody gets burnt. She practises the moves with sticks in the woodshed.","rv_vote":"House of the Seven Ovens."},"vote":{"local":"She'd tell the bees and see what they thought.","edict":"Against. Mabyn's against, and Mabyn has lists."},"wants":"Never to be stung again, and to be allowed to tell the bees about the Edict.","site":"wickhaven_vf0","town":"Wickhaven Farms 1","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:wickhaven_vf0:b":{"name":"Mostyn Brae","age":245,"role":"repainter of the names on the two hundred Named Hives; dull and contented","look":"Looks thirty, white paint under every fingernail, a very neat collar, a brush behind one ear like a clerk's pen.","voice":"Mild and careful; says 'I suppose' a great deal; knows he's dull and says so first.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Mind the wet ones. I've just done Ebba. Again.","lines":["Some of these names I've painted forty times. You get to know a person's letters. Nobody else knows them that well. Not even their families. It's not much, I suppose.","Lightforge wants everything written down. I write things down. It's fine. I don't see what the fuss is. I suppose that is the fuss.","My mother taught every child in the village their letters on these skeps. I paint the names. I've never wanted anything else. It bothers people."],"about":"I repaint the names on the hives. All two hundred. When I finish the last one, the first needs doing again. I'm dull. I know I am. It's very restful.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"His mother was Annis Brae, who ran the hive-school, and Mostyn was the quiet one at the back who liked the brush far more than the bees. He painted letters on anything that would hold still: flat stones from the stream, fence-posts, the backs of his own hands. At eight he painted a whole row of the wrong names on real hives as practice, and his mother rapped his knuckles with a ruler and then, that evening, stood behind him and corrected the curve of his capital A. He was a happy child. Nothing in particular happened to him. He would like that to be written down somewhere, since people keep asking.","schooling":"His mother's hive-school in the wax-shed. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. He learned his letters better than anyone the village had seen, failed bees completely, stung so often his mother kept him out of the terraces, and was slow at sums until he discovered you could paint them. His mother marked his work harder than anyone's, and he thought that fair.","history":"At thirty he took over the repainting when the old painter's hand began to shake, and he has done it ever since, two hundred and fifteen years, moving down the terraces one skep at a time and starting again at the top. He knows the hand of every first painter: which keeper's widow crossed her sevens, which son never finished his loops. After the sickness he painted thirty new names in a single summer and has never quite liked that summer since. He never married, and lives with his sister Annet, who teases him for being forty years behind with every piece of news. Once, a hundred and fifty years ago, he went to Lanthys Vale for the Evening Names, found it far too crowded, and came home the next morning; he still calls it 'my journey'. He'd like a better white paint, one that doesn't yellow in twenty years. That's all, he says. Really.","family":"Sister Annet; his mother's lamp in the Archive, and her name on a skep on the top terrace that he paints last, every time.","hero":{"who":"Gatekeeper Ammon Sael","why":"Forty years on the gate-book and he can give you the first sentence of any page you name. Mostyn thinks that is the most impressive thing any living person does."},"band":{"act":"The Noon Chorale","realm":"Lightforge","why":"The most decorated act in Realm Vision, and everyone's sick of them. He likes that they're always the same. He supposes that's why everyone's sick of them.","rv_vote":"The Noon Chorale."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for whatever Annet lit hers for, as he always has.","edict":"Torn. He supposes against. Mabyn explained it to him twice."},"wants":"A white that doesn't yellow.","site":"wickhaven_vf0","town":"Wickhaven Farms 1","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:wickhaven_vf1":{"name":"Briallen Toms","age":47,"role":"runs the orchard store; sells pear-leather, pear wine and kit; sister of the castle quartermaster; the orchard's lone voice for the Edict","look":"Looks twenty-five, tidy to the point of severity, a tally-slate on her hip just like her brother's, a strip of pear-leather always in one pocket.","voice":"Practical and pointed; impatient with sentiment but not unkind about it.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Pear-leather's two tolls a strip, kit's on the back wall. And no, I won't tell you which tree it's from.","lines":["They've stopped inviting my brother to the Pear Moon. He says he doesn't mind. He minds.","Everyone hates the Edict. I've read it. It's tidy. Tidy isn't wicked. I'll be the only one in this orchard who says so out loud.","My family's tree is in the third row. It's a good tree. It isn't my grandmother. My grandmother is on a shelf in the Vale."],"about":"I run the orchard's store and I sell to the castle, which makes me half a traitor here. My brother's the quartermaster. We have dinner on Restdays and agree about nothing except that pears are fruit.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; she studied it on purpose, for trade"}],"childhood":"Her father was a clerk in the castle stores and her mother an orchard woman, and Briallen and her brother Gerent were the tidiest children anyone in the village had ever seen: shoes paired at the door, pears sorted by size in the bowl. At the Pear Moon when she was seven her great-grandmother ate the first pear off the family tree in front of everyone, choked a little on a bit of skin, laughed and went on eating, and everyone clapped. Briallen didn't clap. She asked, quite loudly, why they were clapping a woman eating a pear. Her mother sent her home. She went, and sorted the pantry, and felt she had been right.","schooling":"The castle gate-school, under Ammon Sael in his first years as gatekeeper, who made every child recite the first line of a gate-book page by heart and never told them why. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. She taught herself Clear from a Lightforge buyer's grammar book at fourteen because it seemed the most exact language there was.","history":"She clerked in the quartermaster's office for ten years beside her brother, then crossed back over to the orchard side at thirty to run the village store, which no one quite forgave either way. She buys from the families, sells to the castle and to Lanthys Vale's markets, and makes the orchard more money than it has ever made, partly by selling pear-leather to visitors at double the price with a label naming the tree it came from, which she privately thinks is nonsense. When a Lightforge buyer left a copy of the Radiant Edict on her counter last spring she read it twice, and found it fair, clear and reasonable, and said so at the meeting-house, and the room went silent in a way she is still rather proud of. She is courting a wake cook's son from Mournhaven, slowly, by letter, in Clear, to annoy him.","family":"Brother Gerent, the castle quartermaster; parents both alive and both cross with her; a great-grandmother's lamp in the Archive.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand","why":"Solbrand wrote a law and put the Solbrand name on it. Nobody in this orchard puts their name to anything but a pear tree, Briallen says, and she means it as a criticism."},"band":{"act":"Noonday","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A perfect pop star never photographed in shadow. Briallen admires anyone who does a job that thoroughly.","rv_vote":"Noonday. Lightforge wins too much because Lightforge is good. She'll say that at the Pear Moon too."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for the Council's warning, as the least bad choice offered. If there were a motion to accept the Edict, she'd light for it.","edict":"For, mostly. She thinks the Realms should know who has sparks, and that Spiritveil's horror of being counted is vanity."},"wants":"A deal between the orchard and the castle: baskets marked by row, not by tree.","site":"wickhaven_vf1","town":"Wickhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:wickhaven_vf1":{"name":"Peder Lowick","age":29,"role":"orchard smith; grafting knives, hoe blades and the stamped tin name-tags on every tree; son of a Shadowdeep tunnel-trader","look":"Looks twenty-five, dark-browed and quick-handed, wears a tin tag on a cord at his throat stamped with nothing at all.","voice":"Wry and quiet; answers a question with a question.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Need a grafting knife? A tag? I stamp them while you wait. You'd be surprised who waits.","lines":["A family from the Vale bought a named sapling to take to Stormreach. It'll be the only mist-pear on a floating rock. Good luck to it.","The Edict wants everyone registered. Some people would rather be a tree in a row with a tag. You can't audit a tree, can you?","I stamp every tag in this orchard. A thousand trees. I know the names better than the families do. One of them isn't dead."],"about":"I'm the orchard smith. Knives, tags, hoe blades. My mother's from here. My father wasn't. That's all I say about it before supper.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"His mother picked pears and his father was a Shadowdeep man who gave only a half-name, Vesk, and came up through the old tunnels under the Wickhaven road selling lamp-black and blind-fish to the inns. Vesk taught Peder hand-cant at the supper table, so the two of them could talk about his mother's temper while she was in the room. He never said his full name; you don't, he said, except to your Keeper. When Peder was twelve his father kissed him on the forehead, said 'Kept', and went back down the tunnel under the road. He has not come up since. Peder waited at the tunnel mouth every evening for a year with a pear in his pocket.","schooling":"The orchard school in the packing shed, where Orchard-mother Corwen Ash taught him the whole Grandmothers' Row by name before she taught him letters. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. He asked later whether the Shadowdeep half of him might have answered a different lamp; it hadn't, he checked the list. He learned to stamp tin at fourteen from the old smith.","history":"He was apprenticed to the orchard smith, Hywel Brock, at fourteen and took the forge at twenty-four when Hywel's lamp went up the road. He makes grafting knives fine enough to split a thread, and he stamps the tin tags for every tree in the orchard, re-stamping the old ones when the letters wear. At twenty he planted a mist-pear at the end of a short row near the stream, grafted from a tree his mother loves, and stamped its tag with the only name he has for the man he's thinking of: VESK. No family name. The village assumes it's for a dead Shadowdeep man and is kind about it. Every autumn since, on the first frost, a dried pear-stone has turned up on his anvil wrapped in a twist of black cloth, though the forge door was barred. He keeps them in a tin. There are nine.","family":"Mother Morvyth, a picker, who will not discuss Vesk; his father, alive somewhere below.","hero":{"who":"Veilrend","why":"Shadowdeep's First Blade has no known face. Neither, now, does Peder's father, and Peder decided at twelve that a hero didn't need one."},"band":{"act":"No Name","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Masked whisper-rap that comes second on purpose. His father used to whisper along to something like it at the supper table.","rv_vote":"No Name. They'll come second; he's helping."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for Councillor Wane's motion to refuse the Edict.","edict":"Against. His father's people have been hunted by lists before."},"wants":"To learn his father's full name, which only his father's Keeper knows.","site":"wickhaven_vf1","town":"Wickhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:wickhaven_vf1":{"name":"Morwenna Ellery","age":612,"role":"the orchard's eldest; daughter of one of Wickhaven's first Lantern Knights; eats the first pear off her mother's tree every Pear Moon","look":"Looks forty, the oldest face in the orchard; hair white as pear blossom; walks with a pear-wood stick grown from her own tree.","voice":"Unhurried and exact, very funny, talks about the dead as if they're in the next room.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Sit on my mother's root, she won't mind. She minded everything else.","lines":["They say the Council's going to move the oldest shelves. They've said that for eighty years. I've said they would for eighty years. One of us will be right.","Lightforge wrote a law to count sparks. I've outlived three laws that did less harm, and nobody remembers them. Laws lapse here when nobody remembers why. I intend to forget this one quickly.","Every Pear Moon I eat the first pear off my mother's tree. Six hundred years. It's never once been ripe. She'd like that."],"about":"I'm Morwenna Ellery. I was here when the first rows went in, which makes me old and also, I'm told, a landmark. I've buried two husbands, outlived a castle wall, and never been further than Mournhaven. I don't need to. Everyone comes here.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native, and the old court Lanthic"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent; 'from before they'd finished building it'"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a few words, all about wine"}],"childhood":"She was born in the waystation when Wickhaven was still mostly timber and the gate-book ran to twelve pages. Her father was Knight Corlan Yew, one of the first of the Lantern Knights to settle; her mother was a sharp, exacting woman who disapproved of the road, the weather and Morwenna's hair. Morwenna carried saplings up from the river nursery in a basket on her back for the first rows, and she remembers the hole for her grandmother's tree being dug in pouring rain, her mother kneeling in the mud refusing all help. She named a sapling after her rag doll and watched it grow until the doll was gone and the tree was enormous.","schooling":"'There was no school. There was my mother, a slate, and the gate-book.' At her Lamp Test the Veil lamp answered faintly. Half-Spark. Her father thought Korrhaven was a rumour put about by people who wanted your children, and nobody pressed it. She learned to read from the gate-book, to count from the rows, and to graft from her mother, who never once said she'd done it well.","history":"She married at eighty, a road-inn keeper with a loud laugh, whose lamp went up at three hundred after a fever. She married again, Iorwerth, a grafter, whose lamp went up two hundred years ago, and she still talks to him on the bench between the trees. Four children, a hundred and forty living descendants whose names she has nearly all of. On the night three hundred years ago when eleven knights held Wickhaven's gate, she was in her kitchen baking knight's pears in cinder-wine for the morning, and when she came down at dawn a full basket and two jars were gone. She has never asked anyone where. She saw the eleven lamps go into the Wick Tower. Forty years ago the frost killed the founding row, her mother's tree among them. Morwenna went home, took down a pot from her windowsill in which she had kept a cutting of that tree alive for five hundred years, out of plain stubbornness, and grafted it back herself, at five hundred and seventy-two, kneeling in the mud refusing all help.","family":"A hundred and forty living descendants; two husbands' lamps; her mother's tree, which is also her mother's tree's great-granddaughter.","hero":{"who":"Knight Corlan Yew, her father","why":"He planted the first pear at Wickhaven, for his own mother, with his sword still on. Nobody wrote it in the gate-book. She remembers it, so it's kept."},"band":{"act":"Mason Grey","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A big quarry baritone that doesn't hurry. She married two men who couldn't sing, and she listens to one who can.","rv_vote":"Mason Grey."},"vote":{"local":"Lights her lamp, on principle, for whatever the oldest councillor proposes, except when it's the High Seer, who'll only say no.","edict":"Warn. Laws lapse in Spiritveil. This one will too, if everybody forgets it hard enough."},"wants":"To watch a great-great-great-grandchild eat the first pear off her mother's tree before she does, just once.","site":"wickhaven_vf1","town":"Wickhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:wickhaven_vf1:b":{"name":"Jory Penrose","age":20,"role":"orchard hand who means to leave for Stormreach with a named sapling on his back","look":"Not finished growing, all elbows, a Stormreach rider's scarf he bought off a carter and wears even in the heat.","voice":"Restless and sharp; mutters Corran Vale lyrics under his breath.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Are you going somewhere? Anywhere? Can I hold your horse while you tell me about it?","lines":["Someone bought a named sapling to take to Stormreach. I asked if they needed someone to carry it. They said no. Twice.","Kaelis Emberwarden's the loudest mouth in Flameborne and they still send Kaelis to the Council. Here, you swear at a pear tree and your aunt in Mournhaven hears about it by supper.","I'm buying my own tree. My grandad's, grafted. I'll carry it to Skyrend on my back if I have to."],"about":"I'm Jory. I'm twenty. I've lived in an orchard my whole life and everyone here's going to live another six hundred years in it. I'm leaving. I keep saying that. I'm actually leaving.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few words, all from Corran Vale lyrics, some of them wrong"}],"childhood":"He was the youngest of the Penroses, who between them own most of a row, and he climbed out of every window in the house before he was six. At nine he ran all the way to the castle gate and told Ammon Sael he'd seen a dragon on the road; Ammon wrote it down carefully, marked it, and gave him a cup of pear-wine anyway, and Jory has never forgiven him for the kindness. At twelve a Stormreach courier came through on a Low Reach horse at full gallop, didn't stop, didn't eat, shouted a greeting over his shoulder, and was gone over the hill. Jory stood in the road with his mouth open until his sister fetched him in.","schooling":"The orchard school in the packing shed, by then kept by Orchard-teacher Mair Glyn, who made each child graft a twig onto a tree every spring and left the dead grafts on as a lesson. Jory's died three years running. Lamp Test at eight: nothing answered, 'not even the Tempest one, which was rude'.","history":"He has worked the family row since twelve, well enough when he's watched. At seventeen he signed on with a Stormreach-bound carter, rode as far as the first inn, and found his grandmother had paid the carter to bring him straight back. At eighteen he began to save, toll by toll, for a named sapling of his grandad Cador's tree, and this spring Peder Lowick stamped him a tag for it. It's in a pot by his bed, three feet high, grafted. He has written, in Corran Vale's style, a shouted apology to his whole family that he means to deliver from the castle roof the morning he goes, and has been practising it, at volume, in the far field. The family have heard it many times and have started correcting the rhymes. He talks to every traveller who'll stand still. He has never seen a skyray.","family":"Parents, five brothers and sisters, a grandmother who pays carters, and a grandfather whose tree he is taking with him.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"Loud, funny and profane, and sent to the Council anyway. Proof to Jory that you can be noisy and still be the one they send."},"band":{"act":"Corran Vale","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Storm-shouted apology rap. Jory's own apology from the castle roof is written in the same rhyme scheme, more or less.","rv_vote":"Corran Vale."},"vote":{"local":"Hasn't lit a lamp; says he'd light for anything that makes a noise.","edict":"Against, mostly because Stormreach is, and he wants to be from there."},"wants":"To eat a pear off his own named tree on a floating rock.","site":"wickhaven_vf1","town":"Wickhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:mournhaven_vf1":{"name":"Tegen Morrice","age":230,"role":"cooper's widow who runs the press village's store; sells barrels, seed-cake and kit; one of the press-folk who turned the Archive's lamps down two hundred years ago","look":"Looks thirty, forearms like a rower's, a cooper's hoop-driver hanging off her belt like a weapon; she smells of crushed seed.","voice":"Plain and unhurried; fond of a long story, and ends each one with a shrug.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Kit, barrels, seed-cake. Seed-cake's for the donkeys on holidays and for you any day you like.","lines":["The old price for Drift oil was set two hundred and twelve years ago. I know. I was there. I was eighteen and I thought it was generous.","Mournhaven says the Drift was here first. So were we. The first press is older than half their jetty.","When the oil ran short I walked to the Vale and turned lamps down for a week. Eight hundred and six of them. I can still feel the little wheels under my thumb."],"about":"I'm a cooper's widow who sells everything a press village needs, and a few things it doesn't. I've been in the Copying Rooms once. I didn't copy a thing. I turned the lamps down so they'd last.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"Her mother walked a press-team and her father kept the donkeys, and Tegen was put on the circle at six, the smallest walker on the first press, singing with the others to keep the pace. She liked it: the screw creaking overhead, the grown-ups' feet going round, the oil coming down the trough like slow honey. At ten she crawled into a seed-bin to hide during a game and fell asleep there, and woke at dusk under a drift of seed with half the village calling her name through the sheds. Her mother found her by the sneezing. Tegen remembers the smell of that bin better than her own wedding.","schooling":"The press-school in the cooperage under Master Wynn Fallow, a cooper who taught letters by branding them onto barrel staves with a hot iron, so that the alphabet went out to the Archive a stave at a time. Barrels still come back to her store with her childhood spelling on them. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor.","history":"At eighteen she stood in the meeting-house while the old price was agreed with Mournhaven's harbour-reeve, and thought nothing of it. At twenty-five she married the cooper's apprentice, Jethro Morrice. At thirty came the bad harvest: the presses ran dry by midwinter, and the press-families walked two days to Lanthys Vale and stood in the Copying Rooms and the stacks for a week, turning every wick down by hand so the oil would stretch and no lamp would go out. Tegen turned eight hundred and six, and read every tag. She ran the cooperage with Jethro for a century and a half and, when his lamp went up sixty years ago, kept the yard and turned the front of it into a store. She taught the press-school for a while after, the Wynn Fallow way, on staves. She has outlasted three harbour-reeves and argues the price with every new one, politely, by telling them the story of the week in the Copying Rooms until they give in or leave.","family":"Jethro's lamp in the Archive; a son who coopers in Mournhaven; a granddaughter on the third press.","hero":{"who":"The Flameborne King","why":"A king who has to answer anybody who says no to him, and usually answers with bread. She'd like to see Mournhaven answer the press-folk with a single loaf."},"band":{"act":"The Bellows","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stadium rock where the crowd pumps the forge, the way the press-teams turn the screw. She saw it once in Hearthmount and walked the whole show.","rv_vote":"The Bellows."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for the press-families' petition for a fair price on Drift oil.","edict":"Warn. And she'd add a line about the week of turning lamps down, for context."},"wants":"For Mournhaven to pay a fair price before she has to remember the old one any longer.","site":"mournhaven_vf1","town":"Mournhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:mournhaven_vf1":{"name":"Barra Quenby","age":38,"role":"smith of the twelve presses' iron collars and screw-shoes; Metalcrown-born, from the Warm","look":"Stocky, a tally plate at her wrist reading 'all born', a foundry burn shaped like a gear-tooth on her forearm; sings off-key at the anvil.","voice":"Loud and cheerful; Shiftcant slang; 'make it' for anything from 'good luck' to 'hurry up'.","greet":"Shift! Lamp's lit, I mean. You need a collar mended? A blade? Make it quick, the third press is squeaking and I can hear it from here.","lines":["Metalcrown asked for a Council seat nine times. Ninth time a polite young engineer called Solvane read it out and they laughed. My mum wrote to tell me. She didn't laugh.","The Edict? Metalcrown smiles at it, they say. I don't. A list is a list. I've been on lists.","Couldn't sleep my first year here. Too quiet. Then I moved in next to the presses. They run every day. Now I sleep like a slagheap."],"about":"Metalcrown girl, from the Warm under the foundries. Married a press-walker who came selling oil to the Reactor Halls and followed him home. Now I keep twelve screw-presses alive and sing badly to all of them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"understands most of it"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"good, loud"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"She was born in the Warm, the pits beneath Iron Throne City's foundries, where the poorest families live and nobody is fitted with anything because nobody can pay for it. Her days ran on the three whistles. She swam in the cooler pools of the Smeltway with the other Warm kids, all orange-lit. At ten she was given her First Tool, a short hammer stamped with her name, and broke its head off on a rivet within the hour. She was so ashamed she sat up all night in her gran's corner fixing it, wedge by wedge, and in the morning her gran turned it over, said 'I made it', and stamped the words again under Barra's name.","schooling":"Shift-school in the Warm under Shift-mother Dorrin Kettle, who taught letters off the stamps on ration-loaves and sums off the whistles. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. 'Nobody in the Warm gets a lamp,' she says, 'except to see by.' First Tool at ten. Foundry apprentice at twelve. Dorrin made the whole class recite what each whistle meant before breakfast, and Barra still can.","history":"She worked a casting line for thirteen years and was good at it. At twenty-five a Spiritveil press-walker, Huw Quenby, came through the Reactor Halls with a wagon of seed-oil the engineers were trying as lubricant, got lost in the Warm, and asked her the way in very polite Bridge. She walked him out. He came back the next season, and the one after. They were joined by the Link, a chain-link each, at twenty-seven, and she came to the press village at twenty-eight. The silence nearly finished her. She took the smithy beside the press sheds so the screws would sing her to sleep, and she has kept all twelve running since: collars, shoes, pins, every bit of iron the oak can't do. At dusk she says her dead's names under the creak of the presses, the way she used to under the Long Whistle at home. Huw will live for centuries after her; she makes jokes about it at dinner, and the village wishes she wouldn't.","family":"Husband Huw, a press-walker; her mother and two brothers in the Warm, who write about the petitions; her gran's First Tool, which she keeps on a nail.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"'One shift, one vote. Even you.' Her grandfather voted in the Foundry Commons and talked about it until he died."},"band":{"act":"Shift Change","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Three singers, one per shift, never on stage together. It sounds like home.","rv_vote":"Shift Change. Metalcrown never gets lamps; she's got one; it's going home."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for the press-families' fair-price petition. At home she'd be a Commons nostalgist, like her grandfather.","edict":"Against. Metalcrown smiles at the Edict; she doesn't."},"wants":"For Huw to keep the presses running long after she's gone, on her collars.","site":"mournhaven_vf1","town":"Mournhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:mournhaven_vf1":{"name":"Olwen Rudd","age":74,"role":"pace-singer on the first press; keeper of the press songs","look":"Looks twenty-five, wide-shouldered, barefoot with soles like boot-leather, a voice you can hear across the valley.","voice":"Sings half her sentences; breathes in time; bossy and warm.","greet":"Lamp's lit! Step in step, love. Oh, you're not on the team. Sorry. Habit.","lines":["Thessaly can tell a harvest from the noise the screw makes. I can tell a marriage from how a team walks. There's a couple on the fourth press who won't last the winter.","Mournhaven wants old-price oil. Let them walk the circle with us for a day. Then let them name a price.","Ianto worked it out once. I've walked about once round the world. In a circle. In a shed."],"about":"I set the pace on the first press. I sing, they walk, the screw turns, the oil comes. If I sing it wrong, every lamp in the Realm's a little dimmer. No pressure. Well. Some pressure.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Her father kept the village donkeys, and Olwen sang to them from the time she could stand, because they walked better for it and because nobody else would listen. At seven the first press's singer had a coughing fit mid-song, the team faltered, and Olwen, standing in the doorway with a bucket, took up the line without thinking. The team picked up the pace and kept walking. Nobody said anything. At the end of the shift the singer, still coughing, put her own straw hat on Olwen's head. At twelve she was put on the second press, and spent her breaks learning drum patterns from the Stormreach carters who came for barrels.","schooling":"The press-school in the cooperage, taught by Tegen Morrice, who branded letters onto barrel staves as her own teacher had. Olwen learned to read off the staves and still reads everything as though it might be a song. Tegen let her sing the alphabet instead of saying it, and the whole class learned it faster, which Tegen now claims was her idea. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor.","history":"She has been pace-singer on the first press since twenty. She knows two hundred press songs, some five hundred years old, some she made up on the circle on a bad day; she is the only person who knows all of them and which press each belongs to. She married Dilys, a cooper, thirty years ago, at the Last Drop, and they have no children and a house full of other people's: half the press-teams eat at their table. Olwen can tell from a team's step who has fought, who is grieving, who is in love, and she has been known to change the song to fix it. Two winters ago she started losing her highest notes. She has told nobody but Dilys. She has started, very quietly, teaching the first press's songs to a sixteen-year-old on the ninth press with a voice like a struck bell, and pretending it is just for fun.","family":"Wife Dilys, a cooper; her father's lamp in the Archive and his donkeys' great-great-grandchildren in the village paddock.","hero":{"who":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","why":"Seventy years old and kneading dough on stage while she sings. Olwen has never seen anyone else do proper work on a stage, and would like to."},"band":{"act":"The Rainmakers","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A drum collective. She stole three of their patterns for press songs and would do it again.","rv_vote":"The Rainmakers."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her lamp for the press-families' fair-price petition.","edict":"Warn."},"wants":"To find a singer who can carry the first press before her high notes are gone.","site":"mournhaven_vf1","town":"Mournhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:mournhaven_vf1:b":{"name":"Ianto Merrow","age":133,"role":"the village's accounts-keeper and donkey-man; has proven the Drift oil price unfair nine ways","look":"Looks twenty-six, ink and seed-dust on his cuffs, abacus beads strung round one wrist like a bracelet, donkey hair all over his coat.","voice":"Quick and precise; laughs nervously after every number.","greet":"Lamp's lit. I'm counting donkeys. Seven. There should be eight. Have you seen a donkey? Brown. Opinionated.","lines":["Mournhaven pays one toll a jar for Drift oil. The Archive pays three. I wrote it on the meeting-house door. Someone wrote 'we know' underneath.","The Edict's just bad sums. You can't audit a memory. There's no unit for it.","I keep the donkeys and the books. The donkeys are easier. They only lie about how tired they are."],"about":"I keep the village's accounts and its donkeys, which are more alike than you'd think. I've proven the old price is unfair nine different ways. Nobody's laughed at the proof. They just haven't read it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting; he learned it for its numbers"}],"childhood":"His father was clerk to the press-master and Ianto counted everything: steps round the circle, pips in a seed-cake, how often each donkey brayed before noon. At eight he worked out that the village was losing about a barrel a month between press and cart, told the press-master, and was patted on the head. Six years later a cracked cooling-trough was found that had been leaking all along, about a barrel a month. He has never fully recovered from being right too early. He spent most of his childhood in the donkey paddock, because the donkeys didn't mind him counting them and leaned on him while he did it.","schooling":"Two years at the Archive's accounts-school in the Vale, a rare place for a press-village boy, under Accountant Hesketh Varr, who taught double-entry and said that every ledger tells two stories and they had better agree. Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor. He was the best in his year at sums and the worst at speaking up in class.","history":"He came home and did the village's books from thirty, and took over the donkeys at forty when Olwen Rudd's father's lamp went up the road and nobody else would. For ninety years he has worked on the Drift price. He has nine proofs, each from a different direction: by barrel, by seed-acre, by walker-hour, by what lamp-oil costs in Lightforge. He has tried four times to present them to Mournhaven's harbour-reeve; each time a Night on the Water came up and the meeting was very properly put off. Once he presented to a committee of the Lantern Council in the Gallery, and they laughed, kindly, at the donkey hair on his coat, and thanked him, and filed it. He keeps every proof in a box under his bed with the donkeys' birth tallies. He is gentle, anxious, and the most stubborn man in the valley.","family":"A widowed mother he cooks for; two sisters on the presses; eight donkeys, each with a name and a ledger page.","hero":{"who":"The Hammer-Bearer","why":"When the Council laughed at the petition, the Councillor without a Realm was the only one who stood up. Ianto has stood in a laughing room with his proofs and nobody stood."},"band":{"act":"Courtbell","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Amapiano with court bells. It sounds like an abacus being played well.","rv_vote":"Courtbell."},"vote":{"local":"Lit his lamp for the press-families' fair-price petition, and attached a proof to it.","edict":"Against. Bad sums."},"wants":"A meeting at which someone reads one of his proofs aloud, all the way to the end.","site":"mournhaven_vf1","town":"Mournhaven Farms 2","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:mournhaven_vf2":{"name":"Fennel Downe","age":310,"role":"Backshelf's trader in wool, cheese and kit; Unlamped a hundred and sixty years ago for saying the Letting aloud at a family dinner","look":"Looks thirty, a shepherd's coat with a thousand mends, hair gone silver on one side of her head only.","voice":"Sardonic and kind; says hard things lightly, as if passing the salt.","greet":"Lamp's lit. No, don't tell me why you're here. Buy something. That's the only question I ask.","lines":["A Mournhaven family's been up the hill twice this month, knocking. Sael lets them knock. They'll be back. They always are.","Some lamps should be allowed to go out. I said that once, out loud, at a family dinner. Hence Backshelf.","I chose Fennel because it grows anywhere and nobody bothers planting it. My old name was very grand. I'll tell it you if you're boring enough."],"about":"I keep the store at Backshelf: wool, cheese, kit, the odd bow. I came up this hill a hundred and sixty years ago with a different name and a lot of opinions. I've kept the opinions.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native, with a high-Vale accent she exaggerates when annoyed"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; her family traded copies with Lightforge"}],"childhood":"She was born into one of the grand copyist-councillor lines of Lanthys Vale, in a tall house with its own lamp-room of ancestors, and was expected to recite forty generations by her tenth birthday. She managed forty-one, for spite. One ancestor's lamp she was set to dust every week, a man whose memory, when you held the lamp, was of hitting a servant until the servant stopped moving. At eleven she blew it out. It was relit within the hour from the family flame, and she was made to sit beside it alone through the whole night, to learn. What she learned, she says, was that nobody had ever asked whether he should be kept.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight: spark-poor, which in her family was a small scandal. Private tutoring from Tutor Gethen Ashwell, who taught her Clear, disputation and the history of the Lantern Council, and who once remarked over a lesson that Spiritveil lets its laws lapse when nobody remembers why they were made, and then asked, very mildly, 'Why not lamps?' She has always taken that as permission.","history":"She trained as a copyist and was good at it, and spent a hundred years copying the lamps of other grand families. At a hundred and forty she began going to a reading circle in a Vale cellar where the Letting was discussed, the old heresy that some lamps ought to be allowed to go out. At a hundred and fifty, at the family's Long Evening dinner, with forty relatives at the table, she said so. Not about everyone. About him. Her family Unlamped her that week: her lamp moved to a back shelf in the Archive, never put out. She walked to Mournhaven, sat a night on the jetty beside a stranger's lantern, and then walked up the hill. At the New Shelf she chose the name Fennel. She has run the store for a hundred and fifty years, teaches Backshelf children their letters when asked, and argues the Letting with anyone who'll stay for a second cup. She writes to her family once a decade. They have never written back, and they have never come up the hill to knock either.","family":"A Vale family she won't name; a hundred and twelve people on the New Shelf she will.","hero":{"who":"Sera Solbrand","why":"The only person ever to use the Judgment of One. Fennel likes people who do the hard thing once, on purpose, and then live with it."},"band":{"act":"Telt","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Punk so rude it's banned at home. She bought a smuggled copy of a Telt broadside from a carter and has it pinned behind the counter.","rv_vote":"Telt, out of solidarity with the banned."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her clay lamp on the New Shelf for Councillor Lanmore's motion to move the oldest shelves. A moved lamp is not an Unlamped lamp, whatever the Council says, and she would know.","edict":"Refuse."},"wants":"For one lamp to be allowed to go out: his.","site":"mournhaven_vf2","town":"Mournhaven Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"smith:mournhaven_vf2":{"name":"Kenver Daw","age":34,"role":"Backshelf's smith (shears, crooks, pans, gate-hooks); one of the very few people actually born in Backshelf","look":"Looks twenty-five, freckled and cheerful, a smoke-black fringe, a grey wool jumper three sizes too big that somebody new knitted him.","voice":"Easy and chatty; plays up being the local boy.","greet":"Lamp's lit! You're new. Everyone's new here except me. I was born here. I'm basically a landmark.","lines":["Sael lights every lamp on the New Shelf herself. A hundred and twelve. Mine's the only one that was lit the day its person was born.","People come up this hill with nothing and we feed them. If Lightforge counted us, we'd have to admit we don't know half their real names. That's the point of us.","I've got two lamps. One in the Archive, one on our shelf. Mum says that makes me twice as kept. I say twice the dusting."],"about":"I'm the Backshelf smith, which mostly means shears, crooks and pans. I'm one of about four people who were actually born here. I like it. Somebody's always arriving, and they're always interesting.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"a dozen signs from a neighbour who came up from somewhere she won't say"}],"childhood":"His mother came up the hill Unlamped and has never told him why; his father was a Mournhaven carter who followed her up a year later and stayed. Kenver grew up with a new face at the supper table nearly every season, and before each one his mother would crouch down and say, 'Don't ask.' So he guessed instead, in his head, and later out loud, and got into a great deal of trouble for it. He learned to shear at six on a patient old ewe. Every dusk he sat with Sael Thorn at the New Shelf while she lit the lamps, and she let him hold the taper for the bottom row.","schooling":"'We didn't have a school. We had forty people who'd each been to a different one.' Fennel Downe taught him letters, in a high-Vale hand he still writes; a former trader taught him sums; a one-time Lantern Knight armourer who had arrived with the second name Grey taught him the forge. Lamp Test at eight, down in Mournhaven: spark-poor.","history":"He apprenticed to Grey at fourteen and took over the forge at twenty-eight, when Grey's clay lamp joined the New Shelf's top row. He makes shears for the grey-wool flocks, mends every pan in Backshelf, hangs gates and forges the iron hooks on which the village carries Mournhaven's lanterns up and down the hill in weather nobody else will walk in, which is half the village's income. He takes his turn on the bad-weather lantern-carries and enjoys them. He's courting Ceinwen, a Mournhaven dyer's daughter, whose family Unlamped her aunt forty years ago, and the aunt lives three doors down from his forge in Backshelf. All four of them know. Nobody has said anything at dinner yet, which in Spiritveil is a sort of agreement.","family":"Mother and father in Backshelf; Ceinwen in Mournhaven, and Ceinwen's aunt, who knits his jumpers.","hero":{"who":"His mother","why":"She walked up a hill with no name and made a whole person out of it. Him. He's the person."},"band":{"act":"Wahai!","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Party reggaeton with pipes. Backshelf has no music of its own, so it has everyone's, and this is the one he dances to.","rv_vote":"Wahai!"},"vote":{"local":"Lit nothing for the Council's motions this season; the Council says Backshelf's clay lamps don't count, so Backshelf lights them all at once, for fun.","edict":"Refuse."},"wants":"To marry Ceinwen without her family and his village going to war over it.","site":"mournhaven_vf2","town":"Mournhaven Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:mournhaven_vf2":{"name":"Vanner","age":22,"role":"newest arrival at Backshelf, three weeks on the hill; learning to shear; hasn't chosen her second name","look":"Thin, a good coat that's been slept in, a copyist's callus on her middle finger, straw in her hair from the byre.","voice":"Guarded and clipped, with sudden dry jokes, then a retreat.","greet":"Lamp's lit. I'm Vanner. For now. Don't ask me what it was before. They told me nobody asks. I'm testing it.","lines":["Somebody knocked last night. Not for me. I hid in the byre anyway. Sael brought me cheese.","The Edict? I've just left a city full of opinions about it. I'm trying sheep instead.","Three weeks and I haven't picked a name. 'Vanner' was painted on the cart that brought me. It's a cart."],"about":"I came up three weeks ago on a wool-cart. I'm learning to shear. I'm bad at it. The sheep are bad at it too, so we're even.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native, Vale accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; she won't say where she learned it"}],"childhood":"Vanner hasn't told Backshelf about her childhood, and Backshelf hasn't asked. What the village knows, it has pieced together from what slips out. She grew up in Lanthys Vale, somewhere near the Copying Rooms; she knows which sills get the honey cakes and which readers stumble on the long names. She mentioned once, shearing, that she learned to read off the backs of lamp-tags because there was nothing else at the right height. She can tell a good wick from a bad one by its smell. She laughed at Kenver Daw's high-Vale handwriting and then went white, as if she'd recognised it.","schooling":"A copying-room school in the Vale, she let slip, though she won't say which teacher. The callus on her finger says years at a desk. Lamp Test: spark-poor, she says, flatly, and nobody has reason to doubt it. She writes a better hand than anyone on the hill and is clearly trying to make it worse.","history":"Three weeks ago she arrived on a wool-cart from Mournhaven with a good coat, a bag of copybooks and no name she'd give. The first night, in Sael Thorn's kitchen, she burned the copybooks in the stove one by one without reading them, except one page, which she folded small and put in her boot. At the New Shelf she said she didn't want to choose yet. Sael lit a clay lamp for her anyway with nothing on the tag. She sleeps in the byre by choice, says the sheep don't talk. She is learning to shear from Gwennol Starling, badly, and has begun, without being asked, to re-letter the New Shelf's worn tags in a neat copyist's hand, which gives away more than anything she's said. When the Mournhaven family came knocking last week she was out of the room before the second knock.","family":"Unknown. She says 'my people' once a day and stops halfway through the sentence.","hero":{"who":"Sael Thorn","why":"Sael lit a lamp for her the first night without asking what to write on it. It's blank. Vanner says it's the best thing anyone has ever done for her, and then changes the subject."},"band":{"act":"Somebody","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"An anonymous pop star nobody can name, who still gets lamps every year. Vanner finds that very encouraging.","rv_vote":"Somebody."},"vote":{"local":"Not lighting anything this season.","edict":"'No comment.' Then, after a while: 'Against. Obviously.'"},"wants":"To pick a name that's hers before winter.","site":"mournhaven_vf2","town":"Mournhaven Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"vill:mournhaven_vf2:b":{"name":"Gwennol Starling","age":470,"role":"shepherd and dog-trainer; the woman whose lamp the Council once moved forward, who came back to Backshelf anyway","look":"Looks thirty-five, cropped silver hair, a shepherd's crook with a small clay lamp hung from the hook.","voice":"Warm and slow, teasing; tells the truth sideways.","greet":"Lamp's lit, pet. You've walked up a hill to a village with no name. Either you're lost or you're one of us. Both are fine.","lines":["Down in Mournhaven they're arguing whose shelves the dead belong on. Up here we just put them on the one we've got.","Lightforge wants to count sparks. The Council once moved my lamp forward. Counting wasn't the hard part. Asking was.","My lamp's on a front shelf in the Archive now. I've never been to see it. My clay one's here. That's the one I dust."],"about":"I'm Gwennol. Second name. I came up here Unlamped two hundred years ago, and a hundred years ago the whole village walked down to the Archive and got my lamp moved forward. Then I came home. Here.","languages":[{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few funeral words, picked up from Frostgrave mourners on the jetty"}],"childhood":"She was born to a family of rowers in Mournhaven and had an oar in her hands by ten, rowing lanterns out along the Long Jetty in the dusk with her father, who was the best rower on the Drift and was told so every day. She loved the water at night more than anything: the road of small lights running out into the black, the creak of the oarlocks, the low voices of families telling stories over their dead. She had a huge, wild laugh that carried right across the Drift and embarrassed her mother at every wake. Her father, she remembers, used to tap the boat's gunwale with two fingers, tap-tap, tap-tap, when he was tired and wanted to go home.","schooling":"Mournhaven's wake-school under Teacher Morfudd Lane. Lamp Test at eight: the Veil lamp answered. Full Spark. She went to Korrhaven, to the Veil Tower, and lasted one knot. The first ghost-habit that crept into her own body was her father's tap-tap on the table, and she found she couldn't bear it, and came home at thirteen without explaining to anyone why.","history":"She rowed for Mournhaven for two and a half centuries. When her father died, she refused to sit his Night on the Water. She rowed his lantern out, tied it to the jetty, and walked back along the boards in front of the whole town and went home. She has never said why, and nobody in Backshelf has asked. Her family Unlamped her within the month. At two hundred and seventy she walked up the hill, chose the name Gwennol, which means a swallow, and took up sheep. A century later an old Backshelf woman, now on the New Shelf's top row, organised the whole village to walk down to the Archive together and ask, very politely, for Gwennol's lamp to be moved forward from the back shelf. The Council, for the first time anyone could remember, said yes. Gwennol thanked them, walked the whole village back up the hill, and has stayed. She trains the best sheepdogs in the hills. Her brother's grandchildren knock every few years now; sometimes she lets them in.","family":"Her father's lamp, which she has not visited; her brother's descendants in Mournhaven; seven dogs; and the whole of Backshelf, which she will tell you is the same thing as family and then laugh at herself for saying.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"The first lantern was a cracked clay cup. Gwennol's is clay too, and she thinks that's the right way round."},"band":{"act":"Under Haskerry","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Whispered folk. After two hundred and fifty years of the jetty she likes music you have to lean in for.","rv_vote":"Under Haskerry."},"vote":{"local":"Lit her clay lamp for the Council's warning. The Council did her one kindness; she gives them one lamp a season in return, and no more.","edict":"Warn."},"wants":"To teach Vanner to row, out on the Drift at night, before Vanner's decided anything else.","site":"mournhaven_vf2","town":"Mournhaven Farms 3","realm":"Spiritveil"},"shop:thornwick":{"name":"Wulfa Coalroad","age":58,"role":"trader, buyer of levy salvage, and the village’s only fluent Kindle-speaker","look":"Broad, short, sun-browned, a Flameborne copper ring through one ear and a Stonehold burden-scar across both shoulders. Wears an apron with eleven pockets and knows what is in every one.","voice":"Switches between Holdspeech and Kindle mid-sentence; haggles like she is doing you a kindness, which she sometimes is.","greet":"Set. Mind the step, the levy cracked it. Buying or selling? I’ll tell you now, I’m better at buying.","lines":["Levy came through at the turn of the moon. Three hundred pairs of boots and not one of them wiped. Half of what’s on my shelf they dropped in the ditch on the way out.","Ashford wants our toll money for their ford road. Fair enough, their road’s ruined. Unfair enough, we didn’t ruin it. Both things at once. That’s the border for you.","My husband’s Flameborne. Thirty-one years. He still argues with the hedge."],"about":"I stood bridge-watch at Hearthward for nine years, which is longer than anyone sensible does. Came home with a Flameborne cooper and a mouth full of Kindle. Now I sell arrows to people going south and buy back the broken ones from people coming north. It evens out.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"fluent, with a Coal Road accent that makes Flameborne folk laugh"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"}],"childhood":"Wulfa was the fourth of five in a toll-cottage so close to the bar she learned to count by the creak of its iron pin. Her father was a hedge-layer who never once finished a hedge and said so cheerfully every autumn. Her mother sold black-and-sloe out of the window. The family’s first marker stands three miles down the Coal Road, which is how they got the name, and Wulfa was taken there on Marker Day every year to eat cake with a great-great-grandfather she knew only as two letters, an H and a C, cut very deep. She was a loud, square child who fought her brothers for the right to help lift the bar and lost every time until she was eleven. At eleven she won, and nobody would tell her whether they had let her.","schooling":"Road-school on the Coal Road under Master Ulf Stannard, who taught letters by making the children read the markers aloud as they walked, and docked a mark for anyone who stepped over a marker’s shadow. Wulfa’s Lamp Test, at eight, lit nothing. She was quick at sums, slow at letters, and learned more Kindle from the Flameborne carters at the bar than Master Ulf could teach her Bridge.","history":"At sixteen Wulfa was one of the two Thornwick sent down to stand bridge-watch at Hearthward. Most come home after two years. She stayed nine. She learned the grey side of the keep and the black side, learned to shout across the courtyard at the Flameborne bridgewarden’s people in their own tongue, and learned to like it. On her sixth First Coal she stood in the Flameborne crowd and watched a fishwife refuse their King to his face and him answer with a loaf, and she went back to the grey side and could not sleep for envy. In her ninth year she married Fen, a Flameborne cooper who mended the keep’s water-barrels, at a hearthstone wedding where he brought a lump of basalt and she brought granite and the two rocks have sat on the same shelf ever since. They came back to Thornwick when her mother’s legs went. She took over the window, then the shop, then the salvage, because every army that passes the bar drops things and somebody may as well be paid for picking them up. She has no children. She has nine nieces and nephews and has lent money to all of them.","family":"Husband Fen, Flameborne-born cooper, who refuses to lay hedge on Laying Day on principle and then lays two. Her brother Tobold runs the sloe-press and owes her for it; neither mentions this.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired & Hollis Brask","why":"She spent nine years inside the keep they built and still thinks it is the best argument anyone has ever made. Two people who never married building one house with two colours of stone is, she says, exactly what her own marriage looks like."},"band":{"act":"Hot Corner","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Fen’s nephews played it in the Hearthward courtyard on a cart until both bridgewardens came out to complain together, which was the only thing she ever saw them do together.","rv_vote":"Her lamp goes to Hot Corner again, and she will hear no talk about loyalty to Stonehold acts from people who have never been south of the bar."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Ulla Kestergate at the Senate, because Ulla is the only road-elder who has eaten slapbread at Hearthward and said thank you in Kindle.","edict":"Against, flatly. She has seen what Flameborne does when someone tries to count its fire and says Stonehold should refuse before the riots spill up the Coal Road and through her shop."},"wants":"To get Thornwick and Ashford to mend the ford road together, with both villages’ money, before the next army comes through.","site":"thornwick","town":"Thornwick","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:thornwick":{"name":"Huld Marchstone","age":34,"role":"village smith, keeper of the Toll Bar’s iron pin, maker of blackthorn-stick ferrules","look":"Very tall, stooped from ducking the forge lintel, beard trimmed square with his own shears, burn-speckled forearms. Always has a blackthorn thorn stuck somewhere in his sleeve.","voice":"Slow and pleased with himself in a mild way; finishes what you were saying for you, usually wrongly.","greet":"Set. If it’s the bar pin you’ve come about, it’s fine. I greased it Tuesday. If it’s something else, also probably fine.","lines":["Every blackthorn stick that leaves Thornwick gets a ferrule off my anvil. That’s six hundred a year. Somewhere there’s a man in Brask leaning on me and he doesn’t know it.","My aunt Agna charges featherweights double. I asked her once how she decides who’s a featherweight. She said, ‘I look.’","I had a very good week last month. Nothing happened. Pin held, forge drew, I ate a lot of stew. People think a smith wants excitement. A smith wants the pin to hold."],"about":"I’m the smith. My father was the smith and his father kept the bar before the smith did. I make what the village needs and I don’t make what it doesn’t. I’m a dull man and I’ve made my peace with it, which I think is the hardest thing a dull man can do.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting, enough to name a price"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a few words, mostly the rude ones carters shout at the bar"}],"childhood":"Huld was born in the forge-loft and grew up under the noise of it, so that years later, staying a night at Anvil Reach where the chisels never stopped, he slept better than he had in his life. He was a pleasant, unhurried boy who liked to watch other children do things. His mother died of a winter cough when he was six; it was sad and ordinary and his father did not remarry, and the two of them ate the same lidded stew four nights a week for nine years and were content. His aunt Agna, the toll-keeper, took him to the bar on market days and let him hold the rope. He was not much use and she told him so. He has loved her for it ever since, because she was the first person who did not lie to him about anything.","schooling":"Road-school on the Coal Road under Mistress Hessa Long, who made each child carry a pebble from the first marker to the last and set it down without dropping it, and who wept openly when one boy did it with his eyes shut. Lamp Test at nine: nothing answered. Huld failed his Shouldering at fifteen, setting the stone down at nine hundred paces, and did it the next spring without stopping. He mentions the second time, not the first.","history":"He took the forge at twenty-two when his father’s eyes went milky, and his father sat on a stool beside the anvil telling him he was holding the tongs wrong until he died at seventy. Huld made the new pin for the Toll Bar the year he took over, the old one having worn to an hourglass, and that pin is the single thing he is proud of: a forearm of black iron that has taken the bar up and down some thirty thousand times. He married Linna Thornroad, a hedge-layer two years older, after a courtship of five years that consisted mostly of him fixing her billhook. They have two daughters, nine and seven, neither of whom shows any interest in iron. When the levy came through this season their quartermaster asked Huld to reshoe forty horses in a day and he did twenty-two and told the man the rest would have to go lame, and the quartermaster stared at him and then laughed and paid for twenty-two. Huld has told this story at the toll-bar every evening since and improves it slightly each time.","family":"Wife Linna, daughters Brede and Wenna, and his aunt Agna Thorne, the toll-keeper, who comes to dinner and criticises the stew.","hero":{"who":"Agna Thorne, toll-keeper","why":"She asks every traveller where they are going and actually listens to the answer. He thinks that is rarer than any spark."},"band":{"act":"Line of Twelve","realm":"Stonehold","why":"His daughters have a painted board of all twelve idols nailed above their bed and he has learned every drill-step so as to be useful at the kitchen table, which he says is his job in this family.","rv_vote":"Line of Twelve, because the girls will check."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind whoever Agna stands behind, and this season that is Elder Bennet Kellway, so there he will be, at the back, being tall.","edict":"Against, mostly because Agna is. When pressed he says a man with no spark has no business telling a man with one to write it in a book."},"wants":"For the bar pin to outlast him, and to be told so by someone who knows iron.","site":"thornwick","town":"Thornwick","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:thornwick":{"name":"Sorrel Thornroad","age":16,"role":"hedge-layer, chosen for this year’s Hearthward bridge-watch","look":"Wiry, freckled, knuckles scratched raw from blackthorn, hair hacked off at the jaw because the hedge kept catching it. A borrowed bridge-watch cloak two sizes too big.","voice":"Fast, bossy, practises sounding stern and keeps breaking into a grin.","greet":"Set! You’re a stranger. Is it Laying Day? It isn’t. Shame. You’d have had to lay a stem.","lines":["I go down to Hearthward at the next moon. Two of us every year. They pick the ones who argue best. I don’t know why they picked me.","The levy smashed the hedge on the east side going through. Forty yards. My mother’s length. She didn’t say anything. She just went out with the billhook the next morning and didn’t come in till dark.","Ashford’s ford was ruined by our soldiers, so we should pay. That’s what I think. Don’t tell my uncle."],"about":"I lay hedge. Everyone here lays hedge, but I lay it fastest. Next month I’m going to Hearthward to stand on a bridge and be shouted at in Kindle, and I am going to shout back. I’ve been practising on the goats.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, learned for the watch"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"halting but enthusiastic, mostly insults Wulfa taught her"}],"childhood":"Sorrel was the youngest of three girls born to Linna Thornroad’s elder sister Mab, and grew up in a cottage whose back wall was a hedge her great-grandmother laid. She could split a stem half-through and bend it without snapping it by six, and by eight she was laying her own short length beside her mother’s, badly, and being made to unpick it. She was a child who asked why. Why the hedge was never finished. Why featherweights paid double. Why the bar took two Stonehold men or four Flameborne ones and whether anyone had ever actually checked. Her middle sister, Isa, was the beauty and the quiet one; her eldest, Rhan, was already married. Sorrel was the one who climbed onto the Toll Bar to watch the carters come up from the south and got her ear boxed by Agna Thorne for it at least once a month.","schooling":"Road-school on the Coal Road under Master Stannard’s son, Dain, who walked them to Hearthward once, all forty miles, so they could see Mile Nought, and made them sleep a night in the keep’s granite half. At her Lamp Test, at eight, the Gravem lamp gave one dull flicker and went out: Half-Spark. She can make a stone a little heavier in her palm if she concentrates. Her little fingernail went grey for a week the first time. She stopped trying.","history":"There is not much yet, and she knows it, and it annoys her. At twelve she won the hedge-race at the Laying Day fair against boys three years older. At fourteen she argued with the Hold-Senate’s road-assessor for an hour over whether the Coal Road’s potholes counted as Thornwick’s or Ashford’s and made him write down two of her points. At fifteen she did her Shouldering in a Sedgemoor sling her aunt bought her and did not stop once, and when she set the stone down at the end she was sick in the ditch and then demanded to do it again. This season, with the levy passing through, she watched three hundred soldiers march east and asked every one she could reach where they were going and why; none of them knew, or would say. The village chose her for the bridge-watch a week later. She suspects it was to get her out of the toll-keeper’s hair. She has begun keeping a list, in Bridge, of every question nobody will answer.","family":"Mother Mab, who taught her to lay hedge and still unpicks her work. Two older sisters. Her father, a drover, is mostly on the Long Roads and sends cheese.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend, Warden of Skyrend","why":"A trader told her the Stormreach Warden stands up in the Free Assembly and opposes the Edict at the top of her lungs. Sorrel wants to be the kind of person who is heard from the next citadel over."},"band":{"act":"Wren of Kesterrow","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Wren is barely older than she is and plays a stone-zither like it is a weapon. Sorrel has a torn sheet of Wren’s words pinned inside her cloak and gets half of them wrong.","rv_vote":"Wren of Kesterrow, and she has already argued with three people about it this week."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to stand in the Senate. If she could, she would stand behind Elder Ulla Kestergate, because Ulla once answered a question from a child at the Anvil Reach market properly.","edict":"Against. She says if Stonehold just refuses, Lightforge will think the Holds are being stubborn, so Stonehold should refuse and say why, plainly, in Bridge."},"wants":"To find out where the levy was going.","site":"thornwick","town":"Thornwick","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:thornwick:b":{"name":"Aldo Southmile","age":81,"role":"retired garrison man, sloe-picker, brewer of the village’s strongest black-and-sloe","look":"Thin as a hedge-stake, back still straight, one ear missing its top, hands purple to the wrist every autumn from sloes. A bearer’s shoulder-pad he was never entitled to, worn because it was a gift.","voice":"Gruff, very slow, long pauses; tells you the end of a story first so you can decide whether you want the middle.","greet":"Set. Sit. Have you waited at the bar yet? Then you’re owed a cup. Don’t drink it fast. It bites.","lines":["There’s a stem in my length of hedge from every Realm. Even Metalcrown. A girl from the slag-hills laid it in the year of the long rain. She didn’t want to. I made her.","Three hundred levy boys went through and not one stopped for a cup. In my day you stopped for a cup. You didn’t know when you’d get the next.","My sloes this year are bitter. Good. Sweet sloes make a liar’s ale."],"about":"I stood garrison on Highmarch’s wall for twenty-six years and never saw a single thing happen. That’s what a good wall is for. I came home, I picked sloes. I’ve picked sloes for thirty years. They’re the same sloes. I like that about them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"garrison-fluent, all orders"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few words picked up from Stormreach riders on the wall"}],"childhood":"Aldo was born in the hard year of the Concord’s 1,115th winter, two years after Stonebarrow’s nineteen-day Carry, and his mother used to say the whole district was still too tired to celebrate a birth. He was the only son of a sloe-grower and a woman who laid hedge faster than any man in Thornwick. He was a solemn, careful boy. At seven he decided the village hedges were badly kept and spent a summer counting every stem in his family’s length and writing the number on a board. It came to four thousand and eleven. His father said, ‘And what will you do with that?’ and Aldo said, ‘Know it,’ and his father laughed so hard he had to sit on the bar.","schooling":"Road-school under old Master Tobin Coalfoot, who was blind and taught entirely by having the children describe each marker to him, letter by letter, and who could tell from their voices when they were guessing. Aldo’s Lamp Test, at eight: nothing lit. He was good at describing and bad at inventing, and the master told him he would make a fine witness and a poor storyteller. Both were true.","history":"He went up to the Highmarch garrison at nineteen because his elder cousin had, and stayed twenty-six years. He rose to wall-sergeant, which he describes as ‘being shouted at from above and below’. He married late, at thirty-eight, a garrison cook called Merryl Highcut who could chip carry-bread into perfectly even pieces with one blow, and they had one son, Daro. Merryl died when Daro was twelve. Aldo left the garrison and brought the boy home to Thornwick and the sloes, and raised him, and did it, he will tell you, about half right. Daro is a drover now on the Kell Road, fifty, with grown children of his own. Aldo laid his hedge-length every Laying Day for thirty years and made every stranger lay a stem, including a frightened young Metalcrown woman in the year of the long rain who would not give her name. He has never told anyone which stem was hers. When the levy marched through this season he stood at the bar holding a jug for an hour and nobody took a cup, and he poured it into the hedge-roots and went home.","family":"Son Daro, a drover, who visits twice a year and brings grandchildren Aldo cannot tell apart. His wife Merryl, dead thirty-one years, whose chipping-knife he still uses.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"Garron put the crown down. Aldo says every man on the wall at Highmarch wanted to put something down and never did, and Garron is the only one who managed it in front of everyone."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Forty old soldiers singing so quietly you have to lean in. Aldo knew two of them on the wall. He says it is the only music he has heard that sounds like a night watch.","rv_vote":"The Lonely Line Choir, though he expects them to come last again and thinks that is honourable."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Bennet Kellway, who was a garrison officer and talks slowly, and who Aldo says will never do anything in a hurry, which is what an old man wants from an elder.","edict":"Torn. He served beside Lightforge wall-auditors at Highmarch who were honest to the bone, and he cannot square them with this. He says Stonehold should send someone to Meridian to ask before it refuses, and is shouted down at the bar for it every evening."},"wants":"To see Thornwick’s hedge get a new stem from every Realm again before he dies, because the old ones are rotting.","site":"thornwick","town":"Thornwick","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:flintmoor":{"name":"Bettrin Echoway","age":44,"role":"trader, fog-guide across the moor, and Flintmoor’s go-between with Spiritveil","look":"Long-faced and pale from the fog, hair in a single grey-streaked braid tied with Spiritveil ribbon, peat-black boots to the knee. Carries a coil of guide-rope even indoors.","voice":"Quiet and dry, slips into slow Lanthic vowels when tired; pauses before telling you a price, as if checking it against the fog.","greet":"Set. You came over the moor? With the fog in? Then you’re either lucky or you followed the lamp. Sit down either way.","lines":["The archivists from Echomere were here again last week with a cart and a very polite letter. The lamp is still on its post. The cart went home empty.","When the fog’s in I charge two tolls a head to guide you over and one for the dog. People complain until they see the Sink.","Flintmoor flints are the best in the Nine. Flameborne says so while it haggles. You can tell a true thing by who’s cross about it."],"about":"My family’s first marker is on the Echomere road, which is why we’re Echoways and why I talk like half a Spiritveil clerk. I sell strike-kits and guide people over the moor. I’ve lost nobody. I say that every time because the day I stop saying it I’ll have lost somebody.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"good spoken, cannot read the script"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"}],"childhood":"Bettrin was raised by her grandmother on the moor’s edge in a turf house so low you entered it on your knees. Her parents were fog-guides on the Echomere crossing, and in the worst winters she did not see them for weeks. Her grandmother, Old Ysa Echoway, taught her to read the moor: the white tufts that meant firm ground, the green that meant death, the way the fog thinned a breath before it lifted. Bettrin was a watchful child who preferred fog to clear days because it made the world small enough to hold. At nine she walked her first traveller over, a Spiritveil copyist who was so frightened he held her hand the whole way and gave her a honey cake at the end. She still remembers the taste.","schooling":"No road-school for her; the moor has no proper Long Road, only the Echomere track, and the nearest school was at Anvil Reach. Her grandmother taught her letters from marker-stones and her father taught her Lanthic numbers so she could price a crossing. At her Lamp Test, taken at Anvil Reach at eight, the Veil lamp flickered once and went dark, which upset everyone and meant nothing; she was spark-poor. She learned more from the copyist’s letters, which he sent for years, than from anybody.","history":"She took over her parents’ crossing at nineteen when they moved to Echomere for her father’s chest, and they never came back to the moor, and she never went to live with them. She guided for twenty years and opened the shop at thirty-nine when her knees began to talk to her. In between, she married a knapper, Coll Flintsey, Old Marrin’s younger son, and they had a daughter who died at two of a fever that came in with the fog. Bettrin does not speak about it, and she lights the Bog Lamp on her household’s Turn with great care, and on those nights she sits with the borrowed flint in her lap until dawn rather than sleeping. She and Coll parted without anger after eleven years; he lives three doors down and they still eat together on Marker Day. When the Echomere archivists began coming for the lamp she was the one who could talk to them in their own tongue, so she has become the village’s voice in the quarrel, which she did not ask for and does well.","family":"Former husband Coll Flintsey, knapper, a friend. Her parents live in Echomere. Her daughter Lia’s initials are on a marker on the Echomere road, cut very small.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"The first lantern was a cracked clay cup somebody refused to throw away. Bettrin thinks that is the whole argument for leaving the Bog Lamp where it is."},"band":{"act":"The Relay Runners","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"The copyist sent her a scratched song-sheet of ‘Nothing Worth Keeping’ when she was twelve and she sang it walking the moor until her grandmother begged her to stop.","rv_vote":"The Relay Runners, because Echomere will be voting for Mirel of the Last Lamp and she will not be bullied."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Morwen Sallowmile, who wants Stonehold to talk to Spiritveil before deciding anything. Bettrin thinks Spiritveil’s warning about the Edict should be heard by somebody who can understand it.","edict":"Against. She has guided Spiritveil folk who were frightened of Lightforge audits for reasons they would not say in Bridge, and she believed them."},"wants":"To settle the lamp quarrel so that Echomere gets something and Flintmoor keeps everything.","site":"flintmoor","town":"Flintmoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:flintmoor":{"name":"Tharn Sinkway","age":27,"role":"smith of steel strikers, fire-kit maker, failed Conclave student","look":"Neither man nor woman, compact and square-handed, peat-dark hair cropped close. The first two fingers of the left hand grey to the second knuckle, kept in a leather finger-stall they take off for no one.","voice":"Short sentences, precise; corrects themselves mid-word; laughs rarely and suddenly, like a strike.","greet":"Set. Striker? Flint’s next door at Marrin’s. Steel’s here. You need both. Everyone forgets that.","lines":["A flint without a steel is a rock. A steel without a flint is a bit of old iron. Flintmoor sells you both separately on purpose. We’re not stupid.","Somebody from Lightforge was through last month asking how many Full Sparks live on the moor. Nobody here can count. That was the answer he got.","I went to Korrhaven for a year and a half. The porridge was the best thing about it. That tells you."],"about":"I make strikers. I’m good at it. I was a Full Spark at the Gravem Tower for three terms and then I came home. Call me Tharn. That’s all of it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"Conclave-fluent, careful"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"can read the evidential endings and hates them"}],"childhood":"Tharn was born a week after the worst fog in fifty years and named for the hollow in the moor where the midwife had to wait it out. Their mother was a peat-cutter, their father a smith, both huge, cheerful and forgetful, and Tharn grew up the tidy one in an untidy house, arranging the forge tools by length while the adults lost them. They never cared to be either a boy or a girl and Flintmoor, after one argument at a Marker Day picnic between two great-aunts, stopped asking. They loved the moment a striker meets flint more than anything: the bright little tear of light. At six they stole their father’s steel and a flint and sat in the peat-shed striking sparks for an afternoon until the shed nearly caught, and their mother, instead of shouting, sat down beside them and watched.","schooling":"Lamp Test at seven at Anvil Reach, where the Gravem lamp came up so bright the examiner put her hand over it. Three Lamp Years at the Anvil Reach spark-school under Master Oda Hewn, who made every child hold a marker-chip in each palm for an hour a day and say aloud when they felt the stone in their hands growing heavier. Then Korrhaven at thirteen, the Gravem Tower. Tharn learned to make a stone weigh twice itself and to count their own grey nails every night.","history":"At Korrhaven they were one of the best in the year, which meant they were set the hardest drills, and in the third term the grey crept from the fingertip to the second knuckle of two fingers in a single winter. The tutors said it was within bounds. Tharn counted, and kept counting, and did not believe the bounds were honest. They wrote home asking whether a person could leave. Their father wrote back one line: ‘The forge is cold without you.’ They left at the end of the term with one knot in their grey sash and have not used a spark since except, once, to hold a collapsing peat-bank long enough to pull a child out, which cost them a nail and which they will not discuss. They took over the forge when their father’s heart went bad, and now make the steel strikers that every Flintmoor kit is sold with, and are known as far as Hearthward for strikers that never chip. They have a sweetheart in Anvil Reach, a cake-baker, and walk there every second Marker Day. When the Lightforge man came asking about Full Sparks this season, Tharn was the one who told him nobody on the moor could count, and the village has been delighted with them since.","family":"Father Haldor Sinkway, retired, sits by the forge criticising; mother Grete, still cutting peat at sixty. Sweetheart Bel in Anvil Reach, who bakes marker cakes.","hero":{"who":"The Silent Sentinel","why":"Turned to stone at the jaw and still in the Senate, speaking by slates. Tharn says it is the only honest picture of what Gravem costs that anyone in Stonehold will put in front of the Senate."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Their drummer’s hands are going to stone and he keeps playing. Tharn saw them at Anvil Reach and stood at the front with their finger-stall off for the whole set, and nobody looked.","rv_vote":"Burden, every year, until the drummer stops."},"vote":{"local":"Stands behind no elder. Tharn goes to the Senate and stands at the very back where the voices blend, because ‘every voice at once’ means every voice, not every elder’s.","edict":"Against, without any doubt. A register of sparks means somebody in Meridian with a book saying how many fingers Tharn has left, and Tharn already keeps that book."},"wants":"To make one perfect striker, and to never be counted by anyone but themself.","site":"flintmoor","town":"Flintmoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:flintmoor":{"name":"Nessa Flintsey","age":12,"role":"apprentice knapper, Old Marrin Flintsey’s granddaughter","look":"Small and sharp-elbowed, flint-dust grey in her eyebrows, fingers wrapped in rag where the flakes have cut her. Carries three throwing stones in her pocket at all times.","voice":"Breathless, boastful, keeps asking whether you saw that.","greet":"Set! Did you see Grandad throw? He struck a spark at forty paces last wedding. I can do six paces. Seven when nobody’s watching.","lines":["It was our Turn on the lamp last night. I carried the spare flint home and slept with it in my hand. It was warm in the morning. Mam says that’s me, not the flint.","If the fog comes in, follow the lamp. Not me. I know the moor, but I lie in fog too. Everyone does. Grandad says it’s the moor doing it.","The archive people want our lady’s lamp. They can’t have her. She came to us."],"about":"I’m a knapper. Nearly. I knock flints into strikers and Grandad says I waste one in three, which is better than Dad did. I’m going to strike a spark at forty paces before I’m fifteen. Watch.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, mostly prices"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"three words of greeting from the fog-travellers"}],"childhood":"There is not much of Nessa’s childhood behind her yet, but all of it has been loud. She is the middle of five Flintsey cousins who live in two houses joined by a knapping-shed, and from the time she could stand she was under the bench picking up flakes. She was a fierce, show-off small girl who learned to throw stones before she learned to read, and broke four windows doing it, two of them her grandfather’s. He made her pay for each with a week of sorting flint by colour. She liked it. She now knows the black from the honey from the banded grey at a glance. Her best friend is a peat-cutter’s boy who is afraid of the Sink, and she takes him past it every day on purpose until he isn’t.","schooling":"She goes three days a week to Bettrin Echoway’s kitchen, where the trader teaches the moor children letters off old guide-tallies and numbers by pricing crossings. Bettrin once made them all walk blindfold from the shop to the Bog Lamp holding a rope, so they would know how far it was in the dark. Nessa’s Lamp Test, last year at Anvil Reach: nothing lit. She cried on the cart home and then threw a stone at a crow and struck a spark off the road, and stopped.","history":"Nessa’s ‘history’, she will tell you, is that she has been on the Turn rota since she was eight, lighting the Bog Lamp for her household one dusk in every sixty, and has never once let it go out, and has once lit it in a gale with her coat over her head and her cousin holding her ankles. She has been to Anvil Reach four times and Echomere once, where an archivist offered her a honey cake and asked her very nicely what she thought of the lamp, and she said it was hers and the lady’s and walked off with the cake. She has thrown stones at three weddings, struck sparks at two. She has been told off by every adult in Flintmoor at least twice and by her grandfather more often than that, and every one of those times he laughed afterwards when he thought she was gone.","family":"Grandfather Marrin Flintsey, the famous thrower. Father Jory, a decent knapper. Mother Wenn, who guides on the moor in spring. Four cousins and an uncle, Coll, who used to be married to the trader.","hero":{"who":"Old Marrin Flintsey, her grandfather","why":"He can hit a flint at forty paces with a thrown stone and make a spark come out of it. She thinks that is better than any spark-magic because anyone could learn it and almost nobody does."},"band":{"act":"Seismic","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Her cousin brought a bass-pipe back from Anvil Reach and played ‘Aftershock’ in the knapping-shed until the flint stacks fell over. That is what she wants music to do.","rv_vote":"Seismic, and she wants them to win the Sparkbeat Cup too, and has bet three throwing stones on it."},"vote":{"local":"Too young for the Senate. She says she would stand behind her grandad, who stands behind Elder Hask Orrowgate, ‘because Hask shouts and so does Grandad’.","edict":"Against, because everyone she knows is. When asked what the Edict is, she says ‘Lightforge wanting to count us’, and that is close enough."},"wants":"To strike a spark at forty paces in front of the whole village.","site":"flintmoor","town":"Flintmoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:flintmoor:b":{"name":"Cobb Moorgate","age":66,"role":"peat-cutter, and the man Flintmoor sends to pull things out of the Sink","look":"Huge, slope-shouldered, face like a peat bank, eyebrows that meet. Peat-brown to the elbows. Walks with a long probing-pole he calls his wife’s brother.","voice":"Rumbling, cheerful, morbid; tells you the worst thing first and then laughs.","greet":"Set. Don’t go near the Sink. I’ve had three out of it in my life and two of them were dead and one of them was a cow.","lines":["The Echomere lot want to move the lamp. Let them try carrying it across the moor in fog. I’ll come along. I’ll bring the pole.","Peat keeps everything. I’ve dug up a boot with the foot still in it. Five hundred years old, the archivists said. Lovely stitching.","My Alda says the moor tells lies in the fog. She would know. She tells the best lies on the moor and she’s never once been in fog."],"about":"I cut peat and I pull people out of bogs. I’m good at the first one and better at the second. I’ve been married forty-four years to the cleverest liar in Stonehold and I believe nearly everything she tells me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native, very broad Moor accent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"bad, by choice"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a little, from a slag-hill cousin of his mother’s"}],"childhood":"Cobb was a giant baby and a giant boy, and from the age of seven his father put a peat-spade in his hands and stood back. He grew up cutting the black turfs in long wet rows and stacking them to dry, and learned early that the moor gives up odd things if you cut deep enough: a bronze pin, a wooden bowl, a hand. His mother was half Metalcrown, the daughter of a slag-hill tinker who had come west and married, and she kept her father’s First Tool, a tiny hammer, on a shelf and never explained it. Cobb used to take it down and look at it. Other boys teased him for the slag-hill blood. He was too big to tease twice.","schooling":"Road-school at Anvil Reach for two winters under Mistress Gudrun Hale, who taught letters by making the children chip their initials into old marker offcuts and kept the best one. She kept his. His Lamp Test, at eight: no lamp answered. He left school at ten because the peat needed him and has never regretted it, though he can read slowly and says so proudly.","history":"He has cut peat on Flintmoor for fifty-five years. At twenty-two he married Alda Turfholme, who could talk a traveller into buying a flint they had already bought, and they had four children, all grown, two of them gone to Gravenhall. At twenty-six he pulled his first body from the Sink, a carter who had ignored the lamp, and the village decided he was the man for it because he was the only one heavy enough not to be dragged in. He has been out to the Sink with his pole and his ropes some forty times since and brought back three: the carter, a cow, and a Spiritveil boy of ten, alive, who now writes to him from Willowmere every Long Evening. Eleven years ago, cutting deep at the Sink’s north edge, he found a mile-marker lying flat in the peat, perfectly kept, with no initials on it at all. Blank. He dug it out, looked at it a long while, and dug it back in. He has told Alda and nobody else.","family":"Wife Alda, forty-four years. Four children, nine grandchildren. A cousin in the slag-hills he has never met but sends peat to every winter.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"She led the Unmarked out to the slag-hills with nothing and made a hammer that was ugly and worked. His grandfather was one of hers. Cobb says it at the bar and enjoys watching who goes quiet."},"band":{"act":"Mason Grey","realm":"Stonehold","why":"A quarry baritone who sings like a man shifting stone. Alda and Cobb danced to him at their fortieth anniversary and Cobb stood on her foot for the whole song and she did not mention it.","rv_vote":"Mason Grey, though he thinks Metalcrown has never had a fair lamp and might throw one to Shift Change to annoy his neighbours."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Hask Orrowgate, who wants Stonehold to refuse the Edict outright, because Hask is loud and Cobb trusts a loud man not to have a quiet plan.","edict":"Against, loudly and for ever. He says the Holds have been told whose names are worth cutting once before, and look what grew in the slag-hills."},"wants":"For someone to ask him a question about the moor he doesn’t know the answer to.","site":"flintmoor","town":"Flintmoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:anvilreach":{"name":"Maud Threeways","age":39,"role":"town trader, outfitter to the road-crews, and organiser of the Long-Name Purse","look":"Neat, small, very upright, ink on her right thumb and granite dust on her left. A cord of chipped marker-stones round her neck, one for every family who has paid into her purse.","voice":"Brisk, organised, quietly furious; counts things on her fingers while she argues.","greet":"Set. Road-crew kit on the left, travellers on the right, complaints about the Senate at the back. That’s where I am most of the day.","lines":["The Senate pays the carvers by the letter for the poor dead. So the short names get cut first. My husband’s grandmother has been on a waiting slate for two years because her name has fourteen letters.","Count the markers going west. If one’s missing it’s here being recut. Spelling. The families always notice. Always.","Chisels in from the forge this morning, mallets from Sedgemoor’s frame-makers. Everything a crew needs. Except a fair wage, but I don’t stock that."],"about":"My family’s first marker is where three roads meet outside the town, so we’re Threeways. I sell to road-crews and I run a purse that pays for long names the Senate won’t. I’m not a nice woman. I’m a fair one, which annoys people more.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, reads Senate rolls"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"reads it slowly; learned from Metalcrown pamphlets"}],"childhood":"Maud grew up above her mother’s marker-cake bakery on the Anvil, the second of two sisters, in a house where the chisels across the square woke her before the birds did. Her father was a road-crew leveller who came home at dusk grey to the eyebrows with dust and fell asleep at the table mid-sentence most nights. She was the child who added up the bakery takings when her mother was too tired, and found, at nine, that a neighbour had been short-paying them for three years. She did not tell her mother. She went round and told the neighbour, in front of his wife, and came home with the money. Her mother was horrified and then baked her a cake with her own initials on it, which she was not allowed to eat because that is for the dead, and which sat on the shelf until it went green.","schooling":"Road-school on the Western Road under Master Peder Coldcut, who walked them ten markers out and ten back every day and made them copy one name from each marker into a slate book, so that by twelve Maud had copied two thousand dead. Lamp Test at eight: nothing lit. She was the best in her road-school at letters and was offered a Bridge clerkship at Gravenhall at fourteen, and turned it down to stay with the bakery.","history":"At twenty she married Arne Underhalmsway, a road-crew foreman from a family whose name is so long the carvers sigh when they see it coming. His grandmother died poor two years ago and the Senate’s letter-pay has kept her on the waiting slate ever since. Maud started the Long-Name Purse that winter: every family with a name over ten letters puts in a toll a month, and the purse pays the carvers to cut the longest waiting names first. There are two hundred families in it now. The carvers like it, the Senate’s clerk at Anvil Reach hates it, and Maud has been to Gravenhall twice to argue it at the Hold-Senate and been told twice, kindly, that every voice is heard. She took over the outfitters’ shop at thirty when its old owner went to live with his daughter. She and Ivo have three boys, all loud, and she has taught each of them to add up a bill before they could read one. Her sister Bel bakes the cakes now.","family":"Husband Arne Underhalmsway, road-crew foreman. Three sons, eleven, nine and six. Sister Bel, who bakes the town’s marker cakes and is walking out with the smith of Flintmoor.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"‘One shift, one vote. Even you.’ Maud has those words chalked on the back of her shop door. She knows exactly how it sounds for a Stonehold woman to admire the last foreman of the Metalcrown Commons, and that is partly why."},"band":{"act":"Shift Change","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Three singers who never stand on a stage together, each one doing a third of the song. She thinks it is the best picture of fair work she has ever heard.","rv_vote":"Shift Change, and she will say loudly at the market that it is time Metalcrown got a lamp from someone."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Ulla Kestergate, who has said in the Senate that a name should cost the same however many letters it has. Maud will believe it when Ulla votes for it.","edict":"Against. She says if Lightforge is going to count every spark it had better first learn to count every dead Stonehold pauper properly, and she has the figures."},"wants":"For the Senate to pay by the name, not the letter, before her husband’s grandmother has been waiting three years.","site":"anvilreach","town":"Anvil Reach","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:anvilreach":{"name":"Ebrin Gravenmile","age":52,"role":"master chisel-smith, temperer of carving steel for the whole town","look":"Lean and grey, leather apron burned through in a pattern like a map, spectacles of Lightforge glass pushed up on his forehead. Clean hands; he washes them between every job.","voice":"Pedantic, exact, oddly gentle; says ‘strictly’ a great deal.","greet":"Set. If you’ve come for a chisel, tell me the stone, the letter and the hand. If you don’t know all three, you don’t need a chisel yet.","lines":["Ebba’s Marker? I’m her line, for my sins. Nine generations. And strictly speaking she was wrong. A marker is for the dead. You don’t cut a living man’s name. Love doesn’t change the rule.","Every chisel I temper is used on a name. I think about that. A bad temper and someone’s grandmother has a chipped H for five hundred years.","There’s a Lightforge glazier passes through who sells me lenses. Honest man. Prices on the outside of the box. I like Lightforge. I don’t like what it’s doing."],"about":"I make chisels. I make them for every carver in Anvil Reach, and every name on the Long Roads from the Anvil to Gravenhall has been cut with my steel or my master’s. I like rules. People think that makes me cold. It makes me reliable.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it well, speaks it stiffly; loves the evidential endings"}],"childhood":"Ebrin was a careful, fearful boy in a family of carvers who all assumed he would carve. He was terrified of cutting a letter wrong, and at nine, set his first practice name on an offcut, he cut the first stroke and could not make the second. He sat with the chisel in his hand until dark. His grandmother, a great carver, found him there and did not scold him. She took him across the square to the forge and put him on the bellows, and he worked them until midnight and was happy for the first time that year. He has been at the forge since. His family still call him ‘the one who couldn’t’, fondly, and he does not mind, because he can make the thing they cannot work without.","schooling":"Road-school on the Gravenhall Road under Mistress Annet Fell, who taught letter-shapes by drawing them in the dust with the butt of a chisel and made the children walk on them so they would learn where the stroke started. Lamp Test at eight: the Gravem lamp gave a faint warmth but no light. Half-Spark, too weak to train. He was best in the school at the forms of letters and worst at writing anything of his own.","history":"He apprenticed to Master Holt Grane at twelve and became the best temperer in the district at thirty. Master Holt died when Ebrin was thirty-four and left him the forge and an apology in a sealed slate, which Ebrin read once and threw in the quench. He married late, at forty, a widowed carver named Tilde Anvilroad, and gained two stepdaughters who carve and argue with him about Ebba every single Marker Day. He writes letters to the Lightforge glazier who sells him spectacles, long letters in his stiff Clear about tempering, and the glazier writes back about glass. They have never met. He keeps the letters in a box marked ‘Correspondence (Technical)’. When the Radiant Edict was first read out at the Anvil Reach market, Ebrin was the only man in the square who asked to see the text. He read the whole thing, there, standing, and handed it back and said it was well-drafted and wrong in its third clause, and walked home.","family":"Wife Tilde, a carver. Stepdaughters Anja and Sif, carvers, who think Ebba was right. His own line goes back to Ebba, and he is not proud of the bit everyone is proud of.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"‘Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.’ He thinks it is the only rule anyone ever made that works on everyone at once."},"band":{"act":"Verdict","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Symphonic metal built like a trial, with exhibits and a sentence. His stepdaughters think it is hilarious that he loves it. He follows each album like a case.","rv_vote":"Verdict. He knows Lightforge wins too much. He does not think that is a reason to vote against a well-made thing."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Morwen Sallowmile, who wants Stonehold to negotiate the Edict clause by clause rather than refuse it whole.","edict":"Torn, leaning towards a narrow yes. He thinks a register of Gravem masters kept by Stonehold itself, recording how far the stone has crept, would save lives. He thinks Lightforge keeping it would not."},"wants":"For somebody to admit, once, at a Marker Day picnic, that Ebba broke the rule.","site":"anvilreach","town":"Anvil Reach","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:anvilreach":{"name":"Hennet Longcut","age":23,"role":"apprentice carver and marker-leveller on a road-crew","look":"Stocky and sunburned, grey dust in the creases of her eyes so she looks permanently amused, which she is. A spirit-level tied round her neck on a cord like a pendant.","voice":"Quick, teasing, a laugh like a dropped bucket; tells everything as if it is the funniest thing that has ever happened to her.","greet":"Set! Going west? Count the markers. I set eleven of them this summer. If any are crooked it was the other crew.","lines":["Short names first, long names later. That’s the Senate’s money. There’s a man waiting called Ossenbrandt Underhallowsby. He’ll be waiting till the roads wear out.","I asked a girl from Sedgemoor to the Marker Day picnic and she said yes, and then I found out she meant her whole family. I’d cut a marker cake. It had my initials on it. By accident. On a marker cake. That’s for the dead. She’s still laughing.","Durra Kesterrow carves left-handed now and it’s neater than my right. I want to be angry about that and I can’t."],"about":"I level markers and cut the small letters the masters don’t want. Mostly I’m out on the road with a crew, sleeping in the cart, setting stones so they stand straight for five hundred years. I like that something I did will still be standing when I’m a chip in somebody’s pocket.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"cheerful and bad"},{"tongue":"Seismic taps","level":"good; crews talk through the road"}],"childhood":"Hennet was the only daughter of a quarry-wife and a road-crew cook, the youngest of four, and spent her early years riding in crew-carts up and down the Long Roads with her father, eating stew off a shovel. She learned to sleep anywhere, eat anything, and talk to anyone, and by six she had friends in Gravenhall, Brask and two Flameborne carters who taught her to swear. Her mother wanted her in the quarry. Her father wanted her in the kitchen. She wanted to be in the cart, so she was. She was a laughing, scrapping child with one front tooth knocked out by a mule, who has been told she should get it replaced for twenty years and has not, because she likes whistling through the gap.","schooling":"She did road-school the proper way, on the march: Master Jory Talmer’s school travelled with the summer road-crews and taught letters from whatever markers they were setting. He made each child sit beside a new-set marker and read its name aloud to it, ‘so the dead know somebody can read them’. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach: nothing lit. She did not care in the least.","history":"She joined her first crew at fourteen as a water-girl and was levelling by sixteen. She did her Shouldering at fifteen with a marker that had a crack in it, so that she carried it the whole mile terrified it would split, and when she set it down it did split, neatly, into two halves, and the crew-master laughed so hard he declared it good luck and had both halves set as the first and last markers of the next road-mile. She has been on Durra Kesterrow’s crew for four years and is learning to cut the small trailing letters of long names, which nobody else wants. This summer, setting markers on the Western Road, she cut her own initials, very small, on the underside of a marker she set, where nobody will see them unless the stone falls. She has not decided if that was a joke. She is walking out, nervously, with Col Reedholt, a Sedgemoor weaver and old Tamsa’s grandson, and has bought a sling from him she does not need.","family":"Parents still on the road with a crew. Three older brothers, two quarrymen and one in the levy that went east this season.","hero":{"who":"Ebba the carver","why":"Ebba cut a living man’s name because he asked her to, and walked past it every day for forty years. Hennet thinks that is the most romantic thing anyone in Stonehold has ever done, and cannot believe the carvers are still arguing about it."},"band":{"act":"Warmstone","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Sultry hot-spring dance music. She first heard it in the crew cart in a hailstorm and danced on the tailgate until she fell off.","rv_vote":"Warmstone. She also wants to give a lamp to Seismic for the Sparkbeat Cup and has been told you cannot vote twice, and is checking."},"vote":{"local":"She has never been to the Senate. If she went, she would stand behind whoever Durra stands behind, because Durra has been grey to the elbow since thirty and still carves.","edict":"Against, mostly. She is a little embarrassed that she has not thought about it as hard as everyone else and listens closely when it comes up, and nods."},"wants":"For Col to come to a Marker Day picnic without the whole of Sedgemoor.","site":"anvilreach","town":"Anvil Reach","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:anvilreach:b":{"name":"Odo Lettermile","age":74,"role":"retired Letter-Counter: the clerk who tallies letters for the Senate’s pay to carvers","look":"Tiny and round, bald as a marker-top, very clean cuffs, an abacus of marker-chips on a string he still fidgets with. Wears his old clerk’s grey cord although he no longer has to.","voice":"Dry, soft, apologetic; produces exact numbers nobody asked for and then apologises for them.","greet":"Set. Oh, you’re not here about a name? Good. I’m retired from names. I’m only here for the bench. It gets the sun at this hour.","lines":["I counted letters for the Senate for forty-one years. Two million, six hundred and some thousand. I can’t stop. I count your name when you say it. Seven, if you’re wondering.","Maud Threeways and her Purse. She’s right, you know. I told the Senate so in my last report. They filed it. Very carefully.","My life has been dull. I know it has. I counted it once. It came to forty-one years of letters and one good wife. I’d do it again."],"about":"I was the Letter-Counter. Every name cut for the poor dead in the Anvil Reach district, I counted, and the Senate paid for. I never cut a letter in my life. I’m very proud of that. Somebody has to just count.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, clerkly"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it for Senate correspondence"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few apologies, learned for a reason"}],"childhood":"Odo was born to a family of pauper carvers who cut names for the Senate’s pay and were always short of coin, and by eight he had noticed that the pay came in by the letter and that his father cut long names faster to earn more. He thought that was cheating and said so, at the dinner table. His father laughed and gave him the tally to keep, and he kept it from that night on: every letter his father cut, in chalk on the back of the door, totalled weekly. The family had never been so well paid. He was a small, polite, invisible child, the kind teachers forget to call on, and he liked it that way, because it left him time to count.","schooling":"Road-school under Master Peder Coldcut, the same who later taught Maud Threeways; even then he made his pupils copy names from markers, and Odo was the only child who also counted the letters and wrote the total underneath. Lamp Test at eight: nothing. He was offered the Letter-Counter’s apprenticeship at thirteen by the old Counter, who had seen the door.","history":"He was Letter-Counter for Anvil Reach from twenty-nine until seventy. He counted every name, refused every bribe (there were a great many), and once spent a winter re-counting the whole of the Western Road because a carver had claimed the Senate owed him for an apostrophe. Odo ruled that an apostrophe is not a letter. The carver has not spoken to him since and the ruling stands. At thirty-six, on Senate business in Gravenhall, he found in the archive an old account of the Great Gale and what the Holds did to Korr’s Span, and the eleven hundred dead at Brightwater, and he went home and could not eat for a week. He has never been to Stormreach. He learned a few shouted Gale apologies from a rider at Hearthward and says them quietly to himself on Marker Day. He married Elsa Moss, a laundress, at forty, and she died last spring. He had her name cut by the Purse, not the Senate, and paid double.","family":"Wife Elsa, dead since spring. No children. A nephew in Brask who writes at Marker Day and spells Odo’s name wrong, which Odo counts as affection.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"The Warden who would not cut his citadel’s chain. Odo read it in a Senate archive and has thought about a man refusing to let go ever since. He knows it is an odd hero for a Stonehold clerk. He keeps it to himself mostly."},"band":{"act":"Plain Speaking","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Country songs with no metaphors in them. He says he can count every word and they all mean what they say. Elsa used to hum one to annoy him.","rv_vote":"Plain Speaking. He has calculated they have a one in thirty chance and thinks that is about right."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind no elder; he stood forty years in the Senate hall as a clerk and says the whole point of ‘every voice at once’ is that the clerk should be one of the voices, and that is where he stands.","edict":"Against, with a footnote. He says Stonehold should refuse it, and should also write down exactly why in a document of no more than three hundred words, because he will count them."},"wants":"To read a Stonehold apology for Brightwater cut on a marker on the Stormreach border, and count its letters.","site":"anvilreach","town":"Anvil Reach","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:stonebarrow":{"name":"Ingrith Barrowfoot","age":61,"role":"trader, shoulder-pad maker, retired bearer","look":"Big-boned and soft now, knees bound in felt, a bearer’s callus like a saddle on her right shoulder. Spectacles on a cord, a sewing awl behind one ear, and a laugh that arrives before she does.","voice":"Warm, gossipy, motherly; calls everyone ‘pet’ in Holdspeech, which is ‘sett-ren’, and doesn’t know the -ren is Kindle.","greet":"Set, pet. You’ve a pack strap rubbing, I can see it from here. Sit down. I’ll pad it. Then you can buy something.","lines":["Don’t step in front of the Hum at dawn and don’t step away from it. Just stand. I’ve seen a Lightforge man run from it like it was a fire. The bearers were very hurt.","Anvil Reach says the carvers walk the first mile first on Marker Day. They cut the names. We carried the names there. Whose name do you think gets to the road first?","I’ve been married forty-one years to a man who snores like a landslide. I don’t sleep. I don’t mind."],"about":"I carried for twenty-eight years until my knees said no, and they said it in front of everybody, halfway down the road with a councillor’s mother on our shoulders. Now I make the pads. Every bearer in the district has my stitching on them. That’s near enough to carrying.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, with Stonebarrow vowels"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a handful of words she thinks are Holdspeech"}],"childhood":"Ingrith was born in the low house nearest the barrow and grew up on stories of the nineteen-day Carry of the 1,113th winter, in which her mother, then eighteen, had borne in relays through knee-deep snow and come home with frostbitten toes she lost two of. Her mother told it as a funny story: the toes, the cold porridge, a councillor’s son who fainted. Ingrith was a big, happy, greedy child who ate bearer’s bread off other people’s neck-cords at funerals and was forgiven every time. She loved the Hum more than anything and could hum every bearer’s version by ten and tell you whose it was by the third note. She played at Carries with her cousins using a sack of turnips for the dead, and was always the one who insisted on humming.","schooling":"Road-school on the Barrow Road under Master Fenn Ashcairn, a retired bearer who taught letters by having the children shoulder a light plank between them and chant the alphabet in step, so that a mistake made somebody stumble. Lamp Test at eight: nothing lit. She was average at letters, very good at songs, and walked the plank better than anyone.","history":"She took her mother’s place among the six at seventeen when her mother’s feet finally went, and carried for twenty-eight years. She learned her Hum from her mother and changed one note, which her mother called vandalism and then hummed herself. She married Harl Barrowfoot at twenty, a quarryman from up the road, at a hearthstone wedding where he brought a rock with a fossil fern in it, and she has never forgiven him for bringing a better rock than her. They have two sons and a daughter; the daughter, Ottie, carries now. Ingrith’s knees went at forty-five, in front of everybody, with a councillor’s mother on the bier, and she did not set it down; she went down on one knee with her shoulder still under it until a bearer from the next relay ran in. She took the shop over from her aunt at forty-six and began making the pads because she could not bear to be away from them. Her pads are sold to bearers in every Hold now. She cries at the start of every Carry she sees, and laughs at herself for it, and nobody in Stonebarrow thinks less of her.","family":"Husband Harl, retired quarryman. Sons Aske and Berrin, a quarryman and a carter. Daughter Ottie, a bearer of the six. Seven grandchildren.","hero":{"who":"Saelen of the Drift","why":"The Lantern of the Dead in Spiritveil, who saw to the lamps of those with nobody. A traveller told her the story and she says it is the same job as hers, done in a different light."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Forty-year love songs. She and Harl have been married forty-one years and she says Spiritveil is the only Realm that writes songs long enough for a real marriage.","rv_vote":"Decades. She thinks Spiritveil gets passed over because nobody else lives long enough to finish listening."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Bennet Kellway because his mother was carried by her six and he came to the barrow afterwards and sat on the ground beside the Bearers’ Bench and not on it. That was enough for Ingrith.","edict":"Against. She says Stonehold carries its own dead and can count its own sparks."},"wants":"To see Stonebarrow and Anvil Reach walk the first mile on Marker Day side by side, carvers and bearers mixed, and to make the pads for it.","site":"stonebarrow","town":"Stonebarrow","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:stonebarrow":{"name":"Fennick Biermile","age":30,"role":"village smith, maker of bier-irons, sling-rings and the pins that hold the Carry together","look":"Slight and short, wiry as a spring, ears that stick out, a quick nervous grin. Forearms ropy with muscle that never seems to add up to size. Wears his forge apron short so it doesn’t drag.","voice":"Quick, defensive, very funny about himself before anyone else can be.","greet":"Set. Yes, I’m the smith. Yes, really. I know. I’ve heard it. Featherweight smith of the bearers’ village. Want a pin?","lines":["Every bier in the district rides on my irons. Two thousand dead have gone down that road on Gorm’s shoulders and my pins. Gorm gets the songs. I get the pins. That’s fine. Pins don’t sing.","They never picked me for the six. Too short. Under the bier your shoulder’s an inch off the line and the whole Carry leans. I measured it. I was right. I hate being right.","Brask Kell held Korr’s Span, and now he’s a Stillman that Stormreach riders throw stones at. One day I’m going up there with a broom."],"about":"I’m the smith. My father wanted a bearer and got me instead. I make the irons that bearers carry the dead on, which is close enough that I can stand in the line on Marker Day and not quite close enough that anybody lets me under the bier.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"halting; teaching himself from a rider’s phrase-slate"}],"childhood":"Fennick was the smallest boy in a village of big shoulders, born to a bearer father who was one of Gorm Hallit’s first six and a mother who mended slings. Everyone said he would grow. He did not grow. At ten he was shorter than his younger sister and at twelve he stopped hoping. He was clever, quick, sharp-mouthed, and learned early to make the joke first. He spent every spare hour in the old smith’s forge because the old smith, Brannagh Pinmile, was also small, and had been told for sixty years she would never lift a bier, and had made every iron the barrow ever used. She let him pump the bellows and called him ‘my other one’.","schooling":"Road-school on the Barrow Road under Master Ashcairn, the plank-walking one, who put Fennick at the end of the plank where his height threw everyone off and said nothing, and Fennick knew exactly why and loved him for it. Lamp Test at eight: the Gravem lamp gave a warm glow and nothing more; Half-Spark. He can make a bier-pin seat itself in iron a little harder, and it greys the tip of a nail, so he does it once a year on the most important pin.","history":"He did his Shouldering at fifteen with a marker-stone almost half his own size and set it down at the end of the mile with his nose bleeding and both knees shaking and the whole village silent, and Gorm Hallit walked over and said, ‘Carry them,’ to him, which is what Gorm says at the end of every mile, and Fennick went behind the barrow and cried for an hour. He apprenticed to Brannagh and took the forge when she died, at twenty-one. He made her bier-irons himself. He married nobody; he is walking out, slowly and comically, with a widowed carter from Sedgemoor who is a head taller and says she likes a man she can see over. In the last year, alone at night, he has been learning the Hum. Smiths do not hum. He knows that. He learned it by listening through the forge wall to bearers resting on the Bench, and he has a version of his own now, and no one to pass it to.","family":"Father Aske Biermile, retired bearer, kind and disappointed. A younger sister, Runa, who carries and is a head taller. Walking out with Gerd, a Sedgemoor carter.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"The anchor-master who held Korr’s Span, now a Stillman Stormreach riders throw stones at. Fennick thinks Brask held what he was told to hold and is being stoned for it longer after than anyone can count, and that someone should at least clean him."},"band":{"act":"Landslide","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Hard rock, very loud, very fast, and the singer is the shortest man on any stage at Korrhaven. Fennick has a scrap of their banner over his anvil.","rv_vote":"Landslide. He knows they will not win. He votes for them out of spite at everyone taller."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Dunmar Highcut, the shortest road-elder in the Senate, and says that is not why, and it is partly why.","edict":"Against, but worried: he says if Stonehold refuses outright the Senate will send the levy somewhere, and the levy needs biers, and the biers need pins."},"wants":"To carry one dead on the Carry, just one mile, under the bier, with the six.","site":"stonebarrow","town":"Stonebarrow","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:stonebarrow":{"name":"Hild Hallit","age":19,"role":"the newest bearer of the six, Gorm Hallit’s grand-niece","look":"Tall, round-faced, wide shoulders and a narrow waist, brown hair in two thick plaits pinned crossways so they don’t swing under a load. A brand-new shoulder-pad, Ingrith’s stitching, worn every day so it will soften.","voice":"Earnest and blunt, says exactly what she thinks, then worries she said it too loud.","greet":"Set. I’m a bearer. I’ve only been one since spring, so ask me again next year and I’ll sound surer.","lines":["You’ll hear the Hum at dawn on the road. Stand still. Don’t step towards it, don’t step away. It isn’t about you. It isn’t about us either. It’s about who’s on our shoulders.","My great-uncle Gorm has carried over two thousand. I’ve carried fourteen. He told me to stop counting. I can’t stop counting.","Anvil Reach carvers want to go first on Marker Day. They can go first. We’ll be right behind them. Humming. Loudly."],"about":"I replaced my uncle Tam in the six this spring when his back went. He taught me the Hum before he stopped. I’ve changed it a bit. Everybody changes it a bit. Mine has a little climb in the middle. Nobody’s said anything yet.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent"},{"tongue":"Seismic taps","level":"basic"}],"childhood":"Hild grew up the eldest of six children in a house built against the barrow’s flank, so that the dead were literally the back wall. Her parents were quarry-folk, not bearers, and the Hallit carrying line passed through her mother’s uncles; she was not expected to carry. She wanted to from the age of five, when she first saw Gorm come up the road at the head of the six with an old woman on the bier and the Hum coming off them like heat. She was a serious, organised child who ran her five siblings like a road-crew and made them do a pretend Carry with the youngest on a door every Marker Day until he was too heavy.","schooling":"Road-school on the Barrow Road under Mistress Ottie Barrowfoot, the trader’s daughter, who carries still and taught on her rest days and made the children walk the first mile of the Carry route counting their steps. Lamp Test at eight: nothing lit, which she was glad of, because she had heard a Gravem spark could grey a bearer’s shoulder and she wanted hers.","history":"She did her Shouldering at fifteen in a Sedgemoor sling and was not tired at the end and was cross about it, and the next week she carried her sister on her back for the same mile to prove a point to nobody. She spent four years as a relay-runner for the six, the one who runs ahead to warn the next village a Carry is coming, and learned every road-mile in the district and every family on it. When her great-uncle Tam’s back went this spring, the six chose her over two older cousins, which nobody expected and two cousins have not forgiven. She has carried fourteen dead. She learned the Hum from Tam in the last week before he stopped, sitting with him at night while he hummed it into her ear, and on her first Carry she changed it, without meaning to, putting in a small climbing figure she had got from a Deep Hold song. On her fourth Carry she realised Gorm was humming the climb too.","family":"Parents Wenn and Ostric Hallit, quarry-folk. Five younger siblings. Great-uncle Gorm Hallit, lead bearer. Great-uncle Tam, whose place she took. Two cousins who are not speaking to her.","hero":{"who":"Gorm Hallit, lead bearer","why":"He has carried two thousand and still hums under every one and still says ‘Carry them’ at the end of each mile as if it were the first. She wants to be still meaning it at two thousand."},"band":{"act":"Deep Hold","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Slow, heavy-flow rap that moves at the pace of a loaded walk. She learned the climb in her Hum from one of their verses and is terrified someone will notice.","rv_vote":"Deep Hold. ‘Ask who’s asking.’ She likes that it is a question and a threat at once."},"vote":{"local":"She has been to the Senate once, with the six, and stood behind Elder Hask Orrowgate, because Gorm did.","edict":"Against, strongly. She says Stonehold should not only refuse, it should walk out of the Concord hall till Lightforge takes it back, and has been told by her mother that this is how wars start."},"wants":"To carry two thousand dead and still hum at the last.","site":"stonebarrow","town":"Stonebarrow","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:stonebarrow:b":{"name":"Wyn Cairnway","age":47,"role":"cairn-builder and dry-stone waller, deaf since early childhood","look":"Thick-set, close-cropped greying hair, watchful pale eyes that read your mouth before you finish. Hands like spades, palms grey with lichen-dust. Barefoot on stone whenever he can be.","voice":"Speaks slowly in a flat, careful voice he cannot hear; mostly talks with his hands and with taps on whatever surface is nearest.","greet":"(He taps the table twice, then lays his palm flat on it.) Set. Talk to my face. I read lips. Not Bridge lips. Holdspeech lips.","lines":["(He presses his hand to the ground.) Carry coming. Far. Four miles. I feel the Hum in the road before anyone hears it. Six feet together. Nobody walks like that but them.","Cairn I built on the north mile? Nine hundred stones. No mortar. It’ll stand when the barrow’s gone. I told the barrow that. It didn’t answer.","The weavers from Sedgemoor cut rope-grass at Ravenscar. Ravenscar says the Stillman wouldn’t like it. The Stillman can’t hear either. Ask me what I don’t like."],"about":"I build cairns over the dead who go under, not to the road. And walls. I lost my hearing when I was three, from a fever, so the world talks to me through my feet. Stonebarrow is a good place for that. Everyone here is walking in step.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native by sight; speaks it in a flat careful voice"},{"tongue":"Seismic taps","level":"expert; the best tapper in the district"},{"tongue":"Hold home-signs","level":"fluent, his own family’s signs"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"reads it a little"}],"childhood":"Wyn heard until he was three and remembers none of it except, he says, a tune, which he thinks was the Hum. The fever that took his hearing came the same winter it took his twin brother, and his parents, quarry-folk, had one grief they could speak of and one they could not. They spoke to him with their hands because they had to and invented, over years, a whole family language: a fist on the chest for home, two fingers walking for the road, a palm pressed down for the dead. His mother put his hand on the barrow-stone when the six came past and he felt the Hum through it, and from then on whenever a Carry passed he lay flat on the road to feel it go. He was a calm, stubborn boy who stacked stones in perfect towers and knocked down anyone else’s that leaned.","schooling":"Road-school would not take him at first; the master said he could not learn by ear. Master Ashcairn went to his parents’ house and offered to teach him by taps, and did, two evenings a week for five years, rapping out letters on the table in the old seismic code until Wyn could read and write and tap faster than his teacher. Lamp Test at eight: nothing lit.","history":"He apprenticed to a dry-stone waller from Brask at fourteen who did not talk much either, and they got on perfectly. He walled the Barrow Road from the village to the Anvil, and built the first of his cairns at twenty, over a beggar who died at the roadside and who nobody would pay a marker for. He has built a hundred and twelve since, for those the Senate will not cut a name for, and he lays one stone in each for every year of the dead person’s life, if he can find out how long it was. He married Hedda Barrowell, a hearing woman who signs badly and cheerfully, when he was thirty, and they have a hearing daughter, Isolde, who is eleven and fluent in both tongues and corrects them both. He is the best tapper on the relay between Ravenscar and Stonebarrow and is sometimes asked to sit in for the tap-keeper there, and the ravens, he says, are rude to him.","family":"Wife Hedda. Daughter Isolde, eleven. His twin brother, dead at three; there is a cairn for him with three stones in it, the first Wyn ever built.","hero":{"who":"Orrin Who Stayed","why":"The first Anchorite, who stayed put on a cliff for those who would come back. Wyn builds things to stay put on purpose. He thinks that is all a cairn is."},"band":{"act":"The Stampers","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Their music is the crowd’s applause, and the stamping comes up through the floor. He went to one show at Anvil Reach and lay on the boards the whole time with his eyes shut.","rv_vote":"The Stampers. It is the only act he has ever heard all of."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Dunmar Highcut, the only road-elder who stopped mid-speech in the Senate to let a tap-message be read in, and waited.","edict":"Against. He says a register will be written in Clear and spoken in Bridge and nobody will think to tap it for people like him, and then they will say he didn’t object."},"wants":"For the Senate to pay for a name on the road for every cairn he has built.","site":"stonebarrow","town":"Stonebarrow","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:highmarch_vf1":{"name":"Juna Wallroad","age":36,"role":"trader in boots and straps, former quartermaster’s clerk on the Highmarch wall","look":"Tall, hawk-nosed, hair scraped back hard, the stiff-backed walk of the garrison. Immaculate boots. A bone tally-stick through her bun in place of a pin.","voice":"Clipped and even, never raises her voice; when she is angry she gets slower and more polite.","greet":"Set. Boots, straps, harness, packs. Measured or off the shelf. Off the shelf is cheaper and worse. Your choice.","lines":["I can’t smell the pits. Nobody in Oakmere can after a year. Brindle says that’s why we don’t believe them. They may be right. I won’t say so outside this shop.","Highmarch has ordered six hundred pairs of boots by First Frost. The levy went east and wore theirs through. Nobody’s told us why they went east. I’ve asked. I asked nicely.","Brindle hides are the best in Stonehold and Brindle knows it. They charge us double and complain about our water. I pay. I write it down."],"about":"I kept the stores on Highmarch’s wall for fifteen years and came down the year they made me a sergeant’s widow. Now I sell the boots I used to sign for. I am good at being calm. People take that as a sign I don’t mind things. I mind most things.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, quartermaster’s Bridge, all lists"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it well enough to check Lightforge invoices"}],"childhood":"Juna was born on the Highmarch wall itself, in a married-quarters room in the thickness of the stone, to a gate-sergeant and a wall-cook, and grew up among soldiers who were kind to her and drank too much. She learned that a loud man was usually harmless and a quiet one was worth watching, and became a quiet one. Her mother had a temper like a kettle and would throw ladles; her father would stand still until it was over. Juna decided early which parent she would be. She was an exact, tidy, private child who kept her treasures in a numbered box and knew if one had been touched. At ten she caught a supply-clerk stealing salt and reported him to the wall-captain in a written list, with dates.","schooling":"The garrison school on the wall, under Sergeant-Tutor Marta Ironside, who taught letters off the duty-rosters and drilled the children on the parapet at dawn whatever the weather; she once marched them through sleet for an hour because a child said it was too cold to learn. Lamp Test at eight, at Highmarch: nothing lit. She was the best in the school at lists, and the worst at songs.","history":"She went straight into the quartermaster’s stores at fourteen and ran them by twenty-two. At twenty-four she married Sergeant Callum Steepdown, a cheerful, careless man who lost his kit three times a year and made her laugh until she cried, which nobody else had ever done. He was killed at twenty-nine, not in battle, but when a wall-crane’s chain snapped in a frost and dropped a block. The chain had been ordered from Metalcrown, and the wrong grade had been sent, and Juna had signed for it without checking the stamp. She came down the mountain to Oakmere six months later and has never been back up. She bought the bootmaker’s shop when old Hamm Tanning died, and runs it as if it were still a garrison store. She has a Brindle woman’s cheese on her shelf, though she complains about the price. She is not unhappy. She sleeps badly and does the accounts at three in the morning.","family":"Her husband Callum, dead seven years; his initials are on a marker at Highmarch she has never visited. Her mother, still on the wall, throwing ladles at sixty-five.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder, the Banked Fire","why":"Torra was tried in Lightforge for ‘concealed anger’. Juna says that is the first time anyone ever put her own life on trial, and she wants to know what the verdict was."},"band":{"act":"Sabra Hearth","realm":"Flameborne","why":"The pop star who refused to kneel to her King at a festival. Callum used to sing her songs badly in the stores. Juna cannot listen to them and does not miss a show.","rv_vote":"Sabra Hearth. She will say she only does it for her husband."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Bennet Kellway, an ex-garrison man who checks the stamp on everything, she says, and who would have caught the chain.","edict":"Against. She has seen what happens when someone in another Realm signs for something they never checked. Stonehold should keep its own registers."},"wants":"To know who at the foundry stamped that chain, and to have them know her name.","site":"highmarch_vf1","town":"Oakmere","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:highmarch_vf1":{"name":"Osric Barkmile","age":45,"role":"smith of fleshing-knives, buckles and tannery hooks; married into Brindle","look":"Barrel-chested, bald and freckled, a beard dyed faintly brown at the tips from tanning water. His wife’s brindled cowhide apron, which he wears to annoy Oakmere.","voice":"Big, laughing, diplomatic; talks to each village in its own way and admits it.","greet":"Set! You’ve come from Brindle? Tell my wife I’ll be home late. You’ve come from Highmarch? Tell them their buckles are ready. You’ve come from nowhere? Sit down anyway.","lines":["I married a Brindle cheesewife twenty years ago. Every supper we have the quarrel. Then we have the cheese. Then we don’t have the quarrel.","Brindle says our water gets in their cheese. My wife’s cheese tastes of three winters. And a little of my aprons. I’ve never told her.","They’re saying in Gravenhall the levy marched east to stand at the Eastern March. Against what? Metalcrown laughs at us, and we march? That makes no sense to me. Probably makes sense to someone."],"about":"I make the knives that strip the hides, and the buckles that hold the straps, and the hooks the hides hang from. I live in Brindle and I work in Oakmere and I walk the mile between them every day, which makes me either a peacemaker or a spy, depending which end of it you ask.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"fair; learned from a Metalcrown journeyman he took on"}],"childhood":"Osric was the son of a tannery foreman and a bark-stripper, and from five he went out with his mother into the oak wood to strip bark in long curls that she let him wear as crowns. He was a sunny, sociable boy who liked everybody and wanted everybody to like each other, and was always surprised when they would not. He brought a Brindle girl home for supper at nine and his father nearly choked on his fish. At eleven he swam down to touch the Drowned Oak at the Oak Swim and came up with a leaf he swore was living, and it was green and wet and obviously from the weed, and he got the first boots off the new hides anyway because the judge was his aunt.","schooling":"Oakmere’s bark-school under Master Leof Pitwell, who taught letters by having the children cut them into strips of bark and peg them out to dry, so every lesson smelled of tannin. Lamp Test at eight at Highmarch: the Gravem lamp lit faintly. Half-Spark. He spent a year in the Highmarch spark-class, did not take, and a nail went grey on his little finger and has stayed grey; he wears it as a joke.","history":"He apprenticed to the old Oakmere smith at thirteen and was the village smith at twenty-four. At twenty-five he married Hesta Brindlemoor, Edda’s niece, the girl he had brought home at nine, at a Cow-Lift wedding in Brindle; they dropped the calf twice and finished on the third go, both of them covered in mud and the calf bawling, and Oakmere and Brindle stood on either side of the lane and cheered the calf. They have two children, one who wants to tan and one who wants to make cheese, and Osric has told each of them the other village is right. He took on a Metalcrown journeyman, Skett, twelve years ago, who came west looking for work and was refused at three forges for his tally plate; Skett is still with him, still quiet, and the best buckle-maker on the mountain. Osric has been reading Metalcrown’s Spark Reactor pamphlets in Shiftcant with Skett at night and making drawings of a bellows he thinks could heat a tannery pit without any fire.","family":"Wife Hesta Brindlemoor, cheese-turner in the Cold Cleft. Son Ewan, fourteen, tanner’s apprentice; daughter Loke, twelve, cheese-girl. His journeyman Skett, who eats with them.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright & Idris Hollowfire","why":"The two who built the first Spark Reactor. Osric loves anyone who made a machine to do what a spark does, so that nobody had to go grey for it. He keeps quiet about this at the Highmarch market."},"band":{"act":"Third Whistle","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Skett played him ‘I Made It’ on a stolen pipe the first winter he worked here, and Osric heard a man saying his own name the only way he was allowed.","rv_vote":"Third Whistle. Metalcrown has never had a lamp from Lightforge and should at least have one from Oakmere."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Morwen Sallowmile, who wants to talk to everyone, ‘because so do I’.","edict":"Torn. He hates the idea of a register, but he thinks Stonehold refusing outright while Metalcrown smiles and Flameborne riots is how the Concord falls apart, and he does not want to have to choose between his apron and his wife’s."},"wants":"To build a fireless pit-heater and get both villages to say it was a good idea on the same day.","site":"highmarch_vf1","town":"Oakmere","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:highmarch_vf1":{"name":"Teg Highmile","age":15,"role":"tannery boy, this year’s Oak Swim winner (disputed)","look":"Lanky and wet-haired, bony shoulders, brown to the wrist already from the pits, a green leaf pressed in oiled cloth and worn on a cord round his neck.","voice":"Cocky, cracking voice, talks very fast to cover how much he cares.","greet":"Set. I touched the highest living leaf on the Drowned Oak this midsummer. Don’t ask Brindle. They’ll lie. Want to see the leaf?","lines":["They say it wasn’t living. It was green. It was wet. It was the highest. What else does a leaf have to do?","The pits stink. I can’t smell them any more. Mam says I smell of them at supper. That means I’m a tanner now, she says.","There was a Stormreach rider through the village last week on her way to Highmarch. Her hair was grey at one side and she was maybe twenty. She sat on our fence and told me about falling off a citadel on purpose. I want to do that."],"about":"I strip hides at the Tanning yard and I won the Oak Swim. I do my Shouldering in autumn. After that I want to go somewhere that isn’t a mountain. I don’t know where yet. Somewhere you can see a long way.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"poor"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"four words, all learned from one rider, mostly ‘Wahai!’"}],"childhood":"Teg was the fourth son of a widowed tanner, and grew up as the one nobody had time for, which he liked. He spent his early years in the lake, in the oak wood, on the roof of the bark-shed, anywhere high or wet or far from his brothers. He learned to swim before he could read and to dive before he could swim properly, and was pulled out of the mere twice by Bram Tanning by his hair. He was a cheerful, reckless, lying sort of boy, not a bad liar but an enthusiastic one, who told every visiting carter a different story about the Drowned Oak. His favourite was that it had walked into the mere on its own because it was tired of Brindle.","schooling":"Bark-school under Master Pitwell’s daughter Aud, who now teaches it, and makes the children recite the names of the hides they will one day tan, and who threw Teg in the mere once for answering back and then jumped in after him. Lamp Test at eight at Highmarch: nothing lit. He cannot sit still and has failed letters three times; he can read a hide-tally perfectly.","history":"He went to the pits at thirteen like his brothers and is good at it, with clever fast hands and no fear of the lime. He has entered the Oak Swim every year since he was ten. This midsummer he dove deeper than anyone ever had, past the weed and into the cold, and grabbed a leaf he said was attached to the very crown, and came up purple and half drowned and won. The leaf was green. The Brindle cheese-boy who came second says he saw Teg pull it off a floating branch on the way up. The judges, two Oakmere and one Brindle, voted two to one. Teg got the first boots off the new hides and wears them everywhere, including to bed. Since the Stormreach rider sat on his fence, he has been jumping off the bark-shed roof with a hide stretched on a frame over his head, trying to glide. He has broken two fingers and a frame.","family":"Mother Anni, a tanner, a widow these ten years, who says she is too busy for his nonsense and keeps his leaf in her apron when he forgets it. Three older brothers at the pits, all larger, all impressed.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"The man who built the first sky bridge so no Realm would be alone. Teg cannot stop thinking about standing on something in the sky that a person made."},"band":{"act":"Kestrel Vane","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A sky-racer pop star who gets older every season from her own sparks. The rider on the fence hummed one of her songs. Teg has whistled it ever since without knowing the words.","rv_vote":"Kestrel Vane. He has told nobody in Oakmere because they would say Stonehold."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to stand in the Senate. He says he would stand behind whoever is nearest the door.","edict":"Against, because ‘if they write you down, you can’t leave’."},"wants":"To stand on Korr’s Span before he is twenty.","site":"highmarch_vf1","town":"Oakmere","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:highmarch_vf1:b":{"name":"Gessa Oakroad","age":88,"role":"retired tanner, keeper of the bark ledger of every Oak Swim winner","look":"Bent nearly double, face like old harness, hands stained mahogany to the elbow and gnarled into hooks. Bright black eyes. A shawl of soft calfskin she tanned herself sixty years ago and has never washed.","voice":"Cackling, crude, very sharp; repeats your question back to you to make you hear how silly it was.","greet":"Set, set. Sit down, you’re blocking my sun. You want to know about the Oak? Everybody wants to know about the Oak.","lines":["My grandmother saw it go down. Storm, middle of the night, one great crack and then a sound like a cow sitting in a bath. She ran down and it was standing up in the water. Standing. She said it looked surprised.","The young ones think Teg cheated. Teg did cheat. I cheated too, in my year. I’ve written them both down. The ledger doesn’t care.","Brindle’s right about the water, you know. Don’t tell them. I want to die before they find out I said so."],"about":"I tanned hides for sixty years. I can tell a Brindle hide from a Highmarch hide with my eyes shut by the way the hair lies. I keep the ledger of every Oak Swim winner since the oak went down. Somebody has to remember who cheated.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native, old-fashioned, full of words the young ones don’t use"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words; refuses on principle"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"swears in it beautifully; learned from a Flameborne lover she does not discuss"}],"childhood":"Gessa was born sixteen years after the great oak slid into the mere, into a village still telling the story, and was raised mostly by her grandmother, who had seen it, because her mother was in the pits from dawn and her father was on the Long Roads with hides. Her grandmother was a bawdy, terrifying old woman who had outlived three husbands and told Gessa about all of them in detail. Gessa grew up wild and dirty and pleased with herself, the girl who would eat anything on a dare, including a raw mere-fish whole. At eight she rowed out alone in a borrowed boat at night to look down at the Oak by moonlight and saw, she swears to this day, a light in its branches, and rowed home and was beaten and has never once said she was wrong.","schooling":"None to speak of. The bark-school then was taught by a man called Wulf Hidebrand who drank, and Gessa went for one winter and then stopped going and nobody noticed. Her grandmother taught her letters from the hide-tallies. Lamp Test at eight at Highmarch: nothing. She bit the examiner, she says, because he had cold hands. Nobody alive can say if this is true.","history":"She went into the pits at eleven and stayed for sixty years, and rose to pit-mistress, which in Oakmere means the one who decides when a hide is ready. At sixteen she won the Oak Swim by holding a leaf in her mouth from the start and producing it at the top, and her grandmother wrote it in the bark ledger and laughed so hard she wet herself. At twenty-three she spent a summer with a Flameborne hide-trader from the Ember March, and he went home, and she never married, and she will tell you about everything else in her life but not about him. She took over the bark ledger when her grandmother died and has recorded every winner for sixty-five years, with notes: ‘cheated’, ‘probably cheated’, ‘honest, poor thing’. She retired from the pits at seventy-one when her hands curled shut. Now she sits outside the tannery gate in the sun and tells the young ones what they are doing wrong. They bring her smoked fish on oak planks and she takes it without thanks.","family":"No husband, no children. Nineteen great-nieces and nephews, of whom she likes four. The Flameborne trader, whose name she has never said aloud.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"She lit the First Hearth in her own kitchen and nobody ever knelt to her. Gessa likes a woman who did a great thing without anybody making a fuss, and she learned the story from the trader."},"band":{"act":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"A seventy-year-old woman who kneads dough on stage while she sings. Gessa says that is the only singer who ever worked as hard as a tanner, and she cried the one time she heard her.","rv_vote":"Old Tamsin, every year she enters, and she says she will vote for her from her marker if she has to."},"vote":{"local":"She has not been to the Senate in twenty years. She says she stands behind Elder Hask Orrowgate ‘from a great distance, with my back to him, where I can’t hear the shouting’.","edict":"Against. ‘I’m eighty-eight. They want to write down what I can do? I can sit. Write that.’"},"wants":"To live to see a Brindle cheesewife win the Oak Swim, just to watch Oakmere’s face.","site":"highmarch_vf1","town":"Oakmere","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:highmarch_vf2":{"name":"Hamo Cleftway","age":50,"role":"trader and cheese-factor, seller of Cleft cheese to the Highmarch garrison","look":"Round, red-cheeked, magnificent moustache waxed into points, a cheese-mallet on his belt like a sword. Boots of obviously Oakmere leather, which he will deny.","voice":"Booming, theatrical, full of injured pride; turns every sale into a speech.","greet":"Set! Welcome to Brindle, where the cows are honest, the cheese is older than your children, and the water was clean until somebody upstream started boiling oak in it.","lines":["Taste that? That’s three winters in the Cleft. Not tanning water. Never tanning water. If you taste bark, it’s your mouth.","I’ve stood best man at thirty-one Cow-Lifts. Thirty-one. I’ve carried the calf for the groom twice when he fell. That’s not in the rules. Nobody’s ever complained.","The garrison takes forty wheels a season now the levy’s gone east. More mouths up there, they say, and fewer hands down here. Something’s coming. Probably weather. Probably not."],"about":"My family’s first marker is at the mouth of the Cold Cleft, which is why we’re Cleftways and why I say our cheese is family. I sell it up the mountain, I buy kit for the drovers, and I keep the quarrel with Oakmere alive because somebody has to and Edda’s too busy with the cows.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native, very loud"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, with flourishes"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a little; learned courting a Spiritveil drover’s daughter who said no"}],"childhood":"Hamo grew up in the cheese-house at the mouth of the Cleft, where the cold came out of the mountain like breath, and he still cannot sleep in a warm room. His father was a cheese-turner, his mother a drover who was away half the year walking herds down the Long Roads to market, and when she came back she brought presents and stories and a different song each time. Hamo was a show-off from birth who performed for the cows because they stood still. His older sister was the clever one. He was the funny one. At ten he climbed the cheese-racks in the Cleft to the top and could not get down, and stayed a night and a day among the three-winter wheels, and was finally fetched with a ladder by Edda Brindlemoor’s mother, who said nothing at all and gave him a slice.","schooling":"Road-school with the summer drovers on the Highmarch Road, under Master Brokk Halse, who taught letters by making the children brand them on scraps of hide with a cold iron, so they learned them backwards and forwards at once. Lamp Test at eight at Highmarch: nothing lit. He was the best at speeches and could not sit through one.","history":"He went on the drives at fifteen with his mother and walked the Long Roads with herds for ten years, as far as Gravenhall and once down to Hearthward, where he ate slapbread and pit lamb and came home talking about it for a winter. He courted a Spiritveil drover’s daughter on the Echomere road with terrible Lanthic and she said no beautifully, and he is still grateful. At twenty-five he married Bettany Coldwell, a cheese-turner, at a Cow-Lift wedding in which they carried the calf the whole length of the village and Hamo talked to it the entire way. Bettany died at forty of a growth, slowly, and Hamo talked to her the entire way as well. He took over the factoring when his sister went to Gravenhall to be a Senate clerk. He sells forty wheels a season to the garrison, and buys his own boots in Oakmere, through a cousin, so as not to be seen.","family":"Wife Bettany, dead ten years. A daughter, Ros, nineteen, who drives cattle and will not marry anyone ‘who can’t lift a calf’. His sister Merrit, a clerk in Gravenhall, who sends Senate gossip.","hero":{"who":"The Hammer-Bearer","why":"His sister wrote from Gravenhall that when Metalcrown’s ninth petition was read and the whole Council laughed, only the Councillor without a Realm stood up. Hamo has never met a Metalcrown man he liked, and that is exactly why he admires it."},"band":{"act":"Mist-Pear","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Wedding R&B. He has been best man at thirty-one weddings and they have played Mist-Pear at twenty of them, because he brings the songsheets.","rv_vote":"Mist-Pear, and he will tell you at length why Spiritveil wedding songs are better than anybody’s, including Stonehold’s."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Hask Orrowgate, who is as loud as he is. He calls it ‘solidarity of the lungs’.","edict":"Against. He says the Holds have refused things for a thousand years and are good at it, and this is a very good thing to refuse."},"wants":"To win the quarrel with Oakmere once, in front of witnesses, and then never mention it again.","site":"highmarch_vf2","town":"Brindle","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:highmarch_vf2":{"name":"Ketta Hornmile","age":33,"role":"farrier-smith, maker of climbing-shoes for Brindle’s cattle, trained Gravem master","look":"Short, solid, freckled, fair hair in a crown of braids. Both hands grey to the second knuckle, smooth and cold, the nails gone to slate. Wears no gloves, ever.","voice":"Cheerful, very direct, faintly scornful of fuss; ends arguments with a shrug.","greet":"Set. Mind the cow. She kicks. So do I, but only when asked.","lines":["Brindle cattle climb because they wear my shoes. Little split irons, one for each half-hoof. Nobody else in Stonehold makes them. Nobody else needs them.","Everyone’s fussing about this Edict. Write me down. My hands already tell anyone who looks what I am and what it cost. A book can’t say more than my fingers.","In the avalanche year the herd stood in the Cleft packed so tight none could fall. My grandmother was in there with them. She said the cows were calmer than the people."],"about":"I was at Korrhaven, the Gravem Tower, for all five years. I finished. I came home because Brindle needed a farrier and I liked cows better than masters. I use my spark once a season, to hold the Cleft’s roof-crack shut when the frost works it. That’s what the grey is from. I don’t regret a knuckle of it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"Conclave-fluent"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"good; her best friend at the Conclave was Frostgrave"},{"tongue":"Old Spark-speech","level":"the Gravem oath-words only"}],"childhood":"Ketta was a cowherd’s daughter and a born climber, and from the time she could walk she was halfway up a slope after a stray calf. Her mother, Edda Brindlemoor’s cousin, knew all the herd and taught her to tell a cow’s mood by its ears. Ketta was a fearless, practical, unsentimental child who did not cry when hurt and did not understand why others did. At six she fell forty feet down the scree after a calf and landed on a ledge with the calf in her arms, and lay there two hours until her father came, and when he reached her she said, ‘You took your time.’ Her parents had six children and she was the only one who ever went to Highmarch for anything but market.","schooling":"Lamp Test at eight at Highmarch, where the Gravem lamp burned up so hot it scorched the bracket. Lamp Years at the Highmarch spark-school under Master Osla Greywall, grey to both shoulders, who made the children carry a full pail of water up the wall stairs without a spark and then with one, to feel what the spark cost. Korrhaven at thirteen, all five years. She was good, not the best. She remembers the Conclave porridge as ‘an insult to oats’.","history":"At Korrhaven she was befriended by a slow, kind Frostgrave girl, Ilka Rimeward, who taught her Rime and patience and how to say ‘probably’ as if it were a promise; they still write. Ketta finished her five years with five knots on her sash and a grey to the first knuckle, and was offered a place on the Senate’s pass-guard at Gravenhall. She came home instead, because old Rolf the farrier was dying and there was nobody else to shoe the herd for the climb. She learned the shoeing from him in his last year, while he lay in a cot by the forge and shouted instructions. Every autumn since, when the frost opens the old crack in the Cold Cleft’s roof, she climbs up into the dark among the wheels and holds the rock shut with Gravem for as long as it takes the masons to pin it, and each time the grey creeps a little further. She is unmarried and has no wish to be; she says she has two hundred cows and they all need her more than a husband would.","family":"Parents, cowherds, still at it. Five siblings, all in Brindle. Edda Brindlemoor is her mother’s cousin. Ilka Rimeward, her Conclave friend in Frostgrave, writes every Thaw.","hero":{"who":"Olin Stair","why":"The Thawborn heretic who carried his sister down the Glass Stair. Ilka told her the story. Ketta once carried a calf down forty feet of scree, and says that is the same story, only Frostgrave makes everything sound sadder."},"band":{"act":"Permafrost","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Black metal whose fans stand perfectly still. Ilka took her to a show at Korrhaven and Ketta stood still for an hour with two hundred Frostgrave teenagers and has never felt calmer.","rv_vote":"Permafrost. She thinks the Frostgrave entries are slow because Frostgrave is honest about how long things take."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Ulla Kestergate, who once came to the Cleft in person to see the roof-crack and put her own hand on it.","edict":"For, which makes her unpopular. She thinks a register of sparks, if it recorded what each one had cost, would stop masters lying to children about the bounds. She would rather Stonehold kept it, but she would sign Lightforge’s book today."},"wants":"To train someone else to hold the Cleft roof, so she can stop before her wrists go.","site":"highmarch_vf2","town":"Brindle","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:highmarch_vf2":{"name":"Alfi Steepway","age":9,"role":"cowherd’s boy, the youngest herder in Brindle","look":"Small, thin, permanently muddy to the knee, with a calf-rope coiled over one shoulder and a big brindled dog called Grudge at his heel. A gap where two front teeth should be.","voice":"Small voice, very serious, asks questions back; makes Shadowdeep hand-signs without knowing what they mean.","greet":"(He makes a little twist of the fingers.) Set. That means hello. A drover showed me. Or it means something else. Grudge, sit.","lines":["My brother says Teg from Oakmere cheated in the Oak Swim. He’s got the branch. He’s keeping it for a wedding. I don’t know why a wedding.","I’ve got my Lamp Test next spring. If a lamp lights I have to go away to school. Edda says the cows will miss me. I don’t want a lamp to light. Is that allowed?","Edda knows all the cows’ names. I know forty. She doesn’t know the reeve’s name. I do. It’s Halvard. I told her and she forgot again."],"about":"I mind the calves on the low slope. I’m nine. I know forty cows by name and a dog called Grudge who isn’t mine but stays with me. When I’m big I want to be like Edda and know all of them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"six or seven signs from a Shadowdeep drover; doesn’t know what most mean"}],"childhood":"Alfi is still in his childhood, and it is mostly cows. He is the youngest of four in a herding family on the steep side of Brindle, where the house has one wall dug into the slope and the goats walk on the roof. He was a sickly baby who was not expected to winter, and did; his mother still says he was stubborn from the first week. He follows Edda Brindlemoor about like a calf and she lets him. Last summer a Shadowdeep drover came through with mules, a quiet woman who called herself only Tisk, and stayed a week at the Cleft. She showed Alfi some hand-signs when nobody was looking because he asked her what she did with her fingers while she talked. He uses them for everything now, mostly wrongly. His mother does not know what they are and thinks he made them up.","schooling":"He goes to the summer road-school when the drovers go out, taught by Master Halse’s granddaughter Sigrun, who teaches letters by having the children write each cow’s name in chalk on its flank before the drive. Alfi cannot write well but he has made sure every cow has the right name. His Lamp Test is next spring and he is very worried about it.","history":"His whole history so far: born in the hard spring, nearly died, didn’t. Walked at one. Fell into the calf-trough at two and was pulled out by the dog that was Grudge’s mother. Started minding calves at seven. Lost a calf in fog at eight and found it again by calling every name he knew until it answered, which took most of the night, and when he brought it home Edda Brindlemoor said ‘Good’ and nothing else, which is the most anyone has ever said to him. Met Tisk the drover. Learned the signs. Watched his older brother Brann come second in the Oak Swim at Oakmere this midsummer and come home furious with a stripped branch under his arm. Has been told by four people that the Edict is about counting sparks and has asked each of them whether it counts cows.","family":"Parents Bera and Garm Steepway, herders. Three older siblings: Brann, fifteen, who came second in the Oak Swim; and two sisters. Grudge, the dog, who belongs to the whole village and has chosen him.","hero":{"who":"Edda Brindlemoor","why":"She knows every one of two hundred cows by name and has never been wrong. He thinks that is the hardest thing anyone in Stonehold can do."},"band":{"act":"The Hollows Kids","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Tisk had a scratched song-slate of theirs, blades on stone and hand-signs and no singing, and played it for him on a rock with her knife. He thought it was the best thing he had ever heard because he could do it too.","rv_vote":"He doesn’t have a lamp to give yet. He would give it to The Hollows Kids, and asked his mother to, and she said she would think about it."},"vote":{"local":"Too young. He says he would stand behind Edda, and when told Edda is not an elder he said she should be.","edict":"He doesn’t understand it. He is against it because it might mean a lamp lighting for him next spring and somebody writing it down."},"wants":"For no lamp to light at his Test, so he can stay with the herd.","site":"highmarch_vf2","town":"Brindle","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:highmarch_vf2:b":{"name":"Sabeth Gorsemile","age":41,"role":"cheese-turner in the Cold Cleft, keeper of the winter tallies","look":"Rangy and long-limbed, short dark hair under a wool cap, cheeks permanently pink from the cold of the Cleft. Thick felt mittens with the thumbs cut off. Smells, faintly and always, of cheese.","voice":"Slow, droll, deadpan; long pauses that turn out to have been jokes.","greet":"Set. You’ve come about cheese. Everyone comes about cheese. I turn it. It takes three winters. You can wait, or you can buy one somebody else turned.","lines":["I tried the Cow-Lift three times with the same calf. By the third time the calf was nearly a cow and very angry with us both. We gave up. Mostly the calf won. Pol and I live together anyway. Brindle has decided that counts.","Every wheel in the Cleft has a tally of its winters cut into the rind. I cut them. If you see a wheel with four, that’s mine. I forgot it. It’s the best one we’ve got.","I like turning cheese. Nobody believes me. It’s quiet, it’s cold and nothing happens for three years. That’s my kind of story."],"about":"I turn the wheels in the Cleft. Two thousand of them, once a fortnight, by hand. My partner Pol drives the cattle. We were going to marry, and then there was a calf. I like a slow life. I picked it on purpose. Not many people can say that.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, unhurried"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few phrases from Thirty Winters songs, sung badly"}],"childhood":"Sabeth was the only child of two cheese-turners who met in the Cleft and, as Brindle tells it, courted for seven years without anyone noticing, including them. She grew up in the cold among the racks, sleeping in a basket on the shelf between two wheels when she was very small, and learned to walk by holding on to rinds. She did everything a little later than other children and a little better; she learned to read at nine and had read everything in Brindle by spring. She was not lonely; she liked being alone and could not understand why people kept trying to fix it. At twelve, when the other children were racing the herds down the slopes, she was sitting at the back of the Cleft trying to count, by candle, how many wheels there were. She got to eleven hundred and fell asleep.","schooling":"The summer road-school under Master Halse, who once let her stay behind and count the Cleft’s wheels instead of going on the drive because she said that was her schooling; he marked her present. Lamp Test at eight: nothing lit. She was unremarkable at everything except arithmetic, which she did in her head for fun and still does.","history":"She went into the Cleft full-time at fourteen and has been there twenty-seven years, and is now the keeper of the winter tallies, the one who decides when a wheel is ready. She knows every rack. She was in the Cleft at twenty-two when the avalanche warning came and the herd was driven in, and she spent the two days at the back holding a lamp while two hundred cows stood packed among the racks breathing, and says it was the most peaceful two days of her life. At twenty-eight she met Pol Highfold, a drover who sang loudly and badly and laughed at her pauses before she had finished them, and they tried the Cow-Lift at twenty-nine, thirty-two and thirty-five, each time with the same calf, which Edda would not let them change. The third time the animal was nearly full grown and sat down halfway. Sabeth laughed until she fell over and has never tried again. She and Pol share a cottage at the top of the village and a slow, comfortable, unmarried life.","family":"Partner Pol Highfold, a drover. Her parents, both alive, both still turning cheese, both slow. The calf, now a large and resentful cow called Third Try.","hero":{"who":"The Lost Warden","why":"Nobody knows who the Lost Warden was. Sabeth finds that restful. She thinks some stories should stay at the back of the Cleft and be turned now and then, not cut open."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Twelve-minute post-rock. She plays one song while turning a single rack and it comes out exactly right. Pol cannot stand them.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters. She doesn’t expect them to win and thinks a Frostgrave presenter sitting down during their entry is the best moment of the whole contest."},"vote":{"local":"She has never gone to the Senate. She says if every voice at once is needed, Gravenhall can come and hear hers in the Cleft. She leans towards Elder Bennet Kellway because he is slow.","edict":"Torn. She cannot see what harm a list does, and cannot see what good, and would like everyone to wait three winters before deciding, like cheese."},"wants":"For nothing to change for the next forty years.","site":"highmarch_vf2","town":"Brindle","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:anvilreach_vf1":{"name":"Gunnora Scarway","age":55,"role":"trader and rope-maker, once the best egg-gatherer on the scar","look":"Lean, weathered, one shoulder higher than the other from an old fall, grey hair in a net of her own knotted cord. Rope-burn scars in neat stripes across both palms.","voice":"Terse, wry, watchful; looks past you at the road while she talks.","greet":"Set. Rope, kit, bread. The rope I made. The bread I didn’t. You’ll be able to tell.","lines":["Sedgemoor sends its weavers up to cut grass at the foot of the scar. They say the rock belongs to everyone. I’ve hung off that rock on a rope of that grass. It belongs to whoever it doesn’t drop.","There’s a man watching the Long Road from the scar day and night. Has been for longer than the village. Don’t worry. He’s stone. Worry about the living ones.","I hear the levy went east past Anvil Reach and the tap came through from the Eastern March two nights after. Oswy won’t say what it said. That tells you something."],"about":"I gathered eggs off that cliff from nine to forty, hanging from rope I made. Then I fell. The rope held. My shoulder didn’t. Now I make rope and sell it to people who are going somewhere, and I watch the road, because everyone in Ravenscar does.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Seismic taps","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"a few breathy words from a Shadowdeep rope-buyer"}],"childhood":"Gunnora was born in a rope-walk, literally; her mother went into labour twisting a cable and would not put it down. The family made rope and gathered eggs, and from the time she could stand Gunnora was walking backwards down the long shed feeding grass into the twist. She was a light, quick, silent child who was sent down the cliff at nine on a rope round her middle, because small children are lightest, and she loved it with a cold, private love she never told anyone about. She would hang in the wind above the Long Road with a basket of raven eggs and watch carts pass a thousand feet below like beetles, and feel nobody in the world could reach her. She was frightened of almost nothing except the Stillman, whose raised hand she could see from her bedroom.","schooling":"The tap-school at the relay station, under old Keeper Aske Thrum, who taught letters and the seismic code together, making the children tap out each letter on the floor as they wrote it, so that she still taps her own name when she signs. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach: nothing lit. She was quick and inattentive and left at twelve to gather eggs full-time.","history":"She gathered for thirty-one years and was the best on the scar for twenty of them, going lower and further than anyone, to the ledges where the raven eggs are bitterest. She married Torvald Ropewright, a rope-maker who never once went over the edge, at twenty-four, and they had two daughters, both of whom gather now. At forty, reaching for a nest under an overhang, the ledge she was standing on came away. She dropped sixty feet and the rope caught her and slammed her into the rock, and she hung there with a broken collarbone and dislocated shoulder for three hours until Torvald and four others hauled her up. The rope was one she had twisted herself, from scar-grass she had cut at the cliff foot. She took over the shop when Torvald’s father died. She sells rope to the whole Long Road and to Stormreach riders passing west, who say it is the best grass rope they have ever handled, and she quietly makes it out of the very grass Sedgemoor wants to cut.","family":"Husband Torvald, rope-maker. Daughters Hela and Ide, egg-gatherers. A grandson, three, who sleeps under the rope-walk bench.","hero":{"who":"Amon Greycloak","why":"The first Shardwatch. A traveller told her he watched an edge of the world for years, from one place, and never left his post. She thinks every person in Ravenscar is doing the same thing in a smaller way."},"band":{"act":"Under Haskerry","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Whispered folk. She heard it first from a Shadowdeep rope-buyer who sang it under her breath in the shop. It sounds like wind on the scar at night.","rv_vote":"Under Haskerry. She knows Shadowdeep comes second on purpose and wants to see what happens if they get a lamp they can’t refuse."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Dunmar Highcut, who once climbed the scar to see the Stillman and did not ask to be roped.","edict":"Against. She says if Lightforge wants to know what’s on every road, it can stand on a cliff for sixty years like the Stillman and watch."},"wants":"To see Ravenscar and Sedgemoor agree that the grass belongs to whoever makes the best use of it, which is her.","site":"anvilreach_vf1","town":"Ravenscar","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:anvilreach_vf1":{"name":"Dagny Westmile","age":26,"role":"smith of tuned tap-hammers for the relay, rope-hooks and climbing irons","look":"Tall and freckled, red hair shaved on one side where an old burn is, the other side long. Deaf in her left ear; she turns her right to you. Tuning forks on a cord at her neck.","voice":"Precise and quick, slightly too loud; corrects your pronunciation of Holdspeech, then apologises, then does it again.","greet":"Set. Speak to my right side, please. The left one’s for listening to rock.","lines":["Every tap-hammer on the relay from here to Gravenhall I tuned. A badly tuned hammer and a message comes through as nonsense. ‘Send grain’ turns into ‘set the rain’. It happened once. Gravenhall sent a rain-gauge.","Ravenscar won’t let Sedgemoor cut the scar-grass. I think that’s daft. I sell Sedgemoor their eel-hooks. Don’t tell Gunnora.","There was a long tap two nights after the levy went past. Long. Oswy sat up with it till dawn. He’s been tapping his fingers on everything twice as fast since."],"about":"I make the hammers the tap-keepers use. Each one rings a little different so you can tell whose message is whose through a mile of rock. I lost the hearing in one ear to a forge-spark when I was sixteen and got better at listening with the other. That’s about all of me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native, very correct"},{"tongue":"Seismic taps","level":"expert"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting; learning it to read a book on bell-founding"}],"childhood":"Dagny was the daughter of the old Ravenscar smith and a tap-keeper’s assistant, and grew up between the forge and the relay station, hearing iron and rock all day. She was a fussy, exact, slightly odd child who noticed that the station’s hammers all sounded different and that the keepers could not hear it. She told them, at seven. They laughed. At eight she proved it by tapping the same message with three hammers and having old Keeper Thrum guess which was which; he got it wrong twice. He gave her the worst hammer to fix. She took it to her father’s forge and reworked the head for a week until it rang clean, and Thrum used it for the rest of his life. She did not have many friends. She had a raven, briefly, which she fed from the Stillman’s bread until it bit her.","schooling":"The tap-school under Keeper Thrum and, after his death, Oswy Tapper, who made the children listen to the rock by pressing an ear to the station’s floor for an hour every morning without speaking. She was the only child who looked forward to it. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach: nothing lit. She learned letters easily and arithmetic badly and the tap-code better than anyone in her year.","history":"She apprenticed to her father at twelve. At sixteen a spark from a badly struck billet went into her left ear and burned the drum, and after a fevered month she could no longer hear on that side, and found that pressing her good ear to stone she could hear further than ever. She took over the forge when her father went to live in the March orchards near Hearthward with his second wife, three years ago. She has tuned and reforged every hammer on the relay from Ravenscar to Gravenhall, walking the line station by station with a pack of forks and files, and is now asked to tune the Senate’s own hammers in the Anvilstone hall. She is quietly in love with nobody and quietly irritated by everyone who asks. She has been teaching herself Clear from a Lightforge book on bell-founding she bought at Anvil Reach, because she has read that Lightforge bell-founders can hear a crack in a casting by its ring, and she wants to know how.","family":"Father Brennan Westmile, retired, in the March orchards near Hearthward with a second wife Dagny likes more than she expected. Her mother, dead when she was ten. No one else, by choice.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"‘The Lamp That Leaned’. A Spiritveil copyist who, the story goes, copied something exactly when everyone wanted him to fix it. Dagny passes on every tap exactly as it comes, even when it is nonsense. She thinks that is the whole job."},"band":{"act":"Calder Glass","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A battle rapper who cannot lie. She likes that every line he says is checked. She can hear his rhythm through the floor at the Anvil Reach inn when the bards play him.","rv_vote":"Calder Glass, although she would like it written down that she thinks Lightforge wins too much."},"vote":{"local":"She has stood in the Senate twice, tuning the hammers, and stood both times behind Elder Ulla Kestergate, who asked her questions about the relay and listened to the answers.","edict":"Torn. She likes a correct record. She does not like who would be keeping it, or the fact that the relay would be used to carry it."},"wants":"To build a hammer so finely tuned that a message could be sent from Ravenscar to Gravenhall in one go without any station passing it on.","site":"anvilreach_vf1","town":"Ravenscar","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:anvilreach_vf1":{"name":"Ancel Ravenmile","age":37,"role":"the newest tap-keeper at the Ravenscar relay, a former Senate copy-clerk","look":"Soft, pale, ink-fingered, a good Gravenhall coat getting ruined by cliff-wind. Raven droppings on one shoulder he has stopped trying to clean. Carries a slate everywhere and writes on it while you talk.","voice":"Anxious, eager, over-explains; laughs at himself in a high nervous bark.","greet":"Set. Oh, set. I’m new. I’m the new tap-keeper. Second tap-keeper. Under Oswy. Did you want a message sent? Please want a message sent.","lines":["New tap-keepers leave carry-bread at the Stillman’s feet on their first day. The ravens eat it if he accepts you. I left mine. They didn’t eat it for nine days. Nine. On the tenth it rained and it went soft and they ate it. Does that count? Oswy says it counts.","The ravens all face one way when they sit on him. Never the same way twice. I’ve been writing it down. Every morning. I have eleven weeks of it. I don’t know what it means. I keep writing.","Gravenhall thinks a tap-keeper sits in a warm room listening to rocks. Gravenhall should try listening to rocks with a raven on its head."],"about":"I copied Senate rolls in Gravenhall for fifteen years. I asked for something that mattered more. They sent me here. I was frightened of the cliff for the first month and of the ravens for the second. Now I’m only frightened of Oswy, and he’s very kind, so that’s fine.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native, Gravenhall polish"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, copy-clerk precise"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it well"},{"tongue":"Seismic taps","level":"learning; slow but accurate"}],"childhood":"Ancel was the only son of a Senate scribe and a Gravenhall seamstress who lived in rooms cut into the inner wall of the Deep Hold, never seeing the sky except on Marker Day. He was a nervous, clever, indoor child who read the Senate rolls his father brought home for fun, and once fainted at the Lift because he had not eaten. His mother was frightened for him, and he grew up frightened for himself in the same way. At ten he was taken out of the Hold on a cart to see the Anchorstone from the outside, and stood in front of it for an hour and wept because it was too big, and has never forgotten that a thing can be too big to look at.","schooling":"The Gravenhall Hold-school under Mistress Ruth Stannick, who taught letters with a rule across the palm for blots and later, in private, told Ancel he had the best hand she had ever seen and should never let anyone hit him with a rule again. Lamp Test at eight: nothing lit. He was the best at copying and the worst at Shouldering, which he passed at the second attempt at sixteen, a year late, carried by sheer embarrassment.","history":"He went into the Senate copy-rooms at seventeen and copied rolls, petitions, levy-orders and road-accounts for fifteen years, and was good, and was invisible. He copied the Silent Sentinel’s slates into Bridge for the record for two years, and says it was the closest he ever came to a conversation with someone great; he still writes in the Sentinel’s short, cut sentences when he is tired. At thirty-two he asked for a posting away from the copy-rooms, and was refused, and asked again every year until this spring, when he was sent to Ravenscar as second tap-keeper under Oswy Tapper. He climbed to the Stillman on his first day with his carry-bread and was so frightened of the ravens he dropped it and had to pick it up. They did not eat it for nine days. The village watched. He did not leave. He has begun learning taps from Oswy at the table and is slow, and Oswy is patient, and taps the rhythm of their lessons on the table long after.","family":"His mother, still in Gravenhall, writes every week asking whether he has fallen off the cliff. His father died four years ago. No wife; he says he has never been brave enough to ask anyone anything until this year.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"The Seer who knotted a vision, so it could be kept and checked. He has written down everything in his life because that is the only way he knows to keep something."},"band":{"act":"Echo Bleed","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A DJ who samples dead singers. ‘Somebody Else’s Summer’. He heard it in a Gravenhall inn and thought it was the first music that sounded like copying: someone else’s voice in a new hand.","rv_vote":"Echo Bleed, for the Sparkbeat Cup too, and he has made a careful chart of their chances."},"vote":{"local":"He copied the Senate for fifteen years and so stands behind nobody; he cannot stop seeing every elder’s mistakes in their own handwriting. If forced, Elder Morwen Sallowmile, who writes clearly.","edict":"Torn. He has copied the first Lightforge draft into Bridge for the Senate and found it well made and frightening, and he would like Stonehold to send back an equally well-made refusal, with no blots."},"wants":"For the ravens to eat his bread on the first day, just once, when he next leaves something.","site":"anvilreach_vf1","town":"Ravenscar","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:anvilreach_vf1:b":{"name":"Osgith Longroad","age":102,"role":"the oldest woman in Ravenscar, former tap-keeper, egg-gatherer, rope-twister, everything","look":"Tiny, folded and brown as a nut, nearly blind, white hair so thin it floats. A blanket of rope-grass she twisted herself. One grey fingertip, the only spark she ever spent.","voice":"High, clear, slow; speaks to the Stillman as if he is in the room, and sometimes to you as if you are him.","greet":"Set. Who’s that? Come here where I can see you. No, closer. You’ve a road face. You’ve been walking.","lines":["He’s turning back. The Stillman. I’ve watched him since I was a girl. His hand’s higher than it was. Nobody believes me. Everyone else hasn’t been watching for ninety years.","Oswy hears the rock. I hear the ravens. They come down to the window at night when a long tap comes. They came last month. Three of them. Sat on the sill.","Sedgemoor wants to cut the grass. Let them. I twisted that grass for seventy years. It grows back. The Stillman doesn’t mind grass. He minds noise."],"about":"I’m a hundred and two. I’ve done everything you can do in Ravenscar: gathered, twisted, tapped, buried. I was the tap-keeper before Oswy’s master’s master. Now I sit by the window and watch the Stillman. Someone has to watch the one who watches.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native, very old forms"},{"tongue":"Seismic taps","level":"expert, though her fingers are slow"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"once good; she’s forgotten most of it and does not care"}],"childhood":"Osgith was born in the Concord’s 1,094th year and was nineteen when Stonebarrow carried for nineteen days, and she remembers seeing them pass on the road below the scar, all humming, all grey with snow. But her childhood was before that, and was mostly the cliff. Her parents were egg-gatherers, and she went down on the rope at seven. She was a solemn, dreamy girl who talked to the Stillman from her window from the time she could talk at all, telling him what she had done each day, because her grandmother told her he was lonely. She believed it absolutely. She brought him bread before it was the custom, she says; she says the custom began with her. Nobody alive can contradict her.","schooling":"Taught letters and taps by her grandmother, the tap-keeper of her day, on the station floor. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach, on a cart, in the year of the long frost: the Gravem lamp flickered for one breath. She was sent to a Lamp Year at Anvil Reach and came home after one term because she missed the cliff. She has used her spark once in her life, to hold a cracked ledge for her husband, and the fingertip went grey and stayed.","history":"She gathered eggs until thirty, twisted rope until fifty, and kept the relay from twenty-five to seventy, the longest anyone has kept it. In the March Wars the warning tapped from here reached Gravenhall a day before any rider; that was long before her, but in her time she tapped through the news of the hard winter, three levy-calls, the fall of the old Kell Road bridge, and every birth in the district for forty-five years. She married Hob Longroad, an egg-gatherer, at twenty, and he died at sixty-one when a ledge went; she says she has been married to the Stillman since, which makes the young ones uneasy. She has outlived both her children. She has nine grandchildren and twenty-two great-grandchildren and says she cannot tell any of them apart and loves them in a lump. She has watched the Stillman every day for ninety-five years, and has kept, on a knotted cord, a knot for every day the ravens faced the road. The cord is longer than her house.","family":"Husband Hob, dead forty-one years. Son and daughter, both dead. Grandchildren and great-grandchildren in every village from Ravenscar to Brask.","hero":{"who":"The Stillman of the Scar","why":"Nobody knows his name. She does not want it. She has talked to him every day of her life and he has never once told her she was wrong."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Throat-singing with a famous forty-second silence. Her great-grandson played it for her on a pipe, and she says the silence is the best music she has ever heard and she has heard a great deal of silence.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime. She says she will vote for the silence in the middle and not the singing."},"vote":{"local":"She has not been off the scar in thirty years. She says she stands behind the Silent Sentinel, ‘because he can’t talk either and has the sense not to try’.","edict":"Against. ‘Lightforge wants to know what we carry. Tell them: everybody. Then shut the door.’"},"wants":"To see the Stillman’s hand finish rising.","site":"anvilreach_vf1","town":"Ravenscar","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:anvilreach_vf2":{"name":"Perrin Sedgeway","age":42,"role":"trader, eel-trapper, and ten years a Concord long-wagon carter","look":"Wiry and brown, a silver crossing pierced and worn as an earring, eel-bites like little white commas on his forearms. A Flameborne scarf, a Stormreach life-cord bracelet, Spiritveil ribbon on his hat: a man who collects Realms.","voice":"Cheerful, fast, a little too frank; says the thing other people keep in their mouths.","greet":"Set! Welcome to the only soft village in Stonehold. Featherweights, they called us. Then they needed slings. Go on, buy a basket. Everyone ends up buying a basket.","lines":["Ravenscar says the Stillman wouldn’t like us cutting grass at the scar-foot. I’ve met him. He didn’t say anything. That’s more than Ravenscar can manage.","I drove long-wagons over the Realm Bridges for ten years. Lightforge to Metalcrown to Flameborne and back. You know what I learned? Everyone thinks their bread is the only real bread.","The Edict? Say it out loud: Lightforge wants a list of everyone who can do magic. Now say it again. Doesn’t get better, does it?"],"about":"I’m a weaver’s son who couldn’t sit still long enough to weave. So I drove wagons across the Nine for ten years, then came home with a cough and a lot of opinions. Now I trap eels and sell baskets. I say what I think. People like it until it’s about them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, all the carters’ slang"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"a few shouted phrases"}],"childhood":"Perrin grew up on the Weaving Floor, under it mostly, in the wet dark between the stilts where the eels slid and the sedge-ends fell through the boards. His mother was a weaver who sat in the third place from the end for thirty years and could plait a sling-cord while scolding six children at once. His father trapped eels. Perrin was the wriggling one, the child who would not sit, who untied the weavers’ bundles for fun and got slapped with a wet sedge-switch daily. He was also the one who talked to every carter on the road and could recite, at eight, the names of thirty Realm Bridge tolls without knowing what half of them were. His mother said he had been born with one foot already out of the door. She was right and did not mind.","schooling":"Sedgemoor’s own school, held on the Weaving Floor under Mistress Rowan Sedgely, who taught letters by weaving them into practice mats and made each child unpick and re-weave their own name until it was perfect; he never once got his right. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach: nothing lit. He was bad at weaving, bad at letters, and very good at talking his way out of both.","history":"At eighteen he signed on as a boy with a Concord long-wagon company and drove the roads between the Realm Bridges for ten years, carrying sedge-baskets out and everything else back. He slept under wagons in Lightforge’s endless noon and couldn’t, ate pit lamb in Hearthmount, was robbed in the Rim and given back his purse the next day by the same thief, watched a storm wedding shouted roof to roof in Stormreach and wept. At a Hollow Market crossing he heard someone speak their full name aloud, an Open-Mouthed heretic, and saw what the crowd did, and came home soon after with a cough from the Metalcrown smoke. He married no one; he had a long thing with a Flameborne wagon-mistress that ended amiably. He took over the village shop at thirty-two and trades baskets and slings with every carter going west. He is the one who started saying ‘try lifting your burden-stone in a bucket’, and it is on a sign above his door.","family":"His mother, eighty, still weaves on the Floor. Five siblings, most of them weavers. Countless cousins. A nephew, Col Reedholt, who is walking out with a carver from Anvil Reach and asks Perrin for advice he does not take.","hero":{"who":"Vey Who Said It","why":"The founder of the Open-Mouthed, who said a full name aloud in Shadowdeep. Perrin saw what saying a thing out loud costs there. He thinks it is the bravest thing a person can do in any Realm."},"band":{"act":"No Knee","realm":"Flameborne","why":"A bakers’-street rap crew at war with Lightforge. The wagon-mistress took him to see them in Hearthmount and he shouted along in bad Kindle until a baker hugged him.","rv_vote":"No Knee. He says Lightforge needs to lose to someone who’s rude about it."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Hask Orrowgate at the Senate and shouts at the right moments. He says the Senate is ‘every voice at once’ and some voices should be louder.","edict":"Against, and organising: he is collecting marks for a refusal-petition from every village on the Anvil Reach road and has two hundred and twelve."},"wants":"For Sedgemoor never to be called featherweight again by anyone who has used one of its slings.","site":"anvilreach_vf2","town":"Sedgemoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"smith:anvilreach_vf2":{"name":"Mabyn Frameroad","age":64,"role":"smith of frame-pins, eel-hooks and weaving-knives; one of the old levy-sling weavers","look":"Short and round, white hair in a cloud, half-moon spectacles. Fingers criss-crossed with fine white sedge-cut scars like ruled lines, and a smith’s burns over them.","voice":"Soft and kind, a little dreamy, given to long stories with no point that turn out to have a point.","greet":"Set, love. Mind the hooks, they bite. Everything in here bites, except me.","lines":["See my fingers? That’s the levy season. Forty-six years ago. We wove three thousand slings in one summer. Every cut is a sling. I could count them if I wanted. I don’t want.","The Ravenscar people say the Stillman wouldn’t want us cutting grass. I don’t think he minds. I think the Ravenscar people mind, and he’s a good excuse.","I was a weaver, and then my hands got too cut to weave, so I learned to make the knives that cut the sedge. Nobody saw the joke but me."],"about":"I wove until the levy summer and then I couldn’t any more. My hands were ruined. So I went to the smith and asked him to teach me something you do with a hammer. I make every knife and hook in Sedgemoor now. I’m soft. People say smiths aren’t soft. Here they are.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a few words of the Willow Choir songs, sung softly"}],"childhood":"Mabyn was a plump, dreamy, kind-hearted girl, the daughter of two weavers, and from the age of five sat on the Floor between them twisting sedge-ends while the grown-ups talked over her head. She loved the sound of the Floor, forty pairs of hands going at once, and would fall asleep to it. She cried easily, at sad stories, at drowned kittens, at sunsets, and the other children teased her for it until one of them, a tough little boy called Bertil, punched the biggest teaser in the face and then cried himself. They have been friends ever since. She wove her First Sling at fifteen out of the softest sedge she could find, and it held the mile, just, and hangs over her bed still.","schooling":"Sedgemoor’s Floor-school under old Mistress Sedgely’s mother, also Mistress Sedgely, who taught the children to weave and read at once by making them weave each letter into a strip before they were allowed to write it. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach: nothing. She was average at reading, very good at weaving, and the best at listening.","history":"The levy summer came when she was eighteen. The Senate ordered three thousand slings and the whole village wove from first light until dark for a season, and Mabyn sat at her place for one hundred and four days and wove until her fingers were cut to ribbons and she was weaving with blood in the sedge. When it was over her hands were too scarred to plait fine work, and the village’s old smith, a gruff kind man called Olaf Frameroad, took her on as an apprentice because, he said, a smith does not need fine fingers, only stubborn ones. She married his son Edric at twenty-four and took over the forge when Olaf died. She and Edric had three children, two weavers and a carter. Edric died twelve years ago of a fall from the Floor’s roof while re-thatching. She has made every weaving-knife and eel-hook in Sedgemoor for forty years, and the knives are so good the Ravenscar rope-makers buy them through a cousin. She gives the young weavers sweets when they cut themselves.","family":"Husband Edric, dead twelve years. Three children; her daughter Gwen weaves at the Floor and her son Ewart drives carts. Seven grandchildren. Her oldest friend, Bertil Hollowmile, eats with her four nights a week.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"The first Penumbrist, who said mercy needs shade. A Lightforge carter told her about Aure, quietly, and Mabyn thinks it is the kindest idea anyone has had in any Realm."},"band":{"act":"The Willow Choir","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Their duet with the echo of a singer four hundred years dead, ‘Nothing Is Lost’. She played it after Edric died, every night for a month, and it helped and she cannot tell you why.","rv_vote":"The Willow Choir. She would vote for any act that sings with someone who is gone."},"vote":{"local":"She stands behind Elder Morwen Sallowmile, who talks gently and wants Stonehold to listen before refusing.","edict":"Against. She thinks people who are frightened of their own sparks, like poor Tharn at Flintmoor, will only be more frightened with a book about them in Meridian."},"wants":"To see Bertil admit he was wrong about the Edict, and not to care when he doesn’t.","site":"anvilreach_vf2","town":"Sedgemoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:anvilreach_vf2":{"name":"Rook Fenmile","age":15,"role":"young weaver, weaving their First Sling ahead of next month’s Shouldering","look":"Gangly, neither boy nor girl and not bothered which, dark curls tied back with a sedge-cord, ears pierced with eel-bone. Fingers already scored with the first fine sedge-cuts.","voice":"Quiet, stubborn, then suddenly fierce; asks questions that make adults change the subject.","greet":"Set. Don’t stand on the sedge, it’s drying. I’m weaving my First Sling. It has to hold a mile. It will.","lines":["The best grass for a sling grows at the foot of Ravenscar. Tough as wire. They won’t let us cut it. So I went up at night. Don’t tell Tamsa. Do tell Ravenscar. No, don’t.","My family’s first marker isn’t anywhere. Everyone else can walk to theirs on Marker Day. Ours was pulled up, Gran says. She won’t say when.","Tamsa Reedholt wove a sling blind for a bet at Anvil Reach. I’m going to weave one blind and then carry my stone in it. That’s two bets."],"about":"I’m Rook. I weave. My Shouldering’s next month and I’m weaving my own sling for it out of grass I wasn’t supposed to cut. If it holds the mile, it hangs over my bed for ever. If it doesn’t, I don’t want to think about it, so I’m not.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"some"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a few words their grandmother uses and won’t explain"}],"childhood":"Rook was raised by a grandmother, Old Nell Fenmile, a weaver, because Rook’s mother went west on the long-wagons and comes home every second winter with presents and leaves again, which everyone agrees is fine and which Rook mostly believes is fine. Rook was a solemn, inward child who liked sorting sedge by toughness and could tell good from bad by feel at five. They never cared to be a girl or a boy; Old Nell said, ‘Then you’ll be Rook,’ and nobody in Sedgemoor argued with Old Nell. On Marker Day when other families walked to their first marker for the picnic, Rook and Nell went to a stretch of empty verge on the Anvil Road where there is a hollow in the ground and nothing in it, and ate their cake there, and Nell would not say why.","schooling":"The Floor-school under Mistress Sedgely the younger, who makes each child weave their own name into a mat at nine and keeps all the mats on a wall; Rook’s is the neatest on it. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach: nothing. Rook was relieved. They read well and ask too many questions, and have been sent out of the Floor-school for it twice.","history":"They have been weaving on the Floor since they were twelve, at the end place nearest the road, where you hear everything. At fourteen they found out from a careless old weaver that the Fenmile marker was pulled up by the Senate road-crews ‘back in the grandmothers’ time’, and that this happened to families who were judged ‘not of the road’, and Rook went home and asked Old Nell directly, and Nell said, ‘Weave,’ and went to bed. This autumn, needing the toughest grass for their First Sling, Rook climbed to the foot of Ravenscar at night three times and cut scar-grass in the dark, and on the third night met a big quiet man laying a single stone at the base of the cliff, who looked at them, nodded, and went on. They have woven half the sling. It is the best thing they have ever made. They have joined, secretly, Maud Threeways’s Long-Name Purse in Anvil Reach, using their own eel money, to pay for a marker with the Fenmile initials on it.","family":"Grandmother Old Nell Fenmile, a weaver, sharp and tired. Mother Marit, a long-wagon carter, mostly away. No father anyone mentions.","hero":{"who":"Tamsa Reedholt","why":"She wove a sling with her eyes shut for a bet and won, and she is the oldest weaver on the Floor and never once let anyone call her a featherweight to her face."},"band":{"act":"The Tether Sisters","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Chain-yard harmony folk. ‘Cut the Chain’. Rook’s mother brought back the song-sheet and Rook sings it at the Floor while weaving, very low, so the old weavers hum along without knowing it’s Stormreach.","rv_vote":"Too young to vote in Rook’s house, by Nell’s rule. They will tell Nell to vote for The Tether Sisters, and Nell will vote for Mason Grey."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to stand in the Senate. They say they would stand at the very front, alone, where everyone could see them.","edict":"Against. They say the Senate already took one list of names and decided whose belonged on the road, and Rook’s family knows what a list does."},"wants":"For the sling to hold, and for there to be a Fenmile marker on the Anvil Road with initials on it.","site":"anvilreach_vf2","town":"Sedgemoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"vill:anvilreach_vf2:b":{"name":"Bertil Hollowmile","age":70,"role":"eel-catcher and stew-maker, Sedgemoor’s most contrary old man","look":"Broad and stooped, enormous red hands, a bald head always under a battered sedge hat, waders worn to every occasion including weddings. A pot of sedge-eel stew permanently on his hip.","voice":"Grumbling, stubborn, reasonable in a way that drives people mad; says ‘mind you’ before every opinion.","greet":"Set. You want stew? You do. Everyone does. It’s the same stew as yesterday. It’s good stew. My life’s been the same stew for seventy years and it’s good stew.","lines":["Mind you, I think the Edict’s fine. Write the sparks down. What’s the harm? Everyone here looks at me like I’ve drowned a calf.","Ravenscar won’t let us cut the grass. Mind you, I wouldn’t let us either. Our weavers would have the whole cliff bald in a season.","I’ve caught eels in the same hollow for sixty years. They’re getting smaller. Or I’m getting bigger. Either way, the stew’s the same."],"about":"I catch eels and make stew and argue with everyone. I’ve done it for seventy years. I know my life’s dull. I like it dull. Mabyn says I’m contrary. I say I just don’t agree with things before I’ve thought about them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"enough for an argument"}],"childhood":"Bertil was the only son of an eel-catcher and a weaver who disagreed about everything and lived happily for fifty years. He grew up in the wet hollow among the eel-traps, a broad, quiet, cross-looking boy who was actually very soft and hid it under scowling. At seven he punched a boy for teasing Mabyn Weft, the girl who cried at sunsets, and then cried himself because he had hurt his hand and because he had never hit anyone before. He was a careful, slow child who liked to do one thing well, and the one thing was eels: he knew their runs, their holes, their moods, the night they would come up the hollow in their thousands in autumn. He never wanted to be anything else, and he was not.","schooling":"The Floor-school under the elder Mistress Sedgely. He hated weaving, could not do it, and was sent out with the eel-traps instead, where his father taught him numbers by counting the catch. Lamp Test at eight at Anvil Reach: nothing lit. He reads slowly and well and says reading fast is for people who want to forget what they read.","history":"He has caught eels in the Sedgemoor hollow since he was ten and has never left Stonehold except once, for a week at Hearthward, which he hated because the food was too hot. He married Sunniva, a weaver, at twenty-three, and they argued cheerfully for thirty-eight years until she died of a fever eight years ago, and he has argued with Mabyn every evening since, because, he says, a man needs somebody to be wrong at. They had no children. He made the stew for every wedding in Sedgemoor for forty years. He reads every pamphlet that comes through the village, from every Realm, slowly, and has read the Radiant Edict’s text in Bridge three times, and come to the conclusion, in a village that thinks otherwise, that it is a sensible law. He also reads the Lightforge Day-Books Perrin brings back from the roads, for fun, and admires them. ‘Write down what you did. What’s the harm?’ People say he would hate it if he had to do it. He keeps one anyway.","family":"Wife Sunniva, dead eight years. No children. Mabyn Frameroad, the smith, his oldest friend, who feeds him and calls him an old fool.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand","why":"The Lawbringer who wrote the Edict. Bertil read the text and says it is careful, plain and well thought through, and that a man who writes like that should be heard before he is refused. Nobody in Sedgemoor speaks to him about it."},"band":{"act":"The Noon Chorale","realm":"Lightforge","why":"The most decorated act in Realm Vision, and everyone is sick of them. He says that is exactly why he likes them: they do the same thing perfectly every year, like eel stew.","rv_vote":"The Noon Chorale. He knows Lightforge wins too much. He says it wins because it is good."},"vote":{"local":"He stands behind Elder Morwen Sallowmile, the only elder who has said in the Senate that the Edict should be read before it is refused.","edict":"For. He thinks counting sparks is no worse than counting letters on a marker, and the Holds have been doing that for a thousand years."},"wants":"For one person in Sedgemoor to agree with him about anything, once, and mean it.","site":"anvilreach_vf2","town":"Sedgemoor","realm":"Stonehold"},"shop:tempest":{"name":"Hesper Quill","age":61,"role":"gate-shop trader at Tempest Hold; sells kit and arrows to outgoing escorts and buys salvage off the ones who come back","look":"Tall, one shoulder carried higher than the other since a fall; white hair cropped rider-short; a life-cord of two hundred and twelve knots wound four times round her left wrist.","voice":"Prices first, then a joke at your expense; counts under her breath in a language nobody at the Hold recognises.","greet":"Wahai. Arrows left, bread right, and don't lean on the counter. It swings.","lines":["The Lightforge clerk asked me how many saddles we've got. I told him 'more than yesterday'. He wrote it down. Bless him.","Gleamford sells us sunwine clear as water and prices it like blood. And we buy it. Every single season we buy it.","Nineteen years I rode. Now I sell the arrows. Same arrows. Less wind in my face, more in my ear."],"about":"Hesper Quill, Tempest, two hundred and twelve on the cord. I rode escort to the Lightforge border till my shoulder came half out on a tilt and never went all the way back. The Hold gave me the gate shop instead of a pension. I'm better at this than I ever was in the saddle, and nobody here wants to hear that.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"kitchen-fluent, from her mother; never used where anyone can hear"}],"childhood":"Hesper was born in the stable bastion, the one that swings hardest, and slept her first year in a feed-crib because it rocked the right way. Her mother, Durra, had come up from Stonehold as a farrier's hand with a horse-buying party bound for Thistledown, met a rider at the Hold gate, and did not go back down with the horses. Durra kept a burden-stone under the bed and did the Lift morning and night with the door shut. Hesper learned to lift it before she learned to ride, and learned by eight that the other children called her 'half-stone' if she answered her mother aloud. So she answered quietly. At her First Fall her father strung the net and her mother tied it, with a knot no Stormreach netter uses, and Hesper fell into it laughing.","schooling":"The Hold's riding-school, under Rider-Sergeant Amsel Crake, who made the little ones ride bareback across the inner yard while the bastion swung and kept a bucket by the gate for the ones who were sick. Hesper used it twice. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp lit low and steady: a Half-Spark. She never went to Korrhaven. She learned to cast just enough to settle a horse in thunder, failed her letters twice, and still writes her sixes backwards.","history":"She rode escort for nineteen years, taking merchant convoys down the long fall to the Lightforge border and back, and tied two hundred and twelve knots doing it. Her hair went white at forty and she stopped noticing. At twenty-six she married Joss Quill, a saddler who sings while he stitches, and since they were both Tempest by citadel-name, nothing changed on the muster roll but the line under hers. At forty-two, on the border road near Gleamford, an orchard cart came round a bend at a walk, on the wrong side, and she put her horse over the ditch rather than through the cart. The horse was fine. Her shoulder was not. The orchard-folk sent a fair, exact letter of apology; she has never answered it. Durra died at seventy and Hesper flew her out to Harrowgate's ledge with the rest of the family, but brought the burden-stone home and keeps it under the counter. Since the Edict talk began she has sold more arrows in a season than in the ten before it, and she has started writing the prices down, which she has never done in her life.","family":"Husband Joss, saddler, loud and content; two sons, both riders, both already streaked at the brow. Her mother's people in Stonehold have never written, and she has never written to them.","hero":{"who":"Vael Korr","why":"'No Realm should ever be alone.' Her mother crossed one, her father met her at the gate, and Hesper is what came of it."},"band":{"act":"The Lonely Line Choir","realm":"Stonehold","why":"She says she likes them because they are quiet and the Hold is not. The truth is they murmur the same way her mother did under the burden-stone.","rv_vote":"Her lamp goes to The Lonely Line Choir, and she will deny it at the top of her voice to anyone who asks."},"vote":{"local":"At the Free Assembly she shouts with Kesh's people, 'chain first, shout after', because she has seen what a fast decision does on a bad road. She thinks Seraphine is right and too quick about it.","edict":"Against. She has sold escort into Lightforge for forty years and never once had to count her own sparks at the border, and she is not going to start for a clerk."},"wants":"To get through one Brightwater remembrance without anyone saying 'stonefoot' in her shop.","site":"tempest","town":"Tempest Hold","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:tempest":{"name":"Merrit Sole","age":37,"role":"Hold smith; forges hoof-irons, lance-heads and bit-rings on an anvil hung in gimbals so it stays level when the bastion swings","look":"Short, thick-armed, burn-freckled; dark hair without a single grey thread, which at the Hold is more remarked on than a scar. Her life-cord is a plain loop of leather with no knots on it at all.","voice":"Flat and exact, says what a thing is made of before what it is for; laughs once, hard, and moves on.","greet":"Shoes, bits or points? Say which. I don't do 'something nice'.","lines":["Brec says the horses are worth it. He's thirty-four. Look at his hair. I'm just saying what's in front of me.","They drill facing south now. I've made more lance-heads this month than all last year. Nobody's told me who for. Nobody has to.","They call my cord 'clean'. Like it's a joke. I'll be shoeing their grandchildren's horses with my own hair still dark. We'll see who laughs."],"about":"Merrit Sole. Full Spark, if you're asking, and I don't cast. I did a year at the Tempest Tower and came home. Iron doesn't take your years off you. It just takes your skin, a bit at a time, and it gives you something back for it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, Conclave-taught"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a few dozen words, all to do with metal"}],"childhood":"Merrit's father, Aun Sole, was a rider the captains loved because he would cast past sense whenever they asked. He took charges for other men's horses, other men's patrols, other men's mistakes, and by the time Merrit was six his hair was white and his hands shook. She remembers him lifting her onto the stable roof to watch a storm come in, and telling her each strike was a coin someone was spending. He died at forty-five, looking seventy, and the captains shouted 'Worth the grey' at his letting so loudly she could not hear the wind. Her mother took up smithing in the Hold forge to keep them in the stable bastion. Merrit pumped the bellows from the age of seven and found she liked a thing that gave you exactly what you put in.","schooling":"The Hold riding-school for three years, then the Lamp Years after the Tempest lamp blazed so bright at her test that the tester dropped her chalk. At thirteen she went to the Tempest Tower at Korrhaven, hated the porridge, loved the library, and spent a year learning to cast cleanly. At the end of it she walked out of the tower with her grey sash, one knot in it, and took the long-wagon home. Her tutor wrote her mother a kind letter. Merrit burned it.","history":"She came back to the Hold at fourteen and told her mother she would be a smith, and her mother handed her the tongs without a word. The riders took a while to forgive a Full Spark who would not ride. Some never did. Merrit learned to forge on the gimballed anvil, a contraption her mother had argued out of the Hold's quartermaster, and by twenty-five she was the better smith of the two. She has read everything she can get about Metalcrown's synthetic sparks, which arrive in Bridge pamphlets bought off traders at Skybridge, and she keeps them in a tin box because the riders would laugh. She has never married. She has shod every horse Brec Vane has ever ridden, and once told him to his face that he would be dead before fifty; he thanked her and asked for a lighter shoe. Since the Edict talk she has been asked twice by captains whether she would ride south if it came to it. Twice she has said she would forge for them and not ride. The second captain did not thank her.","family":"Her mother, Bethen, retired from the forge and now sits too close to it. Her father's life-cord, all three hundred and six knots of it, hangs on a hook above the quench-trough.","hero":{"who":"Kessa Wright and Idris Hollowfire","why":"They built a spark that came out of a machine and not out of a person's years. Merrit thinks that is the most important thing anyone has done in a thousand years, and would never say so in the Hold."},"band":{"act":"Thunderhead","realm":"Stormreach","why":"It is the only music loud enough to work to. She hammers on the off-beat of their slow songs and the apprentices think she is showing off.","rv_vote":"Thunderhead, because a vote should go where your ears already are."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Tamra at the Assembly because Tamra rode and quit and came back sharper, which Merrit respects. She has never once shouted for Seraphine, and keeps quiet about why.","edict":"Torn, and it frightens her. She hates Lightforge counting anyone, but she watched captains spend her father like loose change, and some days a register sounds like a wall between a young rider and a greedy captain."},"wants":"To see a working Metalcrown spark reactor with her own eyes, once.","site":"tempest","town":"Tempest Hold","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:tempest":{"name":"Ludo Brack","age":70,"role":"head of the saddle-cake kitchen; bakes the paper-wrapped cakes with every rider's name on them","look":"Round, bald, flour in the creases of his neck; no knots, no grey streaks, and a laugh you can hear from the stables. He wears a dozen unused paper twists tucked into his apron strings.","voice":"Warm and mischievous, calls everyone by what they eat, gets sentimental and then pretends he was joking.","greet":"You've no twist with your name on it. Sit down. No, don't sit down, what am I saying. Stand there and eat this.","lines":["I bake one cake a day for the Lightforge clerk. Twist says 'Counting'. He eats it. Writes it down in his little book, too. 'Cake, honey, goat. Kind.'","Riders who stop coming for cake, you notice. You notice before the captains do. You keep baking it a week anyway.","Dunmere wants paying for the goat. Dunmere always wants paying. Dunmere is right. Don't tell Dunmere."],"about":"Ludo Brack, Tempest, and no knots on me, never had the spark. Fifty years I've baked for riders. I know who's frightened by what they leave in the paper. You don't need to ride to know a rider.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, and loud"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting, mostly food words"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"a handful of bread words, learned off a Flameborne cook"}],"childhood":"Ludo's family were Hold cooks back to anyone's memory, and he grew up under the long kitchen tables with the dogs, eating what fell. His grandmother, Mother Pim Brack, ran the ovens like a riding drill and made him stand on a stool and shout the orders before he could read them. He was a fat, slow, cheerful child in a citadel that lives for speed, and he did his First Fall at ten so badly that he bounced out of the net and had to be fished off the stable roof. The riders called him 'Bounce' for twenty years. He liked it. He would rather have a name than be quiet, and the kitchen was the warmest place in the Hold, so it was where everyone ended up.","schooling":"He went to the riding-school for two years and spent most of it being sick in Sergeant Crake's bucket. At eight the lamps stayed dark for him. Spark-poor, and nobody minded. He was moved to the kitchen-school, which was his grandmother, a slate and the ovens, and learned his letters by writing riders' names on cake-twists. He could write 'Worth the grey' in nine hands before he could spell his own.","history":"He has baked for the Tempest Riders for fifty-two years. He married Ilsa, a skyray courier out of Galeford, when he was twenty-two; she took his citadel-name and gave up nothing else, and was gone eleven months of the year, and they were happy in the gaps. She died at fifty-one of a fever caught on the Shadowdeep March, and he flew her to Harrowgate himself, on a borrowed ray, with a basket of saddle-cakes for the mourners and none for himself. He has raised four children in the kitchen. Two ride, one is a netter at Harrowgate, one went to Skybridge to be a wright and sends him letters about standing still that make him laugh until he cries. When the Lightforge clerk arrived to count the horses Ludo started baking him a daily cake out of pure mischief, and is now, to his own horror, quite fond of the man. He has been keeping the Hold's goat tally himself since the quartermaster's boy started rounding down.","family":"Wife Ilsa, gone nineteen years; four children; eleven grandchildren, all of whom have his laugh. He quarrels with the quartermaster weekly and enjoys it.","hero":{"who":"Seryn Emberfall","why":"She lit the first great hearth of a Realm with her kitchen fire and nobody ever knelt to her. A cook who changed everything and stayed a cook. He likes that very much."},"band":{"act":"Old Tamsin of the Ovens","realm":"Flameborne","why":"She kneads dough on stage. He saw her once at Korrhaven on a supply run and cried into his coat, then went straight back to the kitchens and kneaded harder.","rv_vote":"Old Tamsin. 'She's seventy and so am I and I want her to win one.'"},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for whatever gets the riders home, which this year means whatever slows things down. Kesh, then. 'Chain first' is a cook's motto, he says, if you swap 'chain' for 'oven'.","edict":"Against, but gently; he says Lightforge is welcome to count everything in his kitchen as long as they eat it after."},"wants":"To bake a cake for a rider he has never baked for, which at his age means someone not yet born.","site":"tempest","town":"Tempest Hold","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:tempest:b":{"name":"Sabeth Rowan","age":19,"role":"junior Tempest Rider, second year; rides dispatch on the border road","look":"Long-legged, wind-chapped, a streak of grey already running back from her left brow through black hair. Thirty-one knots on a new red cord she touches when she talks.","voice":"Fast, quick to bristle, talks in Gale's flat present even in Bridge, and ends every brag with a grin to show it isn't one.","greet":"Wahai! You heading south? Don't. Or do, and tell me what you see.","lines":["We drill facing south twice a day now. Kesh says it's just drill. Kesh says everything's just drill.","The orchard-folk walk the border road like it's a garden path. We gallop it. One day we'll meet in the middle and someone's getting a face full of apples.","Thirty-one knots. My mum had thirty-one when she was twenty-eight. I'm not counting. I'm just good at numbers."],"about":"Sabeth Rowan, Tempest, thirty-one. I ride dispatch. If a letter needs to be in Gleamford by noon and it's already noon, it comes to me. I'll be captain by thirty or I'll be white-haired trying.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, quick, ungrammatical"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting, learned from letters, mostly the endings"}],"childhood":"Her parents ran a horse-yard on a cliff-farm south of the Anvil, chained so close to the long fall towards Lightforge that on a clear day she could see the white roofs of Gleamford. She was the middle of five and the fastest of them, and the only one who wanted to go towards the fall rather than away from it. At ten her First Fall net was strung by her father and her eldest sister, and she jumped before they had finished tying it, which is why the family still tells the story with their hands over their faces. Her mother rode for the Hold until Sabeth was eight, came home with a grey streak and a quieter voice, and never explained either.","schooling":"The Hold riding-school from eleven, under Brec Vane's predecessor, a one-eyed woman called Mag Yarrow who taught her to fall off a horse properly by pushing her off one. At the Lamp Test, at seven, the Tempest lamp flared and kept flaring. She went to the Lamp Years at Skyrend but turned down Korrhaven, to the tester's disgust, because the Hold took riders at fifteen and the Conclave would have kept her till eighteen.","history":"She joined the Tempest Riders at fifteen, the youngest in her year, and was put on dispatch because she weighs nothing and does not stop. She has cast thirty-one times, most of them to throw a horse across a gap the wind was trying to close. Her first knot she tied crying, in a barn, and has never told anyone. On her second run to Gleamford she bought sunwine off a girl at an orchard stall, a Rim-born picker called Adra Whitlow who corrected her Bridge, and they have written to each other every week since. The letters go by Sabeth's own dispatch bag. At her last Count-Off she shouted 'Sabeth Rowan, thirty-one' and the old rider beside her shouted 'Worth the grey' so warmly that she had to look at the floor. She wants a war about as much as a horse wants a fence. She would ride in one before anyone.","family":"Parents and four siblings on the cliff-farm; her mother rides no more and asks Sabeth about her cord every visit, then changes the subject before the answer.","hero":{"who":"Pell of Brightwater","why":"He would not cut his citadel's chain. Sabeth has decided this means you never let go of what you've tied yourself to, which she applies to her horse, her squad and, privately, to Adra."},"band":{"act":"Kestrel Vane","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A sky-racer who ages every season and keeps racing. Sabeth has a poster of her over her bunk, torn at one corner by a horse that ate it.","rv_vote":"Kestrel Vane, obviously, and she'd fight you for asking."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Tamra, the ex-rider, every time she can get leave for the Assembly, and once shouted so hard she lost her voice for the Count-Off.","edict":"Against, hot and sure of it. She has thirty-one knots and every one was hers to spend; no Lightforge court is going to audit what she gave."},"wants":"To make captain before her hair goes the rest of the way.","site":"tempest","town":"Tempest Hold","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:galeford":{"name":"Nim Ostry","age":33,"role":"Galeford's kit and harness trader, and its longest-serving roof-sleeper; twenty-four seasons and no ray","look":"Lanky, sunburnt across the nose and the backs of his hands from twenty-four seasons sleeping out; brown hair with no grey; a bedroll strapped permanently to the shop's back wall.","voice":"Cheerful, self-mocking, ends half his sentences with 'next year'; lights up about rays and trails off about himself.","greet":"Wahai! Bows, packs, bread. Harness too, if you can show me a bond. I can't, so don't feel bad.","lines":["Shadowdeep folk came up and slept on the roofs again. One of them asked me why I'm still up there at my age. Fair question. Lovely voice. Didn't give a name.","Skyrend takes the best young rays every season for the post. Calls it an honour. Honour's what you call it when you can't say no.","Twenty-four seasons. Garrick did thirty-one before his ray came. I've got seven to go before I'm even embarrassing."],"about":"Nim Ostry, Galeford. I sell harness I'm not allowed to wear. Every crossing season I sleep up on the shop roof with a bit of ray-cake in my pocket. Some people say it's sad. It's actually a very good night's sleep.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"a dozen signs, learned off roof-sleeping Shadowdeep visitors"}],"childhood":"Nim was the youngest of seven Ostrys, and all six before him had bonded a ray by thirteen. His mother bonded hers at eight, which is still talked about. He began roof-sleeping at nine, with a blanket and a ray-cake and a sister on each side, and when the sisters' rays came and they stopped sleeping out, he kept going alone. He was a happy child who did not think of himself as unlucky; he thought of himself as next. He knew every ray by its markings and wrote the crossing dates on the inside of the shop door with a burnt stick. His father used to bring him hot thunder-milk at midnight and stand on the ladder in silence, which Nim later realised was a way of being sorry.","schooling":"Tova Kite's roof-school, which meets on her own roof at dusk in crossing season and in her kitchen the rest of the year. She taught harness-knots, ray-calls and Gale letters, and once made every child lie flat and silent on the roof for an hour to feel how the slate moved under a passing ray. The Tempest lamp flickered once for Nim at eight and went out: Half-Spark, and not much of one.","history":"At fourteen a ray landed beside him on the roof in the small hours. He knows because there were claw-marks in the slate in the morning and a smell of chain-moss on his blanket. He had been asleep. It did not come back. He told nobody and went back up the next night. At nineteen he took over the family shop when his father's knees went, and found he had a gift for selling: honest, funny, unembarrassed. He knows every harness in Galeford and every flyer's history. He courted a Skyrend post-courier for three years, Lia Harl, who flew in and out with the mail and finally told him she could not marry a man who was always waiting. They are still friends. She brings him gossip from the capital. He has held the village record for roof-sleeping since he was twenty-eight, when old Garrick's thirty-one-season record became untouchable by simple arithmetic, and the children of Galeford now treat him as half mascot, half warning.","family":"Parents alive and doting; six siblings and their six rays; a niece, Ossa Tern, who sleeps two roofs over and tells him he snores.","hero":{"who":"Edda Frostwraith","why":"She knotted a vision so it would keep. Nim ties one knot on a string every season he sleeps out and doesn't get a ray, so he knows exactly how much he's kept trying."},"band":{"act":"Low Cloud","realm":"Stormreach","why":"R and B about grounded lovers. He says it isn't about him. He knows all the words.","rv_vote":"Low Cloud, because somebody should win for staying on the ground."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts against Skyrend's ray levy every year and for anyone who'll end it. He'd shout for Seraphine if she'd ever once mention Galeford's rays.","edict":"Against, without much heat. He thinks Lightforge should come and sleep on a roof for a season and then see if they still want to count things."},"wants":"One ray. Just one. Any colour.","site":"galeford","town":"Galeford","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:galeford":{"name":"Lenna Whitlow","age":46,"role":"village smith; forges harness rings, buckles and the brass caps of the Lantern Poles","look":"Pale, square-faced, Lightforge-straight in the back; fair hair cut level with her jaw and no grey in it; she wears her sleeves rolled to exactly the same height on both arms.","voice":"Precise, slightly too quiet for Stormreach, keeps adding how she knows a thing out of Lightforge habit, then catching herself.","greet":"Good day. Rings or buckles? I can tell you which of these I made and which I bought, if you care. Most people don't.","lines":["I keep a Day-Book. Still. In Gale. Gale has no past tense, so every page says 'I mend the pole-caps, I eat, I shout at Goldfield.' Like I'm always doing it. I suppose I am.","Goldfield's hawks dive at the young rays. I've written to Goldfield myself, in Clear, properly. They wrote back properly. Then Galeford shouted at them from the roofs. That's two ways of saying the same thing.","The Edict outlaws Penumbrists. My aunt in the Rim keeps a lamp unlit on her windowsill. I know what that means. So will they, now."],"about":"Lenna Whitlow, born in the Rim of Lightforge, Galeford by citadel-name since I married. I make the small iron here. I came for a man and stayed for the rays, and the man knows it and doesn't mind. I still say 'seen' when someone tells me something. They think it's a cough.","languages":[{"tongue":"Clear","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"fluent, with a Rim accent everyone imitates"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"}],"childhood":"She grew up in Lampwick, in the Rim of Lightforge, where the dusk is real and the antler-lamps are lit by hand. Her father was a tanner, her mother a lamp-trimmer; the family Day-Book was kept on the kitchen table and anyone in the street could come in and read it, and her uncle regularly did, to correct their spelling. At her First Telling, at twelve, she told the neighbours she wanted to live somewhere that moved, and was given the told name 'Lenna Who Wants Weather' for a year, which she hated and has since grown fond of. Her aunt Merin kept a lamp unlit in the window every evening and would not say why, and Lenna learned not to ask the way Rim children learn which lamps not to ask about.","schooling":"The Lampwick lamp-school, under Reader Ostwin Pale, who taught letters by making children write a true sentence about the day before they could go home, and kept them until it was true. At the Lamp Test, aged nine, nothing lit; spark-poor. At fourteen she was apprenticed to a Lampwick tinsmith who made antler-lamp brackets and learned that a small thing well made is not a small thing.","history":"At twenty she met Tam Fenner, a Galeford ray-flyer delivering Assembly post to Meridian, who asked her the way to the Noon Hall and then got lost anyway, three days running, outside her shop. She married him within the year in a storm wedding she did not understand a word of. Under Stormreach custom she took his citadel-name, and her father wrote her name in the family Day-Book as 'Lenna Whitlow, of Galeford, by choice', which is the nicest thing he ever wrote. She has made the brass caps for the Lantern Poles for twenty-two years and the harness rings for most of the village's young flyers. She has never flown. Tam's ray, an enormous grey female called Barley, lets Lenna oil her harness and nobody else, which Galeford regards as a scandal. Since the Edict talk began she has had three letters from Lampwick. Each is shorter. The last one was in her aunt's hand and said only 'the window is lit now', and Lenna has been forging pole-caps very late into the night since.","family":"Husband Tam, flyer; two sons, one bonded, one roof-sleeping; parents in Lampwick; Aunt Merin, whose window is lit now.","hero":{"who":"Aure of the Rim","why":"'Mercy needs shade.' Lenna grew up in the shade and found the mercy there. She has never said this aloud in Stormreach, where nobody would understand why it matters."},"band":{"act":"Two Windows","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A Lightforge-Shadowdeep couple singing duets. She married across a Realm herself, and she likes that they sound happy and argue in the harmonies.","rv_vote":"Two Windows. She will also secretly give a second thought to Plain Speaking, which reminds her of her father."},"vote":{"local":"She has only shouted at the Assembly once, last month, against the Edict, and was so startled by the sound of her own voice that she apologised to the person beside her. She likes Whit, the ledger-keeper, because ledgers are a kind of Day-Book.","edict":"Against, as only someone from the Rim can be: she knows exactly what a lamp-check looks like in a street with dusk in it."},"wants":"To get her aunt out of Lampwick before the lamp-checks reach the Rim.","site":"galeford","town":"Galeford","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:galeford":{"name":"Ossa Tern","age":11,"role":"roof-sleeper, second season; runs messages for Tova Kite and steals ray-cake","look":"Small, sharp-chinned, freckled, permanent scab on one knee; hair in a long plait tied off with a piece of old harness; carries a blanket everywhere, even in daylight.","voice":"Fast, bossy, asks three questions before you've answered one; uses grown-up sayings slightly wrong.","greet":"Have you got grain? Tova says if people give grain it means they like the village. Do you? You can say no. Don't say no.","lines":["A Shadowdeep man slept on my roof last week. He didn't want a ray. He just wanted to see all the way round, he said. I gave him my ray-cake. He gave me a look. A nice look.","Goldfield hawks dive at the baby rays. Uncle Nim says don't shout at hawks. I shout at them anyway. Still is dead!","Tova lets me hold her old ray's harness. Not oil it. Never oil it. I asked why. She said 'because'. That's not a reason, it's a door."],"about":"Ossa Tern, Galeford. I'm eleven and I'm going to get a ray this year or next year. Not like Uncle Nim. I'm going to sleep better than him. I've been practising lying still, which is hard, because still is dead.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, mostly from traders, some rude"}],"childhood":"Ossa was born on the fourth night of a crossing season, on a roof, because her mother was a roof-sleeper's teacher's helper and refused to come down. She has been told this so often that she regards roofs as hers by right. Her father mends harness and her mother flies the Skyrend post, gone for weeks and back with stories; Ossa is the eldest of three and feeds the other two ray-cake as if they were rays. She did her First Fall at ten, last year, into a net with the Stranger's Knot tied in Harrowgate, which her mother flew home with specially, and she screamed the whole way down out of pure delight. She has never been anywhere but Galeford and Skyrend and thinks Skyrend smells.","schooling":"Tova Kite's roof-school, now in her third year. Tova once took the whole class out at midnight in a gale and made them stand on the roof edge with their eyes shut and name the wind by its sound; Ossa got four of six and still talks about it. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp gave one fat spark and went out. Half-Spark, the tester said. Ossa said she would take it.","history":"Her eleven years so far have been mostly roofs, rays and being in trouble. At nine she climbed to the top of a Lantern Pole to polish its lantern because she thought it was dirty, and Galeford had to fetch her down with a ladder and three adults, and her name went into the shouted dawn apologies for a week, which she considers fame. At ten she started roof-sleeping and lasted every night of the season, which nobody her age had done in years. She has decided that her uncle Nim is a cautionary tale and has a long list of things he does wrong: he snores, he moves, he smells of the shop. Last season a young female ray circled her roof three nights running and did not land. Ossa has named it Probably. She is certain it will be back. She fed the Shadowdeep sleeper her ray-cake because he looked hungry and because, she says, a ray might be watching and want to see that she shares.","family":"Parents, two little brothers, Uncle Nim who she pretends not to like, and a grandmother in Cloudmere who sends curd she won't eat.","hero":{"who":"Tova Kite","why":"Tova bonded at nine and outlived her ray. Ossa wants the first part. She hasn't thought about the second part yet, and Tova won't let her."},"band":{"act":"The Relay Runners","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Their song 'Nothing Worth Keeping' is daft and fast and she shouts it on the roof when she can't sleep.","rv_vote":"She can't vote yet, but she has told everyone she would give her lamp to The Relay Runners, and also told her mother to."},"vote":{"local":"Too young to shout at the Assembly, which she thinks is unjust. When she can, she says, she'll shout for Tamra, because Tamra was a rider and riders are fast.","edict":"Against, because her mother is against it and because the man from Lightforge who came to Galeford once didn't like her climbing his cart."},"wants":"For Probably to land.","site":"galeford","town":"Galeford","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:galeford:b":{"name":"Garrick Mallow","age":79,"role":"keeper of the Lantern Poles; polishes all hundred lanterns in rotation and checks their lashings after every gale","look":"Wiry and bent, a ray-flyer's leather coat gone soft as cloth; hair white since thirty-five; a life-cord of ninety knots and a separate cord, unknotted, for his ray.","voice":"Slow by Stormreach standards, kindly, given to long stories that end somewhere unexpected and a little sad.","greet":"Wahai, wahai. Mind the poles. They look like they've been there forever. Nothing's been there forever. I just keep them looking it.","lines":["Goldfield wrote again. About the hawks. I took the letter up the tallest pole and shouted it back at them, word for word, in Gale. Lightforge doesn't like hearing its own letters shouted. I don't blame them, it was a dull letter.","Lanterns are never lit unless the rays are lost. Every year I polish them and every year I hope I've wasted my time.","Thirty-one seasons on the roof before mine came. Don't let Nim tell you it can't happen at thirty-three. It can happen at forty. It can happen whenever it likes."],"about":"Garrick Mallow, Galeford, ninety knots. I flew Assembly post for forty-four years on a ray I waited thirty-one seasons for. She's still alive, old girl. Sleeps on my roof. I keep the poles because somebody has to hope for nothing to happen.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, from forty years of post-stations"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a few courtesies from Spiritveil post-houses"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"enough to read a Goldfield letter and be insulted by it"}],"childhood":"Garrick was a quiet, careful boy in a loud family of net-weavers, and the only one of five who never got a ray as a child. He started roof-sleeping at eight. He slept out every season through his whole childhood and youth, and Galeford stopped teasing him around fifteen and started leaving him blankets. He was not sad about it, or not often; he says he had the best view of every crossing in the village for thirty years and learned the rays better than anyone bonded. His mother wove the nets for his First Fall and tied her own knot three times because it never looked right to her. He fell into it slowly, he says, with time to look around.","schooling":"He was taught in his grandmother's net-loft, which served as Galeford's school before Tova Kite's time; old Wenda Mallow taught letters by having children read the knots on finished nets aloud, each one a word. At the Lamp Test, at seven, the Tempest lamp lit hard. He was offered the Lamp Years at Skyrend and turned them down, because the Lamp Years would have taken him off the roofs.","history":"He wove nets and slept on roofs until he was thirty-one, and then on the second night of his thirty-second season a scarred grey ray with a torn wingtip landed beside him at dawn and put its head on his chest. He called her Sorry, because she was late. They flew Assembly post together for forty-four years: Skyrend to Meridian, Skyrend to Lanthys Vale, once all the way to the edge of the Pale with a letter for the Lantern Council so cold his eyelashes froze. He married twice, and outlived both. He has a daughter in Skyrend who wants him to move there and a ray who would never go. Since he grounded Sorry at seventy-four he has kept the Lantern Poles: polishing, lashing, replacing, logging. He is the only person in Galeford who has read every Goldfield letter about the hawks, and he answers each one himself from the top of the tallest pole, in a voice that has carried across the gulf for half a century.","family":"Daughter Irra in Skyrend, who writes every week; two wives gone; his ray, Sorry, who is fifty-three and the oldest in Galeford.","hero":{"who":"Lyria of the Veil","why":"She lit the first Spiritveil lantern in a cracked clay cup. He saw a copy of that cup at a Lanthys Vale post-house, and has thought about it every time he polishes a pole."},"band":{"act":"The Citadel Nine","realm":"Stormreach","why":"His granddaughter took him to an idol show for a joke and he loved it with his whole heart; he knows all nine by name and has opinions on their outfits.","rv_vote":"The Citadel Nine, and he will be there in the crowd with the young ones, shouting."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for Kesh at the Assembly, because Kesh is slow and Garrick trusts slow. He likes Seraphine but thinks she'll burn herself grey before forty.","edict":"Against, and tired. He has carried Lightforge's letters for half his life and he says the Edict reads like a letter someone wrote angry and posted before they'd slept."},"wants":"To die before Sorry does, and to be let go at the Spill with her watching from the roof.","site":"galeford","town":"Galeford","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:cloudmere":{"name":"Tobin Weir","age":47,"role":"cooper and trader; keeps the barrel tally for the daily water-lift to Skyrend and sells kit on the side","look":"Broad, damp, always slightly misted; beard beaded with cloud; ink-stained fingers from a ledger he carries in an oiled-skin case on his hip.","voice":"Fussy and fond, reads you numbers you didn't ask for, then tells you what the water tasted of today as if it were news.","greet":"Wahai. You're wet. Everyone's wet. Bows, packs, bread, all dry, I promise, I've a barrel for it.","lines":["Nine hundred and twelve barrels up the lift this month. Skyrend sent three back with slops on the lids. Upwind. Again.","Mistvale pays for gauze in sour berries. I have a cellar of sour berries. I have sour berries in my dreams.","Today's net-water tastes of the inside of a bell. That's a good day. Yesterday was 'wet stone'. You don't want to know about last Tuesday."],"about":"Tobin Weir, Cloudmere. I count the water. Somebody has to; Skyrend would drink the sky and say it found it lying about. My First Sip answer was 'my mother's kitchen' and they laughed for a year. I'm still trying for a better one.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, ledger-tidy"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"halting, enough to argue a price"}],"childhood":"Tobin's mother, Iva Weir, was Cloudmere's most famous netter, the one whose First Sip answer everybody still quotes: she drank from her first net at sixteen and shouted 'the bit before a storm', and the village went quiet and then cheered for an hour. Tobin grew up under that answer. His father was a cooper who sang the barrel count as he worked, and Tobin learned his numbers that way, in rhythm, fifty barrels to a verse. He was a cautious, neat child who folded his wet clothes even though they would never dry, and he liked the cooperage better than the nets because the cooperage had a roof. At ten, for his First Fall, he asked how many knots were in the net, and counted them himself before he jumped.","schooling":"Cloudmere's net-school, which is held on the windward platform among the Long Nets under the old netter Brannagh Sloe, who taught her pupils letters by writing them in the beading water on the gauze with a finger before the wind took them. You had to read fast. The Tempest lamp stayed dark for him at eight. Spark-poor. He was relieved. He learned book-keeping off a Bridge-speaking trader who stayed one winter and left him an old ledger.","history":"He hung his first net at nineteen, drank the First Sip and said 'my mother's kitchen', meaning it, and Cloudmere laughed until it fell over. He has hung four nets since and never got the answer right, and stopped hanging them at thirty and took over his father's cooperage instead. He is good at it. He took on the barrel tally for the Skyrend lift when the old tally-keeper drowned in a cloud, which happens in Cloudmere about once a decade and which nobody finds funny. He married Hanne, a lift-hand, at twenty-five; she kept her citadel-name, which was also Cloudmere, so the wedding was very short. They have a daughter who nets. Tobin has read every surviving account of the nine-day lull, the only time the nets ran dry, and has a theory about it that he will tell you whether or not you ask. Three seasons ago a Lightforge buyer came up from Clearwater and asked to buy storm-water. Tobin sold him one barrel. Now he sells one a week.","family":"Wife Hanne, a lift-hand; daughter Bel, a netter, who got her First Sip answer right first time ('cold iron and somebody's breath') and tries not to rub it in.","hero":{"who":"Iva Weir, his mother","why":"She said the right thing at the right moment once, in front of everyone, and lived the rest of her life as if it were nothing. He would like to manage either half of that."},"band":{"act":"The Rainmakers","realm":"Stormreach","why":"A drum collective. He says their rhythm is exactly the rhythm of a full barrel hitting the lift-plate, and he times his loading to them.","rv_vote":"The Rainmakers, because they never win and he likes the arithmetic of a long shot."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for Whit, the ledger-keeper on the Triumvirate, and is the only man in Cloudmere who knows the names of all three Chain-Wardens without looking them up.","edict":"Against, mildly. He thinks Lightforge counts the wrong things. He would happily register his barrels. Not his neighbours."},"wants":"To give one First Sip answer that makes Cloudmere go quiet.","site":"cloudmere","town":"Cloudmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:cloudmere":{"name":"Agna Spoke","age":66,"role":"village smith; forges barrel hoops, net-hooks and the long climbing-spikes the netters use on the Long Nets","look":"Short and wide as a barrel, hair cropped grey, one ear a little twisted; her forearms are tattooed with three citadel-names, two crossed out.","voice":"Loud even for Stormreach, always slightly too loud on her left side, swears in three dialects and laughs at her own temper.","greet":"WAHAI. Sorry. Left ear. Dunmere lightning, years ago. Talk into my right, and talk up.","lines":["Skyrend tips its slops off the high chains upwind of the nets. I made a hook to catch them. Twelve feet long. Then I made a bigger one. Assembly says I can't use it. Assembly doesn't say why.","Water came down grey off the east nets. Sorrel tipped it. Good. I'd have tipped Sorrel after it if they hadn't.","Three husbands and I outlasted the lot. One's dead, one's in Thistledown, one's in a sulk. That's the order I liked them in, too."],"about":"Agna Spoke. Born Dunmere, married Thistledown, married Tempest, married Cloudmere. Kept the last name because the last husband's the one I'm still fond of, and he's dead, so he can't spoil it. I make hooks and hoops. I make them well. I make them angry.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, in the Dunmere bellow"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, profane"},{"tongue":"Kindle","level":"swearing only, from a Flameborne coal-trader she once threw in a rain-pond"}],"childhood":"Agna was born on Dunmere in a dry month, which is to say without a strike number, and grew up the only plainly named child on her stretch of the Rod Forest. The others called her 'Nought'. She thumped them. Her father was a rod-smith who planted copper in the rock and let her hold the hammer, and her mother hung goat in storms and came in smelling of burnt hair. Agna was a furious, funny, square little girl who could not sit still and could not keep quiet and did not see why she should. At nine she climbed a rod in a storm to prove she wasn't scared, and a strike hit the rod next to hers, and she came down with her left ear ringing, and it has never really stopped.","schooling":"Dunmere's school was the cave, the one everyone hides in during the worst storms; the teacher, an old herder called Twelve Yeld, taught letters by scratching them in the soot on the cave wall and made them shout every word, because in Dunmere if you can't shout a thing you don't know it. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp flickered once. Half-Spark. She has cast four times in her life, all to stop a fire.","history":"At nineteen she married Fitch Harlow of Thistledown, a post-cutter, when the two rocks drifted together in grazing season, and took his citadel-name, and found within a year that a quiet man and a loud woman can love each other and not be able to live in the same room. She left him at twenty-four and went to Tempest Hold to smith for the Riders, and married a farrier there, Gil Brand, who was handsome and mean, and that lasted four years and ended with her throwing his anvil off the stable bastion. She came to Cloudmere at thirty-one to forge for the cooperage and married Oskar Spoke, a net-mender with gentle hands and no sense of humour, and they were happy for twenty-eight years. He died at sixty, of a slip on the Long Nets, and she climbed up and finished his mend herself, in the rain, with a needle in her teeth like Jory taught her. She has been the Cloudmere smith for thirty-five years. She is responsible for every net-hook on the windward edge and for most of the village's shouted dawn apologies, many of them her own.","family":"No children, by choice. Three husbands: Fitch in Thistledown, Gil in Tempest Hold, Oskar let go at the Spill. A nephew in Dunmere who sends her goat.","hero":{"who":"Torra Cinder","why":"The Banked Fire, tried in Lightforge for 'concealed anger'. Agna has never concealed an angry thought in her life and she thinks a woman tried for hiding hers must have been the bravest person in the Nine."},"band":{"act":"Hot Corner","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Reggaeton crew. She heard them at a Skyrend market and danced in the rain until the trader she'd come to shout at gave up and gave her a discount.","rv_vote":"Hot Corner, loudly, and she'll tell you she means it."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts at the Assembly for whoever will let her use the big hook on Skyrend's slops. She thinks Seraphine is magnificent and should eat more.","edict":"Against. 'Lightforge wants to count what's in me? Come and count it. Bring a lamp. Bring three.'"},"wants":"To catch one barrel of Skyrend's slops on the long hook and send it back up the lift with a note.","site":"cloudmere","town":"Cloudmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:cloudmere":{"name":"Sorrel Wick","age":29,"role":"netter on the east edge of the Long Nets; the one who tipped the grey water","look":"Thin, long-faced, always cold; dark hair under a waxed hood; their hands are cracked from net-water and they wear their life-cord, with six knots, on the outside of their coat.","voice":"Quiet, slow for Cloudmere, careful with words; says 'probably' like a Frostgrave elder and nobody knows where they picked it up.","greet":"Lamp's lit. Oh. Sorry. That's a Spiritveil thing. Wahai. Do you need water?","lines":["It came off the east nets grey at the edges. Cold. Colder than cloud should be. I tipped it. All of it. Over the side. Probably nothing.","Ambrose shouted another apology this morning. That's the fourth this week. I think he likes it.","My sister's at Korrhaven. Tempest Tower. She writes that the porridge is terrible and everyone's whispering. I believe the porridge."],"about":"Sorrel Wick. I work the east nets, the ones closest to the long drop. I've hung three nets and drunk the First Sip three times and I've never been allowed to keep my answer. I'm quiet. In Cloudmere that makes people think you're sad. I'm not. I'm listening.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"courtesies and a little more, learned from a Spiritveil copyist who boarded with the family"}],"childhood":"Sorrel was the elder of two, and their younger sister Tansy was a blaze from the start: loud, quick, sparking at four, setting the washing on fire with a sneeze. Sorrel learned early to be the quiet one in the room, because the room had someone loud already. Their parents were coopers. A Spiritveil copyist, Ithra Vael, boarded with the family for six years on a long contract copying Cloudmere's water-ledgers for the Lantern Archives, and she taught Sorrel Lanthic courtesies and how to sit very still and listen to a house. Sorrel's First Fall was the only one anyone in the village can remember where the child stepped off without a word and didn't make a sound all the way down.","schooling":"Brannagh Sloe's net-school on the windward platform, where Sorrel was the fastest reader of letters written on beading gauze because they never looked away. At eight the Tempest lamp flickered and went out for them: Half-Spark. They have cast six times, each to hold a net still in a gust. Their sister's lamp blazed. Sorrel went to the platform. Tansy went to Korrhaven.","history":"They have netted the east edge for eleven years. Hung their first net at eighteen, drank the First Sip and said 'paper', and was told no; hung their second at twenty-two and said 'the moment a lamp goes out', and the village shuffled; hung their third at twenty-six and said nothing at all, which caused a small Assembly row about whether silence was an answer. It was ruled not. Sorrel has never married, has had two quiet loves and ended both kindly, and lives alone in a room above the cooperage that Tobin Weir rents them for less than he should. Their sister Tansy is in her fourth year at the Tempest Tower and writes every month; lately the letters say the Edict is all anyone talks about, that Lightforge students are being asked to keep lists, and that Tansy has been asked by one of them what her sister does. Last week the water came off the east nets with a grey edge and as cold as Frostgrave, and Sorrel did what anyone would. Mostly.","family":"Parents in Cloudmere; sister Tansy, nineteen, a Full Spark at Korrhaven; Ithra the copyist, who still sends honey cakes every Long Evening.","hero":{"who":"Vaelora Lanthys","why":"She holds a seat on the Council and chose silence. Sorrel has never heard anyone say what she chose it for, and admires her more for that."},"band":{"act":"Isveig","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"She sings the saddest song perfectly and feels nothing. Sorrel thinks that is the most honest performance in Realm Vision and has listened to it in the rain on the platform until their hands went white.","rv_vote":"Isveig, which in Cloudmere is considered an odd thing to admit."},"vote":{"local":"They have never shouted at the Assembly. They go, stand, and listen. If they ever did shout, it would be for Whit, because Whit writes things down.","edict":"Torn. Sorrel hates it, but Tansy is a Full Spark at Korrhaven surrounded by students keeping lists, and a register might at least say what's allowed; secrecy frightens Sorrel more than counting."},"wants":"For one First Sip answer to be kept.","site":"cloudmere","town":"Cloudmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:cloudmere:b":{"name":"Ambrose Fell","age":40,"role":"curd-maker; sets thunder-milk curd in open barrels in the rain; Cloudmere's most frequent dawn apologiser","look":"Tall and stooped, with a rain-shiny bald head and a big gentle face; an apron stiff with curd; voice hoarse every morning from the roofs.","voice":"Earnest, over-apologetic, warm; describes curd like other men describe sunsets.","greet":"Wahai, friend. Try this. No, this one. That one tastes of a cloud that came over the Shadowdeep March. You can tell, it's sulky.","lines":["I shouted an apology this morning to Skyrend for something I thought about them. Not said. Thought. You can't be too careful.","There's a woman in Mistvale I owe an apology. Eight years. I've written it. I've practised it. I've never shouted it. It has to be the right roof.","Each barrel of curd tastes of the cloud that filled it. People think it's a saying. It isn't. I'll prove it. Close your eyes."],"about":"Ambrose Fell, Cloudmere. I make the curd. I also do most of the morning apologies on this rock, mine and some other people's, for a small fee. I've a big voice and a soft heart and a bad habit of saying what I think about jam.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, sentimental"}],"childhood":"Ambrose was born to two curd-makers who argued about the curd every day and never about anything else, and he grew up thinking that was what love sounded like. He was a large, clumsy, sweet child who apologised to furniture. At seven he broke a neighbour's barrel and climbed onto the roof at dawn to shout sorry, as Stormreach children are taught, and found he could shout louder than anybody else his age and that the neighbour came out in her nightdress to shout back that it was all right. By ten he was doing other children's dawn apologies for sweets. His First Fall was into a net with a hole in it, discovered on the way down; he says he went through it and the cloud caught him, like Jory, and everyone says that is not what happened.","schooling":"Brannagh Sloe's net-school, where he was hopeless at reading letters on gauze and very good at shouting them. He was sent home once for apologising to the wind. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the lamps stayed dark. Spark-poor. He learned curd from his parents, in the rain, by tasting, and can tell a cloud that came in off the gulf from one that came off the high chains with his eyes shut.","history":"He took over his parents' curd barrels at twenty-five, when his mother's hands went stiff, and has made them more famous: Cloudmere curd now goes to Skyrend tables with a little slate tag saying which cloud filled it. He has never married. He has asked twice, both times from a roof at dawn, and been told no both times from the next roof over, kindly. At thirty-two he took a cart of curd to a Skybridge market and was set up beside Wenna Strake of Mistvale, who gave him her mist-jam to try. He said, honestly and thoughtlessly, that it tasted of rain. In Cloudmere this is the worst thing you can say about anything. She has not spoken to him since, and he has written her apology, revised it forty times, and never shouted it, because Mistvale is in a fog and he can't see a roof to shout from. Meanwhile he apologises for half the village every dawn: for Agna's temper, for Tobin's ledger, for Skyrend, which never apologises for anything.","family":"His mother, Grete, alive and critical of his curd; his father let go at the Spill six years ago; a cousin in Harrowgate who sings at lettings.","hero":{"who":"Vey Who Said It","why":"She founded the Open-Mouthed by saying her own full name aloud in Shadowdeep. Ambrose thinks saying the thing you're not supposed to say, out loud, is the bravest act there is, and he'd like to manage it once in Mistvale."},"band":{"act":"Corran Vale","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Storm-shouted apology rap. Ambrose knows every bar and has borrowed several for his dawn work.","rv_vote":"Corran Vale, with a shouted apology to every other act for not voting for them."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts at the Assembly for anything that keeps people talking and against anything that ends with a cut chain. He'd shout for Seraphine, then apologise to Lightforge for it at dawn.","edict":"Against, but he has already apologised to Lightforge for being against it, twice, from the highest roof in Cloudmere."},"wants":"To shout his apology to Wenna Strake from a roof she can actually hear.","site":"cloudmere","town":"Cloudmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:skybridge":{"name":"Pernel Sough","age":55,"role":"iron broker and town trader; buys Metalcrown bar-iron for the Chainyards and sells kit and bread to wrights and travellers","look":"Lean, iron-grey at both brows from two charges taken long ago; a broker's waxed coat with iron filings worked into its seams; a tally of prices chalked on the inside of her forearm.","voice":"Dry, quick, careful, never says a price twice the same way; mixes Shiftcant slang into her Gale and enjoys watching wrights not understand it.","greet":"Shift. Sorry. Habit. Wahai. Bows over there, bread here, and if you've any salvage iron I'll tell you honestly what it's worth and then pay you a bit less.","lines":["Metalcrown iron rings clean as a bell. Honest iron. Price goes up every month. You'd think a Realm that honest would have a chair at the table by now. I'm not saying anything. I'm reading you a price.","Stonehold won't sell us a nail since Brightwater. Fine. We don't need them. That's what I tell the yard, anyway.","The wrights sit to eat. Everybody else in Stormreach thinks that's the saddest thing in the world. Try standing at a swage twelve hours. You'd sit."],"about":"Pernel Sough, Skybridge. I buy the iron. Every chain the Realm hangs from started as a bar I signed for. I've two knots on my cord, both from a stupid summer, and I've spent the rest of my life doing sums instead. Sums don't age you. They just make you unpopular.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"good, including the Warm-talk slang"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"halting, enough to read a road-elder's letter slowly"}],"childhood":"Her family were iron-runners: they flew Metalcrown bar-iron from the long-wagon depot at the Realm Bridge to Skybridge in cradles slung under three skyrays at once, and Pernel spent her childhood strapped into a cradle among the bars. She learned to count iron before she could read. Her grandmother kept one heirloom on a nail by the stove: a single Stonehold-forged link, black with age, from the centuries before Brightwater, when Stonehold still sold Stormreach its iron. She would ring it with a spoon and make Pernel listen to how low it sang. 'Older iron,' she said. 'Heavier people.' At her First Fall Pernel asked whether the net was Stonehold rope, and her father looked at her a long time before he said no.","schooling":"The Chainyard school at Skybridge, which teaches wrights' children letters, arithmetic and the notes of chain by ear; the schoolmistress, Hob Tarrant, made pupils count links aloud in every lesson, even poetry. At eight the Tempest lamp lit, low: Half-Spark. At seventeen she charged twice in one summer, showing off to a boy on a cliff-farm, and woke with grey at both brows. She has not cast since.","history":"She went into iron at twenty, buying for the Chainyards on commission, in the years when Skybridge's Metalcrown orders first began to double. She learned Shiftcant in a winter, flew to the Smeltway with a phrasebook and a borrowed ray, and came back with a contract and a set of drinking songs. She has been Skybridge's iron broker for thirty years. She married a link-tester, Abel Sough, took his citadel-name, which was Skybridge already, and has two grown children who are both wrights and both sit down to eat. She knows Metalcrown better than any trader in Stormreach and likes it more than she lets on: its honesty about iron, its shift-bells, its old men who still talk about the Foundry Commons. She is watching the price of bar-iron climb every month and has a fairly exact idea of what it is buying in Metalcrown, and she says so to nobody. Two years ago a Stonehold road-elder at Hearthward wrote to her in careful Bridge offering iron by a quiet road. She said yes.","family":"Husband Abel, a link-tester; two children, both wrights; her father, ninety, who still won't say what Brightwater was.","hero":{"who":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","why":"'One shift, one vote. Even you.' She heard an old Metalcrown smelter say it in a gin-house with tears in his eyes, and wrote it inside her ledger cover."},"band":{"act":"Slag Hill","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"Industrial drill against spark-born privilege. She's a Half-Spark who never got anything for it, and she likes a band that's angry about the right people.","rv_vote":"Slag Hill, and one lamp in her head for Metalcrown generally, because Lightforge will give them none."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Whit at the Assembly, because Whit keeps ledgers and Pernel trusts anyone who'll show you the sums.","edict":"Against. She has also noticed that Metalcrown is smiling about it, and she knows what Metalcrown's smiles cost, because she pays for them by the bar."},"wants":"To keep both iron roads open without either town ever knowing about the other.","site":"skybridge","town":"Skybridge","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:skybridge":{"name":"Rufus Cleat","age":41,"role":"chain-wright of the Skybridge Chainyards; forges bridge-chain on the Great Swage; son of the yard-master","look":"Huge, slow-moving, burn-scarred across both forearms, with his mother's square jaw; dark hair, no grey; sits a little lopsided because he favours his left ear.","voice":"Deep and unhurried, speaks in single facts, turns his head slightly when you talk; fond of a joke that takes a minute to arrive.","greet":"Stand there. No, there. You're swinging. Most visitors swing. It's fine. You'll get used to it or you'll leave.","lines":["Every link that comes off the Swage, Mum listens to. If it rings wrong, it goes back in the fire. Twenty-two years. She hasn't missed one. I've missed a few. Don't tell her.","They call us stonefoot. Then they walk across our chain to their supper. I don't mind. Somebody has to stand still so you lot can run.","Stand-Day's next month. Children last a minute. Visitors fall over. Riders last about as long as children, which they hate."],"about":"Rufus Cleat. I forge chain. My mother runs the yard. My grandmother ran it before that, and I'll run it after if I'm good enough, which I'm not sure. I've never been more than a mile from this anvil. I'd like to see the bridges my chain holds up. Just once.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, slow"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a few iron words; enough to read a bar-stamp"}],"childhood":"Rufus was born in the Chainyards, in the room behind the Swage where the yard-master's family has slept for four generations, and spent his first years asleep under it while it swung. Other children treated him as a curiosity; he did not mind, because he had the Swage. His mother, Maud, taught him to listen to chain the way other parents teach their children to talk: she would ring a link and wait, ring another and wait, until he could hear the flaw. He was slow, steady and big for his age. His First Fall was off the yard gantry into a net of chain-wrights' rope, and he thought, on the way down, that he had never moved so fast in his life, and did not like it.","schooling":"The Chainyard school under Hob Tarrant, who made him count links aloud and who once, during a lesson, stopped the class to ring a chain-link against the window frame and have every child say what was wrong with it. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, nothing lit. Spark-poor, like most wrights. He apprenticed on the Swage at twelve and has never done anything else.","history":"He has forged chain for twenty-nine years. He was made journeyman at twenty and master-wright at thirty-two, the year his mother's ear for chain was tested by the Assembly after a cliff-farm chain parted in a gale; she was cleared again, and he stood behind her at the hearing and said nothing and hasn't forgiven Skyrend since. He married Lin Teague, a Barleycross maltster, at twenty-six; under Stormreach custom she took his citadel-name, as wrights' spouses must, and came to live in the yards, and found standing still harder than she had imagined. They have two children. About ten years ago, after a fever, his right ear began to go. He can still hear chain. He can't hear it as well as his mother thinks he can. He has learned to watch Juniper Rask's face when they test a link and match his verdict to it.","family":"Mother Maud, yard-master; wife Lin; two children, ten and seven, who have both done Stand-Day longer than any rider.","hero":{"who":"Hedda Brannick","why":"The yard-master who was tried three generations ago for standing still for the wrong reasons; she was cleared and walked out of the yards the same day and never stood still again. Rufus thinks she was right to go, and is ashamed of thinking it."},"band":{"act":"The Tether Sisters","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Chain-yard harmony folk. Their song 'Cut the Chain' is banned in the Skybridge yards, and he sings it under his breath at the Swage.","rv_vote":"The Tether Sisters, quietly, on the slip."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for Kesh, 'chain first', which is the closest the Triumvirate comes to a wright's view of the world.","edict":"Against. 'It's a chain forged by people standing still in Meridian. It'll break.' He means it as a wright means it."},"wants":"To walk Korr's Span once, end to end, and look down at the chain.","site":"skybridge","town":"Skybridge","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:skybridge":{"name":"Juniper Rask","age":22,"role":"link-tester in the Chainyards; strikes every finished link and listens for the flaw","look":"Slight, wiry, with a shaved head under a felt cap and a bone-handled tuning hammer on a cord round their neck; ears pierced with tiny iron rings of their own forging.","voice":"Fast, funny, unfiltered, hums constantly, names notes instead of feelings ('that's a sour A').","greet":"Wahai! Hold still. No, really. You're humming. Everyone hums. You're humming a bit flat.","lines":["Metalcrown iron rings clean. Clean as a bell. Stonehold iron rang lower. Older. Don't ask me how I know what Stonehold iron sounds like. I'm twenty-two.","Maud's been right four times about which chain goes next. I think she's right about a fifth. I'm not saying which. Not till she does.","Stand-Day, I can last three hours. Rufus lasts all day. He says it's practice. I think he falls asleep."],"about":"Juniper Rask. I listen to chain for a living. Most people think that's a joke. Most people walk across my chains every day. I came to the yards at fourteen because I could hear things wrong. It's useful here. It's annoying everywhere else.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good, slangy"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a few words, mostly from battle-rap lyrics"}],"childhood":"Juniper was the fourth child of a skyray courier and a cliff-farmer on one of the chained farms south of Skybridge, and the only one who heard the chains. They'd lie awake listening to the farm's anchor-chain creak and could tell their parents by the note when a gale was coming, a day early. At six they told their father that one of the corner chains sounded 'tired'; he laughed, then checked it, then stopped laughing. The farm had the chain replaced and the old one, broken open in the yards, had a crack all the way through. Juniper was a nervy, chattering child who couldn't stand loud music and loved it anyway. Their First Fall was the only one they remember in total silence, because they were listening to the net.","schooling":"A cliff-farm school on the next chain over, taught by an old rider called Sef Arrow who mostly taught by telling stories and making children guess the ending. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp gave a thin hum before it lit; the tester said she had never heard a lamp do that. Half-Spark. At fourteen Maud Cleat heard about the tired chain and sent for them.","history":"They came to the Chainyards at fourteen as Maud's apprentice in the ear, and are the first non-wright in thirty years given the tuning hammer. As a link-tester they may move about the yards, which wrights may not, and they spend their days between the Swage, the test racks and the cooling troughs. They have a reputation in Skybridge for brutal honesty about chain and everything else, and for falling in love loudly with people who cannot hear the difference between a G and an A. They rap, badly, in a Skybridge tavern crew called the Loose Links. They have noticed that Rufus Cleat turns his good ear towards them every time they test, and that his verdicts follow their face, and they have said nothing, because they love the big man and because the chain is still sound. Last month, standing at the yard window, they heard the great Assembly-hall chain at Skyrend sing in a gust and thought it sounded tired.","family":"Parents on the cliff-farm; three older siblings; a younger cousin who wants to be a link-tester too and can't hear anything at all.","hero":{"who":"Maud Cleat","why":"She hasn't left the yards in twenty-two years and knows every chain in Skyrend by its note. Juniper wants that ear, and is a little afraid of the twenty-two years."},"band":{"act":"Calder Glass","realm":"Lightforge","why":"A battle rapper who can't lie. Juniper can't lie about chain. They think Calder is the only performer in Realm Vision who sounds the way a good link does.","rv_vote":"Calder Glass, which Skybridge considers treachery."},"vote":{"local":"They shout for Seraphine at the Assembly whenever she speaks, mostly because she has a voice with no flaws in it.","edict":"Against. 'They want to listen to everybody. They don't know how. I've seen their ears.'"},"wants":"For Maud to say the fifth chain before they have to.","site":"skybridge","town":"Skybridge","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:skybridge:b":{"name":"Ord Karrow","age":63,"role":"keeper of the Chainyard benches; cooks forge-goat on the swage plates and runs the only sit-down kitchen in Stormreach","look":"Massive, slow, bearded, with the heavy shoulders of a man who did the Lift twice a day for forty years; his left little finger is grey stone to the second knuckle.","voice":"Deep Holdspeech rhythm under his Gale, stress on the last word; says very little, and what he says lands.","greet":"Set. Sit. Eat. Nobody minds here.","lines":["Stonehold says Skybridge betrayed it, buying Metalcrown iron. Stormreach says Stonehold betrayed it, at Brightwater. I cook goat. Both sides eat it sitting down.","Rufus is a good wright. Good. His ear's going. Everybody knows but his mother.","Skyrend sends us sympathy because we sit to eat. I send them back carry-bread. Nobody laughs. I laugh."],"about":"Ord Karrow. Born in Kell Deep. Came up with the horse-buyers. Stayed. Skybridge took me as one of its own when Stonehold wouldn't have me back. I cook. I sit. I'm the only man in Stormreach who isn't sorry about it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Gale","level":"fluent, heavy accent, still uses a past tense nobody else has"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Ord was born in Kell Deep, the second son of a road-family whose mile-marker stands nine miles out on the eastern Long Road. He did the Lift every morning with his father and older brother, Durrin, and at fifteen did his Shouldering, carrying a marker-stone a road-mile without setting it down, and was so proud he cried, which in Stonehold is permitted where complaining is not. He was a solid, gentle, unhurried boy who liked cooking more than carrying, which his father tolerated as long as he carried as well. He remembers lidded stew under a dough lid, his grandmother's voice reading the initials on the markers as they walked past, and the first time he saw a skyray flying iron over the passes, and how he couldn't stop looking up.","schooling":"A Stonehold road-school, taught on the march between markers by Road-Elder Hesk Durrow, who made children read the initials of the dead off every marker they passed and say one thing about each: 'carried a good load', 'liked figs', 'did not complain'. At the Lamp Test, aged nine, the Gravem lamp lit faintly: Half-Spark. He never went to Korrhaven. He has cast once, to hold a falling cart, and his finger has been stone since.","history":"At twenty he went as cook with his uncle's horse-buying party, the one Stonehold sends every year to call Thistledown's horses flighty and buy them anyway. On the way back a gale put the whole party down at Skybridge for nine days, where the wrights fed them forge-goat sitting at the yard benches, and Ord thought it was the most sensible thing he had ever seen in Stormreach. He went back the next year, and the next, and on the sixth he did not leave. He had married a Skybridge wright, Annet Brule, and took her citadel-name, and Stonehold, which has not sold Skybridge a nail since Brightwater, sees that kind of choice as choosing sides. His father did not write again. His brother Durrin did, once a year, short letters about roads. Ord has kept the yard benches for thirty-five years, cooks the forge-goat on the swage plates himself, and does the Lift alone in the back room with a stone he brought from Kell Deep. Annet died nine years ago. He sat at her letting at the Spill, which in Stormreach is what you do at a funeral, and so for once he was exactly right.","family":"Wife Annet, let go nine years ago; a daughter who is a wright; his brother Durrin, a road-elder at Hearthward, who writes once a year about roads and never about anything else.","hero":{"who":"Brask Kell","why":"The anchor-master who held Korr's Span and became a Stillman; Stormreach riders throw stones at him on the pass. Ord flew there once, alone, and took the stones off him one by one and put them in a sack. He didn't throw them anywhere."},"band":{"act":"Mason Grey","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Quarry baritone soul. It sounds like Kell Deep. It sounds like his father not writing.","rv_vote":"Mason Grey, and he will not explain it, and nobody in the yards asks."},"vote":{"local":"He does not shout at the Assembly. He stands at the back and raises a hand, which doesn't count. If it did count, it would be for Kesh.","edict":"Against, the Stonehold way: not a riot, just a refusal. 'I'll hold.'"},"wants":"To have one letter from his brother that isn't about roads.","site":"skybridge","town":"Skybridge","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:mistvale":{"name":"Wenna Strake","age":38,"role":"jam-boiler and village trader; sells mist-jam, dried berries, kit and bread from a shop hung on the top terrace","look":"Round-faced, red-cheeked from steam, permanently squinting; brown hair tied up in a cloth stained purple; a bell of her own tied to her apron so customers can find her in the fog.","voice":"Brisk and teasing, watches your face when you taste things, holds a grudge like a pot holds heat.","greet":"There you are. Out of the fog. Try the jam. Go on. I want to see your eyes water.","lines":["Cloudmere takes our gauze-money in berries and then complains about the berries. We know they're sour. They're from Mistvale. That's the point.","Skybridge takes the best berries for the yard kitchens and leaves us the sour ones. That's all of them. They still pick through like there's a choice.","A Cloudmere curd-man once told me my jam tasted of rain. Eight years. I can still hear him. I'd know his voice in any fog."],"about":"Wenna Strake, Mistvale. I boil jam. I sell what's left after the jam. People come down the chains asking for the sweet berries from the frozen winter. I tell them the truth: there aren't any. Then I sell them something nearly as good.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, sales-patter quick"}],"childhood":"Wenna's mother boiled jam and her father chained terraces, and she spent her early years in a basket hung from the terrace chain so her mother could hear her bell while she picked. She was a determined, greedy, cheerful child who ate berries until her mouth was raw. At six she decided to find the sweet berries the old story talks about, and spent a whole season tasting every bush on every terrace, and found none, and was sick in a fog so thick nobody saw. She learned from her mother to judge a jam by the face of the person tasting it. Her First Fall net was tied with a Harrowgate Stranger's Knot her father traded for a pot of jam, which she still thinks was a fair price.","schooling":"Mistvale's terrace-school is taught blindfolded half the time; the bell-keeper before Pell Rook, a thin, strict woman called Aud Tolling, made her pupils learn the bells before their letters and their letters by touch, cut into slate. At Wenna's bell-day, aged eight, she named every bell first time. At the Lamp Test she was spark-poor. She learned sums from selling jam, and Bridge from the Low Reach riders who buy it.","history":"She took over the family jam-boiling at twenty-two and the village shop at thirty, when the old trader moved up to Skybridge for his knees. She has turned mist-jam from a local joke into something the Low Reach patrols buy by the cartload against the gum-sickness, and she is proud of that in a way she shows by charging more. She married Doran Strake, a terrace-chainer, and took his citadel-name, which was also Mistvale, and lost him to a slipped chain six years ago; she does not talk about it, except to say Mistvale was quieter for a season because she rang his bell herself, every evening, until Pell gently took it down. At a Skybridge market eight years ago a Cloudmere curd-maker tasted her jam and said it tasted of rain. She has never forgiven him and has never stopped thinking about it. Last winter she bought a crate of Frostgrave lake-ice off a trader at Skybridge, froze a batch of berries on the vine, and found they came out a little sweeter. She has been selling them, quietly, to people who come asking.","family":"Husband Doran, let go at the Spill six years ago; no children; a mother, eighty, who still tells her when the jam has caught.","hero":{"who":"The Flameborne King","why":"Anyone may refuse him, and he has to answer, usually with bread. Wenna answers every complaint with jam, and she thinks that is the only sensible way to rule anything."},"band":{"act":"Mist-Pear","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Wedding R and B. She had it at her own wedding, from a borrowed music-box, and plays it now on the anniversary, standing at the pot.","rv_vote":"Mist-Pear, because it's sweet and Mistvale could use something sweet."},"vote":{"local":"She doesn't trust any of the three Chain-Wardens, on principle, and shouts for Seraphine at the Assembly because 'at least she shouts back'.","edict":"Against, practically: Lightforge counting sparks means Lightforge counting jam next, and she's not having her prices in anyone's Day-Book."},"wants":"An apology, shouted properly, from a roof she can hear.","site":"mistvale","town":"Mistvale","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:mistvale":{"name":"Marten Gorse","age":30,"role":"village smith and bell-caster; forges terrace-chain fittings and casts the small fog-bells","look":"Narrow, pale, quiet-footed, with dark eyes that seem to adjust to fog faster than other people's; burn-marks on his fingertips from tuning hot bronze; a Shadowdeep hand-cant habit of keeping his hands low.","voice":"Soft, unhurried, says less than he knows; pauses before names as though checking whether he is allowed to say them.","greet":"You found the forge. Most people walk past it twice. Bells, hooks, chain? Say which. Quietly is fine.","lines":["If you hear a bell in the fog that isn't one of ours, walk the other way. Don't stop, don't run. That's what Pell says. Pell's right.","My mother came up from the Shadowdeep March. Mistvale took her in because it couldn't see her face for the first year. By the time it could, it liked her.","Pell retunes a bell every time somebody gets lost. I cast the new ones. He hums the note at me and I make it. I'm not sure he knows how good his ear is."],"about":"Marten Gorse, Mistvale. I make the bells and the hooks they hang from. My mother is from the March, my father was from here. I've a short name and a long one, like her people, and you'll get the short one, like everyone. It's Vesk. The long one is spoken for.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Undertongue","level":"good, from his mother; he never says 'I' in it"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"His mother, a Shadowdeep March woman called Siv, came up into Mistvale's fog as a young courier and fell in love with a bell-caster, Ewan Gorse, who never once asked her full name. Marten grew up between them: Gale in the forge, Undertongue in the kitchen, hand-cant at table, so that his father learned to sign 'pass the jam' and 'stop arguing'. He was a watchful, private child in a village of shouting pickers, and the fog suited him. He could walk the terraces without bells by the age of six. His mother told him his full name at nine, in a whisper, and has never said it again, and he is not sure she remembers she told him, which he keeps to himself. His First Fall was the quietest the village could remember until Sorrel Wick's at Cloudmere.","schooling":"Aud Tolling's terrace-school, where he was best at bells and worst at shouting; Aud once made him shout his bell-names at the end of a lesson and he managed it in a whisper so precise that she let it count. On his bell-day he named every bell, and the hidden one she had hung as a trick. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Veil lamp and the Tempest lamp both stayed dark. Spark-poor. His mother was relieved and would not say why.","history":"He learned casting from his father, who died when Marten was nineteen of a lung-fever from forty years of bronze fumes in a wet valley. Marten took over the forge and the bells. He is good, better than his father, and Pell Rook has come to rely on him for every retuning, humming the note he wants and never quite saying thanks. Marten has never married. He courts, quietly, a Low Reach rider who comes for jam twice a season and writes to him in plain Bridge. His mother still lives in the cottage below the forge, still meets people from the March in the fog at night, and three years ago asked him, in hand-cant, whether he could cast a bell that did not sound like any of Mistvale's. He cast it. Then he cast two more. They hang somewhere in the fog that is not on any terrace, and he has never asked what they guide, or who.","family":"Mother Siv, of a March Well she does not name; father Ewan, let go six years ago; a half-brother in Shadowdeep he has met once, in the dark.","hero":{"who":"Veilrend","why":"Shadowdeep's First Blade, whose face nobody knows. Marten likes a hero you can't make a statue of. He thinks it keeps them honest."},"band":{"act":"Under Haskerry","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Whispered folk. It is the only music he can play in the forge at night without his mother telling him to keep it down.","rv_vote":"Under Haskerry, written on the slip in his smallest hand."},"vote":{"local":"He goes to the Assembly and does not shout, which in Stormreach is close to an opinion. If he had to, he would shout for Tamra, because she listens before she talks.","edict":"Against, the March way: he would not riot, he would quietly make it harder. He suspects his bells already are."},"wants":"To know what his three bells are guiding without having to ask.","site":"mistvale","town":"Mistvale","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:mistvale":{"name":"Ottilie Haw","age":84,"role":"berry-picker, still, on the lowest terrace; Mistvale's oldest working hand","look":"Tiny and bent nearly double, with hands stained purple to the wrist and a fog-white plait to her knees; a bell on each ankle so the village always knows where she is.","voice":"Cackling, sharp, contrary; tells you you're wrong before you've finished, and is often right.","greet":"Give us some grain, then. Go on. Mistvale's been short since the chain slipped in spring. We'll remember. I'll remember. Somebody has to.","lines":["I ate a sweet berry once. A real one. From the frozen winter. Don't tell me it was before I was born. I was there. My mouth was there.","If you hear a bell in the fog that isn't ours, walk the other way. I've heard it three times this year. It's in B flat. Ours aren't.","Wenna's sweet berries taste of Frostgrave. Don't tell her I said. Her jam pays for half the bells."],"about":"Ottilie Haw. Eighty-four, still picking, still standing up to eat. They keep trying to give me a stool. I'll sit down when they let me go off the Ledge and not a day before. I've picked every terrace in this valley. Some of them twice, when nobody was looking.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words, all rude"}],"childhood":"Ottilie was the seventh child of a picker who never stopped picking, even to give birth, and was born on the lowest terrace in a fog so thick the midwife found her by the sound. She has a clear, specific, impossible memory of being four years old, sitting on her mother's shoulders in a frost so hard the fog froze on the vines, and being given a berry that tasted like honey and summer and something burnt. The village records put the frozen winter long before she was born. Her mother, Brin Haw, said nothing either way. Ottilie was a fierce little girl who bit a Skybridge buyer for taking her basket, and did her First Fall at ten off the lowest terrace into fog, and swore she could not see the net, and never let anyone forget it.","schooling":"She had almost none. Her mother needed her on the terraces, and the terrace-school then met only in winter. She learned her bells from her mother, singing; she can name every bell in Mistvale, including the ones that have been retuned since, by both their old and new notes. The lamps stayed dark at her test, aged nine; spark-poor. She learned to read at sixty, from a Low Reach rider who was laid up in her house with a broken leg and was bored.","history":"She has picked berries for seventy-six years. She married twice, a chainer called Os Haw who drank and was kind, and, at fifty, a widowed berry-buyer from Skybridge called Jessop Lane, who moved down into the fog for her, took her citadel-name, and complained about the damp until he died. She has had six children, eleven grandchildren and a number of great-grandchildren she counts differently every time. She has outlived most of them and has stood at the Letting Ledge in Harrowgate more times than she likes, eating spill-bread standing up, refusing to cry in public and crying in the long-wagon home. She has walked a bell-day every year of her life since she was eight, voluntarily, because she says it keeps her ear in. She has heard the strange bell three times this season, always on the lower terraces, always at night, and has taken it up with Pell Rook, who turned his face away and said he was looking into it.","family":"Two husbands gone; four of six children gone; a great-grandson, Bram Sedge, who argues with her for sport and is the only one she lets help her up the chains.","hero":{"who":"Brin Haw, her mother","why":"Her mother picked until the week she died and never once sat down to eat. Ottilie remembers her giving her the sweet berry, whatever the records say."},"band":{"act":"Thirty Winters","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Twelve-minute post-rock. She falls asleep before the end every time and calls it the best sleep of her year.","rv_vote":"Thirty Winters, because she likes a long song and she likes Frostgrave losing slowly."},"vote":{"local":"She is the loudest voice at Mistvale's own shout, at eighty-four, and gets carried to Skybridge for the full Assembly twice a year to shout at the Triumvirate in person. She calls Kesh 'that slow boy' and means it fondly.","edict":"Against, flatly. 'I've had nothing counted in eighty-four years and I've lived. Count yourselves.'"},"wants":"To taste a sweet berry once more before she's let go, a real one.","site":"mistvale","town":"Mistvale","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:mistvale:b":{"name":"Bram Sedge","age":16,"role":"apprentice terrace-chainer; failed his bell-day twice; wants to ride for the Low Reach patrols","look":"Tall, gangly, hair flopping over his eyes, purple-stained teeth; a patrol rider's cast-off scarf he wears even in summer; a bell he isn't supposed to have, tied inside his coat.","voice":"Sardonic, mumbling, pretends not to care about anything, cares loudly about everything the moment you argue.","greet":"Wahai. You lost? Everyone's lost here. That's the joke. It isn't funny. It's just the joke.","lines":["Failed my bell-day twice. Same bell both times. Pell retuned it in between. He says that's not why. That's why.","Gran Ottilie says she ate a sweet berry. Records say she wasn't born. I believe Gran. The records have never been in fog.","There's a bell down there at night. Not one of ours. I'm not saying I followed it. I'm saying I know which terrace it's under now."],"about":"Bram Sedge. Sixteen. I chain terraces and I hate it. I'm going to ride for the Low Reach patrols. They come for jam and they've got horses and they go places that aren't a hole in a cloud. Gran says I'll be back. Gran says everyone comes back for the jam.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, mumbled"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent, from riders"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"three signs Marten taught him, one of them rude"}],"childhood":"Bram's parents are both terrace-chainers, which in Mistvale is the dull, respectable job: you go out, you check the lashings, you come home wet. He is the eldest of four and has spent most of his life minding the other three in fog, which he says is like minding cats in a sack. He grew up at his great-grandmother Ottilie's knee because his mother worked the high terraces, and she taught him the bells by biting his ear when he got one wrong. He was a clever, bored, bad-tempered child who wanted to be anywhere else. At ten, at his First Fall, he jumped with his eyes open into the fog to see if he could see the net, and could not, and has been angry about it ever since.","schooling":"The terrace-school under Pell Rook, who took it over from Aud Tolling. Pell once stood the class on the third terrace and rang a bell so softly that only Bram heard it, and told him he had the best ear in the valley and the worst patience. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp flickered: Half-Spark. He has never cast. He has failed his bell-day twice, both times at the same bell on the fifth terrace, the one Pell retuned after a picker went missing.","history":"His whole life has happened in Mistvale, which is the problem. He apprenticed to the terrace-chainers at fourteen because his father said so. He has read every scrap of print the Low Reach riders leave behind, and can tell you the name of every patrol captain on the steppe. On his second bell-day, last spring, he missed the fifth-terrace bell again, took his blindfold off in a temper, and heard another bell, below, deeper, in a note Mistvale doesn't use. He followed it. In the fog at the bottom of the valley a hooded person with a soft March voice took his arm without a word, turned him round and walked him back up to his terrace, and was gone. He has told nobody. He has started listening for the bell at night, and has tied one of his own inside his coat in case he needs to be found.","family":"Parents, three younger siblings, his great-grandmother Ottilie Haw, whom he calls Gran and loves more than anyone; a cousin in the Low Reach patrols he idolises from a distance.","hero":{"who":"The Hammer-Bearer","why":"When the Council laughed at Metalcrown's ninth petition, the Councillor without a Realm was the only one who stood. Bram thinks being the one who stands up in a room full of people laughing is the best thing anyone can be."},"band":{"act":"Telt","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Punk, banned at home. He has one bootleg song on a scratched music-wire and plays it until his mother takes it off him.","rv_vote":"He'll be old enough to drop a lamp this year and it's going to Telt, out of spite."},"vote":{"local":"Next year is his first full shout at the Assembly and he is going to shout for whoever wants to send the most riders anywhere.","edict":"He says he doesn't care. He cares: against, because the riders are against it, and the riders are the only adults he takes seriously."},"wants":"To get out of the fog and onto a horse.","site":"mistvale","town":"Mistvale","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:windspire_vf2":{"name":"Absalom Tarr","age":50,"role":"rope and net trader; sells rope, kit and spill-bread; buys salvage; a former Assembly herald who can no longer shout","look":"Long, thin and elegant in a faded herald's coat with the brass buttons cut off; a scarf wound high round his throat; storm-grey through both brows from charges taken to carry his voice.","voice":"A precise, beautiful whisper that makes everyone lean in; stage-trained timing, dry as rope.","greet":"(a whisper) Wahai. Lean in. I don't do loud any more. You'll find the prices are quieter too.","lines":["(whispering) Skyrend wants the Letting Ledge widened for Assembly funerals. With banners. Banners. The Spill doesn't read.","(whispering) People ask what's at the bottom. Nobody knows. That's why we let them go there. A place you could find is a place you'd go looking.","(whispering) The Anchorites below the ledge caught my old horn. Brass, herald's issue. They won't give it back. I go down and ask, now and then. They don't say no. They don't say anything."],"about":"Absalom Tarr. I was the loudest man in Skyrend for twenty years. Assembly herald: I called the shouts and carried them across the chains when the wind ate them. Then my voice tore. Now I sell rope in the one quiet village in Stormreach. I find I hear more.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, the old formal Assembly register"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, polished"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good, from formal Council correspondence"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"basic, learned after his voice went"}],"childhood":"Absalom was born in Skyrend to a family of Assembly heralds who had been calling the shouts since anyone wrote it down, which, Stormreach being Stormreach, was not very long ago. He grew up in the gallery of the Assembly hall, under the great chain, listening to his grandmother tell three hundred people whether they had won. He was a vain, theatrical, happy boy who practised projecting into the wind from the citadel edge every morning, and at eight could make his voice carry to the next citadel. At his First Fall he shouted a speech all the way down into the net. He thought the world was made of rooms that wanted to hear him, and it was some time before anyone corrected him.","schooling":"The Lamp Years at Skyrend after the Tempest lamp lit at his test; then two years at the Tempest Tower at Korrhaven, where a Spiritveil tutor, Master Calanthe, taught him rhetoric and once made him give a whole oration in a whisper to prove that a room leans towards what it can't quite hear. He never forgot it. He left before his third knot, to start heralding.","history":"He heralded for the Free Assembly for twenty years, the most famous voice in the Realm. He learned to carry a shout across a gale by charging into his own voice, which no one else had dared, and paid for it with grey at both brows by thirty and thirty-eight knots on his cord. At forty-two, calling a close and furious shout about the bridge tolls, his voice tore in the middle of a word. It has never come back. He spent a year in Skyrend being sorry for himself loudly in hand-cant, and then flew out to Harrowgate for a cousin's letting and found that in Harrowgate people speak at a level he could still reach. He bought the rope trade off a retiring netter and stayed. He has married nobody, and has a long correspondence in Clear with a retired Lightforge court-crier who also lost her voice. When he dropped his old brass herald's horn over the ledge at his first letting, on purpose, the Anchorites below caught it. Three times a season he climbs down to their cave and asks for it back. They have never said anything. He has never quite left.","family":"His herald family in Skyrend, who send him loud letters; no partner; a Lightforge pen-friend, Corin Lampwright, who writes back in the most careful whisper of Clear handwriting.","hero":{"who":"Saelen of the Drift","why":"The Lantern of the Dead: someone who stayed with people at the end and didn't have to say anything. He'd have liked to know that before his voice went."},"band":{"act":"No Name","realm":"Shadowdeep","why":"Masked whisper-rap that comes second on purpose. He thinks whispering on stage is the bravest thing an act can do, and he would know.","rv_vote":"No Name, so they come second again, which is what they want."},"vote":{"local":"He can't shout at the Assembly now, so he writes his shout on a slate and gives it to the loudest netter in Harrowgate to carry. This year it says 'Not the Ledge'. He likes Kesh's slowness and finds Tamra frightening.","edict":"Against. He spent twenty years carrying every voice in the Realm, and the Edict is the first law he has read that wants to carry them all to one place."},"wants":"His horn back, without having to stay still for it.","site":"windspire_vf2","town":"Harrowgate","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:windspire_vf2":{"name":"Ione Lash","age":35,"role":"village smith; forges net-rings, stretcher-hooks and the iron of the Letting Ledge rail","look":"Long-armed and freckled, with copper hair scraped back and a burn shaped like a coin on her neck; wears her life-cord, with eleven knots, wrapped round her hammer handle.","voice":"Cheerful in an odd, funeral-town way; talks about death like weather; quick to laugh and quick to stop.","greet":"Wahai. If you need rings, I've rings. If you need a stretcher-hook, I'm sorry, and I've those too.","lines":["The gap in the rail's exactly as wide as a stretcher. I measure it every month. If it ever gets wider, it won't be the weather.","Skyrend wants the Ledge widened for banners. Essa says over her dead body. Essa's seventy-two. She means it literally and she'd still win.","The Anchorites catch what mourners drop. Mostly rings. Once a baby's shoe. They put it on a ledge in the cave. I went down to look. I came back up."],"about":"Ione Lash. Born out on Cinder Rock, married into Harrowgate, married into the Lash family, which round here is like marrying into the weather. I forge the rail on the Ledge and the rings in every net Essa ties. I like my work. People think that's strange in a funeral village. They should see the alternative.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"}],"childhood":"Ione was born on Cinder Rock, a small mining raft east of Skyrend that smelts the copper Dunmere uses for its rods, and grew up the youngest of five in a family that claimed one of the eleven people Tempest Hold carried out of Brightwater as an ancestor, a claim every other family on Cinder Rock also makes. Her grandmother told the Brightwater story every remembrance and wept furiously at the same place every time. Ione was a practical, curious child who wanted to know how things were made and took apart her father's spring-lock at five to find out. She did her First Fall over a slag-pit net and called it the best moment of her life, and has since said the same of her wedding, her first forging and a very good pie.","schooling":"Cinder Rock's smelter-school, under a soot-faced copper-master called Ebba Rindle, who taught letters on the backs of ingot moulds and once poured a whole lesson's worth of molten copper into a mould shaped like the word 'careful'. At the Lamp Test, aged nine, the Tempest lamp lit: a Full Spark, though a small one. She did the Lamp Years at Skyrend and was offered Korrhaven and turned it down to apprentice in the smelters.","history":"She came to Harrowgate at twenty-two to bring her grandmother to the Letting Ledge, met Essa Lash's youngest son, Corm, who was holding the stretcher, and fell for him at a funeral, which in Harrowgate is considered good luck. They married from opposite roofs in a storm wedding a year later; she took the Harrowgate citadel-name and the Lash family name, and with them the Lash family's long feud with Skyrend over the Ledge. She has been the village smith for ten years. She forged the current Letting rail herself, to the old measure, and checks it monthly. She has eleven knots, all spent holding nets steady in gusts so a stretcher would not tip. She and Corm have two small children and not much money, because Harrowgate funerals are paid for in spill-bread and gratitude. Last spring a Skyrend Assembly steward came to her forge, quietly, and asked what it would cost to forge new rail fittings at double the width. She named a price. He paid half of it on the spot.","family":"Husband Corm Lash, a netter; two children, four and two; mother-in-law Essa Lash, the net-mistress, whom she adores and is slightly afraid of.","hero":{"who":"Olin Stair","why":"He carried his sister down the Glass Stair in Frostgrave. Ione carries the dead to the Ledge every week and thinks carrying someone well is the hardest work in the Nine."},"band":{"act":"Paper Lantern Funeral","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Doom-gaze. She says it is the only band that understands what a funeral sounds like when it's going well.","rv_vote":"Paper Lantern Funeral, because they'll never win and she'd like to see their faces if they did."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts at the Assembly against widening the Ledge, alongside Essa, every time it comes up. She would shout for Tamra on the Triumvirate, who she thinks would actually fly out and look at the Ledge before deciding.","edict":"Against. 'Lightforge wants to know who's got a spark. Fine. Come to Harrowgate and see what it costs. We've the cords.'"},"wants":"To keep the Ledge as it is, and to pay for her children's winter.","site":"windspire_vf2","town":"Harrowgate","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:windspire_vf2":{"name":"Mabry Holloway","age":58,"role":"rope-splicer; lowers food every day on a rope to her husband, who lives as an Anchorite in the cave below the Ledge","look":"Stocky, weathered, grey-haired from age not charges; rope-burn calluses on both palms; a basket and a coil of rope always somewhere on her.","voice":"Blunt, dry, bitter-funny, impatient with sympathy, softer than she sounds once she trusts you.","greet":"Grain? Thank you. I'll take it. Harrowgate notices who brings grain. So do I. I've a husband downstairs who eats like a skyray.","lines":["The Anchorites below the ledge won't move and won't be moved. One of them's my husband. He's been sitting there six years. Still is dead, they say. He's not dead. He's stubborn.","Skyrend wants to widen the Ledge. If they do, they cut the Anchorites out. Let them try. I'll be sitting on the rope.","Every evening I lower his supper. Every morning the basket comes back up empty and clean. Not one word. Thirty years married. I knew him before. He was never a talker."],"about":"Mabry Holloway, Harrowgate. I splice rope. My husband, Edric, went down into the Anchorites' cave six years ago when our boy didn't come back from the March, and he's been sitting there ever since, so there's somewhere still for Callan to come back to. I think he's mad. I bring him supper.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Mabry grew up on a cliff-farm south of the Anvil, chained so close to its neighbours that you could hand bread across, and the eldest of six who were all expected to work. Her father was a farmer who believed in nothing but rope; her mother laughed at everything, including the rope. Mabry was the sensible one, the one who knew where the spare lashings were and which sibling was lying. She learned to splice before she could read, sitting on the edge with her feet hanging into the wind, and was proud of it. Her First Fall was uneventful in a way she remains faintly disappointed by. She saw it first at fourteen, at her grandfather's letting, and remembers thinking that it was the first thing she'd ever seen that didn't need tying down.","schooling":"A cliff-farm school that met in a hayloft, taught by whichever grandmother had the time; the one Mabry remembers, Old Gell, taught knots and numbers together, so that a bowline was a three and a sheepshank was a seven. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, nothing lit. Spark-poor, like her whole family. She left school at twelve to splice full-time.","history":"She married Edric Holloway, a rope-maker from Harrowgate, at twenty-six, when he came to buy hemp from her father and argued with her about the twist for two days. She took his citadel-name and moved to the lip of the Spill and liked it more than she expected, the quiet, the practical funerals, the nets. They had one son, Callan, who wanted to ride from the age of five and went to Tempest Hold at fifteen. Six years ago, at twenty-two, Callan rode a patrol out along the Shadowdeep March and did not come back. No body. No letting. Mabry wanted to fly his cord to the Ledge and let him go. Edric would not. He went down the rope into the Anchorites' cave below the Ledge, sat down with them on the stone, and has not moved since, except as the Anchorites move, which is not at all. Mabry has lowered his supper every evening for six years. She does not believe Callan is alive. She does not believe Edric is right. She has never missed a supper.","family":"Husband Edric, in the cave below; son Callan, missing on the March six years; five siblings on the cliff-farm who want her to come home.","hero":{"who":"Orrin Who Stayed","why":"The first Anchorite. She thinks he was a fool, and she's been married to one for thirty years, and she has come round to thinking that a certain kind of fool is worth keeping."},"band":{"act":"The Willow Choir","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"'Nothing Is Lost', the duet with a dead singer's echo. She cannot listen to it. She has the music-wire anyway.","rv_vote":"The Willow Choir, without listening, which she thinks they would understand."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts at the Assembly with Essa Lash against widening the Ledge, and would shout for anyone on the Triumvirate who'd send riders out along the March to look properly.","edict":"Torn, oddly. 'If one Lightforge clerk could count my son home, I'd let them count everything else. They can't. So no.'"},"wants":"To know, one way or the other, so she can tell Edric to come up.","site":"windspire_vf2","town":"Harrowgate","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:windspire_vf2:b":{"name":"Teodor Lark","age":24,"role":"letting-singer; sings the dead off the Ledge by name; writes songs no one has asked for","look":"Slim, dark, beautiful in a slightly tragic way he knows about and mocks; a scarf over his mouth against the Spill wind; one streak of grey at the right brow from a single charge taken to hold a note.","voice":"Gentle, musical, self-deprecating, slips into song mid-sentence and apologises.","greet":"Wahai. Don't worry, I'm not singing. Not for you. Not today. You look like you'll last.","lines":["Skyrend wants banners on the Ledge for Assembly funerals. Banners flap. You can't sing over a banner. I've tried, at a Warden's letting. The banner won.","My cousin Ambrose in Cloudmere sent me an apology he's been writing for eight years and asked me to make it better. It's very good. It's terrible. I'm turning it into a song.","Realm Vision wants Stormreach to send shouted apologies. Always. I want to send a quiet one. Nobody will let me."],"about":"Teodor Lark. I sing at lettings. People say that's sad. It's the best singing there is: everybody's listening, and nobody's going to request a different song. One day I'll get to Korrhaven for Realm Vision. Probably with something loud. Not by choice.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Rime","level":"a few funeral words, learned off a Frostgrave record"},{"tongue":"Lanthic","level":"a few funeral words, which turn out to be the same ones"}],"childhood":"Teodor was born in Harrowgate to a net-maker and a letting-singer and grew up under the Ledge, where the wind off the Spill carries every sound upward. His mother sang at lettings and practised in the kitchen, and his first memory is of her singing a name over and over until it fitted the tune. He was a dreamy, kind, easily distracted child who sang to the nets as he helped tie them, and lost count of his knots. His First Fall was the first time he ever sang in front of anyone, because he sang on the way down without meaning to, and the net-makers on the ledge above went quiet. His father, a practical man, took him aside afterwards and told him that was all very well, but next time watch the net.","schooling":"Harrowgate's knot-school, taught by Essa Lash, who made the children tie a knot for every line of the letting-song so they would never forget either. He was bad at knots and good at songs. Then the old choir-master, Hob Clary, took him into the letting choir at nine. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp lit, faintly: Half-Spark. He has charged once, to hold a note through a gust.","history":"He has sung at lettings since he was twelve, and led them since Hob Clary's voice went at seventy. He sings each of the dead off by name, with something about them worked in, a habit Hob started and Teodor has made famous; families fly in from Skyrend to have him. He has sung for riders, babies, a skyray once, and a Warden of a small citadel whose family brought banners, which flapped so loudly he lost the tune and has been angry about it for two years. He loves a boy in Skyrend who works the chain-lifts, very quietly, and has written him forty songs and sent him none. He has entered the Stormreach heats for Realm Vision three times with letting-songs and been told each time that Stormreach sends shouted apologies and nothing else. Last month his cousin Ambrose Fell sent him an apology eight years in the writing and asked him to improve it. He has started setting it to music, slow, with a forty-second silence in the middle.","family":"Mother, a retired letting-singer; father, a net-maker; cousin Ambrose Fell in Cloudmere; a boy in Skyrend who doesn't know he's being written to.","hero":{"who":"Hob Clary","why":"The old choir-master who led the letting choir for fifty years and lost his voice singing it, and still comes to every letting to stand at the back and mouth the words."},"band":{"act":"Hallow Rime","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Throat-singing, and the famous forty-second silence. He thinks it is the only moment in Realm Vision where anyone listens.","rv_vote":"Hallow Rime, though it's a Frostgrave act and Harrowgate would rather he didn't say so."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for Seraphine at the Assembly, but only once, and then listens, which he thinks is how shouting should be done.","edict":"Against. 'A register of everyone with a spark is a list of who we'll be singing for. I'd rather not have the list in advance.'"},"wants":"To sing a quiet song at Realm Vision for Stormreach and not be booed.","site":"windspire_vf2","town":"Harrowgate","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:tempest_vf0":{"name":"Seventeen Bray","age":45,"role":"Dunmere's trader; sells kit, bread and dried goat, and chases Tempest Hold for what it owes","look":"Big, red, barrel-chested, a voice like a rockfall; copper burns on his knuckles from handling hot rods; a tally-stick for Tempest Hold's debt as long as his arm, notched on both sides.","voice":"Bellowing, cheerful, profane, says every number twice in case you missed it; laughs at his own jokes before he's finished them.","greet":"SEVENTEEN BRAY! That's me. Seventeen strikes, then I cried. Wahai! What do you want? Shout it, I can't hear anything under six feet of thunder.","lines":["Tempest Hold pays for its saddle-goat late and in promises. I've got a stick of promises here. Look at it. LOOK AT IT.","Thistledown's horses ate our grazing again when the rocks drifted close. I sent them a bill. They sent me a horse. A bad horse. I like the horse.","Lightning's landing odd. Same rod three times then nothing next door. Hennet says stand clear of nineteen. I stand clear of nineteen. I stand clear of eighteen and twenty while I'm at it."],"about":"Seventeen Bray, Dunmere. I sell goat, buy salvage, shout at Tempest Hold. Born in a storm, seventeen strikes, a quiet night by Dunmere standards. I've been loud ever since to make up for it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, maximum volume"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"trade-fluent, all numbers and insults"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"enough to read a Gleamford invoice and shout about it"}],"childhood":"He was born in the herders' cave during a storm and given the number of strikes his grandmother counted between his first breath and his first cry, which was seventeen and which she counted out loud, so that the whole cave chanted it with her. He grew up in the Rod Forest with a pack of other numbered children, Nine and Twenty-Two and a girl called Fifty who bossed them all. He was a huge, boisterous, generous boy who gave away his goat and then ate everyone else's. At ten, at his First Fall, the net was strung between two rods, and a strike hit the left one as he jumped. He landed in the net screaming. He still has not stopped flinching at thunder, and nobody in Dunmere knows.","schooling":"Twelve Yeld's cave-school, where every lesson was shouted and every letter scratched in the soot; Twelve once made the class shout the whole Bridge alphabet in the middle of a storm and counted it a pass if he could hear them. Seventeen was the loudest pupil he ever had. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the lamps stayed dark: spark-poor. He learned sums by counting goat-quarters on the rods.","history":"He took over the Dunmere trade at twenty-three from an aunt who wanted to sit down, and turned it from goat-selling into goat-and-everything-else. He sells dried strike-goat to the Riders, over the border to Gleamford, where people eat it sitting down, and to anyone at Skybridge who will listen. He has a running war with Tempest Hold's quartermaster over the saddle-goat account, which has not been paid on time in nineteen years, and flies to the Hold every month with his tally-stick to shout at the stable bastion. The cook there, Ludo Brack, always gives him a saddle-cake with his name on the twist and sends him off happy and unpaid. He married Fern, a herder, at twenty-eight, took nobody's citadel-name because they were both Dunmere, and has three loud children with numbers for names. He wears beeswax in his ears in storms and tells people it's for the noise.","family":"Wife Fern, a herder; three children called Six, Twenty-Nine and Eleven; an aunt who sits, which Dunmere forgives because she's earned it.","hero":{"who":"Kaelis Emberwarden","why":"The loudest, funniest, most profane person on the Council. Seventeen heard Kaelis once at a Korrhaven market and thought: there, that's what a voice is for."},"band":{"act":"Wahai!","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Party reggaeton with pipes. He plays them on the rods in a storm and dances in the cave doorway.","rv_vote":"Wahai!, as loud as a lamp can be dropped."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts at every Assembly for Tamra, because Tamra once actually paid a Dunmere bill on time when she was a rider-captain, and Dunmere remembers.","edict":"Against, at the top of his voice. 'They want to count Stormreach? Come to Dunmere. We count everything. We've a man called Two-Hundred-and-Six!'"},"wants":"To be paid, in full, on time, once, by Tempest Hold.","site":"tempest_vf0","town":"Dunmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:tempest_vf0":{"name":"Hennet Sparrow","age":59,"role":"rod-smith; forges, plants and replaces the four hundred copper rods of the Rod Forest","look":"Small, wiry, with hair burnt short and white in a spray shape from a strike that passed close; a slate and a chalk on a cord at her neck; green copper dust in every line of her hands.","voice":"Totally deaf since thirty; reads lips, answers in a flat, too-careful voice or on her slate; very dry, very exact.","greet":"(writing on her slate) Face me when you talk. I read lips. Rods, nails, hooks. Don't shout. It doesn't help, it just looks silly.","lines":["(on her slate) Rod nineteen. Three strikes in a night, then the next rock gets none. I measured it. It's the same rod. It's not the same copper.","(aloud, too evenly) Everyone in Dunmere shouts. I can't hear them. I'm the only one here who knows how quiet it is.","(on her slate) Tempest Hold owes us. Seventeen shouts about it. I write it down. Neither works. Writing is less tiring."],"about":"(slowly, aloud) Hennet Sparrow. I make the rods. I lost my hearing to one of them, at thirty. I don't mind. Dunmere is very loud and very beautiful in a storm, and I get the beautiful part.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native; speaks it carefully since going deaf, reads it on lips easily"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"reads and writes well, speaks a little"},{"tongue":"Hand-cant","level":"a few signs, from a Shadowdeep copper-buyer"}],"childhood":"Hennet was born on a calm night, no strikes, to a rod-smith father and a herder mother, and was given an ordinary name, which in Dunmere is noticed. She was a quiet, thoughtful child in the loudest village in the Realm. She liked to sit at the edge of the Rod Forest and watch the strikes come in, counting the gap between flash and crack with her lips. Her father let her into the forge at seven and taught her that copper is soft and honest and that you can tell everything about a rod from its colour after the first strike. She loved him without saying so, which was the only way their house did anything. At her First Fall she asked to fall in silence, and the whole family, somehow, managed it.","schooling":"Twelve Yeld's cave-school, which she hated because it was all shouting; Twelve, who was not unkind, let her write her answers in the soot instead and said it was the neatest soot he had ever seen. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp flickered; Half-Spark. She has never cast. She learned to read properly from her father's copper-buyers' letters, in Bridge, and has read every letter that has come to the forge since.","history":"She took the forge at twenty-five when her father's hands went. She has planted and replaced every rod in the Rod Forest since, some of them three or four times. At thirty, setting a new rod in a storm to save a stray goat, a strike hit the rod beside hers. She woke up in the cave with her hair standing straight up and no sound in the world. She was back at the forge in nine days. She learned to read lips in a year and Dunmere learned to face her when it talked, which for Dunmere was a great act of love. She married a herder, Lark Sparrow, who learned to write on her slate faster than he had ever learned anything, and they have a daughter who herds. For the past six weeks she has measured rod nineteen every morning. The copper is the colour of a rod that has taken a hundred strikes, but it has taken nine. She has written it down. Three nights she has taken it down from its post, and three mornings it was warm in her hands.","family":"Husband Lark, a herder, and their daughter, Saffa; her father, retired and still telling her what colour the copper should be.","hero":{"who":"The Silent Sentinel","why":"Very old, jaw turned to stone, speaks by carved slates. She has never seen him. She carries a slate for the same reason he does, and thinks he must also find most speeches too long."},"band":{"act":"Burden","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Doom trio whose drummer's hands are turning to stone. She can feel their drums through the cave floor when the music-wire plays, and nothing else comes through as clearly.","rv_vote":"Burden, because she can feel them, and that's more than she can say for most acts."},"vote":{"local":"She writes her Assembly shout on a slate and holds it up, which Dunmere now counts as a shout by custom. This season it says 'Pay Dunmere.' She trusts Whit, who writes things down.","edict":"Against. She has lived her whole life with people talking over her, and the Edict reads to her like more of the same."},"wants":"To understand rod nineteen before it understands something about Dunmere.","site":"tempest_vf0","town":"Dunmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:tempest_vf0":{"name":"Three Marl","age":27,"role":"goat-herder; runs the strays in under the rods in storms with Forty-One Hail","look":"Short and quick, sunburnt, with a pale streak at one brow from a charge that drove off a goat-hawk; wears a herder's leather hood with '3' burned into the back, crossed out.","voice":"Fast, funny, bitter about exactly one thing; does not mind being teased about anything except her name.","greet":"Wahai. Before you ask: three. Three strikes. Yes, that's all. Yes, it was a small storm. Yes, I've heard the joke.","lines":["Rod nineteen took three strikes in one night. Three! Same as me. I'm keeping it.","Thistledown's horses come over the gap when the rocks drift together and eat our grazing. I've started herding the horses back with the goats. They don't like it. Nor do the goats.","Forty-One stood under the rods nine storms to bring strays in. I've done four. I'm catching up. She says it's not a race. Everything here's a race."],"about":"Three Marl. Herder. Twenty-seven. Born in a storm so feeble my grandmother called it 'a drizzle with opinions'. Everyone in Dunmere has a big number. Forty-One. Sixty-Eight. A man called Two-Hundred-and-Six. Me, I've got Three. At the Great Turning this year I'm shouting a new one.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent"}],"childhood":"She was born in a small storm in the herders' cave, and old Two-Hundred-and-Six Clatter, who has been the birth-counter for every Dunmere child for forty years, counted three strikes before she cried. Her mother said nothing at the time, and has said nothing since, which Three has always noticed. Three grew up being called 'Drizzle' and 'Not-Much' by the other numbered children and fought every one of them. She was fierce, funny, lonely and very good with goats, which liked her because she ran as fast as they did. She learned the rods from Forty-One Hail, who let her follow her under them in small storms from the age of seven. At ten, at her First Fall, she jumped before the net was tied, as a protest. It was tied. Just.","schooling":"Twelve Yeld's cave-school in its last years; when Twelve died, Forty-One Hail taught the herders' children in the goat-shed, letters on one wall and goat-diseases on the other. Three learned both. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp lit, a Full Spark, which made everyone in Dunmere say that at least something about her was big. She did the Lamp Years at Skyrend and refused Korrhaven to come home to the goats.","history":"She has herded with Forty-One Hail since she was sixteen and has stood under the rods in four storms to bring strays in. She has cast nineteen times, mostly to scare off goat-hawks and once to stop a Thistledown stallion that had got onto the rock in a drift and was trying to eat a herder. She has fallen in and out of love with two herders and a Thistledown horse-breaker, Bastian Fettle, who sneezed through their whole courtship and finally gave up. For years she has been sure that old Two-Hundred-and-Six miscounted her on purpose. He will not discuss it. Her mother changes the subject. At this year's Great Turning, when everyone shouts what they'll change, she intends to shout a new name from the top of the Rod Forest, and has not yet decided what it will be, only that it will be bigger.","family":"Her mother, a herder who will not talk about her birth; an uncle, Nine Marl, dead before she was born, whom nobody mentions either.","hero":{"who":"Forty-One Hail","why":"The head herder, who has stood under the rods in nine storms for strays and has hair the colour of the rock. Three wants that hair and that number, and is working on the first."},"band":{"act":"Seismic","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Bass music, 'Aftershock'. She found it on a music-wire from a Stonehold horse-buyer and says it is the only music in the Nine that sounds as big as a Dunmere storm.","rv_vote":"Seismic, and in the Sparkbeat Cup, which makes Dunmere grumble about Stonehold."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Seraphine at every Assembly, because Seraphine is thirty-one and loud and doesn't care what anybody calls her.","edict":"Against. She has a Full Spark and she has spent nineteen charges of her own life on goats. Nobody in Meridian gets to audit that."},"wants":"A number she chose.","site":"tempest_vf0","town":"Dunmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:tempest_vf0:b":{"name":"Two-Hundred-and-Six Clatter","age":88,"role":"retired herder; Dunmere's birth-counter for forty years; holder of the village record for strikes at a birth","look":"Shrunken, toothless, hair a dun white like the rock itself; walks with a copper rod for a stick; still stands to eat, leaning on it, and insists you notice.","voice":"Creaky, boastful, sly, tells the same three stories in a different order every time; argues his own number upward.","greet":"Two-Hundred-and-Six! That's me. Two hundred and six strikes before I cried. Record. Probably two hundred and seven. Nobody counts like they used to. Give us some grain, I'll tell you about it.","lines":["Lightning's been landing odd these weeks. Same rod three times. Like it's aiming. Back when the chains glowed red, it aimed at everything. I was born in that. I remember it. I was very small.","Lightforge wants to count all the sparks in Stormreach. Good. About time someone counted something properly. Mine was two hundred and seven.","Forty years I counted the births. Never got one wrong. Well. Never got one wrong for a bad reason."],"about":"Two-Hundred-and-Six Clatter. Born in the cave the night the chains glowed red, when the herders sat against the door with every goat on the rock. The midwife counted two hundred and six before I cried. Village record. I've been counting babies ever since. I'm eighty-eight, I'm still standing, and I'm right about everything.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, old Dunmere dialect"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"a few words he refuses to use"}],"childhood":"He was born on the worst night in Dunmere memory, when the rock took so many strikes its chains glowed red and the herders drove every goat into the single cave and sat with their backs against the door. His mother gave birth in the middle of it, among the goats, and the midwife, Old Brindle Cass, counted every strike aloud with the whole cave until he cried at last, which was at two hundred and six. He grew up famous. Every child in Dunmere knew his name before they knew their own number. He was a vain, charming, fast-talking boy who herded well when anyone was watching. At ten he did his First Fall in a storm, on purpose, for the story. He has told that story roughly every week for seventy-eight years.","schooling":"There was no school on Dunmere when he was a boy, only Old Brindle Cass, who taught the children their numbers by making them count strikes in every storm and their letters not at all. He learned to read at forty, out of pride, when a Skybridge trader wrote him a letter and he had to ask his wife what it said. At the Lamp Test, aged nine, nothing lit. Spark-poor, which he has always considered a clerical error.","history":"He herded for fifty years and was good at it and better at telling people how good he was. At thirty, when Old Brindle Cass's eyes went, he took over as Dunmere's birth-counter, and for forty years he stood in the cave at every storm-birth and counted aloud until the baby cried, and gave the child its number. Forty-One Hail is his count. So is Seventeen Bray. So are most of the herders on the rock. He married Dilly, a goat-drier, who laughed at him for fifty-three years and died two winters ago, and he flew her out to Harrowgate himself and stood at the Ledge without a stick and would not sit down. He has nine children and stopped counting grandchildren at thirty. He gave up the counting at seventy and now sits, standing, in the cave doorway in storms and tells children which rods to avoid. Twenty-seven years ago, in a small storm, he counted a girl's birth at nine and said three. He has never told anyone.","family":"Wife Dilly, let go two winters ago; nine children; grandchildren beyond counting; one great-grand-niece, Three Marl, who glares at him.","hero":{"who":"Old Brindle Cass","why":"The midwife who counted him in. She counted two hundred and six strikes aloud on the worst night in Dunmere and didn't lose her place once. He has tried to be her for forty years."},"band":{"act":"The Nine Storms","realm":"Stormreach","why":"White-haired at twenty-five, 'Worth the Grey'. He likes their hair. He also likes that they say they're worth it, and thinks they've earned it.","rv_vote":"The Nine Storms. 'Nine. Good number. I knew a Nine.'"},"vote":{"local":"He has shouted at the Assembly for seventy years and says he once shouted a Warden out on his own. He likes Kesh because Kesh is old enough to be nearly sensible.","edict":"For, as a joke that he half means: 'Let Lightforge count. They can start with me. Two hundred and seven.' He has not read it."},"wants":"To hear the girl he miscounted shout her new name at the Great Turning, and for it to be the right one.","site":"tempest_vf0","town":"Dunmere","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:tempest_vf1":{"name":"Imelda Furlong","age":49,"role":"horse-trader and village shop; sells horses, tack, kit and bread; known across the Low Reach as the one trader who doesn't lie","look":"Tall, lean, weathered, with grey threaded through brown hair from two charges taken in a stampede; always standing, always in boots; reading spectacles on a cord she is embarrassed by.","voice":"Plain, measured, says the bad thing about a horse first; can be very funny without smiling.","greet":"Wahai. I'm standing, you'll notice. Never buy a horse off anyone who sits down to sell it. Now. What do you need and what can you pay?","lines":["The Lightforge clerk came out to count our horses. I counted them for him. Properly. All of them. He said I was the first person in Stormreach who'd done that. Tempest Hold won't thank me.","Stonehold traders call our horses 'flighty' every year and buy them every year. I let them say it. Then I add a crossing to the price for every 'flighty'.","A Thistledown mare raced a Luminar Stag to Meridian and won. The stag's owner wrote it up fair. We've got it framed. Lightforge folk ask to see it. I let them."],"about":"Imelda Furlong, Thistledown. I sell horses, and I tell you exactly what's wrong with each one, which is why people come from Skyrend to buy off me and why the other traders hate me. I was horse-judge three Down-Days running. My hair's still got some thistle in it from the last one.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good; she reads Lightforge horse-ledgers for pleasure"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"bargaining phrases only"}],"childhood":"Her father was a horse-trader who lied as easily as breathing and was loved for it. Her mother was a breaker who never lied in her life and was respected for it, and Imelda spent her childhood watching the two of them sell horses from the same yard and understood early which customers came back. She was a solemn, observant child who could tell a horse's temper by its ears before she could ride. She rode the Tilt Course in a gale at eleven, which is young, on a mare her mother had warned her off, was thrown at the downhill post and remounted, and her mother said nothing all the way home and then gave her the mare. At her First Fall she asked if anyone had checked the net. Nobody had. She checked it.","schooling":"The Thistledown riding-school, taught on horseback across the Reach by an old breaker called Haddon Stirrup, who held class at a canter and made children read their letters off posts as they passed. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp lit, low: Half-Spark. She cast twice in her twenties in a stampede on the Tilt and stopped. She learned Clear from the framed account of the stag race, which she read so often as a girl that she can recite it.","history":"She broke horses for her mother until thirty and then took the yard and the village shop when her father died, and set out to sell horses the way her mother broke them: without a lie. It nearly ruined her for three years and then made her the most trusted trader on the Low Reach. The Tempest Riders buy their remounts on her word, couriers wait a season for her picks, and Skyrend sends buyers who are under instruction to believe nothing anyone else says. She has been horse-judge at Down-Day three years running and finds it a nuisance. She never married. She lives with her sister and her sister's children and is the aunt who tells the truth. When the Lightforge clerk, Davor Clearwater, came out from Tempest Hold to count horses, she counted every one for him, accurately, in his own Day-Book. She knows exactly what the Hold will make of it. She did it anyway.","family":"Sister Ros and three nephews; her mother, still breaking at seventy-four; her father, let go at the Spill fifteen years ago, whom she misses more than she'd like.","hero":{"who":"Arcthen Solbrand","why":"'Say it to me plainly, and then say it to my enemy the same way.' It is written on the wall above her yard gate in her own hand, and she thinks it's the best rule for selling horses ever made."},"band":{"act":"Plain Speaking","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Honest country, no metaphors. She thinks every other act in Realm Vision is lying a little.","rv_vote":"Plain Speaking, every year, and she tells you exactly why at length."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Kesh at the Assembly, because Kesh says what he'll do and then does it, slowly. She thinks Seraphine is honest, and too fast.","edict":"Torn. She admires a law that wants everyone to say what they are. She does not trust the people who will hold the list."},"wants":"To sell one horse in her life to someone who doesn't haggle.","site":"tempest_vf1","town":"Thistledown","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:tempest_vf1":{"name":"Bastian Fettle","age":33,"role":"village farrier and smith; shoes every horse in Thistledown, and sneezes doing it","look":"Stocky, ruddy, with a broken nose from a kick and permanently red eyes; hoof-shavings in his beard; a scarf over his face from midsummer to the end of thistle season.","voice":"Good-humoured, nasal, interrupts himself to sneeze; tells long stories about horses that end with him on the ground.","greet":"Wahai. (sneeze) Sorry. Thistle. It's always thistle. Shoes, nails, a bit of iron? Stand upwind of me, it's safer for both of us.","lines":["Davit's been thrown by every horse in Thistledown. I've been kicked by every horse in Thistledown. We compare. He's winning. I'm counting.","Found a horseshoe on a Stonehold buyer's horse last month. Metalcrown stamp. 'I made it.' Stonehold buying from Metalcrown. Nobody tell either of them.","Down-Day, the whole village rides out to throw thistle in the air. I ride out with a wet cloth on my face and try not to die. I've never been judge. I've never been close."],"about":"Bastian Fettle. Farrier. I was going to be a rider for the Low Reach patrols. Then I found out I can't breathe near thistle, in a village called Thistledown, on the biggest thistle-field in the Nine. So I shoe horses. Horses don't care if you sneeze. Mostly.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"a few words from a Metalcrown nail-trader"}],"childhood":"He was born to a breaker and a post-cutter in a cottage at the downhill end of the Tilt Course, and spent his early years watching horses come down the slope in a gale at full gallop and wanting to be on one. He was a cheerful, sturdy, accident-prone boy who was always being sat on by a pony. Every summer when the thistles seeded he swelled up, wept and wheezed, and his mother wrapped his face in a wet cloth and sent him out anyway, because in Thistledown you don't stay in. At nine, on his first Down-Day, he rode out with the village at full gallop, sneezed so hard he came off, and landed in the biggest thistle-patch on the Reach. He was in bed a week. He came back the next year.","schooling":"Haddon Stirrup's riding-school on the Reach, where he was good at riding and disastrous at reading posts at a canter, because his eyes were usually streaming. Haddon gave him extra lessons indoors in winter, which Bastian remembers as kindness. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, nothing lit; spark-poor. At fourteen he went to the Low Reach patrol trials and was sent home in thistle season, unable to breathe.","history":"He apprenticed to the old Thistledown farrier, Mags Ironside, at fifteen, because a forge is the one place in the village where the air is all smoke and no thistle. He took to it. He has shod every horse in the village and most of the Riders' remounts, and is proud of a pair of shoes he made for a mare that ran the Tilt in a gale and finished. He has been kicked, by his own count, two hundred and twelve times. He courted Three Marl of Dunmere when the rocks drifted together one summer, sneezed through every evening of it, and she finally told him she couldn't marry a man who sounded like a goat with a cold; they still wave across the gap. Last month, shoeing a Stonehold buyer's horse, he pried off a worn shoe and found a Metalcrown maker's mark on the inside: 'I made it'. He kept the shoe. He hasn't told anyone, because he can't work out who it would hurt most.","family":"Parents in the cottage by the Tilt; two sisters who both ride for the Low Reach patrols, which he is very proud of and slightly jealous of; Mags Ironside, retired, who still criticises his nailing.","hero":{"who":"The Lost Warden","why":"Nobody knows who the Lost Warden was. Bastian has decided it was a farrier, because nobody ever notices the farrier, and he tells this theory to every horse he shoes."},"band":{"act":"Updraft","realm":"Stormreach","why":"Trance, 'Freefall'. It is the only music he can listen to with a wet cloth over his face, and it sounds like galloping without having to breathe.","rv_vote":"Updraft, in the Sparkbeat Cup and in the main vote, because it's the closest he's got to a Down-Day."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts at the Assembly for whoever will buy more horses for the patrols, which this year is everyone, which is worrying.","edict":"Against, without being sure why, except that the Lightforge clerk counted his forge as a horse."},"wants":"To come back from one Down-Day with a single thistle in his hair and still be breathing.","site":"tempest_vf1","town":"Thistledown","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:tempest_vf1":{"name":"Hawise Gallow","age":44,"role":"mare-milker; ferments thunder-milk in skins slung from a galloping saddle","look":"Strong, brown, wide-shouldered, with a gap where two front teeth were; a milk-skin over each shoulder at all times; no grey at all, which she is quietly proud of.","voice":"Practical, warm, worried underneath; changes the subject to milk when anything gets close.","greet":"Grain? Bless you. Thistledown keeps a tally of who helps, you know. I keep a better one. Here. Drink this. It's good for the teeth.","lines":["Lightforge sent a clerk out to count the horses. Next they'll count the riders. Then the sparks in the riders. Good, some days. Somebody should.","Never buy a horse off anyone who sits down to sell it. Never buy milk off anyone with all their teeth. They've not been drinking it.","My girl's twenty and grey to the ears. Twenty. She rides for the patrols. She says it's worth it. You're not supposed to say that about yourself. She does."],"about":"Hawise Gallow, Thistledown. I milk the mares and gallop the milk till it froths. People say it's good for the teeth. Look at mine and decide. I've a daughter on the patrols who charges like she's got years to spare. She hasn't. Nobody has.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"halting"}],"childhood":"Hawise grew up the eldest daughter of a mare-milker with bad knees, which meant that from the age of six she rode the milk-saddle, a wooden frame hung with skins of thunder-milk, at a gallop round the Tilt Course twice a day while the milk frothed. She was a sturdy, cheerful, uncomplaining child who was proud of being useful and a bit afraid of being anything else. She lost her first front tooth to a milk-skin swinging back in a gust and her second to a pony and never had either replaced. Her younger brother was the clever one, a Full Spark who went to Korrhaven and came home at twenty with half his hair grey, and she remembers her mother crying at his homecoming and not knowing whether it was pride.","schooling":"Haddon Stirrup's riding-school, which she attended in the gaps between milkings, reading posts at a canter and never quite catching up; Haddon let her bring the milk-saddle to class and taught her letters off the skins. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, nothing lit. Spark-poor. She was relieved. She thinks she always knew what the spark costs before anyone told her.","history":"She took her mother's skins at twenty and has milked and galloped ever since. She married Arn Gallow, a breaker, at twenty-two; they were both Thistledown, so the wedding was a shout across one yard. They had one daughter, Elske, whose Tempest lamp blazed at seven. Elske went through the Lamp Years and Korrhaven and into the Low Reach patrols at seventeen, and by twenty is grey to the ears, with a life-cord thicker than her mother's wrist. Every visit Hawise counts the new knots without meaning to. Her brother died at forty-one looking sixty. When the Edict talk began and every voice in Thistledown went against it, Hawise found she did not agree, and found she could not say so above a mutter. When the Lightforge clerk came, she waited until he was alone and asked him, in bad Bridge, how a person would put a name on the register. He told her, carefully, and wrote down that she had asked.","family":"Husband Arn, a breaker who doesn't know what she did; daughter Elske, twenty, patrol rider; a brother, let go at the Spill.","hero":{"who":"Lyriel Frostwraith","why":"She warned, and they struck her from the record. Stormreach hates Frostgrave's Seers for the Unwarned Gale. Hawise thinks the worst thing in the world is to warn and not be heard, and she admires a Seer who tried."},"band":{"act":"Decades","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"Forty-year love songs. She likes the idea of something long. Everything in Thistledown is fast.","rv_vote":"Decades, and she'll tell anyone who mocks it that at least nobody in it is going grey for the show."},"vote":{"local":"She has started shouting against Seraphine at the Assembly, quietly, which in Stormreach is barely a shout at all.","edict":"For, privately. She thinks a register might be the only thing that ever tells her daughter's captains no."},"wants":"For Elske to reach fifty.","site":"tempest_vf1","town":"Thistledown","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:tempest_vf1:b":{"name":"Fitch Harlow","age":68,"role":"post-cutter; cuts and sets the thistle-wood posts of the Tilt Course; describes himself as the dullest man on the Low Reach","look":"Medium everything, beige everything, a mild face under a hat he has worn for thirty years; thistle-wood shavings on his sleeves; a faded red scarf nailed to the post outside his door.","voice":"Slow, even, mild, self-deprecating to the point of comedy; apologises for being boring and then is.","greet":"Wahai. Fitch Harlow. Posts. I cut the posts. That's it, really. That's the whole of me. Sorry.","lines":["Never buy a horse off anyone who sits down to sell it. I say that. Everyone says that. I've never sold a horse. I'm just passing it on.","Stonehold traders buy thistle-wood off me for walking-sticks. They say it's flighty wood. It's very good wood. I don't argue. I'm not an arguer. Ask my wife. My first wife. Ask Agna.","Davit says you can tell a horse by how it throws you. I can tell a post by how it stands. Nobody's interested. I know."],"about":"Fitch Harlow, Thistledown. Sixty-eight. Post-cutter. I've cut every post on the Tilt Course, most of them twice. I was married once, to the loudest woman in Stormreach, for five years, and it was the most interesting thing that ever happened to me, and I'm still not sure I enjoyed it. I'm the dull one. I don't mind.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, slowly"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"good enough for Stonehold traders"}],"childhood":"Fitch was the only child of a post-cutter and a post-cutter, and grew up in a cottage made of thistle-wood beside a pile of thistle-wood, learning to tell good wood from bad by knocking it. He was a calm, unremarkable, contented child in a village that rides at full gallop everywhere. He was thrown by his first pony and decided, quietly, that that was enough of that. He rode when he had to and walked when he could, which in Thistledown is nearly a crime. He made friends with the Tilt Course posts and the old men who mended them, and learned that if you stood a post right it would outlast the horse, the rider and the man who set it. His First Fall was so uneventful that his parents forgot to tell anyone it had happened.","schooling":"Haddon Stirrup's riding-school for a year, until Haddon gently suggested he would learn more from the post-cutters, which was true. His father taught him letters by carving them into posts. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, nothing lit. Spark-poor. He has never wanted to be anything else, which he admits is part of the problem.","history":"At twenty-one, during a summer when Dunmere drifted close, he met Agna, a rod-smith's daughter with a voice that knocked goats over, and for reasons neither of them could later explain they married from opposite roofs within a month. She took his citadel-name and moved to Thistledown and found him unbearable inside a year, and he found her wonderful and exhausting, and after five years she went to Tempest Hold and he stayed with his posts, which he had taken over at twenty-two when his father died. Neither of them went to the Assembly to unshout the wedding. He nailed the red scarf she shouted her vows in to the post outside his door and has replaced the nail twice. At forty he married again, a widowed courier called Bett, quietly, without shouting, which some say does not count. She died last year. He has cut and set every post on the Tilt Course and keeps a ledger of how each one stands in each gale, which nobody has ever asked to see.","family":"First wife Agna, now Agna Spoke of Cloudmere; second wife Bett, let go last year; no children; a cousin on Dunmere who is always shouting.","hero":{"who":"Garron Stoneheart","why":"'Lift. Then talk.' A king who gave it up and went and stood on a pass. Fitch thinks that's the least dull thing a dull man ever did."},"band":{"act":"The Kept Coals","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stomp-folk bakers, 'Keep Your Coal Lit'. It's steady. You can set a post to it. Bett liked them.","rv_vote":"The Kept Coals, for Bett, who'd have voted for them."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for Kesh, quietly, when he goes, which is not often. He'd shout for anyone who'd leave the Tilt Course alone.","edict":"Against, mildly. 'If they want to count something, they could count the posts. Nobody else ever has.'"},"wants":"For someone, just once, to ask to see his ledger of the posts.","site":"tempest_vf1","town":"Thistledown","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:skybridge_vf1":{"name":"Wynn Harrier","age":31,"role":"Barleycross trader; sells trail-cake by the sleeve, kit and packs to couriers, riders and skyray-flyers","look":"Small, quick, flour to the elbows, with a rope tied where her belt should be; brown hair in two short plaits; laughs with her whole face.","voice":"Chatty, sunny, fast, tells you more about her marriage than you asked; goes very careful the moment Hollinwood comes up.","greet":"Wahai! Cake? It's today's. Soft. You'll never get it soft anywhere else in Stormreach, so don't argue, just eat it.","lines":["Every courier through here wants twice the cake this season. Twice! Either they're flying further or they're not sure when they'll be back for more.","Hollinwood's trees shade the east barley raft. Everyone here's furious about it. I married a Hollinwood woman. I'm furious about it very quietly.","Skybridge sells its beer back to us at three times the price of the barley. Three times! I've done the sums. Then I've had a beer about it."],"about":"Wynn Harrier, Barleycross. I sell the cake. I married across the chains: my wife cuts holly in Hollinwood, and we both kept our own citadel-names, which made both villages shout at us for a month. My belt fell at the wedding. We used rope. I'm still wearing it.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, courier-slangy"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"a little, from Gleamford flour-buyers"}],"childhood":"Wynn grew up over the Barleycross ovens, the youngest of a baker's four, and was given the trail-cake trays to scrape before she could walk. Her grandmother's belt is one of the ones still wrapped round the Crossing Knot from the night the four rafts swung together, and Wynn was taken up the chain at five to touch the burned name on it and told what it meant: you hold on with what you've got. She was a sociable, curious, fearless child who talked to every courier who came through for cake and knew where they were all going. She picked up Bridge from them. At ten, for her First Fall, she insisted on jumping off the barley raft into the gap between two rafts, which nobody does, and her mother has never quite recovered.","schooling":"The Barleycross oven-school, taught by Rinna Thresh's mother, Old Ada Thresh, who made pupils shout their letters like orders and counted a word spelled right only if it was shouted loud enough to be heard on the next raft. Wynn shouted well and spelled badly. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp flickered and went out: Half-Spark. She has never cast; she says she's never needed to hurry that much.","history":"She took over the village shop at twenty-four when the old trader left for Thistledown, and made it the courier's shop, with soft cake for the road and gossip from everywhere. At twenty-six she met Caddie Whinn, a Hollinwood gale-cutter delivering wedges, across the quarrel over the shade, and they fought about the trees for a season and then stopped fighting about anything. They married at twenty-eight, a storm wedding shouted across the chains from the east barley raft to the edge of Hollinwood. Caddie's belt landed. Wynn's fell, down through the chains, and caught in the roots of the Old Holly, where it still hangs a hand's breadth below the Stonehold coin. They shouted again and used rope. Neither would take the other's citadel-name, and both villages shouted about it, and now both villages buy from Wynn and complain to Caddie. Lately couriers have been buying twice the cake, and asking if she can bake it harder, so it keeps.","family":"Wife Caddie Whinn of Hollinwood; parents and three siblings on the ovens; her best friend Dagny Thresh, the smith, who is at war with Hollinwood and pretends Caddie doesn't exist.","hero":{"who":"Brannoc the Paired and Hollis Brask","why":"They built Hearthward across a frontier and never married. Wynn married across a much smaller one and feels she's done one better."},"band":{"act":"Sunwarm","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Summer Latin pop. A Gleamford flour-buyer played it at her stall one August and she danced on the counter.","rv_vote":"Sunwarm, which Barleycross thinks is treason and Caddie thinks is funny."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts at the Assembly for Whit, because Whit is young and keeps the ledger and might one day sort out the beer price.","edict":"Against, cheerfully. 'Lightforge can count my cakes. They can't count what's in them. That's a family recipe.'"},"wants":"For Barleycross and Hollinwood to sit down, once, and eat cake together, standing up.","site":"skybridge_vf1","town":"Barleycross","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:skybridge_vf1":{"name":"Dagny Thresh","age":29,"role":"village smith; forges sickles, shackle-pins and oven doors; younger sister of the head baker","look":"Tall, strong, sun-browned from the barley rafts, with a blacksmith's scorched leather apron and a grey streak at one brow from charges she brags about; her sister's voice, louder.","voice":"Brash, competitive, funny, shouts everything as if it were a challenge, which it usually is.","greet":"WAHAI! Sickles, pins, a new oven door? Say it loud, Rinna's next door and she'll shout over you.","lines":["Hollinwood's trees shade the east barley raft. Shade! On barley! In Stormreach! Someone should do something. I'm someone.","Rinna shouts every order like a vow and keeps it the same way. I shout every order like a dare. I keep it better.","Every courier wants twice the cake. You know why. Something's coming. If it comes, I'm riding south, not baking."],"about":"Dagny Thresh, Barleycross. Rinna's sister. She got the ovens, I got the anvil, and I got the better deal. I've got a Full Spark and twelve knots and I'd use them all if Lightforge comes up the long fall. I'd use six on Hollinwood's trees first.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native, at volume"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent"}],"childhood":"Dagny was the second Thresh daughter, born four years after Rinna, and spent her whole childhood trying to be louder, faster and better than her sister, and occasionally managing one of the three. Their mother, Old Ada, ran the ovens and the oven-school both and treated her daughters like an apprenticeship and a competition. Dagny hated baking. She loved the forge at the end of the raft where old Josse Clatter made oven doors, and she spent every spare hour there being told to go home. She was bold, brash and kind underneath it, and the first to throw her belt across the gap in the children's game of storm weddings. At her First Fall she shouted 'BEAT YOU' to Rinna all the way down.","schooling":"Old Ada's oven-school, shouting letters raft to raft, where she was the loudest and Rinna the most accurate, and their mother marked them accordingly and caused a decade-long fight. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp blazed. She did the Lamp Years at Skyrend and two years at the Tempest Tower at Korrhaven, which she left at fifteen, bored, with her grey sash and two knots, to apprentice to Josse at the forge.","history":"She has been the Barleycross smith since Josse retired, when she was twenty-two. She forges sickles that the barley-farmers swear by and the oven doors her sister swears at. She has spent twelve charges, most of them at the Crossing Knot in gales, holding the great shackle still while the old pins were replaced. She has the streak to show for it and makes sure people see it. She has had several noisy loves and no wedding, because, she says, she hasn't met anyone who can throw a belt that far. She leads the Barleycross complaint about the Hollinwood shade with enormous energy and very little effect. Her best friend Wynn married a Hollinwood cutter, which Dagny handles by not acknowledging Caddie's existence. Since the Edict talk she has started training with a Skyrend rider-captain twice a month and has asked to be put on a list for the riders if it comes to it. Six weeks ago she forged a pruning hook with a forty-foot handle.","family":"Sister Rinna, head baker, whom she fights with daily and would die for; their mother Old Ada, retired; no partner, which she says is Barleycross's loss.","hero":{"who":"Seraphine Skyrend","why":"Thirty-one, Warden of Skyrend, speaker of the Assembly, and against the Edict at the top of her voice. Dagny thinks Seraphine is what Stormreach is supposed to sound like."},"band":{"act":"The Bellows","realm":"Flameborne","why":"Stadium rock where the crowd pumps the forge. She saw them once at Korrhaven and pumped so hard she split her palms.","rv_vote":"The Bellows, and she'll arm-wrestle anyone who says Thunderhead."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Tamra on the Triumvirate, a rider who knows what a charge costs, and for Seraphine at every Assembly she can reach.","edict":"Against, ready to ride. 'They want to count our sparks? They can count them coming up the long fall.'"},"wants":"Sun on the east barley raft, and a place on the rider list.","site":"skybridge_vf1","town":"Barleycross","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:skybridge_vf1":{"name":"Hamon Lowe","age":61,"role":"barley farmer on the east raft, the shaded one; keeps a sun diary; maltster in winter","look":"Soft, round, pink-cheeked, with a farmer's hat pulled low and a hand that drifts to his chest when he climbs; barley chaff in his eyebrows.","voice":"Mild, pleasant, a little evasive, agrees with everyone and then quietly does what he likes.","greet":"Grain? Bless you. Barleycross will be pleased. It was a thin harvest on the east raft. Very shady. Terrible. Hm.","lines":["Hollinwood's trees shade my raft. Everyone's very cross about it on my behalf. Very kind of them. Hm.","Every courier wants twice the cake this season. My wife says that means war. I say it means hungry couriers. We'll see which of us is right. I hope it's me.","I keep a sun diary. How many hours each day on the east raft. Forty years. Fascinating, if you're me. Nobody else is me, thank goodness."],"about":"Hamon Lowe. Barley on the east raft for forty years. Malt for Skybridge in winter. I'm the one the shade falls on, so I'm the one everybody complains for. I let them. It's nice to be fussed over at my age.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"reasonable, from Skybridge maltsters"}],"childhood":"He was born on the east barley raft, a farmer's second son, and grew up in a family that talked about weather the way other families talk about each other. His father kept a weather book, his grandfather kept a weather book, and Hamon was given his own at eight with the sun-hours ruled into columns. He was a placid, dreamy, slightly lazy child who liked lying in the barley in the wind more than harvesting it, and was the only Barleycross boy who could fall asleep on a swinging raft in a gale. At ten he did his First Fall into a barley-sack net and claimed to have dozed off on the way down. In those days Hollinwood's trees didn't reach over the east raft; the shade crept in slowly over his whole life, a branch at a time.","schooling":"Old Ada Thresh's oven-school, where he was neither loud nor accurate and was mostly left alone to draw the shapes of clouds in his slate margins; Ada once told him he would make a good farmer because he was patient enough to wait for barley, which he took as the highest praise. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the lamps stayed dark: spark-poor. He learned malting from his uncle.","history":"He took over the east raft at twenty-one when his elder brother went to the patrols. He has farmed it for forty years and malted for Skybridge every winter, and kept the sun diary every single day, with notes on wind, rain and barley height, in a row of bound books on a shelf above his bed. He married Ilse, a cake-baker, at twenty-five; she is the shrewd one, and she manages the money and the neighbours. They have three daughters, all married away from Barleycross, one to a wright at Skybridge. At fifty-three Hamon had a pain in his chest while scything in the full sun of a hot harvest and lay in the barley for an hour before anyone found him. The healer at Skybridge told him to keep out of the heat. Hollinwood's shade has been falling on the east raft for most of his life, and every year it reaches a little further. Every year the village grows angrier about it on his behalf. Every year he says how dreadful it is, and goes and stands in it.","family":"Wife Ilse, who knows; three daughters; a grandson at Skybridge who wants to be a link-tester like Juniper Rask.","hero":{"who":"Mother Orra","why":"She led the Unmarked to the slag-hills and made an ugly first hammer that worked. Hamon likes ugly things that work, including, he says, himself."},"band":{"act":"The Stampers","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Amapiano where the applause is the beat. He can clap along sitting in the shade without anyone knowing he's sitting.","rv_vote":"The Stampers, gently, without telling Barleycross it's a Stonehold act."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for Kesh at the Assembly, softly, because Kesh doesn't rush anything, including complaints about trees.","edict":"Torn, mostly because he hasn't read it. He thinks registering sparks sounds like a weather book for people, which he can't help liking."},"wants":"For everybody to stop fixing the shade.","site":"skybridge_vf1","town":"Barleycross","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:skybridge_vf1:b":{"name":"Tilda Carrow","age":42,"role":"chaff-carter to Thistledown, and Barleycross's shout-counter at the Free Assembly","look":"Rangy and long-faced, with an Assembly counter's tally-board strapped to her back and chaff in the seams of everything; dark hair cropped short for the wind.","voice":"Even, careful, a little prim, likes numbers more than opinions and says so; dry jokes delivered without a smile.","greet":"Wahai. Tilda Carrow. I count the shouts for Barleycross. I also cart the chaff to Thistledown, which is louder.","lines":["Every courier wants twice the cake. That's not a rumour, that's a number. I counted the sleeves.","The Assembly says the shout against the Edict carried by thousands. I was there. I counted my section. It was big. It was not that big.","Skybridge sells its beer back to us at three times the barley price. I've told the Assembly. The Assembly shouted. The beer's still three times."],"about":"Tilda Carrow. Barleycross shout-counter: at the Free Assembly I stand on the bench with a tally-board and judge whether our raft's shout was loud enough to count. It is a strange job for someone who thinks shouting is a strange way to govern. I cart chaff for money. The horses like me.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, formal"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good reading, halting speech; she reads Lightforge law for pleasure"}],"childhood":"Tilda's father was a chaff-carter and her mother a shout-counter before her, and she grew up in the cart, going back and forth between Barleycross and Thistledown, counting horses, rafts, chain-links and stars. She was a solemn, precise, slightly lonely child who liked to know exactly how many of anything there were, and Stormreach, which never writes anything down, frustrated her from the start. She went to her first Assembly at six on her mother's shoulders, watched three hundred voices drop a Warden, and asked how anyone knew it was three hundred. Her mother said 'you listen', which Tilda thought was not an answer. At her First Fall she counted the knots in the net before she jumped. There were four hundred and eleven. She has never forgotten it.","schooling":"Old Ada Thresh's oven-school, where she was quiet in a school that ran on shouting, and Ada, unexpectedly, let her keep the class tally and read out the scores. She found a Lightforge law-primer in a Thistledown trader's cart at eleven and taught herself Clear from it with a Bridge dictionary. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the lamps stayed dark: spark-poor.","history":"She took over the chaff-cart at twenty and her mother's tally-board at twenty-six, and has counted Barleycross's shout at every full Assembly since. A counter stands on the bench above her section and judges by ear whether it carried; it is honourable, unpaid and argued over endlessly. Tilda is the first counter anyone can remember who also writes the count down, on a slate, which some think is very Lightforge of her. She has never married; she lived for ten years with a Thistledown breaker who wanted her to shout more, and they parted on good terms. She reads Lightforge law the way other people read stories, and has read the Radiant Edict all the way through, twice, in Clear. At the last full Assembly at Skyrend the shout against the Edict was called as overwhelming. Tilda counted her section and the four around it. It was large. It was not what the herald called. She has written to Absalom Tarr at Harrowgate, the old herald, to ask how a count is judged.","family":"Her mother, a retired counter who still argues with the current heralds; a brother who rides the patrols; a niece who wants to be a counter and can count to nine hundred.","hero":{"who":"Ardyn Solbrand","why":"Not for the Edict. For writing the law down, word by word, so anyone can read it and argue with it. She thinks Stormreach would be a fairer place if it wrote one thing down."},"band":{"act":"Verdict","realm":"Lightforge","why":"Symphonic metal structured as trials. Every song has evidence, argument and a ruling. She finds it very soothing.","rv_vote":"Verdict, though she knows exactly how many Stormreach lamps Lightforge acts get, which is very few."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Whit, the ledger-keeper, the one Chain-Warden who counts. As a counter she is not supposed to shout at all, and doesn't, and hates it.","edict":"Torn, leaning for, which she has told nobody: she thinks the Edict is badly made but that a written law is better than three hundred voices in a room."},"wants":"For the Free Assembly to write down one count.","site":"skybridge_vf1","town":"Barleycross","realm":"Stormreach"},"shop:skybridge_vf2":{"name":"Edwy Combe","age":39,"role":"beekeeper and Hollinwood's trader; sells holly-smoke honey, kit and packs; buys salvage","look":"Neat, thin, sting-scarred hands, a beekeeper's veil pushed back on his hat; a careful dresser in a village of woodcutters; smells of smoke and honey.","voice":"Polite, slightly formal, curious about everything technical, talks to bees in a murmur and to people only a little louder.","greet":"Good day. Wahai, I mean. Honey's the dark one, bitter, from the trees. The only tree-honey in Stormreach. Try it on cake. Don't try it on your own.","lines":["Men out of Metalcrown came asking for holly last month. Wanted it for springs, they said. Didn't know iron needed wood. Seems it needs everything now.","Barleycross says our trees shade their barley. Our trees were here first. Nobody planted them. Nobody knows how they got here. That's not an argument, but it's not nothing.","The Stonehold coin on the Old Holly's green now. Benedek writes to Stonehold about it every year. Stonehold writes back every year. It's the longest friendship either of them has."],"about":"Edwy Combe. Beekeeper. Trader. I keep the hives in the wood, which nobody else in the Realm does, and I sell what the wood and the bees make. I'm interested in how things work. In Hollinwood that makes me a bit odd. In Metalcrown, I'm told, it would make me normal.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent"},{"tongue":"Shiftcant","level":"some; he has been learning it from a Bridge-Shiftcant primer"}],"childhood":"His mother kept the hives, and his father was a gale-cutter who was killed by a holly that swung the wrong way in a gale when Edwy was six. He remembers almost nothing of his father except his laugh and the smell of cut holly, and a great deal about bees. He grew up in his mother's hive-sheds, quiet and careful, learning which smoke calms a hive and which angers it. He was a thoughtful, tidy, inquisitive child who took apart everything with a mechanism in Hollinwood, including the village's only clock, and could not put it back, and was made to shout an apology at dawn for a week. His First Fall was into a net his mother strung and he landed on his back looking up at the holly and decided he would never cut it.","schooling":"Hollinwood's wood-school, which meets under the Old Holly and is taught by Wren Hollin, who teaches letters by cutting them into holly bark and lets the children watch the bark grow over them through the year. Edwy was her best reader. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, nothing lit: spark-poor. At fifteen he spent a winter in Skybridge with a cousin, a link-tester, and saw his first Metalcrown iron, and never forgot how cleanly it rang.","history":"He took over the hives at twenty when his mother's hands began to swell from stings, and the Hollinwood trade at thirty. He has made the holly-smoke honey known in Skyrend, where it is now a luxury, and sells it to the Chainyards, who melt it into thunder-milk. He married Isa Fennick, a frame-builder under Wren Hollin, at twenty-five; she took his citadel-name, which was Hollinwood anyway, and has her own opinions about Metalcrown. They have a son of eleven who wants to cut trees, which frightens Edwy more than he says. Last month two engineers from Metalcrown came through with a Skybridge guide and asked about holly: how it bends, how it springs back, how much he could supply. They were polite, exact, and the first people he had met who asked the questions he asks. He told them the village wouldn't sell. Then, at night, he sold them a cartload of coppiced holly out of his own share of the wood.","family":"Wife Isa, a frame-builder; son Aldo, eleven; his mother, retired, stung to the elbows and still opinionated about smoke.","hero":{"who":"Solvane","why":"The young Chief Engineer who read Metalcrown's ninth petition politely while the Council laughed. Edwy thinks being polite in front of people who are laughing at you is a kind of engineering."},"band":{"act":"Oil and Ivory","realm":"Metalcrown","why":"A machinist crooner. He found a music-wire of him in a Skybridge salvage lot and plays it to the bees, who seem calmer for it.","rv_vote":"Oil and Ivory, partly for the music and partly because Metalcrown never gets a lamp from anyone."},"vote":{"local":"He rarely goes to the Assembly, because it is loud and seems to him badly designed. If pressed, he would shout for Whit, who seems to be the only one counting.","edict":"Torn. He doesn't like being counted, but he can see the sense in knowing what you have, and he suspects Metalcrown smiling at it is the most interesting reaction in the Nine."},"wants":"To see how the Metalcrown men use his holly.","site":"skybridge_vf2","town":"Hollinwood","realm":"Stormreach"},"smith:skybridge_vf2":{"name":"Quenna Burr","age":48,"role":"village smith; forges axe-heads, wedge-irons and steam-bending clamps; once the Assembly's storm-caller","look":"Rangy and hard, with hair gone completely white and a face still young under it; a life-cord of a hundred and forty knots she wears doubled round her neck; an old Tempest Tower sash, six knots, used as a forge rag.","voice":"Quiet, deliberate, rarely shouts and so is listened to; speaks of the spark as if it were a debt she paid off.","greet":"Wahai. Axes, wedges, clamps. Ask what you want. I'll tell you if you need it.","lines":["Men out of Metalcrown came asking for holly. Springs, they said. I've seen Metalcrown springs. They don't need holly. They need something holly does that iron doesn't. Think about that.","Hollinwood fines anyone who drops a tree in still air. Laziness, they call it. I'd call it bad timing. Same thing, in a gale.","A hundred and forty knots. I'm forty-eight. You do the sums. I've done mine."],"about":"Quenna Burr. I went to Korrhaven, finished the Tempest Tower, and called storms for the Free Assembly for twenty years. Then I came here, because trees don't argue and an axe takes nothing off you but sweat. I'm not sorry about the knots. I'm just finished with them.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"fluent, Conclave-precise"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"good, from Conclave lectures"},{"tongue":"Old Spark-speech","level":"a handful of oath-words and rune-names, Tower-taught"}],"childhood":"Quenna was the only child of two Skyrend chain-lift hands and grew up riding the lifts between citadels, up and down all day in a cage over the gulf. She was a still, serious child in a city that hates stillness, and the lift-hands called her 'little Anchorite' fondly. She sparked at five by accident, throwing a lift-cage up a chain in a fright, and both parents saw her hair, at the brow, go white for a finger's width overnight. They wept. She didn't understand why. She did her First Fall at ten off a lift-cage, into a net strung across the gulf, and remembers the wind under her as the first time she ever felt the spark want to go somewhere and chose to let it.","schooling":"The Lamp Years at Skyrend, then six years at the Tempest Tower at Korrhaven, where she graduated first of her year with a six-knot sash. Her tutor, Warden-Master Iskel Moor, made every student climb the outside of the Tower in a gale without casting, to learn what the body can do alone. She did it twice. Years later, back at the Tower to lecture as the Assembly's storm-caller, she slipped a note under a frightened first-year's door telling her to go home while she still could. The girl went.","history":"She became the Free Assembly's storm-caller at nineteen, the one who pushes the wind for citadels in trouble, steadies the Assembly hall in a gale, and clears the lanes for funerals. She was very good and very expensive to herself: a hundred and forty knots in twenty years, her hair white by thirty. At thirty-nine, holding a cliff-farm steady through a three-day gale while its chains were replaced, she felt for the first time that she could not tell how many charges she had left, and that frightened her more than the drop. She resigned that winter. She came to Hollinwood, apprenticed herself to the old smith at forty, and took the forge at forty-four. She makes the best axes in the north. She has never married; she has had one long love, a Skyrend herald, which ended when his voice did. Wren Hollin is her closest friend. Twice a season, when a holly frame won't take the steam, Quenna goes out alone in a gale and casts, once, to bend it.","family":"Parents, retired lift-hands in Skyrend; no partner now; Wren Hollin, who is family in all but name.","hero":{"who":"Amon Greycloak","why":"The first Shardwatch: someone who watched the edge so others didn't have to. Quenna did that for twenty years and thinks he'd have understood why she stopped."},"band":{"act":"Permafrost","realm":"Frostgrave","why":"Black metal whose fans stand still. She saw them once at Korrhaven and stood still for an hour in a crowd of strangers and felt, for once, as though she wasn't spending anything.","rv_vote":"Permafrost, and she doesn't care who knows."},"vote":{"local":"She knows all three Chain-Wardens and has worked for each. She shouts for Kesh: 'chain first' is the storm-caller's rule, because she has seen what a fast shout does to a slow chain.","edict":"Against, and she will not register. She has given a hundred and forty charges to Stormreach; Lightforge can read her cord if it wants a number."},"wants":"To bend a frame without casting.","site":"skybridge_vf2","town":"Hollinwood","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:skybridge_vf2":{"name":"Caddie Whinn","age":34,"role":"gale-cutter; fells storm-holly only in a gale, reading which way the trunk will swing","look":"Lean, ropy-muscled, wind-reddened, with a scar through one eyebrow from a swinging branch and her dark hair knotted up with a holly twig; a felling axe on her back and a rope where a belt should be, to match her wife's.","voice":"Cheerful, cocky, quick, given to tall stories; lowers her voice and gets serious only about trees and her wife.","greet":"Grain? You're a good sort. Hollinwood remembers who brings grain. Barleycross remembers who brings complaints. I'm married into both, so I remember everything.","lines":["Men out of Metalcrown came asking for holly. For springs. We said no. I'd swear someone said yes, though. There's a row of fresh coppice stools on the north side and nobody's owned up.","Barleycross says our trees shade their barley. Someone's been cutting the east branches at night. Clean cuts. A smith's hook. I know whose.","Gale-Cutting. You only fell in a gale. You read the swing. Drop a tree in still air and you buy the cake. I've never bought the cake. Never. Ask anyone."],"about":"Caddie Whinn, Hollinwood. Gale-cutter. I married Wynn Harrier of Barleycross across the chains and both villages shouted at us. We kept our own citadel-names. My belt landed. Hers didn't. She's still wearing the rope. So am I, now.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"decent, woodcutter's"}],"childhood":"Caddie was the fourth of six children of a gale-cutter who taught them all to read a tree before they could read a word. She grew up in the holly, climbing it, swinging on it, falling out of it, learning by the age of seven which way a trunk would go in which wind. She was a show-off, a daredevil and a terrible liar. The family told the story of Ansgar Tow, the cutter who let out the chains so the Stonehold buyer's wood drifted off half a mile, so often that she thought he was an uncle. At ten, at her First Fall, she jumped off a branch of the Old Holly itself, which is forbidden, into a net her eldest sister strung in a hurry, and was grounded for a season.","schooling":"Wren Hollin's wood-school under the Old Holly, where she was terrible at letters and the best in the class at reading the bark, which Wren said was also a kind of reading. Wren once took the whole class out into a gale and had each child call which way a sapling would fall before she cut it; Caddie called them all. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the Tempest lamp flickered: Half-Spark. She has charged three times to stop a falling trunk crushing a cutter.","history":"She has been a gale-cutter since she was sixteen. She is the best in Hollinwood at the swing and the most likely to boast about it. She has never been fined for dropping a tree in still air, by her own account, and the whole village knows that is not true: once, at twenty, exhausted, she felled an old holly in a dead calm and blamed a gust nobody else felt, and bought nobody the cake. At twenty-six she took a cartload of wedges to Barleycross and got into a shouting match about the shade with a baker-trader called Wynn, which turned into a season of shouting matches and then into a wedding. They live half in each village, which suits neither. Last week Caddie found clean cuts on the east-side holly, high up, made by a long hook; she knows the shape of every smith's blade in the north, and knows this one was forged in Barleycross. Hamon Lowe has asked her, quietly, not to cut the shade. Wynn doesn't know any of this yet.","family":"Wife Wynn Harrier of Barleycross; five siblings, mostly cutters; her mother, who still reads a tree better than anyone.","hero":{"who":"Ansgar Tow","why":"The Hollinwood cutter who let out the chains so the wood drifted half a mile from the Stonehold buyer and told him he was welcome to come and get it. Caddie thinks that is the funniest and best thing anyone from Hollinwood ever did."},"band":{"act":"Landslide","realm":"Stonehold","why":"Hard rock, and loud enough to cut to. She knows it's a Stonehold act and plays it louder to annoy Benedek.","rv_vote":"Landslide, purely to make the Hollinwood elders choke on their honey."},"vote":{"local":"She shouts for Seraphine at the Assembly and for whoever will keep Barleycross's complaint off the floor.","edict":"Against. 'Lightforge can come and count our sparks in a gale. We'll see which way they fall.'"},"wants":"For her wife and her wood to stop being on opposite sides of anything.","site":"skybridge_vf2","town":"Hollinwood","realm":"Stormreach"},"vill:skybridge_vf2:b":{"name":"Benedek Moss","age":72,"role":"retired honey-keeper; Hollinwood's self-appointed correspondent with Stonehold on the matter of the coin","look":"Tall, stooped, gaunt, with a long white beard he tucks into his belt and ink on every finger; a leather case of letters always under one arm; spectacles mended with holly-twine.","voice":"Pedantic, formal, long-winded, quotes his own letters, delighted by a precise word; secretly very funny.","greet":"Good day to you. Wahai, in the vernacular. You'll have seen the coin on the Old Holly. Stonehold calls it a deposit. It is, I assure you, a gift. I have the correspondence.","lines":["Stonehold calls it a deposit. Hollinwood calls it a gift. I have written to the Hold-Senate every year for forty-four years to clarify. They write back every year. We are very close.","Men from Metalcrown asked about holly for springs. I told them there is a form. There is not a form. I may write one.","Barleycross complains of shade. I have drafted a reply. It runs to nine pages. Wren says nobody will read it. Wren is very probably right."],"about":"Benedek Moss, Hollinwood. I kept the hives for fifty years and now I keep the record. Stormreach does not write anything down. I do. I write to Stonehold about the coin, to Barleycross about the shade, to Skyrend about everything. Someone has to have the last word, and I intend it to be a well-chosen one.","languages":[{"tongue":"Gale","level":"native"},{"tongue":"Bridge","level":"very fluent, written, ornate"},{"tongue":"Holdspeech","level":"reads it slowly, from forty-four years of Stonehold replies"},{"tongue":"Clear","level":"reads it well"}],"childhood":"Benedek was born in Hollinwood to a honey-keeper and a cutter and was, from the start, the child who wanted to know exactly what everything was called. He followed the woodcutters around asking the names of tools and wrote them on holly-bark with charcoal. He was tall, awkward, earnest and dreadful at climbing, and the cutters' children teased him, and he answered them in sentences so long they gave up. His grandmother told him the story of the Stonehold buyer and the coin and said, laughing, 'and Stonehold still says it's theirs'. He was eight, and he asked, very seriously, 'has anyone told them it isn't?' It turned out nobody had, in writing. His First Fall was into the family net, and he wrote an account of it.","schooling":"The wood-school under the Old Holly, taught in his day by Wren Hollin's grandmother, Old Bryony Hollin, who taught letters by bark-cutting and once let him spend a whole winter copying out a Bridge dictionary a trader had left, and then gave him the dictionary. At the Lamp Test, aged eight, the lamps stayed dark: spark-poor. He is mostly self-taught from books left by traders, and owns more books than anyone in Hollinwood, which is eleven.","history":"He kept the Hollinwood hives for fifty years, alongside Edwy Combe's mother, and was a careful, patient, slightly comic keeper who wrote a treatise on holly-smoke that no one has read. He married Orla, a cutter with a big laugh who found him hilarious; she died eight years ago and he wrote her a letter every week for a year and then stopped, because she would have told him to. They had two sons, both cutters, both bored by his letters. At twenty-eight he wrote his first letter to the Stonehold Hold-Senate, in his best Bridge, setting out that the timber-buyer's coin was a gift. A reply came, eight months later, in heavy Holdspeech-flavoured Bridge, explaining that it was a deposit. He has written every year since. Stonehold has replied every year since, by several different clerks. Twenty years ago, one clerk's reply conceded, in a single line, that it might reasonably be called a gift. Benedek has told no one and has gone on writing.","family":"Wife Orla, let go eight years ago; two sons who are cutters; a grandson, Aldo's friend, who has started reading the letters for fun.","hero":{"who":"Ithiel the Copyist","why":"'The Lamp That Leaned.' A copyist who kept the record and is remembered for it. Benedek would like to be remembered, by somebody, for his file."},"band":{"act":"Retell","realm":"Spiritveil","why":"A rapper who repeats every earlier bar. Benedek finds it the only music that respects the importance of the full record.","rv_vote":"Retell, accompanied by a letter to the Realm Vision committee explaining why."},"vote":{"local":"He shouts for Whit at the Assembly, in complete sentences, and once was told by a herald that his shout was too well-constructed to count.","edict":"Against, on drafting grounds: he has read it and thinks the definitions are sloppy, and has written to Meridian to say so."},"wants":"For Stonehold to concede the coin publicly, and, secretly, for them never to.","site":"skybridge_vf2","town":"Hollinwood","realm":"Stormreach"}};
window.VHSTATUES={"keep":{"label":"Iska Vashen","look":"Not a statue but the Gate Hearth: a waist-high open hearth of black basalt in the middle of the Keep's yard, the stone where Iska cooked for the first patrol she raised. An iron grate sits over it, cast with the print of one road-boot pressed into the bar, said to be hers. Sculptors added a heavy bronze cookpot with a lid on a chain; the garrison says Iska never owned a lid in her life ('lids are for people with time'), and the lid has been quietly lost three times and replaced twice.","plaque":"THEN YOU KEEP IT. — She did.","custom":"Every rider going out on a contract takes a coal from the Gate Hearth in a tin and brings the tin back cold when the job is done; the hearth is never allowed to go out, and the gate-lamb is roasted over it after every Flattening. Gethin Durrow tends it on the night wall.","dispute":"Tobin Ashgrove, who has re-forged the grate twice, says the bootprint is a size too small and was cast from the present Lord Vashen's boy-boot, the one that is not on the Boot Post at Clayford; the garrison is split, and the Lord says nothing."},"cindermoot":{"label":"The two speakers of the Long Moot","look":"The Speakers' Hearth: a low round hearth of cinder brick just outside the Moot Ring's north stones, with two stone seats facing each other across the fire at exactly equal height, nobody kneeling, nobody raised. The carver cut both speakers' names into the seat-backs and, for drama, carved two open mouths in the hearth-rim, mid-shout; everyone agrees they were silent by day forty, and children stuff the mouths with moot-seed husks.","plaque":"FORTY DAYS. TWO VOICES. NO WINNER. EAT FIRST.","custom":"On Lost-Voice Day the hearth is lit at dawn and the town's first chalk argument is written on the seats; at dusk the losers wash the walls and the winners wash the Speakers' Hearth, so even winning has a broom in it. Lorcan Moothe sits in Aodh Cullen's seat every year and leaves Mared Ffoulk's empty for Cinderhill.","dispute":"Cinderhill insists Mared Ffoulk's seat was cut a thumb's width lower than Cullen's, as a Cindermoot insult; Cindermoot insists it has sunk. Every spring the Cinderhill delegation measures it with a knotted string, and every spring Dorran Fyle chalks a sarcastic answer under the plaque."},"emberside":{"label":"Hywela Emberly","look":"The Chain Hearth: an iron fire-basket on the channel bank beside the Raking Post, with a length of the original lamb-chain coiled round its base in the shape of a sleeping serpent, the links worn bright where children have sat on it. A small figure of Hywela stands at the basket's side, upright, hands on the chain, looking at the Run, not at anyone watching. The smith who cast her made her tall and young; Gwil Emberly says she was sixty, very short, and had a pipe in her mouth the whole forty days, and he has hung a clay pipe on the figure's belt to correct it.","plaque":"SHE BOUGHT A LAMB.","custom":"At Serpent Supper the lamb shank thrown into the Run is first roasted on the Chain Hearth, and the youngest keeper holds the chain while the oldest throws, as Gwil did for his grandmother; then everyone eats lamb 'so it isn't eating alone'.","dispute":"Smeltgate's guild paid for the casting and wants a plate saying so on the basket; Emberside took the plate off and used it as a coal-cake mould, and the guild has been sent the cakes every Serpent Supper since, by way of answer."},"stillcity":{"label":"Edda Frostwraith","look":"Carved from a single block of the oldest, bluest glacier-ice in the Hall of Kept Words and kept frozen by the Hall's cold for nine hundred years. She sits on a plain stool at a knot-frame, head bowed, both hands in the cord, frost thick in her eyebrows the way every Chrona seer's are. The carvers gave her a seer's long robe she would never have worn at a frame, and the cord in her hands has nine knots. Her real cord, hung in the Knotwall a few yards behind her, has eight.","plaque":"EDDA FROSTWRAITH. She saw it. She tied it. It held.","custom":"Apprentice knotwrights touch the last knot on the statue's cord before their first paid commission, and say nothing. The ice there has worn into a smooth hollow, so the ninth knot is nearly gone, which the knotwrights say is the statue correcting itself.","dispute":"The Prophet Court says Edda's knotted vision proves a seen future belongs to the one who sees it, and cites her in every ruling against the Auction Hall. The oracle-brokers say Edda was paid for the vision by the family it concerned, and have a three-hundred-year-old bill of sale they bring out every time. Hekla Orsk, apprentice, has noticed the new-dyed cord nobody saw tied hangs within arm's reach of Edda's own."},"icewatch":{"label":"Olin Stair","look":"Not a statue anyone admits to making. Every spring, when the new Glass Stair is cut, someone carves a small figure into the ice wall of the landing beside the ninetieth step: a man bent nearly double, a girl on his back with her arms round his neck, both faceless, roughed out in a few strokes. It melts each thaw and comes back each cut, a little different, like the stair itself. The carver always gives the girl two boots, though the story says she lost one on the way down.","plaque":"No plaque. Someone scratches four words into the ice beneath it each year: 'He carried her down.'","custom":"Climbers who reach the ninetieth step touch the girl's foot for luck before the hardest part of the climb. Children are lifted up to do it. Nanna Brede, who has never been told Olin was a heretic, does it twice, once for herself and once for her brother.","dispute":"The Still City has written to Icewatch four times asking that a Thawborn not be honoured on the Stair its couriers use. Icewatch replies that the figure is a brother carrying his sister, not a heresy, and that in any case it will stop being carved the day the Still City pays the toll at the fair rate. Stair-master Brenna Tolle says she has no idea who carves it, and keeps a sketch of every year's figure pinned beside her sketches of the stair."},"hoarfen":{"label":"Arvid Sel","look":"A tall birch post in the meeting-house yard, carved by a peat-wife a year after the swarm, with an iron hook forged by Arvid's son Joran holding his old word-cord at the top. Below the hook the peat-wife carved a man mid-stride, arms thrown wide, running. She meant it kindly. She showed what the village said had happened. There is no child anywhere on the post.","plaque":"A slate nailed at the foot: 'ARVID SEL. He moved. Be the stone.'","custom":"At the end of every Stone Hour, when the bell rings again and the village unfreezes, the children who lasted the hour without a twitch go and lay a hand flat on the post before they get their knot. Tuva Stray has never once been allowed to.","dispute":"Half the village thinks a post showing a man running is a warning and should stay. The other half, mostly the younger peat-cutters, think it's a cruelty to keep a man's worst moment carved in birch in front of his son's forge. Joran Sel has never said a word on either side. He re-forged the hook three winters ago when it rusted, and made it stronger."},"dawnwatch":{"label":"Captain Merrit Halbrand","look":"White limestone, life-size, set at the far end of the Dusk Gallery facing outward over the Rim. She stands with one elbow on a carved copy of the brass rail and an open Day-Book in her other hand, the pages carved so finely you can read the first line. Meridian's sculptors put a lit lamp at her feet; she never carried one on the wall, and every Rim-born soldier knows it. They also carved the Day-Book with nine fewer pages than a real one of that year, which only Ottar Kelbrand has noticed.","plaque":"MERRIT HALBRAND, CAPTAIN. SHE SAW. SHE SAID. SEEN AND SAID.","custom":"At Lamp-Down the youngest soldier on the wall puts out the gallery lamp, then reaches across and lays two fingers on the open stone page before sitting down. Rim-born soldiers also snuff the carved lamp at her feet with a thumb, which does nothing, and do it anyway.","dispute":"Meridian wants the plaque to add 'and was judged by the Assize', which is true; Dawnwatch's Rim-born half say it's also true she was not punished, and want that added too. Since the Edict outlawed the Penumbrists, a Meridian clerk has asked whether a statue of an officer who sheltered them should stand on a Sentinel wall at all, and Captain Adra Varbrand has answered him in writing, at length."},"candlewick":{"label":"Aure of the Rim","look":"Grey Rim sandstone, a little under life-size, a woman sitting on a low wall with her hands open in her lap and her face lifted, carved by Candlewick chandlers' ancestors and set beside the moot-house under the Dipping Wheel. The original had a deep stone hood drawn forward so that her face sat in shadow at every hour; a Meridian restoration a century ago 'cleaned' the hood back to a fold at her neck so her face could be seen, and the chisel marks of that are still visible if you run a hand over her hair.","plaque":"AURE. SHE SPOKE AT DUSK. WE LISTENED. (Below, smaller, added by the restorers: 'In the light, all is seen.')","custom":"When a child snuffs their First Wick at the First Telling, they carry the warm stub to Aure's open hands and leave it there overnight. Kit Rendle broke the custom this year by keeping theirs.","dispute":"The Edict has outlawed the Penumbrists by name, and a Meridian order now requires the statue to be lit 'from all sides at all hours' so it cannot be used as a meeting-place. On the Rim, at dusk, that takes a great many candles. Candlewick pays the Dawnwatch wax toll on every one of them, to light the statue of the woman whose teaching is now a crime, and Oriel Lampwright has written to the captain asking whether the toll can at least be waived for this."},"goldfield":{"label":"Annick of the Common","look":"Polished brass on a white stone plinth in front of the Long Mews: a girl of about twelve in a herding smock, barefoot, one arm raised, with a Radiant Hawk landing on her wrist. Lothar Bellbrand's grandfather cast it. The sculptor gave the hawk a sealed letter in its talons, because the Assize paid for the statue; every Goldfield child knows that in the real story the hawk had her lunch, and there is a small bread-roll scratched into the plinth by a hundred years of children's knives.","plaque":"ANNICK OF THE COMMON. THE FIRST HAWK OF GOLDFIELD CAME FOR HER LUNCH. SHE LET IT STAY.","custom":"Children leave a crust on the plinth on the first day of each season. A hawk always takes it before the next bell, and the child whose crust is taken first is let to ring the Feather-Letting handbell that year.","dispute":"The Assize has asked twice for the plaque to say she 'trained the first courier for the Assize', which is how its own records put it. Goldfield refuses: the Assize took every hawk Annick's grandchildren bred, early in the Concord, and the village bought them back one pair at a time over twenty years. With the Assize asking for twenty hawks again this season, someone has started tying a black jess round the brass hawk's leg every night, and the mews-keepers take it off every morning without asking who."},"prismport":{"label":"Ebbe Kilnsby","look":"Cast in witness-glass, life-size, a skinny boy of perhaps fourteen holding a lump of raw glass up to his eye, on the harbour mole beside the Great Lens. Because it is witness-glass, he is nearly invisible at noon; you see him properly only when the Lens's white spot passes over him and he lights from inside. The casters gave him eyebrows. No kiln-boy of his day had any, and every glassmaker in town touches their own bare brow when they pass.","plaque":"EBBE KILNSBY. HE LOOKED THROUGH IT FIRST.","custom":"On Cracking Day the first swing of the hammer on the mole is made by whoever poured the year's best witness-pane, at a flawed pane held up beside Ebbe, so that he 'sees it go'. Quillon Panewell has taken the first swing nine times.","dispute":"The Witness Courts across Clearwater claim the statue was cast from a flawed batch of witness-glass that should have been smashed on Cracking Day centuries ago, and that keeping it on the mole breaks the very rule Prismport is famous for. Prismport says that a flaw nobody can find isn't a flaw. Thea Lensman says that at the high-summer angle she has seen a hairline inside his raised hand, and has written it down, and has not told the Courts."},"irontown":{"label":"Mother Orra","look":"Not a statue but an engine plate: a cast-iron plate the size of a door, riveted to the side of Empty Hand, the first furnace at the top of the slag-mountain. It shows a woman's two hands closed round a hammer haft, and nothing else of her. Beside the plate, in an iron cage, hangs the hammer itself, lopsided, the head pitted, the haft replaced so often that the cage-keeper's tally says eleven times. The plate's casters gave her hammer a clean, symmetrical head. The real one beside it is ugly, as the stories say, and the two hang side by side, so that every visitor sees the difference.","plaque":"MOTHER ORRA. FIRST HAMMER. UGLY. IT WORKED. I MADE IT.","custom":"On the day a child is given their First Tool at ten, they are brought up to Empty Hand and lay their palm on the caged hammer's head, and say out loud the first thing they intend to make. Most children say something small. Ermengarde Clinker said 'trouble', and her gran clipped her ear, and she has been proud of it for seventy-one years.","dispute":"The engine-keepers swear the head is the original and only the haft has been replaced; old Commons families say the head cracked two hundred years ago and was recast in secret, and that a recast hammer is a lie hung beside a plate. The Synthesis Council would like to move both into the Reactor Halls, 'for preservation', and the crest has made it plain it would not survive the move."},"slagmoor":{"label":"The drowned third shift","look":"The Cap: the round iron plug Torvald Pickhaft forged to seal the flooded shaft, the width of a wagon, riveted in four rings and set flush with the pithead floor beside Old Grandmother. Its face carries twenty-three names punched letter by letter, and a twenty-fourth space at the end, deliberately left smooth. Torvald has never said whose it is. In wet weather it sweats, and the village says the shaft is breathing.","plaque":"SHIFT THREE. NOT UP. WE LISTEN.","custom":"Every night at Last Up the triangle is struck three times. On the anniversary of the flood it is struck a fourth time, on the Cap itself, with the butt of a pick, and the whole village waits on the pithead in silence until the iron stops humming, which takes longer than anyone expects.","dispute":"There is good spark-ore under that shaft, and the Synthesis Council would like it reopened. Gertrud Dross has the numbers for what it would earn. Jet Tamper says the props down there have been underwater for nineteen years and nobody is going down to find out what's left of them. Torvald Pickhaft says the Cap comes off when he's under it, which some people take as a threat and others as a promise."},"furnacerow":{"label":"Foreman Tibb Anvil","look":"The Anvil seat on the Commons Bench, kept empty, with a small cast plate bolted to the backrest showing Tibb Anvil standing, tall and broad, holding up a single ballot token. The Row's own stories say he was a short man whose feet didn't reach the ground when he sat, and the seat proves it: there is a little iron footrest welded under the Anvil place that no other seat has. The plate-maker, a city man, made him tall. The Row has never forgiven the plate and has never removed it either.","plaque":"ONE SHIFT, ONE VOTE. EVEN YOU. — T. ANVIL, LAST FOREMAN OF THE FOUNDRY COMMONS","custom":"Nobody sits in the Anvil seat, except on Speaker's Night, when the child Speaker does, all night, with their feet on Tibb's footrest. Crispin Cope sat there last year and his feet reached it exactly, which the Row took as a very good sign.","dispute":"Ironwatch wants the whole Bench cleared for drilling, and has ordered it. Within the Row the older quarrel is about the plaque: half the families say 'Even you' was said to a visiting Dominus who tried to speak without a shift behind him; the other half say Tibb said it to his own son, who wanted to skip a vote to go to the Smeltway. Ada Lathe knows which, and won't say."},"hollowgate":{"label":"Veilrend","look":"A standing figure in black iron in the gatehall, under the hanging bones of the Stormreach skyray. A hooded cloak with nothing inside the hood: the casters hollowed it right through, so you see the wall behind. Only the hands are carved in full, one open at the hip, one holding a short blade flat against the chest. The sculptor's chisel slipped on the left hand and gave it an extra knuckle groove; nobody has ever fixed it, and recruits say it is the only true detail on the figure.","plaque":"(no words; a single pebble set into the plinth)","custom":"Before their first Changing of Hands, each recruit reaches into the empty hood and touches the back of it in silence. Rell does it before every drill. Old sentries say if your hand comes out cold you will keep your pebble.","dispute":"Whether the blade faces in or out. The garrison says it is held flat to the chest, a guard; Mosk, who has cleaned it, insists the edge turns very slightly outward and that the original casters meant it as a warning, and the keep has been quietly furious about it for a decade."},"unmadecity":{"label":"The Four Bearers who carried the","look":"Four hooded figures in black glass on the first terrace of the Hollow Market, carved from behind only, walking away from the viewer towards the rock wall, an empty chair raised between them on their shoulders. Their hoods tilt down. You can walk all the way round them, but the wall is too close to see their fronts, and the glass on that side is left rough and uncut. The chair on their shoulders is real, an old one, and is replaced whenever it rots.","plaque":"They did not speak.","custom":"Each year, before Yse leaves to dust the Empty Chair on the Council floor, the market falls silent while they dust the glass chair on the Bearers' shoulders with the same cloth. Market children climb the chair at night on a dare; it is very bad luck to sit in it.","dispute":"Murmur claims that one of the four Bearers was from Murmur and that the figure second from the left has a rope-bridge spinner's stoop. Hush says nobody knows who the four were, which is the point, and has refused three times to let a Murmur carver near the statue to check."},"nightfen":{"label":"The Bandaged Woman","look":"A figure of black fen-oak at the head of the oldest cellar stair, half-risen out of the ground as if climbing up into air. Her face and eyes are wrapped entirely in a carved bandage, so there is no face at all, only folds. One hand gropes forward; the other holds the hand of a much smaller figure still below the ground, of which only the fingers show. The carver gave her bare feet, which the old fen folk say is wrong: nobody came up barefoot, they came up in Undercroft felt.","plaque":"Forty days. Longer.","custom":"On Black Lip Night every child in Nightfen ties a strip of sacking, cut from that year's Sack Posts, round the figure's groping wrist. By the end of the harvest the arm is wrapped to the shoulder. The strips are burned at the next harvest and the ash dug into the inkroot beds.","dispute":"Which grandmother she is. Aun says the figure is her mother, who pulled the first sack over a Lightforge lamp with her old bandage; Hask's family say it is Hask's grandmother, whose bandage he keeps in his boot. Lightforge patrols complain that she faces the border road as a provocation; Nightfen turned her to face the road on purpose."},"murkwater":{"label":"The Unchalked Door","look":"A single door of grey cistern slate set upright in a frame at the end of the Door Wall, with no house behind it, only rock. It is carved with a knocker shaped like a closed hand and a latch nobody can lift. There is no figure and no name. The slate has never been chalked by its own owner, because nobody remembers who he was; the village tells it that the first old caster to come here wrote his half-name on this door every morning until one morning he couldn't, and no child came by.","plaque":"Read it to him.","custom":"Every dawn the child who reads the Door Wall (now Lom) chalks whatever half-name comes into their head on the Unchalked Door, reads it aloud, and wipes it off. The old casters listen from their steps. Newcomers to the village are asked to chalk it once on their first morning.","dispute":"Whether it should be left blank. Brisk wants a proper plaque with a list of every caster who has died in Murkwater, written down, for the record; the old casters, including Hent, say that writing them down is the very thing they came here to escape, and Brisk's motion at his village meeting failed by two votes to one."},"lanterncity":{"label":"Ithiel the Copyist","look":"A life-size bronze copyist seated at a real stone copying-desk by the Copying Rooms' street door, quill lifted, head bent, with a working bronze lamp on a jointed arm that leans out over the page. The face was rubbed smooth by centuries of apprentices' thumbs and is now nearly blank. The sculptor gave the copyist ink-stains on both hands; every copyist in the Vale will tell you that only the writing hand inks, and that the sculptor plainly never copied anything. The page under the quill is bronze too, and blank: nobody could agree what to put on it.","plaque":"ITHIEL THE COPYIST. The lamp leans. Copy it as it is.","custom":"A new copyist leaves the first honey cake of their career on Ithiel's bronze desk, not on their own sill, and the bronze lamp is lit every dusk at the same moment as the Lit Mountain. Children sit in the bronze lap to watch the Evening Names when the steps are crowded.","dispute":"The desk's footings are cut into the same rock as the oldest shelves' supports, and if the shelves go to Willowmere a faction of copyists wants Ithiel to go with them. Orlaith Vensel has said she will sit in the bronze lap until they carry her off too. Caddoc Vire, who has looked at the footings, says nobody is carrying anything anywhere until he's braced them."},"wickhaven":{"label":"The Eleven who held the gate","look":"Not a statue: eleven iron lamp-brackets set in an arc into the stone of the gate arch, each forged as a raised open hand holding a lamp lit from that knight's lamp in the Wick Tower. The smith who made them three hundred years ago used one mould for all eleven hands, though the tower book records that Knight Maudry Fenn had lost two fingers before the night began. Halcyra Dunmoor polishes them every autumn and has never mentioned it.","plaque":"ELEVEN HELD. THE ROAD WENT ON.","custom":"Travellers telling their Gate Story put a palm on the lowest bracket while they speak, and Gatekeeper Ammon Sael is said to write the stories of those who touch it a little more kindly. Every year on the anniversary the garrison eats knight's pears under the arch at dawn, with cinder-wine, and nobody can quite say when that started.","dispute":"A petition to add a twelfth bracket 'for those who served on the stair that night' comes up every few decades. It always fails, because Halcyra Dunmoor, the last person alive who was on that stair, stands up at the meeting and votes against it, and will not explain."},"mournhaven":{"label":"Saelen of the Drift","look":"A black-timber figure, twice life-size, standing at the very end of the Long Jetty with one arm stretched out over the water and a real lantern hanging from the hand. The face has been worn smooth by a thousand years of Drift weather and nobody minds. The carver gave Saelen a hand closed round the lantern ring; every Mournhaven rower knows you never hold a lantern out over open water, you tie it, so each year the rowers knot a fresh silver mourning-cord from the wooden wrist to the ring, on an iron cord-ring Ruan Teague forges new every spring.","plaque":"SAELEN OF THE DRIFT. Keeper of the dead before there were shelves.","custom":"The first lantern rowed out on any night is tied to Saelen's cord before any other hook is used, and whoever rows it says the dead's name to the statue first. On a full night, the families say, the road of lights runs out of Saelen's hand.","dispute":"The Lantern Council has asked, twice, to have a copy of the figure carved for the Gallery steps in Lanthys Vale. Mournhaven refuses, and says the original was keeping the dead before the Archive had a single cave, which is exactly the point of the quarrel over whose shelves the dead belong on."},"mournhaven_vf2":{"label":"The first woman of Backshelf","look":"No statue and no face: a plain shepherd's crook of grey thorn-wood set upright in a block of hill-stone by the meeting-house door, its hook worn bright at the inside curve. Hanging from the hook is a small clay lamp, unglazed, with a blank tag. Visitors from the Vale always assume something has been left off. Nothing has.","plaque":"A blank slate, deliberately left uncarved.","custom":"Every newcomer touches the crook on their first night, before the Second Naming, whether they keep their name or not. Sael Thorn lights the clay lamp on the crook first each dusk, before any lamp on the New Shelf.","dispute":"Every so often someone in Mournhaven, or a copyist up from the Vale, offers to search the Archive's back shelves and find out who the founder was, so her name can be carved on the slate. Backshelf always says no: she chose not to say, and they keep that. Fennel Downe argues, mostly to be difficult, that she may simply have been waiting for somebody to ask."},"anvilreach_vf1":{"label":"The Stillman of the Scar","look":"Not carved but a man become stone: grey granite-flesh gone the colour of the scar, standing at the cliff-top facing the Long Road, one hand raised a little from the hip as if about to speak or wave. His cloak has turned to stone mid-swing. Ravens nest in the crook of his raised arm and their droppings have whitened his shoulders. Below him, a marker-shaped slate set by the village; there are no sculptor’s errors, only weather’s: the wind has worn his face smooth on the west side, so that from the road he seems to have no features at all, and from the village side he looks like somebody’s uncle.","plaque":"(On the slate at his feet, cut in the Silent Sentinel’s short style.) HE STAYED TO SEE THE ROAD. SET. — and beneath, in a later hand, a row of tap-marks that spell the same words in seismic code.","custom":"Stillman’s Bread: every new tap-keeper climbs to him on their first day and leaves carry-bread at his feet; if the ravens eat it he has accepted them. The village also keeps an unspoken second custom: nobody shouts within sight of him. Children who forget are made to stand beside him for an hour in silence.","dispute":"Sedgemoor’s weavers cut rope-grass at the foot of the scar and say the rock belongs to everyone; Ravenscar says it belongs to the Stillman. Old Osgith Longroad insists his raised hand has risen a thumb’s width in ninety years, which half the village calls nonsense and the other half refuses to discuss; and someone keeps leaving single stones at the cliff-foot below him, a slowly growing cairn that Ravenscar has not yet decided whether to allow."},"anvilreach":{"label":"Ebba’s Marker","look":"A plain granite road-marker at the first mile west of Anvil Reach, waist-high and leaning very slightly towards the town, its top polished by six centuries of hands. The same two initials are cut into its face twice: once high up, deep and square and a little showy, in a young carver’s hand; and once below, forty years later, shallower, slightly shaky, the strokes finished with a small flourish nobody else on the Long Roads uses. Carvers say the second cut is the better one. Neither cut shows whose initials they are beyond the two letters, and Anvil Reach has deliberately never agreed on the man’s full name.","plaque":"None. Anvil Reach refuses to add one. The nearest thing is a chip-sized hollow worn at its foot where families on Marker Day touch the stone before they walk on.","custom":"On Marker Day, after the Chip Box is opened, couples who have quarrelled walk out to Ebba’s Marker together and put their hands on the two cuts, one each, young and old, before they eat their marker cake. Carvers pretend they do not do this. Most of them have.","dispute":"Whether Ebba was allowed to cut a living man’s name at all. Ebrin Gravenmile, nine generations down from her, says strictly she broke the oldest rule of the road; his stepdaughters and every young carver say she proved it. A quieter dispute: some of the forty-one markers cut with a bad batch of chisels are on this stretch of the Western Road, and nobody wants to be the one to say Ebba’s neighbours are cracking."},"stonebarrow":{"label":"The Unset Bier","look":"A full-size bier carved from one block of grey granite and set, absurdly and deliberately, on six granite shoulders: six squat pillars carved like bearers’ shoulder-pads, so that the stone bier floats a bearer’s height off the ground at the foot of the barrow, beside the Bearers’ Bench. Nineteen notches are cut along its rail, one per day. The sculptor carved snow on the bier but forgot to carve any on the shoulders, so the bearers seem to have walked out of a different, warmer winter; Stonebarrow children are told this is because the bearers were too warm with work for snow to settle on them.","plaque":"(Cut along the bier’s foot, in plain Holdspeech.) NINETEEN DAYS. NOT ONCE SET DOWN. CARRY THEM.","custom":"Every new bearer of the six, on the evening of their first Carry, ducks under the stone bier and stands there between the pillars for as long as it takes to hum the Hum through once, in their own version. Old bearers say you can tell who will last by whether they come out smiling.","dispute":"Anvil Reach paid for half the stone and carved the notches, and says that gives the carvers the right to walk the first mile first on Marker Day; Stonebarrow says the carvers cut nineteen notches in an afternoon and the bearers walked nineteen days. And Ingrith Barrowfoot’s mother, who carried in that winter, always said there should be twenty notches, not nineteen, and would never say what the twentieth day was."},"tempest":{"label":"Pell of Brightwater","look":"A life-size figure of beaten iron, hung by its own chain from the inner wall beside the Brightwater Cord, so it swings with the bastion and turns its face to whatever wind is coming. He holds a single great chain-link against his chest with both fists. The smiths who made it gave him a full head of dark hair, as if he had never charged once; every remembrance the riders climb up and paint grey streaks back from his brow, and every spring the rain takes them off. They also left off his life-cord, so the riders made him one.","plaque":"PELL OF BRIGHTWATER. HE DID NOT CUT IT. (Below it, scratched by a hundred riders' knives: WORTH THE GREY.)","custom":"At every Count-Off, when a rider does not come back, the rider who stood beside them climbs up and ties a knot in Pell's cord. On Brightwater night the whole cord is cut down and flown to Harrowgate and let go off the Ledge, and a new, bare one is tied on him the next morning.","dispute":"The statue swings, so the Hold has to decide which way to chain it still on calm days. For centuries it rested facing east, towards Brightwater's ruins and the Stonehold road. Since the Edict talk the riders drill facing south, and the younger captains have started chaining Pell to face Lightforge. Hesper Quill keeps quietly turning him back east in the night, and nobody has caught her."},"skybridge":{"label":"Vael Korr","look":"The only statue in Stormreach that does not move: a striding figure made from a single unbroken chain, coiled and welded by the wrights into the shape of a man with one hand held out across the yard floor, as if offering it to someone on a bridge. It stands on the Chainyard floor beside the Great Swage. There is one gap in the coil, under the left ribs, where the wrights left a link out on purpose for the knife. His feet are cast from old Stonehold links, black and low-ringing, salvaged from before Brightwater.","plaque":"VAEL KORR. NO REALM SHOULD EVER BE ALONE. FORGED STANDING STILL.","custom":"Stand-Day begins when the yard-master touches Korr's outstretched hand, and ends when the youngest child in Skybridge manages to stand still long enough to touch it too. In some years that is a minute. In one famous year, a girl of six held out until the night bell.","dispute":"Half of Skybridge wants the Stonehold-iron feet recast in Metalcrown iron, which rings truer and does not remind anyone of Brightwater. The other half, led by the old wrights and by Ord Karrow, who oils the feet every week, says that a statue of the man who said no Realm should be alone should keep one Realm's iron in it whether Skybridge likes it or not. Juniper Rask says the feet ring better than anything in the yard, and won't say why that might matter."},"windspire_vf2":{"label":"Orrin Who Stayed","look":"Not a statue anyone commissioned: a seated figure carved straight out of the rock wall of the Anchorites' cave below the Letting Ledge, by the Anchorites themselves, a stroke at a time over generations. He sits, which in Stormreach is the pose of a funeral, with his hands open on his knees, palms up, and his face turned up towards the Ledge. The face has never been finished: each Anchorite adds a line, and none will be the one to complete it. His lap is full of things mourners have dropped: rings, buttons, a herald's brass horn, a baby's shoe.","plaque":"(scratched, not carved, at his feet) ORRIN STAYED. SOMEONE CAME BACK.","custom":"Mourners at a letting who cannot bear to let all of someone go drop one small thing a hand's breadth short of the Spill, where the Anchorites can catch it. The Anchorites set it in Orrin's lap. Nobody in Harrowgate admits to doing this. Everybody's family has.","dispute":"Skyrend's plan to widen the Letting Ledge for Assembly funerals would cut through the cave roof and take Orrin's head off. Essa Lash and the netters, who think the Anchorites are fools, are fighting the widening to save him anyway, because, Essa says, 'a fool who catches what you drop is still catching it'. Ione Lash's secret fittings and Mabry Holloway's husband are both on the wrong side of that rock."},"tempest_vf0":{"label":"The Door-Sitters","look":"A copper relief, green with age and still faintly warm in storms, cast into the outside of the herders' cave door from rods melted down after that night. Five herders sit with their backs to the door, shoulders together, heads down against the wind, goats crowded at their feet. Sitting is the funeral pose in Stormreach, and the sculptor made them look, Dunmere says, too much like mourners and not enough like people who were furious. The midwife who counted a baby into the world inside that cave the same night is not on it at all.","plaque":"THEY SAT SO WE COULD STAND.","custom":"Everyone in Dunmere stands to attention passing the door, even herders running strays. A child born in a storm is carried past it the next morning, and the strike count is chalked on the copper beside the sitters' feet. The rain always washes it off by evening, and that is considered lucky.","dispute":"Two-Hundred-and-Six Clatter has spent seventy years demanding that the midwife, Old Brindle Cass, be added to the door, standing, counting. Tempest Hold riders want 'Worth the grey' cut under the plaque; Dunmere refuses, because not one of the five had a spark or cast a charge that night, and that, Dunmere says, is the whole point. Lately Hennet Sparrow has been measuring the door with the same callipers she uses on rod nineteen."}};

/* People of the Nine Realms: "People you have met" in the Record. Data: window.VHPEOPLE (by NPC id), window.VHSTATUES (by site). */
(function () {
  var P = window.VHPEOPLE || {}, ST = window.VHSTATUES || {};
  function S() { try { return window.__vh && window.__vh.S(); } catch (e) { return null; } }
  function esc(t) { return String(t == null ? "" : t).replace(/[&<>"]/g, function (c) { return { "&": "&amp;", "<": "&lt;", ">": "&gt;", '"': "&quot;" }[c]; }); }
  var css = document.createElement("style");
  css.textContent = ".vhppl details{border:1px solid #3a2e1c;border-radius:8px;margin:.35rem 0;padding:.4rem .6rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,.18)}.vhppl summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#e8c070}.vhppl summary em{font-style:normal;font-weight:400;opacity:.6;font-size:.85em}.vhppl p{margin:.3rem 0}.vhppl b{color:#d8b878;font-weight:600}.vhppl .q{font-style:italic;opacity:.88}.vhppl .dim{opacity:.6}.vhppl h4{margin:.6rem 0 .2rem;font:600 .75rem/1 var(--sans,system-ui);letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#b89a60}";
  document.head.appendChild(css);
  function row(lab, v) { return v ? "<p><b>" + lab + ".</b> " + esc(v) + "</p>" : ""; }
  function person(id) {
    var p = P[id]; if (!p) return "";
    var lang = (p.languages || []).map(function (l) { return l.tongue + (l.level ? " (" + l.level + ")" : ""); }).join("; ");
    return "<details><summary>" + esc(p.name) + " <em>· " + esc(p.age) + " · " + esc(p.role) + "</em></summary>" +
      '<p class="q">“' + esc(p.about) + "”</p>" + row("Looks", p.look) + row("Voice", p.voice) + row("Speaks", lang) +
      row("Childhood", p.childhood) + row("School", p.schooling) + row("Life", p.history) + row("Family", p.family) +
      (p.hero ? row("Hero", p.hero.who + ". " + (p.hero.why || "")) : "") +
      (p.band ? row("Favourite act", p.band.act + (p.band.realm ? " (" + p.band.realm + ")" : "") + ". " + (p.band.why || "") + (p.band.rv_vote ? " This season's lamp: " + p.band.rv_vote : "")) : "") +
      (p.vote ? row("Their vote", (p.vote.local || "") + (p.vote.edict ? " On the Edict: " + p.vote.edict : "")) : "") + row("Wants", p.wants) + "</details>";
  }
  function html() {
    var s = S(), met = (s && s.ppl) || {}, ids = Object.keys(met).filter(function (k) { return P[k]; }), total = Object.keys(P).length;
    var h = '<div class="sec">People you have met · ' + ids.length + "/" + total + '</div><div class="vhppl">';
    if (!ids.length) h += '<p class="dim">Every trader, smith and townsperson you talk to is written here: where they grew up, who taught them, who they look up to, the music they love and how they vote.</p>';
    var by = {}; ids.forEach(function (k) { var p = P[k], key = (p.realm || "") + "|" + (p.town || p.site); (by[key] = by[key] || []).push(k); });
    var lastR = "";
    Object.keys(by).sort().forEach(function (key) {
      var r = key.split("|")[0], town = key.split("|")[1]; if (r !== lastR) { h += "<h4>" + esc(r) + "</h4>"; lastR = r; }
      h += '<p class="dim" style="margin:.4rem 0 .1rem">' + esc(town) + "</p>" + by[key].map(person).join("");
    });
    var sm = Object.keys(met).filter(function (k) { return k.indexOf("stat:") === 0 && ST[k.slice(5)]; });
    if (sm.length) { h += "<h4>Monuments</h4>"; sm.forEach(function (k) { var v = ST[k.slice(5)]; h += "<details><summary>" + esc(v.label) + "</summary><p>" + esc(v.look) + "</p>" + (v.plaque ? '<p class="q">' + esc(v.plaque) + "</p>" : "") + row("Custom", v.custom) + row("The argument", v.dispute) + "</details>"; }); }
    return h + "</div>";
  }
  var mo = new MutationObserver(function () {
    var sb = document.getElementById("sbody"); if (!sb || sb.querySelector(".vhppl")) return;
    var h2 = sb.querySelector("h2"); if (!h2 || h2.textContent.trim() !== "The Record") return;
    var anchor = sb.querySelector(".vhpla") || sb.querySelector(".vhfal") || h2.nextElementSibling || h2;
    if (anchor.closest && anchor.closest("div") && anchor.classList && anchor.classList.contains("vhpla")) anchor = anchor.parentElement;
    var w = document.createElement("div"); w.innerHTML = html(); anchor.insertAdjacentElement("afterend", w);
  });
  function arm() { var sb = document.getElementById("sbody"); if (sb) mo.observe(sb, { childList: true, subtree: true }); else setTimeout(arm, 500); }
  arm();
})();
